window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 681, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Orchard Foods Ltd is in liquidation. Its floating charge assets realised £200,000 after the costs of realisation. Claims include employee wage arrears within the statutory limit, VAT of £30,000 owed to HMRC, £150,000 owed to Silverbank plc under a floating charge, and £60,000 owed to unsecured trade creditors. The company has no fixed charge assets. Assuming the prescribed part applies, in what order are these claims met from the floating charge assets?", "options": {"A": "HMRC for the VAT first because the Crown has priority, then employee wage arrears, then Silverbank, then the unsecured trade creditors", "B": "Employee wage arrears and HMRC equally, then the unsecured creditors in full, then Silverbank, with no prescribed part set aside", "C": "Employee wage arrears, then Silverbank, then HMRC for the VAT, then the prescribed part, with the unsecured creditors left to share the remainder", "D": "Silverbank first, then employee wage arrears, then HMRC for the VAT, then the unsecured trade creditors share whatever balance remains after those claims", "E": "Employee wage arrears, then HMRC for the VAT, then the prescribed part for unsecured creditors, then Silverbank, with any balance to the unsecured creditors"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Employee wage arrears, then HMRC for the VAT, then the prescribed part for unsecured creditors, then Silverbank, with any balance to the unsecured creditors): Out of floating charge assets, liquidation costs are paid first, then ordinary preferential creditors such as employee wage arrears within the limit, then secondary preferential creditors such as HMRC for VAT. The prescribed part is then set aside for the unsecured creditors, before the floating charge holder is paid, and any balance after Silverbank goes to the unsecured creditors. HMRC lost general Crown priority, and its VAT claim ranks as secondary preferential only after employee claims. Silverbank cannot take ahead of preferential creditors from floating charge assets."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Rachel Doyle died owning a freehold house worth £450,000, which her executors do not wish to sell yet, and a modest amount of cash. Inheritance Tax is payable on the estate. The executors have little cash available and want to know about spreading the payment. They ask the solicitor which statement correctly describes the instalment option for Inheritance Tax payable on death. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Instalments are available only for lifetime gifts, so tax on assets passing on death must be paid in one sum on delivery of the account", "B": "Tax on land, including a house, can be paid in ten yearly instalments, the first due six months after the end of the month of death", "C": "Tax on land can be paid in ten yearly instalments, but the first instalment is not due until a grant of representation has been obtained", "D": "Tax on land can be paid in ten yearly instalments, and the instalments remain payable on their yearly dates even if the land is sold", "E": "Tax on any asset, including cash, can be paid in five yearly instalments, provided the personal representatives elect within the first year"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tax on land, including a house, can be paid in ten yearly instalments, the first due six months after the end of the month of death): Tax attributable to land, and to certain business assets, can be paid by ten equal yearly instalments, and the first falls due on the ordinary due date, which is the end of the sixth month after the month of death. The option does not extend to cash. It is not linked to the grant, and the tax itself must still be paid before the grant if it is not paid by instalments. On a sale of the property the outstanding instalments become payable at once."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Kyle and Piers spend an afternoon drinking heavily at a private airfield. Piers, a trained pilot, proposes a flight in his light aircraft, and Kyle, who is sober enough to see that Piers is badly intoxicated, agrees to go with him. Shortly after take off Piers crashes the aircraft, and Kyle is seriously injured. Kyle sues Piers in negligence. Piers pleads volenti non fit injuria and, in the alternative, contributory negligence. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Volenti will fail, because the Road Traffic Act 1988 prevents the defence being used against a passenger in any powered vehicle", "B": "Volenti will succeed, but only if Kyle signed a written waiver before the flight, because consent must be express", "C": "Volenti will fail and damages will be reduced under the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 for Kyle's share of the fault", "D": "Volenti will fail, because mere knowledge of the pilot's drunken state cannot amount to consent to the risk of injury", "E": "Volenti is likely to succeed and defeat the claim, because Kyle knew the extent of the danger and freely accepted it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Volenti is likely to succeed and defeat the claim, because Kyle knew the extent of the danger and freely accepted it): Volenti requires full knowledge of the nature and extent of the risk and a free agreement to accept it. Kyle saw that Piers was severely intoxicated and chose to fly, so he accepted a risk that was obvious and extreme. The statute that blocks the defence in motor cases applies to road vehicles under compulsory insurance, not to an aircraft. Consent can be implied from conduct, so a written waiver is not needed."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Rhys Dunmore is interviewed under caution about a burglary, having first received legal advice. He answers no comment to every question. At his trial he gives evidence that he was at a friend's house at the time and names the friend, which he never told the police. The prosecution invites the jury to draw an inference. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "No inference is possible, because interview silence can be used only if the defendant is later charged with an offence of dishonesty", "B": "An inference will be drawn by the jury, because reliance at trial on an unmentioned fact has that mandatory effect", "C": "The jury may convict on the strength of the inference alone, because silence at interview is treated as corroboration of guilt", "D": "The jury may draw such inferences as appear proper if he could reasonably have been expected to mention the alibi when questioned", "E": "An inference may be drawn only if the alibi witness was named in the caution that the police administered"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The jury may draw such inferences as appear proper if he could reasonably have been expected to mention the alibi when questioned): Section 34 of the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994 allows the court or jury to draw such inferences as appear proper where the defendant relies at trial on a fact that he failed to mention when questioned under caution, and which he could reasonably have been expected to mention. The inference is discretionary, not mandatory. Under section 38(3) a person cannot be convicted solely because of an inference, so silence is no substitute for evidence. The caution itself does not need to name the alibi witness."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Chloe Brennan and Imran Siddiqui intend to start a graphic design business together and will contribute equal amounts of money. They want to keep their financial results private, avoid any registration at Companies House, and be taxed on the profits as their own personal income. They have not yet thought about limiting their personal liability. Which of the following business vehicles best matches what they want?", "options": {"A": "Two sole traders trading under one name, because neither needs to register and each keeps separate private accounts and tax returns", "B": "A private limited company, because its accounts stay confidential to its shareholders and its profits are taxed as the directors' own personal income", "C": "A private company limited by guarantee, because it need not file any accounts and its members are taxed personally on the profits it earns", "D": "A general partnership, because it needs no registration, publishes no accounts and taxes each partner on a share of the profits earned", "E": "A limited liability partnership, because it is formed informally by agreement between the members, files no accounts and taxes each member on a share of the profits"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A general partnership, because it needs no registration, publishes no accounts and taxes each partner on a share of the profits earned): A partnership under the Partnership Act 1890 arises from carrying on a business in common with a view to profit, and it needs no registration or public filing. The partners are taxed individually on their shares of the profits, which matches the clients' wishes, although their personal liability is unlimited. A company or an LLP must register and file accounts at Companies House, and a company pays corporation tax on its own profits rather than passing them to its owners. Two people trading together cannot each be a sole trader, so that suggestion simply describes a partnership."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "After a disturbance at a rally, a city council bans all protests of any kind in the city centre for twelve months. Taylor Reid, who had planned a small silent vigil, wishes to challenge the ban as an interference with her right to peaceful assembly under article 11. Her solicitor must decide which standard of review the court will apply to the council's decision. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court asks only whether the ban was authorised by the statute under which the council made it, which decides the challenge", "B": "The court applies proportionality, asking whether a less intrusive measure could have met the council's aim and whether a fair balance was struck", "C": "The court applies only the Wednesbury test, because judicial review of any public body decision is limited to asking whether it was manifestly unreasonable", "D": "The court applies proportionality, but only to ask whether the council's aim was legitimate, and not to consider alternative measures", "E": "The court asks only whether the council acted in good faith, because policing of public order is for the executive and not the court"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The court applies proportionality, asking whether a less intrusive measure could have met the council's aim and whether a fair balance was struck): Where a Convention right is engaged, the court reviews the interference for proportionality, which asks whether the aim is important enough, whether the measure is rationally connected to it, whether a less intrusive measure could achieve it and whether a fair balance has been struck. A blanket twelve month ban on all protests, including a silent vigil, invites precisely that scrutiny. Wednesbury review alone is too narrow, and good faith or vires are not the whole inquiry. A partial test that skips alternatives is not the full approach."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "A small firm acted last year for Hartley Ltd, whose owner Mr Hartley is now the key prosecution witness in an arson case against Dilys, who is accused of setting fire to his warehouse. Dilys asks the firm to defend her. The firm still holds confidential files on Hartley Ltd's finances showing serious debts, which would help undermine Hartley's evidence. The same two solicitors would handle both matters. Which of the following best describes what the firm should do?", "options": {"A": "Act for Dilys and apply to court for permission to use the information, because only a court can override a duty of confidentiality", "B": "Act for Dilys and tell her that a conflict exists, because informing her satisfies the duty of confidentiality owed to Hartley Ltd", "C": "Act for Dilys and use the information, because duties to a former client end when the retainer comes to an end", "D": "Act for Dilys but not use the information, because an information barrier around the files protects the former client", "E": "Decline to act for Dilys, because it cannot use the former client's confidential information for her and cannot disclose it to her"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to act for Dilys, because it cannot use the former client's confidential information for her and cannot disclose it to her): Confidentiality to a former client continues after the retainer ends. The firm cannot give Dilys the benefit of what it knows, and it cannot act for her while withholding material information that she is entitled to, because her interests would suffer. A barrier is not realistic when the same two solicitors would handle both matters. The proper course is to decline Dilys's instructions."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya is convicted at the Crown Court of two burglaries of dwellings committed in different towns two months apart, against different victims. The judge has decided on a sentence of three years for each offence. Priya has no other matters outstanding. Which of the following best describes how the judge should approach whether the sentences run concurrently or consecutively?", "options": {"A": "Concurrent terms are usual, because consecutive terms are reserved for offences arising from a single incident or series of events", "B": "Consecutive terms are usual for offences on separate occasions against different victims, subject to a check that the total is just and proportionate", "C": "Either is open, and the totality principle applies only where the sentences are passed on different days rather than at a single hearing", "D": "Concurrent terms are required because both offences are of the same type and are dealt with at the same sentencing hearing by one judge", "E": "Consecutive terms must be imposed, and the total is simply the sum of the two terms without adjustment"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Consecutive terms are usual for offences on separate occasions against different victims, subject to a check that the total is just and proportionate): Where offences are committed on different occasions against different victims, the guideline approach is to impose consecutive sentences. The judge must then step back and apply totality, reducing the individual terms if the overall total would be disproportionate. Concurrent sentences are for offences closely linked in time, victim or conduct. The suggestion that totality matters only for sentences passed on different days misstates the principle, which applies at any single hearing."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Hari and Isla bought a house in joint names in 2010, each paying half of the deposit, and made no declaration about beneficial shares. In 2013 Hari moved out and bought a flat in his sole name. From 2013 Isla paid all the mortgage, insurance and repairs on the house, and Hari paid nothing towards it. Hari now says the house is held equally. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "Shares are fixed by their initial contributions of one half each, because a resulting trust determines the shares", "B": "The house is held equally, because the presumption of equal shares for joint owners continues after separation", "C": "Isla has only a right to be repaid what she spent, because her payments cannot alter the shares of joint owners", "D": "The court may find that intentions changed and impute shares to Isla above half, on the whole course of dealing", "E": "Isla takes the whole interest, because Hari abandoned the property when he bought another home in his own name"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court may find that intentions changed and impute shares to Isla above half, on the whole course of dealing): Under Jones v Kernott, a presumption of equal shares in a joint names home can be displaced if the parties' intentions changed after the purchase, and where the court cannot find the actual intention it may impute one that is fair. Isla alone paid for the house for many years while Hari contributed nothing, which supports a larger share for her. Hari does not lose all his interest by buying elsewhere. The initial equal contributions do not freeze the shares for all time."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Ms Green is a solicitor acting for Patel Fabrications Ltd and its director, Mr Patel, in a bank loan of £200,000. The bank requires Mr Patel's wife, who has no role in the business, to charge the family home as security and to take independent legal advice. Mr Patel asks Ms Green to advise Mrs Patel, and Ms Green realises that proper advice about the risks might lead Mrs Patel to refuse, which would harm her existing clients' interests. What should Ms Green do?", "options": {"A": "Advise Mrs Patel but disclose to Mr Patel the content of her advice, because he is already her client in the transaction", "B": "Advise Mrs Patel with Mr Patel's consent alone, because the conflict is his to waive as the borrowing director", "C": "Advise Mrs Patel only on the meaning of the documents and leave her to judge the financial risk herself", "D": "Advise Mrs Patel and the bank together, since sharing one solicitor will avoid delay and reduce cost for all parties", "E": "Decline to advise Mrs Patel because of the conflict with her existing clients, and tell them she must receive advice from another solicitor"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to advise Mrs Patel because of the conflict with her existing clients, and tell them she must receive advice from another solicitor): A guarantor in Mrs Patel's position needs advice that is genuinely independent, covering the risks, including that she could lose her home. Ms Green cannot give it because her duty to her existing clients conflicts with what Mrs Patel needs to be told, and conflicts of interest with existing clients cannot be managed by consent from only one side. Passing on Mrs Patel's confidences would breach the duty of confidentiality owed to her. Limiting the advice or sharing a solicitor with the bank would not meet the independence required."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Reeve's will, signed and witnessed in 2020, has a clause leaving £5,000 to his friend Tilda. The figure has been struck through and £10,000 written above it. Mr Reeve initialled the margin, but no witness did, and nothing shows when the change was made. He has now died, and the original figure can still be read beneath the deletion. What is the effect of the change?", "options": {"A": "The alteration is valid if Tilda proves that Mr Reeve intended £10,000, because intention can overcome the lack of witnesses", "B": "The alteration is presumed to have been made before execution, so Tilda takes £10,000 as the testator's final intention", "C": "The whole clause is revoked by the deletion, so Tilda takes nothing and the sum falls into residue", "D": "The alteration is presumed to have been made after execution and is invalid, so the gift is £5,000 while the original words remain legible", "E": "The alteration is valid, because the testator's initials in the margin satisfy the signature requirement for alterations"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The alteration is presumed to have been made after execution and is invalid, so the gift is £5,000 while the original words remain legible): Section 21 of the Wills Act 1837 provides that an alteration is invalid unless executed like a will, which means signed and witnessed by two witnesses, although initials of the testator and witnesses in the margin can satisfy this. Here no witnesses initialled, and the alteration is presumed to have been made after execution. It is therefore ineffective, and the original £5,000 stands because the words are still legible. Intention cannot cure the missing formalities."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Jake Ormerod, a business owner, was named in a speech by an MP in a House of Commons debate, in which she said that Jake had 'obtained public contracts by bribery'. The statement is untrue and was reported in newspapers. Jake wants to sue the MP for defamation in respect of the words spoken in the chamber, and he asks his solicitor to issue the claim at once, telling her he does not care about the cost. How should the solicitor advise?", "options": {"A": "Issue the claim against the MP, because privilege does not protect a statement that the speaker knew to be untrue", "B": "Advise that parliamentary privilege bars a claim over the words spoken, and decline to issue it because it is not properly arguable", "C": "Issue the claim after asking the Speaker to waive privilege, because the Speaker may allow proceedings to be brought", "D": "Issue the claim, because Jake has the right to clear his name and the court can decide whether privilege applies", "E": "Issue a claim for malicious falsehood instead of defamation, because that cause of action is outside the scope of parliamentary privilege"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise that parliamentary privilege bars a claim over the words spoken, and decline to issue it because it is not properly arguable): Article 9 of the Bill of Rights 1689 protects freedom of speech and debates in Parliament, so words spoken in proceedings in the chamber cannot be questioned in any court. That protection is absolute, so it does not depend on good faith. A solicitor must not bring a claim that is not properly arguable and must give honest advice, so Jake's willingness to pay does not change the position. A different cause of action based on the same words would meet the same bar."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Bernard Okafor was the estate owner of an unregistered house and, in 2010, granted an option to purchase to Willow Properties Ltd. The option is a Class C(iv) land charge. Willow registered it under the name 'Bernie Okafor', the name by which the owner was commonly known. His name on the title deeds is Bernard Okafor. In 2024, Bernard sold the house to Chidi Eze, who searched the land charges register in the name Bernard Okafor and found nothing. Is Chidi bound by the option?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the option is an equitable interest, so the doctrine of notice applies and Chidi can be bound by it", "B": "No, because options over unregistered land cannot be registered as land charges and bind only a buyer with notice", "C": "No, because registration under a name other than the owner's full name is ineffective, so Chidi takes free", "D": "Yes, because any registration, wherever made, gives actual notice of the charge to every later purchaser of the land", "E": "Yes, because the name used was the one by which Bernard was known, which is enough to make the registration effective"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because registration under a name other than the owner's full name is ineffective, so Chidi takes free): A land charge must be registered against the full name of the estate owner as it appears in the title documents. Registration in a variant name does not protect the charge, because a search in the correct name will not reveal it, and a purchaser who searches properly is not bound. The doctrine of notice does not operate where an interest is registrable under the Land Charges Act 1972. An option is a Class C(iv) land charge, and Willow's failure to register correctly leaves the option void against Chidi."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Corvid Systems Ltd agreed to install an accounting platform for Fenwick Builders Ltd for £60,000, with a clause promising a telephone helpdesk during working hours throughout the first year. The contract does not say whether the helpdesk promise is a condition. In month two the helpdesk was unavailable for two days because of a power failure. Fenwick, which has used the platform successfully since go-live, wrote to Corvid terminating the contract with immediate effect. Which of the following best describes the effect of Fenwick's letter?", "options": {"A": "Termination was valid, because the helpdesk promise was a fundamental term and Corvid failed to remedy the failure within the same week", "B": "Termination was valid, because Fenwick gave notice promptly and the court will not examine how serious the helpdesk failure was or its effect on Fenwick's business", "C": "Termination was wrongful unless the outage deprived Fenwick of substantially the whole benefit of the contract, which on these facts it did not", "D": "Termination was invalid, because a promise ancillary to the main supply is a warranty and a warranty gives damages only", "E": "Termination was valid, because any breach of an express promise entitles the innocent party to treat the contract as ended"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Termination was wrongful unless the outage deprived Fenwick of substantially the whole benefit of the contract, which on these facts it did not): The term has not been labelled a condition and is not one by statute or precedent, so it is an innominate term. For such a term the right to terminate depends on whether the breach deprives the innocent party of substantially the whole benefit it was to receive. A two day outage on a platform in successful use does not do so, so Fenwick's letter was itself a repudiatory breach. The conclusion that termination was invalid for the wrong reason overlooks that a serious enough breach of an innominate term would permit termination."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "A court finds that Mr Ford was subjected to undue influence by his brother Len, who procured clause 5 of Mr Ford's will, giving Len a holiday cottage. Mr Ford's will, signed in 2025, has six clauses, and the residue goes to his two children. The other clauses reflect Mr Ford's own wishes and were not affected by Len's pressure. The executors ask how the court will deal with the will. What is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The court will refuse to admit clause 5 to probate and admit the remainder of the will, because only the part procured by influence fails", "B": "The will is admitted in full, but Len holds the cottage on constructive trust for the residuary beneficiaries", "C": "Clause 5 stands, but the other beneficiaries may rescind the gift, as they could for a lifetime gift procured by influence over the donor", "D": "The will is admitted in full, because undue influence in a will is dealt with only by a family provision claim", "E": "The whole will is invalid and probate is refused, because undue influence taints every part of the document it infects"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court will refuse to admit clause 5 to probate and admit the remainder of the will, because only the part procured by influence fails): A will procured by undue influence is invalid, but where the influence affected only part of it, the court can admit the rest to probate and omit the tainted words. Clause 5 was the product of Len's pressure, so it is excluded, and the remaining clauses, which reflect Mr Ford's genuine wishes, take effect. The remedy lies in the grant of probate itself, not in a trust or in rescission, and a family provision claim is a different matter."}, {"id": 696, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Leona Cardwell lives in a house registered in the sole name of her partner, Marcus. She contributed £40,000 towards the purchase price and so holds a beneficial interest under a trust of land. Leona worries Marcus may sell and wants to protect her interest on the register. The house is Marcus's main asset, and Leona intends to remain living there. Which of the following would be the most appropriate entry for Leona's solicitor to apply for?", "options": {"A": "A unilateral notice, because Leona can enter it without Marcus's agreement and it will protect her interest against any buyer", "B": "A caution against first registration, because Leona's interest arises under a trust and cautions protect equitable rights", "C": "No entry, because her interest will bind any buyer as an overriding interest while she lives in the property", "D": "An agreed notice, because a beneficial interest under a trust of land is a proprietary interest that may be protected by notice", "E": "A Form A restriction, which stops registration of a sale by Marcus alone and so prevents overreaching of her share"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A Form A restriction, which stops registration of a sale by Marcus alone and so prevents overreaching of her share): A beneficial interest under a trust of land cannot be protected by a notice because section 33 of the Land Registration Act 2002 excludes interests under trusts of land. The appropriate protection is a restriction, and the standard Form A restriction stops a disposition by a sole proprietor that produces capital money from being registered without a court order. That prevents Marcus alone from overreaching Leona's share. Her actual occupation gives some protection, but only while she remains there and only against some purchasers, so a restriction is the safer entry."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Stone Trustees Ltd is a trust corporation that holds itself out as having specialist skill in the management of family trusts. It is appointed as trustee of a trust that holds a portfolio of shares and a let property. A beneficiary asks what standard of care the statutory duty of care under the Trustee Act 2000 imposes on the company. Which of the following best states that standard?", "options": {"A": "A duty to achieve the best possible result for the beneficiaries, with the success of each decision judged using hindsight", "B": "Such care and skill as is reasonable in the circumstances, given the special knowledge it claims and the standard expected of professionals", "C": "The same standard as a lay trustee, because the statute draws no distinction between professional trustees and unpaid trustees", "D": "A duty that applies only when the trustee is exercising a power of investment, and not when it is exercising other powers", "E": "The care that an ordinary prudent person of business would take in managing his own affairs, as stated in the older case of Speight v Gaunt"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Such care and skill as is reasonable in the circumstances, given the special knowledge it claims and the standard expected of professionals): Section 1 Trustee Act 2000 requires a trustee to exercise such care and skill as is reasonable in the circumstances. It takes account of any special knowledge or experience the trustee has or claims to have, and of the higher level reasonably expected of a trustee acting in the course of a business or profession. The standard is therefore higher for Stone Trustees Ltd than for a lay trustee. It does not guarantee results, and it applies to many powers, not only investment."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Marwood Ltd franchised a food outlet to Pellow, and the agreement provided that on early termination for breach Pellow would pay Marwood £30,000 representing lost fees. Pellow stopped trading after four months and Marwood claims £30,000. Pellow argues that the clause is a penalty because the sum exceeds Marwood's actual loss of about £18,000. Marwood shows that the sum is its standard figure protecting its brand and network. Which statement best states the test the court will apply to the clause?", "options": {"A": "The clause is a penalty if it is extravagant and unconscionable compared with the greatest loss that could conceivably follow from the breach", "B": "The clause is a penalty if it is not a genuine pre-estimate of the loss Marwood would suffer on early termination of the agreement", "C": "The clause is a penalty only if it imposes a detriment out of all proportion to any legitimate interest of Marwood in the performance of the contract", "D": "The clause is a penalty if the franchisee had no genuine opportunity to negotiate it, because inequality of bargaining power makes the sum unenforceable", "E": "The clause is a penalty if the sum payable exceeds the actual loss Marwood proves it suffered, measured at the date of the breach"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The clause is a penalty only if it imposes a detriment out of all proportion to any legitimate interest of Marwood in the performance of the contract): The modern test is whether the clause is a secondary obligation that imposes a detriment out of all proportion to any legitimate interest of the innocent party in the performance of the primary obligation. Legitimate interest can go beyond compensation for loss, and here Marwood's brand and network protection counts. The genuine pre-estimate test is no longer the criterion. Exceeding actual loss is not decisive by itself, and bargaining strength is only a factor in assessing the clause."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Isla holds a yearly periodic tenancy of a paddock, which began on 1 March 2020 and is not protected by any statute. The rent is paid annually and the parties agreed nothing about how the tenancy should end. In May 2026 the landlord decides to end the arrangement and asks what notice to quit the common law requires. Which of the following correctly states the notice the landlord must give?", "options": {"A": "Six months' notice, which may expire on any day because the period of the tenancy is a year", "B": "Not less than half a year's notice, expiring on the last day of a year of the tenancy", "C": "Three months' notice, expiring on a quarter day, because the landlord may choose the shorter period", "D": "Four weeks' notice, expiring on any day, because that is the minimum notice for a periodic tenancy", "E": "One full year's notice, expiring on the next anniversary of the start of the tenancy"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Not less than half a year's notice, expiring on the last day of a year of the tenancy): At common law a yearly periodic tenancy ends only on notice of at least half a year, expiring on the last day of a year of the tenancy. This tenancy began on 1 March, so the notice must expire at the end of a tenancy year. Four weeks is the minimum for weekly or monthly periods, and a full year's notice is not required. The notice cannot expire on an arbitrary day or on a quarter day unless the parties agreed it."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Tom Burrows is a solicitor attending Wesley Ghosh, who is under arrest for burglary. In a private consultation Wesley admits to Tom that he committed the burglary. He then says he intends to tell the police that he was at his mother's house all evening. Disclosure so far has been limited to the time and address of the burglary. What should Tom do?", "options": {"A": "Disclose the admission to the interviewing officer, because the duty to the court outweighs the duty of confidentiality here", "B": "Let Wesley give the alibi once he has read it to Tom, because that makes it a prepared statement", "C": "Withdraw immediately and tell the police the firm can no longer act, because a confession means the solicitor cannot continue", "D": "Say nothing and sit through the interview, because privilege means a solicitor owes the police no duty of honesty", "E": "Advise Wesley that Tom cannot assist a false account, and that Wesley may instead choose to give no comment"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Advise Wesley that Tom cannot assist a false account, and that Wesley may instead choose to give no comment): A solicitor must not mislead the police or assist a client to give a false account, as this would breach the SRA Principles on honesty and the Code of Conduct. The admission is confidential and privileged, so it cannot be disclosed to the police. Tom can, however, continue to act and can advise Wesley that he is entitled to say nothing, while explaining the risk of an adverse inference. A confession does not oblige a solicitor to withdraw, although Tom would need to consider whether he could still act if Wesley insisted on giving the false alibi."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Orrin Hall Ltd hired the Marlowe Theatre for a one night charity concert on 12 June and paid a £5,000 hire fee in advance. Before the concert, a fire caused by a fault in the theatre's wiring made the hall unusable. Neither party was negligent. Orrin Hall wants its fee back and Marlowe accepts that the contract has been frustrated. Which of the following best states the legal effect of the frustrating event?", "options": {"A": "The contract was discharged from the date of the fire, but the fee stays with Marlowe because the loss lies where it falls under the common law", "B": "The contract is voidable at Orrin Hall's election, and the fee is recoverable only if it rescinds within a reasonable time after the fire", "C": "The contract is void from the outset, so the fee is recoverable in full and Marlowe cannot deduct any expenses it incurred in preparing the hall", "D": "The contract was discharged automatically on the date of the fire, and the fee is recoverable less any just sum for Marlowe's expenses", "E": "The contract continues until one party gives notice of termination, and the fee is recoverable only as damages for Marlowe's failure to provide the hall"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The contract was discharged automatically on the date of the fire, and the fee is recoverable less any just sum for Marlowe's expenses): Frustration discharges the contract automatically and without any election or notice, from the time of the frustrating event. Money paid before that time is recoverable under section 1(2) of the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943. The court may allow the payee to keep a just sum for expenses incurred before discharge, up to the amount paid. The old rule that losses lie where they fall no longer applies, and no breach has occurred, so damages are not the remedy."}, {"id": 702, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Brightwell Engineering Ltd is served with a claim form and particulars of claim on 1 June. Its solicitors think the contract's exclusive jurisdiction clause names the Irish courts. It is now day 10 after service and the acknowledgment of service is due in four days. The client wants the claimant told quickly that the claim is defended and says that a quantum report from its surveyor is nearly ready. Which step should the solicitors take now?", "options": {"A": "Write to the claimant relying on the clause and take no step until judgment is entered, then apply to set it aside on jurisdiction", "B": "Issue a Part 11 application immediately without an acknowledgment of service, because the objection goes to the court's power to hear the claim", "C": "File a defence on the merits and reserve the jurisdiction point, since a reservation of rights in the defence preserves the objection", "D": "File an acknowledgment of service and a defence together, then raise jurisdiction at the case management conference when the clause is argued", "E": "File an acknowledgment of service now, then issue a Part 11 application with supporting evidence within 14 days after filing it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (File an acknowledgment of service now, then issue a Part 11 application with supporting evidence within 14 days after filing it): Part 11 requires the defendant first to file an acknowledgment of service and then to apply within 14 days of filing it, supported by evidence. Filing the acknowledgment alone does not concede jurisdiction. Going straight to an application skips the required first step, while pleading a defence or waiting for the case management conference risks being treated as having accepted jurisdiction. Silence invites a default judgment and an uphill application to set it aside."}, {"id": 703, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Iona Mackay is a solicitor acting for Rory Pearce in a damages claim for £90,000. Rory's written instructions are not to settle for less than £75,000 without speaking to him. The defendant's solicitors send an open offer of £70,000, valid for seven days. Iona thinks the offer is generous given the evidence. Rory is trekking in a remote area and cannot be contacted for ten days. A colleague suggests that Iona should accept now. What should Iona do?", "options": {"A": "Accept the offer subject to Rory's approval, because a conditional acceptance can be withdrawn if he disagrees", "B": "Let the offer lapse, because Rory's instructions exclude any settlement below £75,000 and no further steps are needed", "C": "Accept the offer, because the duty to act in the client's best interests permits acceptance where the offer is generous", "D": "Accept the offer after taking the colleague's advice, because partners can authorise settlement on behalf of a client", "E": "Try to tell Rory of the offer at once and ask the defendant's solicitors for more time, and not accept without his authority"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Try to tell Rory of the offer at once and ask the defendant's solicitors for more time, and not accept without his authority): A solicitor acts on the client's instructions and cannot settle outside the authority given, even where she thinks the offer is good. The client must be told promptly of any offer, so Iona should try every means of reaching Rory and ask for an extension. A purported conditional acceptance would not bind the defendant or be safe for Rory, and letting the offer lapse without telling him fails the duty to inform. The best interests duty does not allow the solicitor to replace the client's decision with her own."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Corrie Haulage Ltd lost a fast track claim for £22,000 brought against it by a customer. The trial was heard in the county court by a district judge, who gave a reasoned judgment on 2 June. Corrie's directors believe the judge misunderstood the contract terms. The company's solicitor, who also notes that the customer's solicitors have already asked about payment, is asked how the company can challenge the decision. What is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Appeal to a circuit judge as of right, because appeals from district judges need no permission, within 28 days", "B": "Appeal to a High Court judge, with permission from the district judge alone, within 14 days of the decision", "C": "Appeal to a circuit judge, with permission from the district judge, within seven days because the claim is on the fast track", "D": "Appeal to a circuit judge in the county court, with permission from the district judge or the appeal court, within 21 days", "E": "Appeal to the Court of Appeal, with permission from the district judge, within 21 days of the decision"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Appeal to a circuit judge in the county court, with permission from the district judge or the appeal court, within 21 days): An appeal from a district judge's final decision in the county court lies to a circuit judge. Permission is required, and it may be sought first from the lower court and, if refused, from the appeal court. The appellant's notice must be filed within 21 days of the decision unless the lower court directs otherwise. A High Court appeal or the Court of Appeal is the wrong destination, and the track does not alter the time limit."}, {"id": 705, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "You act for Marcus and Ola, an unmarried couple buying a house in joint names. Marcus is contributing £90,000 and Ola £30,000. Both ask you to act for them. At the first meeting Marcus says the house should be held in shares reflecting contributions, but Ola says they agreed to share equally. They each ask you to say who is right. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Act for both and advise each in turn, because acting for joint purchasers is permitted where they have a common aim", "B": "Act for both and prepare a declaration of equal shares, because an equal split is the starting point for joint owners", "C": "Explain that you cannot advise both on the shares, because their interests conflict, and suggest separate advice", "D": "Act for Marcus only, because he provides most of the money and is more likely to need advice", "E": "Act for both and prepare a declaration of proportionate shares, because the larger contribution should decide the question"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that you cannot advise both on the shares, because their interests conflict, and suggest separate advice): On the question of how the beneficial interests are held, Marcus and Ola have conflicting interests, and a solicitor cannot act for both where there is a conflict, unless an exception such as a substantial common interest applies. Here the real issue between them is how the beneficial interests should be split, so no such exception fits. The solicitor should explain that they need separate advice on that point and may continue to act on the purchase itself if the conflict can be managed. Choosing a split for them would favour one client."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Mohammed, a solicitor, meets a new client, Karol, to advise on selling his company. Karol says he has £400,000 in cash from unreported earnings and asks how the money could pass through the firm's client account as a property deposit so that it looks like the proceeds of a loan. The meeting was an ordinary legal advice consultation and the firm has not yet opened a file. Which of the following is the correct approach?", "options": {"A": "Mohammed may report only if Karol agrees, because disclosing a privileged communication requires the client's informed consent in each case", "B": "Mohammed must keep the conversation confidential, because legal advice privilege protects everything a client says to a solicitor during a consultation", "C": "Mohammed must contact the police directly, because the nominated officer has no role where the person concerned is a prospective client", "D": "Mohammed need not report, because no retainer has yet been agreed and so the firm owes the prospective client no duties", "E": "Mohammed must report to the nominated officer, because privilege does not protect communications furthering a criminal purpose"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Mohammed must report to the nominated officer, because privilege does not protect communications furthering a criminal purpose): Information received in privileged circumstances need not be reported, but that protection is lost where the communication is made with the intention of furthering a criminal purpose. Karol is asking how to disguise criminal money as a loan, so the iniquity exception applies and Mohammed holds a suspicion he must report internally. Duties under the money laundering regime apply to prospective clients as well as retained ones. The report goes to the nominated officer, who decides whether to inform the National Crime Agency."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Marsha Kelleher is advising a trade creditor of Tamworth Joinery Ltd, which has entered administration. A bank holds a floating charge created in 2021 over all the company's assets, and the company's net property is substantial. The creditor is unsecured and asks whether anything is set aside for creditors like it before the bank is paid. Which of the following correctly states the rule?", "options": {"A": "No part is set aside unless the floating charge holder agrees, because the charge holder is entitled to all the net property the charge covers", "B": "A prescribed part of the net property subject to the floating charge must be set aside for unsecured creditors, ahead of the floating charge holder", "C": "The prescribed part is set aside from fixed charge realisations as well as floating charge realisations, and it ranks ahead of the fixed charge holder", "D": "The prescribed part is payable to unsecured creditors only after the floating charge holder has been paid in full from the charged assets", "E": "The prescribed part is set aside for preferential creditors, who rank ahead of unsecured creditors in sharing the floating charge net property"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A prescribed part of the net property subject to the floating charge must be set aside for unsecured creditors, ahead of the floating charge holder): For floating charges created after September 2003, the insolvency legislation requires a prescribed part of the net property to be made available for unsecured creditors. It comes out of floating charge assets before the charge holder is paid and is subject to a statutory cap. It does not touch fixed charge assets, and the chargee's consent is not needed. Preferential creditors are dealt with separately and are paid before the prescribed part."}, {"id": 708, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Mohammed Said is buying a registered freehold property from Priscilla Dunn. His solicitor wants to protect his position between exchange of contracts and registration of the transfer, so that no later entry on the register can affect his application. She submits an official search of the whole of the registered title using the correct form. For how long does the official search certificate protect Mohammed's priority?", "options": {"A": "15 business days from the date of exchange of contracts, after which a fresh search must be made", "B": "30 business days from the day the search is delivered, if the application to register is delivered in that time", "C": "Until the Land Registry completes the registration, provided the application is received before the end of the following month", "D": "30 calendar days from the date of the search, which is the period set out in the Land Registration Rules", "E": "Until completion of the purchase, because the certificate protects the buyer for as long as the contract remains in force"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (30 business days from the day the search is delivered, if the application to register is delivered in that time): An official search with priority under the Land Registration Rules gives the buyer a priority period of 30 business days, beginning on the day the search is delivered. An application to register the transfer delivered within that period takes priority over any entry made after the search. Calendar days are not used, and the period does not run from exchange or last until completion. A fresh search is needed if the period expires before the application is lodged."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Dan, annoyed with Jack in a club, punches him once intending only to give him a bloody nose. Jack falls backwards, hits his head on the floor and suffers a fractured skull, which is a really serious injury. Dan did not foresee the possibility of serious injury, though he did foresee that Jack might be hurt in some way. Is Dan guilty of an offence under section 20 Offences Against the Person Act 1861?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because section 20 requires only foresight of some physical harm, not of the serious harm that actually resulted", "B": "Yes, because the prosecution need only prove he committed an unlawful act of violence, since the harm that results fixes his liability", "C": "No, because section 20 requires proof that Dan foresaw really serious harm as a possible result of the punch", "D": "Yes, because he foresaw the risk of serious harm to Jack in the circumstances, as a reasonable person would have done", "E": "No, because section 20 requires proof that Dan intended to inflict grievous bodily harm and he intended only a bloody nose"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because section 20 requires only foresight of some physical harm, not of the serious harm that actually resulted): The mens rea of section 20 is intention or recklessness as to causing some physical harm, and the defendant need not foresee harm of the seriousness that actually occurs. Dan foresaw that Jack might be hurt, which is enough, and the fractured skull is grievous bodily harm. A requirement of intent to cause grievous harm belongs to section 18. Liability is not constructive, because foresight of some harm is still required."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Transport Select Committee of the House of Commons is investigating delays in a major rail upgrade managed by a government department. It wishes to question the department's permanent secretary and obtain internal project reports. The Secretary of State says the department will provide a letter and will not send officials, as its minister has already answered questions in the chamber. The committee's chair wants to know what powers the committee has. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The committee has power to impose a fine on the department, because it may enforce attendance by sanctions of its own", "B": "The committee may summon witnesses and require papers, and refusal can be reported to the House, which can treat it as contempt", "C": "The committee can compel attendance by obtaining a court order, because Parliament has no power to enforce its own requests directly", "D": "The committee can invite witnesses, because ministers decide which officials attend, and has no power to demand papers", "E": "The committee can require papers but not officials, because convention protects civil servants from being questioned by Parliament"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The committee may summon witnesses and require papers, and refusal can be reported to the House, which can treat it as contempt): Select committees scrutinise government departments and may send for persons, papers and records. Refusal to comply can be reported to the House, which can treat it as a contempt of Parliament. The Secretary of State's letter does not displace this, although in practice the committee and the government usually negotiate who attends. Committees cannot fine a department and do not need a court order to exercise their powers."}, {"id": 711, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Rosie befriends Arthur, a lonely elderly man, and persuades him to give her £8,000 from his savings, which he does by valid bank transfer. Arthur has capacity and understands the transfer, which is a completed gift under civil law. Rosie intended to benefit herself from his generosity and a jury could find her conduct dishonest. Rosie is charged with theft. Which of the following best describes whether she has appropriated the money?", "options": {"A": "She has appropriated it, but dishonesty is established as a matter of law once a transfer of this kind is shown", "B": "She has appropriated it, but only if she used deception to obtain it, since a transfer by consent is otherwise not an appropriation", "C": "She has appropriated it, because appropriation can occur even where the owner consents and the gift is valid, leaving dishonesty to be decided", "D": "She has not appropriated it, because an assumption of the owner's rights requires an act done without his consent or against his wishes", "E": "She has not appropriated it, because a valid gift passes ownership to her and so the money no longer belongs to another"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She has appropriated it, because appropriation can occur even where the owner consents and the gift is valid, leaving dishonesty to be decided): Appropriation is any assumption of the rights of an owner, and it is not negated by the owner's consent or by a valid gift. Consent and the passing of title go to dishonesty, not to the actus reus. The question of dishonesty is for the jury under the Ivey test. Rosie has therefore appropriated the money, and the case turns on whether she acted dishonestly."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Tomas, a pickpocket, slides his hand into Greta's coat pocket on a train, intending to steal her wallet. The pocket is empty because Greta left her wallet at home. Tomas is arrested by a transport officer as he withdraws his hand. He is charged with attempted theft under the Criminal Attempts Act 1981. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Tomas is guilty, because an attempt is made out even where the facts make the full offence impossible, judged on the facts he believed", "B": "Tomas is guilty of theft itself, because touching another person's coat with intent to steal is an appropriation of property", "C": "Tomas is not guilty, because there was no property in the pocket to be appropriated and so the actus reus of theft could not occur at that moment", "D": "Tomas is guilty only if it was likely that the pocket would contain something worth taking, as shown by the usual habits of travellers", "E": "Tomas is not guilty, because his act was preparatory only and he had not yet appropriated anything belonging to Greta"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tomas is guilty, because an attempt is made out even where the facts make the full offence impossible, judged on the facts he believed): Section 1(2) of the Criminal Attempts Act 1981 provides that a person may be guilty of attempt even though the facts make commission of the offence impossible, and section 1(3) tests this on the facts as the defendant believed them to be. Tomas believed there was a wallet to steal. Reaching into the pocket is an act more than merely preparatory. There is no need to show a likelihood of valuables, and theft itself fails because nothing was taken."}, {"id": 713, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Larkin Ltd has agreed to take a ten year lease of a warehouse from Quarry Estates Ltd. The landlord's freehold title is registered and the register shows a charge in favour of Northern Bank plc. Larkin's solicitor has not yet seen any consent from the bank. The landlord's solicitor says the charge is irrelevant because the lease is at a rent and will not reduce the value of the bank's security. What should Larkin's solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Proceed without consent, because the lease takes priority as soon as it is registered at the Land Registry", "B": "Ask only that the bank is notified after completion, because notice of the lease is enough to bind the chargee", "C": "Proceed without consent, because a lease at a market rent binds the chargee whatever the terms of the charge may say", "D": "Insist on the bank's consent before completion, because a lease granted without the chargee's consent may not bind the bank", "E": "Require the landlord to redeem the charge before completion, because a lease cannot be granted while a charge is registered"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Insist on the bank's consent before completion, because a lease granted without the chargee's consent may not bind the bank): A chargee's consent is usually needed for the owner to grant a lease, and a lease granted without it may not bind the chargee, who could then seek possession free of it. Larkin's solicitor must therefore obtain consent before completion. Later notice cannot cure the lack of power, and the lease does not take priority merely because it is registered. The landlord does not need to redeem the charge, since a consent is enough."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Ruddock, a warehouse worker, is injured when a ladder collapses beneath him. His employer, Penrose Logistics, bought the ladder new from a reputable supplier and inspected it before issuing it. The collapse is caused by a manufacturing defect that neither the employer nor the supplier could have detected, resulting from the manufacturer's negligence. The manufacturer is insured and traceable. Mr Ruddock wishes to sue his employer. Which of the following best describes the employer's liability?", "options": {"A": "Penrose is liable under the Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969, because a third party was at fault", "B": "Penrose is not liable, because it took reasonable care in selecting the supplier and inspecting the ladder before use", "C": "Penrose is not liable, because Mr Ruddock's only remedy lies against the manufacturer for supplying a defective product", "D": "Penrose is liable only if the supplier was also negligent, since the Act requires fault in the chain of supply", "E": "Penrose is liable, but only if it knew or should have known the ladder was defective when issued"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Penrose is liable under the Employers' Liability (Defective Equipment) Act 1969, because a third party was at fault): Under the 1969 Act an employer is liable for injury caused by defective equipment provided for the employer's business where the defect is attributable to the fault of a third party, such as the manufacturer. The employer's own reasonable care in selecting and inspecting the ladder does not matter. Mr Ruddock may therefore claim against Penrose directly, and Penrose can pursue the manufacturer for an indemnity. Knowledge of the defect is not required."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Gemma Okafor was granted unconditional bail to attend the magistrates' court on 5 May on a theft charge. She did not attend because her young child was taken to hospital that morning, and she telephoned the court but could not get through. She handed herself in at the court the next day. She is now charged under the Bail Act 1976 with failing to surrender. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "She commits no offence, because surrendering voluntarily the next day removes liability for the earlier failure", "B": "She commits no offence unless the prosecution proves beyond reasonable doubt that she had no reasonable cause", "C": "She has committed an offence, because failing to surrender is strict liability and the reason is relevant only to the sentence imposed", "D": "She commits no offence if she proves on the balance of probabilities reasonable cause and surrender as soon as practicable", "E": "She has committed an offence, but it can be tried only in the Crown Court because bail offences are indictable"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She commits no offence if she proves on the balance of probabilities reasonable cause and surrender as soon as practicable): Section 6 of the Bail Act 1976 makes it an offence to fail without reasonable cause to surrender, and also to fail to surrender as soon as reasonably practicable after a failure that had reasonable cause. The burden of proving reasonable cause lies on the defendant, to the civil standard. Gemma's child's admission to hospital is capable of amounting to reasonable cause, and she surrendered the next day. Voluntary surrender alone does not remove liability, and the suggestion that the offence can be tried only in the Crown Court is wrong."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Sunil is a strong swimmer relaxing on the bank of a village pond when he sees a six-year-old boy, a stranger, slip from a landing stage into deep water. Sunil has no connection with the boy or the pond, and could have pulled him out with no risk to himself. He does not move, and the boy suffers a brain injury from lack of oxygen before another walker rescues him. The boy's parents bring a claim in negligence against Sunil. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Sunil is liable, but damages are reduced because the boy's parents failed to supervise a child of that age", "B": "Sunil is liable, because his ability to rescue without risk to himself created a duty to act that he breached", "C": "Sunil is not liable, because there is no general duty to rescue a stranger from harm he did not create", "D": "Sunil is not liable, because the owner of the landing stage alone could owe the boy a duty in respect of the pond", "E": "Sunil is liable, because the boy's vulnerability and the obvious foreseeability of serious harm gave sufficient proximity between them"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sunil is not liable, because there is no general duty to rescue a stranger from harm he did not create): The common law imposes no general duty to act to protect another person from harm that the defendant did not create. A duty to take positive steps needs something extra, such as a special relationship, an assumption of responsibility, control of the danger or a prior creation of the risk, and none appears here. Ability to help easily, however morally compelling, does not turn a bystander into a person under a legal duty. Foreseeability of the boy's harm therefore does not assist his parents."}, {"id": 717, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr and Mrs Osei apply for a mortgage to buy a modest family house. The lender instructs a surveyor, Corbett and Lyle, to value it, and the Oseis pay the valuation fee. The report states that it is for the lender's use and that no responsibility is accepted to the buyers. The surveyor negligently misses serious structural movement. The Oseis rely on the report, buy the house, and discover that its true value is £40,000 less. They sue the surveyor. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The surveyor owes a duty, but the disclaimer is automatically void under statute for negligence causing financial loss", "B": "The surveyor owes a duty to the Oseis, and the disclaimer is subject to the reasonableness test, which it is unlikely to pass", "C": "The surveyor owes a duty, and the disclaimer is valid because the Oseis could have commissioned their own survey", "D": "The disclaimer is effective, because a clear exclusion of responsibility prevents any assumption of responsibility to the buyers", "E": "No duty is owed, because the surveyor was instructed by the lender and the report was prepared for the lender alone"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The surveyor owes a duty to the Oseis, and the disclaimer is subject to the reasonableness test, which it is unlikely to pass): The surveyor knew the buyers would rely on the valuation for a modest house purchase and that they paid the fee, so a duty arises despite the instruction coming from the lender. A disclaimer of liability for negligence is effective only if it is fair or reasonable, under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 for a consumer notice and the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 otherwise. For an ordinary family purchaser relying on the lender's valuation, a disclaimer is unlikely to be reasonable. It is not void automatically, and the option of a second survey is not what the court asks."}, {"id": 718, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Brook & Co solicitors held £180,000 in client account for the executors of Mr Alan Roe for nine months while a property sale completed. The firm's client account is an ordinary instant-access account paying very little interest. At the end of the administration the executors ask whether the estate is entitled to any interest, and the partner suggests that the account's low rate means nothing is due. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "A sum is due only for the period after the first six months, because the firm may keep interest on funds held for shorter periods", "B": "A sum equal to the rate on the firm's own office account is due, because the firm may measure it against its own deposit rate", "C": "No sum is due, because interest earned on an ordinary client account belongs to the firm to cover banking costs", "D": "The firm must pay the estate a fair sum in place of interest, based on the amount and period held, unless it used a designated deposit account", "E": "A sum is due only if the executors asked in writing at the start for the money to be placed on deposit under the client care arrangements"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The firm must pay the estate a fair sum in place of interest, based on the amount and period held, unless it used a designated deposit account): A firm must account to the client for a fair sum in place of the interest the client would have earned on money held in client account, having regard to the amount and the period, or alternatively place the money in a designated deposit account. The low rate on the firm's account does not remove the duty. No written request is needed and there is no initial period in which the firm may keep the interest."}, {"id": 719, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Zoe, Yan and Xavier agree to rob a petrol station by threatening the cashier. Zoe will drive, Xavier will keep watch and Yan will go in. Neither Zoe nor Xavier knows Yan has brought a knife. During the robbery Yan stabs the cashier, intending to cause really serious harm, and the cashier dies. Yan is convicted of murder. Which of the following best describes the liability of Zoe and Xavier?", "options": {"A": "They are guilty of robbery only, because liability for the death rests solely with the principal who used the knife", "B": "They are guilty of manslaughter only if each knew Yan was carrying a weapon, since the unlawful act must be known to the secondary party", "C": "They are guilty of murder if the Crown shows that a reasonable person would have foreseen that a weapon might be used", "D": "They are guilty of murder, because they took part in a joint enterprise and death was a foreseeable consequence of an armed robbery", "E": "They are not guilty of murder as they lacked intention to assist serious harm, but each may be guilty of manslaughter and robbery"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (They are not guilty of murder as they lacked intention to assist serious harm, but each may be guilty of manslaughter and robbery): After Jogee, murder liability for a secondary party needs an intention to assist or encourage the principal to kill or cause really serious harm, which Zoe and Xavier lacked. They did, however, intend to assist a robbery, which is a dangerous unlawful act that led to death, so they may be convicted of manslaughter as well as robbery. They need not know about the knife. Foreseeability alone, subjective or objective, no longer founds murder."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Daniel claims that a county council owed him a duty of care when its volunteer tree safety officer negligently advised his late neighbour that a boundary oak was safe, and a branch later fell on Daniel's car. No reported decision involves a local authority advisory scheme of this kind, so established categories of duty do not clearly cover the facts. The council argues that Daniel must first satisfy the three stage Caparo test in full. Which of the following best describes the approach the court should take to deciding whether a duty of care is owed?", "options": {"A": "It should ask whether the council assumed responsibility to the world at large and treat that as conclusive of duty", "B": "It should refuse a duty where the defendant is a public authority, because policy bars liability for advisory functions", "C": "It should find a duty whenever harm to the claimant was reasonably foreseeable, since foreseeability is the sole requirement today", "D": "It should use established categories of duty and, in a novel case, develop the law incrementally by analogy", "E": "It should apply the three stage Caparo test mechanically to every claim, asking foreseeability, proximity and fairness in turn"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It should use established categories of duty and, in a novel case, develop the law incrementally by analogy): In the modern approach Caparo is a guide for novel cases and not a test to be run in every claim. Where existing authority covers the facts, the duty is simply recognised. In a new situation the law develops step by step by analogy with established categories, and the Caparo considerations inform that process. Foreseeability is necessary but does not found a duty by itself, which is why a mechanical three stage analysis, the tempting answer here, misdescribes the law."}, {"id": 721, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Ana lends Ben her crowbar knowing he plans to break into a shop that night. Later that afternoon Ana phones Ben and says she wants nothing more to do with it, but she does not ask for the crowbar back or contact the police. That night Ben uses the crowbar to force the shop door. Ana is charged as a secondary party to burglary. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Ana's withdrawal is ineffective unless she informed the police, because only a report to the authorities can cancel earlier assistance", "B": "Ana's withdrawal is ineffective because the agreement to burgle had already been made, and withdrawal is possible only before any agreement is reached with Ben", "C": "Ana's withdrawal is effective, because her assistance was a single act completed before the burglary rather than continuing involvement", "D": "Ana's withdrawal is effective, because she communicated her change of mind to Ben before the offence took place", "E": "Ana's withdrawal is ineffective, because she took no reasonable steps to undo her assistance, such as retrieving the crowbar or warning the police"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ana's withdrawal is ineffective, because she took no reasonable steps to undo her assistance, such as retrieving the crowbar or warning the police): To withdraw effectively a secondary party must communicate the withdrawal unequivocally and, where assistance has been given, take reasonable steps to neutralise it. Ana only told Ben she was out, while the crowbar stayed in his hands. A report to police is one reasonable step but not the only one, and retrieving the tool would have sufficed. Withdrawal is possible after an agreement, provided it comes before the offence is under way and is effective."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Hill's will appoints his friends Ian and Jo as executors. Jo is working abroad for a year and does not want to act now, but would like to keep the right to act later. Ian is willing to apply immediately, and the estate includes a house to be sold. What is the correct way to deal with Jo's position?", "options": {"A": "Letters of administration with will annexed must be granted, because Jo has not agreed to prove the will", "B": "Probate can be granted to Ian with power reserved to Jo, who may later apply to prove the will", "C": "Ian cannot apply until Jo returns, because every executor named must join in the application", "D": "Jo is treated as having renounced, because she has not joined in the application", "E": "Jo must renounce, losing all later right to act, before probate can be granted to Ian alone"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Probate can be granted to Ian with power reserved to Jo, who may later apply to prove the will): Where one executor wishes to act and another wants to leave it for the time being, probate can be granted to the first with power reserved to the second. Jo keeps the right to prove the will later and need not renounce. Ian can proceed, and the house can be sold by him. Letters of administration with will annexed are needed only when no executor will prove."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Rosedale Library Users Group challenges a council decision to close three libraries. The High Court finds that the council failed to consult properly, which is an unlawful procedural failing. However, the council produces unchallenged evidence that its budget position meant the closures would have been decided in the same way after a proper consultation. The council asks the court to refuse relief. No exceptional public interest in granting relief is shown. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court may refuse relief only if the claim was issued out of time, because the likely outcome is irrelevant to relief", "B": "The court must award damages to the group, because a consultation failing is a wrong for which compensation is the usual remedy, as in tort", "C": "The court must refuse relief, because it appears highly likely that the outcome would not have been substantially different without the failing", "D": "The court may refuse relief only if the claimant fails to show that the closures were irrational on the merits", "E": "The court must grant a quashing order, because a proved procedural failing by a public body entitles the claimant to relief"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court must refuse relief, because it appears highly likely that the outcome would not have been substantially different without the failing): Under section 31(2A) of the Senior Courts Act 1981 the court must refuse relief where it appears highly likely that the outcome would not have been substantially different if the conduct complained of had not occurred, unless there is an exceptional public interest. The council's unchallenged evidence meets that test and no exceptional interest is shown. The usual discretion to grant a quashing order is therefore displaced. Damages are not a normal judicial review remedy, and time limits are a separate matter."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A lease granted in 2019 required the tenant to pay rent quarterly. Vic, the original tenant, assigned the lease in 2022 and gave an authorised guarantee agreement to the landlord. The assignee, Wes, has paid no rent for the last four quarters. The landlord now serves a notice on Vic claiming all four quarters, the earliest of which fell due eleven months ago and the latest two months ago, and this is the first communication to Vic about the arrears. Which of the following best describes Vic's liability?", "options": {"A": "Vic is liable for all four quarters, because the six-month period runs from the date the landlord discovered the default", "B": "Vic is liable for none of the arrears, because the landlord failed to warn him of any default when it first occurred", "C": "Vic is liable for all four quarters, because the notice was served within the period of the authorised guarantee agreement", "D": "Vic is liable for the latest quarter only, because a notice must be served within three months of each sum falling due", "E": "Vic is liable for the two most recent quarters only, because the notice was served too late for the earlier arrears"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Vic is liable for the two most recent quarters only, because the notice was served too late for the earlier arrears): Under section 17 of the 1995 Act a landlord can recover fixed charges such as rent from a former tenant under an authorised guarantee agreement only if it serves notice within six months of the sum falling due. Quarters due eleven and eight months ago are out of time, but those due five and two months ago fall within six months before the notice. Vic is therefore liable for the two most recent quarters. The period runs from the due date, not from when the landlord discovered the default."}, {"id": 725, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Imogen Fraser is tried for wounding with intent. The prosecution's main witness, Mr Gill, gave a signed witness statement to police describing the attack but died of natural causes before trial. The prosecution applies to read his statement to the jury. Imogen's counsel objects that Mr Gill can no longer be cross-examined. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The statement is inadmissible, because the right to cross-examine prosecution witnesses is fundamental and no hearsay gateway can override it", "B": "The statement is admissible under the hearsay gateway for a dead witness, because it would have been admissible had he given oral evidence", "C": "The statement is admissible only if it was made on oath, because an unsworn statement carries no evidential weight", "D": "The statement is admissible only if Imogen agrees, because hearsay can be introduced by the prosecution only with defence consent", "E": "The statement may be read only if the judge first finds that Mr Gill's account is supported by independent evidence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The statement is admissible under the hearsay gateway for a dead witness, because it would have been admissible had he given oral evidence): Where the maker of a statement is dead, the Criminal Justice Act 2003 allows the statement to be admitted as proof of what it says, so long as it would have been admissible if given orally. Imogen's counsel may still argue that the statement should be excluded for unfairness, and the jury is told to weigh it with care. Consent, an oath and corroboration are not conditions of the gateway."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Brightwell Plastics owed £60,000 to Tarn Supplies. At a meeting, the director Adeyemi orally promised Tarn, 'If Brightwell does not pay, I will pay you', in return for Tarn agreeing not to sue Brightwell for three months. Tarn did not sue, Brightwell did not pay, and Tarn now claims against Adeyemi. Nothing was recorded in writing. Adeyemi's liability, if any, is secondary to Brightwell's debt. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Adeyemi is liable, because Tarn gave consideration by forbearing to sue and consideration makes an oral guarantee enforceable", "B": "Adeyemi is not liable, because a promise to answer for another's debt is void unless made by deed, whether or not it is signed", "C": "Adeyemi is liable, because the writing requirement applies only to promises by consumers and not to promises made by company directors", "D": "Adeyemi is liable only if Tarn proves it relied on the promise, because reliance can replace the signed writing for guarantees", "E": "Adeyemi is not liable on the promise, because a guarantee is unenforceable unless evidenced in writing signed by the guarantor"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Adeyemi is not liable on the promise, because a guarantee is unenforceable unless evidenced in writing signed by the guarantor): A promise to answer for the debt of another, where the promisor's liability is secondary, is a guarantee and must be evidenced in writing signed by the guarantor under the Statute of Frauds 1677. An oral guarantee is not enforceable, although it is not void. Consideration is present but does not remove the formality, and a deed is not required. The rule applies to everyone and not only to consumers."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Priya taps Kofi on the shoulder in a busy corridor to attract his attention. Kofi, who is startled, jerks away and falls against a radiator, bruising his arm. Kofi alleges that Priya committed a battery. Priya had no intention of causing injury and touched him lightly and politely. Which of the following best describes whether a battery was committed?", "options": {"A": "A battery was committed, because any touching without express consent is unlawful and the resulting injury shows force was applied", "B": "No battery, because force must be applied directly with the body and the fall rather than the tap caused the injury", "C": "No battery, because a battery requires hostility and Priya's gentle touch showed no hostile intent towards Kofi", "D": "No battery, because touching of this kind is part of the ordinary conduct of daily life and is generally accepted", "E": "A battery was committed, because Priya was reckless as to the risk of Kofi being startled by an unexpected touch"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No battery, because touching of this kind is part of the ordinary conduct of daily life and is generally accepted): Physical contact that is generally acceptable in everyday life, such as a tap on the shoulder to attract attention, is not a battery because there is an implied consent to such touching. Hostility is not an element, and express consent is not needed for ordinary contact. The injury from the fall does not turn an accepted touch into an unlawful one. Priya also lacked the intention or recklessness as to unlawful force."}, {"id": 728, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "While investigating title to a registered freehold shop, you obtain official copies. One entry records that the registered proprietor is Meridian Estates Ltd with title absolute, and another records a restriction preventing any disposition without the written consent of a named company. You are preparing a note for a trainee explaining where each entry sits in the register. In which part of the official copies would you expect to find the proprietor's name, the class of title and the restriction?", "options": {"A": "The charges register, because it lists burdens on the land and every entry that limits what the owner may do with it", "B": "The title plan, because it identifies the registered land and records the owner and any limit on disposition by that owner", "C": "The property register, because it describes the land and the estate and records rights that benefit it, including restrictions on disposal", "D": "The index map, because it identifies which parcels are registered and lists the current owner and any limits on sale", "E": "The proprietorship register, because it records who owns the estate, the class of title and any restriction on dealing with it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The proprietorship register, because it records who owns the estate, the class of title and any restriction on dealing with it): The proprietorship register names the registered proprietor, states the class of title and records restrictions on the owner's power to dispose of the estate. The property register describes the land and the rights benefiting it. The charges register records burdens such as mortgages and covenants, and the title plan only shows the extent of the land. The index map is a search tool and does not form part of the register entries."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "You act for Gina, executor of the late Mr Hale. A neighbour, Nick, claims that Mr Hale promised him a field and that he relied on the promise. Gina tells you that she found a signed note by Mr Hale in his desk saying, 'Nick can have the top field.' She says she wants to burn the note because without it Nick has little evidence. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Continue to act without comment, because it is the executor's decision whether to preserve her late friend's papers", "B": "Advise her to keep the note but not mention it, because disclosure is required if Nick's solicitor asks for it by name", "C": "Advise that the note must be preserved and disclosed if proceedings are issued, and refuse to take any step to conceal or destroy it", "D": "Advise her to transfer it to you, because a document held by a solicitor is protected by legal professional privilege", "E": "Advise that she may destroy it, because an executor owes duties to the beneficiaries named in the will"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise that the note must be preserved and disclosed if proceedings are issued, and refuse to take any step to conceal or destroy it): A solicitor must not mislead the court or others and must not assist a client to conceal or destroy documents that are relevant to a dispute. The note is evidence of the promise, and it would have to be disclosed in any proceedings, so destroying it would risk serious consequences for Gina and the solicitor. Putting a pre-existing document in the solicitor's hands does not make it privileged. The correct step is to explain these duties and refuse to help, not to continue without comment."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Tanner and Rye LLP act for the claimant in a multi track personal injury claim. The defendant served its orthopaedic expert's report on 3 March. The claimant's solicitor thinks the report is unclear about the effect of an earlier injury and wants to put questions to the expert, and then possibly a further set of questions on the expert's views about prognosis. No order has been made about written questions. Which of the following correctly states what the rules allow?", "options": {"A": "Questions may be put within 28 days, but only with the court's permission, and only where there is no agreed single joint expert", "B": "Questions may be put at any time before trial and may seek the expert's views on other issues, provided the other side is copied in", "C": "Questions may be put within 28 days, and the answers will form part of the report only if the other side agrees", "D": "Questions may be put once, within 28 days of service of the report, and must be for the purpose of clarification of the report", "E": "Questions may be put within 14 days of service of the report and may be used to challenge the expert's conclusions"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Questions may be put once, within 28 days of service of the report, and must be for the purpose of clarification of the report): CPR 35.6 allows a party to put written questions to an expert about the report once only, within 28 days of service, and normally only to clarify the report. Permission is not needed unless the court orders otherwise or the expert is a single joint expert instructed by the court. The expert's answers are treated as part of the report. The questions are not a vehicle for challenging conclusions or exploring wholly new issues, which would need permission or a separate application."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Parkside Print Ltd produces high-quality art reproductions and uses ink and paper so delicate that even slight vibration spoils them. Next door, Gable Builders Ltd carries out ordinary, careful demolition work on weekdays, causing vibration that would not trouble a normal business. Parkside's prints are ruined and it sues Gable in private nuisance. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because demolition is an activity unsuited to a commercial area and so is automatically unreasonable", "B": "The claim fails, because the defendant's work was carried out on weekdays, which is conclusive proof that it was reasonable", "C": "The claim is likely to fail, because the harm arose only from the claimant's abnormally sensitive use of its land", "D": "The claim fails, because private nuisance protects only against physical damage to buildings and not against damage to goods", "E": "The claim succeeds, because any interference with a claimant's use of its land is actionable once harm was foreseeable to the defendant"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claim is likely to fail, because the harm arose only from the claimant's abnormally sensitive use of its land): A defendant is not liable in private nuisance where the damage occurs only because the claimant's use of its land is unusually sensitive, since the interference would not be a nuisance to an ordinary occupier. The demolition was ordinary, careful and carried on at normal times, so it would not have troubled a normal business. Foreseeability of harm to this claimant does not alone make the activity unreasonable, and private nuisance can protect against interference with use, so the physical damage argument is wrong. Weekday working is relevant but not conclusive, and the sensitivity point is what decides the case."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Kyle, aged 16, is convicted at the youth court of unlawful wounding. He lives with his mother and has several previous convictions for violence and has breached a youth rehabilitation order. The court decides, after a pre-sentence report, that only custody is appropriate and asks about the length of a detention and training order. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The order must be for between 4 and 24 months, but it may be imposed only on offenders aged under 15", "B": "The order must be for 4 to 24 months, in a prescribed length such as 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18 or 24 months", "C": "The order must be for between 2 and 12 months, since that is the limit of the youth court's powers", "D": "The order may be for any term up to 24 months, including intermediate lengths such as 5 or 7 months", "E": "The order must be for between 6 months and 3 years, matching the powers of the adult magistrates' court"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The order must be for 4 to 24 months, in a prescribed length such as 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18 or 24 months): A detention and training order has a term of at least 4 months and at most 24 months, and the term must be one of the prescribed lengths of 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18 or 24 months. Half is served in custody and half under supervision in the community. It is available for 15 to 17 year olds generally, and for 12 to 14 year olds only if they are persistent offenders."}, {"id": 733, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ms Taylor buys a newly built house from a developer, Brickfield Homes. The house was built for the developer by Quillan Construction, which has no contract with Ms Taylor. Within a year, cracks appear because the foundations were negligently designed, and the cost of repairing them is £48,000. No one has been injured and the cracks have not yet made the house unsafe to occupy. Ms Taylor sues Quillan Construction in negligence for the repair cost. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because a builder owes a duty to future owners to construct foundations with reasonable care and skill", "B": "The claim fails, but would succeed once the cracks made the house a danger to health and safety", "C": "The claim fails, because the cost of repairing a defect in the house itself is pure economic loss, not recoverable in negligence", "D": "The claim succeeds, because the defect was in the structure of a dwelling and so is treated as physical damage", "E": "The claim fails, because liability for defective buildings lies with the local authority that approved the plans and not the builder"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claim fails, because the cost of repairing a defect in the house itself is pure economic loss, not recoverable in negligence): The cost of putting right a defect in the building itself, with no injury and no damage to other property, is pure economic loss. Pure economic loss is not recoverable in negligence outside the recognised exceptions, such as an assumption of responsibility, and there is none between Quillan and Ms Taylor. A builder may owe a duty in respect of personal injury or damage to other property, but not for the cost of repairing the defect. Even a dangerous defect does not turn that cost into recoverable damage."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Anika, Boris and Chen run a small design business as partners without any written agreement. Anika contributed £80,000 as capital, Boris £20,000, and Chen contributed no capital but works full time in the business. They never discussed salaries or interest on capital. The business has made a profit of £90,000 for the year. How is the profit divided between them?", "options": {"A": "Equally after Chen receives a salary, because the default rules reward a partner who works full time", "B": "Equally after each partner receives interest on capital, because the default rules reward capital provided to the business", "C": "Equally, £30,000 each, because the default rule shares profits equally and ignores the capital each partner contributed", "D": "In the ratio of the time each partner spends working in the business, because profit follows effort", "E": "In the ratio of capital contributed, so Anika receives £72,000 and Boris £18,000, because capital shows each partner's stake"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Equally, £30,000 each, because the default rule shares profits equally and ignores the capital each partner contributed): Under the default rules in section 24 of the Partnership Act 1890, partners share profits and losses equally. Unequal capital does not change the shares, and no partner is entitled to a salary or to interest on capital unless the agreement says so. Each partner therefore receives one third of the £90,000, which is £30,000."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Delmar Ltd obtained an interim injunction stopping Cairn Packaging Ltd from selling a product pending trial, having given the usual cross-undertaking in damages. Cairn lost £140,000 of sales in the six months before trial. At trial the judge finds that Delmar's claim fails and discharges the injunction. Delmar acted honestly throughout. Cairn asks whether it can recover its lost sales. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Cairn can recover automatically, because the discharged injunction obliges Delmar to pay the amount claimed without proof of loss", "B": "Cairn can recover its costs of the application only, because lost sales are too remote under the undertaking", "C": "Cairn cannot recover, because the undertaking is enforced only if Delmar acted in bad faith in obtaining the injunction", "D": "Cairn may seek an inquiry as to damages under the undertaking, since Delmar's honest conduct does not prevent the claim", "E": "Cairn cannot recover, because an undertaking in damages applies to without notice injunctions and not to those granted on notice"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cairn may seek an inquiry as to damages under the undertaking, since Delmar's honest conduct does not prevent the claim): The cross-undertaking protects a defendant who is restrained by an injunction that is later shown to have been wrongly granted. If the claimant loses at trial, the defendant can apply for an inquiry as to damages caused by the injunction, and must prove its loss. The claimant's honesty is not a defence, since the undertaking is not based on fault. It applies to injunctions granted on notice as well as without notice, and lost sales caused by the order can fall within it."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "At a party Ben, joking, points a revolver at his friend Chris and pulls the trigger. Both men genuinely believe the gun cannot fire because neither chamber aligned with the barrel contains a bullet, though in fact the cylinder rotates before firing. Chris laughs and is not frightened. The gun fires and Chris dies. Ben is charged with unlawful act manslaughter. Which of the following is the best analysis of whether the offence is made out?", "options": {"A": "Ben is not liable for any homicide offence, because a defendant who acts in jest cannot owe a duty of care to a willing friend", "B": "Unlawful act manslaughter is made out, because pointing a loaded gun at someone is dangerous and a sober person would see the risk of harm, whatever Ben believed", "C": "No unlawful act is established, because Chris did not fear violence and Ben lacked the mental element of assault, so no base offence exists", "D": "Ben is not liable because his honest belief that the gun was safe negates the danger element, even though a base offence existed", "E": "Unlawful act manslaughter is made out, because Ben intentionally performed the act of pulling the trigger and that act caused the death"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No unlawful act is established, because Chris did not fear violence and Ben lacked the mental element of assault, so no base offence exists): Unlawful act manslaughter needs a criminal base offence with its own mental element. Assault requires the victim to apprehend immediate force and the defendant to intend or be reckless about that, and neither exists here because Chris laughed and Ben believed the gun was safe. Without a complete base offence the offence fails, however dangerous the act looks in hindsight. Danger and causation are only relevant once an unlawful act has been proved."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Orchard Plant Hire Ltd and Kestrel Quarries Ltd signed a supply contract containing a clause providing that any dispute arising from it shall be referred to arbitration under the Arbitration Act 1996. A dispute arose over unpaid invoices of £90,000. Kestrel, ignoring the clause, issued a claim in the county court. Orchard has not yet taken any step in the proceedings other than acknowledging service and now wishes to enforce the clause. What is the most likely outcome of an application by Orchard?", "options": {"A": "The court will strike out the claim, because an arbitration clause removes the court's jurisdiction and Kestrel must start the arbitration afresh", "B": "The court has a discretion to stay, but will refuse because the claim concerns unpaid invoices and is therefore a debt matter suited to litigation", "C": "The court must stay the claim, because the dispute falls within a valid arbitration agreement and Orchard has not answered the substantive claim", "D": "The court will refuse a stay, because Orchard acknowledged service and has thereby submitted to the jurisdiction of the county court", "E": "The court will grant a stay if Kestrel consents, because arbitration depends on the agreement of both parties at the time of the dispute"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court must stay the claim, because the dispute falls within a valid arbitration agreement and Orchard has not answered the substantive claim): Section 9 of the Arbitration Act 1996 requires the court to stay legal proceedings brought in respect of a matter the parties agreed to refer to arbitration, unless the agreement is null and void, inoperative or incapable of being performed. The application must be made before the applicant takes a step to answer the substantive claim, and acknowledging service is not such a step. The tempting submission that Orchard has submitted to the court fails for that reason. The remedy is a stay rather than a strike out, so the court keeps the claim if the arbitration cannot proceed."}, {"id": 738, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Fennimore Ltd has agreed to buy a ground floor unit that has been used for six years as a florist's shop. Its client intends to use it as an accountancy office. Both uses fall within Class E (commercial, business and service) of the Town and Country Planning (Use Classes) Order, and no building works are proposed. No condition on the existing permission restricts the use. The client asks whether planning permission is needed for the change. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "No permission is needed, because a change between uses within the same use class is not a material change of use", "B": "No permission is needed, because since the 2020 reforms no commercial change of use has been treated as development", "C": "No permission is needed only if the change takes place within ten years of the last use of the unit as a shop", "D": "Permission is needed, because a florist's shop and an accountancy office serve different customers and so differ in character", "E": "Permission is needed, because each different business activity is a separate use and a change of activity is a material change of use"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No permission is needed, because a change between uses within the same use class is not a material change of use): A material change of use is development needing permission, but the Use Classes Order provides that a change from one use to another within the same class is not development. Retail and offices both sit in Class E, so moving from a florist's shop to an accountancy office needs no permission, provided no condition on the existing permission prevents it. The difference in customers is irrelevant when the classes match. The 2020 reforms merged uses into Class E but did not remove control of changes between classes."}, {"id": 739, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Aisha, who works at a removals depot, takes her manager's van from the yard without asking so that she can move her sister's furniture. She intends to return it by 6 pm the same day and does so, with a full tank of fuel, in the same condition. The manager reports the matter to the police. Which of the following offences is Aisha most likely to have committed?", "options": {"A": "Criminal damage, because use of the van for a purpose not authorised by the owner altered its condition", "B": "Aggravated vehicle taking, because the van was driven on a public road without the owner's permission", "C": "Theft of the van, because she dishonestly assumed the rights of the owner and used it for her own purposes", "D": "Taking a conveyance without the owner's consent, because she took the van for her own use without any intention of keeping it", "E": "No offence, because she returned the van undamaged with a full tank and the owner suffered no loss from the borrowing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Taking a conveyance without the owner's consent, because she took the van for her own use without any intention of keeping it): Taking a conveyance without consent requires a taking for the defendant's own or another's use without the owner's authority, and it does not need an intention to permanently deprive. Aisha returned the van the same day, so theft fails, since a borrowing of that kind does not meet the deeming rule. Return in good condition is no defence to the taking offence. The aggravated form requires dangerous driving, an accident or damage."}, {"id": 740, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Sanjay Kapoor bought a freehold house for £410,000. After completion he finds the roof is badly damaged by long-term water ingress. The house was sold under the Standard Conditions of Sale, his survey was a basic valuation, and he made no enquiry about the roof. The seller said nothing about the roof and gave no replies of any kind on its condition. Can Sanjay claim against the seller?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the Standard Conditions oblige the seller to hand over the house free from disrepair at completion", "B": "Yes, because the roof is a latent defect and sellers must disclose latent defects in the physical state of the property", "C": "No, because the seller need not volunteer physical defects, and the buyer is expected to inspect and make enquiries", "D": "No, but only because completion has taken place, and he could have refused to complete had he found the damage earlier", "E": "Yes, because the seller knew of the damage and failing to disclose a known defect is a breach of the contract"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because the seller need not volunteer physical defects, and the buyer is expected to inspect and make enquiries): The rule of caveat emptor means a buyer takes the physical condition of the property as found, and a seller need not volunteer physical defects unless asked or unless a statement made is false. Sanjay made no enquiry and received no misleading reply, so he has no claim. The duty to disclose latent defects applies to title, not to physical condition. The conditions do not require the seller to repair the house before completion."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Ashby Windows Ltd was sued by a customer, Mr Kowalski, for £45,000. Ashby made a Part 36 offer of £40,000 with a 21 day relevant period, which Mr Kowalski did not accept. At trial the court awarded him £38,000. The trial lasted two days and Mr Kowalski incurred substantial costs before and after the offer expired. Mr Kowalski's solicitor says that as the winner he should recover all his costs. The judge sees no reason of injustice. How will the court deal with costs?", "options": {"A": "Mr Kowalski recovers his costs up to the end of the relevant period but pays Ashby's costs afterwards, with the sums set off", "B": "No order for costs is made either way, because neither party has beaten the other's offer", "C": "Mr Kowalski pays all of Ashby's costs from the date of the claim form, because the offer showed the claim to be unreasonable", "D": "Mr Kowalski recovers all his costs, because winning at trial makes him the successful party whatever the offer said", "E": "Mr Kowalski recovers costs on the indemnity basis to the end of the period but nothing afterwards, as a penalty for refusing"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mr Kowalski recovers his costs up to the end of the relevant period but pays Ashby's costs afterwards, with the sums set off): Under CPR 36.17(3), where a claimant fails to obtain judgment more advantageous than a defendant's Part 36 offer, the court will order the claimant to pay the defendant's costs from the date on which the relevant period expired, together with interest on those costs. The claimant keeps costs for the period before expiry. Because the judgment of £38,000 is less than £40,000, the offer was not beaten, and success at trial does not displace this default."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for a company buying a freehold shop with a flat above, both let on short tenancies. The seller's solicitor has sent a draft contract on the Standard Commercial Property Conditions and proposes to supply the Property Information Form and the Fittings and Contents Form as pre-contract replies, saying that the Protocol applies. Completion is planned for six weeks' time. What is the best response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the forms because the Protocol applies to every freehold sale, and details of the tenants can be gathered from them", "B": "Decline to raise enquiries, because a buyer of commercial property takes it as seen and the seller has no duty to reply", "C": "Ask for the Commercial Property Standard Enquiries, because the residential forms and Protocol are not designed for a let commercial property", "D": "Accept the forms but ask for the Standard Conditions of Sale instead, because the commercial conditions do not suit a property with a flat", "E": "Ask only for the leases, because Commercial Property Standard Enquiries are used only for leasehold sales"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask for the Commercial Property Standard Enquiries, because the residential forms and Protocol are not designed for a let commercial property): The Protocol and its forms were created for residential conveyancing and do not cover tenancies, service charges or the other issues of a let commercial property. The recognised commercial enquiries are the Commercial Property Standard Enquiries, which the buyer's solicitor should request. Caveat emptor does not stop a buyer asking questions, and the leases alone would not answer matters such as arrears, disputes or insurance."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Brightwood Ltd holds a 25-year lease of a warehouse from Tessa. Barry guaranteed the company's obligations under the lease. The company goes into liquidation, and the liquidator disclaims the lease as onerous property. Tessa asks whether Barry can still be pursued for rent and other sums falling due after disclaimer. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Barry remains liable on his guarantee, because disclaimer ends the lease between the company and Tessa but does not release a guarantor", "B": "Barry is released, because the lease has come to an end and so there is nothing left for him to guarantee under it", "C": "Barry is liable only for rent that fell due before the disclaimer, because the guarantee ends when the lease ends", "D": "Barry is liable only if the court approves the disclaimer, because the liquidator needs permission to end the lease", "E": "Barry is liable only if Tessa first sues the liquidator personally for the unpaid sums and fails to recover them"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Barry remains liable on his guarantee, because disclaimer ends the lease between the company and Tessa but does not release a guarantor): A liquidator can disclaim an onerous lease, which ends the rights and liabilities of the company under it from the date of disclaimer. Section 178 of the Insolvency Act 1986 provides that disclaimer does not affect the liability of any other person, and the courts have held that this preserves a guarantor's liability for sums falling due after disclaimer. Barry therefore remains liable. A liquidator does not need court approval to disclaim, and Tessa need not pursue the liquidator first."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Sable Timber Ltd contracted on 1 May to sell 200 tonnes of oak planks to Yardley Joinery Ltd, delivery to be made by 15 June. The contract states that time for delivery is of the essence. Sable delivered on 18 June, and Yardley rejected the goods and ended the contract by letter on 19 June. Sable had offered a 5% discount in a call that morning, which Yardley declined, and the market price has since fallen. Sable argues that a three day delay caused Yardley no real loss. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Yardley was entitled to terminate, because a stipulation making time of the essence is a condition and any breach of it permits termination", "B": "The contract ended automatically on 15 June, so Yardley was not obliged to accept the late delivery or to send a letter", "C": "Yardley could terminate only if it proves that the delay caused serious loss, because the effect of the breach decides the remedy", "D": "Yardley should first have served notice making time of the essence, because a late delivery alone does not entitle a buyer to end the contract", "E": "Yardley was limited to damages, because a delay of three days is minor and the goods were still delivered"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yardley was entitled to terminate, because a stipulation making time of the essence is a condition and any breach of it permits termination): Parties can make a term a condition by expressly stating that it is essential, and a stipulation that time is of the essence has that effect. Once the delivery date was missed, Yardley could terminate however short the delay and whether or not loss followed. The effect-of-breach test belongs to innominate terms, so it does not help Sable. A repudiatory breach does not end the contract by itself, which is why Yardley's letter of termination mattered."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Priya Nair runs a bakery in Leeds. A catering customer, an individual living in Bradford, has not paid an invoice for £14,500 issued last spring, and there is no dispute that the debt is owed. Priya has not yet started any proceedings and asks in which court her claim for the money would normally be issued. She does not need any special remedy beyond payment of the sum. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The Crown Court, because it hears the most serious cases and a claim for an unpaid commercial debt is serious enough to be heard there", "B": "The County Court, because it has general civil jurisdiction and a money claim of this value is too small to start in the High Court", "C": "The King's Bench Division of the High Court, because debt claims must be issued there once they exceed the small claims limit of £10,000", "D": "The Magistrates' Court, because it deals with debts and minor civil disputes between individuals and small businesses in its local justice area", "E": "The Court of Appeal Civil Division, because the debt is undisputed and so no trial is needed before an appellate court can order payment"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The County Court, because it has general civil jurisdiction and a money claim of this value is too small to start in the High Court): The County Court is the court of first instance for most civil claims in England and Wales, and an individual's debt claim of £14,500 is within its jurisdiction. The High Court is generally used where a claim is worth more than £100,000 or a statute requires it, so the small claims figure is the wrong benchmark. The Magistrates' Court and Crown Court deal mainly with criminal matters, and the Court of Appeal hears appeals rather than starting claims."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Dev intends to kidnap a schoolchild for ransom. He goes to a school carrying a rope, tape and a large knife in a rucksack and hides in the boys' toilets. He is found by a teacher before any child has come near. Dev has not made contact with any pupil. He is charged with attempted kidnapping. What is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "He is guilty of attempt, because carrying the rope, tape and knife into school grounds is itself an act that goes beyond preparation", "B": "He is guilty of attempt, because his intention to kidnap is clear and he has gone far beyond the point of no return", "C": "He is guilty of attempt, because the Act requires only an intention to commit the offence and an act towards it", "D": "He is likely to be found not guilty of attempt, because he had not embarked on the crime proper and his acts were merely preparatory", "E": "He is not guilty, because attempted kidnapping requires proof that a victim was identified and approached, which cannot be shown"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He is likely to be found not guilty of attempt, because he had not embarked on the crime proper and his acts were merely preparatory): An attempt requires an act that is more than merely preparatory, and the jury asks whether the defendant had embarked on the crime proper. As in R v Geddes, being at the place with the tools but without contact with any victim is likely to be regarded as preparation only. A clear intention is necessary but not enough by itself. The point of no return is not the test, and an act need not be directed at an identified victim if it is otherwise more than preparatory."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Pet Welfare Act 2025 is an Act of the UK Parliament. Section 50 says the Act 'extends to England and Wales'. Section 12, which requires pet shops to display licences, states that it 'applies in relation to England only', and a separate Senedd Act governs pet shops in Wales. Cerys Bevan owns a pet shop in Monmouth, in Wales, and a pet shop in Hereford, in England. She asks which licence display rule applies to each shop. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Section 12 applies to the Monmouth shop but not the Hereford shop, because the Act extends to Wales and the Senedd Act is secondary", "B": "Section 12 applies to the Hereford shop but not the Monmouth shop, because its application is limited to England", "C": "Section 12 applies to both shops, because the Act extends to England and Wales and an extent clause determines application", "D": "Section 12 applies to neither shop, because a UK Act cannot regulate a subject that is devolved to Wales", "E": "Section 12 applies to both shops until the Senedd Act is commenced, at which point it ceases to apply in Wales"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Section 12 applies to the Hereford shop but not the Monmouth shop, because its application is limited to England): Extent and application are different questions. The Act extends to England and Wales, which means it forms part of the law of both, but a provision may be expressed to apply in relation to a narrower area. Section 12 is expressed to apply in relation to England only, so it governs the Hereford shop. The Monmouth shop is regulated by the Senedd Act on the devolved subject, so the licence display rule in section 12 does not reach it."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ms Quinn made a will in 2019 leaving everything to her sister. In 2024 she signed a new will leaving everything to her niece, but only one person witnessed it. Believing the 2024 will was valid, she burned the 2019 will, intending to revoke it. She has now died. The niece relies on the 2024 will, and the sister has a photocopy of the 2019 will. What is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The 2024 will is invalid, and the 2019 will may be admitted by dependent relative revocation, proved by the photocopy", "B": "The 2019 will stays revoked by the burning, so the whole estate passes under the intestacy rules", "C": "The burning does not revoke the 2019 will, because destruction revokes a will only if done before witnesses", "D": "The 2024 will is valid, because her clear intention to benefit the niece cures the defective witnessing", "E": "The 2019 will is revoked, but the court will give effect to the 2024 will by its power to dispense with formalities"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The 2024 will is invalid, and the 2019 will may be admitted by dependent relative revocation, proved by the photocopy): Burning with intention to revoke is a valid method of revocation, but her act was based on the belief that the 2024 will worked. The 2024 will fails because it had only one witness. The doctrine of dependent relative revocation treats the revocation as conditional on the new will being valid, so the 2019 will can be admitted, with a copy used to prove its terms. Intention cannot save an improperly witnessed will."}, {"id": 749, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Fenwick Haulage Ltd and Orme Storage Ltd were both sued by Cleary Foods Ltd after a warehouse fire in 2022. On 1 September 2025 Fenwick settled Cleary's claim for £90,000, a sum agreed in good faith, without admitting liability. Fenwick now believes that Orme is equally responsible and wants to recover half through a Part 20 claim under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978. It is now March 2026. What is the time limit for Fenwick's contribution claim?", "options": {"A": "Six years from the fire in 2022, because the contribution claim is founded on the original tort", "B": "Six years from the date of settlement, because contribution claims follow the limitation period for simple contract debts", "C": "Two years from the fire, because the contribution claim must be brought within the period for the original claimant", "D": "Three years from the date on which Fenwick first knew that Orme was also at fault, as in a personal injury claim", "E": "Two years from the date of the settlement agreement, so the claim must be brought by 1 September 2027"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Two years from the date of the settlement agreement, so the claim must be brought by 1 September 2027): A contribution claim under the 1978 Act is subject to a two year limitation period which runs from the date on which the right to contribution accrued, here the date of the settlement or judgment that fixed Fenwick's liability. Time therefore does not run from the fire. Because the settlement was on 1 September 2025, the last date is 1 September 2027. The six year periods for tort and contract do not apply to a contribution claim."}, {"id": 750, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Kinloch Estates intends to grant Beatrix Walsh a five year lease of a retail unit excluded from the security of tenure provisions of Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. On 1 March it served the required warning notice on her. On 5 March she signed a simple declaration that she had received the notice and accepted the consequences. She entered into the lease on 25 March, and the lease refers to the notice and the declaration. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The exclusion is invalid, because a statutory declaration sworn before a solicitor is required for every exclusion", "B": "The exclusion is valid only if Beatrix Walsh also took independent legal advice on its effect before signing the declaration", "C": "The exclusion is invalid, because the warning notice must be served at least 28 days before the lease is granted", "D": "The exclusion is valid, because notice was given at least 14 days before commitment and a simple declaration was enough", "E": "The exclusion is invalid, because the court's approval of the agreement is required before the lease is granted"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The exclusion is valid, because notice was given at least 14 days before commitment and a simple declaration was enough): Since 2004 a landlord and tenant can exclude security of tenure without a court order if the landlord serves the warning notice and the tenant makes a declaration before becoming contractually bound. Where the notice is served at least 14 days before commitment, a simple declaration is enough, and a statutory declaration is needed only if the period is shorter. Here 24 days passed between the notice and the lease, so the simple declaration was effective. Court approval was the pre 2004 requirement, and the lease correctly refers to the notice and declaration."}, {"id": 751, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The High Court holds that a provision of the Housing Act 2019 is incompatible with a tenant's right to respect for her home under article 8, and makes a declaration of incompatibility under section 4 of the Human Rights Act 1998. The tenant, Aisha Rahman, asks her solicitor what effect this has on the provision and on her own possession proceedings, in which the council relied on that provision. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The provision remains valid, and Aisha is entitled to damages automatically against the Crown because of the incompatibility", "B": "The provision remains valid and enforceable, the declaration does not bind the parties, and Parliament decides whether to amend the law", "C": "The provision is void from the date of the declaration, so the council's possession claim must be dismissed as founded on invalid law", "D": "The provision remains valid, but the declaration binds the parties, so the council cannot rely on it in the possession proceedings", "E": "The provision is struck out of the statute and Parliament must repeal it within six months after the court's declaration"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The provision remains valid and enforceable, the declaration does not bind the parties, and Parliament decides whether to amend the law): Section 4 allows a court to declare a provision of primary legislation incompatible with a Convention right, but the declaration does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the provision. It also does not bind the parties to the proceedings. Parliament and the government decide whether to change the law, for example by a remedial order under section 10. Aisha therefore gets no direct change to her possession case from the declaration alone, and damages do not follow automatically."}, {"id": 752, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Six years ago, Anita Rowe, a solicitor in Brighton, acted for Mr and Mrs Delaney in their divorce. The file has been closed and archived. At a dinner party Mrs Delaney's new partner, who is a mutual friend, mentions that he has heard rumours about her finances and asks Anita to confirm what was disclosed in the financial proceedings. Which of the following correctly states Anita's duty?", "options": {"A": "Her duty extends to confidential information that was not disclosed in open court, so she may confirm anything that was in the financial proceedings", "B": "Her duty continues but she may share information with a mutual friend who is also a close friend of the former clients", "C": "Her duty ended when the retainer ended and the file was closed, so she may speak about matters long concluded", "D": "Her duty continues for a further three years after the retainer, so after that period she is free to confirm the facts", "E": "Her duty of confidentiality continues to former clients, so she may disclose nothing unless they consent or the law permits it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Her duty of confidentiality continues to former clients, so she may disclose nothing unless they consent or the law permits it): The duty to keep the affairs of current and former clients confidential survives the end of the retainer and does not expire after a set period. Anita may disclose only where the clients consent or disclosure is required or permitted by law. Friendship with the clients, or the fact that some information once came out in proceedings, gives no authority to confirm the financial details. Her best course is to decline politely to discuss the matter."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Victor is charged under section 18 Offences Against the Person Act 1861 after striking Luis with a bottle, causing a serious head wound. Victor tells his solicitor he was sober that night and meant to cause Luis really serious harm, but he proposes to say in evidence that he was too drunk to form any intent. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Disclose Victor's admission to the court before trial, because a solicitor who knows the defence is false must tell the court of it", "B": "Advise Victor that the solicitor cannot call false evidence or run that case, because the court must not be misled", "C": "Run the intoxication case as instructed, because the solicitor owes a duty to follow the client's instructions on the facts of the defence", "D": "Run the case but not examine Victor on his sobriety, because the solicitor then avoids positively asserting the falsehood", "E": "Cease acting at once and return the papers, because a client who proposes to give false evidence leaves the solicitor no option but to withdraw"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise Victor that the solicitor cannot call false evidence or run that case, because the court must not be misled): Voluntary intoxication can be relevant to the specific intent of section 18, but only if it is true, and here Victor's own account is that he was sober. The solicitor must not mislead the court or call evidence known to be false, and must advise Victor accordingly. Confidentiality prevents disclosure of the admission. Withdrawal becomes necessary only if Victor insists on giving false evidence after proper advice."}, {"id": 754, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Probate of Mrs Kaur's will was granted on 1 March. The will left 'my shares in Pelham Ltd to my nephew Raj'. Her instructions to the drafting solicitor had been to leave them to her nephew Rav, and a typing error went unnoticed. On 20 October the executors realise the mistake and Rav's mother asks whether the will can be corrected. Which statement best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Rectification is available only if applied for within six months of the grant, and the court has no power to extend that period", "B": "Rectification requires proof of fraud by the typist, because a clerical slip alone does not allow the court to correct a will", "C": "Rav can recover the shares only by a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975, not by rectification", "D": "Rectification is unavailable, because the court may interpret the words of a will but cannot alter them", "E": "The court can rectify a clerical error, and though over six months have passed since the grant, it may permit a late application"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court can rectify a clerical error, and though over six months have passed since the grant, it may permit a late application): Section 20 of the Administration of Justice Act 1982 allows rectification where a will fails to carry out the testator's intentions because of a clerical error or a failure to understand instructions. An application should be made within six months of the grant, but the court has a discretion to permit a later application. Here the error is a typing slip supported by the instruction evidence, so rectification is available. Construction alone could not turn Raj into Rav."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Bellamy and Co acts for the claimant in a commercial dispute. When the defendant's disclosure bundle arrives, it contains an email from the defendant's solicitor to the defendant headed 'Privileged and confidential: advice on weaknesses in our defence', which is clearly a mistake. The Bellamy partner is aware that the client would benefit from reading it, and the trial is three months away. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the defendant's solicitors promptly and not use the email without the court's permission, since privilege is not lost by an evident mistake", "B": "Use it to prepare cross examination but keep it out of evidence, because the restriction covers only its use as evidence", "C": "Read it and use it at trial, because inspection of a document by the other side removes its privilege for all purposes", "D": "Keep it on file without relying on it and say nothing to the sender, because the risk of error lies with the party who disclosed it", "E": "Apply to the court for permission to use it before telling the sender, because notification would alert the defence to the error"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell the defendant's solicitors promptly and not use the email without the court's permission, since privilege is not lost by an evident mistake): Where a privileged document is inadvertently disclosed, CPR 31.20 allows the receiving party to use it only with the court's permission, and where the mistake would have been obvious to a reasonable solicitor the court will usually restrain its use. The solicitor's duties of honesty and fairness require prompt notification to the sender, so keeping quiet or exploiting the content for cross examination would be improper. Privilege belongs to the defendant and is not waived by a slip."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Two fourteen-year-old pupils, Kira and Joel, are play-fighting with wooden rulers in a classroom while their teacher is out of the room. Kira swings her ruler, and a splinter flies off and strikes Joel in the eye, causing serious injury. Joel sues Kira through his litigation friend, arguing that an adult would have realised the danger of swinging a ruler near a face. Which of the following best describes the standard of care applied to Kira?", "options": {"A": "She is not judged by an objective standard, because children below sixteen cannot owe a duty of care to their peers", "B": "She is judged against a reasonable adult supervisor, since the school had the duty to prevent the incident in her place", "C": "She is judged by her own personal maturity and awareness, so a less mature child is held to a lower standard", "D": "She is judged against an ordinary child of her age, so liability depends on whether such a child foresaw the risk", "E": "She is judged against a reasonable adult, because the activity was inherently dangerous and the injury was to the eye"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She is judged against an ordinary child of her age, so liability depends on whether such a child foresaw the risk): A child defendant is held to the standard of an ordinary reasonable child of the same age, not the standard of an adult and not a subjective standard of the particular child. The question is whether a child of fourteen would have appreciated the risk of injury from swinging a ruler near a classmate's face. The danger of eye injury does not raise the standard to that of an adult. The school's separate supervisory duty does not change what is expected of Kira herself."}, {"id": 757, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Miguel and Joanne are executors of their late mother's will and have instructed Harrow & Co solicitors. The residuary beneficiaries are Miguel, Joanne and their brother Stefan, who lives abroad. Stefan has written to the solicitor asking for a copy of the estate accounts and an update on progress. Miguel tells the solicitor not to reply because he does not trust Stefan and wants to finish the administration quietly. The solicitor acts only for the executors. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action?", "options": {"A": "Follow Miguel's instruction, because the solicitor acts only for the executors and owes no duty to give information to a beneficiary", "B": "Cease acting for the executors at once, because an instruction to withhold information from a beneficiary creates a conflict with the solicitor's duties", "C": "Reply to Stefan that accounts will be supplied only once the administration is complete, because a beneficiary has no earlier entitlement to information", "D": "Send Stefan the accounts directly without telling the executors, because the solicitor's duty to disclose is owed to the beneficiary", "E": "Explain that executors owe a duty to account to beneficiaries, and advise Miguel and Joanne to give Stefan reasonable information and accounts"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Explain that executors owe a duty to account to beneficiaries, and advise Miguel and Joanne to give Stefan reasonable information and accounts): The executors owe a duty to account to the beneficiaries, and a residuary beneficiary is entitled to reasonable information about the estate. The solicitor acts for the executors and must keep their confidences, but also must not mislead or assist a breach of duty, so the right step is to advise them to comply. Ignoring Stefan, or telling him no information is due until the end, would help the executors breach their duty. Ceasing to act is a possible later step only if they refuse to follow proper advice."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Neil Okafor was injured when a scaffold collapsed at a site run by Greystone Builders Ltd on 3 January. Proceedings were served on Greystone on 10 February, and its period for filing an acknowledgment of service runs to 24 February. Neil's solicitor wants to apply for an interim payment on 17 February, because Neil has lost earnings and needs funds for treatment. What is the position on timing?", "options": {"A": "The application cannot be made until the period for filing the acknowledgment of service has expired, so it is premature on 17 February", "B": "The application may be made now, because a personal injury claimant can seek an interim payment at any stage after issue", "C": "The application can be made now but will be heard only after the defendant has filed its defence to the claim and the time has run", "D": "The application cannot be made until the defendant has served a defence, since evidence on liability is needed first", "E": "The application cannot be made until the defendant has admitted liability or judgment has been entered on liability"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The application cannot be made until the period for filing the acknowledgment of service has expired, so it is premature on 17 February): A claimant may not apply for an interim payment before the period for the defendant to file an acknowledgment of service has expired. Greystone's period ends on 24 February, so an application on 17 February is premature. An admission of liability or a judgment is one route to an order, but it is not a precondition of applying. Nor is a defence needed before the application can be made."}, {"id": 759, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Hafod Care Ltd runs residential homes in Wales. The Senedd passed an Act giving the Welsh Ministers power to make regulations about staffing ratios 'for the purpose of protecting residents'. The Welsh Ministers then made regulations requiring every home to employ a Welsh speaking manager, which they said would promote the Welsh language. Hafod's director, who is also a friend of one Minister, asks how to challenge them. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Bring a claim under the Human Rights Act only, because subordinate legislation of the Welsh Ministers can be challenged only on Convention grounds", "B": "Bring a claim in the county court for a declaration, because regulations made by Welsh Ministers are not amenable to judicial review in England and Wales", "C": "Ask the Senedd to annul the regulations, since only the Senedd can consider whether Welsh Ministers have exceeded the power given by its own Act", "D": "Apply for judicial review in the Administrative Court, arguing the regulations were made for a purpose outside the enabling power and so are ultra vires", "E": "Make a reference directly to the Supreme Court, which alone hears challenges to subordinate legislation made under an Act of the Senedd"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply for judicial review in the Administrative Court, arguing the regulations were made for a purpose outside the enabling power and so are ultra vires): Subordinate legislation made by the Welsh Ministers is reviewable in the same way as that made by Westminster ministers, and a claim in the Administrative Court, which can sit in Wales, is the usual route. The enabling Act confines the power to protecting residents, so regulations pursuing a language policy are open to attack as made for a purpose outside the power. A possible role for the Senedd does not remove the court's jurisdiction, and the Supreme Court does not hear such challenges at first instance. The director's friendship with a Minister has no bearing on vires."}, {"id": 760, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Alan and Bina are the only trustees of a settlement. The trust instrument names nobody as having power to appoint new trustees. Bina dies, leaving Alan as the only continuing trustee. Alan wishes to appoint his friend Cora as a new trustee alongside him. All the beneficiaries are adults and live in England. Which of the following correctly states how Cora can be appointed?", "options": {"A": "Only the court can appoint Cora, because the trust instrument is silent and the statutory power of appointment cannot be used", "B": "Cora can take office only after notice of the vacancy has been published and no beneficiary has objected within 28 days", "C": "Bina's personal representatives alone can appoint Cora, because the power of appointment passes with Bina's estate on death", "D": "Alan, as continuing trustee, can appoint Cora by deed under section 36 Trustee Act 1925, since the instrument names nobody else", "E": "Alan can appoint Cora only if all the beneficiaries approve the appointment unanimously and in writing before it takes effect, as the statute demands"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Alan, as continuing trustee, can appoint Cora by deed under section 36 Trustee Act 1925, since the instrument names nobody else): Where a trustee has died and the instrument names no person with a power to appoint, section 36 Trustee Act 1925 gives the power to the surviving or continuing trustees. Alan can therefore appoint Cora by deed without the court or beneficiary consent. The personal representatives of the last surviving trustee have the power only when no trustee remains. No notice procedure is required."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "In January 2025, Corbett Roofing Ltd sold its delivery van, worth about £12,000, to Ian, the brother of its sole director, for £3,000. The company was already unable to pay its debts when the sale took place. A petition for compulsory winding up was presented in October 2026 and the liquidator wants to challenge the sale. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The liquidator can seek an order restoring the position, because the sale was an undervalue transaction within two years of the onset of insolvency", "B": "The liquidator cannot succeed if Ian acted in good faith, because good faith on the part of the buyer defeats any claim", "C": "The liquidator can succeed only if the company received nothing for the van, because partial payment prevents any undervalue claim", "D": "The liquidator must prove the director intended to put assets beyond creditors' reach, because an undervalue transaction needs proof of fraudulent intent", "E": "The liquidator cannot succeed, because the sale took place more than six months before the petition and so falls outside the relevant time"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The liquidator can seek an order restoring the position, because the sale was an undervalue transaction within two years of the onset of insolvency): A transaction at an undervalue, where the company receives consideration significantly less in value than it gives, can be set aside if made within two years before the onset of insolvency while the company was unable to pay its debts. Here the van worth £12,000 was sold for £3,000 about twenty-one months before the petition, and insolvency at the time is shown. No intent to defraud need be proved, which is the separate transaction defrauding creditors provision. The statutory defence looks at the company's good faith purpose of carrying on business, not the buyer's honesty."}, {"id": 762, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A will trust created in 2016 holds a fund for Lucy, aged 9, contingently on her reaching 25. The trust instrument does not mention maintenance or exclude section 31 Trustee Act 1925. Lucy's parents ask the trustees to pay her school fees from the trust income. The trustees want to know the test that governs the exercise of their power. Which of the following correctly states it?", "options": {"A": "They may apply income up to a limit of £10,000 per year, which is the figure fixed by the statute for maintenance", "B": "They may apply capital and income up to one half of her presumptive share, with the consent of any person who has a prior interest in the fund", "C": "They may apply such income as is reasonable for her maintenance, education or benefit, having regard to her age and requirements", "D": "They must pay out the whole of the income each year, whatever her needs, because section 31 requires all income to be applied", "E": "They may apply income only if her parents request it in writing, since the consent of parents is a condition of the power"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They may apply such income as is reasonable for her maintenance, education or benefit, having regard to her age and requirements): Section 31 Trustee Act 1925 gives trustees a discretion to apply income for the maintenance, education or benefit of a minor with a contingent interest that carries the income, so far as reasonable in all the circumstances. They must have regard to the beneficiary's age and requirements and any other income available. Capital and the one half or whole limit belong to section 32. There is no annual cap and no parental condition."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Neil Burgess repaired the roof of his elderly neighbour Mabel's house in April without being asked, and nothing was said about payment. In June, Mabel, who was grateful, promised in a signed letter to pay him £500 'for the roof work'. She has not paid, and Neil sues. Mabel did not request the repair at any stage and the letter was not executed as a deed. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Neil succeeds, because a promise made in a signed letter takes effect as a deed and so needs no consideration", "B": "Neil succeeds, because the signed promise is itself a contract and the roof repair provides sufficient consideration whatever its value", "C": "Neil fails, because the repair was completed before the promise was made and past consideration is not good consideration", "D": "Neil fails, because a promise between neighbours lacks intention to create legal relations, and consideration is not in issue", "E": "Neil succeeds, because Mabel received a clear benefit from the work and the courts enforce a promise to repay a benefit conferred"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Neil fails, because the repair was completed before the promise was made and past consideration is not good consideration): Consideration must be given in exchange for the promise, so an act completed beforehand is past consideration. The narrow exception needs the work to have been requested in advance and payment to have been understood, and neither applies here. A signed letter is not a deed, so it does not escape the need for consideration. The social presumption is not the real reason Neil fails."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Dunmore Fabrication Ltd is suing a former supplier for breach of contract. Its key evidence about what was agreed at a meeting is contained in a signed letter from a former employee, who has since emigrated to Canada and cannot be traced. The company's solicitor intends to rely on the letter at trial as proof of what the employee saw and heard. The supplier's solicitor has said the letter is hearsay and must be excluded. Which of the following best describes the position in the civil proceedings?", "options": {"A": "The letter is admissible once the court has given permission, which is granted where the maker is unable to attend the trial", "B": "The letter is inadmissible unless the supplier agrees, because hearsay in civil cases requires the consent of the opposing party", "C": "The letter is admissible, and no notice is needed, because hearsay contained in a signed document is treated as first hand evidence", "D": "The letter is admissible as hearsay, but the company must serve notice of its intention to rely on it", "E": "The letter is inadmissible, because hearsay is excluded in civil proceedings unless the maker has died or cannot be found abroad"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The letter is admissible as hearsay, but the company must serve notice of its intention to rely on it): Under the Civil Evidence Act 1995 hearsay is not excluded in civil proceedings merely because it is hearsay. The party who wants to rely on it must give the other side notice under CPR Part 33, which allows the opponent to challenge it. The court then decides how much weight to give it, considering whether the maker could have been called and whether the statement was made at the time of events. Death or absence abroad is not a condition of admissibility, and the supplier's consent is not required."}, {"id": 765, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ben is walking beside his adult brother Karl on a pavement when a car mounts the kerb and kills Karl in front of him. Ben is unhurt and not in physical danger, but he suffers a recognised psychiatric illness from witnessing the death. The driver was negligent. Ben sues the driver as a secondary victim. The driver accepts that Ben saw the accident with his own unaided senses and was close in time and space. Which of the following best describes the remaining requirement Ben must satisfy?", "options": {"A": "Ben must prove that the driver foresaw him personally as present, since a duty to a secondary victim needs actual knowledge", "B": "Ben must prove that he was in physical danger himself, because a secondary victim needs a reasonable fear for his safety", "C": "Ben need prove nothing further, because a blood relationship between siblings is presumed to give a close tie", "D": "Ben must prove that Karl was financially dependent on him, because such dependency establishes the necessary closeness", "E": "Ben must prove a close tie of love and affection with Karl, because the relationship of brothers is not presumed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ben must prove a close tie of love and affection with Karl, because the relationship of brothers is not presumed): A secondary victim must prove a close tie of love and affection with the primary victim. The tie is presumed only for spouses and for parents and children, so a sibling must prove it with evidence of the relationship. The other control mechanisms are already conceded. Physical danger to Ben is not required, and financial dependency is not the test."}, {"id": 766, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Callum, who has drunk a large amount of whisky voluntarily, sees his friend Pete swing an arm playfully to clap him on the back. In his drunken state Callum honestly believes Pete is about to punch him, and he headbutts Pete, breaking his nose. A sober person would plainly have seen it was a friendly gesture. Callum is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm. Which of the following best explains the likely outcome of his plea of self-defence?", "options": {"A": "The plea fails, because a defendant cannot rely on a mistaken belief in the need for force that was induced by voluntary intoxication", "B": "The plea fails, because the belief was unreasonable and self-defence requires that any mistake be one a sober person could have made", "C": "The plea succeeds, because Callum honestly believed he was about to be attacked and honest belief is the test whether or not it was reasonable", "D": "The plea succeeds, but only as a partial defence reducing the charge to common assault because the force was disproportionate", "E": "The plea fails, because Callum used force first and a pre-emptive strike is not lawful where the threat is not yet immediate"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The plea fails, because a defendant cannot rely on a mistaken belief in the need for force that was induced by voluntary intoxication): Section 76(5) of the Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008 provides that a defendant cannot rely on a mistaken belief attributable to voluntary intoxication, reflecting O'Grady and Hatton. Callum's honest mistake arose from his drinking, so it cannot found self-defence. The failure is not because the belief was unreasonable, since an honest but unreasonable mistake is otherwise acceptable. Pre-emptive force can also be lawful, and self-defence is a complete defence, not a partial one."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Rosalind instructs a firm on a boundary dispute with a neighbour. At the first meeting she asks the solicitor how much it will cost. The solicitor says nobody can predict the cost of disputes and that she will be billed hourly as the matter goes on. The firm holds no other information at this stage, although similar disputes have typically cost between £6,000 and £12,000 to resolve at the firm. Which of the following best states the solicitor's obligation?", "options": {"A": "The firm must give the best possible information on likely overall cost at the outset and update Rosalind as the matter progresses", "B": "The firm must give the best possible information only if the client asks in writing, because oral requests need not be answered fully", "C": "The firm need only explain its hourly rates at the outset and may leave any cost estimate until the first bill is delivered", "D": "The firm must give a fixed quotation at the outset, because estimates are not permitted for contentious work of this type", "E": "The firm is obliged to provide cost information only when the matter has been running for more than three months"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm must give the best possible information on likely overall cost at the outset and update Rosalind as the matter progresses): The SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors requires a solicitor to give clients the best possible information about the likely overall cost of the matter, both at the outset and as it progresses. Explaining only an hourly rate does not meet that standard when the firm has experience of similar cases to draw on. The duty does not depend on a written request and does not require a fixed fee. It continues as the matter develops, so updates are needed when the picture changes."}, {"id": 768, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Pendle Care Ltd is defending an employment claim in the county court. Its solicitor has drafted a witness statement for the care home manager, Mrs Okonkwo, which the solicitor wrote after a short meeting. The draft uses formal legal phrasing such as 'it is averred' and includes a paragraph giving the solicitor's view that the claim is weak. Mrs Okonkwo has said she would sign whatever the firm prepares, and exchange is due in a week. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the draft but add that some matters are belief, because a statement of truth covers all its contents without identifying sources", "B": "Leave the draft unchanged, because the witness need only read it through and sign the statement of truth before exchange", "C": "Keep the paragraph on the merits, because a witness statement may carry the solicitor's opinion if the witness endorses it by signing", "D": "Have her swear the statement before a commissioner for oaths, because an unsworn statement carries no evidential weight at trial", "E": "Revise the statement so it is in her own words and confined to evidence, as the court may discount a statement drafted otherwise"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Revise the statement so it is in her own words and confined to evidence, as the court may discount a statement drafted otherwise): A witness statement must, so far as practicable, be in the witness's own words and set out only matters of fact within her knowledge, identifying what is information or belief and its source. A statement drafted in the solicitor's phrasing that includes argument risks being struck out or given little weight, and signing a statement of truth that the witness has not truly adopted would mislead the court. A sworn affidavit is not needed for ordinary trial evidence in the county court."}, {"id": 769, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ms Fenwick consults her GP in March with a lump, and he negligently fails to refer her urgently. A referral is made nine months later and cancer is diagnosed. Expert evidence is that in March her chance of long-term survival with prompt treatment was 45 per cent, but because of the delay it fell to 25 per cent. She has since died and her husband, as executor, asks his solicitor whether a claim is worthwhile. What should the solicitor advise as the most accurate statement of the position?", "options": {"A": "The claim is unlikely to succeed, because with prompt treatment she more likely than not would have died anyway", "B": "The claim should succeed for 20 per cent of her losses, reflecting the reduction in her chance of survival caused by the delay", "C": "The claim should succeed in full, because the delay made a material contribution to her death after the failure to refer", "D": "The claim is unlikely to succeed, because a GP cannot be liable for failing to refer where a specialist later treated her", "E": "The claim should succeed for 45 per cent of her losses, reflecting the chance of survival she had before the delay"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The claim is unlikely to succeed, because with prompt treatment she more likely than not would have died anyway): In clinical negligence a claimant must prove on the balance of probabilities that the breach caused the outcome. Her chance of survival with prompt treatment was below 50 per cent, so she would probably have died anyway and the delay was not a but-for cause of death. English law does not compensate the loss of a less than evens chance of a better outcome as such. The solicitor must give this honest, realistic advice rather than encourage a claim that will probably fail."}, {"id": 770, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Brennan died leaving a short will that is rational on its face and was signed and witnessed in accordance with the Wills Act 1837, with a full attestation clause. His niece, who is excluded from the will, alleges that he lacked testamentary capacity because he suffered a stroke three years before signing. The executor intends to apply for probate and the niece has entered a caveat. At the outset, how does the burden of proving capacity operate?", "options": {"A": "The executor must prove capacity from the outset, because a person propounding a will must establish each validity requirement from the start", "B": "Capacity is presumed only for a professionally drafted will, so the executor must prove it for a will made without a solicitor", "C": "The niece must prove incapacity throughout, because the presumption of capacity remains in place once the will has been duly executed and attested", "D": "Capacity is presumed from due execution and a rational will, but if the niece raises a real doubt, the executor must then prove capacity", "E": "No burden arises until the court orders a medical report, because capacity is judged only on the evidence of experts"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Capacity is presumed from due execution and a rational will, but if the niece raises a real doubt, the executor must then prove capacity): Due execution of a rational will raises a presumption of capacity, so the executor does not begin by proving it. Once the niece produces evidence that raises a real doubt, such as the stroke here, the burden of proving capacity falls on those propounding the will. The presumption is therefore rebuttable in practice, which is why the niece does not carry the burden for the whole case. Nothing in the rules makes an expert report a condition for the burden to move."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Kofi, a self-employed electrician, wires a customer's kitchen carelessly, leaving a live connection exposed behind a cupboard. A teenage son is electrocuted and dies a year later. An expert will say Kofi's work fell below the standard of a reasonably competent electrician and that a civil claim in negligence would succeed. The Crown wishes to charge gross negligence manslaughter. Which of the following best explains the next hurdle for the prosecution?", "options": {"A": "The prosecution must prove Kofi was subjectively aware of the danger, because gross negligence manslaughter requires proof of recklessness", "B": "The judge decides whether the conduct was grossly negligent, and the jury decides only whether the breach caused the death", "C": "The prosecution succeeds once it proves a breach of duty causing death, because civil negligence of that degree is sufficient for criminal liability", "D": "The prosecution must show the exposed wire was an unlawful act directed at the victim, which is the test for the offence", "E": "The jury must be satisfied the breach was so bad, in the circumstances, as to be truly criminal, and mere civil negligence is not enough"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The jury must be satisfied the breach was so bad, in the circumstances, as to be truly criminal, and mere civil negligence is not enough): In gross negligence manslaughter the jury, not the judge, decides whether the breach of duty was gross, meaning so bad that it should be judged criminal and deserving of punishment. Proof of a civil breach is only a starting point. Subjective awareness of risk is not required under the Adomako approach. The prosecution must also prove a serious and obvious risk of death, but the immediate question is whether the negligence crosses the criminal threshold."}, {"id": 772, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Corwen Supplies Ltd issued a claim form on 9 February 2026 against Brindle Ltd, whose registered office is in Bristol. On Thursday 12 February 2026 the court posted the claim form and particulars of claim to Brindle's registered office by first class post. There are no bank holidays in the relevant period. On what date is the claim form deemed served on Brindle?", "options": {"A": "Friday 13 February 2026, because first class post is deemed served on the next day after posting", "B": "Thursday 12 February 2026, because service is complete on the day the court places it in the post", "C": "Tuesday 17 February 2026, because deemed service runs from the second business day after the day on which the claim is received", "D": "Saturday 14 February 2026, because a claim form is deemed served on the second day after posting", "E": "Monday 16 February 2026, because deemed service of a claim form is the second business day after the step of posting"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Monday 16 February 2026, because deemed service of a claim form is the second business day after the step of posting): A claim form is deemed served on the second business day after the claimant or the court completes the step required for service, and for first class post that step is posting. Posting on Thursday 12 February gives Friday as the first business day and Monday 16 February as the second. Saturday and Sunday are not business days. The rule counts from posting and not from receipt, so Tuesday is wrong."}, {"id": 773, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Lena submits an online insurance claim for a stolen laptop, attaching a forged purchase receipt, knowing she never owned the laptop. The insurer's system automatically flags the receipt as altered, the claim is refused and no payment is made. Lena is charged with fraud by false representation under section 2 of the Fraud Act 2006. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The offence cannot be committed where the representation is made to an automated system, because only a person can be deceived", "B": "The offence is complete once the dishonest false representation is made with the required intention, even if nobody is deceived and no gain results", "C": "The offence is made out only if the insurer suffered financial loss, so Lena is liable only if the insurer later pays", "D": "The offence requires proof that Lena obtained money, so the absence of payment means no offence was committed", "E": "The offence requires that the insurer was actually deceived by the representation, so Lena is guilty only of an attempt"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The offence is complete once the dishonest false representation is made with the required intention, even if nobody is deceived and no gain results): Fraud by false representation is a conduct offence. It is complete when the defendant dishonestly makes a representation that is false, knowing it is or might be, intending to make a gain or cause loss. Nobody needs to be deceived and nothing needs to be obtained, so the refusal of the claim does not matter. Section 2(5) also provides that a representation can be made to a system or device, so the automated flag is not a defence."}, {"id": 774, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Trustees of a £500,000 fund lend £150,000 on a mortgage of a house, paying out the money before the legal charge is registered, contrary to their instructions to the solicitors. The borrower later defaults and the sale of the house raises only £90,000. The court finds that, had the charge been registered the day after completion, the same loss would have occurred. The beneficiaries claim compensation for the shortfall. Which of the following best states the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "They cannot recover compensation, because the loss was not caused by the breach and would have occurred anyway", "B": "They recover the shortfall of £60,000, because trustees must restore the fund to its earlier state whenever they depart from instructions", "C": "They recover half of the shortfall, since fault is shared between the trustees and the defaulting borrower", "D": "They recover the whole £150,000, because releasing trust money ahead of security makes the trustees strictly liable for what was paid out", "E": "They can recover only if they prove that the trustees acted dishonestly, since a technical breach does not carry any right to compensation"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They cannot recover compensation, because the loss was not caused by the breach and would have occurred anyway): Equitable compensation for breach of trust requires a causal link between the breach and the loss, assessed on a but-for basis as in Target Holdings v Redferns and AIB v Redler. The same loss would have occurred if the charge had been registered next day, so the breach did not cause it. Trustees are not strictly liable for any departure from instructions. Dishonesty is not required where causation is shown, but here it fails."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Calder Machinery sent Ravenscroft Ltd a quotation for a machine at £50,000 on its standard terms, which included a price variation clause. Ravenscroft replied with an order on its own terms that excluded any price variation, and included a tear off slip stating that Calder accepted the order on the terms stated in it. Calder signed and returned the slip and delivered the machine. Calder now relies on the price variation clause to claim £6,000 more. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Neither set of terms governs, because the conflict between the forms prevents any contract being formed and Calder is limited to a reasonable price", "B": "Calder's terms govern, because the first party to put forward standard terms prevails where its quotation was clear and complete", "C": "Ravenscroft's terms govern, but only because Calder delivered the machine, since conduct rather than the signed slip created the contract", "D": "Ravenscroft's terms govern, because its order was a counter offer which Calder accepted by returning the signed slip, so Calder cannot rely on its own variation clause", "E": "Calder's terms govern, because its quotation was the offer and its terms remain unless the buyer rejects each of them expressly in writing"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ravenscroft's terms govern, because its order was a counter offer which Calder accepted by returning the signed slip, so Calder cannot rely on its own variation clause): A reply that adds or changes terms is a counter offer and ends the original offer. Ravenscroft's order on its own terms was a counter offer. Calder accepted it, in terms, by signing and returning the slip, so the contract was made on Ravenscroft's terms. The first form does not prevail, and the conflict does not prevent a contract being formed. There is no price variation clause in the contract."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Carys attacks her business partner Vikram with a hammer intending to kill him. Believing he is dead, she rolls his body over a cliff to make it look like an accident. Post mortem evidence shows Vikram was alive when he went over the edge and died from exposure at the foot of the cliff. Carys argues that she had no intention to kill when she committed the act that actually caused death. Which of the following is the most likely outcome on a murder charge?", "options": {"A": "She is guilty, because the attack and the disposal form one series of acts and she held the intent at the start of it", "B": "She is not guilty of murder, because the act that caused death was done believing Vikram was already dead and so without intent to kill", "C": "She is guilty only of manslaughter, because the fatal act occurred after her intention to kill had come to an end", "D": "She is guilty, because an intention formed at any point before death is sufficient even where the later act is separate and unplanned", "E": "She is not guilty of murder, because the hammer blows were not the operating cause of death and cannot be linked to the intent"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She is guilty, because the attack and the disposal form one series of acts and she held the intent at the start of it): Where a defendant's conduct forms a single series of acts, it is enough that the mens rea existed at some point during that series, and the coincidence requirement is satisfied. The attack and the disposal were part of one plan, and the intention to kill at the start covers the whole sequence. The belief that Vikram was dead does not break the link. The distinction is between a genuine series and separate, unrelated acts."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Gareth Pryce, an associate, is reviewing the file of a client, Marden Foods Ltd, whose claim for breach of a supply contract (breach on 3 June 2020) was never issued. He discovers that the supervising partner's diary entry recorded the wrong limitation date and that the six year period expired on 3 June 2026. The client has not been told. The partner suggests waiting until the firm's insurers have been consulted before saying anything. What should Gareth do?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the insurers' view before telling the client, because the firm owes a duty to its insurer to avoid prejudicing the policy", "B": "Ask the defendant to agree not to rely on limitation, and tell the client only if the defendant refuses", "C": "Tell the client that the claim is likely to be defended on limitation but that the firm will pursue it, without admitting any error", "D": "Issue the claim at once and apply to the court to disapply the limitation period under section 33, then inform the client of the outcome", "E": "Make sure the client is told promptly about the error and may seek independent advice, and report it to the firm's compliance officer"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Make sure the client is told promptly about the error and may seek independent advice, and report it to the firm's compliance officer): The SRA Code of Conduct requires a solicitor to ensure that clients are told promptly of any act or omission that could give rise to a claim against the firm, and that they may want independent advice. The firm's own interests, including those of its insurers, cannot come before that duty to the client. The discretion in section 33 applies to personal injury claims, not contract claims, so issuing late would not save the claim. Gareth should also report the matter within the firm so that the compliance officer can deal with it."}, {"id": 778, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Delphine signed a contract with Garrick Joinery to buy fitted wardrobes for £8,000, with delivery 'by the end of May, subject to the usual conditions of trade'. Neither party can say what the usual conditions are. Delphine paid a £2,000 deposit. Garrick, now facing higher timber costs, says the whole contract is void for uncertainty. The price, the goods and the delivery date are clear. Which of the following is the best advice for Delphine?", "options": {"A": "The meaningless phrase can be severed and the contract enforced, because the essential terms are clear and performance has begun", "B": "The phrase is an agreement to agree that suspends the contract until the parties settle it, so neither side is yet bound", "C": "The whole contract fails for uncertainty, because a meaningless term shows that the parties did not reach agreement on the essential matters", "D": "The contract is enforceable only if the court replaces the phrase with the conditions commonly used between joiners and customers", "E": "The phrase is a condition of the contract, so Garrick's failure to define it entitles Delphine to terminate and recover damages"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The meaningless phrase can be severed and the contract enforced, because the essential terms are clear and performance has begun): A meaningless phrase that is not essential can be ignored, leaving a contract that is otherwise complete. Here the goods, price and delivery date are certain and Delphine has paid a deposit, so the parties have shown they regard the contract as binding. The phrase added nothing the contract needed. Garrick cannot use it to escape the bargain, and the court does not have to replace it."}, {"id": 779, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Rhian is a Welsh speaker charged with possession of cannabis, and her trial is listed in a fortnight at the magistrates' court in Swansea. Rhian says she wants the hearing in Welsh, and two defence witnesses, her friends, speak Welsh as a first language. Her solicitor, Rhodri, has not yet told the court anything about language. Which of the following best describes what Rhodri should do?", "options": {"A": "Advise Rhian to give evidence in English, because a trial listed at short notice cannot accommodate Welsh", "B": "Ask the court to approve Welsh for Rhian only and not for the witnesses, as the right belongs to defendants", "C": "Arrange a private interpreter at Rhian's expense, because the court is not required to provide interpretation for a fluent English speaker like her", "D": "Wait until the hearing begins and then ask the bench to adjourn so an interpreter can be found for the witnesses", "E": "Tell the court promptly that Welsh will be used so interpretation can be arranged, and that Rhian need give no reasons"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Tell the court promptly that Welsh will be used so interpretation can be arranged, and that Rhian need give no reasons): Any party, witness or other person in legal proceedings in Wales may speak Welsh, subject to notice under the rules. Rhodri should act in Rhian's best interests by giving the court prompt notice so that interpretation is arranged at public expense and the trial is not delayed. She is not required to explain her choice. The right extends to her witnesses as well, so seeking it for her alone is wrong."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Thornby Care Ltd engaged Wilder Training Ltd under a services agreement for a fixed term of twelve months ending on 31 December 2025. It provides that the agreement continues for successive twelve month periods unless either party gives at least three months' written notice before the end of the current term. Thornby's managing director, who was busy with an inspection, posted a signed notice on 15 October 2025 stating that Thornby wished to end the agreement. Wilder received it on 17 October. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The notice was too late to stop renewal because it was due by the end of September, so the agreement runs until 31 December 2026", "B": "The notice is invalid and the agreement renews, because notice posted but not signed by both parties cannot be effective", "C": "The agreement ends on 17 January 2026, three months after notice was received, because late notice takes effect after the stated period", "D": "The agreement ended on 31 December 2025, because notice was given before the end of the current term", "E": "The agreement ended on 31 December 2025, because a fixed term contract cannot continue after expiry without a new signed agreement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The notice was too late to stop renewal because it was due by the end of September, so the agreement runs until 31 December 2026): The agreement renews automatically unless notice is given at least three months before the term ends, which meant by the end of September 2025. Notice given in mid October was out of time, so the contract rolled into a further twelve month period to 31 December 2026. A fixed term can provide for renewal, so expiry on 31 December does not end it. Late notice does not take effect on some other date, and a unilateral notice does not need signature by both parties."}, {"id": 781, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Kofi is buying a registered house from Hal and has read the register, which shows nothing about the shared drive. His surveyor noted a clearly visible tarmac drive running alongside the house, with a neighbour's car parked on it. After completion, the neighbour, Ines, claims a legal easement of way over the drive acquired by long use before Kofi bought, which was never entered on Hal's title. Ines had used the drive almost daily. Which of the following best describes Ines's right against Kofi?", "options": {"A": "It binds Kofi only if he had actual knowledge of the right when he completed the purchase", "B": "It does not bind Kofi, because rights acquired by long use are equitable and need notice on the register", "C": "The easement binds Kofi as an overriding interest, because it is legal and was obvious on a reasonable inspection of the land", "D": "It does not bind Kofi, because a legal easement binds a buyer of registered land only if entered on the register", "E": "It binds Kofi only if Ines was in actual occupation of the house at the date of completion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The easement binds Kofi as an overriding interest, because it is legal and was obvious on a reasonable inspection of the land): A legal easement can be an overriding interest under Schedule 3 paragraph 3 of the Land Registration Act 2002 if the buyer knew of it, or it would have been obvious on a reasonably careful inspection, or it was used within the year before the transfer. A drive in clear view with a car parked on it is obvious, and Ines's use was almost daily, so the easement binds Kofi without registration. Actual knowledge is not required, and the occupation rule concerns beneficial interests, not easements."}, {"id": 782, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Mirela is a director of Quill Ltd, a private company with three shareholders. The board resolved to lend her £25,000, interest free, so that she could buy a car for personal use. The loan was paid but no vote of the members was taken. Another director, Owen, later discovers this and asks what the position is. Quill is solvent and has not yet demanded repayment. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Prior member approval by ordinary resolution was needed, so the company may avoid the loan and Mirela may have to account for any gain", "B": "The loan is void automatically because member approval was missing, so Mirela has no obligation to repay the money and Quill cannot recover it", "C": "No member approval was needed because Quill is a private company, and the statutory requirement applies only to public companies", "D": "Board approval was enough, because loans of up to £50,000 to a director are exempt from the member approval requirement", "E": "Member approval was needed, but it required a special resolution of 75% of the members rather than an ordinary resolution"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Prior member approval by ordinary resolution was needed, so the company may avoid the loan and Mirela may have to account for any gain): A loan to a director above £10,000 needs prior approval of the members by ordinary resolution. The £50,000 exemption covers only expenditure for the purposes of the company, which a personal car is not. Without approval the loan is voidable at the company's option, not void, and the director may be liable to account for any gain and indemnify the company. The rule applies to private companies as well."}, {"id": 783, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Marlow Tools Ltd was run by its directors, who took deposits from customers for goods they knew the company could not deliver, intending to pay older suppliers with the money. The company's accountant, Ms Pryce, knew of this plan and prepared accounts that hid the position, although she is not a director. The company is now in liquidation. Against whom can the liquidator seek a contribution to the company's assets for fraudulent trading?", "options": {"A": "The directors only, because fraudulent trading liability is confined to directors and shadow directors who actually ran the company's affairs and took the deposits", "B": "Ms Pryce only, because the directors are protected by limited liability unless they gave personal guarantees to the company's creditors", "C": "The directors and Ms Pryce, but only if the liquidator proves she personally benefited from the customer deposits", "D": "The directors and also Ms Pryce, because anyone knowingly party to carrying on the business with intent to defraud creditors may be ordered to contribute", "E": "No one, because a liquidator may seek contributions only for wrongful trading, which requires negligence rather than dishonesty"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The directors and also Ms Pryce, because anyone knowingly party to carrying on the business with intent to defraud creditors may be ordered to contribute): Fraudulent trading under section 213 Insolvency Act 1986 can be committed by any person who was knowingly party to carrying on the business with intent to defraud creditors, so it is not limited to directors. Ms Pryce knew of the plan and helped to conceal it, so she may be ordered to contribute alongside the directors. The liquidator need not prove she gained personally. Wrongful trading is a separate claim against directors that does not need dishonesty, and limited liability gives no protection where fraud is shown."}, {"id": 784, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Greg drinks eight pints of beer voluntarily at a pub and, in a fight, punches Ali in the face, breaking his nose. Greg says he was so drunk that he did not realise what he was doing and has no memory of it. He is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm under section 47 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Voluntary intoxication is a defence if it prevented Greg from forming any mental element, because every offence requires proof of a guilty mind", "B": "Voluntary intoxication is a defence, because Greg did not take the drink to give himself courage and had no plan to hurt anyone", "C": "Voluntary intoxication is no defence, because section 47 is a basic intent offence and drinking voluntarily supplies the recklessness required", "D": "Voluntary intoxication reduces liability to common assault, because section 47 requires intention as to the actual bodily harm caused", "E": "Voluntary intoxication is a defence only if Greg was so drunk that he lacked capacity to act voluntarily, as in a state of automatism"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Voluntary intoxication is no defence, because section 47 is a basic intent offence and drinking voluntarily supplies the recklessness required): Under DPP v Majewski, voluntary intoxication is not a defence to a basic intent offence, because becoming voluntarily intoxicated is itself a reckless course of conduct. Section 47 needs only the mental element of assault or battery, so recklessness suffices. Greg's loss of memory and extreme drunkenness do not change the rule. The defence of lacking intent is only open for specific intent offences, and Greg did not drink with any special aim."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A claims management company, Redbridge Claims Ltd, offers a high street firm in Wolverhampton a payment of 15 percent of any damages recovered in each road traffic accident injury claim it passes to the firm. The firm's partners note that the company is itself regulated and that the arrangement would be disclosed to clients. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The firm cannot pay the fee, because referral fees for personal injury claims are prohibited whether or not disclosed or the recipient is regulated", "B": "The firm may pay the fee if it is a fixed sum rather than a percentage of damages, because percentage fees are prohibited", "C": "The firm may pay the fee, because the recipient is a regulated company and the arrangement does not compromise the clients' interests", "D": "The firm may pay the fee, because disclosure to clients in writing before instructions are accepted is the requirement that applies to referral fees", "E": "The firm may pay the fee if the client consents in writing after receiving independent advice about the arrangement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm cannot pay the fee, because referral fees for personal injury claims are prohibited whether or not disclosed or the recipient is regulated): Paying or receiving a referral fee in respect of a personal injury claim is prohibited by statute and by the SRA rules. Disclosure to the client, the recipient's regulated status and the client's consent make no difference. The disclosure approach applies to permitted referral arrangements in other types of work. Changing from a percentage to a fixed sum would still be a referral fee."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A junior official in the Department for Rural Affairs misread the law and sent out thousands of incorrect grant refusals. The error was not known to the Secretary of State, Helen Caldwell, who had issued no instructions on the matter and acted promptly to correct it when told. An opposition MP argues that Helen must resign because the department made an error. A civil servant asks which of the following best describes the convention of individual ministerial responsibility here.", "options": {"A": "Helen must resign, because a minister takes personal blame for every mistake made by officials in her department", "B": "Helen should write to the Prime Minister, because collective responsibility covers the department's error and Parliament has no role", "C": "Helen is bound to answer the question in court, because the convention is a legal rule that a court can enforce", "D": "Helen has no duty to answer to Parliament, because civil servants are answerable for their own errors in the department", "E": "Helen must explain the error to Parliament and say how it will be corrected, but she need not resign for an official's mistake"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Helen must explain the error to Parliament and say how it will be corrected, but she need not resign for an official's mistake): Individual ministerial responsibility means a minister is accountable to Parliament for the policies and conduct of their department, which includes giving an account and putting things right. It does not require resignation for every error by an official, and resignation is typically linked to personal fault or serious failings. Helen was not involved and acted promptly, so explanation and correction are the expected response. The convention is political, not legal, so courts do not enforce it."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Tessa Bright, a solicitor, acts for Grafton Garage Ltd, which is sued for £30,000 for a van delivered and not paid for. At a meeting the managing director tells Tessa that the debt is owed in full and that the van works perfectly. He asks Tessa to file a defence denying the debt and verified by a statement of truth, simply so that payment is put off for six months. He has no funds to pay now. What should Tessa do?", "options": {"A": "File a defence denying the debt but advise the managing director to sign the statement of truth personally, since the signature is his", "B": "File a defence making no admission that puts the claimant to proof, because the claimant carries the burden of proving the debt owed", "C": "Apply to be removed from the court record at once and tell the claimant's solicitors the reason, since the instructions were improper", "D": "Refuse to plead a defence she knows is false, and advise on admitting the claim with an offer to pay by instalments", "E": "File the defence as instructed, because a solicitor must follow a client's lawful instructions and the court can later decide the truth"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refuse to plead a defence she knows is false, and advise on admitting the claim with an offer to pay by instalments): A solicitor must not mislead the court or knowingly allow it to be misled, and a defence that denies a debt which the client says is owed is misleading, whether the statement of truth is signed by the client or the solicitor. Putting the claimant to proof is available only where the defendant genuinely cannot admit or deny the allegation. Following client instructions does not excuse a false pleading, and confidentiality forbids telling the claimant why. The proper course is to advise an admission with a time to pay offer."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Quartz Design Ltd, a small studio, applies for an interim injunction to stop a former director opening a rival studio in breach of a six month restrictive covenant. It shows a serious issue to be tried. The evidence also shows that, if the injunction is wrongly refused, Quartz's lost profits for the period can be calculated and the former director has ample funds to pay them. Quartz offers a cross-undertaking in damages. How will the court most likely decide the application?", "options": {"A": "Grant the injunction, because the balance of convenience favours the party who would otherwise lose a covenant it bargained for", "B": "Grant the injunction, because a serious issue to be tried and a cross-undertaking are enough to secure relief", "C": "Refuse the injunction, because restrictive covenants can be enforced by final order only after a full trial", "D": "Refuse the injunction, because damages would be an adequate remedy for Quartz and the defendant could pay them", "E": "Refuse the injunction, because Quartz has not shown that it is more likely than not to win at trial, which Cyanamid requires"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refuse the injunction, because damages would be an adequate remedy for Quartz and the defendant could pay them): Under the American Cyanamid approach the court asks whether there is a serious issue to be tried, then whether damages would be an adequate remedy for the claimant, and only then looks at the balance of convenience. Quartz's loss is calculable and the defendant can pay, so damages would be adequate and the injunction would be refused. A serious issue and a cross-undertaking are not enough by themselves. The claimant need not show that success is more likely than not, and covenants can be enforced by interim injunction in a suitable case."}, {"id": 789, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Imran Haque is buying his first flat in Derby and is comparing different types of legal service provider. He has been told that, besides solicitors, some licensed conveyancers can act on his purchase and that each type of provider is overseen by its own regulator. He asks which body authorises and regulates licensed conveyancers. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The Bar Standards Board, which regulates barristers and entities that carry on reserved conveyancing activities", "B": "The Intellectual Property Regulation Board, which authorises specialist practitioners who provide property transfer services", "C": "The Council for Licensed Conveyancers, which authorises licensed conveyancers and sets the standards they must meet in practice", "D": "CILEx Regulation, which authorises chartered legal executives and licensed conveyancers under one common set of practice rules", "E": "The Legal Services Board, which authorises individual licensed conveyancers directly and hears complaints from their clients"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The Council for Licensed Conveyancers, which authorises licensed conveyancers and sets the standards they must meet in practice): Licensed conveyancers are authorised and regulated by the Council for Licensed Conveyancers, an approved regulator under the Legal Services Act 2007. CILEx Regulation covers chartered legal executives and has its own rules. The Bar Standards Board regulates barristers, and the IPReg board regulates patent and trade mark attorneys. The Legal Services Board oversees all the approved regulators but does not authorise individual practitioners."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Kestrel Print Ltd, the defendant to a negligence claim, was directed to serve its witness statements by 4pm on 10 April. It served them on 13 April because its operations manager was ill. The trial is not until October, no other date in the timetable has moved, and the claimant, Mr Rahman, was not put to any extra cost. Mr Rahman refuses to agree to the late statements, and Kestrel applies to the court for relief so that its witnesses may be called. Mr Rahman says the company has given no good reason. What is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Relief is likely to be refused, because any failure to meet a court deadline must be treated as serious unless the other side consents", "B": "Relief is likely to be refused, because the defendant offered no good reason for the delay and a bad explanation alone defeats the application", "C": "Relief is likely to be granted, but only on condition that the defendant first pays the claimant's costs of the application in advance", "D": "Relief is not available, because the relief from sanctions rule applies to unless orders and not to sanctions imposed by the rules", "E": "Relief is likely to be granted, because a three day delay with no effect on the trial date is not serious or significant at the first stage"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Relief is likely to be granted, because a three day delay with no effect on the trial date is not serious or significant at the first stage): The court first asks whether the breach is serious or significant. A delay of three days that does not threaten the trial date or add cost is neither, so the court will normally grant relief without needing to dwell on the reason. The reason for the default is considered at the second stage, and a weak explanation does not by itself defeat a trivial breach. Witness statements served late fall within the rule on sanctions, so the relief jurisdiction does apply."}, {"id": 791, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Mr and Mrs Patel jointly applied to Summit Bank for a loan of £90,000 to buy a business, and both signed a legal charge of their jointly owned home to secure it. Both were present when the bank explained the loan terms and neither was recommended to take separate advice. Mrs Patel now says her husband pressured her to agree and that she did not understand the risk to the home. She asks to set the charge aside against the bank. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The claim is unlikely to succeed, because a joint loan for the couple's shared purpose does not put the bank on inquiry", "B": "The claim should succeed, because the bank knew the parties were married and failed to insist on separate legal advice for Mrs Patel before the charge was signed", "C": "The claim should succeed, because Mrs Patel can show actual undue influence by her husband and that suffices against the bank", "D": "The claim should succeed to the extent of her half share, because a charge can bind only the interest of a willing owner", "E": "The claim is unlikely to succeed, but only because the bank advised both borrowers at the same meeting"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The claim is unlikely to succeed, because a joint loan for the couple's shared purpose does not put the bank on inquiry): A lender is put on inquiry where a loan is made to one spouse and the other stands surety. Where the loan is made to both spouses jointly for their joint purpose, the bank is not put on inquiry, and it need not insist on separate advice. Mrs Patel's allegation of pressure by her husband does not help unless the bank is affected by it, and that depends on notice. Marriage on its own is not enough, and the reason for failure is the nature of the loan, not the joint meeting."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Gareth was dismissed by his employer after eight years and wishes to claim unfair dismissal in the employment tribunal. He is on a low income and receives universal credit. He asks a solicitor whether he can get civil legal aid to pay for representation. The solicitor knows that the claim concerns only unfair dismissal and does not involve any allegation of discrimination, unlawful deduction or human rights breach. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Legal aid is available, because dismissal causes loss of employment, which the Act treats as a serious matter affecting livelihood", "B": "Legal aid is available for tribunal claims that the Legal Aid Agency considers have reasonable prospects, provided Gareth passes the means test", "C": "Legal aid is available, because a claimant on universal credit satisfies the means test and the merits test for any tribunal claim", "D": "Civil legal aid is not available as of right for this claim, because unfair dismissal is outside the matters listed in the scope of the Act", "E": "Legal aid is available for employment claims if Gareth first obtains a certificate from the tribunal confirming the claim raises a point of law"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Civil legal aid is not available as of right for this claim, because unfair dismissal is outside the matters listed in the scope of the Act): Civil legal aid is available only for matters listed in Schedule 1 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012, and the claimant must also satisfy the means and merits tests. Unfair dismissal is not listed, whereas discrimination claims are. Passing the means test and having good prospects cannot bring an out of scope claim within the scheme. Exceptional case funding is a narrow route and is not available merely because the claim is out of scope."}, {"id": 793, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Rowan Pickering is sued by Strand Supplies Ltd for £30,000 for goods delivered in June. Mr Pickering admits to his solicitor, in confidence, that he received the goods and has not paid, but he wants a witness statement resisting the summary judgment application to say that no goods were ever delivered. He says the claimant has lost its delivery notes. The hearing is in ten days. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Disclose to the court and the claimant that the client has admitted receipt, because confidentiality gives way to the duty to the court", "B": "Draft the statement as instructed, because the claimant bears the burden of proof and the solicitor is not the witness", "C": "Draft the statement as instructed but require Mr Pickering to sign it himself, so that responsibility for its truth is his alone", "D": "Decline to advance the untrue account, tell the client why, and carry on acting only if the defence is limited to putting the claimant to proof", "E": "Draft the statement but say that Mr Pickering 'does not recall' delivery, which is not a positive assertion of fact and so is safe"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to advance the untrue account, tell the client why, and carry on acting only if the defence is limited to putting the claimant to proof): A solicitor must not mislead the court and must not put forward evidence that he knows to be untrue, whatever the client instructs. The solicitor may still act, and Mr Pickering may put the claimant to proof of delivery without asserting a false case. Softer phrasing such as not recalling would still create a false impression. The solicitor must keep the admission confidential, so disclosing it to the court is wrong, and the client's signature does not relieve the solicitor of professional responsibility."}, {"id": 794, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Marcus Hale is arrested at his home on suspicion of burglary at 7.15 am on a Tuesday. He arrives at the police station at 8.00 am, and the custody officer authorises his detention at 8.20 am. He has not been charged and no extension of detention has been sought. By what time must the first review of his detention take place, and who must carry it out?", "options": {"A": "By 8.00 pm, twelve hours after his arrival, by the custody officer who authorised his detention at the police station", "B": "By 2.20 pm, six hours after detention was authorised, by an officer of at least inspector rank who is not involved in the investigation", "C": "By 1.15 pm, six hours after the arrest, by a superintendent who has not previously been involved in the case or its investigation", "D": "By 4.15 pm, nine hours after the arrest, by an inspector who is not involved in the investigation of the burglary", "E": "By 5.20 pm, nine hours after detention was authorised, by the custody officer who first authorised his detention at the police station, with no senior officer involved"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (By 2.20 pm, six hours after detention was authorised, by an officer of at least inspector rank who is not involved in the investigation): The first review under PACE section 40 must take place no later than six hours after detention was first authorised, which here is 8.20 am, giving 2.20 pm. It must be carried out by a review officer of at least inspector rank who has not been involved in the investigation. Later reviews follow at intervals of no more than nine hours. Counting from arrest or from arrival at the station is wrong because the period runs from the authorisation of detention, and the custody officer who authorises detention is not the review officer."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Dan is walking through a park at dusk when Earl, a stranger, runs towards him with an arm raised. Dan honestly believes Earl is about to attack him and punches him first, breaking his nose. Earl was in fact running to hand Dan a dropped wallet. A sober reasonable person would not have thought Earl a threat. Dan is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and pleads self-defence. Which of the following correctly states how his belief is treated?", "options": {"A": "Dan can rely on self-defence only if he reasonably feared death, since a pre-emptive punch is lawful only against a threat to life", "B": "Dan is judged on the facts as he honestly believed them to be, and an unreasonable belief is only evidence about whether he held it", "C": "Dan can rely on self-defence only if his mistaken belief was also a reasonable one, because the test is wholly objective", "D": "Dan cannot rely on self-defence, because no attack was actually taking place and the defence needs an unlawful threat in fact", "E": "Dan can rely on self-defence if his belief was honest, but only if the jury finds he tried to retreat before punching when Earl approached"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dan is judged on the facts as he honestly believed them to be, and an unreasonable belief is only evidence about whether he held it): Self-defence is assessed on the facts as the defendant honestly believed them to be, even if the mistake was unreasonable, following Gladstone Williams and now section 76 of the Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008. The unreasonableness is evidence that the jury can weigh in deciding whether the belief was genuinely held. The force used must then be reasonable in those believed circumstances. A threat to life is not required and there is no duty to retreat, though a failure to do so is relevant."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Naledi is in a dispute with her former husband about the division of the family home and pensions in financial remedy proceedings. She cannot afford hourly rates and asks a solicitor for a conditional fee agreement with a success fee of 50 per cent. The solicitor believes the case is strong. The matrimonial property is valued at £900,000 and the husband has instructed leading counsel. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "A conditional fee agreement cannot be used for family proceedings of this kind, so the solicitor should discuss a private retainer or loan", "B": "A damages-based agreement is the only form of contingency funding the solicitor may offer, and it is permitted for family proceedings", "C": "A conditional fee agreement is permitted if the client agrees in writing and takes out after the event insurance against adverse costs", "D": "A conditional fee agreement is permitted, but the success fee is limited to 25 per cent of the assets awarded to the client", "E": "A conditional fee agreement is permitted, but only where the husband has substantially greater resources, as the court will then allow the uplift"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A conditional fee agreement cannot be used for family proceedings of this kind, so the solicitor should discuss a private retainer or loan): Family proceedings cannot be the subject of an enforceable conditional fee agreement, and damages-based agreements are likewise unavailable for them. The strength of the case, the size of the assets and the other side's resources do not change that rule. Written consent and insurance do not make an otherwise barred agreement enforceable. The solicitor should explain the restriction and discuss alternatives such as a private retainer, staged payments, a loan or a request for a costs allowance."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Esme Whitlock instructs a solicitor to incorporate Harrow Bakery Ltd, a private company limited by shares, which will adopt the model articles without amendment. Esme and her brother will be the only subscribers and directors. The solicitor is preparing the online application to Companies House and wants to be sure which documents and statements must be delivered with the application. Which of the following correctly states what must be delivered?", "options": {"A": "A memorandum of association, the articles of association in every case, a shareholders' agreement and a statement of proposed officers", "B": "A memorandum of association, a statement of proposed officers and a statement of compliance, with details of share capital filed within one month afterwards", "C": "A memorandum of association, a statement of the company's proposed trading activities, and a solvency declaration signed by all the subscribers", "D": "A memorandum, a statement of capital and initial shareholdings, a statement of proposed officers, a statement of compliance and PSC information", "E": "A statement of capital, a statement of proposed officers and the articles, with no memorandum because the articles now replace it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A memorandum, a statement of capital and initial shareholdings, a statement of proposed officers, a statement of compliance and PSC information): The Companies Act 2006 requires the memorandum, an application containing the statement of capital and initial shareholdings, a statement of proposed officers, a statement of compliance and the people with significant control information. Where no articles are delivered the model articles apply, so articles need not be filed here. A shareholders' agreement is a private contract and is never filed on incorporation. The memorandum survives under the 2006 Act as a short statement that the subscribers wish to form a company and take shares."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Oliver breaks into a house at night intending to steal jewellery. He carries a loaded handgun in his coat pocket, which he brought in case he is disturbed, though he does not take it out. While inside, he steals a watch. Oliver is charged with aggravated burglary under section 10 of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following best describes his liability?", "options": {"A": "He is not guilty, because the firearm must have been taken into the house for the specific purpose of injuring a person", "B": "He is not guilty, because aggravated burglary requires him to have used or threatened to use the firearm during the burglary", "C": "He is guilty of simple burglary only, because aggravated burglary is reserved for cases where a person is injured in the house", "D": "He is guilty, but only if the handgun was loaded and capable of being fired when the burglary took place", "E": "He is guilty, because he had a firearm with him at the time of the burglary and need not have used or threatened to use it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (He is guilty, because he had a firearm with him at the time of the burglary and need not have used or threatened to use it): Aggravated burglary is committed where a person commits a burglary and at the time has with him a firearm, imitation firearm, weapon of offence or explosive. For a firearm no intention to use it is needed, and it need not be used or shown. An imitation would suffice, so being loaded is not essential. Injury to a person is not an element."}, {"id": 799, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Raj, an experienced property developer, agreed orally with Sue, a landowner, that she would sell him her farm if he obtained planning permission, with the price to be settled later and the details to be agreed in a formal contract. Both knew that nothing yet bound them. Raj spent £150,000 on the planning application. Sue then refused to proceed. Raj claims proprietary estoppel. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails, because in a commercial deal Raj knew nothing bound Sue and took the risk she might withdraw", "B": "The claim fails, because the assurance was not made in writing signed by both parties", "C": "The claim succeeds, because an oral agreement for the sale of land is enforceable once the claimant has acted on it", "D": "The claim fails, because proprietary estoppel cannot apply to land that is registered", "E": "The claim succeeds, because Raj spent money in reliance on Sue's assurance that she would sell"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The claim fails, because in a commercial deal Raj knew nothing bound Sue and took the risk she might withdraw): In Yeoman's Row v Cobbe the claimant was an experienced developer who knew the arrangement was not legally binding, and the claim failed because he could not reasonably rely on an assurance in that commercial setting. Spending money at his own risk does not make it unconscionable for Sue to withdraw. An oral contract for the sale of land is not enforceable and part performance is not available. Registered land is not excluded from estoppel, and the claim fails for lack of reasonable reliance."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Lauren is a senior associate at a regional firm. Her colleague Marcus is considered for partnership. One partner tells the committee that Marcus should not be promoted because he is obviously gay and would not suit the firm's conservative clients. Marcus is in fact heterosexual and married. The committee rejects him on that basis, and he brings a claim for sexual orientation discrimination. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Marcus can bring a claim only for harassment, because the remark was an unwanted comment related to sexual orientation and the decision was separate", "B": "Marcus has no claim, because the view was a stereotype held by one partner and not part of a formal firm policy", "C": "Marcus can bring a claim for indirect discrimination, because the committee applied a general requirement about client fit that disadvantages gay men", "D": "Marcus can claim direct discrimination, because treatment based on a perceived protected characteristic is covered even if he lacks it", "E": "Marcus has no claim, because he is not gay and so does not himself possess the protected characteristic of sexual orientation"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Marcus can claim direct discrimination, because treatment based on a perceived protected characteristic is covered even if he lacks it): Section 13 of the Equality Act 2010 covers treating a person less favourably because of a protected characteristic, and this extends to discrimination by perception and by association. The committee rejected Marcus because it believed he was gay, so the reason for the treatment was sexual orientation even though the belief was wrong. The decision of the committee is the firm's act, so it does not matter that the view began with one partner or that no policy existed. Indirect discrimination does not fit because no neutral provision, criterion or practice was applied."}, {"id": 801, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Stellar Tyres Ltd manufactured a tyre with a hidden fault in the internal steel belt, which arose from careless quality control at its factory. It sold the tyre to a garage, which fitted it to Maya's car in its sealed condition and without any means of discovering the flaw. Five weeks later the tyre burst on a motorway and Maya was injured. An expert confirms the flaw originated in manufacture. Maya sues Stellar Tyres Ltd in negligence. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Maya succeeds only if she bought the tyre directly from Stellar, because the duty depends on a contract with the manufacturer", "B": "Maya fails unless she shows that Stellar actually knew of the particular fault before the tyre was sold to the garage", "C": "Maya succeeds, but liability rests with the garage alone, since the garage was the last party to handle the tyre", "D": "Maya fails, because the garage's act of fitting the tyre is a new intervening act that breaks the chain of causation", "E": "Maya is likely to succeed, because the garage could not have found the flaw and Stellar owed her a duty as an ultimate user"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Maya is likely to succeed, because the garage could not have found the flaw and Stellar owed her a duty as an ultimate user): The tyre reached Maya in the state in which it left the factory, with no reasonable opportunity for the garage to detect the hidden flaw, and Stellar's careless quality control caused the defect. Those facts satisfy the manufacturer's duty to ultimate consumers, and the garage's ordinary fitting does not break the chain of causation. Knowledge of the particular flaw is not required, since carelessness in the production process is sufficient. No contract is needed, so a purchase from the manufacturer is unnecessary."}, {"id": 802, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Rashid was injured on 14 March 2024 when a scaffold collapsed at a building site where he was working as a labourer. He knew immediately that he had been injured and that the employer's negligence was the cause. He instructs a solicitor in October 2026 about a personal injury claim in negligence. No other factors affect limitation. Which of the following correctly states when the primary limitation period for his claim expires?", "options": {"A": "On 14 March 2039, because the fifteen year longstop applies to personal injury claims from the date of the accident", "B": "On 31 December 2027, because the three years runs from the end of the calendar year of the accident", "C": "On 14 September 2027, being three years and six months from the accident, to allow time for a pre-action letter of claim", "D": "On 14 March 2030, because six years runs from the date of the negligence for personal injury claims in tort", "E": "On 14 March 2027, being three years from the accident, which was also his date of knowledge of the claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (On 14 March 2027, being three years from the accident, which was also his date of knowledge of the claim): Personal injury claims in negligence are governed by section 11 of the Limitation Act 1980, which gives three years from the date the cause of action accrued or, if later, the date of knowledge. Here Rashid knew of the injury and its cause at once, so both dates are 14 March 2024 and the period ends on 14 March 2027. The six year period is the general tort period and does not apply to personal injury. Nothing in the Act extends the period for pre-action correspondence."}, {"id": 803, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Anil Chaudhry, a director of Pelham Fabrics Ltd, asks a solicitor to explain how funding the company by bank loan differs from funding it by issuing new shares. The company is profitable and pays corporation tax. The board is comparing the two before a meeting next week and wants a short, accurate summary of the legal and tax differences. Which of the following statements correctly states one difference?", "options": {"A": "Interest on a loan is generally deductible as a business expense for corporation tax, whereas dividends on shares are paid out of profits after tax", "B": "A company may borrow only with shareholder approval by special resolution, whereas share capital may be returned to shareholders without any formality", "C": "Lenders may vote at general meetings and share in surplus assets, whereas shareholders are entitled only to repayment of a fixed sum plus interest", "D": "Interest must be paid only from distributable profits, whereas dividends are payable to shareholders even where the company has made losses", "E": "On insolvency, shareholders rank ahead of unsecured lenders, because share capital is treated as a prior debt owed by the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interest on a loan is generally deductible as a business expense for corporation tax, whereas dividends on shares are paid out of profits after tax): Loan interest is a cost of the business that reduces taxable profit, while dividends are a distribution of profit already taxed. Lenders have contractual rights only and no vote or share in surplus assets, which belong to the shareholders. Dividends need distributable profits, but interest is a debt payable when due. Shareholders rank behind creditors on insolvency, and share capital cannot be returned freely because of capital maintenance rules."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Cerys Pugh is a witness in a contract claim listed for trial at the county court hearing centre in Cardiff. She is a first language Welsh speaker but is also fluent in English. Her solicitor is advising her on whether she may give her evidence in Welsh. Which of the following statements correctly states the position under the Welsh Language Act 1993 and the associated Practice Direction?", "options": {"A": "She may do so if every other party agrees to the hearing proceeding partly in Welsh", "B": "She may do so if the case is heard before a judge who speaks Welsh and sits in Wales", "C": "She may give evidence in Welsh in a court sitting in Wales, if she gives notice so that interpretation is arranged", "D": "She may do so if she pays for an interpreter, because the court has no duty to provide translation services", "E": "She may do so if the judge gives permission after she shows that she cannot give evidence adequately in English"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She may give evidence in Welsh in a court sitting in Wales, if she gives notice so that interpretation is arranged): In legal proceedings in Wales, any party, witness or other person who desires to speak Welsh may do so, and the courts treat the Welsh and English languages on a basis of equality. The person should tell the court office in good time so that arrangements, including interpretation, can be made. There is no need to show that English is inadequate, or to obtain the agreement of other parties. The court arranges interpretation and the judge need not speak Welsh."}, {"id": 805, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Tomasz and Agata Kowal are buying a freehold bungalow built in the 1960s, and the sellers cannot find any plans of the drains. Their solicitor wants to know whether the bungalow's foul and surface water drainage connects to the public sewer, and whether a public sewer or water main runs through the garden. Which search should be made, and with whom?", "options": {"A": "A Land Registry index map search, made with HM Land Registry, which shows pipes and sewers affecting the registered title", "B": "An environmental search, made with a commercial provider, which reveals sewer connections and the position of public water mains", "C": "A local land charges search, made with the council, which reveals public sewer connections as financial charges on the land", "D": "A local authority enquiries search, made with the council, which reveals drainage connections and the position of mains in the garden", "E": "A drainage and water search, made with the area's water and sewerage company, which reveals connections and public sewer positions"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A drainage and water search, made with the area's water and sewerage company, which reveals connections and public sewer positions): Drainage and water information is held by the water and sewerage company, and the standard drainage and water search is made with that company. It shows whether foul and surface water drain to the public sewer and where public sewers and mains lie. The council search covers planning, highways and similar matters, and an environmental report deals with contamination and flood risk. The Land Registry index map does not record pipework."}, {"id": 806, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Two lay trustees of a £600,000 trust invest £250,000 in a single overseas property fund recommended to them by a friend. They obtain no advice from an authorised person and do not consider whether advice is needed. The trust instrument is silent about investment. Two years later the fund falls in value by 40 per cent and the beneficiaries sue. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "There is a breach, but the beneficiaries' only remedy is the removal of the trustees and not compensation", "B": "There is a breach only if the trustees' failure to take advice is proved to have caused the fall in value of the fund", "C": "They breached the duty to take and consider proper advice, since they could not reasonably conclude that advice was unnecessary", "D": "There is no breach, because the statutory duty to take advice applies only to investments of more than £1 million", "E": "There is no breach, because lay trustees who invest in good faith are held only to a standard of honesty and sincerity in their dealings"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They breached the duty to take and consider proper advice, since they could not reasonably conclude that advice was unnecessary): Section 5 Trustee Act 2000 requires trustees to obtain and consider proper advice before exercising a power of investment, unless they reasonably conclude that it is unnecessary. A large single investment on a friend's recommendation makes such a conclusion unreasonable. Breach does not depend on honesty or on causation, although compensation does, and the remedies include compensation as well as removal. The duty has no minimum value."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Okoro's will leaves 'the whole of my residue to my wife Carla, but if she dies before me, to my brother Dev'. Mr Okoro and Carla divorced and the final order was made in 2024. They have two adult children, who are not mentioned in the will. Mr Okoro died in 2026 without making a new will. Who takes the residue?", "options": {"A": "Dev and the children equally, because the divorce triggers the intestacy rules for the share that would have gone to Carla", "B": "The children, because the gift to Carla fails and the will does not expressly provide for failure by divorce", "C": "Carla, because the divorce does not affect a gift made while the testator was married to her", "D": "The residue passes under the intestacy rules, because the gift over to Dev applies only where Carla died before the testator in fact", "E": "Dev, because Carla is treated as having died on the date of the final order, so the gift over to Dev takes effect"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Dev, because Carla is treated as having died on the date of the final order, so the gift over to Dev takes effect): On a final order of divorce, the former spouse is treated as having died on the date of the order, so a gift over conditional on her dying before the testator takes effect. The will here expressly gives the residue to Dev in that event. The children do not take, because the will provides for the failure and intestacy does not arise. A contrary intention in the will could change the result, but none appears."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Halden Marine Ltd is the claimant in a contract dispute in the county court. A district judge ordered that unless the claimant serves its list of documents by 4pm on Friday 6 March, its claim will be struck out. The fee earner dealing with the file was on leave, nobody covered the diary entry, and the list was served on Tuesday 10 March. The defendant has written to say the claim stands struck out. The supervising solicitor wants to preserve the claim and must also decide what to tell the court about the default. What is the best course?", "options": {"A": "Write to the court explaining that the list has now been served, since late compliance cures the breach and revives the claim", "B": "Appeal the unless order to a circuit judge, because the short deadline made the sanction unfair on a claimant who was unaware of it", "C": "Issue a fresh claim form on the same facts, because a claim struck out for default can be brought again within limitation", "D": "Apply promptly for relief from sanctions under CPR 3.9, supported by a witness statement frankly explaining the missed diary entry", "E": "Apply to vary the unless order under CPR 3.1(7), because the court can rewrite its own order after a sanction has taken effect"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply promptly for relief from sanctions under CPR 3.9, supported by a witness statement frankly explaining the missed diary entry): An unless order operates automatically, so the claim was struck out when 4pm passed without service. The only proper route is an application for relief from sanctions under CPR 3.9, made promptly and tested under the Denton approach. The solicitor owes a duty to the court and must be frank in the evidence about how the default arose, rather than glossing over the diary failure. Varying the order or serving late without an application does not undo a sanction that has already taken effect."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Penn died. He held a house with his wife as joint tenants, a life policy written in trust for his children, and a savings account of £60,000 in his sole name. The bank will release funds only on production of a grant. His widow wonders whether a grant is needed for any of the assets, and whether his small pension should affect the answer. For which asset is a grant needed?", "options": {"A": "Only the savings account, because the house passes by survivorship and the policy proceeds are paid to the trustees", "B": "The house and the savings account, because any interest in land requires a grant", "C": "None, because the bank has discretion and the estate can be administered without any grant", "D": "The policy and the savings account, because proceeds paid on death pass through the estate", "E": "All three assets, because every asset held by a person at death must be vested by a grant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Only the savings account, because the house passes by survivorship and the policy proceeds are paid to the trustees): A grant is needed for assets in the deceased's sole name that form part of the estate, such as the savings account where the bank requires it. The house passes automatically to the wife by survivorship, and the policy proceeds are payable to the trustees, so neither needs a grant. The pension is a distraction. A bank's discretion here has been removed by its stated policy."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Elmbridge Catering Ltd owed Bright Ltd, an unconnected supplier, £25,000. Nine months before it entered administration, the company paid the debt in full after Bright threatened to stop supplying goods the company needed to trade. The company was insolvent when it paid. The administrator thinks other creditors were treated unfairly and asks whether the payment can be set aside as a preference. Which of the following is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The payment can be challenged, because the relevant time for a preference is two years before the onset of insolvency whoever the recipient is", "B": "The payment cannot be challenged as a preference, because it was made outside the six month relevant time that applies to an unconnected person", "C": "The payment can be challenged, because the relevant time for any preference is one year before onset of insolvency for every recipient", "D": "The payment cannot be challenged, because paying a creditor under commercial pressure is a complete defence to any preference claim", "E": "The payment can be challenged, because the company was insolvent and the supplier's threat to stop supplying proves that the company desired to prefer Bright"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The payment cannot be challenged as a preference, because it was made outside the six month relevant time that applies to an unconnected person): A preference claim must be brought in respect of a payment made within the relevant time, which is six months before the onset of insolvency for an unconnected person and two years for a connected person. Bright is unconnected and was paid nine months before administration, so the claim fails on timing before the court reaches the company's state of mind. Pressure from a supplier would in any event make it harder to show the required desire to prefer, though it is not a formal defence. The insolvency of the company at the time is necessary but not enough."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Ardent Print Ltd is in creditors' voluntary liquidation. Northbank holds a floating charge over stock and debtors created in 2022 and properly registered. A different lender holds a fixed charge over the factory, which is dealt with separately. The stock has been sold for £120,000. Unpaid employees' wages and holiday pay are preferential claims, and trade suppliers are unsecured. Which of the following best describes how the £120,000 is applied?", "options": {"A": "It is applied to the costs of liquidation, then to Northbank, then to preferential creditors, because a registered floating charge ranks ahead of preferential debts", "B": "It is applied to the costs of liquidation, then to preferential creditors, then to a prescribed part for unsecured creditors, and finally to Northbank", "C": "It is paid to Northbank in full, because a floating charge holder takes the proceeds of the charged assets ahead of any other creditor", "D": "It is paid to the lender with the fixed charge over the factory, then to preferential creditors, then to Northbank, as fixed charges rank above floating ones", "E": "It is applied to preferential creditors, then to Northbank, with unsecured creditors sharing only in assets that are not subject to any charge"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is applied to the costs of liquidation, then to preferential creditors, then to a prescribed part for unsecured creditors, and finally to Northbank): Floating charge realisations bear the costs of the liquidation, and then preferential creditors are paid ahead of the charge holder. A prescribed part of the net property is then set aside for unsecured creditors, and Northbank receives what remains. The fixed chargee's priority relates only to the factory, which is not part of this fund. Treating the floating chargee as paid first overlooks the statutory ranking."}, {"id": 812, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Simon Gill died with a will appointing his daughter, Rachel, as executor. Simon and his wife, Pam, had a joint building society account with a balance of £35,000, which Pam still holds. Rachel's solicitor, Mr Dyer, is compiling the estate ledger, and Rachel tells him to include the £35,000 as an estate asset because Pam 'will not need it'. Pam has not been asked and has not agreed. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action for Mr Dyer?", "options": {"A": "Include half of the £35,000 on the ledger as estate money, because a joint account is divided equally between the estate and the survivor", "B": "Ask Pam to pay the £35,000 into the firm's client account at once, because the executor's instruction is binding on her", "C": "Treat the money as passing to Pam by survivorship and not as an estate asset, so it cannot be put on the estate ledger or used", "D": "Hold the £35,000 on the ledger as a contingent estate asset until Pam signs a written release, because survivorship rights cannot be assumed", "E": "Include the £35,000 on the ledger as estate money, because executors must collect every asset in which the deceased had an interest"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Treat the money as passing to Pam by survivorship and not as an estate asset, so it cannot be put on the estate ledger or used): A joint account normally passes to the survivor by survivorship on death, so the balance is Pam's and does not form part of Simon's estate. Mr Dyer cannot properly record it as estate money or ask Pam to pay it in, since that would treat another person's property as the estate's. He should explain this to Rachel, who cannot instruct him to do otherwise. Holding it on the ledger would also misrepresent the position."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Kestle Print agreed to supply labels to Orchard Drinks for 12 months at an agreed price. After three months Kestle wrongly refused to continue. The following week Kestle offered to resume supply on the original terms with one week's delay. Orchard refused out of irritation, accepted Kestle's earlier repudiation as ending the contract and bought labels from another supplier at a higher price. It now claims the extra cost as damages. Which of the following best states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Orchard recovers the full extra cost, because the duty to mitigate arises only once the contract has been affirmed and not once it has been terminated", "B": "Orchard's loss is reduced only if Kestle proves that the offer was made in writing and with a guarantee of timely delivery", "C": "Orchard's recoverable loss is reduced, because it acted unreasonably in declining a straightforward offer to perform the original contract", "D": "Orchard recovers the full extra cost, because an innocent party is not required to deal again with the party who repudiated the contract", "E": "Orchard recovers nothing, because the duty to mitigate requires acceptance of any reasonable offer and it has therefore waived its claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Orchard's recoverable loss is reduced, because it acted unreasonably in declining a straightforward offer to perform the original contract): The duty to mitigate applies after termination as well as when a contract continues. An innocent party can be expected to accept an offer from the wrongdoer to perform on the original terms where doing so is reasonable and not humiliating. Orchard refused a plain offer on the same terms, so it cannot recover loss it could thereby have avoided. The result is a reduction in damages, not forfeiture of the claim, and no written form is needed for the offer."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Marek Zielinski issued a claim in the County Court for £18,000 for defective building work. The claim was allocated to the fast track and tried over one day by a district judge, who gave judgment for the defendant. The trial took place in March and the judgment was handed down in April. Marek wishes to challenge the district judge's decision and believes the judge misapplied the relevant law. Which of the following best describes the appeal route?", "options": {"A": "An appeal lies as of right to a High Court judge, because the High Court supervises all decisions of County Court district judges", "B": "An appeal lies to the Supreme Court, because the case turns on a point of law and Marek has lost at the first hearing", "C": "No appeal lies, because a fast track decision is final and the remedy is to apply to the same judge to set it aside", "D": "An appeal lies directly to the Court of Appeal, because it hears every appeal from a district judge once a final judgment is given", "E": "An appeal lies to a circuit judge in the County Court, and permission to appeal must be obtained before the appeal can proceed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (An appeal lies to a circuit judge in the County Court, and permission to appeal must be obtained before the appeal can proceed): Appeals from a district judge in the County Court go to a circuit judge in the County Court, and permission is needed from either the lower court or the appeal court. The Court of Appeal normally hears only second appeals, and only where it gives permission because an important point of principle or practice arises or there is some other compelling reason. The High Court and Supreme Court are not in the appeal chain for this type of decision. The fast track affects the procedure for the trial, not the right to seek permission to appeal."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Wade, who has a recognised medical condition, severe depression, has been charged with murder after killing his neighbour during an argument about noise. His solicitor intends to run diminished responsibility, and Wade, who has no legal training, asks who must prove what at trial. Which of the following correctly states the burden and standard of proof on diminished responsibility?", "options": {"A": "Wade must prove the defence beyond reasonable doubt, because it reduces murder to manslaughter and so demands a high standard", "B": "Wade need only raise sufficient evidence of the defence, after which the Crown must disprove it on the balance of probabilities", "C": "Wade must prove the elements of diminished responsibility on the balance of probabilities, and if he does so he is convicted of manslaughter", "D": "The judge decides the issue on the medical evidence alone, and the jury is bound to accept any agreed expert opinion", "E": "The Crown must disprove diminished responsibility beyond reasonable doubt once Wade has adduced some evidence of an abnormality of mental functioning"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wade must prove the elements of diminished responsibility on the balance of probabilities, and if he does so he is convicted of manslaughter): Diminished responsibility under section 2 of the Homicide Act 1957 is a partial defence that the defendant must establish. The burden is a legal one on Wade, and the standard is the civil standard, the balance of probabilities. The shifting burden described in the second and third suggestions belongs to loss of control, where the defendant need only adduce sufficient evidence. Expert evidence is important but the jury decides and is not bound to accept it."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Fiona has been a director of Cobble Ltd for three years. Last month she bought 25% of the shares in Ansell Ltd, which has a supply contract with Cobble that was signed two years ago. Cobble's board knows nothing of her new shareholding. The contract has fourteen months left to run and is worth about £80,000 a year. What must Fiona do under the Companies Act 2006?", "options": {"A": "She must notify Companies House within 28 days of buying the shares so that the public record shows her interest", "B": "She need do nothing, because the declaration duty applies only to proposed transactions and this contract is already in force", "C": "She must declare her interest to the members at the next general meeting, because members appoint the board", "D": "She must declare the nature and extent of her interest to the other directors as soon as is reasonably practicable", "E": "She must obtain approval of her interest by ordinary resolution of the members before the contract is allowed to continue"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She must declare the nature and extent of her interest to the other directors as soon as is reasonably practicable): Section 182 requires a director to declare an interest in an existing transaction, as soon as reasonably practicable, to the other directors. Fiona's interest arose after the contract was signed, but the duty still applies because the company is a party to it. Failure to declare is a criminal offence. Member approval is not required for a declaration of interest, and Companies House is not the recipient."}, {"id": 817, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Before announcing a major reform of immigration appeals, the government did not consult the devolved governments, although a convention suggests it ordinarily should on matters affecting them. Alun Thomas, a campaigner, instructs a solicitor to seek a declaration in the High Court that the government has breached the convention and that the reform is therefore unlawful. The government accepts that the convention exists but disputes that the court can rule on it. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The court will grant the declaration, because a convention that the government accepts exists becomes enforceable in the courts", "B": "The court will quash the reform, because failure to consult is a breach of natural justice that renders it void", "C": "The court will rule that the convention has hardened into a rule of law after long practice, so it binds ministers", "D": "The court will refuse to consider the convention, because it cannot decide whether any convention exists or what it requires", "E": "The court may recognise that the convention exists, but it will not enforce it or declare the reform unlawful for breaching it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court may recognise that the convention exists, but it will not enforce it or declare the reform unlawful for breaching it): Conventions are rules of political practice that are not enforced by the courts. A court may recognise that a convention exists, as it may have to do to decide a legal question, but it cannot rule on its operation or scope. The government's failure to consult the devolved governments may be a political matter but it does not render the reform unlawful. Length of practice does not turn a convention into law."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Hart died intestate in 2026, domiciled in England. He was survived by his wife and by two adult children. His net estate, after debts and funeral costs and excluding personal chattels, is £500,000. For this question, assume the statutory legacy for a surviving spouse is £322,000. How is the estate divided under the intestacy rules?", "options": {"A": "The wife takes £322,000 and the children share the remaining £178,000 on the statutory trusts, with nothing further to her", "B": "The wife takes £322,000 plus half of the remaining £178,000, so £411,000, and the children share £89,000", "C": "The wife takes £322,000 and a life interest in half the remainder, and the children take the capital on her death", "D": "The wife and the children each take half of the estate, so the wife receives £250,000 and the children share £250,000", "E": "The wife takes the whole £500,000 outright, because a surviving spouse ranks ahead of children when the estate is modest"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The wife takes £322,000 plus half of the remaining £178,000, so £411,000, and the children share £89,000): Where an intestate leaves a spouse and issue, the spouse takes the personal chattels, the statutory legacy and half of the residue absolutely, and the issue take the other half on the statutory trusts. After the legacy of £322,000, the residue is £178,000, so the wife takes £89,000 more, a total of £411,000, and the children share the other £89,000. The life interest provisions were abolished in 2014."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Karen Dolan is a solicitor acting for Pembroke Tech Ltd in a claim for £400,000 against Nordhal AS, a Norwegian company. She is applying without notice for permission to serve the claim form out of the jurisdiction. In reading documents provided by her client she has found a signed agreement showing that the parties agreed to refer all disputes to arbitration in Oslo. Her client's managing director says that this is irrelevant and that Karen should say nothing about it. What should Karen do?", "options": {"A": "Disclose it if the client consents in writing, because the duty of confidentiality to Pembroke Tech Ltd continues", "B": "Disclose the arbitration agreement to the court, because on a without notice application there is a duty of full and frank disclosure", "C": "Disclose the agreement to Nordhal AS after the order is made, because disclosure to the defendant satisfies the duty owed to the court", "D": "Say nothing about it, because it is for the defendant to raise the arbitration agreement when it challenges jurisdiction", "E": "Make the application on the facts favourable to the client but avoid any false statement, since silence on adverse points is permitted"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Disclose the arbitration agreement to the court, because on a without notice application there is a duty of full and frank disclosure): An applicant who proceeds without notice must give the court full and frank disclosure of all material facts, including those that harm its case, and a solicitor must not mislead the court or allow it to be misled. An arbitration agreement is clearly material to whether England is the appropriate forum. The duty to the court overrides the client's wish for silence, and confidentiality does not permit withholding it. Disclosure to the defendant after the order does not meet a duty that is owed to the court at the time of the application."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Aisha Bello was robbed in an unlit street and saw the robber's face only briefly. Police arrest Ben Crowe, whom she has never met, in the same area an hour later. In interview Ben denies being the robber. Aisha has told the police that she believes she could identify the man who robbed her. Ben is willing to take part in an identification procedure. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "No procedure is needed, because one is held only after a witness has given the police a detailed written description of the robber", "B": "A procedure is needed only if at least two witnesses say that they could identify the robber, since one witness is insufficient", "C": "No procedure is needed until Ben has been charged, because identification evidence cannot be tested before the charge decision is made", "D": "No procedure is needed, because one is held only if the suspect first asks for it, and Ben has merely denied involvement", "E": "A formal procedure must be held, because Ben is available, his identity is disputed and a witness may be able to identify him"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A formal procedure must be held, because Ben is available, his identity is disputed and a witness may be able to identify him): Code D requires an identification procedure where a witness has identified or may be able to identify a suspect, the suspect disputes being the person concerned, and the suspect is available and consents. Ben has never met Aisha, who says she could identify the robber, so the conditions are met. There is no requirement for a prior written description, for the suspect to request a procedure or for a second witness, and the timing is not linked to the charging decision. Holding a fair procedure protects the reliability of any later identification evidence."}, {"id": 821, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "In a claim worth about £70,000, the defendant, Quill Marine Ltd, made a Part 36 offer on 1 June 2026 to pay £40,000 in full settlement of the claim. The offer stated that it was open for acceptance for 21 days. The claimant, Seaton Ltd, did not respond until 20 July 2026, when with trial fixed for November it accepted the offer by written notice. The parties have not agreed costs. What is the usual costs consequence?", "options": {"A": "Seaton must pay all of Quill's costs from the date of the offer, because acceptance after expiry means the claimant lost", "B": "Seaton recovers its costs up to the end of the 21 days but must pay Quill's costs from then until acceptance", "C": "Each side bears its own costs, because the acceptance of a Part 36 offer ends the court's costs jurisdiction", "D": "Seaton recovers all its costs up to the date of acceptance, because a Part 36 offer accepted at any time carries the same costs result", "E": "Quill pays Seaton's costs up to the expiry of 21 days and also its own costs afterwards, because it made the offer"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Seaton recovers its costs up to the end of the 21 days but must pay Quill's costs from then until acceptance): A Part 36 offer accepted within the relevant period entitles the claimant to its costs up to acceptance. Where acceptance comes after the relevant period has expired, the usual order gives the claimant its costs only up to the date the period ended and makes it pay the offeror's costs from that date until acceptance. Quill is the offeror, so Seaton bears Quill's costs for the period after the 21 days. The simple rule that costs follow the claimant fails because the acceptance came late."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Beatrice, an elderly widow, grants her neighbour Gavin a lasting power of attorney over her finances. Gavin, who is meant to pay her care home fees from her savings, instead dishonestly transfers £20,000 to his own account to pay off his debts. Beatrice has not yet noticed. Gavin is charged with fraud under section 4 of the Fraud Act 2006. Which of the following best explains why section 4 is the most apt offence?", "options": {"A": "Gavin appropriated property belonging to another, and section 4 is the offence of dishonestly taking property of another with intent to deprive", "B": "Gavin held a position in which he was expected to safeguard Beatrice's finances, and he dishonestly abused it intending a gain for himself", "C": "Gavin failed to disclose information to Beatrice that he was under a legal duty to disclose, which is the conduct required by section 4", "D": "Gavin acted dishonestly in breach of contract, and section 4 applies to a breach of any contractual duty owed to another person", "E": "Gavin made an express false statement to the bank about the transfer, which section 4 requires as the conduct element of the offence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gavin held a position in which he was expected to safeguard Beatrice's finances, and he dishonestly abused it intending a gain for himself): Section 4 applies where a person occupies a position in which they are expected to safeguard, or not act against, another's financial interests, and dishonestly abuses it intending gain or loss. An attorney under a lasting power of attorney clearly occupies such a position. No false statement or legal duty to disclose is needed, which distinguishes sections 2 and 3, and the offence is not a form of theft. A simple breach of contract does not create the necessary position of trust."}, {"id": 823, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Tomasz Wiśniewski is a solicitor at a high street firm. He has no higher rights of audience. Rachel Okafor, a longstanding client, has been sent for trial in the Crown Court on a charge of fraud by false representation and has asked Tomasz to conduct her trial himself, saying she trusts him and cannot afford a second lawyer. He has done several Crown Court appeals and is confident he could cope. The trial is in five months. What should Tomasz do?", "options": {"A": "Accept the instruction, because his experience of Crown Court appeals shows he is competent to conduct a trial as the advocate", "B": "Accept the instruction, provided Rachel signs a written waiver confirming she understands he has no higher rights of audience", "C": "Appear for Rachel at the plea hearing and the trial, and instruct counsel if the judge objects to his representation", "D": "Explain that he lacks rights of audience for the trial and, with her agreement, instruct an advocate while he continues to run the case", "E": "Decline the case and tell Rachel to find another firm, because a solicitor without higher rights cannot act in any Crown Court case, whatever the stage reached"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain that he lacks rights of audience for the trial and, with her agreement, instruct an advocate while he continues to run the case): Conducting a Crown Court trial as advocate is a reserved activity for which Tomasz needs higher rights, and neither experience nor client consent supplies them. Acting without authorisation would breach his duty to act within the law and to provide a competent service. The proper course is to remain as the solicitor with conduct of the case and, with Rachel's agreement, instruct a barrister or solicitor-advocate to appear. Declining the whole case is unnecessary, because a solicitor can still prepare the case and instruct an advocate."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mrs Lowe's will leaves the whole of her residue to her daughter Priya. Priya died two years before Mrs Lowe, leaving two children, Quin and Rae, who are both alive at Mrs Lowe's death. Mrs Lowe also has a surviving son, Sam, who is not mentioned in the will. Mrs Lowe's will contains no gift over and no statement about the failure of gifts. Who takes the residue?", "options": {"A": "The residue is divided equally between Sam, Quin and Rae, because the gift to Priya lapses to all the testator's issue", "B": "Quin and Rae take equally, because section 33 of the Wills Act 1837 substitutes the issue of a child who predeceases", "C": "Sam takes, because the gift to Priya lapses and the residue passes under the intestacy rules", "D": "Priya's estate takes, because the gift to her vested when the will was made", "E": "Quin and Rae take only if the will expressly names them, because a lapsed gift can be saved only by express words"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Quin and Rae take equally, because section 33 of the Wills Act 1837 substitutes the issue of a child who predeceases): Section 33 of the Wills Act 1837 applies where a testator's child or remoter descendant is given a gift and dies before the testator leaving issue who survive. The gift does not lapse but passes to those issue as if the beneficiary had survived. Priya is Mrs Lowe's child and Quin and Rae are living, so they take Priya's share equally. Sam is not entitled, and no express words are needed unless the testator wishes to exclude the rule."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Marcus Bell is a chartered surveyor who owns a rental house. While selling it to Nina Joshi he said, 'In my professional opinion the roof is sound.' He knew from a recent inspection that it leaked badly and had not told anyone. Nina bought the house for £280,000 and found the leaks two weeks later. She had been offered a survey but declined it. Which of the following best describes whether Marcus made an actionable misrepresentation?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because an opinion from someone who knows the facts and does not hold the opinion implies a statement of fact that is false", "B": "No, because Nina was offered a survey and her failure to take it means she was not induced by the statement", "C": "Yes, but only if Marcus's statement was made in writing, because oral statements about land cannot found a claim in misrepresentation", "D": "Yes, but only as a breach of contract, because a statement made during negotiations becomes a term once the buyer relies on it in deciding to buy", "E": "No, because a statement of opinion about the condition of the property is not a statement of fact"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because an opinion from someone who knows the facts and does not hold the opinion implies a statement of fact that is false): A statement of opinion is not usually a misrepresentation, but it can be where the speaker does not hold the opinion or knows facts that make it unreasonable, because it implies that he knows facts supporting it. Marcus knew the roof leaked, so his opinion carried a false implied statement of fact. Nina's decision not to commission a survey does not defeat the claim, because a representee is not required to check what the representor says. Whether the statement became a term depends on other factors and does not decide the issue."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Gareth, a consumer, paid Luton Plumbing £900 to replace his boiler. The firm fitted the correct model but made a poor connection, which caused a leak that damaged his floor. The contract said nothing about the standard of work to be provided. Gareth intends to sue for the cost of repairing the floor and wants to rely on an implied term. Which of the following is the term that would help him most?", "options": {"A": "The term that the boiler matches its description as agreed, implied for goods by the Consumer Rights Act 2015", "B": "The term that the work will be done to the standard of the best fitter in the plumbing trade, implied at common law", "C": "The term that the firm will compensate any consequential damage, implied by custom of the plumbing trade", "D": "The term that the boiler is fit for its particular purpose, implied by section 14(3) of the Sale of Goods Act 1979", "E": "The term that the service will be performed with reasonable care and skill, implied by the Consumer Rights Act 2015"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The term that the service will be performed with reasonable care and skill, implied by the Consumer Rights Act 2015): The Consumer Rights Act 2015 implies into a consumer contract for services a term that the trader will perform with reasonable care and skill. Luton fitted the right boiler but connected it badly, so the fault concerns the workmanship and not the goods. The standard is reasonable skill and not the best in the trade, and no custom of paying consequential loss exists. Gareth can recover the foreseeable cost of repairing the floor as damages."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Rhys is walking home late when he sees Edna, an elderly woman he has never met, collapse on the pavement outside her house. He could easily call an ambulance but, being in a hurry, walks on. Edna lies there overnight in freezing weather and dies of hypothermia. Rhys has no relationship with her and has not undertaken any care of her. Which of the following best describes his criminal liability for her death?", "options": {"A": "He is liable for gross negligence manslaughter, because a moral duty to assist a person in danger is recognised by the criminal law", "B": "He is liable for manslaughter by unlawful act, because walking away from a person in obvious distress is itself an unlawful omission", "C": "He is liable for gross negligence manslaughter, because he was able to summon help easily and his omission was a substantial cause of her death", "D": "He is not liable for her death, because a stranger owes no legal duty to act and a bare omission does not found liability", "E": "He is not liable, because her death was caused by the cold rather than by anything Rhys did, so causation cannot be established"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He is not liable for her death, because a stranger owes no legal duty to act and a bare omission does not found liability): The general rule is that there is no liability for a pure omission unless the defendant owed a recognised legal duty, such as a family relationship, a voluntary assumption of care, a contract, an official position or the creation of a dangerous situation. A stranger who merely passes by owes none of these, so the gross negligence manslaughter route fails at the duty stage however easy a phone call would have been. The tempting argument that ability to help creates a duty confuses moral and legal obligation. Causation is not the real obstacle here, since the omission would have been a cause had a duty existed."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Claire is a trustee of a will trust that holds 30 per cent of the shares in Linden Ltd. At the company's annual meeting she learns, through the trust's shareholding, that the company is about to win a large contract. She buys shares in Linden Ltd with her own money and later sells them for a profit of £40,000. The trust did not have the funds to buy the shares, and the trust instrument says nothing about trustees making profits. A beneficiary challenges her. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "Claire must account to the trust for the profit made through her position and information as trustee, although the trust suffered no loss", "B": "Claire may keep the profit if she acted honestly, since honesty is a complete defence to a claim based on the duty not to profit from the trust", "C": "Claire must account only for profit on shares bought with trust money, not for gains on shares bought with her own funds", "D": "Claire must pay equitable compensation for the lost opportunity of the trust, but she may keep the profit that she made", "E": "Claire may keep the profit, because the trust had no money to buy the shares and so lost nothing through her purchase"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Claire must account to the trust for the profit made through her position and information as trustee, although the trust suffered no loss): A fiduciary must not profit from a position of trust, and liability to account does not depend on loss to the trust, bad faith, or the use of trust money. Claire obtained her opportunity through the trust's shareholding and the knowledge gained from it, which brings the profit within the rule in Boardman v Phipps. Honesty and the trust's inability to buy do not displace the rule. The remedy is an account of the profit, not a compensation award that leaves her with the gain."}, {"id": 829, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for the buyer of a freehold house. The seller's completed Fittings and Contents Form states that the integrated kitchen appliances, the fitted wardrobes and the curtains are included in the sale. The draft contract, on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition), makes no mention of these items. Your client is relying on them being included and exchange is next week. What is the most appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Rely on the form, because every statement in a pre-contract form automatically becomes a term of the contract when the parties exchange", "B": "Exchange and then ask the seller's solicitor to confirm the list in a letter, because a letter after exchange is binding", "C": "Rely on the form and take no action, since a false statement in it gives the buyer a right to rescind after completion", "D": "Ask for the list of included items to be written into the contract, because statements in the form do not become contractual terms unless incorporated", "E": "Ask the seller to hand over the items on completion day, because fixtures pass automatically with the land without any need for a contract term"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask for the list of included items to be written into the contract, because statements in the form do not become contractual terms unless incorporated): Replies and forms are statements made before the contract, and they become binding terms only if the contract incorporates them, so the safe course is to have the items listed in the contract before exchange. A letter after exchange does not alter the contract already made. Fitted wardrobes may pass as fixtures, but curtains and appliances are matters for express agreement, which is why the list should be in the contract."}, {"id": 830, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "In March 2025 Tanner Developments Ltd sued Crossley Roofing Ltd for breach of contract over a defective flat roof installed in April 2019, pleading breach of the workmanship term. The claim form was issued in time. It is now September 2026, the six year period has expired, and the claimant's solicitor realises that the same facts would support a claim in negligence against Crossley. The solicitor asks whether the particulars can be amended to add it. What is the position?", "options": {"A": "The court may give permission, because the new claim arises out of substantially the same facts as the claim already pleaded", "B": "The court cannot give permission, because a new cause of action cannot be added once limitation has expired, even where it arises from the same facts", "C": "The court may give permission only if the claimant shows that it was mistaken about the identity of the party it sued", "D": "The court may give permission only if the defendant consents, because limitation is a right it cannot lose without agreement", "E": "The court must give permission, because amendments are allowed as of right before the first case management conference"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court may give permission, because the new claim arises out of substantially the same facts as the claim already pleaded): After limitation has expired the court may still allow an amendment adding a new claim, but only if the new claim arises out of the same or substantially the same facts as a claim already in the proceedings. Here the negligence claim rests on the same roof installation, so permission is possible. It is a matter of discretion, not of right, and the defendant's consent is not the test. The mistaken identity rule concerns changing parties, which is not what the claimant wants."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Blythe, a welder, suffers a small burn on his lip when molten metal splashes onto him through the negligence of his employer's safety officer. Unknown to anyone, the lip tissue contained a precancerous condition, and the burn triggers malignant growth. Mr Blythe develops cancer and dies three years later. His widow sues the employer. The employer accepts that a minor burn was foreseeable but argues that cancer was not. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The employer is not liable for either, because a minor burn of this kind would not foreseeably cause any lasting harm", "B": "The employer is liable for the cancer, but damages are reduced because the claimant's predisposition was a contributory fault", "C": "The employer is liable only if it knew of the precancerous condition before the burn occurred, since the rule needs actual knowledge of the weakness", "D": "The employer is liable only for the burn, because the cancer was a type of damage that was not reasonably foreseeable", "E": "The employer is liable for the cancer, because it takes its victim as it finds him once a burn of that type was foreseeable"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The employer is liable for the cancer, because it takes its victim as it finds him once a burn of that type was foreseeable): Where the type of harm, here a burn to the lip, was foreseeable, the defendant is liable for its full extent even if the claimant's pre-existing vulnerability made it far worse. This is the egg-shell skull rule, under which the defendant takes the victim as found. Knowledge of the weakness is not needed, and the weakness is not contributory fault. The argument that the cancer was unforeseeable confuses the type of damage with its extent."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Anita Bhatt exchanged contracts on 3 March to buy a freehold house for £310,000 on the Standard Conditions of Sale, with completion fixed for 31 March. On 12 March a fire damaged the kitchen. The seller's insurers say the policy covers the loss, and Anita has not arranged buildings insurance because the house is not hers until completion. Which of the following best describes Anita's position?", "options": {"A": "She may rescind and recover her deposit, because the contract is frustrated once the property is substantially damaged before completion", "B": "She must complete, but the repair cost is shared equally, so the price is reduced by half of the cost of repair", "C": "She can rescind within ten working days of learning of the damage, because the conditions give a buyer a right to withdraw after a material loss", "D": "She can require the seller to reinstate the kitchen or compensate her before she completes, because the conditions keep the risk with the seller until completion", "E": "She must still complete at the full price, because risk passed to her on exchange, and she should have insured the house from that date"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She can require the seller to reinstate the kitchen or compensate her before she completes, because the conditions keep the risk with the seller until completion): Under the Standard Conditions of Sale the seller must transfer the property in the same physical state as at the date of the contract, so the risk of damage stays with the seller until completion, whatever the common law position between exchange and completion. Anita is therefore not obliged to complete as if nothing had happened and can insist on reinstatement or compensation. Frustration and rescission do not arise for kitchen fire damage that leaves the house usable, and the fixed ten working day withdrawal right does not exist. Her not having insured is not a breach on her part because the seller's insurance and obligation remain in place."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Lund, aged 88 and partially sighted, signed a new will leaving his estate to his neighbour Ms Voss. Ms Voss found a draft online, typed it herself, took him to sign it and chose the two witnesses. The will is formally valid and has an attestation clause. Mr Lund's nephew alleges that Mr Lund never knew and approved the contents, and Ms Voss relies on the attestation clause. Who bears the burden of proving knowledge and approval?", "options": {"A": "The nephew must prove want of knowledge and approval, because a duly executed will carries a presumption of it that cannot be displaced", "B": "Ms Voss need prove only due execution, because the burden passes to a challenger once two witnesses have signed", "C": "No one bears a burden, because the attestation clause is conclusive proof that Mr Lund read and understood the will", "D": "Ms Voss must prove knowledge and approval, because her part in preparing the will and benefiting under it raises suspicion", "E": "The nephew must prove fraud to a high standard, because want of knowledge and approval can only be raised as part of a fraud claim"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ms Voss must prove knowledge and approval, because her part in preparing the will and benefiting under it raises suspicion): Normally due execution raises a presumption that the testator knew and approved the contents. Where a beneficiary prepares the will or takes a major part in procuring it, the circumstances excite the suspicion of the court and the propounder must affirmatively prove knowledge and approval. Mr Lund's age and poor sight add weight to that suspicion. An attestation clause is useful evidence but is not conclusive, and the nephew does not have to allege fraud."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Barry is charged with criminal damage after a window was smashed at an ex-partner's house. He admits to his solicitor that he threw the brick but, before the trial, tells her that he wants to give evidence that he was at his mother's home all evening. His mother is willing to support this. The solicitor thinks the prosecution case is weak. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action for the solicitor?", "options": {"A": "Advise Barry that she cannot call him or his mother to give evidence of the alibi, though she may still test the prosecution case", "B": "Call Barry's mother only, because her evidence is her own and the solicitor does not know her account is false", "C": "Inform the court of Barry's admission, because the duty not to mislead the court overrides the duty of confidentiality to him", "D": "Continue to act and call Barry but not refer to the alibi in questions, because the solicitor then avoids asserting it herself", "E": "Call both witnesses as instructed, because the solicitor is the client's agent and the client decides which evidence is called"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise Barry that she cannot call him or his mother to give evidence of the alibi, though she may still test the prosecution case): A solicitor must not mislead the court and must not put forward or call evidence known to be false. Barry has admitted the act, so the alibi is known to be untrue, and the mother's supporting account is part of the same falsehood. The solicitor need not tell the court of the admission and may still put the prosecution to proof. If Barry insists on giving false evidence after proper advice, the solicitor would have to stop acting."}, {"id": 835, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Anjali is a trustee of a will trust that owns a house. She decides to buy the house from the trust. The house is advertised openly, an independent valuer gives a figure of £300,000, and Anjali pays exactly that price. The trust instrument does not authorise trustees to purchase trust property. One of the beneficiaries objects to the sale. Which of the following best states the legal effect of the sale?", "options": {"A": "The sale stands, because the independent valuation and open advertising show that the price Anjali paid was fair to the beneficiaries", "B": "The sale is voidable by any beneficiary, however fair the price, because a trustee cannot buy trust property from herself", "C": "The sale is voidable only if the objecting beneficiary can prove that the price was below the market value of the house", "D": "The sale stands if Anjali shows that her co-trustee agreed to it in writing at the time of the transaction", "E": "The sale is void from the outset, so no beneficiary need take any step in order to set it aside"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The sale is voidable by any beneficiary, however fair the price, because a trustee cannot buy trust property from herself): The self-dealing rule makes a purchase of trust property by a trustee voidable by any beneficiary, however fair the price and however open the sale. The rule is not concerned with whether the trustee took advantage, but with preventing the conflict between duty and interest. The sale is voidable, not void, so a beneficiary must act to set it aside. The agreement of a co-trustee cannot authorise it."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trustee wrongly pays £30,000 of trust money into the bank account of her friend Lena, who does not know where the money came from and gives nothing for it. Lena's account already held £70,000 of her own money, so the balance became £100,000. Lena then spends £50,000 on a holiday, leaving £50,000. The beneficiaries want to trace into the account. Which of the following correctly states their claim?", "options": {"A": "They can claim £35,000, being 70 per cent of the remainder, because Lena's own contribution is given priority", "B": "They can claim the whole remaining £50,000, because trust money in a mixed fund is treated as spent last by an innocent recipient", "C": "They can claim £30,000 as a charge on Lena's account, since an innocent volunteer holds the full amount on constructive trust", "D": "They can claim a proportionate share of the remaining £50,000, which is 30 per cent, because Lena's own money is not presumed spent first", "E": "They can claim nothing, because the £50,000 spent is treated as drawn from trust money before Lena's own money"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They can claim a proportionate share of the remaining £50,000, which is 30 per cent, because Lena's own money is not presumed spent first): Where an innocent volunteer mixes trust money with her own, equity does not presume that either fund was spent first, so the loss and the remaining balance are shared rolled in proportion to the contributions. The trust contributed 30 per cent, so it takes 30 per cent of the remaining £50,000. The rule that a trustee spends her own money first applies only to wrongdoing trustees, and not to an innocent volunteer."}, {"id": 837, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Declan stabs Owen once in the abdomen during a fight, puncturing his bowel. Owen is taken to hospital and operated on. He remains seriously ill and dies two days later of peritonitis arising from the original wound. A defence expert says a different surgical technique might have saved him, though the treatment given was within the range of acceptable practice. Declan is charged with murder. Which of the following best describes the causation position?", "options": {"A": "Causation is established only if the stab wound was the sole cause of death, which the expert evidence shows it was not", "B": "Declan's act remains an operating and substantial cause of death, because the treatment was acceptable and did not break the chain of causation", "C": "Declan is not the legal cause, because death occurred two days after the stabbing and the delay shows the wound was not immediately fatal", "D": "The chain of causation is broken, because the intervention of doctors is a new act that relieves Declan of responsibility for what followed", "E": "The chain of causation is broken, because a better surgical technique existed and Owen's death was therefore caused by the choice of treatment rather than by the stabbing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Declan's act remains an operating and substantial cause of death, because the treatment was acceptable and did not break the chain of causation): The defendant's act need only be a more than minimal cause and an operating one at the time of death. Medical treatment breaks the chain only where it is so independent of the defendant's act and so potent that it makes the original wound merely part of the history, which usually needs grossly negligent treatment. Here the wound was still the operating cause of the peritonitis and the treatment was within acceptable practice. A delay of two days does not matter, since the wound was still operating throughout."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Greta runs a gallery under a ten year lease with a tenant's break clause exercisable on the fifth anniversary by not less than six months' written notice. The clause also requires the tenant to give vacant possession and pay all rent due at the break date. Greta served notice on the landlord five months and three weeks before the break date. She has paid all rent, and the landlord's agent recently inspected and noted that the unit is ready for handover. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The break clause may be saved by the court, because relief from forfeiture extends to a late break notice served by a tenant", "B": "The break clause was not validly exercised, because the notice was short and break conditions must be strictly complied with", "C": "The break clause was validly exercised, because rent was paid and possession can be given, which satisfies the other conditions", "D": "The break clause was validly exercised, because the landlord's silence and the agent's inspection waived the notice period", "E": "The break clause was validly exercised, because a notice a few days short is treated as substantial compliance with the clause"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The break clause was not validly exercised, because the notice was short and break conditions must be strictly complied with): Conditions attached to a break clause are construed strictly, and a notice that is short of the period required is ineffective. The landlord's inspection and silence do not amount to waiver of the notice requirement. Relief from forfeiture does not apply to a failed break notice because the lease is not being forfeited. Greta remains bound to the lease and should check future break dates."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Nadia, a carer, endured years of abuse from her partner Colm. During one violent argument, in which he threatened to kill her, she lost self-control and stabbed him with a kitchen knife. Colm survived after emergency surgery. The Crown charges Nadia with attempted murder, accepting that she intended to kill. Nadia's solicitor considers advising her to rely on loss of control. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "Loss of control can be raised only if the Crown agrees to substitute a charge of wounding with intent, which is a lesser alternative", "B": "Loss of control is unavailable because Colm survived, but diminished responsibility would apply to attempted murder as a partial defence", "C": "Loss of control is available, because the attack was provoked by a threat of serious violence and the jury must then acquit", "D": "Loss of control can reduce attempted murder to attempted manslaughter, because the defence applies to every offence requiring an intention to kill", "E": "Loss of control is a partial defence to murder only and cannot reduce attempted murder, although the circumstances remain relevant to sentence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Loss of control is a partial defence to murder only and cannot reduce attempted murder, although the circumstances remain relevant to sentence): The partial defences of loss of control and diminished responsibility are statutory and apply only to a charge of murder, reducing it to manslaughter. Nadia is charged with attempted murder, so neither partial defence is available. The abuse and threats she suffered are still highly relevant mitigation when the court passes sentence. A successful partial defence never leads to a complete acquittal."}, {"id": 840, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Nadia was made bankrupt on a creditor's petition on 3 February 2026. Her unsecured debts total £48,000 and the trustee in bankruptcy is collecting in her assets. She has complied with all her duties to the trustee and the court has made no order suspending her discharge. Her solicitor is asked when the bankruptcy will end in the ordinary case. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "She is discharged two years after the order, but only if she applies to the court and shows she has cooperated with the trustee", "B": "She is discharged when creditors have been paid in full, because the bankruptcy ends only once every proved debt has been satisfied by the trustee", "C": "She is discharged twelve months after the first meeting of creditors, because the period runs from that meeting and not from the order", "D": "She is discharged three years after the date of the order, because the bankruptcy continues until the trustee has realised all of her property", "E": "She is automatically discharged one year after the date of the bankruptcy order, although some restrictions and income obligations may continue"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (She is automatically discharged one year after the date of the bankruptcy order, although some restrictions and income obligations may continue): Under section 279 Insolvency Act 1986 a bankrupt is automatically discharged one year after the date of the bankruptcy order, unless the court suspends discharge, for example for non cooperation. Discharge releases her from most bankruptcy debts but does not stop the trustee dealing with assets already vested, or an income payments agreement or order, from continuing. The period runs from the order, not from a creditors' meeting, and nothing requires creditors to be paid in full first."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Joy Anders buys a house with a long rear garden overlooking a valley. The seller, Percy Arkwright, assures her that the neighbouring owner, Cora Lennox, signed a deed granting the house the right to an uninterrupted view across Cora's field. Joy completes. A year later Cora plans to build a barn that will block the view. Joy asks her solicitor whether the right to a view could have been an easement. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "The right to a view is an easement after twenty years of uninterrupted enjoyment have given the house prescriptive rights", "B": "The right to a view is an easement because it accommodates the dominant house and the two plots have different owners", "C": "A right to a view cannot be an easement, so the deed does not stop the barn, though an express covenant against building might do so", "D": "The right to a view is an easement if it was granted by deed, because the parties' intention decides what can be an easement", "E": "The right to a view is a right to light by another name, so it is an easement and protects Joy from the barn"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A right to a view cannot be an easement, so the deed does not stop the barn, though an express covenant against building might do so): The law does not recognise a right to a view as an easement because it is too vague and would unreasonably restrict development of the neighbouring land. A deed that purports to grant it therefore creates no easement. Rights to light, by contrast, can be easements, but that is a different right with a different test. Joy could look for an express restrictive covenant against building, which is capable of protecting a view, but the deed in question does not give her one."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Zoe, Yusuf and Xavier are trustees of a family trust. Zoe, who alone deals with the trust's day to day affairs, misapplies £90,000 of trust money. Yusuf and Xavier have never looked at the accounts and have left everything to Zoe. Zoe has no assets and cannot repay anything. The beneficiaries claim against all three trustees. Which of the following best states the position of Yusuf and Xavier?", "options": {"A": "Each is liable only for the amount that Zoe's estate cannot pay, after the beneficiaries have exhausted every claim against her personally", "B": "Each can be pursued for the whole £90,000, because trustees are jointly and severally liable and cannot leave administration to one", "C": "Each is liable only for one third of the £90,000, because liability for a breach is divided equally among all the trustees", "D": "Neither is liable, because only the trustee who actually committed the breach can be made liable for the loss", "E": "Each is liable only if he personally received some part of the £90,000 that Zoe misapplied"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Each can be pursued for the whole £90,000, because trustees are jointly and severally liable and cannot leave administration to one): Trustees act together and are jointly and severally liable for a breach of trust, so a passive trustee who stood by is as liable as the active one. The beneficiaries may recover the whole sum from any of them, and the paying trustees can then seek contribution. Receipt is not needed, and there is no requirement to sue Zoe first. Leaving the administration to one trustee is itself a failure of duty."}, {"id": 843, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Fairbanks Farm sold 200 acres to Pennick for use as a sheep farm. During negotiations the seller's agent said, 'I believe the land would carry about 2,000 sheep.' Neither the agent nor the seller had farmed sheep, and Pennick, an experienced sheep farmer, inspected the land himself. The land can carry only 800. The written contract says nothing about capacity. Which of the following is the best analysis of the agent's statement?", "options": {"A": "It is a representation of fact, because any statement about what land can carry is a statement of existing fact", "B": "It is an honest opinion given by someone with no special knowledge, so it is neither a term nor a statement of fact", "C": "It is a term, because it was made in negotiations by the seller's agent and concerned a matter central to the price paid", "D": "It is a term of the contract, because a seller who gives an opinion on the use of land takes responsibility for the result", "E": "It is a collateral warranty enforceable in its own right, because Pennick bought the land in reliance on what the agent said"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is an honest opinion given by someone with no special knowledge, so it is neither a term nor a statement of fact): A statement of genuine opinion by a person who has no special knowledge is not a statement of fact and is not intended as a promise. The agent said 'I believe' and had never farmed sheep, while Pennick was an expert who inspected the land himself. That makes a term or collateral warranty unlikely, and the statement cannot ground a misrepresentation claim without more."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ms Cole died intestate and unmarried. She had one biological child, Jon, aged 30. Her stepson Rhys, whom she raised from the age of four, was never adopted by her. Ms Cole's parents are alive. Rhys says that after twenty years in her household he should share the estate with Jon. Who is entitled to Ms Cole's estate?", "options": {"A": "Jon takes half and Ms Cole's parents take half, because parents rank alongside issue where there is a stepchild", "B": "Jon takes the estate, but Rhys can claim half of it by right as a person maintained by the deceased", "C": "Ms Cole's parents take the whole estate, because Jon and Rhys cannot both be recognised as her children", "D": "Jon and Rhys share equally, because a child treated as a child of the family is issue", "E": "Jon takes the whole estate, because issue rank before parents and a stepchild who was not adopted is not issue"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Jon takes the whole estate, because issue rank before parents and a stepchild who was not adopted is not issue): Issue take before parents under the intestacy rules, so Jon takes the whole estate. A stepchild who has not been adopted does not count as the deceased's issue, however long he lived in the family. Rhys might have a claim for reasonable financial provision under the 1975 Act, since a child of the family can apply, but that depends on the court's discretion and is not a right to a share. The parents take nothing while Jon survives."}, {"id": 845, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Your client Ross, a tenant, wants to assign his shop lease to Sue for a premium. He asks your firm to act for Sue too, offering a reduced fee. Sue has not instructed anyone and is negotiating the amount of the premium and which fixtures are included. You are aware of problems with the landlord's consent that Ross has not told Sue. Which of the following is the correct response?", "options": {"A": "Act for Sue only, because she has not yet instructed another firm and Ross can find fresh advisers", "B": "Act for both and disclose the landlord's consent problems to Sue, because that removes the risk of conflict", "C": "Act for both, because Ross and Sue both want the assignment to complete and so have a substantially common interest in the transaction", "D": "Decline to act for Sue, because the parties are negotiating against each other and the conflict could not be managed by consent", "E": "Act for both if each signs a written consent, and keep the information about the landlord's consent confidential from Sue"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to act for Sue, because the parties are negotiating against each other and the conflict could not be managed by consent): Two clients negotiating the price and terms of the same transaction have conflicting interests, and the exception for a substantially common interest does not fit a negotiation between buyer and seller. Written consent cannot solve the problem where you would hold information material to one client that you could not share. Disclosure without Ross's consent would breach confidentiality. Dropping Ross to act for Sue would breach duties owed to a current client, so the firm should act for Ross only."}, {"id": 846, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Hollis Joinery LLP has supplied bespoke kitchens to Tamsin Retail Ltd for eight years. A disagreement over defective worktops worth £35,000 has arisen. Both directors say they want to keep trading together, want to keep the matter out of the public record and want to decide the outcome themselves rather than have anyone impose it. The contract contains no dispute resolution clause. Hollis's solicitor is considering the methods available. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Propose mediation, because a neutral facilitator helps the parties reach their own private settlement and nothing binds either side unless they agree", "B": "Ask a respected industry figure to act as expert determiner, because the expert's decision on quality will bind both parties and is reached privately", "C": "Issue proceedings in the county court, because a judge will decide the dispute quickly and the parties can still settle later on privileged terms if they wish", "D": "Begin court proceedings and apply for a stay, because the court can then appoint a mediator whose decision the parties must follow", "E": "Agree to arbitration, because the tribunal's award is private and binding, and the arbitrator will be chosen by the parties to preserve goodwill"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Propose mediation, because a neutral facilitator helps the parties reach their own private settlement and nothing binds either side unless they agree): The directors want privacy, a continuing relationship and control of the result, and only mediation leaves the outcome entirely in their hands because a settlement binds them only if they sign it. Arbitration is private but the arbitrator imposes a binding award, so it fails the control requirement even though goodwill may be better preserved than in court. Litigation is public as a rule and adversarial. An expert determination also produces an imposed decision, and a mediator never decides the dispute."}, {"id": 847, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya completed the purchase of a house in Leeds on Wednesday 3 June. The purchase price was £450,000 and stamp duty land tax is payable. Her solicitor is responsible for the SDLT return and holds the funds for the tax in the client account. The buyer is not a first-time buyer and no relief applies. By when must the return be filed and the tax paid to avoid a late filing penalty?", "options": {"A": "Before completion on 3 June, because the tax is a condition of the buyer's mortgage and of registration of the title", "B": "Within thirty days of completion, which means by Friday 3 July, because that period runs from the effective date of the transaction", "C": "Within thirty business days of completion, because the period matches the priority period given by an official search", "D": "Within seven days of completion, which means by Wednesday 10 June, because the return must accompany the application to register", "E": "Within fourteen days of completion, which means by Wednesday 17 June, because the period runs from the effective date"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Within fourteen days of completion, which means by Wednesday 17 June, because the period runs from the effective date): The SDLT return must be filed and the tax paid within fourteen days after the effective date, which is normally completion. Completion on Wednesday 3 June gives a deadline of Wednesday 17 June. A thirty day period is wrong and so is a deadline linked to the priority search, which concerns registration. The tax is paid after completion by the solicitor from funds the buyer has provided."}, {"id": 848, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Sutton Fabrics Ltd obtained a judgment for £30,000 against Mr Brennan, a private individual who owns his house, subject to an existing mortgage to a high street lender. Sutton obtained an interim charging order over the house and later a final charging order, and has registered it at the Land Registry. The company's director assumes the order lets Sutton instruct an estate agent. Which of the following best describes the effect of the final charging order?", "options": {"A": "It secures the debt against the property, but Sutton must apply separately for an order for sale, which the court decides", "B": "It allows Sutton to instruct estate agents to sell after 28 days' notice to the debtor, without any further court order", "C": "It lapses unless Sutton enforces by sale within six months, because charging orders are time limited", "D": "It ranks ahead of the existing mortgage lender, because a charge created by court order outranks a private security", "E": "It gives Sutton immediate possession and the right to collect rent, because a charging order operates as a transfer of title"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It secures the debt against the property, but Sutton must apply separately for an order for sale, which the court decides): A charging order imposes a charge over the debtor's interest, which secures the judgment debt but does not itself authorise a sale. The creditor must apply to the court for an order for sale, and the court decides whether sale is appropriate, taking into account the debtor's circumstances and the other interests in the property. The charge ranks behind the earlier mortgage and gives no right to possession, rent or title."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A ship's engineer negligently allows a large quantity of furnace oil to spill into a harbour. The oil spreads to a wharf where welding is being carried out and, after several days, floating cotton waste soaked in oil ignites from molten metal and the wharf burns down. At the time, expert opinion was that furnace oil on water would not ignite in these conditions, although the oil would foreseeably foul the wharf's slipways. The wharf owner sues for the fire damage. Which of the following best describes the result?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails, because the welding was an intervening act of the wharf owner that broke the chain of causation", "B": "The claim succeeds, because the fire was a direct physical consequence of the spill however unforeseeable the damage", "C": "The claim succeeds, because some damage to the wharf from the oil was foreseeable and so all resulting damage is recoverable", "D": "The claim fails, because fire damage was not damage of a type that was reasonably foreseeable from the oil spill", "E": "The claim fails, because remoteness is tested by the extent of the damage, and the fire was far greater than expected"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The claim fails, because fire damage was not damage of a type that was reasonably foreseeable from the oil spill): Damage is recoverable only if it is of a kind that was reasonably foreseeable when the breach occurred. Fouling of the slipways was foreseeable, but fire from oil on water was not, so the fire damage is too remote. Directness of the physical sequence is no longer the test. The extent of damage need not be foreseeable, so the size of the fire is not the point, and the welding was not an independent act that broke the chain."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Lakeland Fabrics Ltd agreed to deliver goods in four monthly instalments to Quarry Retail Ltd, with payment on each delivery. After the second delivery, with the last two deliveries and payment for them outstanding, both companies' managers agreed by email to end the contract and release each other from future obligations. Nothing was signed as a deed. After a price rise on the market, Lakeland claims that the emails were ineffective and demands that Quarry accept the remaining goods. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The emails are ineffective, because Quarry gave no fresh consideration for being released from its duty to accept the goods", "B": "The emails are effective only as a variation, so Lakeland must still deliver but may claim damages for early termination", "C": "The contract was validly discharged by agreement, because each party's release of the other's outstanding obligations was good consideration", "D": "The emails are effective, but only once the parties have returned any goods delivered, because discharge requires restitution first", "E": "The emails are ineffective, because discharge of a contract requires a deed unless one party has already fully performed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The contract was validly discharged by agreement, because each party's release of the other's outstanding obligations was good consideration): Where each party still has obligations to perform, an agreement to end the contract is supported by consideration because each gives up its right to the other's performance. The emails therefore operate as a binding discharge by agreement, without any deed. Lakeland cannot withdraw merely because the market moved in its favour. The idea that no consideration passed applies only where one side has already performed in full."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Yusuf is buying a flat from his uncle for £280,000 with a mortgage advance of £224,000. His solicitor acts for Yusuf and the lender. Two days before completion, Yusuf mentions that his uncle has agreed to give him £30,000 in cash after completion, which will not appear in the contract. The lender's handbook requires the solicitor to report any discount, incentive or cashback. Yusuf asks the solicitor to say nothing because the loan might be refused. What must the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the lender only that the transaction is not at arm's length, because that is sufficient disclosure of the arrangement", "B": "Complete but report the cashback to the lender after completion, because disclosure matters only once the funds have been advanced", "C": "Tell Yusuf that the lender must be informed, and if he refuses to allow disclosure, cease acting for him and the lender", "D": "Disclose to the lender and continue to act for Yusuf, because the duty of confidentiality to Yusuf is overridden by the lender's instructions", "E": "Complete and say nothing, because the arrangement is private between buyer and seller and arises only after completion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell Yusuf that the lender must be informed, and if he refuses to allow disclosure, cease acting for him and the lender): In a joint retainer the solicitor must pass relevant information to the lender, and a cashback is exactly what the lender's handbook asks to be reported. Yusuf must be told this and given the chance to agree to disclosure. If he refuses, a conflict arises between the two clients and the solicitor cannot continue for the lender, and cannot properly continue for Yusuf either. Staying silent or disclosing late would mislead the lender in breach of the duty to act with integrity."}, {"id": 852, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Imogen bought a second-hand sofa from Yarrow Furniture after the salesman said, 'This is a genuine 1960s design, from a Scandinavian maker.' She had no specialist knowledge and relied on the statement. She later found that it was a 1990s copy. The salesman was an expert on mid-century furniture, and age was important to Imogen's decision to buy. Which of the following best describes the legal status of the statement?", "options": {"A": "It is likely to be a term, because the seller had greater expertise and Imogen relied on the statement in deciding to buy", "B": "It is a mere representation, because statements made during negotiations before the contract was signed cannot form a term", "C": "It is a term, but only if the statement was recorded in the final sales document signed by both parties", "D": "It is a term, because any pre-contract statement that is material to the buyer's decision becomes a term automatically", "E": "It is a mere representation, because a statement about the age of an item is an opinion and not a term"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is likely to be a term, because the seller had greater expertise and Imogen relied on the statement in deciding to buy): Whether a statement is a term turns on the parties' intention judged objectively, and relevant factors include the importance of the statement, the relative expertise of the speaker, and reliance. The salesman was an expert, Imogen was not, and she relied on the statement. These factors point towards a term. Oral statements can form terms and materiality alone is not decisive."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Nina Walsh, aged 38, married Oliver six years before he died. She works full time and earns a good salary. Oliver left his estate of £400,000 to his two children from his first marriage and nothing to Nina. Nina brings a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 as a surviving spouse. The children argue that the marriage was short. Which of the following best describes how the court will take the length of the marriage into account?", "options": {"A": "It will limit any award to what she would have received on divorce, because the spouse standard is tied to divorce principles as a maximum", "B": "It will consider what Nina might have received on divorce as one factor, but that figure is neither a ceiling nor a floor on provision", "C": "It will award her the equal share she would have had on divorce, because the spouse standard applies the sharing principle as a fixed rule", "D": "It will refuse the claim, because a marriage of under ten years cannot support a claim for more than maintenance", "E": "It will ignore the divorce comparison, because the Act applies a maintenance test and the comparison belongs only to matrimonial proceedings"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It will consider what Nina might have received on divorce as one factor, but that figure is neither a ceiling nor a floor on provision): For a spouse the court must have regard to the provision she might have received on divorce, but it is one factor among those listed and does not set a maximum or a minimum. Nina's age, earning capacity and the short length of the marriage will all be weighed. The Act does not impose a minimum duration for a spouse's claim, and the comparison is not ignored."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A contractor's employee negligently fractures a water main, and the escaping water undermines the foundations of Mrs Larkin's house, which stands empty for several months while she is working abroad. The cost of repair is £30,000. While the house is empty and open to the street, squatters enter and cause £12,000 of damage to the interior. Mrs Larkin sues the contractor's employer for both amounts. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The employer is liable for both amounts, because the house was left in a damaged state and so was foreseeably vulnerable to entry", "B": "The employer is liable for both amounts, but may recover the squatters' share from them by seeking contribution as a joint tortfeasor", "C": "The employer is liable for the foundation damage but not the squatters' damage, as their deliberate acts broke the chain of causation", "D": "The employer is liable for both amounts, because the type of damage, physical harm to the house, was reasonably foreseeable", "E": "The employer is not liable for either amount, because the squatters' later conduct ends its responsibility for the whole loss"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The employer is liable for the foundation damage but not the squatters' damage, as their deliberate acts broke the chain of causation): The deliberate wrongful act of a third party normally breaks the chain of causation unless the defendant should have anticipated it as a likely consequence of the breach. The water main damage did not make entry by squatters likely in the way required, so the interior damage is attributed to them. The foundation damage came directly from the negligence and remains recoverable. Foreseeability of physical damage to the house does not extend responsibility to the squatters' own deliberate acts."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Ines, a solicitor, drafts a will for a longstanding client. The will names Ines as a trustee and includes a clause excluding the statutory duty of care under the Trustee Act 2000 for all trustees. Ines does not discuss the clause with her client, who signs the will at the end of the meeting. Ines inserted the clause because she is concerned about her own exposure. Which of the following best describes Ines's position?", "options": {"A": "The clause is valid and the conflict is cured if the client signs a short written waiver without receiving any advice about it", "B": "Ines may act as trustee provided she tells her co-trustees about the clause once the trust has begun to operate", "C": "No conflict arises, because a clause excluding liability is standard in will trusts and the client signed it voluntarily", "D": "The clause is void, because the statutory duty of care cannot be excluded by anything in the trust instrument", "E": "The clause is permissible, but Ines has an own-interest conflict and must explain its effect and recommend independent advice"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The clause is permissible, but Ines has an own-interest conflict and must explain its effect and recommend independent advice): The duty of care in the Trustee Act 2000 can be excluded by the trust instrument, so the clause is not void. Ines benefits personally from it, so there is a significant risk of a conflict between her interest and her client's under the SRA Code, and a standard clause does not remove that risk. She should explain the clause and recommend independent advice before the client signs. An uninformed waiver or later notice to co-trustees does not cure the conflict."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mrs Trent brings her husband, aged 80, to a solicitor's office to make a new will. The draft cuts out his two children and leaves everything to Mrs Trent. She insists on staying in the meeting and answers most of the solicitor's questions on his behalf. Mr Trent appears content and nods in agreement. The solicitor has no medical concerns about capacity. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Refuse to act, because a spouse who stands to benefit means the solicitor cannot take instructions from the client", "B": "Allow her to stay, because she is his wife and he has not objected to her presence at the meeting", "C": "Agree to her request but have Mr Trent sign a statement that no one pressured him, to be kept with the will", "D": "Ask Mrs Trent to wait outside and take instructions from Mr Trent alone, to confirm his wishes are his own", "E": "Take instructions with her present, but record her contributions on the file so the extent of her involvement is clear"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ask Mrs Trent to wait outside and take instructions from Mr Trent alone, to confirm his wishes are his own): A solicitor must be satisfied that the will expresses the client's own free wishes, and where a beneficiary dominates a meeting the proper safeguard is to see the client alone. Mr Trent's apparent contentment is not reliable while his wife answers for him. Recording her role or obtaining a declaration does not replace private instructions. Refusing to act would deny the client a service when a simple step can resolve the concern."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Seb approaches Mina in a car park, says that he knows where her children go to school and tells her he will hurt them next week unless she hands him her wallet now. Terrified, Mina hands over her wallet containing £300 and Seb walks off. Seb intended to keep the cash. Mina was not afraid of being hurt that day. He is charged with robbery under section 8 of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following is the most accurate statement?", "options": {"A": "He is not guilty of theft or robbery, because Mina handed over her wallet voluntarily and so there was no appropriation", "B": "He is not guilty of robbery, because a threat to a third party cannot amount to a threat of force, which must be aimed at the victim herself", "C": "He is guilty of robbery, because the threat caused Mina to hand over property out of fear for other people", "D": "He is not guilty of robbery, because the fear was not of force then and there, though theft and blackmail remain possible", "E": "He is guilty of robbery, because the threat of force against Mina's children was made in order to steal"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He is not guilty of robbery, because the fear was not of force then and there, though theft and blackmail remain possible): Robbery requires that the defendant puts or seeks to put a person in fear of being then and there subjected to force. A threat to hurt children next week is a threat of future force, so robbery fails. Threats to third parties can in principle qualify, so that is not the reason. The facts still support theft, since consent induced by threats does not prevent appropriation, and blackmail through an unwarranted demand with menaces."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Following an investigation, the SRA concludes that Dennis Whitfield, a solicitor in Cardiff, dishonestly misled a client about the progress of a claim over several months to hide a missed limitation date. The SRA considers that the proper sanction is for his name to be removed from the roll of solicitors. Which of the following correctly describes how this outcome can be reached?", "options": {"A": "The Legal Ombudsman can direct removal of the name once it has upheld the client's complaint of poor service", "B": "The SRA can make the order itself through its own adjudicators, as it has power to remove any solicitor from the roll", "C": "The Law Society's Council can vote to strike him off following the SRA's report, because the Law Society represents solicitors", "D": "The client must apply to the High Court for an order striking him off, because the SRA can only recommend sanctions", "E": "The SRA must bring the case before the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal, because only the Tribunal can order that a solicitor be struck off the roll"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The SRA must bring the case before the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal, because only the Tribunal can order that a solicitor be struck off the roll): Striking a solicitor off the roll is a sanction that only the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal can impose, so the SRA prosecutes the case before it. The SRA's own decision makers can rebuke, fine up to a limit and impose conditions, but cannot remove a name from the roll. The Legal Ombudsman deals with service complaints and offers redress, not discipline. The Law Society as representative body has no role in striking off."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Licensing of Premises Act 2024 authorises the Secretary of State to make regulations prescribing fees 'to cover the cost of administering the licensing scheme'. Regulations approved by both Houses set a fee of £2,000 per premises, which the department openly intends to use to raise general revenue for other programmes. Ironhill Ltd, a small cafe operator, wishes to challenge the regulations. Which of the following best states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The regulations are valid until Parliament amends them, because the Secretary of State alone decides what costs of administration include", "B": "The regulations can be challenged by judicial review as ultra vires, because Parliament's approval does not extend the power beyond what the Act authorises", "C": "The regulations can be challenged only by a declaration of incompatibility, because a court cannot quash a statutory instrument approved by Parliament", "D": "The regulations are immune from challenge in the courts, because both Houses have approved them and so have given them the force of primary legislation", "E": "The regulations can be challenged only on the ground of irrationality, because the fee falls within the general subject matter of the Act, so no vires point arises"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The regulations can be challenged by judicial review as ultra vires, because Parliament's approval does not extend the power beyond what the Act authorises): Delegated legislation is valid only if made within the power conferred, and the courts can review whether a minister has stayed within it. The Act allows fees to cover the cost of the scheme, so a charge designed to raise general revenue goes beyond the purpose of the enabling power. Approval by both Houses does not turn a statutory instrument into an Act, so the court may quash it as ultra vires. A declaration of incompatibility belongs to the Human Rights Act and is not the route here."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ryan Fletcher is tried for fraud by false representation. He does not give evidence, but his counsel puts to the complainant, Mrs Lund, that she is a persistent liar, that she has a conviction for fraud, and that she has made false claims to insurers before. Ryan has three previous convictions for dishonesty. The prosecution applies to adduce them. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The convictions cannot be admitted, because an attack on a witness's credibility is not an attack on character", "B": "The convictions cannot be admitted, because the attack gateway applies only if the defendant personally gives evidence at trial", "C": "The convictions are admitted automatically, because once the gateway is engaged the court has no discretion to exclude them", "D": "The convictions may be admitted under the attack on another person's character gateway, because counsel's imputations count, subject to the court's discretion", "E": "The convictions are admissible only if they are for fraud, because dishonesty offences of other kinds are not relevant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The convictions may be admitted under the attack on another person's character gateway, because counsel's imputations count, subject to the court's discretion): A defendant is treated as making an attack on another person's character where questions asked on his behalf carry an imputation of dishonesty or other reprehensible conduct. It does not matter that Ryan stays out of the witness box. The prosecution can then rely on his convictions through the attack gateway, but the court can still refuse if admitting them would have such an adverse effect on fairness that they ought not to be admitted."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Hartley LLP acts for the executors of Mrs Zara Khan. The firm holds £50,000 for the estate in client account. The fee earner has completed the probate work and prepared a bill for £3,600 plus VAT but has not yet sent it. The executors have told the firm orally that it may take its fees from the funds. The cashier asks whether the money can be transferred to the office account now. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "The transfer may be made now, because the executors' oral authority is sufficient and the bill can follow after the money is moved", "B": "The transfer may be made once the executors countersign the bill, because the rules require the client's written approval of the amount", "C": "The transfer may be made on the date the bill is prepared, because delivery is needed only where the firm is itself a personal representative", "D": "The transfer may be made only once the grant has been closed and the estate is fully distributed, because fees rank behind the beneficiaries", "E": "The transfer may be made only once the bill or written notification of costs has been delivered to the executors, and recorded on the ledger"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The transfer may be made only once the bill or written notification of costs has been delivered to the executors, and recorded on the ledger): Client money can be moved to the office account to pay the firm's costs only when a bill or other written notification of costs has been given to the client, and the transfer must be recorded in the ledger. The executors' oral permission is not a substitute for delivery. Their countersignature is not required, and the firm need not wait for the estate to be wound up before taking properly billed fees."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Gareth and Helen Morris are trustees of a house held on trust for themselves and for Gareth's adult daughter, Ruth, who lives there and has a beneficial interest. The trust instrument says nothing about consultation. Gareth and Helen decide to sell the house and do not tell Ruth, who objects when she learns of it. The buyer pays the price to Gareth and Helen jointly. Which of the following best describes the trustees' duty to Ruth under the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996?", "options": {"A": "They must consult Ruth and must follow her wishes, because she lives in the property and has a beneficial interest", "B": "They must obtain Ruth's written consent before selling, because an adult beneficiary in occupation has a veto over any disposition", "C": "They need not consult Ruth, because trustees hold the legal title and may decide on a sale without reference to any beneficiary", "D": "They must consult Ruth only if the sale proceeds exceed her share, because the duty arises on distribution of the money", "E": "They must consult Ruth where practicable and give effect to her wishes so far as consistent with the trust"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (They must consult Ruth where practicable and give effect to her wishes so far as consistent with the trust): Section 11 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 requires trustees of land, in exercising any function relating to the land, to consult beneficiaries of full age who are beneficially entitled to an interest in possession, so far as practicable, and to give effect to their wishes so far as consistent with the general interest of the trust. Ruth lives in the house and has a beneficial interest, so she falls within the duty. The duty is not a veto. The sale would still overreach her interest because the money is paid to two trustees."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Leanne Fox is preparing a short guide to how an Act of Parliament becomes law. Both the House of Commons and the House of Lords have passed the Rural Services Bill without amendment, and it has been sent to the monarch. A client asks what role, if any, the monarch has at this stage and whether the monarch could decline to approve it. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "The monarch decides whether the Bill becomes law by choosing to give or withhold assent in each case according to personal views", "B": "The monarch gives Royal Assent, and by convention the monarch does not refuse assent to a Bill passed by both Houses", "C": "The monarch has no role, because the Bill becomes law on completing its third reading in the second House of Parliament", "D": "The monarch must be consulted by a minister, who gives binding legal advice that decides whether assent is given", "E": "The monarch gives assent after the Privy Council has approved the Bill, and assent can be delayed for a year"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The monarch gives Royal Assent, and by convention the monarch does not refuse assent to a Bill passed by both Houses): A Bill becomes an Act when it has passed both Houses, or has been passed under the Parliament Acts, and receives Royal Assent. The legal power to give assent rests with the monarch, but a constitutional convention requires it to be given, and assent has not been refused since 1708. The monarch does not choose according to personal views. A Bill does not become law on its third reading, and there is no step of Privy Council approval or delay."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Priscilla Marsh bought a registered freehold cottage in 2026 for value. The register contained no entry about chancel repair liability, and no search for it was made. A parochial church council now writes demanding £28,000 towards repairs to the parish church chancel, relying on a historic liability of the cottage's land. The council has never registered a notice against the title. What is the most likely position?", "options": {"A": "Priscilla is liable, because chancel repair is an overriding interest that binds every owner of land within the parish whether or not it is on the register", "B": "Priscilla is liable, because a buyer who fails to make a chancel repair search is treated as having notice of the liability", "C": "Priscilla is not liable, because chancel repair liability was abolished by statute and cannot be enforced against any landowner", "D": "Priscilla is not liable, because the liability no longer binds a buyer of registered land as an overriding interest unless it is protected by notice", "E": "Priscilla is liable but only for half the sum, because the liability is shared with the owners of the neighbouring cottages"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Priscilla is not liable, because the liability no longer binds a buyer of registered land as an overriding interest unless it is protected by notice): Chancel repair liability lost its status as an overriding interest in registered land in October 2013, so since then it binds a buyer only if a notice is entered on the register. The council did not protect its claim, and Priscilla bought for value, so she takes free of it. The liability has not been abolished, which is why a search is still sensible, but failing to search does not give the buyer notice. No apportionment between neighbours is involved."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Your firm holds client money for its client Ned. A trustee, Pia, pays £80,000 into your firm's client account on Ned's behalf, to be used to clear Ned's debts. Before you pay anything out, you learn from a family member that the sum came from a trust of which Pia is a trustee and that she had no power to give it to Ned. Ned says it is a loan. Which of the following is the most appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Pay it out but tell Pia afterwards, because the firm owes duties to its client and not to the trustee", "B": "Pay it out as instructed, because the firm received it only as agent and so has no liability", "C": "Not pay the money out, and take further instructions and advice, because dealing with it now risks accessory liability", "D": "Return the money to Pia at once without telling Ned, because the firm has no duty to explain the reason for the refund", "E": "Hold the money and tell Ned the beneficiaries' names so they can claim directly, because the firm has to reveal what it knows"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Not pay the money out, and take further instructions and advice, because dealing with it now risks accessory liability): Having learned that the money may be trust property misapplied in breach of trust, the firm can no longer deal with it on the client's say-so without risking involvement in the breach. The sensible course is to hold the money, take advice and speak to the client about the position. The firm's earlier receipt as agent does not protect it from later dealings made with knowledge. Returning the funds secretly or revealing the client's affairs to others would breach duties of honesty and confidentiality."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Rosalind Aitken holds a five year lease of a clothes shop, granted by Dunmore Estates, which is protected by Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 and expires on 31 December 2026. She wants a new lease and serves a request for a new tenancy under section 26 on 20 March 2026, proposing a start date of 1 January 2027. Dunmore Estates does not respond. Rosalind's solicitor must decide when to issue the court application for a new tenancy. Which of the following is the correct timing?", "options": {"A": "Between two and six months after the request, matching the six month minimum notice period for the new tenancy", "B": "At any time before the current lease expires, because the right to apply continues while the tenancy continues under section 24", "C": "Between four and six months after the request, once the landlord has had full time to serve a counter-notice", "D": "Within two months after the request, since the landlord's counter-notice must be given within that period and the tenant must then apply promptly", "E": "Not before two months have passed from the request unless the landlord serves a counter-notice, and before the date specified in the request"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Not before two months have passed from the request unless the landlord serves a counter-notice, and before the date specified in the request): Under section 29A of the 1954 Act, where a tenant makes a section 26 request the court will not entertain an application before two months have passed from the request, unless the landlord has served a counter-notice. The application must also be made within the statutory period, which runs to the date specified in the request. The four month limit was the position before the 2003 reforms. The six month figure relates to the notice period for the new tenancy start date. Missing the statutory period would lose the right to a new tenancy."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Ironbridge Marine Ltd is claimant in a multi-track claim worth £450,000 against Salcombe Fabrication Ltd. The court has listed the first case management conference for 28 May. Directions questionnaires are due next week, and Ironbridge's solicitor is used to filing costs budgets with the questionnaire, as the firm does on smaller claims. The solicitor asks when the claimant's costs budget must be filed and served in this claim.", "options": {"A": "At least seven days before the case management conference, so that the other side can comment on it", "B": "At the case management conference itself, where the judge will consider the budget and approve it", "C": "With the directions questionnaire next week, because every costs budget is filed with the questionnaire", "D": "Not later than 21 days before the first case management conference, which here is 7 May", "E": "Within 28 days of the order allocating the claim to the multi-track, even if the conference is later"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Not later than 21 days before the first case management conference, which here is 7 May): In a multi-track claim of £50,000 or more, costs budgets must be filed and exchanged not later than 21 days before the first case management conference. For a conference on 28 May that date is 7 May. Only claims valued below £50,000 have the budget filed with the directions questionnaire, which is the habit the solicitor is bringing from smaller cases. Producing the budget at the conference itself would defeat its purpose, since the judge needs time to consider it."}, {"id": 868, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Coralie owns a flat on the second floor. A water pipe in the flat above, owned by Bryn, burst during a hard frost and flooded Coralie's ceiling and carpets. Bryn had lagged the pipes and checked them each winter. Bryn used the water supply only for ordinary domestic purposes. Coralie wants to claim without proving fault. Which of the following best describes her prospects under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher?", "options": {"A": "They are poor, because an ordinary domestic water supply is a natural use of land, and so the rule does not apply", "B": "They are good, because water stored in a pipe above another's flat is inherently dangerous if it escapes", "C": "They are good, because the rule imposes liability for escapes caused by a natural event such as frost, which is no defence", "D": "They are good, because Bryn brought water onto his land for his own purposes and it escaped to Coralie's land", "E": "They are poor, because the rule is limited to escapes of fire and cannot apply to the escape of water"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They are poor, because an ordinary domestic water supply is a natural use of land, and so the rule does not apply): The rule requires a non-natural use of land, meaning an extraordinary and unusual use rather than an ordinary one. A domestic water supply to a flat is an ordinary use, so Bryn's pipes fall outside the rule. That is why Coralie cannot avoid proving fault by relying on Rylands v Fletcher. It is true that water escaped from Bryn's land to hers, and the rule is strict, but those points do not meet the non-natural use requirement. The claim that the rule is confined to fire is wrong, as water can qualify in suitable cases such as a reservoir."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Dominic Hart and for his lender, Northern Building Society, on the purchase of a freehold house. The mortgage offer states a price of £320,000. After the offer, Dominic negotiated a reduction to £296,000 with the seller, and exchange is planned for Friday. Dominic says the lender need not be told because its valuation was £320,000 and the loan amount is unchanged. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Ask the seller to put the original price in the contract and record the reduction in a separate side letter", "B": "Tell Dominic that the lender must be told of the reduced price before exchange and, if he will not agree, stop acting for both", "C": "Tell the lender only after completion, because the Handbook requires reports at completion rather than before exchange", "D": "Proceed without telling the lender, because the loan amount and the valuation are unchanged and the price is a matter between buyer and seller", "E": "Tell the lender yourself without consulting Dominic, because your duty to the lender overrides every duty owed to him"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell Dominic that the lender must be told of the reduced price before exchange and, if he will not agree, stop acting for both): Acting for borrower and lender, a solicitor must report to the lender anything that a prudent lender would want to know, and a significant price reduction changes the loan to value picture. The solicitor should explain this to Dominic and, if he refuses to allow disclosure, cannot continue because confidentiality and the duty to the lender conflict. Waiting until completion defeats the purpose of the report, and a side letter would be a deception of the lender."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Jade is woken at 3am in her own home by a burglar, Lee, going through her jewellery. Frightened, she hits him once on the arm with a heavy torch, breaking it. Lee is not armed and might have been scared off by shouting. Jade is charged with causing grievous bodily harm and relies on self-defence. Which of the following best describes how the court assesses the force she used?", "options": {"A": "As a householder facing a trespasser, disproportionate force may still be reasonable, but grossly disproportionate force will not be", "B": "Jade has a complete defence because the intruder was in her home, and the reasonableness of her force is not examined", "C": "Jade's failure to shout a warning or retreat to another room means her force was necessarily unreasonable", "D": "Jade can rely on the defence only if she honestly feared that Lee was armed or intended to kill her", "E": "The force must be strictly proportionate to the threat from the intruder, so a blow causing a broken bone to an unarmed intruder was excessive"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (As a householder facing a trespasser, disproportionate force may still be reasonable, but grossly disproportionate force will not be): Section 76(5A) of the Criminal Justice and Immigration Act 2008 applies in householder cases where the defendant is in their home and the other person is a trespasser. Force that is disproportionate is not necessarily unreasonable, but force that is grossly disproportionate is. Jade's single blow in her home at night is unlikely to be grossly disproportionate. The defence is not automatic, and an ability to retreat is only one factor."}, {"id": 871, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Imogen, aged 25 and of normal intelligence, throws a glass bottle from a flat balcony onto the street below as a joke, without giving any thought to whether anyone might be hurt. The bottle strikes and injures a passer-by, Noor, who needs stitches. Imogen is charged with an offence that requires recklessness as to causing harm. Which of the following best describes whether Imogen was reckless?", "options": {"A": "Imogen was reckless, because an ordinary prudent person would have recognised the obvious risk and she failed to consider it", "B": "Imogen was reckless, because throwing a glass bottle onto a public street is a dangerous act that itself proves she took an unjustified risk", "C": "Imogen was not reckless, because recklessness requires that she was actually aware of a risk of harm and she gave it no thought", "D": "Imogen was not reckless, because she did not intend any harm and recklessness is established only where there was an intention to injure", "E": "Imogen was reckless, because an adult of normal intelligence is presumed to foresee the consequences of an act of that kind"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Imogen was not reckless, because recklessness requires that she was actually aware of a risk of harm and she gave it no thought): Since R v G the test for recklessness is subjective: the defendant must be aware of a risk that harm will occur and must go on to take it when it is unreasonable to do so. Imogen gave no thought to any risk, so the first limb is not satisfied, however obvious the danger would have been to others. The old objective approach no longer applies. Recklessness is also distinct from intention, so the lack of an intent to injure does not by itself answer the question."}, {"id": 872, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Beacon Law acts for Meadow Hospital Trust in a pending civil claim by the family of a patient who died. A nurse, Zadie, is arrested for gross negligence manslaughter over the same death, and the Trust asks the firm to represent her at the police station as well. The Trust's internal report suggests inadequate staffing, not Zadie's conduct alone, caused the failure, and Zadie wishes to say so. What should the firm do?", "options": {"A": "Act for both, provided each client gives informed written consent and information barriers are set up between the two teams", "B": "Decline to act for Zadie, because her wish to blame the Trust conflicts with the Trust's defence of the claim, so the firm cannot act for both", "C": "Act for both and advise Zadie not to mention staffing during interview, so that no conflict arises between the clients", "D": "Act for Zadie and cease to act for the Trust only after the police interview has taken place and the investigation is complete", "E": "Act for Zadie only, and disclose the Trust's confidential report to her because her liberty is at stake"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline to act for Zadie, because her wish to blame the Trust conflicts with the Trust's defence of the claim, so the firm cannot act for both): The SRA Code prohibits acting for two clients in the same or a related matter where their interests conflict, unless there is a substantial common interest or they compete for the same objective, which is not so here. Zadie wants to blame systemic failings that the Trust is defending against, so the conflict is real and cannot be solved by consent or information barriers. The firm also owes confidentiality to the Trust and could not use its report for Zadie. Advising her to stay silent about staffing would compromise her defence and the firm's duty to act in her best interests."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "In 2024, Mr Kurt signed a will leaving everything to his sister Ines. The will states: 'I make this will in contemplation of my marriage to Elsa and intend it to remain valid after that marriage.' Mr Kurt married Elsa in 2025 and died in 2026 without making another will. Elsa claims that the marriage revoked the will. Is she correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the will gives nothing to Elsa, and a will made in contemplation of marriage must benefit the intended spouse", "B": "No, but the will is revoked as to half the estate, which passes to Elsa under the intestacy rules", "C": "No, because the will was made in contemplation of marriage to Elsa, so marrying her did not revoke it", "D": "No, because marriage revokes a will only if the testator also has children", "E": "Yes, because marriage revokes any earlier will, whatever it says about the marriage"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because the will was made in contemplation of marriage to Elsa, so marrying her did not revoke it): Under section 18 of the Wills Act 1837, marriage revokes an earlier will unless it appears from the will that it was made in contemplation of that marriage. Mr Kurt's will names Elsa and shows he intended it to continue, so the marriage to her does not revoke it. The will need not benefit Elsa, and there is no rule of half revocation. Elsa may still have other claims, but not on the basis of revocation."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya Mehta holds 30 per cent of the shares in Westerly Gardens Ltd, whose articles are the unamended model articles. Shareholders holding the majority of shares are unhappy with a decision the directors are about to take on a supplier contract and want to stop it. They ask how shareholders can direct the directors on a specific matter. Which of the following correctly states the rule?", "options": {"A": "The shareholders can pass a special resolution directing the directors to take, or refrain from taking, specified action, but it does not invalidate earlier acts", "B": "The shareholders can write to the board requiring a particular decision, and the directors must comply within fourteen days or be in breach", "C": "The shareholders can pass an ordinary resolution at a general meeting that binds the directors on any management decision, including pending contracts", "D": "The shareholders can give binding directions by special resolution, and the direction also invalidates any earlier act of the directors on that matter", "E": "The shareholders can act only by removing the directors by special resolution, because the model articles give the board exclusive management powers"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The shareholders can pass a special resolution directing the directors to take, or refrain from taking, specified action, but it does not invalidate earlier acts): Under the model articles, the directors manage the company's business, but the shareholders may by special resolution direct them to take or refrain from specified action. That needs 75 per cent of the votes, not a simple majority. The direction does not retrospectively invalidate anything the directors did before it was passed. Removal of directors is a separate power exercisable by ordinary resolution, and no informal letter from members binds the board."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Greaves Engineering employs Aisha as a machine operator. It hires an independent firm, SafeGuard Services, to maintain and fit guards on its presses, and SafeGuard fits a guard negligently so that it fails to protect the operator's hands. Aisha is badly hurt. Greaves had no reason to doubt SafeGuard's competence and had not seen the guard fitted. Aisha sues Greaves in negligence. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Greaves is liable only for half of the damages, with Aisha bearing the remainder because she operated the press", "B": "Greaves is not liable, because an independent contractor's negligence is a novus actus that breaks the chain of causation", "C": "Greaves is not liable, because it took reasonable care in selecting a competent independent contractor to maintain the machinery", "D": "Greaves is liable only if it knew that the guard had been fitted negligently before the accident occurred", "E": "Greaves is liable, because its duty to provide safe plant and a safe system is non-delegable and it cannot pass responsibility on"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Greaves is liable, because its duty to provide safe plant and a safe system is non-delegable and it cannot pass responsibility on): An employer's duty to provide safe plant, equipment and a safe system of work is personal and cannot be delegated. Employing a competent independent contractor does not discharge it, so the contractor's negligence is treated as the employer's own breach. Greaves's lack of knowledge, and its careful selection of SafeGuard, are therefore no defence to Aisha's claim. It may recover from SafeGuard under contribution or contract."}, {"id": 876, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Willowbrook Dairies Ltd issued a claim form on 1 June for £35,000 for breach of a supply contract, with particulars attached. The claim form has not yet been served. The claimant's solicitor then discovers that the wrong contract date was pleaded and that a further £8,000 invoice should be added to the claim. No one else has seen the documents. What is the position on amendment?", "options": {"A": "The claimant needs the court's permission, because a claim form that has been issued can be changed only by order", "B": "The claimant needs the court's permission, because a claim form that has been issued can be changed only by order of the court", "C": "The claimant must withdraw the claim and issue fresh proceedings, because an issued claim cannot be changed before service", "D": "The claimant may amend without consent or permission, because the statement of case has not yet been served on any other party", "E": "The claimant may amend without permission only if it does so within 14 days of issue and before the first hearing"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The claimant may amend without consent or permission, because the statement of case has not yet been served on any other party): Permission or consent is needed to amend a statement of case once it has been served, but a party may amend at any time before it has been served on another party. Willowbrook's claim has been issued but not served, so it can correct the date and add the invoice without anyone's agreement. The fact of issue does not matter, and there is no 14 day window. The claimant may need to pay an additional court fee for the increased sum."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Hugh Dalgleish ran a catering business as a sole trader for ten years. He then formed Dalgleish Foods Ltd, transferred the business to it in return for fully paid shares, and became its sole shareholder and sole director. The company has since gone into insolvent liquidation owing £80,000 to unsecured trade suppliers for goods supplied after incorporation, none of whom asked for a personal guarantee. The suppliers want to recover from Hugh personally. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Hugh is not liable, because the company is a separate legal person and his fully paid shares leave him owing nothing to its creditors", "B": "Hugh is liable, because the business existed before incorporation and its debts remain the personal debts of the former sole trader", "C": "Hugh is liable up to the nominal value of his shares, because shareholders stand behind the company's debts to that extent", "D": "Hugh is not liable, but only if the suppliers knew they were dealing with a limited company and its name was displayed on documents", "E": "Hugh is liable, because a company owned and run by a single person is treated as that person's agent for debts it cannot meet"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hugh is not liable, because the company is a separate legal person and his fully paid shares leave him owing nothing to its creditors): Under Salomon v Salomon a company is a legal person separate from its members, even where one individual owns all the shares and runs the business. Hugh's only exposure as a shareholder is any unpaid amount on his shares, and these are fully paid. The debts were incurred by the company after incorporation, so they are the company's debts. Nothing in the facts shows a sham or evasion of an existing obligation, so there is no basis for lifting the veil."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Under a building contract between Quill Ltd and Rathbone Construction, Rathbone promised to pay £50,000 to Quill's landlord, Ferris, as a premium. The contract expressly gave Ferris the right to enforce that payment. Ferris learned of the term and emailed Rathbone to say he was relying on it. Quill and Rathbone then agreed to delete the premium term without consulting Ferris, who objects. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The variation does not bind Ferris, because he communicated his assent to Rathbone and so the parties cannot remove his right without his consent", "B": "The variation binds Ferris, because his right arises only once the contract is performed and has therefore not yet become effective against Rathbone", "C": "The variation binds Ferris unless the original contract contained a clause stating that the premium term could not be varied", "D": "The variation does not bind Ferris, but only because he provided consideration by relying on the promise of the premium", "E": "The variation binds Ferris, because the contracting parties can change their contract and a third party has no say in how they do so"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The variation does not bind Ferris, because he communicated his assent to Rathbone and so the parties cannot remove his right without his consent): Once a third party with an enforceable right has communicated assent to the promisor, the original parties cannot vary or cancel the right without the third party's agreement. Ferris told Rathbone he was relying on the term, so he has assented. His right does not depend on performance and no consideration is needed. No anti-variation clause is required, so the deletion does not bind him."}, {"id": 879, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Gordon Hale married Karen when her daughter, Priya, was nine. They lived together as a family for fifteen years, Gordon paid Priya's school and university fees and treated her as his own child. Priya, now thirty, earns a good salary and lives independently. Gordon died last month leaving his whole estate to a cat charity. Karen died some years ago. Priya wants to claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 and asks whether she is eligible to apply. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "No, because an adult who supports herself cannot bring a claim for provision even if she would otherwise fall within a category of applicant", "B": "Yes, but only if Gordon formally adopted her, since treatment as a child of the family goes to the court's discretion and not to eligibility", "C": "Yes, as a child of the family, because Gordon treated her as his own child in relation to his marriage to Karen", "D": "Yes, but only if she was being maintained by Gordon immediately before his death, as every stepchild must show", "E": "No, because only the deceased's own biological or adopted children fall within the category of child entitled to apply under the Act"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, as a child of the family, because Gordon treated her as his own child in relation to his marriage to Karen): A person treated by the deceased as a child of the family in relation to a marriage can apply, whatever her age, so Priya is eligible. Being financially independent does not take her outside the category, though it will count heavily against her on the question of reasonable provision for her maintenance. She does not need to have been maintained by Gordon or to have been adopted."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A testator leaves £25,000 to a parish council 'for the purpose of providing some useful memorial to myself'. The testator's family says the gift fails. The parish council argues that it can hold the money for that purpose, and the testator's will leaves the residue to a cousin. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The gift fails, because it is a purpose trust with no beneficiary to enforce it and sits outside the exceptions", "B": "The gift is saved by cy près, because the testator's purpose can be applied to a similar charitable object", "C": "The gift is valid as an anomalous trust for the maintenance of a monument and is enforceable by the council", "D": "The gift is valid, because a gift to a parish council is a gift to a public body and so charitable", "E": "The gift is valid, because a purpose trust takes effect if the parish council is willing to carry out the purpose"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The gift fails, because it is a purpose trust with no beneficiary to enforce it and sits outside the exceptions): A trust for a non-charitable purpose is invalid because there is no human beneficiary to enforce it, except for a few anomalous cases such as particular tombs and animals. A vague gift for a 'useful memorial' is not charitable and does not fall within those exceptions. The identity of the council as trustee does not change the character of the gift. The cy près doctrine cannot save a gift that never had a charitable purpose, so the money falls into residue."}, {"id": 881, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Callan is charged with causing grievous bodily harm with intent after punching a stranger outside a bar. He tells his solicitor that he drank eight pints voluntarily and genuinely thought the man was reaching for a weapon, although the man was simply taking out a phone. Callan wants to plead self-defence and refuses to consider any other approach. How should the solicitor advise?", "options": {"A": "Explain that a mistake induced by voluntary drink cannot support self-defence, give an honest view of the prospects, and leave the plea to Callan", "B": "Explain that self-defence will fail, and present the case at trial without mentioning it, so as not to waste the court's time", "C": "Refuse to act, because a client who rejects the solicitor's advice on the merits cannot be represented under the Code of Conduct", "D": "Plead guilty on his behalf at the first hearing, because the solicitor must act in the client's best interests whatever he instructs", "E": "Explain that self-defence is available on his honest belief however it arose, so it is reasonable to put the defence to the jury for them to decide"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that a mistake induced by voluntary drink cannot support self-defence, give an honest view of the prospects, and leave the plea to Callan): A solicitor must give the client advice that is competent and honest about the merits, so Callan must be told that a mistaken belief caused by voluntary intoxication cannot support self-defence. The solicitor should explain the realistic prospects and the benefits of an early guilty plea, but the decision on plea and defence is the client's. Refusing to act merely because he disagrees would not be justified. The solicitor must follow lawful instructions and cannot enter a plea or abandon a defence without his agreement."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Hallam Packaging Ltd intends to sue Greenfield Plastics Ltd for £45,000 unpaid for deliveries made last year. No pre-action protocol applies to this type of claim, so the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct and Protocols governs. Hallam's solicitor has sent a letter of claim setting out the facts and the remedy sought, and the dispute is straightforward. Within what time is Greenfield expected to send its full written response?", "options": {"A": "Within twenty eight days of the letter, because that is the usual period allowed for a defence", "B": "Within three months of the letter, which is the standard period for all cases under the Practice Direction, however simple", "C": "Within fourteen days of the letter, which is the period regarded as reasonable in a straightforward matter", "D": "Within twenty one days of the letter, because that is the period for accepting a Part 36 offer", "E": "Within seven days of the letter, because a debt claim requires a faster reply than other matters"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Within fourteen days of the letter, which is the period regarded as reasonable in a straightforward matter): The Practice Direction expects a defendant to respond within a reasonable time, which is 14 days in a straightforward case and no more than three months in a very complex one. This is a simple unpaid invoices dispute, so 14 days is the expected period. The 28 day figure belongs to the time for a defence after proceedings have started. The 21 day figure is the usual relevant period for a Part 36 offer and has no application to the response to a letter of claim."}, {"id": 883, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Odette bought a camper van from Burnley Motors on 14 June, after Burnley's manager told her on 10 June, 'It has done only 30,000 miles.' On 14 June she signed a detailed written contract that said nothing about mileage. The van had done 80,000 miles. Odette had read the document before signing, and she asks you whether the manager's statement formed a term of the contract. Which of the following is the most likely classification?", "options": {"A": "A term, because it related to a fact within the seller's knowledge and it persuaded Odette to enter the contract", "B": "A mere representation, because a statement of fact made before the contract is a term only if repeated orally when the document is signed", "C": "A term, because the manager made it and had superior knowledge of the van's history, and Odette relied on it", "D": "A term, because a statement the manager knew to be false must have been intended to bind Burnley", "E": "A mere representation, because the written contract came days later and its silence on mileage suggests the statement was no term"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A mere representation, because the written contract came days later and its silence on mileage suggests the statement was no term): When a statement is made some days before a detailed written contract that omits it, the courts infer that the parties did not intend it to be a term, which was the approach in Routledge v McKay. The time gap and the careful written document show that mileage was left out. Superior knowledge and inducement count in favour of a term but are outweighed here. Odette's remedy, if any, lies in misrepresentation rather than breach of a term."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Mina owned two adjoining cottages. She let Cottage A to Joe on a five-year lease, and throughout the lease Joe regularly wheeled his bins along a passage beside Cottage B, which Mina occupied herself, with her casual permission. Mina then sold Cottage A freehold to Joe by a transfer containing the usual general words conveying the land together with all ways and privileges appertaining to it. Mina later sold Cottage B to Priya, who has blocked the passage. Which of the following best states Joe's position?", "options": {"A": "Joe has no easement, because section 62 applies only where the use was continuous and apparent and necessary for reasonable enjoyment", "B": "Joe has no easement, because a use enjoyed only with the landlord's casual permission is a mere licence that cannot ripen into an easement on a later sale", "C": "Joe has an easement, but only an equitable one, because the transfer did not expressly grant a right of way over the passage", "D": "Joe probably has an easement under section 62 LPA 1925, because occupation was diverse and the general words converted his permitted use into a right", "E": "Joe has no easement, because an easement can pass on a sale only where it is strictly necessary for access to the land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Joe probably has an easement under section 62 LPA 1925, because occupation was diverse and the general words converted his permitted use into a right): Section 62 operates on a conveyance of land with general words and can turn a precarious right, enjoyed by permission, into a legal easement where there was diversity of occupation before the grant. Here Joe was a tenant and Mina occupied the cottage over which he exercised the use, so occupation was diverse. The Wheeldon v Burrows conditions of continuous and apparent use and necessity are not needed for section 62. The fact that the use depended on Mina's permission is exactly the situation that section 62 can convert."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "On 12 August, a county council refused Gareth Pugh's application for a school transport grant for his daughter. Gareth wants to challenge the decision by judicial review on the ground that the council ignored its own published criteria. He consults a solicitor in late October, and asks how long he has to issue the claim. Which of the following best states the time limit?", "options": {"A": "The claim must be filed within twenty eight days of the decision, which is the period for appeals against council decisions", "B": "The claim must be filed promptly and within three months at most, and delay inside that period can still defeat it", "C": "The claim must be filed within twelve months of the decision, because that is the period for challenging public authority decisions", "D": "The claim must be filed within six years of the decision, which is the usual limitation period for claims founded on statute", "E": "The claim must be filed within three months, and a claim filed on any day inside that period is automatically in time"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim must be filed promptly and within three months at most, and delay inside that period can still defeat it): CPR Part 54 requires a judicial review claim form to be filed promptly and in any event within three months of the date the grounds first arose. The courts treat promptness as a separate requirement, so delay even inside three months can justify refusing permission or relief. Gareth should therefore act without waiting. The six year and twelve month periods belong to other kinds of claim, and twenty eight days is not the general period."}, {"id": 886, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Karim Siddiqui is charged with burglary and has twice failed to attend court in earlier cases. The magistrates are willing to grant bail if a surety is provided. His friend Anna Walsh, who has savings of about £5,000 and says she trusts him, offers to stand surety in the sum of £2,000. Anna asks his solicitor what her obligation would be. Which of the following correctly describes Anna's position?", "options": {"A": "Anna would be responsible for securing Karim's attendance, and if he absconds the court may order her to forfeit all or part of the sum", "B": "Anna would also guarantee Karim's good behaviour, so she could forfeit the sum if he committed a further offence while on bail pending trial", "C": "Anna would have to pay the £2,000 into court before bail was granted, so that the court held it as security", "D": "Anna would be released from liability once Karim attended his first hearing, as a surety covers only that hearing", "E": "Anna would owe the full £2,000 as soon as Karim failed to attend, without any further hearing of her circumstances"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Anna would be responsible for securing Karim's attendance, and if he absconds the court may order her to forfeit all or part of the sum): A surety undertakes to secure the defendant's attendance at court and is bound by a recognisance for a stated sum. If the defendant fails to surrender, the court may order forfeiture of all or part of that sum after considering the surety's means and the circumstances, so there is a further hearing. A surety does not guarantee good behaviour, only attendance. Unlike a security, the money need not be deposited in advance, and the obligation lasts until the defendant surrenders as required."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Larchmont Ltd is a private company wishing to buy back shares from a departing shareholder for £80,000. Its distributable profits are only £20,000, but it has substantial share capital and other reserves, and the directors believe it will remain solvent for a year. They plan to pass the necessary resolution on 1 October and complete the purchase on the same day. The solicitor is asked whether the plan is lawful. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Payment out of capital is unlawful, because a company may fund a buyback only from distributable profits or the proceeds of a fresh share issue", "B": "Capital can fund the shortfall after a solvency statement, auditor's report and special resolution, with payment five to seven weeks later", "C": "Payment out of capital is lawful if the directors make a solvency statement, and payment may be made on the same day as the resolution", "D": "Payment out of capital is available only to public companies, which may use capital if the articles allow and members approve by ordinary resolution", "E": "Payment out of capital is lawful if a special resolution is passed and an auditor reports, with no solvency statement needed from the directors"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Capital can fund the shortfall after a solvency statement, auditor's report and special resolution, with payment five to seven weeks later): A private company may buy back its own shares partly out of capital where distributable profits are insufficient, but only through a strict procedure. The directors must make a solvency statement, the auditor must report, and the members must approve by special resolution. Payment must then be made not earlier than five weeks, and not later than seven weeks, after the resolution. Public companies cannot use capital, and completing on the day of the resolution would breach the timetable."}, {"id": 888, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Cobalt Packaging Ltd granted a debenture to Harbour Bank. The debenture describes a 'fixed charge' over all present and future book debts. However, the company remains free to collect the debts into its ordinary current account at another bank and to use the money for its day to day trading without the bank's consent. The company later goes into administration. How is the charge over the book debts likely to be classified?", "options": {"A": "As a fixed charge, because the debenture describes it as fixed and the label chosen by the parties determines the nature of the charge", "B": "As a fixed charge, because book debts are identifiable assets that exist at the date of the debenture and can be specifically described", "C": "As a floating charge, but only after crystallisation, since until then the debenture creates no charge over the book debts", "D": "As a floating charge, because the company is free to collect and use the proceeds in the ordinary course of business despite the label", "E": "As an equitable lien rather than a charge, because the bank has no control over the account into which the proceeds are paid"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (As a floating charge, because the company is free to collect and use the proceeds in the ordinary course of business despite the label): The court looks at the substance of the rights granted, not the label. A charge is fixed only if the chargee controls the asset and its proceeds, and here the company can use the money freely in trading, which is the mark of a floating charge as in Re Spectrum Plus. Identifiable assets such as book debts can be subject to either kind of charge. The charge exists from the date of the debenture and crystallises later, and it does not depend on crystallisation to be a charge."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Imogen Hartley is preparing written submissions that rely on the mischief rule as it was stated in Heydon's Case. Her supervisor asks her to set out the questions the court asks, in order. She is advising a client who is accused of an offence under a recent statute, and Imogen wants to start with the first question in the sequence. Which of the following is the first question that the mischief rule asks?", "options": {"A": "What is the true reason for the remedy that Parliament appointed in the Act?", "B": "What remedy has Parliament resolved and appointed to cure the disease of the commonwealth?", "C": "What would an ordinary reasonable reader take the words of the section to mean on their own?", "D": "What was the common law position before the Act was passed to change it?", "E": "What mischief or defect did the common law fail to provide for, which the Act addresses?"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (What was the common law position before the Act was passed to change it?): Heydon's Case sets four questions in sequence: the common law before the Act, the mischief or defect for which the common law did not provide, the remedy Parliament resolved to cure it, and the true reason for the remedy. The first step is therefore to establish the common law position before the Act. The other questions come later, and asking what the words mean on their own is the approach of the literal rule rather than the mischief rule."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Okafor, aged 84, instructs a solicitor to prepare a new will leaving his whole estate to his live-in carer instead of his two adult children, who benefit under his current will. His GP's records show a diagnosis of moderate vascular dementia six months ago, although his daughter says he has good days and bad days. He wants to sign the new will next week, and the solicitor expects the children to contest it. What is the most appropriate step for the solicitor to take before the will is executed?", "options": {"A": "Have him sign while he is having a good day, and seek medical opinion only if a claim is later brought by the children", "B": "Proceed without medical evidence, because capacity is presumed and any challenger would carry the burden of proving that he lacked it", "C": "Decline to act, because a person with a diagnosis of dementia cannot make a valid will under the law of England and Wales", "D": "Ask the daughter to confirm that he has lucid intervals and rely on her written account of his understanding as evidence", "E": "Arrange for the GP to assess his capacity before signing, have the GP witness the will, and keep a note of the assessment"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Arrange for the GP to assess his capacity before signing, have the GP witness the will, and keep a note of the assessment): Where capacity is in real doubt, the golden rule is that an independent medical practitioner should assess the testator at the time and, ideally, witness the will and record the assessment. A diagnosis of dementia does not itself remove capacity, so declining to act is wrong. The presumption of capacity helps in court but does not excuse a solicitor from taking precautions, and medical evidence gathered after a dispute begins carries much less weight. The daughter is an interested relative, so her account would not be reliable evidence."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Tariq is charged with shoplifting and has a hearing at the magistrates' court. At the police station he told his solicitor, Anwen, that he took the items. He now instructs her to enter a not guilty plea and to say to the court that he left the shop without realising the goods were in his bag. Which of the following is the correct approach for Anwen?", "options": {"A": "She may enter a not guilty plea and test the prosecution case, but cannot advance a positive account she knows is untrue", "B": "She may run his instructed account, because her duty is to follow client instructions and the court decides what is true", "C": "She must advise him to plead guilty, and may continue to act only if he agrees to change his plea", "D": "She may continue to act but must tell the court about his admission, because her duty to the court outweighs confidentiality", "E": "She must stop acting immediately, because a client who has confessed guilt cannot properly plead not guilty on her advice"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She may enter a not guilty plea and test the prosecution case, but cannot advance a positive account she knows is untrue): A solicitor must not mislead the court, but a client who admits guilt is still entitled to plead not guilty and put the prosecution to proof. Anwen can therefore test the evidence and argue that it is insufficient. She cannot assert his innocence or put forward the account that he did not realise the goods were there, which she knows is untrue. She is not required to withdraw, and she must not reveal what he told her, as that would breach confidentiality."}, {"id": 892, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Eastgate Logistics Ltd is the claimant in a multi track claim worth £400,000 against a supplier. Costs budgeting applies. The claimant's solicitor did not file and serve its costs budget until the day after the date fixed for exchange, which was seven days before the first case management conference. The delay was due to a computer fault. The defendant's budget was filed in time and the conference is fixed for next week. What is the likely consequence for the claimant's budget?", "options": {"A": "The claimant may file a budget up to 14 days after the conference without any application, because the time limit is advisory", "B": "The claimant forfeits all recovery of costs in the claim, because late filing of a budget removes any right to costs", "C": "It will be treated as comprising only applicable court fees, unless the court grants relief from that sanction on an application", "D": "The claimant's budget is disregarded and the court will fix the budget by reference to the defendant's figures", "E": "The claimant will pay only the court fee on the late application, as that is the sole consequence of late filing"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It will be treated as comprising only applicable court fees, unless the court grants relief from that sanction on an application): Under CPR 3.13 and 3.14, a party who fails to file a budget on time is treated as having filed a budget limited to applicable court fees, unless the court orders otherwise. That severe sanction is the default, and relief must be sought under the usual principles. It does not remove all rights to costs outside the budgeted phases in principle, but recovery would be drastically limited. The deadline is mandatory, not advisory, and the court does not adopt the opponent's figures."}, {"id": 893, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Ian, a joiner, becomes angry with his lodger and sets fire to the lodger's room at night to frighten him into leaving, knowing the lodger is asleep inside. The lodger dies in the fire. Ian says he did not want the lodger to die. The prosecution relies on oblique intention to prove the mens rea for murder. According to the leading authority, which of the following must the jury find before it may infer intention?", "options": {"A": "That death or serious injury was a virtual certainty as a result of Ian's actions and that Ian appreciated this was so", "B": "That death or serious injury was a probable consequence of Ian's actions and that Ian foresaw it as a likely consequence of setting the fire", "C": "That Ian desired the result, since a purpose to bring it about is required for every form of intention", "D": "That a reasonable person would have foreseen death or serious injury as a possible result of the fire", "E": "That death or serious injury was a virtual certainty, whether or not Ian himself realised that it was so"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (That death or serious injury was a virtual certainty as a result of Ian's actions and that Ian appreciated this was so): Under Woollin the jury may infer intention if death or serious harm was a virtual certainty as a result of the defendant's act and the defendant appreciated that such was the case. Probability alone is too low a threshold, and a purely objective certainty without the defendant's appreciation of it is not enough. Desire is not needed, because oblique intention exists precisely for cases where the result was foreseen but not wanted. An objective reasonable person test would turn intention into negligence."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Fatima was convicted after a trial at the magistrates' court of assault. She believes the bench wrongly admitted a hearsay statement, which is a point of law, and that its findings of fact were also wrong. She asks whether she can both request a case stated and appeal to the Crown Court. Her solicitor is preparing advice. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She can ask for a case stated, and keeps her Crown Court appeal if the High Court dismisses her case", "B": "She can pursue both routes together, because the Crown Court deals with the facts and the High Court deals only with the law", "C": "She can appeal to the Crown Court on the facts, but case stated is available only to the prosecution", "D": "She can appeal to the Crown Court on the hearsay point only, because the Crown Court cannot reopen the facts", "E": "She can choose a case stated on the law, but doing so ends her Crown Court appeal against that conviction"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (She can choose a case stated on the law, but doing so ends her Crown Court appeal against that conviction): A defendant may seek a case stated from the magistrates on a point of law or excess of jurisdiction, but applying for it abandons any right to appeal to the Crown Court against the same conviction. The Crown Court appeal is a full rehearing on facts and law, so Fatima loses that opportunity. The two routes cannot run side by side, and the right is not revived if the High Court rejects her case."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Your client, Mrs Oyelaran, wants a trust under which £80,000 will be held after her death for 'my friends', with her executor choosing who benefits and in what amounts. She says she has many friends in several towns and does not want to name anyone. She is firm that the wording should be short and simple, and she has already booked a holiday for next week. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice for you to give?", "options": {"A": "Draft as instructed, because the client has chosen the wording and a solicitor must act within the client's instructions", "B": "Advise that the trust is valid if the executor is also the sole trustee, because certainty then rests on one person's judgement", "C": "Advise that 'friends' is too uncertain a class to be valid and recommend a defined list or class, then draft that wording", "D": "Draft as instructed, because uncertainty about the class can be resolved by the court after her death", "E": "Draft as instructed, because a trust for friends with an executor choosing the beneficiaries satisfies the certainty of objects requirement"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise that 'friends' is too uncertain a class to be valid and recommend a defined list or class, then draft that wording): A discretionary trust needs a class whose members can be identified by the is-or-is-not test, and 'friends' is conceptually uncertain because there is no objective way to say who qualifies. A solicitor must act competently and in the client's best interests, which includes warning of the defect and proposing wording that works, such as named individuals or defined relationships. Following instructions without advising would put the client at risk of a failed trust, so the instruction cannot override the duty to advise. Appointing the executor does not turn an uncertain class into a certain one."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "In 2019 Roy Ellis transferred his house, worth £350,000, to his daughter, Pam, by deed. Roy continued to live there rent free, with no agreement that he should pay for his occupation. Pam has never lived at the house. Roy died in 2026, when the house was worth £420,000. His estate otherwise has a value of £200,000. The executors ask the solicitor how the house is treated for Inheritance Tax on Roy's death. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The house is excluded from Roy's estate, because a gift to a child is exempt if the child takes legal ownership by deed", "B": "The house is a potentially exempt transfer, but it is brought back into charge at £420,000 because Roy died within fourteen years", "C": "The house is a gift with reservation of benefit, so its value at death of £420,000 is added to Roy's estate", "D": "The house is a potentially exempt transfer that has become exempt, because Roy survived the gift by more than seven years", "E": "The house is included at its value of £350,000 on the date of the gift, because the donor's tax position is fixed at that date"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The house is a gift with reservation of benefit, so its value at death of £420,000 is added to Roy's estate): Because Roy continued to enjoy the house rent free, the gift was made subject to a reservation of benefit, so for Inheritance Tax the house remains part of his estate and is taxed at its value at death. The seven year rule does not rescue it, because the benefit was retained to the end. The value at the date of the gift is irrelevant, and a deed does not make a gift exempt."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Hannah Doyle, buying a registered freehold house from Mr Barlow for £295,000. The charges register shows a registered charge in favour of Eastern Bank, which Mr Barlow will repay from the sale proceeds. Hannah's mortgage offer requires a clear title on completion. She asks whether she should insist on seeing the bank's discharge before she completes. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Ask for a retention from the price until the discharge reaches the register, because an undertaking does not bind a solicitor professionally", "B": "Require Eastern Bank to join in the transfer, because a charge can be removed only by a deed from the chargee on completion", "C": "Insist on the discharge before exchange of contracts, because a buyer cannot enter a contract for property that is subject to a registered charge", "D": "Accept the seller's solicitor's undertaking to redeem on completion and send the discharge, which is normal and must be performed", "E": "Complete without any undertaking and pay the price direct to the bank, because only the bank can lawfully release its own charge"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Accept the seller's solicitor's undertaking to redeem on completion and send the discharge, which is normal and must be performed): In a standard sale the seller's mortgage is repaid from the proceeds of sale at completion, and the buyer is protected by the seller's solicitor's undertaking to redeem and to supply the discharge. A solicitor must perform an undertaking, which is why it is accepted in practice. Contracts can be exchanged while a charge exists, and neither a retention nor the lender's joinder in the transfer is needed. Hannah can therefore rely on the undertaking."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Una is charged with murder of her husband. Both the prosecution and defence psychiatrists agree that she was suffering from a recognised medical condition, major depression, which substantially impaired her ability to form a rational judgment. However, Una had texted a friend beforehand saying she wanted him dead for his life insurance. The Crown still contests diminished responsibility. Her solicitor is advising on the likely approach at trial. Which advice is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The judge should withdraw the defence from the jury, because evidence of financial motive means the condition cannot have impaired her judgment", "B": "The text message is inadmissible on diminished responsibility, because motive is relevant only to whether the defendant intended to kill", "C": "The jury is bound to accept the agreed expert evidence, and Una must be convicted of manslaughter because the psychiatrists agree on the medical condition", "D": "The jury must acquit of murder and manslaughter, because agreed expert evidence of a recognised medical condition amounts to a complete defence", "E": "The jury must consider all the evidence, and may reject the defence despite agreed medical evidence if the impairment did not explain the killing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The jury must consider all the evidence, and may reject the defence despite agreed medical evidence if the impairment did not explain the killing): For diminished responsibility the abnormality must also provide an explanation for the defendant's acts, meaning it caused or was a significant contributory factor. The jury decides that issue on all of the evidence, including the text message about insurance money. Agreed expert opinion is persuasive but the jury may reject it if the wider evidence points to a different explanation. The defence is only partial, so success produces manslaughter and not an acquittal, and the judge would leave it to the jury given the agreed evidence."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A manager at Colefield and Dunn, a two-office firm, withdrew £8,000 from the client account to cover a temporary shortfall in office funds, repaying the money the next day. The firm's Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration (COFA) learns of this during a monthly reconciliation. The manager points out that no client lost money and that the sum was repaid in full. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Raise the matter at the next partners' meeting and report only if the partners decide it is serious", "B": "Wait until the firm's annual accountant's report and report the breach then, because that is the usual reporting date", "C": "Report the breach to the SRA promptly, because using client money to fund the firm is a serious breach even though it was repaid", "D": "Make no report, because the breach was remedied the next day and no client suffered loss", "E": "Report the breach only if it recurs, because a single short lived withdrawal is a minor technical matter"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the breach to the SRA promptly, because using client money to fund the firm is a serious breach even though it was repaid): Using client money for the firm's own purposes is a serious breach of the Accounts Rules, and the COFA must report to the SRA promptly. Repayment the next day shows the breach was remedied but does not undo it, and the absence of loss does not make it minor. The COFA cannot leave the decision to the partners or wait for a later reporting date. The duty to report promptly is personal to the compliance officers."}, {"id": 900, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Dr Shah, a hospital consultant, fails to review a patient's deteriorating observations over a weekend, in breach of the hospital's clear protocol. The patient develops a serious but treatable complication and dies. Experts say that at the time of the breach there was a serious and obvious risk of lasting injury, though death was not obviously likely. Dr Shah is charged with gross negligence manslaughter. Which of the following correctly identifies the risk the prosecution must prove?", "options": {"A": "A serious and obvious risk of serious injury, because the offence concerns negligence causing harm that later results in a death", "B": "A risk of some harm that a sober and reasonable person would recognise, as in unlawful act manslaughter", "C": "A subjective awareness by Dr Shah of a high probability of death, because gross negligence requires proof of recklessness", "D": "Any foreseeable risk of death from the omission, provided that the omission breached the hospital's own written protocol", "E": "A serious and obvious risk of death, assessed at the time of the breach, and not merely a risk of serious injury or illness"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A serious and obvious risk of death, assessed at the time of the breach, and not merely a risk of serious injury or illness): Following Rose and Broughton, gross negligence manslaughter requires a serious and obvious risk of death at the time of the breach, judged objectively. A risk of serious injury or illness is not enough, and the experts here put death as not obviously likely. Subjective awareness of the risk is not required, since the test is objective. A breach of internal protocol is relevant to breach of duty but cannot replace the necessary risk of death."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Eira Lloyd is a solicitor advising on an Act of Senedd Cymru that regulates private water supplies in Wales. The Act was passed and published in both English and Welsh. Her client's English text and the Welsh text appear to differ slightly in how they describe a duty to test supplies. Eira wants to know what status the two texts have. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The two texts are equally authoritative, and the court will read them together to find the meaning that fits both", "B": "Neither text is authoritative alone, because the Act takes effect after the Counsel General certifies one agreed text", "C": "The text laid first before the Senedd prevails, and the other text is treated as a translation of it", "D": "The English text prevails, because English is the language of the courts and a conflict is resolved by the English version", "E": "The Welsh text prevails, because the Act applies in Wales and the Senedd is a Welsh institution"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The two texts are equally authoritative, and the court will read them together to find the meaning that fits both): Acts of Senedd Cymru are made in both English and Welsh, and the two texts have equal status. A court faced with an apparent difference reads them together and looks for the meaning that is consistent with both. Neither language prevails automatically, and neither text is treated as a translation of the other. Eira should check both texts and the context before advising on the testing duty."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Aunt Marian lent her nephew Peter £8,000. Some years later she told him she no longer expected repayment and he need not repay it, but she gave him nothing in writing. She continued to say the same until she died. Her will appointed Peter executor and left everything to her cousin. Peter obtains the grant of probate. The cousin demands that Peter repay the £8,000. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Peter must repay, because the debt was not released by deed and the aunt's words cannot discharge it", "B": "Peter need not repay, because Marian's intention to forgive continued until death and he became her executor", "C": "Peter need not repay, because an oral statement that a debt need not be repaid is a binding contractual waiver", "D": "Peter must repay, because an imperfect release of a debt is not enforced in equity and he is only a volunteer", "E": "Peter need not repay, because he is a beneficiary of the estate and can set the debt against any share"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Peter need not repay, because Marian's intention to forgive continued until death and he became her executor): The rule in Strong v Bird applies where a donor intends to release a debt and that intention continues until death, with the donee later becoming the donor's executor or administrator. The vesting of the estate in Peter completes the imperfect gift by merging the debt. Marian's words alone would not discharge it, and Peter is not a beneficiary under the will, so set-off against a share is no answer. The cousin's demand for repayment therefore fails."}, {"id": 903, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Gwen Pryce is a solicitor at Ashby Wren LLP. Her client, Dilip Rao, agreed by email with his landlord that a new five year lease of his workshop would carry rent of £24,000 a year. Gwen's trainee drafted the lease, which both parties signed, but it states rent of £42,000 a year because two digits were transposed. Neither party noticed until the first quarter's invoice arrived. The landlord says the signed document governs. What should Gwen do?", "options": {"A": "Advise Dilip that the lease is void for common mistake, so that he may leave the premises and owe no rent for his period of occupation", "B": "Advise Dilip that the signed lease is conclusive and that he must pay the stated rent, because earlier emails cannot vary a written lease", "C": "Seek rectification for Dilip without mentioning the error to him until the court has ruled, to avoid unsettling the client at this early stage of the dispute", "D": "Ask the landlord to agree a deed of variation and tell Dilip about the error only if the landlord refuses to cooperate", "E": "Tell Dilip promptly of the firm's mistake, advise him to seek rectification of the lease, and explain that he may have a claim against the firm"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Tell Dilip promptly of the firm's mistake, advise him to seek rectification of the lease, and explain that he may have a claim against the firm): The signed lease does not match the continuing common intention shown in the emails, so the court can rectify it to record the agreed rent. Gwen's own firm made the error, so her duty of honesty and her duty to put matters right require her to tell Dilip promptly and to explain that a claim may arise. Waiting for the court or for the landlord to refuse would conceal a known mistake. Treating the lease as void misunderstands the remedy, because the parties agreed a lease and only the figure was recorded wrongly."}, {"id": 904, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Dan prepares a syringe of heroin for his friend Lia, who is addicted, and hands it to her. Lia, an adult who understands the risk, injects herself in front of him and later dies from an overdose. Dan knew heroin was a controlled drug and had supplied it without charge. The Crown charges Dan with unlawful act manslaughter. Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": {"A": "Dan is liable only if he intended Lia to die, because death from a drug overdose requires proof of that intention", "B": "Dan is not liable, because Lia's free, voluntary and informed decision to inject herself breaks the chain of causation from his supply", "C": "Dan is not liable, because supplying a drug is an omission rather than an act and omissions cannot found unlawful act manslaughter", "D": "Dan is liable, because he prepared the syringe himself and so participated directly in the administration of the drug", "E": "Dan is liable, because supplying a controlled drug is an unlawful act and a reasonable person would recognise the risk of some harm to the user"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dan is not liable, because Lia's free, voluntary and informed decision to inject herself breaks the chain of causation from his supply): Supplying a controlled drug is an unlawful act and is dangerous, but liability also needs the act to cause death. Where an informed adult freely and voluntarily self-administers, her own act breaks the chain, as the Court of Appeal and House of Lords held in Kennedy. Dan only supplied and prepared the drug, and did not administer it to her. Liability would be different if he had injected her himself, but that is not these facts."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Hawthorn Properties let a shop to Imran Qureshi on a written lease used wholly for commercial purposes. The quarter's rent of £6,000 has been unpaid for three weeks. Today a certified enforcement agent instructed by Hawthorn arrives at the shop intending to take control of the stock and the till, saying the landlord has authority under the commercial rent arrears recovery procedure. No notice has been sent to Imran. Which of the following best describes whether the agent may take control of goods today?", "options": {"A": "Yes, provided the landlord first forfeits the lease by peaceable re-entry so that the tenant's goods may lawfully be taken", "B": "No, because the arrears must first exceed three months' rent before the procedure becomes available to the landlord", "C": "Yes, because once rent is unpaid the landlord may take control of goods at once if the lease is a written commercial lease", "D": "No, because the landlord must first give the tenant at least seven clear days' written notice of enforcement", "E": "No, because the landlord must first obtain a county court judgment for the arrears before any goods may be taken"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because the landlord must first give the tenant at least seven clear days' written notice of enforcement): Commercial rent arrears recovery lets a landlord of wholly commercial premises under a written lease take control of the tenant's goods without a court order, provided at least seven days' rent is outstanding. The landlord must first give the tenant a notice of enforcement with at least seven clear days' notice, so nothing may be taken today. No judgment is needed, which is the point of the procedure. The procedure is available only while the tenancy continues, so forfeiting the lease would remove it."}, {"id": 906, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Contracts for the sale of a freehold house were exchanged on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) with completion due on 15 June. The buyer's mortgage funds arrived five days late because of a lender delay, and completion took place on 20 June. The purchase price was £300,000 and the buyer paid a deposit of £30,000. The seller claims compensation. What is the seller entitled to claim?", "options": {"A": "Nothing, because time is not of the essence unless a notice to complete has been served and the delay was short", "B": "Compensation for the five days of delay, as interest at the contract rate on the balance of the price, paid by the buyer", "C": "Only the loss the seller can prove, because the conditions give no fixed measure of compensation for late completion", "D": "The whole deposit of £30,000, because the buyer broke the contract by failing to complete on the date fixed", "E": "Damages for the delay, but only once the seller has served a notice to complete and the buyer has again failed to complete"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Compensation for the five days of delay, as interest at the contract rate on the balance of the price, paid by the buyer): The Standard Conditions provide automatic compensation where completion is late: the party at fault pays interest at the contract rate on the balance of the price for the period of the delay. The seller does not need to prove loss, serve a notice, or make time of the essence to claim it. Forfeiting the deposit is a remedy only for a buyer who fails to complete after a valid notice to complete."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mrs Ibrahim is injured in a road accident caused by a negligent driver and is unable to work for five months before trial, losing net earnings of £2,400 each month. She also suffers a lasting back injury and ongoing pain, and she has spent £310 on physiotherapy and paid £85 in prescription charges. Her solicitor is preparing the schedule of loss and must classify the heads of damage correctly. Which of the following correctly classifies the heads of damage as special or general?", "options": {"A": "Out of pocket expenses and pain, suffering and loss of amenity are special damages, while lost earnings to trial are general", "B": "Lost earnings to trial and pain, suffering and loss of amenity are special damages, while expenses are general damages", "C": "Lost earnings to trial and expenses are special damages, while pain, suffering and loss of amenity are general damages", "D": "Lost earnings to trial are general damages, while expenses and pain, suffering and loss of amenity are special damages", "E": "All heads are general damages because personal injury damages are assessed by a judge using the Judicial College guidelines"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lost earnings to trial and expenses are special damages, while pain, suffering and loss of amenity are general damages): Special damages are losses that can be calculated at the date of trial, such as past loss of earnings and expenses incurred. General damages are losses that cannot be precisely quantified, including pain, suffering, loss of amenity and future loss. Lost earnings to trial are therefore special damages, not general. The use of Judicial College guidelines applies to the non-pecuniary heads only."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ms Patel is called by the prosecution as the victim of a street robbery. In examination in chief the prosecutor asks her, 'The man who took your bag was wearing a red jacket, wasn't he?' The identity of the robber and his clothing are both disputed by the defendant, who is represented by a solicitor advocate. Which of the following best describes how the question should be treated?", "options": {"A": "It is objectionable only if the witness has been declared hostile, since leading questions are otherwise permitted", "B": "It is objectionable only because it is hearsay, as the witness is repeating what the prosecutor has just said to her about the robber and his jacket", "C": "It is a leading question, and the defence can object because it is not allowed on disputed matters without leave", "D": "It is permissible, because a question that can be answered yes or no does not suggest which answer is wanted", "E": "It is permissible, because examination in chief allows any question about matters within the witness's own knowledge"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is a leading question, and the defence can object because it is not allowed on disputed matters without leave): A question that puts the desired answer into the witness's mouth is a leading question. In examination in chief leading questions are not permitted on contested matters unless the court gives leave, and the red jacket is disputed here. A yes or no format can still suggest the answer, so that argument fails. Hostility is relevant to when leave to lead may be given, not to whether the objection can be made."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Acacia Ltd holds a 20 year lease of a warehouse granted by Brookes Ltd. Acacia Ltd has granted a five year underlease of part of the warehouse to Zenith Ltd. Acacia Ltd then buys the freehold reversion from Brookes Ltd, and the transfer is completed and registered at HM Land Registry. Nothing is said about the lease or the underlease in the transfer. Which of the following best describes the effect on the lease and the underlease?", "options": {"A": "The lease merges with the freehold and ends, but Zenith's underlease survives and the freeholder takes subject to it", "B": "The lease continues until its contractual expiry, because merger takes effect only if the parties execute a deed of surrender", "C": "Both the lease and the underlease end, because the underlease depends on the lease for its existence and cannot outlast the lease under which it was granted", "D": "The lease and freehold merge only if Zenith Ltd consents, because the underlease is carved out of the lease", "E": "The lease merges and ends, and Zenith's underlease becomes a licence because no landlord's estate remains to support it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The lease merges with the freehold and ends, but Zenith's underlease survives and the freeholder takes subject to it): Merger occurs when the same person comes to hold both the lease and the immediate reversion, so the lease is extinguished unless a contrary intention is shown. Acacia Ltd now holds the freehold, which is the reversion on the underlease, so Zenith Ltd's underlease continues with Acacia Ltd as its landlord. The underlease does not depend on the continuation of the lease because the freeholder's estate now stands behind it. No deed of surrender is needed and the subtenant's consent is not required."}, {"id": 910, "domain": "land_law", "question": "In 2004 Frances Doyle sold part of her unregistered freehold garden to a neighbour, Owen Kemp, who covenanted for himself and his successors not to build on the plot. Frances registered nothing. In 2019 Owen sold the plot to Yasmin Chowdhury, who bought for full value, took the legal title and had no knowledge of the covenant. Yasmin begins building, and the land is still unregistered. Which of the following best describes whether Frances can enforce the covenant against Yasmin?", "options": {"A": "No, because a restrictive covenant is a Class D(ii) land charge and is void against a buyer of the legal estate for value if unregistered", "B": "Yes, because Yasmin's lack of knowledge does not matter and the burden of a restrictive covenant passes with the land to every successor", "C": "No, because the burden of a covenant cannot pass to successors in title at law and cannot be enforced against them in equity", "D": "Yes, because Frances can rely on the doctrine of notice, as Yasmin ought to have discovered the covenant from the title deeds", "E": "Yes, because Frances can register a Class D(ii) land charge now, which takes effect against Yasmin from the date of the original covenant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because a restrictive covenant is a Class D(ii) land charge and is void against a buyer of the legal estate for value if unregistered): In unregistered land, a restrictive covenant made after 1925 is a Class D(ii) land charge under the Land Charges Act 1972. Section 4(6) makes an unregistered D(ii) charge void against a purchaser of the legal estate for money or money's worth. The doctrine of notice has no role where the interest is registrable, so Yasmin's knowledge or lack of it does not matter. Registering now would not bind Yasmin, who has already purchased."}, {"id": 911, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Greyfriars Secure Services Ltd, a private company, runs a prison under a contract with the Ministry of Justice. A prisoner, Ben Walker, claims that officers employed by the company used excessive force on him, in breach of article 3. The company says it is a private body and so cannot be liable under the Human Rights Act 1998. Ben's solicitor considers a claim under section 7. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The company is a core public authority, so section 6 applies to everything it does, including its acts as an employer and landlord", "B": "The company is not caught by section 6, because only bodies established by statute can be public authorities for those purposes", "C": "Section 6 applies only to the Ministry of Justice, which alone remains answerable for the prison it has contracted out to others", "D": "The company is a hybrid public authority, so section 6 applies to its acts in running the prison, though not to its private acts", "E": "The company is a public authority only for acts done under statutory powers, not for acts under its contract"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The company is a hybrid public authority, so section 6 applies to its acts in running the prison, though not to its private acts): A private body that exercises functions of a public nature is a public authority under section 6(3)(b) in respect of those functions, but not for acts that are of a private nature. Running a prison is a public function, so the company's use of force on a prisoner is covered. It is a hybrid and not a core public authority, so its employment or property dealings are outside section 6. Contracting out does not remove the Convention duty."}, {"id": 912, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Imogen holds 40% of the shares in Pearce Ltd, a family company in which she was also a director until the other shareholders voted her out. Trust between the family members has broken down. The majority has offered to buy her shares at a fair value fixed by an independent accountant. Imogen refuses the offer and petitions for just and equitable winding up. What is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The petition is likely to succeed, because the court must order winding up once a shareholder shows that she has been removed as a director of the company", "B": "The petition is likely to fail, because she has unreasonably refused a fair offer to buy her shares, which is an adequate alternative remedy", "C": "The petition is likely to fail, because only a shareholder holding 50% or more of the shares may present such a petition", "D": "The petition is likely to succeed, because the breakdown of trust in a family company is enough to justify winding up", "E": "The petition is likely to fail, because a petition for winding up on the just and equitable ground is available only to creditors of the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The petition is likely to fail, because she has unreasonably refused a fair offer to buy her shares, which is an adequate alternative remedy): A contributory may petition for winding up on the just and equitable ground, and breakdown of trust in a quasi-partnership can justify it. However, the court will refuse the petition where the petitioner has unreasonably refused an adequate alternative remedy. A fair offer for her shares at an independent valuation is such a remedy. There is no minimum percentage, and the ground is not limited to creditors."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Oscar was convicted of robbery by a jury at the Crown Court ten days ago and sentenced to six years. His counsel advised after the trial that there are no arguable grounds of appeal. Oscar, who has served no previous custodial sentence, tells his solicitor, Bethan, that he wants to appeal. Bethan wants to act properly for him. Which of the following is the correct course?", "options": {"A": "Explain the 28 day time limit and the risk of a loss of time order, and prepare the application if he insists", "B": "Refuse to act further, because a solicitor must not pursue an appeal that counsel has advised has no merit", "C": "Lodge the notice immediately without further advice, because the appeal is his right and the risks need not be explained", "D": "Advise that the appeal must be made to the Crown Court on notice within 15 business days of conviction", "E": "Tell Oscar that the notice can be lodged within 56 days of sentence, so there is no urgency about acting"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain the 28 day time limit and the risk of a loss of time order, and prepare the application if he insists): Notice of an application for leave to appeal to the Court of Appeal must be given within 28 days of conviction or sentence. Bethan should advise honestly that counsel sees no arguable ground, that the court can order that time served is not counted if the application is hopeless, and that the decision to proceed is Oscar's. If he insists, she should help prepare the application in time. Refusing to act, or acting without advice, would fail her client care duties."}, {"id": 914, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Gareth signs a document stating, 'I hereby transfer my 1,000 shares in Delta Ltd to my friend Hana to hold on trust for my granddaughter Isla.' He signs no stock transfer form, gives nothing to Hana and tells nobody. Gareth dies a month later. His shares remain registered in his name, and his will leaves everything to his son. Isla claims that the shares belong to her. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Isla takes the shares, because a gift to a granddaughter is supported by natural love and affection as consideration", "B": "The trust is not constituted, because Gareth intended a transfer and a failed transfer is not read as a declaration", "C": "The trust is constituted, because Gareth's signed document is a declaration that he holds the shares on trust for Isla", "D": "The trust is constituted, because Hana became trustee when Gareth signed the document, even though she was not told", "E": "Isla takes the shares, because Gareth did everything within his own power and equity treats the gift as complete"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The trust is not constituted, because Gareth intended a transfer and a failed transfer is not read as a declaration): Gareth chose to create the trust by transferring the shares to Hana as trustee, and he never signed the transfer form that would have put legal title in her. Equity will not construe a failed transfer as an alternative declaration of trust by the settlor. The exception in Re Rose does not help, because Gareth had not done everything in his power. Isla gave no consideration, and love and affection do not count, so she cannot enforce the incomplete gift."}, {"id": 915, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Wexley Retail Park comprises twenty shop units. The freehold is owned by an overseas investment company that has never visited the site. Bayside Management Ltd is appointed to manage the car park, walkways and lighting under a contract and employs staff who patrol, clean and maintain these areas daily. A shopper trips on a broken paving slab in the walkway. Which of the following best describes who is an occupier of the walkway for the purposes of the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957?", "options": {"A": "The tenants of the shop units, because they benefit most from the visitors who use the walkway to reach their shops", "B": "Whichever party is named on the insurance policy covering the park, because the insured party is treated as the occupier", "C": "The overseas investment company only, because occupier status depends on ownership of the freehold, and that company holds the title to the whole park", "D": "No person, because a walkway used by the public at large is not occupied by any individual or company", "E": "Bayside Management Ltd, because an occupier is a person with sufficient control over the premises, which does not depend on owning the land"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Bayside Management Ltd, because an occupier is a person with sufficient control over the premises, which does not depend on owning the land): Occupation turns on control over the premises, not on title, so a person with sufficient control over the state of the land owes the duty. Bayside manages, patrols and maintains the walkway, so it has the necessary control. More than one person can be an occupier, but the owner company, with no day to day involvement, is not shown to share that control on these facts. The tenants control only their own units, and the name on an insurance policy has no bearing on the question."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Redfern Logistics Ltd has missed a deadline in a case management order made on a contested application, and a sanction has followed under the rules. Its solicitor, Priya, is asked by the managing director how the court will decide an application for relief from that sanction. The director hopes the court will begin by looking at how strong the company's underlying claim is. Priya must explain the approach the court takes under CPR 3.9 and the guidance in Denton. Which of the following correctly states that approach?", "options": {"A": "The court asks whether the other party has suffered actual financial loss and grants relief if none, without any wider consideration of the case", "B": "The court decides whether the breach was deliberate, then whether the applicant has offered to pay costs, then whether the trial date is at risk", "C": "The court considers whether the applicant's solicitor was at fault and refuses relief where the fault lay with the legal representative rather than the client", "D": "The court examines the merits of the underlying claim first and refuses relief where the claim is weak, whatever the nature of the breach", "E": "The court assesses how serious the breach is, then why it happened, then weighs all the circumstances to deal with the application justly"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court assesses how serious the breach is, then why it happened, then weighs all the circumstances to deal with the application justly): The Denton approach has three stages: how serious or significant the breach is, why the default occurred, and then all the circumstances of the case, giving particular weight to the need for litigation to be conducted efficiently and at proportionate cost and to enforce compliance with rules and orders. The merits of the claim are not the starting point. Fault by a legal representative is a factor within the reason for the default, not a free standing bar to relief."}, {"id": 917, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "You act for Stratton Gym Ltd. Its membership terms state that it 'accepts no liability for any injury, loss or damage however caused to members using its equipment'. A member, Joel, was injured when a worn cable snapped on a weights machine after the gym failed to maintain it, and a laptop he carried was damaged. The terms were properly incorporated. Stratton's director says members rarely read the terms and asks you to leave the clause as drafted. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The clause is effective if clearly drafted and properly incorporated, so it can remain in the membership forms unchanged for the new season", "B": "The clause is effective against both the injury and the laptop claims, because members accept the risk by choosing to join", "C": "The clause cannot exclude liability for negligently caused injury, so it should be redrafted, and leaving it risks misleading members about their rights", "D": "The clause is void in all respects, so it should be withdrawn and Joel's claims will succeed in full", "E": "The clause can remain, provided the forms also invite members to read it and sign an acknowledgement of the exclusion"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The clause cannot exclude liability for negligently caused injury, so it should be redrafted, and leaving it risks misleading members about their rights): In a consumer contract a term cannot exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence, however clearly it is drafted or incorporated. Other loss, such as damage to the laptop, is subject to the fairness test and not an automatic ban. A solicitor must act in the client's best interests and not mislead others, so the gym should not keep a term that gives members a false impression. Redrafting is therefore the correct advice."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Oliver Grant is buying his first home for £240,000 with a loan of £192,000. He wants the lowest possible monthly payments and has an investment fund that he expects will mature to pay off the capital at the end of the term. He does not intend to reduce the debt during the term. Which type of mortgage matches his wishes?", "options": {"A": "An offset mortgage, where his savings are set against the loan so that he pays interest only on the balance after deduction", "B": "A fixed-rate mortgage, where the rate is fixed for the term so his monthly payments stay at the same level throughout", "C": "An interest-only mortgage, where he pays only interest each month and must separately provide a way of repaying the capital at the end", "D": "A repayment mortgage, where each payment covers interest and some capital so that the debt reduces to nil by the end of the term", "E": "A tracker mortgage, where the interest rate follows a chosen base rate and the capital is repaid with the interest each month"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An interest-only mortgage, where he pays only interest each month and must separately provide a way of repaying the capital at the end): With an interest-only mortgage the borrower pays only the interest during the term, so the monthly cost is lowest, and the capital is repaid at the end from another source such as Oliver's fund. A repayment mortgage reduces the debt every month. Tracker and fixed-rate loans describe how the interest rate is set, not whether capital is repaid, and an offset mortgage reduces interest by netting savings against the balance."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Halden Marine Ltd has issued a claim for £250,000 against Sergio Marin, an individual whose home is in Spain, and permission to serve out of the jurisdiction has already been granted. Mr Marin has responded to pre-action letters by email but has refused to provide a postal address and told Halden's solicitor that he will not accept service. The solicitor believes that Mr Marin reads the email address he uses each day. Two months remain of the claim form's validity. What is the best course?", "options": {"A": "Apply to dispense with service under the rules, because the defendant is deliberately avoiding service and cannot be found by the claimant", "B": "Serve by first class post to his last known address in England, because that remains good service on a defendant who has left", "C": "Wait until the claim form validity has nearly ended, then apply for an extension so that the court can approve service by email", "D": "Serve by email, because Mr Marin has used email and is therefore taken to have agreed to accept service that way", "E": "Apply to the court for an order authorising service by email, relying on Mr Marin's evasion as the good reason"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Apply to the court for an order authorising service by email, relying on Mr Marin's evasion as the good reason): The court may authorise service by an alternative method where there is a good reason, and a defendant's deliberate evasion of service together with a working email address is such a reason. The application should be made now, while time remains, rather than after a failed attempt. Using email without an order is ineffective because Mr Marin has not agreed in writing to accept service that way. Dispensing with service is exceptional and is not needed when a workable alternative exists."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A new paralegal at a firm in Bristol asks which of her tasks involve a reserved legal activity under the Legal Services Act 2007. Her supervisor explains that only some legal work is reserved, and that carrying on a reserved legal activity without authorisation or an exemption is a criminal offence, while the remainder of legal work can be undertaken by anyone. Which of the following tasks is a reserved legal activity?", "options": {"A": "Acting as mediator between two neighbours in a boundary dispute where no court proceedings have been issued", "B": "Preparing a transfer deed for registered land, to be lodged at HM Land Registry, on a sale of a client's house", "C": "Drafting a will for an elderly client and advising her on how the gift of her house will take effect", "D": "Advising a company on the likely merits of a breach of contract claim and the level of damages it might recover", "E": "Sending a letter before claim to a debtor and negotiating a settlement of the debt on behalf of the client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Preparing a transfer deed for registered land, to be lodged at HM Land Registry, on a sale of a client's house): Reserved instrument activity includes preparing a transfer of land that is to be registered, so only an authorised person or someone exempt may do it. Will drafting is not reserved, although preparing the papers for a grant of probate is. Conduct of litigation covers issuing proceedings and formal steps in them, so pre-action letters and negotiation fall outside it. Legal advice and mediation can be provided by anyone."}, {"id": 921, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Pell Marine agreed to refit a client's yacht for £60,000. The client paid £20,000 on signing. The contract was frustrated on 1 July, before the refit was finished. Before that date Pell spent £9,000 on materials and labour in preparation, and the client obtained no valuable benefit from that work. The Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 applies and gives the court a discretion over any sum Pell may keep. Which of the following best states the likely position?", "options": {"A": "Pell may keep the £20,000 as of right, because its expenses were incurred before frustration and the Act protects the payee's expectation loss", "B": "The client can recover £11,000 only, because the Act requires the price to be apportioned rateably to the work done up to frustration", "C": "Pell may retain £9,000 as of right, because the Act entitles the payee to deduct all expenses incurred before the frustrating event", "D": "The client can recover the £20,000 less a sum the court considers just for Pell's expenses, up to £9,000, so at least £11,000 is repayable", "E": "The client can recover the £20,000 in full, because the Act requires repayment of money paid and does not allow expenses to be set off against it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The client can recover the £20,000 less a sum the court considers just for Pell's expenses, up to £9,000, so at least £11,000 is repayable): Under section 1(2) of the 1943 Act, money paid before frustration is recoverable, but the court may allow the payee to retain or recover a just sum for expenses incurred, limited to the sum paid or payable. Pell's expenses of £9,000 are the ceiling on what it may keep, so the client recovers at least £11,000. The allowance is discretionary, so retention is not automatic. There is also no rule that the price is apportioned rateably, so the proposed £11,000 figure is a minimum rather than a fixed result."}, {"id": 922, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Rosa and Miguel Santos are buying a freehold house with a mortgage. They want certainty about their payments for five years because their budget is tight, and they expect interest rates to rise. They are considering a five-year fixed rate and a variable rate tracking the Bank of England base rate. Which statement best describes the effect of choosing the fixed rate?", "options": {"A": "Their payments will stay level until the lender decides to raise its standard variable rate during the fixed period", "B": "Their payments will follow the base rate for five years, so they will gain only if rates stay below the lender's rate", "C": "Their payments will stay level for five years, and they can repay or remortgage during that period without any charge or penalty", "D": "Their payments will fall automatically if the base rate falls, and the lender may charge a fee only if the property is sold", "E": "Their payments will stay level for five years, but an early repayment charge will probably apply if they repay or remortgage then"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Their payments will stay level for five years, but an early repayment charge will probably apply if they repay or remortgage then): A fixed rate protects the borrower from rises in the rate for the fixed period, so the payments are certain, which suits a tight budget. The price of that certainty is an early repayment charge if the loan is repaid or moved before the period ends. A tracker follows the base rate and can rise or fall, and a standard variable rate belongs to the lender's discretion, so neither gives the certainty the Santos clients want."}, {"id": 923, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Under a trust of shares set up by her late father, Lena has a vested interest in the income for life. The trustees are Mr Hart and Mrs Ng. Lena telephones Mr Hart and says she wants her interest to be held for her nephew Mo from now on. Mr Hart agrees and makes a note of the call. Nothing is signed. Lena later changes her mind and claims her interest back. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Lena keeps her interest, because the trustees did not both agree and every trustee must consent to a disposition", "B": "Mo takes the interest, because an oral direction to a trustee is enough to transfer an equitable interest in shares", "C": "Mo takes the interest, because Lena's call was an oral declaration of a new trust of personalty, which needs no formalities", "D": "Mo takes the interest, because the trustee's file note of the call is written evidence signed by a person with authority", "E": "Lena keeps her interest, because disposing of a subsisting equitable interest must be in writing signed by the disposer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Lena keeps her interest, because disposing of a subsisting equitable interest must be in writing signed by the disposer): Lena is disposing of an existing equitable interest, which under section 53(1)(c) of the Law of Property Act 1925 must be in writing and signed by Lena or her authorised agent. A telephone call and an unsigned note by the trustee do not satisfy that. Lena is not creating a new trust of property she owns outright, so the informal rule for declarations of trusts of personalty does not help Mo. Trustee consent is not the issue, and the disposition fails for lack of formality."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Hale Agrochem Ltd stores 20,000 litres of weedkiller in a tank at its depot, in an industrial area. Without any negligence, a corroded valve fails and the weedkiller flows through a drain onto Yvonne's neighbouring farm, killing her organic crop. Weedkiller of this type is known to destroy crops on contact. Yvonne sues Hale under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Yvonne succeeds, but only if she can also show that Hale was in breach of an environmental statute", "B": "Yvonne fails, because lawful commercial storage in an industrial area is an ordinary use of land to which the rule cannot apply", "C": "Yvonne fails, because the rule covers only the escape of water, fire or gas and not a liquid chemical", "D": "Yvonne is likely to succeed, because bulk storage of such chemicals is a non-natural use and the escape caused foreseeable crop damage", "E": "Yvonne fails, because the rule applies only where the defendant has been negligent, and Hale took reasonable care"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yvonne is likely to succeed, because bulk storage of such chemicals is a non-natural use and the escape caused foreseeable crop damage): Bulk storage of chemicals capable of destroying crops is a classic example of a non-natural use of land, even in an industrial area, because it creates an exceptional risk if the thing escapes. The weedkiller escaped from Hale's land onto Yvonne's, and the destruction of crops was foreseeable damage. The rule is one of strict liability, so Hale's reasonable care does not defeat it, and it is not limited to water, fire or gas. A breach of statute is not an element."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Tindall Estates, a landlord whose letting agent is a RICS member, is negotiating a lease of a small shop with Amir, a sole trader with no legal experience. Amir's solicitor notes that the agent has not provided a copy of the Code for Leasing Business Premises or any heads of terms. The agent says the Code is only a voluntary guide that the landlord can ignore. Which of the following best describes the status of the Code?", "options": {"A": "It binds every landlord by implication into the lease, because it forms part of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954", "B": "It is a code of practice of the Solicitors Regulation Authority, so only solicitors acting for landlords can be disciplined for ignoring it", "C": "It is not law, but a RICS professional statement that members are expected to follow, so non-compliance may lead to regulatory action", "D": "It is a voluntary guide with no consequence for the agent, because only the landlord is bound by the terms of the lease", "E": "It is a statutory code, so a landlord who ignores it commits an offence and the lease may be set aside"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is not law, but a RICS professional statement that members are expected to follow, so non-compliance may lead to regulatory action): The Code for Leasing Business Premises is not legislation and cannot be enforced as a legal term of the lease. It is a RICS professional statement, so a RICS member such as the letting agent is expected to comply and may face regulatory consequences if not. It is not issued by the SRA and does not form part of the 1954 Act. Amir's solicitor can still use it to press for good practice."}, {"id": 926, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ms Pereira is injured at work and sues her employer, Calder Foods Ltd, claiming that its safe system of work was inadequate. Calder's solicitor reviews documents provided by the client and finds an internal safety audit, written two years before the accident, warning that the machine in question lacked adequate guarding. The document is unhelpful to Calder. The managing director tells the solicitor to leave it out when disclosure is given because it is not mentioned in the pleadings. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Withhold the audit, because only documents that support a party's own case need be disclosed under the standard rules", "B": "Withhold the audit unless the claimant makes a specific request for it, as the duty depends on a request", "C": "Disclose the audit only if the court orders specific disclosure, as standard disclosure is a matter of discretion", "D": "Disclose the audit but claim privilege over it, because it was created for the company's own internal purposes", "E": "Explain that the audit must be disclosed because it adversely affects Calder's case, and cease acting if the client refuses"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Explain that the audit must be disclosed because it adversely affects Calder's case, and cease acting if the client refuses): Standard disclosure requires a party to disclose documents on which it relies and those that adversely affect its own case or support another party's case. The audit falls within the second category, whether or not the pleadings mention it. A solicitor's duty to the court overrides the client's instructions, so the solicitor must advise disclosure and must stop acting if the client insists on withholding it. An internal safety audit is not privileged merely because it is internal."}, {"id": 927, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Rutherford Gears Ltd is a private company whose shares are not traded on any market. It has just bought back 5 per cent of its ordinary shares from a former employee, paying for them out of distributable profits and following the correct procedure. The directors want to keep the shares in the company's name and resell them next year to new managers. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The shares can be held in treasury, but only for twelve months, after which they must either be resold or cancelled by the company", "B": "They cannot be held in treasury because only shares traded on a recognised market qualify, so they must be cancelled", "C": "The shares can be held in treasury if the articles expressly allow it and the shareholders approve by special resolution", "D": "The shares can be held in treasury, because they were bought out of distributable profits and the company may resell them for cash", "E": "The shares must be cancelled, because any shares bought back from a former employee must be cancelled whatever their later use"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They cannot be held in treasury because only shares traded on a recognised market qualify, so they must be cancelled): A company may hold bought-back shares in treasury only if they are qualifying shares, meaning shares listed or traded on a recognised market, and they are bought out of distributable profits. Rutherford's shares are not traded, so they cannot be held in treasury whatever the articles say. The company must cancel them and issue new shares later if it wishes to give shares to managers. The fact that the seller was an employee is not the reason."}, {"id": 928, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "An Act empowers the Secretary of State to make regulations on the eligibility of claimants for a disability benefit but does not require any particular eligibility rules. The Secretary of State makes regulations that deny the benefit to claimants living in care homes. Marta Novak, who lives in a care home, says the regulations breach her Convention rights, and her solicitor considers a claim. Which of the following is the best advice about the court's powers?", "options": {"A": "The court can only read the regulations compatibly under section 3, because quashing is reserved to Parliament and not available to a court", "B": "The court can only make a declaration of incompatibility, because a court has no power to quash any subordinate legislation made by a minister or department", "C": "The regulations are valid, because they were made under a power given by an Act, which makes them part of primary legislation", "D": "The court can quash the regulations as unlawful, because the Act does not require the incompatible rule, so a declaration of incompatibility is not the proper remedy", "E": "Marta must apply to the European Court of Human Rights, because domestic courts have no power over Convention incompatibility"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court can quash the regulations as unlawful, because the Act does not require the incompatible rule, so a declaration of incompatibility is not the proper remedy): Subordinate legislation that is incompatible with a Convention right is unlawful under the Act and can be quashed or disapplied, unless the enabling primary legislation prevents the incompatibility being removed. Here the Act leaves the eligibility rules to the Secretary of State and does not demand the exclusion, so the court has full power over the regulations. A declaration of incompatibility is reserved for cases where primary legislation itself stands in the way. Regulations do not become primary legislation, and domestic courts can grant remedies first."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trustee transfers a valuable painting, which belongs to the trust, to a dealer in breach of trust. The dealer pays a full market price of £90,000, believes the trustee had authority to sell, and has no reason to suspect otherwise. The trustee spends the money and has no other assets. The beneficiaries want to recover the painting. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "They can recover the painting from the dealer, since an equitable interest in trust property binds every person to whom it is transferred", "B": "They can recover the painting only if they first pay the dealer the £90,000 that he paid for it", "C": "They can follow the painting only if they first sue the trustee and that claim fails because he cannot pay", "D": "They cannot follow the painting from the dealer, a purchaser for value without notice, but they may sue the trustee personally", "E": "The dealer holds the painting on constructive trust, since receipt of trust property in any circumstances creates a liability to the trust"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They cannot follow the painting from the dealer, a purchaser for value without notice, but they may sue the trustee personally): A bona fide purchaser of the legal estate for value without notice takes free of the beneficiaries' equitable interest, so following the painting against the dealer fails. The beneficiaries are left with personal claims against the trustee and may be able to trace into any proceeds still identifiable in his hands. The dealer's good faith also means he is not liable as a constructive trustee. There is no duty to sue the trustee first."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Weston Print Ltd is insolvent and its directors have convened a members' meeting. The members passed a special resolution to wind the company up voluntarily and nominated Ms Dalby as liquidator. The creditors' decision procedure that followed nominated Mr Ahmed instead. Nobody has applied to the court to challenge the position. Which of the following states who becomes the liquidator?", "options": {"A": "Neither takes office, because competing nominations require the court to appoint an independent liquidator from the official receiver's list", "B": "Mr Ahmed becomes liquidator only if the court first approves his nomination, because a creditors' nominee needs the court's consent before he can act as liquidator", "C": "Mr Ahmed becomes liquidator, because the creditors' nominee takes office unless a director or member successfully applies to the court within seven days", "D": "Ms Dalby becomes liquidator, because the directors convened the meeting and the company's wishes prevail in an insolvent liquidation", "E": "Ms Dalby becomes liquidator, because the members' nominee takes office where the members passed the winding up resolution first"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mr Ahmed becomes liquidator, because the creditors' nominee takes office unless a director or member successfully applies to the court within seven days): In a creditors' voluntary liquidation, if the creditors and the members nominate different people, the creditors' nominee is the liquidator. A director or member may apply to the court within seven days of the creditors' nomination for the members' nominee, or another person, to be appointed instead. Here no application has been made, so Mr Ahmed takes office without any need for the court's approval. The members' special resolution begins the winding up but does not decide who conducts it."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Silverlake Ltd has two shareholders, Ms Quinn and Mr Tate, each owning 50 per cent of its shares. They instruct a firm of solicitors, which has no Financial Conduct Authority authorisation, to advise on and negotiate a sale of all the shares to Moreland plc, which will take control of the business. The firm will also prepare the share transfer forms. The partner asks whether the firm is carrying on a regulated activity. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "No, because the sale of a body corporate exclusion applies where the buyer takes day-to-day control of the company's affairs", "B": "No, because the exclusion applies where the sellers hold less than half of the shares, as that shows the sale is not a business disposal", "C": "Yes, because arranging the transfer of shares in any company is arranging deals in investments, which needs authorisation", "D": "Yes, because the exclusion is limited to sales of shares in public companies listed on a recognised investment exchange", "E": "No, because the firm is a firm of solicitors, and solicitors are exempt from the general prohibition whatever work they do"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because the sale of a body corporate exclusion applies where the buyer takes day-to-day control of the company's affairs): Advising on and arranging a share sale would ordinarily be a regulated activity, but the Regulated Activities Order excludes a sale of a body corporate where the buyer acquires day-to-day control of the company. The exclusion covers sales of private company shares and is not limited to listed companies. It applies here because Moreland plc will take control of the business through the purchase of all the shares. Being a firm of solicitors does not create an exemption, so the firm relies on the exclusion itself."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Sana is a chartered accountant and the finance director of Tern Ltd, a private company. She signed off the annual accounts without checking whether the quarterly VAT returns had been filed. They had not, and HMRC imposed penalties of £25,000 on the company. Sana says she should be judged only against the general knowledge, skill and experience expected of a director carrying out her functions. Which of the following best describes the standard applied to Sana under section 174 of the Companies Act 2006?", "options": {"A": "She is judged only by what a reasonable layperson would know, because the standard is the same for every director of a private company", "B": "She is judged by the standard of a reasonable accountant in practice, because professional rules replace the statutory test for directors who hold professional qualifications", "C": "She is judged by the higher of the objective standard for her role and the actual skill and experience she holds as an accountant", "D": "She is judged by the lower of her own skill and the general standard, because she was entitled to rely on other staff to file the returns", "E": "She is judged only by her own actual knowledge and skill, so the standard of a reasonable director in her position is irrelevant to the claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She is judged by the higher of the objective standard for her role and the actual skill and experience she holds as an accountant): Section 174 sets a combined test: the care, skill and diligence of a reasonable person with the general knowledge, skill and experience expected of someone in her functions, and also those she actually has. The standard applied is the higher of the two, so Sana's accountancy qualification raises the bar. The objective limb is a floor and never replaces her own skill. Her argument that only the general standard applies therefore fails on these facts."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Warren Platt, aged 30, is charged with robbery, an offence triable only on indictment. He is brought before the magistrates' court at his first hearing. The prosecutor has served the initial details of the prosecution case, and Warren has said that he intends to plead not guilty. His solicitor wants to know what will happen. Which of the following correctly describes the procedure that applies at that hearing?", "options": {"A": "The court may keep the case itself if both prosecution and defence agree to summary trial of the robbery", "B": "The court sends the case to the Crown Court only after he has indicated a not guilty plea at the hearing", "C": "The court conducts committal proceedings to test whether there is sufficient evidence to justify trial in the Crown Court", "D": "The court takes his plea and, on a not guilty plea, holds an allocation hearing to decide where he will be tried", "E": "The court sends the case to the Crown Court straight away, without taking a plea or considering mode of trial"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court sends the case to the Crown Court straight away, without taking a plea or considering mode of trial): Robbery is an indictable only offence, so under section 51 of the Crime and Disorder Act 1998 the magistrates' court must send the defendant forthwith to the Crown Court for trial. The magistrates do not take a plea, hold an allocation hearing or consider mode of trial, since the Crown Court alone can try the offence. Committal proceedings are not part of the procedure for an indictable only offence. Agreement between the parties cannot confer summary jurisdiction on the magistrates."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A fund is held on trust 'for Anna for life, and after her death for Brian absolutely'. Both are adults. The fund is invested and produces £6,000 of income each year. Brian says he is entitled to half the income each year, because he is entitled to the capital. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Anna is entitled to the income, but Brian's interest is contingent until she dies and so is not yet vested", "B": "The trustees may choose how to divide the income between them, because the trust gives them a power to do so", "C": "Brian is entitled to half the income, because a vested interest in the capital carries a right to income now", "D": "Anna is entitled to all the income during her life, and Brian has a vested interest in remainder in the capital", "E": "Anna and Brian share the income equally, because each has a fixed interest under a fixed trust"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anna is entitled to all the income during her life, and Brian has a vested interest in remainder in the capital): A trust for A for life and then B absolutely gives successive fixed interests. Anna holds a vested interest in possession and takes all the income while she lives, and Brian holds a vested interest in remainder, whose enjoyment is postponed. His interest is not contingent because nothing but Anna's death has to happen. No discretion is given to the trustees, and the interests are not concurrent, so no share of income passes to Brian now."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Your client, Mr Faraday, told his niece orally two years ago that he held his flat on trust for her. He now asks you to prepare a document recording the trust and dated with that earlier date, so that it can be shown to his mortgage lender. He says the arrangement was real and he simply never wrote it down. His niece lives abroad and has not asked for anything. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Prepare it with the earlier date but add a note that it was written later, which removes any risk of misleading the lender", "B": "Prepare the document with the earlier date, because the trust genuinely existed from then and the date only records the truth", "C": "Decline to prepare any document, because a trust declared orally cannot be recorded in writing after the event", "D": "Prepare a document carrying the true current date that records the earlier oral declaration, and decline to backdate it", "E": "Prepare an undated document, leaving the client to insert whichever date he considers accurate before it is used"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prepare a document carrying the true current date that records the earlier oral declaration, and decline to backdate it): Written evidence of an earlier declaration of trust can be created later, but the document must be honest about when it was made. Backdating would be misleading and would breach the duty not to mislead others or abuse a position. A document dated today that says an oral declaration was made two years ago serves the client and the niece and deceives nobody. Leaving the date blank only invites the client to make the same false statement."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Eastgate Foods Ltd agreed in 2023 to supply Marlow Cafes Ltd with all its coffee beans for three years at a fixed price of £9 per kilo. The price clause is clear, and both sides negotiated it with legal advice. Bean prices have since doubled. Eastgate, which has a large depot in London, now argues that commercial common sense requires the clause to be read as allowing a reasonable increase. Marlow insists on the fixed price. How will the court most likely approach the clause?", "options": {"A": "It will apply the words literally and disregard the factual background known to both parties when they contracted, as the clause was drafted with advice", "B": "It will look at the parties' pre-contract negotiations to decide what each side privately intended the price clause to mean, and give effect to Eastgate's intention", "C": "It will give the words their natural meaning in context, and will not use commercial common sense to rescue Eastgate from a bad bargain", "D": "It will read the clause against Eastgate as drafter and treat the price as capable of rising with the market, because ambiguity is resolved against the supplier", "E": "It will imply a term allowing a reasonable price increase, because the contract cannot work commercially if the supplier loses money on each kilo"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It will give the words their natural meaning in context, and will not use commercial common sense to rescue Eastgate from a bad bargain): The modern approach asks what a reasonable person with the background knowledge of the parties would take the words to mean, giving the clear language its natural meaning. Commercial common sense is a check where wording is open to two readings, not a tool to rewrite a clear term that has turned out badly for one side. Negotiations are inadmissible to show what the parties meant. The reading against the drafter and implied term arguments fail because the clause is clear and the contract works without any added term."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Baby Isla is born prematurely and needs oxygen. A junior doctor negligently allows her to receive excess oxygen for several hours. Isla later develops a serious eye condition. Medical evidence shows that the excess oxygen is one of five separate possible causes of the condition, each as likely as the others, and that premature babies commonly develop it whatever the treatment. Isla's parents sue the hospital. Which of the following best describes the effect of this evidence on causation?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because a premature baby is a vulnerable claimant and the hospital must prove it did not cause the harm", "B": "The claim fails unless Isla proves the excess oxygen probably caused or materially contributed, as increased risk is not enough", "C": "The claim fails, because where there are several possible causes the court must reject the claim on causation grounds in clinical cases", "D": "The claim succeeds, because the negligent oxygen exposure created a risk of the condition and so shifts the burden to the hospital", "E": "The claim succeeds in part, because there was a one in five chance that the excess oxygen caused the condition"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim fails unless Isla proves the excess oxygen probably caused or materially contributed, as increased risk is not enough): The burden of proving causation stays on the claimant. Where there are several possible causes, showing that the defendant's breach merely increased the risk is not enough outside the narrow exception for mesothelioma. Isla must show that the excess oxygen probably caused or materially contributed to the condition, and the evidence does not do that. A court does not however reject every such claim automatically, since evidence could still tip the balance."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Salma Qureshi is injured in a workplace accident. Liability is admitted, with damages to be assessed, but the defendant argues that Salma was 25 percent responsible. Her solicitor values the claim at £120,000 before any reduction for contributory negligence. The defendant is insured and there is no counterclaim or set off. Salma applies for an interim payment. What is the proper approach to the amount?", "options": {"A": "The sum the defendant offers to pay, because the court cannot order more than the defendant volunteers", "B": "A reasonable proportion of £90,000, because the likely judgment is first reduced to take account of contributory negligence", "C": "A reasonable proportion of £120,000, because contributory negligence is a matter for the final trial and not the interim stage", "D": "One half of £90,000 as a fixed amount, because interim payments are capped at half the likely sum", "E": "The full £90,000, because once liability is admitted the court orders the whole likely judgment after the reduction"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A reasonable proportion of £90,000, because the likely judgment is first reduced to take account of contributory negligence): The court may order an interim payment of no more than a reasonable proportion of the likely amount of the final judgment, and in assessing that figure it must take into account contributory negligence and any set off or counterclaim. The likely judgment here is £90,000 after the 25 percent reduction, so the proportion is taken from that sum. Using £120,000 ignores the deduction. The whole likely sum is never ordered, and there is no fixed one half cap."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ms Ford died intestate, unmarried, with no civil partner. She had three children: Lucy, aged 20, Mark, aged 17 and unmarried, and Nina, who died last year leaving two sons, Oliver and Pete, both under 18. Ms Ford's net estate is £240,000. How is the estate divided between the family members under the statutory trusts?", "options": {"A": "Lucy and Mark take one third each and Oliver and Pete one sixth each, with the shares of Mark, Oliver and Pete held until 18", "B": "Lucy, Mark, Oliver and Pete take one quarter each, because issue living at death share equally", "C": "Lucy takes everything now, because Mark, Oliver and Pete are under 18 and cannot hold property", "D": "Lucy and Mark take half each, because grandchildren take only if every child has predeceased", "E": "Lucy and Mark take one third each and Nina's third passes to them as the surviving siblings"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lucy and Mark take one third each and Oliver and Pete one sixth each, with the shares of Mark, Oliver and Pete held until 18): On the statutory trusts the estate is divided per stirpes among the children, so there are three shares, with Nina's third passing to her children. Oliver and Pete therefore take a sixth each. Interests vest at 18 or earlier marriage or civil partnership, so Mark's, Oliver's and Pete's shares are held on trust until then, while Lucy takes hers absolutely. Dividing in quarters would ignore the per stirpes rule."}, {"id": 940, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Your firm acts for Nina, who is buying a flat for £300,000, and for her lender, using the lender's standard instructions. Nina tells you in confidence that the seller has agreed to give her £30,000 back in cash after completion, which the lender has not been told about, and that she has declared the full price on the mortgage application. She asks you to say nothing. Which of the following is the correct course of action?", "options": {"A": "Disclose the information to the lender at once without telling Nina, because the duty to the lender overrides her confidentiality", "B": "Continue to act for the lender only and refer Nina to another firm, without disclosing the cash back to either", "C": "Explain that the information must be disclosed to the lender, and stop acting for both clients if Nina refuses to allow disclosure", "D": "Complete the purchase for Nina and tell the lender only that you have no knowledge of any matter affecting its security", "E": "Keep the information confidential and complete, because Nina is the client of longer standing and the lender has the protection of its own valuation"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain that the information must be disclosed to the lender, and stop acting for both clients if Nina refuses to allow disclosure): The firm owes duties to both clients and the cash back is material to the lender's decision to lend, so it must be disclosed. The proper approach is to tell Nina that disclosure is needed and to seek her consent. If she refuses, the confidentiality owed to her conflicts with the duty to the lender, and the firm cannot continue for both. Misleading the lender, or disclosing secretly, would breach the Code, and continuing for the lender alone would still leave the firm holding information it could not use."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Brightwater Finance Ltd lent £300,000 to Orchard Bakery Ltd under a debenture creating fixed and floating charges, executed on 3 March. A trainee at the solicitors' firm acting for the lender was to register it at Companies House but did not do so. On 8 April the supervising solicitor discovers this. The company is still trading and paying interest. The supervising solicitor is worried about the firm's position. What is the correct approach?", "options": {"A": "Register the charge immediately and say nothing to the lender, because late registration cures the problem and the company is still paying interest", "B": "Tell the lender promptly, but explain that the charge is void against the company itself and the debt cannot now be recovered from it", "C": "Tell the lender promptly and advise that the charge remains fully effective, since registration affects only priority and not validity against a liquidator", "D": "Register the charge now and tell the lender only if the company later enters insolvency, because until then the client has suffered no loss", "E": "Tell the lender promptly about the missed deadline, and seek a court order extending time, since the charge is vulnerable if the company becomes insolvent"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Tell the lender promptly about the missed deadline, and seek a court order extending time, since the charge is vulnerable if the company becomes insolvent): The 21 day period for registering the charge has expired, so the charge is void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors unless the court extends time. The debt remains payable and the charge is valid against the company, which is why the risk matters mainly on insolvency. The firm has made an error that may harm the client, so the solicitor must promptly tell the lender and put matters right, including considering the firm's own conflict and insurance position. Waiting for a loss to appear or filing quietly would breach the duty to be open with the client."}, {"id": 942, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Olufemi Adeyemi died owning a house and some investments. His executors, Chidi and Nneka, are concerned that creditors or other claimants of whom they are unaware may emerge after they have distributed the estate. They have heard that personal representatives can protect themselves by giving notice of their intention to distribute. They ask the solicitor what notices are needed to gain the protection of the Trustee Act 1925 against claims they know nothing about. Which of the following correctly states what is required?", "options": {"A": "Individual letters to every creditor identified from the deceased's papers, giving three months, because public notices suit only unknown beneficiaries", "B": "A notice in the London Gazette only, giving at least one month for claims, because local newspaper advertising is optional for a solvent estate", "C": "Notices in the London Gazette and a local newspaper where land is situated, each giving at least two months for claims, plus reasonable searches", "D": "A notice in a local newspaper where land is situated, giving at least two months, because the London Gazette is used only for insolvent estates", "E": "Notices in the London Gazette and a local newspaper, giving at least six months for claims, which is the usual period for a claim on an estate"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Notices in the London Gazette and a local newspaper where land is situated, each giving at least two months for claims, plus reasonable searches): To protect themselves under section 27 of the Trustee Act 1925, personal representatives advertise in the London Gazette and in a newspaper circulating where any land is situated, giving at least two months for claims, and make the reasonable searches the circumstances call for. The Gazette is needed for all estates and one notice alone is not enough. The protection covers only claims of which the representatives had no notice, so known creditors must still be dealt with directly."}, {"id": 943, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Karim Ahmed wants to buy a flat in London. The seller holds the flat under a lease granted by the freeholder for 99 years, with about 70 years unexpired. The title is currently unregistered. Karim's solicitor advises that completion of the purchase will trigger compulsory first registration of the lease. Which of the following best explains why registration is compulsory in this case?", "options": {"A": "A transfer of a lease with more than twenty one years left to run triggers first registration under the 1925 legislation", "B": "A transfer of any lease, whatever its remaining term, triggers first registration under section 4 of the Land Registration Act 2002", "C": "A transfer of a lease triggers first registration only if the freehold is itself registered at the time of transfer", "D": "A transfer of a lease with more than seven years left to run triggers compulsory first registration under the 2002 Act", "E": "A transfer of a lease of any length triggers registration only when the flat is bought with a mortgage from a regulated lender"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A transfer of a lease with more than seven years left to run triggers compulsory first registration under the 2002 Act): Section 4(1) of the Land Registration Act 2002 makes first registration compulsory on the transfer for valuable consideration of a leasehold estate with more than seven years left to run. Here about seventy years remain, so the trigger is met on completion. The rule is not tied to the lender, and the freeholder's registration status does not matter. A lease of seven years or fewer is not compulsorily registrable on transfer."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Zack Hollis gave his son £200,000 in cash five years and two months ago. Zack died last week having made no other lifetime gifts, and his death estate is worth £520,000. The nil rate band is £325,000 and the rate of Inheritance Tax on death is 40 per cent. The executors ask the solicitor whether taper relief is likely to help Zack's son with any tax on the gift. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "No, because taper relief applies only to chargeable lifetime transfers and not to potentially exempt transfers that become chargeable on death", "B": "Yes, because the gift was made more than four years before death, so its value is cut by forty per cent before tax is calculated", "C": "No tax arises on the gift as it is within the nil rate band, so taper relief has nothing to reduce, though it uses up some band", "D": "No tax is payable on the gift because it is exempt after more than three years, so the nil rate band remains fully available to the estate", "E": "Yes, because taper relief reduces the tax on the whole estate by sixty per cent for deaths between five and six years after a gift"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No tax arises on the gift as it is within the nil rate band, so taper relief has nothing to reduce, though it uses up some band): Taper relief reduces the tax payable on a failed lifetime gift, not the value of the gift, and it matters only where the gift exceeds the available nil rate band. Zack's gift of £200,000 is within the band, so no tax arises on it and there is nothing to taper. The gift still counts, however, and uses £200,000 of the band, leaving only £125,000 for the death estate. Taper relief does not apply to the tax on the estate."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Aiden loses his temper outside a pub and throws a large stone at a group of men, intending to hit them. The stone misses the group and smashes the window of the pub, causing £600 of damage. Aiden did not think about the pub window and had no thought of damaging it. He is charged with criminal damage and the prosecution argues that his intention to hit the men can be transferred to the window. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "He is guilty, because transferred malice applies to any offence where the defendant intended some harm and caused different harm", "B": "He is guilty, because the intention to hurt the men is sufficient to make the damage to the window unlawful", "C": "He is not guilty, because malice towards a person does not transfer to property, so he lacked the mens rea for criminal damage", "D": "He is guilty, because the actus reus is complete and recklessness is established by the obvious risk to nearby property", "E": "He is not guilty, because transferred malice operates only where the intended victim is actually struck, so a miss leaves nothing to transfer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He is not guilty, because malice towards a person does not transfer to property, so he lacked the mens rea for criminal damage): Transferred malice only applies where the same type of offence is committed against a different victim, so intent to hurt a person cannot be transferred to property damage. Criminal damage requires intention or subjective recklessness as to damaging property, and Aiden gave no thought to the window. The objective approach to recklessness is no longer law. The prosecution therefore cannot supply the mens rea, even though the damage plainly occurred."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Seaview Bakery Ltd borrowed £100,000 from Coastal Finance plc in January 2025. In March 2026 the company granted Coastal a floating charge over its stock to secure the existing £100,000 and a new advance of £20,000 paid to the company that day. Coastal is unconnected with the company. The company went into administration in August 2026 and was insolvent when the charge was created. What is the effect on the floating charge?", "options": {"A": "The charge is invalid in full, because it was created within twelve months of onset of insolvency while the company was insolvent", "B": "The charge is valid for the full £120,000, because it was created more than six months before the administration began", "C": "The charge is valid for the full £120,000, because only charges given to connected persons are open to challenge under the avoidance rules", "D": "The charge is valid only to the extent of the new £20,000 advance, because a recent floating charge is invalid for past debts", "E": "The charge is valid for the full £120,000 if it was registered at Companies House within the permitted period after creation"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The charge is valid only to the extent of the new £20,000 advance, because a recent floating charge is invalid for past debts): A floating charge created within twelve months before the onset of insolvency, or two years for a connected person, is invalid unless it secures new value, namely money paid or goods or services supplied to the company at or after creation, plus interest. Coastal is unconnected, the charge fell within twelve months, and the company was insolvent when it was created, so the avoidance rule applies. The charge therefore stands only as security for the £20,000 of fresh money and falls away for the older £100,000. Registration at Companies House is a separate requirement and does not cure avoidance."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Ross has suffered from alcohol dependence syndrome for ten years, diagnosed by his doctor as a recognised medical condition. After a heavy drinking session driven by his craving, he kills his flatmate in an argument. Psychiatrists agree the dependence meant he could not exercise self-control over his drinking. He is charged with murder and relies on diminished responsibility. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The defence fails, because abnormality caused by drink is voluntary intoxication, which cannot found diminished responsibility on any footing", "B": "The defence succeeds, because the experts agree the impairment, and the jury is bound to accept agreed medical evidence on the point", "C": "Alcohol dependence syndrome can be a recognised medical condition, so the jury must decide whether it substantially impaired his self-control and explains the killing", "D": "The defence is available only if the dependence itself resulted from an earlier mental illness unconnected with his drinking behaviour", "E": "The defence is available only if Ross was sober when he killed, since intoxication at the time of the act excludes it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Alcohol dependence syndrome can be a recognised medical condition, so the jury must decide whether it substantially impaired his self-control and explains the killing): Alcohol dependence syndrome is capable of being a recognised medical condition, unlike ordinary voluntary acute intoxication, which is not. The jury must then decide whether it substantially impaired a listed ability and provides an explanation for the killing. Acute intoxication on the day can be considered separately and is disregarded when judging the impairment. The jury is never bound to accept expert agreement, and the dependence need not derive from another illness."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Fran, in a playful mood at a party, shoves her friend Jude from behind, intending to make him jump. Jude trips over a loose rug, strikes his head on the fireplace and dies. Fran did not foresee any serious injury. She is charged with unlawful act manslaughter. Which of the following best describes the test for whether the act was dangerous?", "options": {"A": "The act is dangerous if it caused death, since the danger is assessed with hindsight by reference to the harm that actually resulted in this incident", "B": "The act is dangerous if a sober and reasonable person would foresee a serious and obvious risk of death resulting from it", "C": "The act is dangerous only if it was directed at the victim with an intention to cause some physical injury to him", "D": "The act is dangerous if a sober and reasonable person would recognise a risk of some physical harm to Jude, though not of serious harm", "E": "The act is dangerous only if Fran herself foresaw a risk of serious injury, since the test of danger is subjective"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The act is dangerous if a sober and reasonable person would recognise a risk of some physical harm to Jude, though not of serious harm): The dangerousness requirement is objective: would a sober and reasonable person recognise the risk of some harm from the act, not necessarily serious harm. A shove from behind onto a hard surface meets that test even though Fran saw no real danger. A serious and obvious risk of death is the different requirement for gross negligence manslaughter. Fran's own foresight is not the test, and danger is judged at the time of the act and not with hindsight."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Imran Qureshi contracted in writing to buy a house from Dora Whitlock for £300,000, with completion on 30 June. In April they agreed in conversation and by text message that the price would fall to £290,000 because Imran would complete a week earlier, on 23 June, and he did so. Nothing further was signed. Both are individuals and neither used a solicitor for the later arrangement. Dora now says the price remains £300,000. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The price is now £290,000, because section 2 applies only to the formation of the original contract and not to later changes", "B": "The price is still £300,000, because varying the price of a land contract must comply with the signed writing rules in section 2", "C": "The price remains £300,000, because the variation was not made by deed and a deed is required to vary any land contract", "D": "The price is now £290,000, because Imran gave fresh consideration by completing earlier and the parties recorded the change in a text message", "E": "The price is now £290,000, because Imran gave consideration by completing a week early, and consideration is all a variation requires"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The price is still £300,000, because varying the price of a land contract must comply with the signed writing rules in section 2): A contract for the sale of land must be made in writing signed by both parties under section 2 of the Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989, and a variation of a material term such as the price must meet the same rule. The conversation and text message do not satisfy it, so the original price stands. Consideration for the change, earlier completion, is not enough on its own. A deed is not what the statute demands, so that reasoning fails even though it reaches the same figure."}, {"id": 950, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "You act for Mulberry Estates, which has agreed in principle to buy a depot from Dray Haulage. Dray's finance director has sent a letter saying, 'We expect to proceed, but this letter is not intended to be legally binding.' Mulberry's director plans to spend £40,000 on demolition planning in reliance on it and asks whether Mulberry can safely rely on the letter. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The letter becomes binding if Mulberry accepts it in writing, because acceptance converts a statement of expectation into a contract between the parties", "B": "Mulberry should proceed, because a solicitor should not discourage a client from a commercial course that the client has already decided to take", "C": "The letter creates a binding contract, because it was sent between commercial parties and so it is presumed that they intended legal consequences", "D": "The letter is expressly non-binding, so any spending is at Mulberry's own risk, and a binding interim or lock-out agreement should be sought first", "E": "Mulberry can recover its spending as reliance loss, because the letter induced the expenditure and is therefore enforceable in estoppel"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The letter is expressly non-binding, so any spending is at Mulberry's own risk, and a binding interim or lock-out agreement should be sought first): The commercial presumption of legal intention is rebutted where the parties say in clear words that a document is not intended to be binding. Mulberry therefore has no contract and no enforceable right if Dray withdraws. A solicitor must act in the client's best interests and give the client the information needed to make informed decisions, so the risk must be explained. The sensible protection is a binding agreement, such as a lock-out or interim contract, before significant money is spent."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Larkin Engineering Ltd sued Pell Logistics Ltd for £60,000. Pell filed a defence. Larkin has now concluded that its claim is weak and serves a notice of discontinuance on Pell, and files it at court. No interim injunction has been granted and no interim payment has been made. No agreement about costs has been reached. Which of the following best states the costs position?", "options": {"A": "Larkin pays Pell's costs from the date the defence was filed, because earlier costs were incurred before a claim was pleaded", "B": "Each party must bear its own costs, because a claimant who discontinues before trial is not treated as having lost", "C": "Larkin is liable for costs if Pell applies and the court is satisfied that Pell had a good defence", "D": "Larkin must also pay Pell's costs of preparing for trial, because discontinuance counts as a judgment for the defendant", "E": "Larkin is liable for Pell's costs incurred up to the date on which the notice was served, unless the court orders otherwise"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Larkin is liable for Pell's costs incurred up to the date on which the notice was served, unless the court orders otherwise): A claimant who discontinues is liable for the defendant's costs up to the date on which notice of discontinuance was served, and the court may order otherwise. No costs for future trial preparation arise because the proceedings end on discontinuance, and the notice is not a judgment. Permission would have been needed had an interim injunction or payment been made, but neither applies here. Costs start from the beginning of the claim, not from the defence."}, {"id": 952, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Tia has been a director of Quill Software Ltd, an unrelated trading company, for three years. She acquired 12% of its ordinary shares fifteen months ago by subscribing for new shares at par. A buyer has offered £400,000 for all her shares and wants to exchange contracts this week, which would give her a large gain. She asks how she can reduce the capital gains tax. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Proceed now and claim the relief, because three years as a director satisfies the qualifying period even though the shares were held for only fifteen months", "B": "Delay exchange of contracts until she has held the shares for two years, because business asset disposal relief needs a two year qualifying period before disposal", "C": "Delay until she has held the shares for five years, because that is the period the relief requires for shares in a trading company", "D": "Proceed now, because the relief applies only to the disposal of an entire unincorporated business and not to shares in a company", "E": "Proceed now, because the relief is available to any director with a holding above 5%, whatever the period of ownership of the shares"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delay exchange of contracts until she has held the shares for two years, because business asset disposal relief needs a two year qualifying period before disposal): Business asset disposal relief on shares needs the company to be a trading company, the individual to be an officer or employee and hold at least 5% of the shares and voting rights, all for two years ending with the disposal. Tia's directorship has run for three years but she has held the shares for only fifteen months, so the condition is not yet met. Delaying exchange of contracts until the two year period is complete would let her claim the relief on the sale. The relief is not limited to unincorporated businesses and there is no five year rule."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Vera lent £50,000 to Quinn under an ordinary written loan agreement, which allowed Quinn to use the money as he wished. Quinn used it to buy a van, which he still owns, and then became insolvent. Vera is an unsecured creditor and asks whether she can trace her money into the van in equity to gain priority over Quinn's other creditors. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "She cannot trace because the van is a physical asset, and equity can trace only into money held in bank accounts", "B": "She can trace, because her money can be identified in the van, and identifiable proceeds give priority over other creditors", "C": "She cannot trace, because a debtor under an ordinary loan holds no property for the lender, so there is no fiduciary relationship or equitable interest", "D": "She can trace, but only to a proportionate share if Quinn mixed the loan with his own savings before buying the van", "E": "She can trace only if she registered a charge over the van before Quinn became insolvent, since equitable tracing needs a registered security interest in the asset"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She cannot trace, because a debtor under an ordinary loan holds no property for the lender, so there is no fiduciary relationship or equitable interest): Tracing in equity needs a starting point in which the claimant retains an equitable proprietary interest, usually through a fiduciary relationship or trust. A loan passes full ownership of the money to the borrower, and Quinn held it for himself, so Vera has only a personal claim as an unsecured creditor. Identifiability of the money in the van does not create a proprietary right, and registration is not the test. Equity can trace into other assets, such as a van, but only from a proper starting point."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Marlow Studios Ltd is taking a five year lease of a shop under a full repairing and insuring lease. The landlord will insure the building and recover the premium from the tenant. The draft contains a tenant covenant to keep the premises in good repair, with no exception for damage by insured risks. The client is worried that a fire could leave it responsible for rebuilding costs. What is the best advice to the client?", "options": {"A": "Accept the clause but seek a rent suspension, because suspension of rent on its own cancels the tenant's repairing duty", "B": "Rely on the landlord's insurance, because a tenant is released from the repairing covenant when the damage is covered by insurance", "C": "Negotiate an express exclusion for damage by insured risks, because the repairing covenant otherwise extends to fire damage", "D": "Accept the clause, because the law implies an exception for damage by insured risks into any covenant to repair", "E": "Insure the unit in the tenant's own name as well, because that removes the repairing obligation for insured damage"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Negotiate an express exclusion for damage by insured risks, because the repairing covenant otherwise extends to fire damage): A full repairing covenant covers all damage, including damage by fire, unless the lease expressly excludes it. The landlord's insurance gives a source of funds but does not by itself release the tenant from the duty to repair. The usual negotiated protection is an exclusion for insured risks, with a rent suspension while the premises are unusable. Insuring separately does nothing to remove the covenant."}, {"id": 955, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Moses is a trustee of a shop lease held for a young beneficiary, Jade. As the lease nears its end, the landlord tells the trustees that he will not renew it to the trust, but he offers Moses a new lease personally at the market rent. Moses takes the new lease in his own name, paying the rent from his own pocket. The shop is now worth considerably more. Jade's guardian challenges Moses. Which of the following best states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Moses must account for any profit but may keep the lease itself, since a trustee's duty reaches only profit and not the lease property he took", "B": "Moses may keep the lease provided that he pays Jade reasonable compensation for the value that the trust would have enjoyed", "C": "Moses may keep the lease if he acted honestly and agreed the rent at the commercial market rate with the landlord", "D": "Moses must hold the new lease on trust for Jade, because it arose through his office, whatever the landlord's refusal to renew to the trust", "E": "Moses may keep the lease, because the landlord refused to renew to the trust, so the trust had no opportunity that Moses could take from it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Moses must hold the new lease on trust for Jade, because it arose through his office, whatever the landlord's refusal to renew to the trust): The rule in Keech v Sandford is strict: a trustee who obtains a renewal of trust property by reason of his office holds it on trust, even where the landlord would not have renewed to the trust. Honesty, a fair rent and compensation are irrelevant because the rule removes the temptation to put self before the trust. The remedy reaches the lease itself, so Jade may require Moses to hold it for her."}, {"id": 956, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Fenwick Components Ltd is a private company with one class of ordinary shares held by three shareholders. Its articles do not restrict the directors' power to allot shares and contain nothing about pre-emption. The directors want to allot 1,000 new ordinary shares to an outside investor, Zoe Hartley, for £50,000 in cash. No resolution has yet been passed on the matter. Which of the following correctly states what the directors must do?", "options": {"A": "Offer the shares to all existing shareholders, but only if the shares are to be issued for non-cash consideration such as property", "B": "Obtain an ordinary resolution authorising the allotment, after which the shares may be allotted to Zoe without offering them to existing shareholders", "C": "Allot to Zoe directly, because directors of a private company with one class of shares need no authority and pre-emption applies only to public companies", "D": "Offer them to existing shareholders pro rata, unless the statutory right is disapplied by the articles or a special resolution", "E": "Obtain a special resolution to authorise the allotment, because directors cannot allot any shares unless shareholders have specifically authorised them"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Offer them to existing shareholders pro rata, unless the statutory right is disapplied by the articles or a special resolution): A private company with a single class of shares may allot without a shareholder resolution unless its articles prohibit it, so the directors have authority here. Separately, section 561 of the Companies Act 2006 requires equity securities issued for cash to be offered to existing ordinary shareholders pro rata. That right applies to private companies and is disapplied only by the articles or a special resolution. An authority resolution does not disapply it, and the rule does not apply to non-cash issues."}, {"id": 957, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Cobalt Retail Ltd, together with its predecessor in the same business, has carried on a business from a shop for 15 years under a protected tenancy. The landlord served a valid section 25 notice opposing renewal on the ground of intention to demolish and reconstruct, and at the hearing the court was satisfied that the ground is made out. The rateable value of the shop is £20,000 and the relevant multiplier is one. Which of the following is the compensation payable to the tenant?", "options": {"A": "£40,000, because compensation is doubled where the tenant and its predecessors in the business have occupied for at least 14 years", "B": "Nothing, because compensation is payable only where the landlord has failed to establish a ground of opposition in court and the tenant has won", "C": "£80,000, because the double rate for 14 years applies to a figure already doubled for the redevelopment ground", "D": "£40,000 plus the tenant's reasonable removal costs, because disturbance is compensated as a separate head under the Act", "E": "£20,000, because only one times the rateable value is payable unless the lease itself granted a term of more than 14 years"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (£40,000, because compensation is doubled where the tenant and its predecessors in the business have occupied for at least 14 years): Compensation is payable where the landlord succeeds only on ground (e), (f) or (g), which includes the redevelopment ground relied on here. It is the rateable value multiplied by the appropriate multiplier, which is one, and it is doubled where the premises have been occupied for the purposes of the same business for at least 14 years before termination. Cobalt Retail Ltd and its predecessor have done so for 15 years, so the figure is £40,000. The length of the lease is irrelevant and there is no separate head for moving costs."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Morrow Ltd, a subsidiary of a listed group, agreed to operate a supermarket in Penrith Estates' new retail park for 20 years, the lease obliging it to keep the store open during trading hours. After four years Morrow decided the store was unprofitable and began closing it. Penrith Estates asks the court to order Morrow to keep trading and, failing that, to award damages. Which of the following is the best advice on the order Penrith Estates seeks?", "options": {"A": "A court will order Morrow to keep trading, because contracts relating to land are specifically enforceable and the lease relates to an interest in land", "B": "A court is unlikely to order Morrow to keep trading, because it would need continuing supervision and could cause hardship, leaving Penrith to damages", "C": "A court will not grant an order, because equitable relief is unavailable to a landlord where the tenant has already begun to close the store", "D": "A court will order Morrow to keep trading, because the covenant is clear and a specific order is the usual remedy where damages are hard to quantify", "E": "A court will not grant an order, because specific performance is available only where the claimant has not claimed damages in the alternative"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A court is unlikely to order Morrow to keep trading, because it would need continuing supervision and could cause hardship, leaving Penrith to damages): Specific performance is discretionary, and the courts decline to order a party to carry on a business or to perform a continuing obligation requiring supervision. An order to trade would involve the court in repeated oversight and might cause real hardship. That the contract relates to land does not remove the discretion. Pleading damages in the alternative is permitted, and the start of closure does not itself bar relief, so damages are the likely outcome."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Aisha Rahman is a solicitor who has not obtained higher rights of audience. She represented a client in the Magistrates' Court, where the client was convicted after a trial and given a community order. The client wants to appeal against the sentence to the Crown Court. Aisha wants to know whether she herself may appear for the client at the Crown Court appeal hearing or must instruct an advocate. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She may appear, because a solicitor has rights of audience in every court in England and Wales once she is admitted", "B": "She may appear, because a solicitor has rights of audience on an appeal to the Crown Court from a case she conducted below", "C": "She must instruct a barrister, because the Crown Court is a higher court and an advocate with higher rights must appear there", "D": "She must instruct a solicitor-advocate or barrister, because rights in the Crown Court cover committals for sentence but not appeals from magistrates", "E": "She may appear if the client first obtains the Crown Court judge's permission for representation by a solicitor"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She may appear, because a solicitor has rights of audience on an appeal to the Crown Court from a case she conducted below): A solicitor without higher rights has a right of audience in the Crown Court in proceedings that began in the Magistrates' Court, including an appeal, where the solicitor or her firm acted for the client below. Aisha conducted the case in the Magistrates' Court, so she may appear. The general position is that conducting a Crown Court trial needs higher rights, which is the point the tempting answer relies on. Solicitors do not hold rights in every court merely by admission."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Crestline Ltd, an English company, has a contract with Van Houten BV, a company incorporated in the Netherlands with no place of business in England. The contract is governed by Dutch law but includes a clause stating that the English courts shall have jurisdiction to determine any dispute arising out of it. Crestline wishes to sue for breach and serve the claim form on Van Houten in the Netherlands. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Crestline cannot serve in the Netherlands, because the English courts have no power over a foreign company with no place of business here", "B": "Crestline must obtain permission by showing the contract is governed by English law, because Dutch law governs this contract", "C": "Crestline may serve without the court's permission, because the contract contains a term giving the English courts jurisdiction", "D": "Crestline must first ask the Dutch court to confirm that it accepts the jurisdiction clause before serving", "E": "Crestline must obtain permission under the Hague Convention, because service in a European state requires the court's authority"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Crestline may serve without the court's permission, because the contract contains a term giving the English courts jurisdiction): The rules allow a claim form to be served out of the jurisdiction without permission where the contract contains a term that the English court shall have jurisdiction to determine the claim. The governing law of the contract does not affect that route, though it would matter for the gateways needing permission. The English court has power over a defendant properly served under the rules. No approval from the Dutch court is required before service."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Winterbourne Advisers, an independent financial adviser, refers clients to Hallam Legal in Lincoln for wills and trusts. It proposes that Hallam Legal pay it a share of its fees on each referral, on condition that the firm's wills for those clients always use Winterbourne's own trust products. Hallam Legal's managing partner says the clients will benefit from the convenient package. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the condition, because fee sharing with a regulated financial adviser is permitted whatever conditions the adviser imposes", "B": "Accept the condition if the managing partner personally reviews each will, because that preserves the firm's independence", "C": "Accept the condition, because clients can still decline the trust products and the arrangement is disclosed to them", "D": "Decline the condition, because it would stop the firm advising independently in each client's best interests and so breach the SRA Principles", "E": "Decline only the fee share while still using the adviser's trust products, because the fee share is the cause of the conflict"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline the condition, because it would stop the firm advising independently in each client's best interests and so breach the SRA Principles): A firm must be able to give independent advice in the best interests of each client, and a referral arrangement that dictates which products must be used removes that freedom. The condition is the real problem, not only the fee share. Disclosure to clients, partner review or the adviser's regulated status cannot restore the independence the condition destroys. The firm should refuse the condition and act only on the advice each client needs."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Nadia Frost is a solicitor in a commercial team. Her client, a consultancy, asks her to prepare a services agreement with a client company showing a fee of £20,000. The consultancy explains that the true fee is £50,000, with £30,000 to be paid in cash outside the books so that VAT and tax on that part will not be paid. Nadia has acted for this consultancy for years and wants to keep its business. What should Nadia do?", "options": {"A": "Draft the agreement showing the lower fee, but warn the client in writing of the tax risk and keep that advice on file", "B": "Refuse to draft the false agreement, because she would be assisting tax evasion, and consider with her firm's MLRO whether a suspicious activity report is required", "C": "Refuse to draft the agreement and explain to the client her reason for making a report, so that the client can correct matters", "D": "Draft the agreement as instructed, because the duty of confidentiality means she cannot report the client's plan to anyone", "E": "Draft the agreement showing the full fee of £50,000 and send it to the other party without telling her client first"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refuse to draft the false agreement, because she would be assisting tax evasion, and consider with her firm's MLRO whether a suspicious activity report is required): Drafting a document that records a false fee to hide income from the tax authorities would involve Nadia in a dishonest scheme and a likely money laundering offence, so she must not act on those instructions. Confidentiality does not protect a client's plan to commit a crime. Because the unpaid tax is criminal property, she should consult the firm's money laundering reporting officer about a report. Telling the client that a report is being considered risks tipping off, so a warning alone or an explanation of the report is wrong."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Commons passes the Health Services Reform Bill, an ordinary public Bill, in the 2025 session, and sends it to the Lords more than a month before the end of that session. The Lords reject it. In the next session, more than a year after the Commons second reading in the first session, the Commons passes the identical Bill again, and the Lords reject it again. The Bill is not a money Bill and does not seek to extend the life of Parliament. Can the Bill be presented for Royal Assent without the consent of the Lords?", "options": {"A": "No, because the Lords' consent is needed unless the Bill passes the Commons in three successive sessions over two years", "B": "Yes, and the Bill takes effect after a further one year delay from assent, which gives the Lords time to reconsider", "C": "No, because the Lords may reject an ordinary Bill any number of times and the Commons cannot override that rejection", "D": "Yes, it can be presented for Royal Assent without the Lords' consent, because the Parliament Acts conditions are satisfied", "E": "Yes, but the monarch must first approve the use of the Parliament Acts procedure in a separate step before assent"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yes, it can be presented for Royal Assent without the Lords' consent, because the Parliament Acts conditions are satisfied): Under the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949, a Bill that is not a money Bill and does not extend the life of Parliament may be presented for Royal Assent without the Lords' consent if it is passed by the Commons in two successive sessions, at least a year after its second reading in the first session, and is rejected by the Lords each time. Those conditions are met here. The old requirement of three sessions was reduced to two in 1949. There is no extra approval step and no further delay after assent."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Ahmed orally agreed with Bella that she could occupy a flat he owned for two years at a rent matching local market rents, and he handed her the keys. Bella took possession at once and has paid rent monthly. There is no written document and no deed. A year into the term, Ahmed's mortgage lender asks whether Bella holds a legal estate in the flat. Which of the following best describes her position?", "options": {"A": "She holds only an equitable lease, because a legal lease must be created by deed whatever its length and whether or not possession was given", "B": "She holds a legal lease, because a term of three years or less at the best rent, taking effect in possession, needs no deed", "C": "She holds a legal lease only if the oral agreement has been confirmed in writing signed by both parties", "D": "She holds no estate, because an oral agreement to grant a lease is void for lack of formality", "E": "She holds a legal lease only if it has been registered at HM Land Registry within two months"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She holds a legal lease, because a term of three years or less at the best rent, taking effect in possession, needs no deed): Section 52 of the Law of Property Act 1925 requires a deed for a legal lease, but section 54(2) excepts a lease taking effect in possession for three years or less at the best rent reasonably obtainable without a premium. Bella's two year term at a market rent, with immediate possession, fits the exception, so no deed or writing is needed. Registration is required only for leases of more than seven years. An informal agreement within the exception is not void but creates a legal estate."}, {"id": 965, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Eve is 17 and is the only witness to a burglary of her family home. The defendant has pleaded not guilty and the trial is listed at the Crown Court in two months. Eve is anxious about giving evidence in the same room as him, although she has no learning difficulty and nobody has threatened her. The prosecution is considering applying for special measures. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Eve is entitled to give evidence by live link as of right, and the court has no discretion to refuse", "B": "Eve is eligible only if she can show she has been intimidated by the defendant or his associates", "C": "Eve is eligible only if the offence charged is sexual or violent, since young witnesses in other cases give evidence in the usual way", "D": "Eve is eligible as a vulnerable witness because she is under 18, and the court decides which measures would improve her evidence", "E": "Eve is eligible only if a doctor certifies that anxiety is likely to diminish the quality of her evidence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Eve is eligible as a vulnerable witness because she is under 18, and the court decides which measures would improve her evidence): A witness who is under 18 at the time of the hearing is eligible for assistance as a vulnerable witness under the Youth Justice and Criminal Evidence Act 1999. Eligibility does not depend on the type of offence or any proof of intimidation. The court then decides whether any measure, such as screens or a live link, would be likely to improve the quality of her evidence, and which one. A measure is therefore not hers as of right."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Hasan Qadir is charged with burglary and is represented by solicitor Fiona Nash. At the first meeting he admits to Fiona that he committed the burglary, but he still wants to plead not guilty and put the prosecution to proof. He does not wish to give evidence. The trial is in the Crown Court in six weeks. Which of the following best describes what Fiona may do?", "options": {"A": "Continue to act and run a positive defence of alibi, because the client is entitled to the defence he chooses", "B": "Continue to act but advise Hasan to plead guilty, and withdraw if he refuses to follow that advice", "C": "Continue to act and put the prosecution to proof, but not advance a positive case that Hasan is innocent or call evidence suggesting it", "D": "Stop acting at once and report the admission to the court, because she has learned that her client is guilty", "E": "Continue to act and challenge prosecution witnesses by suggesting they have fabricated their evidence, because the client is entitled to a full defence"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue to act and put the prosecution to proof, but not advance a positive case that Hasan is innocent or call evidence suggesting it): A client's admission of guilt does not require the solicitor to stop acting, because every defendant is entitled to have the prosecution prove its case. Fiona must not mislead the court, so she may not assert his innocence or advance a defence she knows to be false, such as an invented alibi. She may test the prosecution evidence and argue it falls short of proof. She must not reveal the admission to the court, and advising a guilty plea is permitted but not compulsory."}, {"id": 967, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Vivian Cole, who made no gifts in the previous two tax years, gives £8,000 in cash to her niece in June 2026 to help with a house deposit. The annual exemption is £3,000 per tax year, and any unused part can be carried forward for one year only. Vivian is married and in good health, and the gift is not made in consideration of the niece's marriage. She has made no other gifts to anyone this year. How is the gift treated for Inheritance Tax?", "options": {"A": "£6,000 is covered by this year's exemption and last year's unused exemption, and the remaining £2,000 is a potentially exempt transfer", "B": "The whole £8,000 is a chargeable lifetime transfer, because the gift exceeds the annual exemption available for the year", "C": "£3,000 is covered by the annual exemption and the remaining £5,000 is a potentially exempt transfer, because an earlier unused exemption cannot be added", "D": "£9,000 is covered by exemptions from three years, so the whole £8,000 is exempt and need not be reported", "E": "£250 is exempt under the small gifts exemption and the remaining £7,750 is a potentially exempt transfer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (£6,000 is covered by this year's exemption and last year's unused exemption, and the remaining £2,000 is a potentially exempt transfer): The annual exemption of £3,000 for 2026 to 2027 can be added to any unused exemption from the previous tax year, which is a further £3,000 because Vivian made no earlier gifts. That covers £6,000 of the gift. The balance of £2,000 is a gift to an individual and is a potentially exempt transfer. The carry forward is limited to one year, so earlier years are not available."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Your firm acts for Marlow Ltd on its general commercial matters. Dev, the managing director and a 50% shareholder, asks the firm to act for him personally in selling his own warehouse to Marlow Ltd at a price he proposed. Both Dev and the company want the firm to handle the whole transaction to save cost. Dev is confident that Marlow Ltd will accept his price and that the sale can be completed within a month. Which of the following is the correct advice under the SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors?", "options": {"A": "The firm may act for both because they share a substantial common interest in completing the sale quickly and cheaply", "B": "The firm may act for both because Dev controls the company, so there is in substance only one client to protect", "C": "The firm cannot act for both, because their interests on price and terms conflict and no exception to the conflict rule applies", "D": "The firm may act for both if each gives informed written consent, because consent removes the conflict between buyer and seller", "E": "The firm may act for both if separate teams are used with an information barrier, which adequately manages the conflict between them"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The firm cannot act for both, because their interests on price and terms conflict and no exception to the conflict rule applies): Seller and buyer negotiating price and terms have opposing interests, so there is a conflict in the same matter. The exceptions allowing a firm to act, such as a substantial common interest or competing for the same objective, do not fit a sale between two parties on different sides. Consent and information barriers do not cure a genuine conflict of this kind. The firm should therefore not act for both."}, {"id": 969, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Dmitri owns 500 bottles of the same vintage claret, stored in a warehouse mixed in with other stock. He signs a document stating that he holds 200 bottles of it on trust for his daughter Elena. No bottles are separated, labelled or otherwise set apart before Dmitri becomes insolvent. Elena claims 200 bottles as her property against his trustee in bankruptcy. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Elena's claim succeeds, because the bottles are identical, so she takes a proportionate share of the whole stock as a co-owner", "B": "Elena ranks as a secured creditor for the value of 200 bottles, because her interest arose under a binding promise", "C": "Elena's claim succeeds, because a declaration signed by the owner is enough to constitute a trust of the bottles", "D": "Elena's claim fails, because a trust of chattels must be created by deed and Dmitri signed only a document", "E": "Elena's claim fails, because no particular bottles were identified, so the subject matter of the trust was uncertain"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Elena's claim fails, because no particular bottles were identified, so the subject matter of the trust was uncertain): A trust of tangible goods needs certainty of subject matter, and which of the 500 bottles belonged to Elena was never settled. Unlike a trust of a fungible mass of intangible assets, a trust of part of a physical stock fails unless the trust property is segregated. Dmitri's signed declaration might have been enough to constitute the trust had it been certain, but certainty is the problem here. There is no deed requirement for a trust of chattels, and Elena therefore has only an unsecured claim at best."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trust fund is held for 'such of the children of Ruth as reach 21, in equal shares'. Ruth has two children, Tom aged 20 and Una aged 16. Tom wants the trust ended and the fund shared now, and Una's parents agree. The trustees ask whether Tom and Una can bring the trust to an end by agreement. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "No, because a contingent interest cannot be varied by agreement even if every beneficiary is an adult", "B": "Yes, because Tom is over 18 and Una's parents can give consent on her behalf as her guardians", "C": "No, because every beneficiary must be an adult and together entitled, and Una is a minor who cannot consent", "D": "No, because a trust for a class of children can be ended only by the trustees using their power of advancement", "E": "Yes, because both children are the only living objects and their agreement binds any child who is later born"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because every beneficiary must be an adult and together entitled, and Una is a minor who cannot consent): The rule in Saunders v Vautier needs every beneficiary to be an adult of full capacity and between them absolutely entitled. Una is 16 and cannot consent, and a parent cannot give a binding consent for her to end a trust. Further children of Ruth might also qualify, so the class may not be closed. The court could approve a variation on Una's behalf under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958, but the beneficiaries cannot end the trust by agreement alone."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Neil, who runs a small guest house, installed a boiler made by Thermex Ltd, fitted by a plumber. Through a manufacturing fault caused by Thermex's carelessness, the boiler's casing failed. The boiler was destroyed, and escaping hot water ruined the kitchen floor. Neil sues Thermex Ltd in negligence for the cost of a replacement boiler and for the cost of a new floor. Which of the following best describes what he can recover?", "options": {"A": "He can recover for the floor, which is damage to other property, but not for the boiler, which is pure economic loss", "B": "He can recover for the boiler but not for the floor, because the defective product is the direct loss of the buyer", "C": "He can recover for neither, because negligence protects against personal injury only and not damage to property", "D": "He can recover for both, but only if he bought the boiler from Thermex directly rather than through a plumber", "E": "He can recover for both, because the defect caused physical damage and negligence allows recovery for all losses it causes"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He can recover for the floor, which is damage to other property, but not for the boiler, which is pure economic loss): A defect in a product that damages only the product itself is treated as pure economic loss, because it is the loss of the bargain, and the buyer's remedy lies in contract against his seller. Damage to other property, such as the kitchen floor, is physical damage that negligence compensates if caused by the manufacturer's carelessness. The boiler cost is therefore not recoverable from Thermex, while the floor is. The claim is not limited to personal injury, and a direct purchase is not a condition of a negligence claim."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Marlowe Brewery Ltd obtained ordinary planning permission to build a loading yard beside a residential street and then began loading lorries at 4 am. Residents whose sleep is disturbed sue the brewery in private nuisance. The permission did not change the street's residential character and the grant was a routine local decision. The brewery argues that planning permission is a defence. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The argument fails, because ordinary planning permission authorises no nuisance unless it changes the locality's character", "B": "The argument succeeds, because the council's grant means the residents' only remedy lies in judicial review of the decision", "C": "The argument succeeds, because planning permission is statutory authority and so provides a complete defence to nuisance", "D": "The argument fails, because planning permission is irrelevant to nuisance and cannot be considered by the court", "E": "The argument succeeds as to the injunction but not damages, because planning permission bars an order restricting the business"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The argument fails, because ordinary planning permission authorises no nuisance unless it changes the locality's character): A routine planning permission does not amount to statutory authority to commit a nuisance, because the planning authority does not decide private rights between neighbours. It can matter only where a strategic planning decision changes the character of the locality, so that the noise is judged against the new character. Here the area remained residential, so the brewery gets no defence. Planning permission is not wholly irrelevant, as it may inform the assessment of reasonableness, but that falls short of a defence."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "In 2017 Merrion Estates granted Kestrel Ltd a 15 year lease of a warehouse. In 2020 Kestrel Ltd assigned it, with consent, to Fontaine Ltd and gave an authorised guarantee agreement. In 2023 Fontaine Ltd assigned it, with consent, to Quayside Ltd and also gave an authorised guarantee agreement. Quayside Ltd has now defaulted on the rent. Merrion Estates seeks to recover the arrears from the other parties. Which of the following best describes who is liable for the arrears?", "options": {"A": "Quayside Ltd, Fontaine Ltd and Kestrel Ltd, because each authorised guarantee agreement covers every later assignee until the lease ends", "B": "Quayside Ltd as current tenant and Fontaine Ltd under its guarantee, but not Kestrel Ltd, whose guarantee ended when Fontaine Ltd assigned", "C": "Fontaine Ltd and Kestrel Ltd, but not Quayside Ltd, because the landlord must first pursue guarantors before the current tenant", "D": "Quayside Ltd and Kestrel Ltd only, because the original tenant remains liable for the whole term of a lease granted in 2017", "E": "Quayside Ltd alone, because the 1995 Act released both earlier tenants automatically when each assigned, whatever guarantees they had agreed to give"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Quayside Ltd as current tenant and Fontaine Ltd under its guarantee, but not Kestrel Ltd, whose guarantee ended when Fontaine Ltd assigned): The lease was granted after 1995, so each tenant is released on assignment except to the extent it gave an authorised guarantee agreement. Kestrel Ltd guaranteed only Fontaine Ltd's performance, and that guarantee ended when Fontaine Ltd assigned to Quayside Ltd. Fontaine Ltd gave its own guarantee of Quayside Ltd, so it can be pursued for Quayside Ltd's default as well as Quayside Ltd itself. The landlord is free to choose whom to sue and need not pursue guarantors in any order."}, {"id": 974, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A man called at Quill Jewellers Ltd's shop and, with convincing manners, said he was Sir Anthony Reeve, a well-known conductor. He chose a £5,000 ring and paid by cheque, which Quill accepted after seeing a forged document in that name. He left with the ring and sold it for cash to Dev Mistry, an honest buyer who knew nothing of the fraud. The cheque was dishonoured and the rogue has vanished. Quill had not told anyone that it was avoiding the contract before Dev bought. Which of the following best describes whether Quill can recover the ring from Dev?", "options": {"A": "No, because the contract was voidable for fraud, not void, and Dev obtained good title before the contract was avoided", "B": "No, because title passed to the rogue on delivery of the ring and Dev, as a later purchaser, takes the same title", "C": "Yes, because a seller deceived by a forged document does not truly consent to the sale, so Dev acquired no title", "D": "Yes, because Quill intended to deal only with Sir Anthony Reeve, so the contract was void for mistake and no title passed", "E": "Yes, because Dev did not check the rogue's identity before paying cash and so was not a purchaser in good faith"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because the contract was voidable for fraud, not void, and Dev obtained good title before the contract was avoided): Where parties deal face to face, the seller is presumed to intend to contract with the person in front of them, so a mistake as to identity does not make the contract void. The contract was voidable for fraud, and since Quill had not avoided it before Dev bought in good faith without notice, Dev obtained good title under the voidable title rules in the Sale of Goods Act 1979. The cheque being dishonoured is a consequence of the fraud and does not undo the sale to Dev. Honest failure to check identity is not bad faith."}, {"id": 975, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A will leaves £60,000 to trustees 'to advance the education of the descendants of Joyce Rowan', who died in 1990. About 25 of her descendants are living at the testator's death, and all are members of the testator's own family. No charity is named in the gift. The trustees ask whether the trust is a valid charitable trust. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is valid and charitable, because the Charities Act 2011 removes the need for public benefit in education trusts", "B": "It is void, because a trust for a class that includes unascertained future descendants is uncertain", "C": "It is charitable, because the advancement of education is a recognised charitable purpose that needs no further test", "D": "It is not charitable, because the class is defined by a family relationship and so the trust lacks the required public benefit", "E": "It is charitable, because a trust for the education of a large family is for the public benefit"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is not charitable, because the class is defined by a family relationship and so the trust lacks the required public benefit): A charitable trust needs both a charitable purpose and public benefit. Education is a charitable purpose, but a gift for a class defined by family relationship, like the descendants of one person, fails the public benefit test because of the personal nexus between the beneficiaries. The size of the family does not change this. The Charities Act 2011 does not remove the public benefit requirement for any category of purpose."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Nature Reserve Act prohibits 'bringing any vehicle into the reserve'. Hannah Doyle, a visitor, collapsed in the reserve with a suspected stroke. A paramedic, Lewis Grant, drove an ambulance through the main gate to reach her and was charged under the Act. The prosecution argues that an ambulance is plainly a vehicle. His solicitor argues that Parliament cannot have meant to criminalise saving a life. A court that applies the golden rule would most probably take which approach?", "options": {"A": "Look for the mischief the Act was passed to remedy, and ask whether the ambulance caused that harm before deciding", "B": "Apply the words exactly as written and convict, because an ambulance is a vehicle and any absurd result is for Parliament to remedy by amendment", "C": "Read the word in its ordinary sense first, then, finding the result absurd, depart from it minimally to allow an emergency exception", "D": "Rewrite the provision broadly to give effect to Parliament's wider purposes and strike out the offence for emergency services", "E": "Decide that the word vehicle is ambiguous, then consider Hansard debates to find which meaning the minister intended"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Read the word in its ordinary sense first, then, finding the result absurd, depart from it minimally to allow an emergency exception): The golden rule starts from the literal meaning but allows the court to depart from it where it would produce an absurd or repugnant result. A narrow modification, here an implied exception for emergency vehicles, avoids absurdity while keeping the rest of the prohibition intact. The literal rule would convict because an ambulance is a vehicle, but that is the outcome the golden rule is meant to avoid. The mischief rule and wholesale rewriting go further than the golden rule permits."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Fenner Logistics, a business, contracted with Halo Software Ltd for a bespoke tracking system on Halo's standard written terms. A clause limited Halo's liability for any breach to the fees paid, £25,000. A flaw in Halo's code caused Fenner losses of £400,000. Fenner is of similar size to Halo and negotiated some other terms, but not this clause. Halo says the cap is valid. Which of the following best describes how the cap will be assessed?", "options": {"A": "The cap is subject to the fairness test in the Consumer Rights Act 2015, because the standard terms were not negotiated", "B": "The cap is valid if Halo can show that Fenner read the clause before signing, because the Act applies only to hidden terms", "C": "The cap is subject to the reasonableness test in the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, because Fenner dealt on Halo's standard terms", "D": "The cap is valid, because both parties are businesses of similar size and the Unfair Contract Terms Act does not apply to commercial agreements", "E": "The cap is void, because the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 makes any limitation of liability for breach of contract ineffective"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The cap is subject to the reasonableness test in the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, because Fenner dealt on Halo's standard terms): Where one party deals on the other's written standard terms, a clause limiting its liability for breach must satisfy the reasonableness requirement under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977. The Act applies between businesses and does not make such clauses automatically void. Reasonableness is judged at the time of contracting, taking account of factors such as bargaining strength and insurance. The Consumer Rights Act 2015 applies only where a consumer is involved."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Anita Joshi died with assets worth £48,000, including a car and savings. Her debts total £75,000, made up of an unsecured bank loan, credit card debts, £4,200 of funeral expenses, and £2,000 owed to a friend, Pauline, who lent her money last year. Her will leaves £10,000 to her brother. The executor, her husband Sanjay, feels morally bound to repay Pauline in full immediately. The estate is insolvent. Which of the following is the best advice to Sanjay?", "options": {"A": "Repay Pauline in full now, because a debt owed to an individual friend ranks ahead of institutional lenders and credit card debts", "B": "Pay the brother's legacy first, because the directions in the will take priority and the creditors can then claim against the beneficiary", "C": "Treat the funeral expenses as an ordinary unsecured debt ranking equally with the bank loan, because insolvent estates remove priority for expenses", "D": "Pay the funeral and administration expenses first, then debts in the statutory order, sharing equally among unsecured creditors, with nothing for the brother", "E": "Pay the unsecured creditors in the order in which their demands arrived, because personal representatives may prefer earlier claimants"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pay the funeral and administration expenses first, then debts in the statutory order, sharing equally among unsecured creditors, with nothing for the brother): An insolvent estate is administered in the statutory order: secured creditors may rely on their security, then funeral, testamentary and administration expenses, then preferential debts, then ordinary unsecured debts sharing equally, with legacies last. Sanjay's feelings about Pauline do not alter this, and paying her ahead of others would expose him to personal liability. Legacies, including the brother's, cannot be paid because the creditors are not paid in full."}, {"id": 979, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Fenwick Golf Club is a private members' club run by a committee under rules agreed by its members. The committee expels Raj Anand after a disciplinary meeting, relying on a rule permitting expulsion for conduct harming the club's reputation. Raj says the process was unfair and wants to challenge the expulsion by judicial review in the Administrative Court. Which of the following best describes whether judicial review is available?", "options": {"A": "Judicial review is not available, because the club's power comes from contract and not from statute or a public function", "B": "Judicial review is available, because any decision reached without a fair hearing may be challenged in the Administrative Court", "C": "Judicial review is available, because the committee's decision has seriously affected a right which Raj enjoyed as a member", "D": "Judicial review is available, because the club has a monopoly of golf in the area and so performs a public function", "E": "Judicial review is available, because the Human Rights Act applies to any private body that disciplines its members"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Judicial review is not available, because the club's power comes from contract and not from statute or a public function): Judicial review supervises bodies exercising public functions or powers derived from a public source. The club's committee acts under rules that members accept by contract, so the source of power is private and the proper remedy lies in contract, such as a declaration that the rules were breached. A serious effect on the claimant, a local monopoly or an unfair process does not change that source. The Human Rights Act binds public authorities and does not extend to a members' club."}, {"id": 980, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ethan Cole, aged 35, is charged with burglary of a dwelling, an either way offence. At the plea before venue hearing he indicates that he will plead guilty. The facts include a night-time entry and a vulnerable victim, and he has one previous conviction for theft. The magistrates conclude that their sentencing powers are insufficient for the offence. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "The magistrates commit him to the Crown Court for trial, where he can plead again and be tried by a jury", "B": "The magistrates convict him on his plea and may commit him to the Crown Court for sentence because their powers are insufficient", "C": "The magistrates must pass sentence themselves, because a guilty plea binds the court to the limits of its own powers", "D": "The magistrates send the case to the Crown Court for trial, because a guilty plea to an either way offence is dealt with only there", "E": "The magistrates adjourn for an allocation hearing to decide jurisdiction, because plea is taken only after allocation"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The magistrates convict him on his plea and may commit him to the Crown Court for sentence because their powers are insufficient): At plea before venue, an indication of a guilty plea to an either way offence is treated as a plea of guilty in the magistrates' court, and the court convicts. If the magistrates consider their powers are inadequate, they may commit the defendant to the Crown Court for sentence. The case is not sent for trial because there is nothing to try. Allocation comes after plea before venue, and only if the defendant indicates a not guilty plea or gives no indication."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Aldous Retail Ltd is the claimant in a fast track claim against a delivery company. Trial is listed for a date in six weeks. Aldous' solicitor believes that the defendant cannot genuinely dispute that a consignment was delivered on 4 June, but the defendant's defence neither admits nor denies the point. The solicitor wants to save the cost of calling a witness to prove it. Which of the following is the correct step?", "options": {"A": "Apply to the court for permission to serve a notice to admit, because a notice cannot be served without an order", "B": "Serve a notice to admit the fact no later than seven days before the trial, because notices may be served up to the last week", "C": "Serve a notice to admit that fact no later than 21 days before the trial, as refusal to admit can later affect costs", "D": "Serve a notice to admit with the claim form, because admissions can be requested only before the defence has been filed", "E": "Send a request for further information, because a notice to admit is available only on the small claims track"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serve a notice to admit that fact no later than 21 days before the trial, as refusal to admit can later affect costs): CPR 32.18 allows a party to serve a notice requiring another party to admit a fact or part of its case, and the notice must be served no later than 21 days before trial. No permission is needed. If the other side unreasonably declines to admit, that conduct can be taken into account on costs, which encourages sensible concessions. The device is available in the usual civil tracks, not only the small claims track."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A 2026 Act of Parliament requires food retailers to use a particular label on imported goods. Greenfield Foods Ltd points out that the label conflicts with a directly applicable EU regulation which became assimilated law at the end of 2020. It argues that EU law ranks above domestic statutes, as the Factortame case once decided, and asks that the Act be disapplied. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "The Act is disapplied, because the Factortame rule survives for conflicts with assimilated law made before the end of 2020", "B": "The assimilated regulation prevails, because the principle of EU supremacy continues to apply to all assimilated direct legislation", "C": "The Act prevails, because the supremacy of EU law no longer applies and assimilated law ranks below a later Act of Parliament", "D": "The Act prevails only if it expressly says so, because a later statute cannot override assimilated law by implication", "E": "Neither prevails and the Supreme Court must refer the question to the Court of Justice, which decides conflicts between laws"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The Act prevails, because the supremacy of EU law no longer applies and assimilated law ranks below a later Act of Parliament): The principle of EU law supremacy no longer operates in domestic law, and the European Communities Act 1972 has been repealed, so Factortame style disapplication of Acts is no longer available. Assimilated law is domestic law with a defined status and ranks below primary legislation, so a later Act of Parliament prevails in a conflict under the ordinary rule on later statutes. No express words are needed. References to the Court of Justice are no longer possible."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "In 2010 a trustee, Ed, took £25,000 from the trust bank account and used it to pay off his own debts. The trustees' accounts were poorly kept and the beneficiaries only noticed the gap in 2025. In 2026 an adult beneficiary issues a claim against Ed to recover the £25,000. Ed argues that more than six years have passed. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "The claim is not barred, because section 21(1) sets no limitation period for fraud or for recovering property taken by the trustee", "B": "The claim is barred, because the period is twelve years where a breach is dishonest and the money was used in the trustee's own affairs", "C": "The claim is barred, because the six year period applies equally to deliberate and to honest breaches of trust", "D": "The claim is barred unless the beneficiary was under a disability throughout the period since the breach occurred", "E": "The claim is not barred only if Ed is still solvent, because the claim then becomes a personal debt without any time limit"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The claim is not barred, because section 21(1) sets no limitation period for fraud or for recovering property taken by the trustee): Section 21(1) Limitation Act 1980 provides that no period of limitation applies to an action in respect of any fraud or fraudulent breach of trust to which the trustee was a party, or to recover trust property or its proceeds in the possession of the trustee or previously received and converted to his use. Ed took the money for his own debts, which falls within both limbs. The six year period in section 21(3) therefore does not apply. His solvency is irrelevant."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Kingsley Brothers Ltd claims £34,000 from Ashby Retail Ltd for goods sold. In its defence, Ashby admits owing £10,000 but disputes the remaining £24,000 as defective. There are no complex issues, and the trial would last less than a day with no expert evidence. The claimant's solicitor thinks the claim belongs on the intermediate track because it is for £34,000. Which track will the court most likely allocate?", "options": {"A": "The fast track, because the £34,000 is reduced by 25 percent for the risk of failure when the court allocates", "B": "The intermediate track, because the claimant's pleaded value controls the track unless the defendant counterclaims", "C": "The intermediate track, because the court looks at the full £34,000 claimed in deciding the track", "D": "The small claims track, because the dispute is only about defective goods and the claim is admitted in part", "E": "The fast track, because the court disregards the £10,000 admitted and the sum in dispute is £24,000"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The fast track, because the court disregards the £10,000 admitted and the sum in dispute is £24,000): When the court decides a track by value, it disregards any amount not in dispute, interest, costs and contributory negligence. Ashby admits £10,000, so the value in dispute is £24,000, which is within the fast track limit of £25,000. The trial is short and without expert evidence, so the fast track fits. The pleaded figure of £34,000 is not the test, and there is no discount for the chance of failure."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "At a general meeting of Halden Ltd held in a hotel conference room, the chair declared an ordinary resolution to approve a property sale lost on a show of hands: four members voted against and two for. The two who voted in favour, Imran and Joy, together hold 70% of the voting rights. The articles do not restrict the right to demand a poll. Imran and Joy wish to overturn the result. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "They may demand a poll only if at least five members join the demand, whatever the size of their shareholdings", "B": "They must accept the result, because a decision taken on a show of hands cannot be reversed at the same meeting by the members who lost it", "C": "They must call a fresh meeting on 21 days' notice, because the resolution was lost and cannot be put again", "D": "They may demand a poll only with the chair's agreement, because the decision to hold one rests with the chair", "E": "They may demand a poll, because members holding at least 10% of the voting rights can do so, and votes are then counted by shares"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (They may demand a poll, because members holding at least 10% of the voting rights can do so, and votes are then counted by shares): On a show of hands each member has one vote, which can produce a result unlike the shareholdings. A poll can be demanded by members holding at least 10% of the voting rights, and the articles here do not restrict it. Imran and Joy hold 70%, so they qualify, and on a poll votes are weighted by shares. They need neither the chair's consent nor a new meeting."}, {"id": 986, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Cotswold Law has delivered a bill for £2,400, including VAT, to its client Mrs Ferreira for a completed sale. The client account also holds £5,000 for her, paid in yesterday as the deposit on her new purchase, which is due to exchange tomorrow. The cashier proposes to transfer £2,400 to the office account today to settle the bill. Mrs Ferreira has not been consulted. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "The transfer may be made, because a bill has been delivered and costs may be taken from any client money held for her", "B": "The transfer may be made, if the firm tells her afterwards, since delivery of the bill is enough to authorise it", "C": "The transfer should not be made, because costs can be taken from client money only when the matter has ended", "D": "The transfer should not be made, because the money is held for the deposit and cannot pay costs without her agreement", "E": "The transfer may be made, if Mrs Ferreira is told orally and no objection is raised before exchange"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The transfer should not be made, because the money is held for the deposit and cannot pay costs without her agreement): A firm may transfer client money to its office account to pay a bill only where the money is properly available for that purpose. The £5,000 was paid in for a specific reason, the deposit, so using part of it for costs would leave the deposit short and defeats the purpose for which it was held. The delivered bill does not change that unless the client agrees to the use. The firm can ask her for authority, or invite payment from other funds, before the cashier acts."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Whitby Tools Ltd is a private company with ordinary shares. Retiring shareholder Gordon Aziz wishes to sell his fully paid 10 per cent holding back to the company for £60,000, under a written contract that will be an off-market purchase. The company has distributable profits of £90,000 and its articles contain no restriction on buybacks. The directors ask the solicitor to outline a valid procedure. Which of the following best describes it?", "options": {"A": "A special resolution approving the contract, with the price paid in instalments over two years out of distributable profits, then cancellation", "B": "No shareholder resolution, because directors of a private company may buy back shares using any available company cash under the model articles", "C": "A special resolution approving the contract, full payment on completion out of distributable profits, then cancellation of the shares by the company", "D": "An ordinary resolution approving the contract, followed by payment out of distributable profits and the shares being held in treasury for resale", "E": "A special resolution approving the contract, with payment out of the share premium account because the shares were originally issued at a premium"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A special resolution approving the contract, full payment on completion out of distributable profits, then cancellation of the shares by the company): An off-market purchase needs a contract approved in advance by special resolution. The price must be paid in full when the shares are purchased, and a private company funding the buyback from its own resources must use distributable profits unless it follows the capital route. The company has enough profits here. Because the company is private, the shares cannot be held in treasury and must be cancelled."}, {"id": 988, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "The contractual completion date under a contract on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) was Friday 5 June. The buyer has not completed and the seller is ready, able and willing to do so. On Monday 8 June the seller's solicitor serves a notice to complete in accordance with the contract. Assume no bank holidays fall in June. What is the last day on which the buyer can complete before time becomes of the essence?", "options": {"A": "Tuesday 23 June, because the ten working days start to run from the day after the notice is deemed received", "B": "Thursday 18 June, because the notice gives ten calendar days from the date on which it was served", "C": "Monday 22 June, because the buyer has ten working days after the day on which the notice is given", "D": "Friday 19 June, because the period of ten working days includes the day on which the notice was served", "E": "Monday 15 June, because the notice requires completion within five working days of service of the notice"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Monday 22 June, because the buyer has ten working days after the day on which the notice is given): A notice to complete requires completion within ten working days, excluding the day on which the notice is given. Counting from Monday 8 June, the ten working days run from Tuesday 9 June and end on Monday 22 June. Including the day of service gives 19 June, and counting calendar days gives 18 June, both of which misapply the rule. After the period expires time is of the essence and the buyer is in breach."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "During a party, Ravi gives Shona a hard playful shove from behind, intending only to make her stumble. Shona falls and fractures her wrist. Ravi did not think about the possibility of any injury. He is charged under section 47 Offences Against the Person Act 1861. Which of the following best describes the mens rea the prosecution must prove?", "options": {"A": "Negligence as to the risk of injury, because the offence is aimed at carelessness leading to bodily harm", "B": "Intention or recklessness as to the assault or battery only, with no need to prove that he foresaw any actual bodily harm", "C": "Intention to cause really serious harm, because the offence requires proof of the same intent as wounding with intent under section 18", "D": "Intention or recklessness as to causing some actual bodily harm, since the harm is the central element of the offence", "E": "Foresight of some physical harm of a minor kind, because section 47 requires the same mens rea as a section 20 offence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intention or recklessness as to the assault or battery only, with no need to prove that he foresaw any actual bodily harm): Section 47 requires only the mens rea for the underlying assault or battery, plus the fact that actual bodily harm was occasioned. The prosecution need not prove that the defendant intended or foresaw any harm. A fractured wrist is actual bodily harm, so Ravi's liability turns on his intention or recklessness as to the battery. The higher fault elements belong to sections 18 and 20."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "For six weeks Dev makes silent phone calls to Laura at her home, intending to frighten her. Laura, previously healthy, is diagnosed by a psychiatrist with a clinical anxiety disorder caused by the calls. She is not physically hurt. Dev is charged under section 47 Offences Against the Person Act 1861. Which of the following best describes his liability?", "options": {"A": "He is not guilty, because actual bodily harm is confined to physical injury and a mental condition cannot qualify however it was caused", "B": "He is guilty, because any fear or distress caused by the calls amounts to actual bodily harm without proof of illness", "C": "He is not guilty of the offence, because words and silence cannot amount to an assault when no physical act takes place", "D": "He can be guilty, because silent calls can be an assault and a recognised psychiatric illness counts as actual bodily harm", "E": "He is guilty, but only if the calls were made with intent to cause psychiatric injury, since foresight of harm is required"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He can be guilty, because silent calls can be an assault and a recognised psychiatric illness counts as actual bodily harm): Silent telephone calls can amount to an assault where the victim fears immediate unlawful violence. Actual bodily harm includes recognised psychiatric illness, though not mere emotions such as fear or distress, so expert evidence is needed. No intention or foresight of the harm itself is required for section 47. Dev's liability therefore depends on proof of the assault and the diagnosed condition."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Cleo and Dan, an unmarried couple, buy a house in their joint names for £250,000. Cleo pays £200,000 and Dan pays £50,000. The transfer form contains no declaration of their beneficial shares and there is no other document. They separate, and Cleo says she should have 80 per cent. Which of the following states the starting point a court will apply?", "options": {"A": "The starting point is that shares match their purchase contributions, so Cleo takes 80 per cent unless Dan shows otherwise", "B": "The starting point is that Dan has no beneficial interest, since he contributed less than a quarter of the price", "C": "The starting point is that the beneficial interests are equal, and Cleo must show that they intended unequal shares", "D": "The starting point is a resulting trust, because the couple's contributions to the purchase price were unequal", "E": "The starting point is that shares are fixed by what is fair, after the court looks at the whole course of dealing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The starting point is that the beneficial interests are equal, and Cleo must show that they intended unequal shares): Where a family home is bought in joint names and nothing else is said about the beneficial interests, the starting point in Stack v Dowden is that equity follows the law, so the shares are equal. The party seeking unequal shares has to show a different common intention, which can be inferred from the whole course of dealing. The size of the contributions is relevant evidence but not the starting point. Fairness comes in only where the intention has to be imputed."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Orchard Foods Ltd claims £70,000 from Penhale Machinery Ltd for breach of a contract to supply packing equipment. The claim is defended. The trial is expected to last two days, with oral expert evidence from one expert on each side, and there is no other complexity. The claimant's solicitor has experience of high value cases and wants the claim heard on the multi-track for its flexibility. To which track will the court most likely allocate it?", "options": {"A": "The multi-track, because any claim needing oral expert evidence at trial is outside the fixed costs tracks", "B": "The intermediate track, because the value is between £25,000 and £100,000 and the trial and expert evidence fit its limits", "C": "The fast track, because the trial lasts under three days and one expert each is within the fast track's limits", "D": "The multi-track, because the claimant has asked for it and the court follows the party who wants greater procedural flexibility", "E": "The small claims track, because the claim is a contract dispute with a single issue between two commercial parties"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The intermediate track, because the value is between £25,000 and £100,000 and the trial and expert evidence fit its limits): The intermediate track is for claims valued between £25,000 and £100,000 where the trial is not expected to exceed three days, oral expert evidence is limited to one expert per party in each field, and the case is not unusually complex. Orchard's claim meets each of those limits, so the court is likely to allocate it there. The fast track covers claims up to £25,000, so this claim is too valuable for it. Allocation depends on value and complexity, not on the preference of one party."}, {"id": 993, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Dominic Walsh and his colleague Priya Nair both live in Leeds. While on a conference trip to France, Dominic was driving a hire car and collided with a French lorry, injuring Priya. Priya suffered a fractured wrist and returned to England. She sues Dominic in the High Court of England and Wales for negligence. The claim is a non-contractual obligation arising from a tort and the Rome II rules, as retained in English law, apply. Which law will the court apply to the claim?", "options": {"A": "The law of the place where the hire car was registered, because that is where the vehicle is based", "B": "French law, because the damage occurred in France and the place of damage is the general rule for torts", "C": "French law, because the place where the event giving rise to damage occurred governs every road traffic claim heard in England", "D": "English law, because both parties lived in England when the damage occurred, which displaces the place of damage rule", "E": "The law chosen by the claimant, because a tort victim may select the most favourable law after the damage"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (English law, because both parties lived in England when the damage occurred, which displaces the place of damage rule): The general Rome II rule applies the law of the country where the damage occurs, which here would be France. That rule gives way where the claimant and the person claimed to be liable both have their habitual residence in the same country when the damage occurs, so English law governs. A choice of law after the event needs the agreement of all parties, so the claimant cannot simply select. The registration of the hire car plays no part in the analysis."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Zainab is the sole executor named in her uncle's will. She has not applied for probate. After his death she paid his funeral bill from his current account and sold his car at auction, keeping the proceeds in her own account until the estate was sorted. She now says she does not want the burden of acting and wishes to renounce. Can she do so?", "options": {"A": "No, because selling the car was an act of administration beyond funeral arrangements, so she has intermeddled and can no longer renounce", "B": "Yes, because paying the funeral expenses and selling the car were both necessary steps to preserve the estate", "C": "Yes, if the residuary beneficiaries consent in writing, because renunciation needs their approval rather than any step taken in the probate registry", "D": "No, because a person named as executor is bound to act and cannot refuse the office", "E": "Yes, because renunciation is open to any executor who has not yet taken out a grant of probate"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because selling the car was an act of administration beyond funeral arrangements, so she has intermeddled and can no longer renounce): An executor may renounce before acting, but acts of administration such as selling assets amount to intermeddling and prevent later renunciation. Paying a funeral bill alone would not usually do so, but selling the car and holding the proceeds goes further. Zainab is therefore treated as having accepted the office. The fact that no grant has been taken out is not the test, and no beneficiary consent is needed in any case."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Wren Plant Hire paid £18,000 in advance to Ostler Cranes for the hire of a crane for six months from 1 April. In March Ostler told Wren it would not supply any crane because it had sold the machine. Wren accepted that as ending the contract and has received no crane or any other benefit. Ostler has incurred no expenses in the matter. Wren is considering whether to claim its money back or to sue for damages. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Wren can recover only a proportion of the £18,000, because restitution of prepayments is limited to the value of the unperformed share of the six months", "B": "Wren can recover the £18,000 only by claiming damages for breach, because restitution is unavailable where the payee is in breach of the contract", "C": "Wren can recover the £18,000 as money paid for a consideration that has totally failed, or claim damages for its losses instead, but not both", "D": "Wren can keep any claim to the £18,000 only as a deposit, because money paid in advance is forfeit unless the contract gives a right to a refund", "E": "Wren can recover the £18,000 only if it first affirms the contract and gives Ostler a final chance to supply a crane within a reasonable time"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wren can recover the £18,000 as money paid for a consideration that has totally failed, or claim damages for its losses instead, but not both): Once Wren accepted the repudiation and terminated, it could recover money paid on the basis of a consideration that failed totally, because it received none of the promised performance. This restitutionary claim lies against a party in breach, and an innocent party may choose it instead of damages. Nothing was performed, so there is nothing to apportion. A prepayment is different from a deposit, which can be forfeited, and an election to affirm would defeat the claim."}, {"id": 996, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "The three lay trustees of a £700,000 trust use their statutory power to appoint a fund manager to manage the whole portfolio. They agree no written policy statement and, over three years, they never ask the manager for a report. The manager invests heavily in one speculative company and the trust loses £60,000. The trust instrument says nothing about delegation or about liability. The beneficiaries sue the trustees. Which of the following best describes the trustees' position?", "options": {"A": "They are liable only if they gained a personal benefit from the speculative investment made by the manager", "B": "They are liable as insurers of the fund, because trustees guarantee the performance of any agent they choose to appoint", "C": "They may be liable if they failed to show reasonable care in appointing the manager and reviewing its work, although the manager made the choices", "D": "They are not liable, because trustees who delegate to a properly chosen professional agent are released from responsibility for that agent's decisions", "E": "They are liable only if the trust instrument expressly required them to review the performance of an agent they appointed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They may be liable if they failed to show reasonable care in appointing the manager and reviewing its work, although the manager made the choices): Trustees who delegate under the Trustee Act 2000 remain subject to the statutory duty of care when selecting the agent, setting its terms and reviewing it. These trustees agreed no policy statement and never reviewed the manager, which suggests a breach of that duty. Delegation does not release them from responsibility, but they are not insurers either. The duty to review arises under the statute, whatever the instrument says."}, {"id": 997, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trustee sells 5,000 shares held by the trust at £2 each, in breach of trust, because the trust instrument required them to be kept until a beneficiary reached 25. The shares are now trading at £5 each and the beneficiary is 22. The beneficiaries want to know how the trustee's liability is measured. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The trustee must restore the fund to the position it would be in but for the breach, so he must pay for the shares at today's value", "B": "The trustee is liable only if the beneficiaries first prove that he made a personal profit from the sale of the shares", "C": "The trustee must repurchase the shares if able to do so, but no payment is due if they cannot be bought back", "D": "The trustee must pay only interest on the sale price from the date of sale, because equity treats a breach as a simple debt", "E": "The trustee need only restore £2 per share, the price actually received, because that was the loss at the date of the sale"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The trustee must restore the fund to the position it would be in but for the breach, so he must pay for the shares at today's value): Equitable compensation aims to put the trust fund in the position it would have been in had the breach not occurred, and the loss is assessed with hindsight at the time of trial. The shares are now worth more, so the cost of replacing them is the measure. The trustee's profit is not needed for liability, and the remedy is not limited to interest. Where repurchase is not possible, payment of the current value is still required."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A statute passed in 2003 is accepted to be a constitutional statute because it created an important new constitutional arrangement. In 2025 Parliament passed a general Act on public bodies that, read literally, conflicts with a key provision of the 2003 statute, but the 2025 Act does not mention the 2003 statute or show an intention to alter it. Luca Bianchi, a business owner affected, wants to rely on the 2025 Act. Which of the following best describes the likely legal position?", "options": {"A": "The court will declare the 2025 Act invalid, because it conflicts with a constitutional statute and the judges can review its validity", "B": "The 2025 Act prevails because it is later in time, but a court may suspend its effect while Parliament reconsiders", "C": "The 2003 statute prevails, because Parliament has given constitutional statutes entrenched status that no later Act can change without a referendum", "D": "The 2025 Act prevails, because the doctrine of implied repeal applies to every Act of Parliament including a constitutional one", "E": "The earlier statute is not impliedly repealed, because a constitutional statute can be displaced by express words or specific words showing that intention, not by general words"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The earlier statute is not impliedly repealed, because a constitutional statute can be displaced by express words or specific words showing that intention, not by general words): Constitutional statutes are an exception to the normal rule of implied repeal. They are not impliedly repealed by a later general Act, and Parliament must show its intention to alter them by express words or specific words that make that intention unmistakable. The 2025 Act does neither. The court has no power to strike the 2025 Act down or suspend it, and constitutional statutes are not entrenched against express repeal by Parliament."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trust gives its capital equally to three siblings, Pia, Quentin and Rhys, aged 25, 27 and 29, contingently on each reaching 30. No other person has any interest in the fund, and the trust has no power to accumulate. The siblings are mentally capable and want the trustees to hand over the whole fund to them now, in equal shares. The trustees are reluctant, pointing to the age condition. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "They can only do so with the court's approval, which must be given on behalf of the beneficiaries", "B": "They cannot, because the age condition in the instrument binds the trustees, who have no power to vary it for adult beneficiaries", "C": "They can end it only for Rhys, who is closest to thirty, because each beneficiary must act alone", "D": "They can only do so if the trustees agree, since beneficiaries cannot compel a trust to come to an end", "E": "They may end the trust and call for the fund, being adult, of sound mind and together absolutely entitled to all of it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (They may end the trust and call for the fund, being adult, of sound mind and together absolutely entitled to all of it): Under the rule in Saunders v Vautier, beneficiaries who are all adult and of sound mind and who are together entitled to the whole fund, with no other person having an interest, can direct the trustees to end the trust and transfer the property. The age condition protects only these beneficiaries, so they may waive it together. Trustees cannot refuse, and no court approval is needed for competent adults."}, {"id": 1000, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Cartwright Properties Ltd has been served with an enforcement notice by the local planning authority and has a right of appeal to the Planning Inspectorate that it has not used. The company's director tells his solicitor to issue a judicial review claim against the notice, saying that the real aim is to delay enforcement for six months while he sells the site. Counsel has advised that no ground of review is properly arguable. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Issue the claim but choose only the weakest ground, because that limits exposure to a wasted costs order while still gaining delay", "B": "Issue the claim but describe the purpose as protecting the company's rights, because honest delay is a legitimate aim in litigation", "C": "Issue the claim after the director signs a written indemnity, because the risk of an adverse costs order then rests wholly on him", "D": "Decline to issue the claim, explaining that it is not properly arguable and that the statutory appeal is the right route", "E": "Issue the claim on the director's instructions, because a solicitor must act on instructions and leave the court to decide on the merits"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to issue the claim, explaining that it is not properly arguable and that the statutory appeal is the right route): A solicitor must not put forward submissions that are not properly arguable and must not misuse the court's process, for example by issuing a claim purely to delay. Acting on instructions does not override these duties to the court, and a client indemnity does not make an unarguable claim proper. Judicial review is also a remedy of last resort, so the unused statutory appeal is a further reason for refusing permission. The solicitor should explain this and advise on the appeal."}, {"id": 1001, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Agricultural Marketing Act empowers a Minister to refer complaints from producers about milk prices to an independent committee of investigation, where the Minister thinks fit. Producers in one region complain, but the Minister declines to refer, saying that a referral would embarrass the government before an election and might be used by opponents. A producers' cooperative asks a solicitor whether the refusal is open to challenge. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "The refusal cannot be challenged, because the Act says only that the Minister may refer when he thinks fit, which gives him unreviewable discretion", "B": "The refusal is open to challenge only if the Minister gave no reasons, because a duty to give reasons arises in every case", "C": "The refusal is open to challenge only for bias, because a decision made for political reasons shows that the Minister was not impartial", "D": "The refusal is open to challenge only for breach of legitimate expectation, since the producers have been told nothing about the policy", "E": "The refusal is open to challenge as illegality, because the Minister relied on an irrelevant consideration and frustrated the purpose of the Act"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The refusal is open to challenge as illegality, because the Minister relied on an irrelevant consideration and frustrated the purpose of the Act): A discretion given by statute must be used to promote the policy and objects of the Act, and the courts will review a refusal based on irrelevant considerations or one that frustrates the Act's purpose, as in Padfield. Avoiding political embarrassment is not a proper reason for refusing to refer complaints that the Act is designed to send to the committee. The words 'thinks fit' do not make the decision unreviewable. Bias, legitimate expectation and reasons are not the natural grounds on these facts."}, {"id": 1002, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "On 3 February, Gwen Pritchard wrote to Ibrahim offering to sell him her vintage motorcycle for £6,500, saying nothing about how long the offer would stay open. Ibrahim did not reply. On 10 August he posted a letter accepting and enclosing a cheque. Gwen had by then been offered more by another buyer and refused to sell to Ibrahim. He claims that a contract exists. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "No contract exists, because an offer made by letter can be accepted only within fourteen days of the date on which it was written", "B": "No contract exists, because Gwen's dealings with the other buyer revoked her offer to Ibrahim before he sent his acceptance in August", "C": "A contract exists, because Ibrahim posted his acceptance and the postal rule makes acceptance effective on posting even after a long delay", "D": "A contract exists, because the offer stayed open until Gwen expressly revoked it and Ibrahim accepted before any revocation was communicated to him", "E": "No contract exists, because an offer with no stated duration lapses after a reasonable time and six months had passed before Ibrahim accepted"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No contract exists, because an offer with no stated duration lapses after a reasonable time and six months had passed before Ibrahim accepted): Where an offer states no time limit it ends after a reasonable time, and what is reasonable depends on the subject matter. A motorcycle offered by letter in February would not still be open in August, so there was nothing left for Ibrahim to accept. The postal rule fixes when an effective acceptance takes place but cannot revive an offer that has lapsed. Gwen's dealings with the other buyer are irrelevant because revocation must be communicated, and none was."}, {"id": 1003, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Aldermoor Ltd leased shop premises to Beatrix at £2,400 a month. In March, because of roadworks outside, Aldermoor wrote promising to accept £1,200 a month 'until the works finish'. Beatrix paid £1,200 monthly until the works ended in October. In December, Aldermoor demanded the £1,200 shortfall for each of the eight months. Beatrix had in fact been trading at a loss throughout. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Beatrix cannot resist the claim, because promissory estoppel can be used as a cause of action but not as a defence to a debt", "B": "Beatrix can resist the claim, because Aldermoor's promise, which she relied on, estops it from recovering the reduction for the period of the works", "C": "Beatrix cannot resist the claim, because her payment of a lesser sum gave Aldermoor no fresh consideration for its promise to accept less", "D": "Beatrix can resist the claim only if she shows that she paid the lower sum in return for some practical benefit given to Aldermoor in exchange", "E": "Beatrix can resist the claim, and the promise also reduces the rent to £1,200 for the whole of the remaining term of the lease"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Beatrix can resist the claim, because Aldermoor's promise, which she relied on, estops it from recovering the reduction for the period of the works): Promissory estoppel prevents a party who has made a clear promise to waive rights from going back on it when the other has relied on it. Here Aldermoor promised a reduced rent for the period of the works and Beatrix acted on it, so Aldermoor cannot claim the arrears. The estoppel is suspensory, so full rent can be demanded again once the works ended. Consideration is not needed, and her losses are irrelevant."}, {"id": 1004, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Delmar Solicitors closed the file on the estate of Mrs Lena Frost three years ago after the final distribution left a nil balance on the client ledger. The practice manager proposes to destroy the file, together with the ledger entries, bank statements and bills for that estate, to clear storage, saying that the nil balance means no accounting record is needed. The firm remains authorised and has not closed. Which of the following is the most appropriate response from the firm's compliance officer?", "options": {"A": "They may be destroyed now, because the client ledger shows a nil balance and so no record of the estate is required", "B": "They must be kept for six years, but only the ledger and not the bank statements or bills, which can be destroyed", "C": "They may be destroyed now, because records need to be kept for only three years from the date the final balance reached nil", "D": "Keep the accounting records, including ledger and bank statements, for at least six years from the last entry, so they cannot be destroyed yet", "E": "They must be kept for twelve years, because accounting records for probate matters follow the limitation period for claims on deeds unless a fixed storage period applies"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Keep the accounting records, including ledger and bank statements, for at least six years from the last entry, so they cannot be destroyed yet): Accounting records, including ledgers, bank statements and bills, must be kept for at least six years from the date of the last entry. A nil balance does not shorten that period, and the records cannot be selectively discarded. The estate was closed only three years ago, so the firm must retain everything for a further three years at least."}, {"id": 1005, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Eldridge Print Ltd is being sued by a customer, Mr Haldane, in a claim already issued in the county court. Eldridge's solicitor wrote to Mr Haldane's solicitor offering £25,000 'in full and final settlement, open for 14 days'. The letter did not mention Part 36. Mr Haldane rejected it and recovered £27,000 at trial. Eldridge's solicitor says the offer carries Part 36 consequences for costs. Is that right?", "options": {"A": "No, because a defendant can only offer settlement by a Calderbank letter, and Part 36 offers must be made by the claimant", "B": "No, the letter is not a Part 36 offer, but the court may still consider it under the general costs rules", "C": "Yes, a written settlement offer with a stated time limit becomes a Part 36 offer once it has been served on the other party", "D": "No, so the court cannot take the offer into account, because only Part 36 offers have any effect on costs", "E": "Yes, because the court treats any offer expressed to be in full and final settlement as made under Part 36"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, the letter is not a Part 36 offer, but the court may still consider it under the general costs rules): To be a Part 36 offer under CPR 36.5, the offer must be in writing, state that it is made pursuant to Part 36, specify a period of not less than 21 days and say whether it relates to the whole claim. This letter fails those requirements. It is therefore a non Part 36 or Calderbank type offer, which the court may take into account under CPR 44.2 when exercising its discretion on costs. Both claimants and defendants can make Part 36 offers."}, {"id": 1006, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Brooke and Lyle, a firm of estate agents in Taunton, offers Tamsin Hurley, a solicitor at a local firm, £120 for each conveyancing client the agents introduce to her firm. The firm already has a good working relationship with the agents. A new client, Mr Imran Wells, is about to be introduced on a purchase of a flat. The matter is not a personal injury claim. Which of the following is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The arrangement is permitted provided Mr Wells is told in writing of the fee before instructions are accepted and the firm's advice stays independent", "B": "The arrangement is prohibited, because referral fees for conveyancing instructions are banned in the same way as personal injury referral fees", "C": "The arrangement is permitted provided the firm passes the £120 on to Mr Wells as a reduction in its fee", "D": "The arrangement is permitted if the SRA has approved it in advance and the estate agents are themselves regulated", "E": "The arrangement is permitted without telling Mr Wells, because the fee is paid by the firm and not by the client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The arrangement is permitted provided Mr Wells is told in writing of the fee before instructions are accepted and the firm's advice stays independent): Referral fees outside personal injury are lawful, but the client must be told about the arrangement in writing before instructions are accepted, and the firm must keep its advice independent of the referrer. Mr Wells is entitled to know that the agents benefit from introducing him. The statutory ban applies to personal injury, so it does not apply to a conveyancing purchase. There is no need for SRA approval and no duty to pass the fee on to the client."}, {"id": 1007, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Ellis and his sister Ruth bought a house in 2005 as joint tenants, Mr Ellis contributing 80 per cent of the price. His will, made in 2022, leaves 'my house, 14 Mill Lane, to my nephew Sam' and the residue to his daughter. Mr Ellis died in 2026 and no notice of severance had ever been served. Who is entitled to the house?", "options": {"A": "Mr Ellis's daughter, because the gift to Sam fails and the residue passes to her", "B": "Ruth and Sam each take half, because the will severs the joint tenancy at the date of death", "C": "Ruth takes a one fifth share and Sam takes four fifths, because shares follow the contributions to the price", "D": "Ruth, because the tenancy was not severed and she takes the house by survivorship, outside the will", "E": "Sam, because a specific gift of the house in a will takes effect over survivorship"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ruth, because the tenancy was not severed and she takes the house by survivorship, outside the will): Property held as joint tenants passes automatically to the surviving joint tenant, and so does not form part of the deceased's estate. A will cannot sever a joint tenancy, and shares are not apportioned by contributions while the tenancy continues. Ruth therefore takes the house in full by survivorship. The gift to Sam has nothing to bite on, and nothing falls into the residue."}, {"id": 1008, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Elsie was sentenced at the Crown Court to four years' imprisonment for burglary. She appeals with leave to the Court of Appeal on the ground that the sentence was manifestly excessive. In argument the judges say that, on the facts, they think four years was in fact rather lenient. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court may increase the sentence by up to one third, as a penalty for bringing an unmeritorious appeal", "B": "The court may increase the sentence once the Attorney General has been invited to join the same hearing", "C": "The court cannot increase the sentence on her appeal, and may dismiss the appeal or substitute a lower sentence", "D": "The court may order a retrial before a different judge so that a fresh and heavier sentence can be passed after hearing from both sides", "E": "The court may increase the sentence, because it has a free hand once the sentence is before it on appeal"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court cannot increase the sentence on her appeal, and may dismiss the appeal or substitute a lower sentence): On an appeal against sentence by the defendant, the Court of Appeal cannot deal with her more severely than the Crown Court did. It can dismiss the appeal, quash the sentence and substitute a lower one. Increasing a sentence requires a separate Attorney General's reference for an unduly lenient sentence, which must be made within 28 days. A loss of time order is the only added consequence for a hopeless appeal."}, {"id": 1009, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "In March 2022 Hana attacked Rik with a hammer intending to kill him. Rik was left in a persistent vegetative state and was cared for at home. He died in November 2026 from complications that the medical evidence attributes to the injuries. Hana has not previously been prosecuted over the attack. The Crown now wishes to charge her with murder. What is the position on bringing proceedings?", "options": {"A": "A murder charge is barred, because under the year and a day rule death must occur within a year and a day of the act", "B": "A murder charge needs the personal consent of the Director of Public Prosecutions, because the delay exceeds three years from the attack", "C": "A murder charge can be brought without any consent, because Hana intended to kill and the three year rule applies only to manslaughter", "D": "A murder charge can be brought, but only with the Attorney General's consent, because death occurred more than three years after the injury was inflicted", "E": "Only attempted murder can be charged, because a death occurring after so long a delay is treated as a separate intervening event"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A murder charge can be brought, but only with the Attorney General's consent, because death occurred more than three years after the injury was inflicted): The year and a day rule was abolished by the Law Reform (Year and a Day Rule) Act 1996, so delay does not itself bar a murder charge. The Act does, however, require the Attorney General's consent where the injury was inflicted more than three years before death. The medical evidence still attributes the death to the hammer attack, so causation is intact. Consent from the Director of Public Prosecutions is not the statutory requirement, and the rule applies to murder and manslaughter alike."}, {"id": 1010, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Tilly Marsh bought an unregistered freehold house from Oscar Hale on 3 March and received the signed transfer deed on completion. Her solicitor was busy and made no application to HM Land Registry. On 15 June, Tilly realises that nothing has been done. The house has not been sold or mortgaged since completion, and Tilly has been living there. Tilly is anxious about her ownership. Which of the following correctly describes the effect of the failure to apply for first registration within two months?", "options": {"A": "The legal estate reverts to Oscar, who holds it on a bare trust for Tilly, pending her application", "B": "The legal estate is held by Tilly subject to a penalty fee, which the Land Registry will charge on a late application", "C": "The legal estate stays with Tilly, but she loses the right to rely on the transfer against a later purchaser", "D": "The legal estate reverts to Oscar outright, and Tilly keeps only a personal claim for return of the purchase price", "E": "The transfer is void and Tilly acquires no interest, because the failure to register within two months invalidates the contract itself"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The legal estate reverts to Oscar, who holds it on a bare trust for Tilly, pending her application): Under sections 6 and 7 of the Land Registration Act 2002, a transfer of unregistered land triggers a two month period for applying for first registration. If the period expires without an application, the transfer becomes void so far as it transferred the legal estate, and the transferor holds the legal estate on a bare trust for the transferee. Tilly therefore has an equitable interest and must apply for registration, an extension being possible for good reason. Her claim is not merely personal, and there is no fixed penalty fee."}, {"id": 1011, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Rowan Yates, a solicitor, acts for a seller, Marcus Fell, and gives an undertaking to the seller's existing lender to redeem the mortgage from the sale proceeds on completion. Completion takes place and £210,000 is received. Marcus then instructs Rowan to pay him all of the proceeds because he has agreed to pay off the lender himself the following week. The lender has not agreed to any change. Which of the following is the correct course for Rowan?", "options": {"A": "Redeem the mortgage from the proceeds as undertaken, because Marcus's instructions cannot release Rowan and only the lender can vary it", "B": "Hold the proceeds on client account until Marcus and the lender agree a new arrangement, which delays the redemption", "C": "Pay Marcus as instructed, because the client is the principal and Rowan must follow his lawful instructions about his own money from the sale", "D": "Pay part of the proceeds to Marcus and keep enough to redeem the mortgage in full next week", "E": "Pay Marcus as instructed once he signs an indemnity in favour of the firm against any loss from the unredeemed mortgage"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Redeem the mortgage from the proceeds as undertaken, because Marcus's instructions cannot release Rowan and only the lender can vary it): An undertaking binds the solicitor personally and can be released or varied only by the person who received it. Marcus is not that person, so his instructions and any indemnity he offers cannot excuse Rowan. She must redeem the mortgage from the proceeds on the terms she gave, even though this goes against his wishes. Holding the money or paying out in part would also fail to perform the undertaking within the time promised."}, {"id": 1012, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "In 2015 a trustee makes an unauthorised investment that loses £70,000. The trust gives income to Cal, now aged 70, for life, with the capital then to Bea. Cal is still alive. Bea's interest is in remainder and has not yet fallen into possession. In 2026 Bea wishes to sue the trustee for the loss. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "The claim is barred unless Bea shows that the trustee concealed the breach, since only concealment can extend time", "B": "The claim is not barred, since Bea can sue only after Cal's death and any earlier claim would be premature", "C": "The claim is not barred, because for a future interest time under section 21(3) does not begin until the interest falls into possession", "D": "The claim is not barred, because beneficiaries receive a fresh six years from the date they first learn of the trust", "E": "The claim is barred, because time began to run in 2015 and a remainder interest gives the beneficiary no extra time"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claim is not barred, because for a future interest time under section 21(3) does not begin until the interest falls into possession): Section 21(3) Limitation Act 1980 provides that the right of action of a beneficiary with a future interest does not accrue until the interest falls into possession. Bea's interest in remainder has not done so while Cal is alive, so the six years has not started to run for her. Concealment is not needed, and the claim is not premature because she can sue now. The fresh start from knowledge of the trust is not a rule."}, {"id": 1013, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Marcus pleads guilty at the magistrates' court to criminal damage. He committed it while on bail for an unrelated assault charge, which was later discontinued. He also has a conviction for criminal damage from two years ago, and he has expressed genuine remorse. His solicitor expects the court to treat some features as aggravating. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The earlier conviction is aggravating automatically, whatever its age or its relevance to the present offence, because the court looks at record alone", "B": "Bail is aggravating only if the assault charge ended in conviction, so the discontinued charge adds nothing to the sentence", "C": "The court must treat commission on bail as aggravating, and the recent similar conviction may be too", "D": "Bail is aggravating, but the previous conviction cannot be considered because it was dealt with by a different court", "E": "Neither feature is aggravating by statute, and the court may rely on them only as general background to sentence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court must treat commission on bail as aggravating, and the recent similar conviction may be too): Under the Sentencing Act 2020 the court must treat the commission of an offence while on bail as an aggravating factor, and the fate of the earlier charge does not change that. A previous conviction is treated as aggravating where, having regard to its nature and the time elapsed, it can reasonably be so treated, which a similar conviction two years ago can. The court can take into account convictions from any court. Remorse is a separate mitigating factor and does not cancel the aggravation."}, {"id": 1014, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Whitcombe Law is an exempt professional firm for the purposes of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000. A client, Mrs Banerjee, has instructed it to administer her late husband's estate, which includes shares in several listed companies. The executors ask the firm to advise on which shares to sell to raise cash for inheritance tax, and the firm will receive a small commission from a stockbroker for placing the sale. Which of the following is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The firm may advise and arrange the sales as activities arising from the probate work, if incidental and the commission is accounted for to the client", "B": "The firm may advise, but it must first obtain full Financial Conduct Authority authorisation, because advising on listed shares cannot be incidental to probate work", "C": "The firm may keep the commission as additional remuneration, because the exemption removes the duty to account to the client for third party payments", "D": "The firm cannot advise on the sale of any shares, because exempt professional firms are prohibited from carrying on investment business of any kind", "E": "The firm may advise only if the executors are themselves authorised persons, because the exemption covers dealings with authorised persons only"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm may advise and arrange the sales as activities arising from the probate work, if incidental and the commission is accounted for to the client): Part 20 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 allows an exempt professional firm to carry on regulated activities that arise out of, or are complementary to, the provision of its professional services, provided they are incidental. Selling shares to fund inheritance tax is part of administering the estate, so it falls within that exemption. The firm must still account to the client for any commission it receives, as the exemption depends on the firm not being separately remunerated for the activity. Authorisation of the executors is not required."}, {"id": 1015, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ms Bright is hit by a negligently driven van while crossing the road and sues the driver's employer. In conference she tells her solicitor that she was crossing against a red pedestrian signal, though she believes the driver was speeding. She now wants the particulars of claim to say that the signal was green. The solicitor knows that the employer's insurer will argue contributory negligence. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Plead that the signal was green as instructed, because the solicitor can rely on the client's own account of the events in dispute", "B": "Cease acting at once and report the client to the court for misleading the solicitor about the facts", "C": "Plead nothing about the signal and prepare a witness statement saying Ms Bright cannot recall the colour of the light", "D": "Refuse to plead a green signal, advise that damages may be reduced for contributory negligence, and continue on a truthful basis", "E": "Plead that the signal was green but warn the client that she must correct it if cross-examined at trial"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refuse to plead a green signal, advise that damages may be reduced for contributory negligence, and continue on a truthful basis): A solicitor must not mislead the court or allow it to be misled, so a statement the solicitor knows to be false cannot be pleaded. The correct course is to refuse the instruction, advise Ms Bright that her damages may be reduced for contributory negligence, and continue only if she agrees to a truthful case. Her conduct would reduce the award, not remove it, since the driver was speeding. A misleading omission is no better than a false statement, and reporting the client is not required."}, {"id": 1016, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Meera Chaudhry died leaving an estate of £90,000 and unsecured debts of £35,000. Her son, Anil, who is the sole executor, arranged a funeral costing £4,800 for a dignified service with a wake for close family. He paid the undertaker from his own pocket. Anil asks the solicitor how the funeral expenses rank when he deals with the estate and whether he can be reimbursed. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "Reasonable funeral expenses are a first charge on the estate, ahead of the unsecured debts, so Anil can reimburse himself from the estate", "B": "Funeral expenses can be paid from the estate only after all legacies and the debts have been satisfied, as the lowest ranking claim", "C": "Funeral expenses are repaid in full from the estate whatever their amount, provided the executor holds the receipt for the payment in the circumstances of the estate", "D": "Funeral expenses are the personal liability of the family members who arranged them and cannot be repaid from the estate assets", "E": "Funeral expenses rank equally with the unsecured debts, so Anil can recover only a proportionate share of the cost from estate assets"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reasonable funeral expenses are a first charge on the estate, ahead of the unsecured debts, so Anil can reimburse himself from the estate): Reasonable funeral expenses are a priority claim on the estate and are paid before ordinary unsecured debts. Anil as executor had the duty to arrange the funeral, and he is entitled to be reimbursed from estate assets for what he reasonably spent. They do not rank behind debts or legacies. The limit is reasonableness: an extravagant funeral may be cut back, so receipts alone do not secure full repayment."}, {"id": 1017, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Samira wishes to bring an employment tribunal claim for unfair dismissal and discrimination and has no funds to pay a solicitor. The firm offers a damages-based agreement under which it will receive 40 per cent of any compensation awarded, including its expenses, and nothing if she loses. The agreement is in writing and signed by both. Samira later wins and is awarded £50,000. Which of the following best describes the effect of the 40 per cent term?", "options": {"A": "The agreement is void as to the unfair dismissal element, because the cap applies to discrimination awards but not other compensation", "B": "The agreement is enforceable, because the 50 per cent cap for commercial claims applies and the agreement was in writing", "C": "The agreement is enforceable, because a cap applies to the solicitor's profit costs and the expenses were excluded from the percentage", "D": "The agreement is valid for the first 25 per cent only, because the personal injury cap applies to all employment compensation", "E": "The agreement is unenforceable, because the payment exceeds the 35 per cent cap for damages-based agreements in employment tribunal claims"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The agreement is unenforceable, because the payment exceeds the 35 per cent cap for damages-based agreements in employment tribunal claims): The Damages-Based Agreements Regulations 2013 cap the payment at 35 per cent of the compensation in employment tribunal claims, and the payment must include the solicitor's expenses. An agreement that does not comply is unenforceable. The 50 per cent figure is the general cap for other civil claims and 25 per cent applies to personal injury. The cap is not split between heads of loss within a single award, and the fact that the agreement was written and signed does not cure the breach."}, {"id": 1018, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Dalesman Productions spent £30,000 on pre-contract preparation, then agreed on 1 March to film a series at Whitby Studios for £50,000. It spent a further £20,000 preparing, which it would have spent in any event. Whitby wrongfully cancelled on 1 April. Dalesman cannot prove what profit it would have made, because the series' success is unknowable. Whitby says that Dalesman cannot show Whitby's breach caused the loss. Which of the following is the best advice on the damages Dalesman can claim?", "options": {"A": "Dalesman may claim only nominal damages, because it cannot prove the profit it lost and reliance loss is unavailable where expectation loss is uncertain", "B": "Dalesman may claim the £50,000 contract price, because where profit cannot be proved the price is the measure of damages", "C": "Dalesman may claim only the £20,000 spent after the contract, because expenditure before the contract was not incurred in reliance on it", "D": "Dalesman may claim its wasted expenditure, including the £30,000 pre-contract spend, as reliance loss, unless Whitby shows it would not have been recouped", "E": "Dalesman may claim both its £50,000 expected price and its £50,000 of expenditure, because expectation and reliance losses are cumulative"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dalesman may claim its wasted expenditure, including the £30,000 pre-contract spend, as reliance loss, unless Whitby shows it would not have been recouped): A claimant who cannot prove expectation loss may instead claim wasted expenditure as reliance loss, and this can include expenditure incurred before the contract if it was within the parties' contemplation. The burden then falls on the defendant to show that the claimant would not have recouped it had the contract been performed. Expectation and reliance loss are alternatives and cannot be added together. The contract price is not the measure of damages for a breach by the party that was to be paid."}, {"id": 1019, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Priya, a consumer, bought a new washing machine from Heath Appliances for £520 online. Two weeks after delivery the drum bearing failed and the machine could no longer spin clothes. Heath offers a repair, but Priya wants her money back. She has not used the machine since the failure, and it is now 18 days after delivery. Which of the following is the best advice on her rights?", "options": {"A": "Priya must first allow Heath one attempt at repair or replacement, because the short-term right to reject arises only after that remedy fails", "B": "Priya may reject the machine, but the refund is reduced to reflect the value she obtained from using it during the 18 days before it failed", "C": "Priya may claim only damages for the loss in value, because the right to reject is lost once goods have been delivered and used", "D": "Priya may reject only if she can prove that the defect existed when the goods were delivered, because the burden of proof rests on her", "E": "Priya may exercise the short-term right to reject and claim a full refund, because the goods are not of satisfactory quality and 30 days have not passed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Priya may exercise the short-term right to reject and claim a full refund, because the goods are not of satisfactory quality and 30 days have not passed): Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, a consumer may reject goods that are not of satisfactory quality within 30 days and receive a full refund. The trader cannot insist on a repair first when the short-term right is used, and there is no deduction for use in that period. Any fault appearing within six months is presumed to have existed at delivery, so the burden is on Heath, not Priya."}, {"id": 1020, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Marchmont Textiles Ltd sued a supplier for £60,000 damages and, 10 weeks before trial, served a Part 36 offer to accept £50,000 which complied with the formal requirements and gave the minimum period. The supplier did not respond. At trial the court awarded Marchmont £65,000 on the same issues. Marchmont's solicitor is considering what to ask for on costs and interest. The judge sees no reason of injustice in the circumstances. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Standard costs and interest at the judgment rate, because the consequences follow only if the defendant accepts after the relevant period", "B": "Enhanced interest, indemnity costs, and an additional amount of 10 per cent of the damages, unless the court finds it unjust", "C": "Indemnity costs only, because the additional amount and enhanced interest apply to a defendant's offer that the claimant fails to beat", "D": "Costs on the standard basis, because the judgment beat the offer by only £15,000 and the consequences require a margin of 10 per cent", "E": "Enhanced interest and indemnity costs, but no additional amount, because that amount applies only on claims over £500,000"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Enhanced interest, indemnity costs, and an additional amount of 10 per cent of the damages, unless the court finds it unjust): Where a claimant's Part 36 offer is not accepted and the judgment is at least as advantageous to the claimant, CPR 36.17(4) gives the court power to order enhanced interest of up to 10 per cent above base rate, indemnity costs from the expiry of the relevant period, interest on those costs, and an additional amount of 10 per cent of the damages awarded on a claim of this size. These follow unless it would be unjust. No margin is needed beyond matching or beating the offer."}, {"id": 1021, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Ifeoma is a conveyancing solicitor acting for a buyer, Mr Dunn. During the matter she comes to suspect that part of the purchase money is the proceeds of drug dealing. She considers that reporting would damage the relationship and decides to complete the purchase on the agreed date and mention her concerns to the nominated officer afterwards. Mr Dunn then becomes the registered owner. Which of the following best describes her position?", "options": {"A": "Ifeoma has a defence, because reporting to the nominated officer after completion protects her from liability for the completed purchase", "B": "Ifeoma commits no offence, because suspicion is not enough and she must have actual knowledge that the funds derive from crime", "C": "Ifeoma risks the arrangements offence, because completing on suspicion without prior disclosure and consent facilitates acquisition of the funds", "D": "Ifeoma commits no offence, because only the buyer who knows the funds are criminal can commit an offence in relation to the purchase", "E": "Ifeoma commits an offence only if Mr Dunn is later convicted of drug dealing, because the offence depends on a conviction for the predicate crime"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ifeoma risks the arrangements offence, because completing on suspicion without prior disclosure and consent facilitates acquisition of the funds): Section 328 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 is committed where a person enters into or becomes concerned in an arrangement which they know or suspect facilitates the acquisition, retention, use or control of criminal property. Suspicion is sufficient, and no conviction for the underlying crime is needed. The defence is an authorised disclosure made before the act with consent, not a report after completion. Completing the purchase after suspicion arose therefore exposes Ifeoma, and the proper course was to report first and wait for consent."}, {"id": 1022, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "At the trial of a contract claim in the county court, Mr Ellison, the claimant, is the first witness and his witness statement has been admitted as his evidence in chief. His barrister, who is asking some further questions to expand on the statement, says to him: 'You told the defendant's manager that the delivery date was essential, did you not?' This is the central disputed fact in the case. The defendant's advocate rises to object. How is the judge likely to rule?", "options": {"A": "The objection will fail, because leading questions are permitted whenever the witness is a party to the proceedings", "B": "The objection will be upheld, because leading questions on a contested matter are not allowed in examination in chief", "C": "The objection will be upheld, because leading questions are improper in all stages of questioning, including cross examination", "D": "The objection will fail, because any matter that appears in the witness statement may be put to the witness in leading form", "E": "The objection will fail if the judge gives permission, which is routinely granted to expedite evidence on the central issue"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The objection will be upheld, because leading questions on a contested matter are not allowed in examination in chief): In examination in chief a party may not lead his own witness on contested matters, because the evidence must come from the witness. Leading is allowed on introductory or undisputed points, and in cross examination, where it is the norm. Here the question suggests the answer on the very point in issue, so the judge is likely to uphold the objection and require the question to be rephrased. A witness statement does not remove the restriction when further oral evidence is given."}, {"id": 1023, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Kestrel and Marsh LLP, a firm with 60 staff, introduces a rule that all fee earners must be available for client meetings at 7.30 am each weekday. Priya, a part-time solicitor who takes her children to school every morning, is the only member of her team unable to attend, and she says the rule disadvantages women. The firm replies that the rule applies to everyone equally. Which of the following best states the test the tribunal will apply to Priya's indirect discrimination claim?", "options": {"A": "The rule is lawful because it applies to all fee earners equally, so no comparison between different groups of staff is needed", "B": "Priya must show the rule treats her less favourably than a named male colleague in materially identical circumstances, as a direct discrimination claim would require", "C": "The rule must put women at a particular disadvantage, Priya must suffer it, and the firm must be unable to show proportionality", "D": "Priya must show the firm intended to disadvantage women, because indirect discrimination requires proof of a discriminatory motive behind the rule", "E": "The firm need only show a genuine business reason for the rule, and the tribunal will not weigh its effect on women"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The rule must put women at a particular disadvantage, Priya must suffer it, and the firm must be unable to show proportionality): Indirect discrimination under section 19 of the Equality Act 2010 arises where a provision, criterion or practice applied to everyone puts persons sharing a protected characteristic at a particular disadvantage, puts the claimant at that disadvantage, and cannot be shown to be a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim. Equal application is the starting point of the claim and not a defence. Intention is irrelevant, and no individual comparator is needed because the comparison is between groups. A bare business reason fails unless the tribunal is satisfied the means are proportionate."}, {"id": 1024, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Colin Marsh died intestate in April, leaving an estate worth £310,000 that includes the matrimonial home, which was in his sole name. His widow, Frances, intends to apply for letters of administration and is the only person entitled. Before the grant she signed a contract to sell the home to a buyer, believing she could act at once as the person entitled to his estate. The buyer's solicitors have asked for evidence of her authority to sell. Which of the following best describes Frances's position?", "options": {"A": "She could sell as the surviving spouse, because land passing on intestacy to a spouse vests in her immediately without any grant without needing any grant of representation", "B": "She could sell because a person entitled on intestacy has the same powers as any beneficiary who has taken possession", "C": "She had authority from the date of death, as an executor would, though she needs the grant to prove title on completion", "D": "Her authority as administrator comes from the grant and not from death, so she had no power to contract to sell before it was issued", "E": "She could sell because the estate is below the value above which a grant of representation is needed to deal with land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Her authority as administrator comes from the grant and not from death, so she had no power to contract to sell before it was issued): An executor's authority derives from the will and arises on death, but an administrator's authority derives only from the grant. Until the grant is issued, nobody has power to sell Colin's land, so Frances could not validly bind the estate. Being the surviving spouse does not change this, and there is no value threshold that lets a person sell land without proof of title. The buyer's request for evidence of authority is therefore well founded."}, {"id": 1025, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A shop lease allows the tenant to assign with the landlord's written consent, such consent not to be unreasonably withheld. The tenant, Ellis Ltd, wants to assign to Fenn Ltd, a retailer with strong accounts and good references. The landlord, which hopes to redevelop the whole parade, refuses consent solely so that Ellis Ltd will be forced to surrender the lease to it on poor terms. Which of the following best describes the landlord's refusal?", "options": {"A": "It is unreasonable, because withholding consent to achieve a purpose collateral to the landlord and tenant relationship is not a proper ground", "B": "It is reasonable, because the covenant requires consent and the landlord has given its decision without delay", "C": "It is unreasonable, because the landlord has not proposed an alternative tenant to take the shop on better terms", "D": "It is reasonable, because a landlord may protect the value of its reversion and its own redevelopment plans for the whole parade when deciding on consent", "E": "It is unreasonable only if Fenn Ltd would pay a higher rent than Ellis Ltd pays under the lease"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is unreasonable, because withholding consent to achieve a purpose collateral to the landlord and tenant relationship is not a proper ground): A landlord may refuse consent only on grounds relating to the landlord and tenant relationship, such as the financial standing of the assignee or the effect on the premises. Refusing in order to force a surrender is a purpose collateral to that relationship and is not a reasonable ground. The landlord's wish to protect its redevelopment plans does not turn a collateral aim into a legitimate one, and a quick decision does not cure an improper reason. The landlord has no duty to offer a replacement tenant."}, {"id": 1026, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A firm completed a residential conveyancing matter in 2021 and has closed its file. In 2026 the practice manager reviews the file storage policy and asks how long the firm must keep the client ledger entries, bank statements and other accounting records for that matter under the SRA Accounts Rules. The firm keeps its records electronically and has no other policy. Which of the following states the minimum retention period?", "options": {"A": "Twelve years, because that is the limitation period for actions on a deed such as the transfer", "B": "One year after closure, because the Accounts Rules require records only until the file is archived", "C": "Three years, because accounting records need be kept only for the period during which HMRC may inspect the firm's returns", "D": "Six years, because the Accounts Rules require accounting records to be kept for at least six years", "E": "Fifteen years, because that is the longstop limitation period for a negligence claim for latent damage"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Six years, because the Accounts Rules require accounting records to be kept for at least six years): The SRA Accounts Rules require a firm to keep its accounting records, including client ledgers and bank statements, for at least six years. The period is a regulatory minimum and does not depend on the limitation period for claims on the underlying transaction. Limitation periods of twelve and fifteen years may be reasons for the firm to keep its client file longer, but they do not set the accounting records rule. Electronic storage is acceptable provided the records can be produced when needed."}, {"id": 1027, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for both Damien Okoro and his mortgage lender on the purchase of a registered house. A neighbour tells the solicitor that the seller's former partner, Janine, still lives at the property and claims a share. Damien is keen to complete and says that the lender need not be told because it will upset the sale. The lender's instructions require the solicitor to report any matters that could affect its security. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Report the claim to the lender without telling Damien, because the duty to the lender overrides any duty owed to a borrower", "B": "Continue to act for both and ask Janine to sign a waiver of her rights, which removes any need to inform the lender", "C": "Tell Damien the lender must be informed, and cease acting for the lender if he refuses to allow disclosure", "D": "Withhold the claim from the lender and obtain a title indemnity policy, because insurance removes the risk to the lender's security", "E": "Complete the purchase and mention the claim only if the lender asks, since a neighbour's remark amounts to hearsay"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell Damien the lender must be informed, and cease acting for the lender if he refuses to allow disclosure): A solicitor acting for a lender and a borrower must pass to the lender any information material to its decision to lend, including an occupier's claim that could bind the property, under the SRA Code of Conduct and the lender's instructions. Damien's wish for silence cannot override this. The solicitor must explain this to him, and if he refuses consent to disclosure a conflict arises, so the solicitor must stop acting for the lender and may have to stop for Damien as well. Insurance or a waiver does not cure non-disclosure."}, {"id": 1028, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Hannah Okafor inherits a freehold farm in Shropshire from her uncle. She intends to grant a neighbour a right of way over the farm track and to let a barn to a business tenant for ten years. Before advising on either transaction, her solicitor reminds her that, since 1925, a limited number of estates can exist as legal estates in land. Which of the following correctly identifies the estates that can exist at law under section 1 of the Law of Property Act 1925?", "options": {"A": "The fee simple absolute in possession and a fee simple subject to a condition, since each gives the holder outright ownership of the land", "B": "The term of years absolute and an equitable lease, since both allow the holder to occupy the land under a written agreement", "C": "The fee simple absolute in possession and a life estate, since each confers a present right to occupy the land for a defined period", "D": "The fee simple absolute in possession and the term of years absolute, which alone can exist as legal estates under the 1925 legislation", "E": "The term of years absolute and a fee tail, since both were expressly preserved as legal estates by the property legislation of 1925"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The fee simple absolute in possession and the term of years absolute, which alone can exist as legal estates under the 1925 legislation): Section 1(1) of the Law of Property Act 1925 limits legal estates to the fee simple absolute in possession and the term of years absolute. Hannah's freehold falls into the first category and the proposed ten year letting will create the second. A life estate and a conditional or determinable fee simple can exist in equity but not at law, and a fee tail cannot be a legal estate. An equitable lease is by definition not a legal estate until it is created by deed."}, {"id": 1029, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Maya and Cian Fitzpatrick own a house as beneficial joint tenants. Maya wants to ensure that her share will pass under her will to her daughter from an earlier relationship, rather than to Cian on survivorship. She asks her solicitor how she can sever the joint tenancy without Cian's agreement. Which of the following is the most straightforward way for Maya to achieve this?", "options": {"A": "Make a will leaving her share to her daughter, which severs the joint tenancy when it is signed and witnessed", "B": "Serve written notice on Cian under section 36(2) of the Law of Property Act 1925 declaring her wish to sever", "C": "Tell Cian orally that she no longer wishes to be a joint tenant, which severs the tenancy once he hears it", "D": "Register a restriction at the Land Registry, which takes effect as a severance of the joint tenancy from the date of entry", "E": "Move out of the house and stop paying towards it, which operates as a severance once a year has passed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Serve written notice on Cian under section 36(2) of the Law of Property Act 1925 declaring her wish to sever): A joint tenant can sever unilaterally by serving written notice on the other joint tenants under section 36(2) of the Law of Property Act 1925, which shows an immediate intention to sever. A will does not sever, because the right of survivorship takes effect on death and overrides it. An oral statement lacks the required form. Entering a restriction protects the position but does not itself sever. Leaving the property has no effect on the nature of the tenancy."}, {"id": 1030, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Lyle and Maura are partners in a garden design business. While visiting a client's property to take measurements, Lyle carelessly reversed the firm's van into a greenhouse, causing £12,000 of damage. Maura was in another city and knew nothing of the visit. The firm's assets are modest. The client wishes to recover the full amount and asks who may be sued. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Both are liable, but each only for half the damage, because partners share firm liabilities in equal proportions", "B": "Both Lyle and Maura are liable jointly and severally, because the wrong was committed in the ordinary course of the firm's business", "C": "Only Lyle is liable, because Maura took no part in the wrong and knew nothing of the visit", "D": "Neither is personally liable, because a claim for a wrong done in the course of the business lies only against the firm's assets", "E": "Both are liable, but only jointly, so the client must sue Lyle and Maura together in one claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Both Lyle and Maura are liable jointly and severally, because the wrong was committed in the ordinary course of the firm's business): A partnership is not a separate legal person, and the firm is liable for wrongs committed by a partner in the ordinary course of its business. In tort the liability of the partners is joint and several, so the client may sue either or both for the whole sum. Maura's absence does not help her. The rule that partners are only jointly liable applies to contract debts."}, {"id": 1031, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Fenwick Ltd is taking a ten year lease of a retail unit. The landlord's draft lease contains an absolute covenant prohibiting the tenant from making any alterations to the unit. Fenwick Ltd plans to remove a non-structural partition wall to improve the layout and says that, under the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, the landlord could not unreasonably refuse consent. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The landlord may refuse, because the 1927 Act rule on improvements applies only where the covenant is qualified by a consent requirement", "B": "The landlord may refuse, but only after the tenant has given a month's notice, because the statute requires notice of proposed improvements", "C": "The landlord may refuse only if the works would reduce the value of the unit, because the statute then gives the tenant compensation instead", "D": "The landlord cannot refuse consent unreasonably, because the 1927 Act implies that standard into any covenant against improvements", "E": "The landlord may refuse, because the 1927 Act applies only to leases of residential property and not to shops"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The landlord may refuse, because the 1927 Act rule on improvements applies only where the covenant is qualified by a consent requirement): The Landlord and Tenant Act 1927 section 19(2) makes a consent requirement subject to a proviso that consent is not to be unreasonably withheld for improvements. It does not convert an absolute prohibition into a qualified one. The draft lease contains an absolute covenant, so the landlord may refuse the works. Fenwick Ltd should seek to have the covenant changed to a qualified covenant before the lease is granted."}, {"id": 1032, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A tenant sublet part of an office to a third party on 1 April without the landlord's consent, in breach of the alienation covenant. The landlord learned of this on 15 April. On 25 June, a quarter day, the landlord demanded and accepted the quarterly rent from the tenant. On 20 July the landlord served a section 146 notice complaining of the unlawful subletting and intending to forfeit. Which of the following best describes the validity of the landlord's attempt to forfeit?", "options": {"A": "The right to forfeit for that breach has been waived, because the landlord demanded and accepted rent with knowledge of the breach", "B": "The notice is valid, because acceptance of rent affects only breaches of the rent covenant and not other breaches such as unlawful subletting", "C": "The notice is valid, because it was served within a reasonable time of the breach, which is all that section 146 requires", "D": "The right to forfeit is waived only if the landlord accepted rent on two or more consecutive quarter days", "E": "The right to forfeit is waived only if the landlord also confirmed in writing that it accepted the subletting"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The right to forfeit for that breach has been waived, because the landlord demanded and accepted rent with knowledge of the breach): A landlord who, with knowledge of a breach, does an unequivocal act recognising the lease continues, such as demanding or accepting rent due after the breach, waives the right to forfeit for that breach. The breach here was a once-and-for-all one, and the landlord knew of it by 15 April, yet demanded and accepted rent on 25 June. The later section 146 notice cannot revive the right. Waiver does not depend on written confirmation or on repeated acceptance."}, {"id": 1033, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Rashid buys a packaged protein bar from a corner shop and gives it to his sister Lina. When she eats it, she cuts her mouth on a sliver of glass that was baked into the bar at the factory of Bartlett Foods Ltd. The bar was sealed and could not have been inspected by the shop or by Lina. Which of the following best states Lina's position in negligence against Bartlett Foods Ltd?", "options": {"A": "She can sue only if the injury was caused by food or drink, as the manufacturer's duty is limited to consumables", "B": "She cannot sue unless she shows that Bartlett Foods knew of the glass and chose to sell the bar anyway", "C": "She cannot sue, because only Rashid, as purchaser, has a claim, and that claim lies in contract against the shop", "D": "She can sue Bartlett Foods, because manufacturers owe ultimate consumers a duty where the product cannot reasonably be inspected", "E": "She can sue only the shop, because a manufacturer owes duties to a retailer and not to those who later consume the product after sale"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She can sue Bartlett Foods, because manufacturers owe ultimate consumers a duty where the product cannot reasonably be inspected): A manufacturer owes a duty of care to the ultimate consumer where the product reaches the consumer in the form in which it left the manufacturer, with no reasonable possibility of intermediate examination, and carelessness in preparation is likely to cause injury. Lina is a foreseeable consumer even though she had no contract. Privity is no obstacle in tort, and the duty is not confined to food and drink. She must still prove carelessness and causation, but she need not show that the company knew of the specific glass."}, {"id": 1034, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Imogen instructed a firm to bring a claim against a former business partner, on hourly rates, with an initial estimate of £8,000 to the end of disclosure. The case is now in disclosure and costs of £7,000 have been billed. The supervising solicitor expects that a further dispute over documents will add about £5,000. She is considering waiting until the next scheduled bill before saying anything, to avoid worrying the client during the dispute. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action?", "options": {"A": "Tell Imogen promptly, ideally before the extra work, that the estimate will be exceeded, explain why and give a revised estimate", "B": "Stop all work until Imogen has signed a new client care letter, because the estimate was a limit on the scope of the retainer", "C": "Tell Imogen only once costs actually exceed £8,000, because the firm must give updates only when an estimate has been breached", "D": "Complete the document dispute and report the overspend on the next bill, because the original estimate was only a guide and not a promise", "E": "Tell Imogen about the overspend only if she asks, because the duty to update arises on a client request for information"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell Imogen promptly, ideally before the extra work, that the estimate will be exceeded, explain why and give a revised estimate): A solicitor must keep the client informed of the likely overall cost as the matter progresses, which includes warning the client when an estimate is likely to be exceeded. Telling Imogen now lets her decide whether to continue, adjust her approach or seek other funding before the cost is incurred. Waiting for the next bill or for a question denies her that decision. The duty arises on the foreseeable overspend, not only on actual breach, and it exists whether or not she asks."}, {"id": 1035, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Brook and Co LLP was in serious financial difficulty. Callum, a member, knew its debts exceeded its assets and that insolvent liquidation was unavoidable. Eight months before the LLP went into liquidation he withdrew £50,000 as his profit share. The liquidator wants to recover it. Callum says the sum was his lawful share of profits. The LLP agreement allows monthly drawings. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "He cannot be required to repay, because profit drawings authorised by the LLP agreement are protected from a later claim by the liquidator", "B": "He is liable only for LLP debts above the amount of his capital, as for partners in a general partnership", "C": "He is liable only if the withdrawal was made within three months before the winding up began", "D": "He is liable only if the liquidator proves that he acted fraudulently when he made the withdrawal from the LLP", "E": "The court may order him to contribute up to £50,000, because he withdrew property within two years when he knew liquidation was unavoidable"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court may order him to contribute up to £50,000, because he withdrew property within two years when he knew liquidation was unavoidable): In an LLP the court may order a member to contribute where the member withdrew property within the two years before winding up, knowing that the LLP could not avoid insolvent liquidation. Callum's drawing eight months earlier falls within that window and he had the required knowledge. Fraud need not be shown, and the fact that the agreement allowed drawings gives no protection."}, {"id": 1036, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Parliament passes the Local Services Act 2025, which changes the law on a devolved area of Welsh education. The Senedd has voted to refuse legislative consent, and the Welsh Government objects strongly. Gwyn Parry, a school governor, wishes to bring a claim arguing the Act is unlawful and unenforceable in Wales because the Sewel convention required Senedd consent. The convention is recognised in statute for Wales. What is the likely outcome of his challenge?", "options": {"A": "The challenge will succeed, because a convention recognised in statute becomes a legal rule that a court can enforce", "B": "The challenge will fail, because Parliament remains sovereign and courts do not enforce the convention, even though it appears in statute", "C": "The court will declare the Act incompatible with devolution, because the convention gives the Senedd a veto that binds Parliament", "D": "The challenge will fail, but the Act takes effect in Wales after the Senedd gives its approval in a later vote", "E": "The challenge will succeed, because the Act affects a devolved area and Westminster has no power to legislate on it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The challenge will fail, because Parliament remains sovereign and courts do not enforce the convention, even though it appears in statute): Parliament is sovereign and may legislate for Wales on any matter, including a devolved one. The Sewel convention is now recognised in statute, but the Supreme Court has held that the courts can recognise the existence of a convention while not enforcing it or policing its limits. Gwyn's claim that the Act is unlawful therefore fails. The Senedd's refusal has political consequences but it does not create a veto, and the Act is in force without any later approval."}, {"id": 1037, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Zane swings a pool cue at Pete in a pub argument, foreseeing that he might cause Pete really serious injury but not wanting that result and acting mainly to scare him. The blow breaks Pete's skull. Zane is charged under section 18 Offences Against the Person Act 1861 with causing grievous bodily harm with intent. Which of the following best describes the position on the mens rea of the offence?", "options": {"A": "Recklessness as to causing grievous bodily harm suffices, so Zane's foresight of the risk is enough for conviction", "B": "The prosecution must prove that Zane intended to kill, because section 18 is reserved for cases of attempted murder", "C": "The prosecution must prove intention to cause grievous bodily harm, so foresight of a risk alone is not enough", "D": "The prosecution must prove only that Zane intended some physical harm, since section 18 follows the same mens rea as section 20", "E": "Foresight of grievous bodily harm as probable is conclusive evidence of intention, so Zane must be convicted of the offence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The prosecution must prove intention to cause grievous bodily harm, so foresight of a risk alone is not enough): Section 18 is a specific intent offence: the prosecution must prove an intention to cause grievous bodily harm. Foresight of risk is only evidence from which a jury may find intention, and it is not conclusive. Recklessness is the section 20 standard. An intent to kill is not needed for section 18."}, {"id": 1038, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A health authority told five severely disabled residents, when they moved into a purpose built care home, that it would be their home for life. Years later, the authority decides to close the home to save money and move the residents to a distant facility. It says it considered the promise but that financial pressures justify the decision. The residents ask whether the promise is enforceable in judicial review. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the residents relied on the promise, and reliance alone makes any promise by a public body binding on it", "B": "No, because a public authority cannot be bound by earlier statements, since that would unlawfully restrict its freedom to change policy", "C": "No, because legitimate expectation protects only procedural rights, such as consultation, and the court cannot enforce a substantive outcome", "D": "No, because the promise is enforceable only if it was recorded in a contract, and a mere assurance creates no public law right", "E": "Yes, because a specific promise to few people may be enforced where breaking it is an abuse of power that no public interest overrides"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, because a specific promise to few people may be enforced where breaking it is an abuse of power that no public interest overrides): A substantive legitimate expectation can be enforced where the promise was clear and directed at a small group, and where going back on it would be so unfair as to be an abuse of power, unless an overriding public interest justifies the departure. The promise of a home for life to five disabled residents fits this pattern, and cost saving alone is not usually enough to override it. The courts do accept substantive expectations in such cases. Reliance alone does not make any promise binding."}, {"id": 1039, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Basil Ferris's company supplied faulty software to Lowther Ltd in breach of contract on 11 January 2019. The six year limitation period expired on 11 January 2025. On 10 January 2025, Ferris's solicitor delivered the claim form and the correct fee to the court office. The court, which was short of staff, did not issue the form until 15 January 2025. Lowther's solicitor argues the claim is out of time because the issue date is after expiry. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The claim is out of time, because proceedings are begun on the date shown on the sealed claim form", "B": "The claim is in time if Ferris obtains a discretionary extension under section 33 of the Limitation Act 1980", "C": "The claim is out of time, because the claim form must also have been served on Lowther before limitation expired", "D": "The claim is in time, because proceedings are brought for limitation purposes when the court receives the claim form and fee", "E": "The claim is in time, because the court has power to backdate the issue date where its own delay caused the claim form to issue late"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The claim is in time, because proceedings are brought for limitation purposes when the court receives the claim form and fee): For limitation, a claim is brought when the court receives the claim form together with the fee and the request to issue, and not when the court later seals it. Ferris's solicitor delivered both on 10 January 2025, before the period ended. Service within the limitation period is not required, because the four month validity period for service is separate. Section 33 relates to personal injury claims and is irrelevant here, and the court does not need to backdate anything."}, {"id": 1040, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Fenwick Print Ltd wishes to claim £85,000 from a former customer for goods sold and delivered under a standard written contract. The sum is liquidated, liability is expected to be disputed on a simple factual point, and no specialist issues arise. Fenwick's solicitor is considering where to start the claim. Which of the following is the most appropriate court?", "options": {"A": "The High Court, because the county court cannot hear a money claim worth more than £50,000 unless it is a personal injury claim", "B": "The county court, because the claim is under £100,000 and has no complexity or importance justifying the High Court", "C": "The High Court, because the claim value exceeds the fast track limit and therefore requires a High Court judge to try it", "D": "The county court small claims track, because the claim is a simple debt with a single disputed point", "E": "The Commercial Court, because the claim arises from a commercial contract and that court hears all such claims"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The county court, because the claim is under £100,000 and has no complexity or importance justifying the High Court): The county court has unlimited financial jurisdiction for money claims, and under the Practice Direction a claim should not start in the High Court unless its value is more than £100,000 or it is of a complexity or importance that warrants it. This is a simple debt claim of £85,000, so the county court is appropriate. The Commercial Court is for commercial disputes of a type and size that need it, not for every contract claim. The small claims track is limited to claims of £10,000 or less."}, {"id": 1041, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Tasha Greene's will leaves £25,000 to her cousin, Wilhelmina Frost, with the residue to a hospice. Wilhelmina left her last known address twenty years ago, and the executors' inquiries, including searches of public records and an advertisement in the national press, have found no trace of her. The executors have given notice under the Trustee Act 1925 and the period has expired. They want to distribute the whole estate and be protected from personal liability. Which of the following is the most appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Rely on a statutory declaration from a cousin that Wilhelmina died abroad, because a declaration of belief protects the executors", "B": "Pay the £25,000 to the hospice as part of residue, because a legacy to a missing person lapses once reasonable inquiries have been made", "C": "Distribute to the hospice now, because expiry of the statutory notice period protects the executors against all later claims, including Wilhelmina's", "D": "Apply for a Benjamin order authorising distribution on the footing that Wilhelmina is dead, because the notice protects only against unknown claims", "E": "Retain the £25,000 in the estate account until Wilhelmina is found, because executors cannot distribute residue while a legacy is unpaid"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply for a Benjamin order authorising distribution on the footing that Wilhelmina is dead, because the notice protects only against unknown claims): The statutory notice protects personal representatives only against claims of which they had no notice, and Wilhelmina is a known beneficiary, so it does not help. A Benjamin order lets the court authorise distribution on a stated assumption, such as that the missing person died before the testator, after proper inquiries. A legacy does not lapse simply because the legatee cannot be found. A declaration from a relative gives no judicial protection."}, {"id": 1042, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Claude Pearce died in January 2026. During the administration his estate received £8,000 of bank interest and £6,000 of rent from his flat. The sole residuary beneficiary is his daughter, Maya, who pays higher rate income tax. The executors will make a final distribution next year and ask the solicitor how the income is taxed. Claude also earned income earlier in the tax year before his death. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice about the income received by the estate?", "options": {"A": "No income tax is paid by the executors during the administration, and Maya pays tax on all the income when it is distributed to her", "B": "The executors pay tax at Maya's own marginal rate, because the estate's income is treated as her income as it arises", "C": "The executors pay income tax on estate income at the basic rate, and Maya receives it net with a tax credit and pays any higher rate herself", "D": "The executors pay tax at the higher rate once the estate's income exceeds the higher rate threshold, and Maya receives the income free of further tax", "E": "The executors pay basic rate tax on the income, and Maya has no further tax to pay, since the basic rate payment discharges her liability"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The executors pay income tax on estate income at the basic rate, and Maya receives it net with a tax credit and pays any higher rate herself): Income arising to an estate in the administration period is taxed on the executors at the basic rate, or the ordinary dividend rate, whatever the size of the estate. When the income is paid to the residuary beneficiary she receives it with a tax credit for the tax paid and is taxed on the gross amount at her own rate. Maya, as a higher rate taxpayer, therefore owes the extra tax herself. Claude's earlier income is taxed on his own return."}, {"id": 1043, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Mr and Mrs Okoro engaged Rhodes Surveyors to inspect a house they planned to buy, telling Rhodes that they wanted a property away from an aircraft flight path. Rhodes negligently reported that there was no significant aircraft noise. After they moved in, constant noise caused them considerable distress, although the house is worth what they paid. The Okoros claim damages in contract. An important object of the contract was to give them peace and enjoyment of the property. Which of the following best states what damages they may recover?", "options": {"A": "Damages for loss of enjoyment, because an important object of the contract was to give peace of mind, even though no financial loss is shown", "B": "Damages for loss of enjoyment, because distress is recoverable in every breach of contract where it was reasonably foreseeable", "C": "Damages for distress only if they prove a diminution in the market value of the house caused by the aircraft noise", "D": "No damages, because the house is worth what they paid and non-pecuniary loss is not recoverable in contract", "E": "Damages for the cost of moving house, because the only recoverable loss is expenditure wasted by relying on the surveyor's report"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Damages for loss of enjoyment, because an important object of the contract was to give peace of mind, even though no financial loss is shown): Damages for disappointment or loss of enjoyment are recoverable in contract where an important object of the contract was to provide pleasure, relaxation or peace of mind. The Okoros told Rhodes of their wish to avoid noise, which made that an important object. It does not matter that the property is worth what they paid. Foreseeability of distress alone is not sufficient in an ordinary commercial contract, so the object of the contract is the deciding feature."}, {"id": 1044, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Harriet Dunn died in October 2025 leaving her whole estate of £480,000 to her son, Ben. Ben, who is wealthy, would like £100,000 to pass instead to his own daughter, Lily, and wants this to be treated for Inheritance Tax as a gift made by Harriet and not by him. In June 2026 Ben asks the solicitor to prepare the necessary document. Ben will receive nothing in return from Lily. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Ben can achieve this by a deed of variation, but only if every beneficiary under the will, including those who take nothing, signs it", "B": "Ben can do this by a written deed of variation made within two years of death that states the Inheritance Tax read-back applies", "C": "Ben can achieve this by a deed of variation, but it must be made within six months of death to be effective for tax", "D": "Ben can achieve this by a deed of variation, which applies for Inheritance Tax automatically without any statement in the deed, provided Lily agrees", "E": "Ben can achieve this only by making an outright gift to Lily, which is treated as his own potentially exempt transfer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ben can do this by a written deed of variation made within two years of death that states the Inheritance Tax read-back applies): A beneficiary can redirect an inheritance by a written deed made within two years of death, with no consideration given, and the redirection is treated for Inheritance Tax as made by the deceased if the deed contains a statement to that effect. Ben made no payment and is within time, as the death was in October 2025. A beneficiary who is not giving anything up need not sign. Without the statement in the deed the transfer would be treated as Ben's own gift."}, {"id": 1045, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Marcus Whitby, aged 28, is arrested and charged with robbery after a mobile phone was taken from a passer-by by use of threats of force. He is charged alone, and the case is listed before the magistrates' court for his first hearing. His solicitor asks the court how it will deal with the question of venue for trial. Robbery is triable only on indictment. What is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The court may keep the robbery itself if the prosecution agrees that it falls at the lower end of seriousness", "B": "The court will send him to the Crown Court for trial, without asking for a plea indication or considering allocation", "C": "The court will commit him for trial after reviewing the written evidence, because committal proceedings remain the route for serious offences", "D": "The court must hold an allocation hearing, because every adult defendant is entitled to a decision on mode of trial", "E": "The court takes his plea first and sends the case to the Crown Court only if he pleads not guilty"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The court will send him to the Crown Court for trial, without asking for a plea indication or considering allocation): Indictable-only offences are sent to the Crown Court at the first hearing under section 51 of the Crime and Disorder Act 1998. The magistrates have no mode of trial decision to make and do not take a plea. Allocation applies only to either-way offences, and old-style committal for trial has been abolished for these cases. The magistrates simply deal with bail and any legal aid issues before sending."}, {"id": 1046, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Bella was burned by a hair straightener that overheated because of a defect. She bought it from Nina, who runs a stall at a weekend market. The straightener carries no brand name, and the manufacturer was overseas and unidentified. Two weeks after her injury Bella asked Nina in writing who supplied it. Nina did not reply for six months. Bella sues Nina under the Consumer Protection Act 1987. Which of the following best describes Nina's position?", "options": {"A": "Nina is liable as if she were the producer, because she failed to name her supplier within a reasonable time of a proper request", "B": "Nina is liable only if she knew about the defect when she sold the straightener, because supplier liability depends on knowledge", "C": "Nina is liable only if Bella can first show that the overseas manufacturer is insolvent and cannot be sued", "D": "Nina is not liable, because the Act imposes liability on producers, and a trader who only sells goods is outside it", "E": "Nina is liable only if the product carried her own name or brand, because only own branders are treated as producers"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nina is liable as if she were the producer, because she failed to name her supplier within a reasonable time of a proper request): A supplier of a defective product is treated as its producer if the injured person asks the supplier to identify the producer, an own-brander or the importer, and the supplier fails to do so, or to name its own supplier, within a reasonable time. Bella's written request came soon after the injury, and six months of silence is not reasonable. Knowledge of the defect and own branding are not required, and the claimant does not have to show the manufacturer is insolvent. A supplier who identifies its supplier in time avoids liability."}, {"id": 1047, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Hamza and Layla Idris are beneficial joint tenants of their flat, which is subject to a mortgage. After a breakdown in their relationship, Layla's solicitor sends a notice of severance to the flat by recorded delivery, and it is delivered on Wednesday. Hamza was on holiday that week and the notice sat unopened in the hallway. Layla was knocked over by a car and died on Thursday. Hamza returned on Saturday and opened the notice. Layla left a will leaving all her property to her sister. Who is entitled to Layla's beneficial share?", "options": {"A": "Hamza, because notice of severance has no effect unless the register is altered to show a tenancy in common before death", "B": "Hamza, because severance takes effect only when the notice is actually read by the person on whom it is served", "C": "Layla's sister, because the notice was validly served on delivery, so the tenancy was severed before Layla died", "D": "Hamza, because severance requires written notice signed by both joint tenants, and Hamza did not sign", "E": "Layla's sister, because the will severed the joint tenancy when it was made and the notice merely confirmed that earlier act"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Layla's sister, because the notice was validly served on delivery, so the tenancy was severed before Layla died): Under section 36(2) of the Law of Property Act 1925, written notice of a desire to sever, given to the other joint tenants, severs the tenancy. A notice sent by registered post or recorded delivery to the last known address is treated as served when it is delivered, whether or not the recipient reads it. Layla's notice was delivered before her death, so the joint tenancy had already become a tenancy in common, and her share passes under her will to her sister. Hamza's signature and an alteration of the register are not needed, and a will on its own does not sever."}, {"id": 1048, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A firm acts for Aaron Silva in buying a flat. He posts one cheque for £30,000, made up of the £28,000 deposit and £2,000 owed on the firm's bill, which the firm delivered last week. The cashier asks how to deal with the mixed payment. Exchange is due the same week. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to handle the cheque?", "options": {"A": "Pay the whole cheque into the office account, then promptly transfer the £28,000 deposit to the client account", "B": "Hold the cheque until it has cleared, then split the proceeds between the accounts on the day of exchange", "C": "Pay the whole cheque into the client account, then promptly move the £2,000 for the delivered bill to the office account", "D": "Return the cheque to Aaron and ask him to send two separate cheques before anything is paid in", "E": "Pay the whole cheque into the client account and leave the £2,000 there until the end of the matter"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pay the whole cheque into the client account, then promptly move the £2,000 for the delivered bill to the office account): A single cheque that cannot be divided and contains client money should be paid into the client account without delay. The firm has delivered its bill, so the £2,000 is office money, and the firm must then transfer it out promptly rather than leave it mixed with client money. Paying the cheque to the office account would put the deposit outside the client account, and holding the cheque or sending it back only delays matters. Prompt payment in followed by prompt transfer keeps the accounts rules."}, {"id": 1049, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Elena Voss, who is selling her freehold house in Norwich. The buyer's solicitor has sent the standard enquiry asking whether the seller has had any disputes with neighbours. Elena has told you in confidence that she had a heated boundary argument with the neighbour last year, which was not resolved. She instructs you to answer 'no' because she fears the sale will fall through. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Reply that the seller does not know of any dispute, because she regards the argument as finished even though it remains unresolved", "B": "Send the reply 'no' as instructed, adding that it is given on the client's instructions so that you are not personally misleading anyone", "C": "Tell the buyer's solicitor about the boundary argument yourself, because your duty not to mislead the buyer overrides your duty of confidentiality", "D": "Advise Elena that the reply must be accurate, and decline to send an answer you know is untrue, ceasing to act if she insists", "E": "Send the reply 'no' because the doctrine of caveat emptor places the risk on the buyer, who can check the position independently"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise Elena that the reply must be accurate, and decline to send an answer you know is untrue, ceasing to act if she insists): A solicitor must not mislead others, including the other side, and must not pass on a reply known to be untrue, even on the client's instructions. The solicitor also owes the client confidentiality, so the correct step is to advise Elena, decline to send the false answer, and cease to act if she maintains her instruction. Telling the buyer's side directly breaches confidentiality, and caveat emptor does not allow a false statement. A carefully worded evasive reply is just as misleading."}, {"id": 1050, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Imogen Ballard and Callum Reid agree in a meeting that Imogen will sell Callum her cottage for £195,000, with the completion date to be fixed later. They shake hands, and Callum immediately pays £500 for a survey and arranges a mortgage offer. No draft contract is prepared and nothing is signed. Imogen then receives a higher offer from a stranger and tells Callum she is withdrawing. Callum wants to enforce the oral agreement. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "A contract exists in equity, because land contracts need no formalities until the transfer is made and the deposit is paid", "B": "A contract exists, because the parties agreed the price and the property and Callum acted on the agreement by paying for the survey", "C": "No contract exists, as section 2 of the 1989 Act requires writing signed by both parties, though a claim in proprietary estoppel may be worth exploring", "D": "A contract exists once the doctrine of part performance is applied, because Callum has spent money in reliance on the sale", "E": "No contract exists, because section 2 of the 1989 Act is satisfied by nothing short of a deed executed by both parties"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No contract exists, as section 2 of the 1989 Act requires writing signed by both parties, though a claim in proprietary estoppel may be worth exploring): Section 2 of the Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1989 requires a contract for the sale of land to be in writing, to contain all the agreed terms, and to be signed by or for each party. An oral agreement, even one followed by reliance, does not satisfy this. The old doctrine of part performance no longer rescues it. Callum may still have a claim in proprietary estoppel if Imogen encouraged him and he relied to his detriment, but that is a separate route and not a claim on a contract."}, {"id": 1051, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Nadia owns a house in an estate where each owner has the benefit of a valid, enforceable restrictive covenant against building on front gardens. Her neighbour Omar, who took subject to the covenant with notice, has laid foundations for a large porch on his front garden. Nadia has not delayed and has done nothing to suggest she accepts the porch. She asks what remedy the court will usually grant. Which of the following is the best statement of the position?", "options": {"A": "Specific performance is the usual remedy, requiring Omar to demolish the porch and restore the garden to its former state", "B": "An injunction is the usual remedy, although the court may award damages in its place where an injunction would be oppressive", "C": "Damages are the usual remedy, because a restrictive covenant is a contractual promise and equitable relief is not available for it", "D": "An injunction must be granted if breach is shown, because the court has no discretion to award damages in its place", "E": "The sole remedy is for Nadia to apply to the Upper Tribunal to have the covenant discharged or modified"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An injunction is the usual remedy, although the court may award damages in its place where an injunction would be oppressive): Breach of a valid restrictive covenant is normally met by an injunction, which is the equitable remedy suited to negative obligations. The court has a discretion to award damages instead, for example where an injunction would be oppressive to the defendant, so an injunction is not automatic. Nadia has not delayed or acquiesced, which supports the usual remedy. The Upper Tribunal can discharge or modify a covenant only on an application by the burdened owner, and it is not a remedy open to Nadia."}, {"id": 1052, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A firm is instructed by Brightwater Holdings Ltd, a private company, on a share purchase. The shares are held by Ms Lindqvist (40 per cent), Mr Chua (30 per cent), a pension trust (20 per cent) and an employee (10 per cent). The managing director, who gives the instructions, holds no shares. The firm wants to complete customer due diligence promptly because the client is keen to start. Which of the following is the correct approach to identifying beneficial owners?", "options": {"A": "The firm must verify Ms Lindqvist, Mr Chua and the pension trust, because holdings of 20 per cent or more count as beneficial ownership", "B": "The firm must verify every shareholder, because the regulations require identity checks on all owners whatever the size of their holding", "C": "The firm must verify Ms Lindqvist alone, because only the largest single shareholder is treated as the beneficial owner of a company", "D": "The firm need only verify the managing director, because the person giving the instructions is the individual whose identity matters", "E": "The firm must identify and take reasonable steps to verify Ms Lindqvist and Mr Chua, because each owns over 25 per cent of the shares"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The firm must identify and take reasonable steps to verify Ms Lindqvist and Mr Chua, because each owns over 25 per cent of the shares): For a body corporate, a beneficial owner is an individual who ultimately owns or controls more than 25 per cent of the shares or voting rights. Ms Lindqvist and Mr Chua each exceed that figure, so both must be identified and reasonable measures taken to verify them. The pension trust and the employee fall below the threshold, and the managing director is relevant to authority to instruct rather than ownership. The firm should also understand the ownership and control structure of the company."}, {"id": 1053, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Ravi sells his old van to Don, a private buyer, for £4,000. Ravi knows the chassis is badly corroded and the van is unsafe, but Don does not ask about its condition and Ravi says nothing about it. There is no fiduciary relationship between them and no statute or contract imposes a duty to disclose. Ravi is charged with fraud by failing to disclose information under section 3 of the Fraud Act 2006. What is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Section 3 is made out, because Ravi knew of the defect and intended to profit from the sale by his silence about it", "B": "Section 2 is made out, because silence about a known defect is an implied false representation that the van is roadworthy", "C": "Section 4 is made out instead, because the seller of a vehicle occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard the buyer's financial interests", "D": "Section 3 is made out, because a moral duty to disclose a dangerous defect to a buyer is sufficient to found liability", "E": "Section 3 is not made out, because there was no legal duty to disclose the corrosion on an ordinary private sale between strangers"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Section 3 is not made out, because there was no legal duty to disclose the corrosion on an ordinary private sale between strangers): Section 3 requires a legal duty to disclose, which can arise from statute, from the terms of a contract, from a fiduciary relationship or from the custom of a trade or market. An ordinary private seller owes none of these to a buyer who does not ask. A moral duty is not sufficient. Section 4 needs a position of trust, which an arm's length seller does not hold, and silence alone is not a representation for section 2."}, {"id": 1054, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ms Adeyemi is knocked off her bicycle by a van negligently driven by Mr Paxton's employee and is taken to hospital with a deep cut to her arm. A junior doctor treats the wound but carelessly omits to check for a foreign object, and a fragment of glass left in the wound causes an infection requiring a second operation. Ms Adeyemi sues the van driver's employer for the infection and second operation. The employer argues that the doctor's carelessness broke the chain of causation. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome of that argument?", "options": {"A": "It will fail, because careless medical treatment is foreseeable and breaks the chain only if it is grossly negligent", "B": "It will fail, but only because the claimant was not herself at fault in being treated by the junior doctor", "C": "It will succeed, because the hospital rather than the van driver was the immediate cause of the infection and second operation", "D": "It will succeed, because any careless medical treatment by a professional is an independent act that breaks the chain of causation", "E": "It will fail, because the hospital owes its own duty to the claimant and so shares liability with the employer equally"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It will fail, because careless medical treatment is foreseeable and breaks the chain only if it is grossly negligent): Further harm from negligent medical treatment of a foreseeable injury remains within the original wrongdoer's responsibility unless the treatment is so grossly negligent that it eclipses the original wrong. Mere carelessness in checking the wound does not meet that standard. The employer therefore remains liable for the infection and the operation, and may have contribution rights against the hospital. That the hospital was the immediate cause is not the legal test."}, {"id": 1055, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Callum, a self-employed plumber, is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm. It is alleged that he struck his 14-year-old stepson, the son of his wife Dina, during an argument at their home. Dina saw it happen and has told the police that she will not give evidence against her husband. The prosecution wishes to call her. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Dina is competent but cannot be compelled, because a spouse cannot be made to give evidence against the other", "B": "Dina is compellable only if the boy is her biological child, since a stepchild is not covered by the exception", "C": "Dina is competent and compellable for the prosecution, because the alleged offence involves an assault on a person under 16", "D": "Dina is compellable only if the alleged assault was on Dina herself, rather than on a child living in the home", "E": "Dina is compellable only if the court first rules that her evidence is essential to the case, since spouses are generally protected in this way"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dina is competent and compellable for the prosecution, because the alleged offence involves an assault on a person under 16): A spouse is competent for the prosecution and is compellable only in the cases in section 80 of PACE, which include an assault on or injury to a person who was under 16. The boy is 14, so the exception applies whether or not he is Dina's own child. The general protection for spouses therefore does not help Callum on these facts. There is no separate test requiring the court to find her evidence essential."}, {"id": 1056, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Karim has exchanged contracts to buy a detached house from Dilys for £420,000. The contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) without amendment, and the property is insured by Dilys. Before completion, a fire destroys most of the house, leaving it unusable for its purpose as a dwelling. Karim has paid a ten per cent deposit. Which of the following best describes Karim's position?", "options": {"A": "He may rescind and recover his deposit, because the standard condition lets a buyer rescind where the property is unusable for its purpose", "B": "He must complete, but he is entitled to the insurance money Dilys receives, because a seller holds insurance proceeds on trust for the buyer", "C": "He may rescind only if Dilys could not reasonably have insured the property, because that condition governs the buyer's right to rescind", "D": "He may not rescind but may require a reduction in the price, because the standard condition only compensates a buyer for physical damage", "E": "He must complete and pay the full price, because the buyer bears the risk of loss from the moment contracts are exchanged"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He may rescind and recover his deposit, because the standard condition lets a buyer rescind where the property is unusable for its purpose): The Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) displace the old common law rule that risk passes on exchange: the seller keeps the risk until completion in the sense that a buyer may rescind where the property becomes unusable for its purpose. A fire that leaves the house unusable as a dwelling meets that test, so Karim may rescind and have his deposit returned. The suggestion that he must complete because risk passed on exchange states the common law position that the contract has replaced. The condition restricting rescission to damage that could not have been insured applies to the seller's right, not the buyer's."}, {"id": 1057, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Callum Rhodes has been sent to the Crown Court for trial on a charge of burglary under section 51 of the Crime and Disorder Act 1998 after his first appearance at the magistrates' court. Callum, who is on bail, has not yet indicated how he will plead in the Crown Court. His solicitor is preparing for the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing and Callum asks what will happen at it. Which of the following best describes the hearing?", "options": {"A": "He will not be asked to plead, because the hearing deals with bail and disclosure until the trial judge is allocated", "B": "He will be arraigned and enter his plea, and if he pleads not guilty the judge will give directions and fix a trial date", "C": "The judge will decide whether the evidence is sufficient and dismiss the charge if the prosecution cannot show a case to answer", "D": "The judge will hear the full prosecution case, because the hearing is a mini-trial to decide whether the case should proceed", "E": "Only the lawyers attend, because defendants are excused and enter pleas in writing within a set period afterwards"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (He will be arraigned and enter his plea, and if he pleads not guilty the judge will give directions and fix a trial date): The Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing is the first Crown Court hearing after sending. The defendant is arraigned, so the charge is put and a plea entered. If the plea is not guilty, the judge identifies the issues, gives directions for preparing the case and sets a trial date. It is a case management hearing and does not test the strength of the evidence."}, {"id": 1058, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya Raman is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm, an either-way offence. At the plea before venue stage, her solicitor indicates that she will plead guilty. The magistrates hear the prosecution facts and note that Priya has a recent conviction for a similar offence of violence. They form the view that their sentencing powers may be inadequate for this offence. What is the most likely course the magistrates will take?", "options": {"A": "Convict her on her plea, then commit her to the Crown Court for sentence if they decide their powers are insufficient", "B": "Remit the case to the Crown Court, which then decides whether to accept her guilty plea and obtain a report", "C": "Sentence her up to their maximum themselves, because committal for sentence is available only for indictable-only offences", "D": "Adjourn to allocate the case, because the allocation decision must be made before any plea is entered at the first hearing", "E": "Send her to the Crown Court for trial, because a guilty plea to an either-way offence is not accepted in the magistrates' court"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Convict her on her plea, then commit her to the Crown Court for sentence if they decide their powers are insufficient): A guilty plea indicated at plea before venue means no allocation or trial decision is needed, and the magistrates treat her as convicted. They then decide whether their own sentencing powers are enough given the seriousness and her previous record. If not, they may commit her to the Crown Court for sentence. The court does not send her for trial, since there is no contested issue to try."}, {"id": 1059, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Alan, aged 24 and of previous good character, is convicted after trial at the Crown Court of burglary of a shed. The judge decides that the offence passes the custody threshold and that a term of 18 months is the shortest commensurate with its seriousness. Alan has a job and a settled home. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The term cannot be suspended, because a suspended sentence is available only for terms of twelve months or less in the Crown Court", "B": "The term can be suspended only if Alan agrees to its terms, because a suspended sentence needs the offender's consent to the operational period", "C": "The term cannot be suspended, because suspension is available only for offences tried summarily in the magistrates' court", "D": "The term must be suspended, because Alan has no previous convictions and has a job and a home", "E": "The term may be suspended as it is two years or less, and the judge must consider whether custody is still needed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The term may be suspended as it is two years or less, and the judge must consider whether custody is still needed): A custodial sentence of two years or less can be suspended, so an 18 month term is eligible. The judge must then decide whether it is appropriate to suspend, looking at factors such as the prospect of rehabilitation, strong personal mitigation and the effect of immediate custody on others. Suspension is a matter of judgement, not an entitlement based on good character or a stable home. It does not require Alan's consent."}, {"id": 1060, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Zena bought a cycle helmet made by Crest Sports Ltd, sold in a box stating it protects against impacts at up to 20 mph. Riding at 12 mph, she fell, and the helmet shell split on impact because of a flaw in the moulding process, causing a head injury that a sound helmet would have prevented. Crest used reasonable care in its factory. Zena sues Crest under the Consumer Protection Act 1987. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Zena succeeds only if she bought the helmet directly from Crest, because the Act protects only the direct purchaser", "B": "Zena fails, because Crest took reasonable care in its factory and liability under the Act depends on proof of negligence by the producer", "C": "Zena is likely to succeed, because the helmet's safety fell below what people are entitled to expect and fault need not be shown", "D": "Zena succeeds only if the helmet was made in the United Kingdom, because the Act applies to domestic producers", "E": "Zena fails, because a defect for the purposes of the Act must lie in the design and not in the manufacturing process"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Zena is likely to succeed, because the helmet's safety fell below what people are entitled to expect and fault need not be shown): Under the Act a product is defective where its safety is not such as persons generally are entitled to expect, taking account of its presentation and the use to which it could reasonably be put. A helmet sold as protective at 20 mph that splits at 12 mph falls short of that expectation. Liability is strict, so Crest's reasonable care in the factory does not answer the claim, and there is no need for the claimant to be the buyer or for the helmet to be made in the UK. A manufacturing flaw is as much a defect as a design flaw."}, {"id": 1061, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Lindqvist Interiors contracted to fit out the new restaurant of Marsh Hotels and to complete by 1 March, the contract stating that time was of the essence for completion. Lindqvist finished the work to a good standard on 3 March. The delay did not stop the restaurant opening and Marsh Hotels lost no trade as a result. Marsh Hotels wishes to know whether it may lawfully terminate the contract and refuse to pay the price. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Marsh Hotels may not terminate, because a delay of two days causing no loss does not deprive it of substantially the whole benefit of the contract", "B": "Marsh Hotels may terminate, because lateness in a building contract is a repudiatory breach by default unless the contract expressly allows a short grace period", "C": "Marsh Hotels may not terminate, because it must first serve notice fixing a reasonable final date for completion and Lindqvist must then fail to meet it", "D": "Marsh Hotels may terminate, because the express term made timely completion a condition, so its breach gives a right to terminate", "E": "Marsh Hotels may terminate, but only if it proves that the late completion caused financial loss that damages would not adequately cover"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Marsh Hotels may terminate, because the express term made timely completion a condition, so its breach gives a right to terminate): Where the parties expressly provide that time is of the essence, the term is a condition. Breach of a condition entitles the innocent party to terminate however slight the practical effect of the breach. The seriousness test applies to innominate terms, not to a term the parties have promoted to a condition. A notice making time of the essence is needed only where time was not already of the essence, so it does not help Lindqvist."}, {"id": 1062, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Greenfold Ltd makes bespoke furniture and needs £400,000 to buy new machinery with a useful life of seven years. Its shareholders are unable to invest more and do not want to share control with an outsider. Cash flow is steady except in winter, when sales fall. A bank offers a seven year term loan at a fixed rate, secured by a fixed charge over the machinery. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Ask a director for an unsecured loan repayable on demand, because it avoids bank security and keeps control with the existing shareholders", "B": "Accept the term loan, because it keeps control with the shareholders and matches the machinery's life, if winter repayments can be met", "C": "Issue new ordinary shares to an outside investor, because dividends are discretionary and there is nothing to repay in the winter months", "D": "Use a bank overdraft, because it is cheap and flexible and can be left outstanding for the whole seven years without renegotiation", "E": "Take a short term bank loan repayable within a year, because shorter borrowing costs less interest and needs no security over the machinery"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accept the term loan, because it keeps control with the shareholders and matches the machinery's life, if winter repayments can be met): A fixed term loan over the asset's life suits a long term purchase and leaves control with the existing shareholders, which is their stated priority. Security over the machinery is a normal condition and the winter dip is a risk to be planned for. An overdraft is repayable on demand and unsuited to long term funding. Equity from an outsider dilutes control, and a one year loan or a demand loan would impose repayment pressure at the wrong time."}, {"id": 1063, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Jodie Hartley is charged with affray after a disturbance outside a bar. At the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing in the Crown Court she pleads not guilty. She accepts that she was present but disputes that she used or threatened violence. When the judge asks what issues are in dispute, her counsel replies that the prosecution must prove everything and the defence will say more at trial. What does the Criminal Procedure Rules approach to case management require?", "options": {"A": "The judge cannot make directions about issues, because that is a matter reserved for the trial judge at the start of the trial", "B": "The defence need say nothing, because the burden rests on the prosecution and the defendant may keep her position private until trial", "C": "Each party must actively assist the court by identifying the real issues, so the defence should say what is genuinely disputed", "D": "Only the prosecution has case management duties, because the defendant's privilege against self-incrimination removes her duties", "E": "The defence must serve its full witness statements, because the judge orders both sides to exchange evidence at this hearing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Each party must actively assist the court by identifying the real issues, so the defence should say what is genuinely disputed): The Criminal Procedure Rules impose a duty on every participant to prepare and conduct the case in a way that furthers the overriding objective, which includes identifying the disputed issues. Jodie can still make the prosecution prove its case, but counsel must indicate that presence is accepted and that violence is in issue. Defence witness statements are not routinely served. The judge is entitled to direct the parties on issues at this hearing."}, {"id": 1064, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mrs Lewis's will appoints her friend Cara as sole executor, with no substitute, and leaves her residue to her adult nephews Dan and Eli equally. Cara has formally renounced her right to act. Dan and Eli are both willing to apply for a grant. What type of grant is needed, and who has priority to apply?", "options": {"A": "Letters of administration under the intestacy rules, with Mrs Lewis's nearest relatives having priority to apply", "B": "A grant of probate to the solicitor who drafted the will, because the court appoints a professional executor", "C": "Letters of administration with will annexed, and the residuary beneficiaries Dan and Eli have priority to apply", "D": "A grant of probate to the nephews, because beneficiaries named in a will become executors on renunciation", "E": "A limited grant to the nephews, because renunciation by the sole executor limits the grant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Letters of administration with will annexed, and the residuary beneficiaries Dan and Eli have priority to apply): When no executor is able or willing to act, the grant is letters of administration with will annexed, since there is a will but no executor. The Non-Contentious Probate Rules give priority to the residuary beneficiaries, so Dan and Eli can apply. A grant of probate is possible only to a named executor. The intestacy rules do not apply because there is a valid will."}, {"id": 1065, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Wei sprays washable water-based paint on the exterior wall of a leisure centre owned by a council, producing a protest slogan. The paint can be removed with a pressure washer, which takes two council workers a full morning and uses cleaning materials costing £60. Wei is charged with criminal damage under section 1(1) of the Criminal Damage Act 1971. Which of the following best describes whether the property has been damaged?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because damage includes temporary impairment of the appearance or usefulness of property where it requires time and expense to put right", "B": "Yes, because any act that alters the appearance of property belonging to another is damage, whatever effort is needed to restore it", "C": "No, because the cost of cleaning is below the level at which the law treats interference with property as criminal damage", "D": "No, because damage requires a permanent alteration to property and washable paint leaves the wall structurally unchanged", "E": "Yes, but only if the council had previously prohibited graffiti on the wall by a posted notice"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because damage includes temporary impairment of the appearance or usefulness of property where it requires time and expense to put right): Damage is a question of fact and degree and includes temporary impairment of value or usefulness, such as needing time and money to clean a surface. A permanent alteration is not required. Not every change of appearance qualifies, so the cost and effort of restoration matter. No prior notice is needed."}, {"id": 1066, "domain": "land_law", "question": "In 2010 Bridget sold part of her land to Colin, who covenanted not to build on the northern boundary, for the benefit of Bridget's retained land. The covenant was never entered on the charges register of Colin's title, which is registered. In 2020 Colin sold to Dee, a buyer for value, whose solicitor searched the register and found nothing. Dee began building on the boundary. Bridget's successor, Ewan, now seeks an injunction. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Dee is bound if she knew about the covenant when she bought, because a buyer with notice takes subject to it", "B": "Dee is not bound, because the covenant was never protected by a notice on the register and she bought for value", "C": "Dee is bound, because the covenant is an equitable interest that overrides a registered title as an overriding interest", "D": "Dee is bound, because the burden of a covenant passes with the land in the same way as a positive obligation", "E": "Dee is bound, because a restrictive covenant touches and concerns the land and runs in equity with the burdened land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dee is not bound, because the covenant was never protected by a notice on the register and she bought for value): In registered land a restrictive covenant is an equitable interest that must be protected by a notice on the charges register to bind a later buyer for value. A restrictive covenant is not an overriding interest. Because nothing was entered, Dee takes free of it under section 29 of the Land Registration Act 2002, whether or not she suspected it, and Ewan has no injunction against her. Tulk v Moxhay principles of touching and concerning land do not displace the registration rules."}, {"id": 1067, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Gareth Lowe, a solicitor in Manchester, writes a series of abusive and threatening messages on social media about a neighbour with whom he is in a dispute over a boundary hedge. The messages are posted from his personal account, mention no client and have nothing to do with his practice. The neighbour reports them to the SRA. Which of the following best describes Gareth's position?", "options": {"A": "He may breach the Principle on public trust, because a solicitor's conduct outside practice can damage confidence in the profession", "B": "He cannot be in breach, because a solicitor's private life is protected unless a client has been affected by the behaviour in the course of the retainer", "C": "He may be in breach only if he has been convicted of a criminal offence in respect of the messages", "D": "He may be in breach only if the neighbour has complained to the Legal Ombudsman and the complaint has been upheld", "E": "He cannot be in breach, because the SRA Principles apply to conduct carried on in the course of practice"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He may breach the Principle on public trust, because a solicitor's conduct outside practice can damage confidence in the profession): The duty to act in a way that upholds public trust and confidence in the profession can be engaged by a solicitor's private conduct. Threatening and abusive messages are the kind of behaviour that may damage that confidence even though no client is involved. Neither a conviction nor an upheld Ombudsman complaint is needed before the SRA may act. The SRA looks at the nature and seriousness of the conduct when deciding on action."}, {"id": 1068, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "On Monday a buyer, Isla Morgan, hands a firm of solicitors a cheque for £25,000, which is her deposit for a house purchase with exchange expected on Thursday. A trainee suggests keeping the cheque on the file until Thursday and paying it in then, so that the client ledger does not show a balance for several days. The firm's practice is to pay in client cheques on the day they are received. Which of the following best describes what the firm should do?", "options": {"A": "Pay the cheque into the client account only once it has cleared, to avoid using other clients' money", "B": "Pay the cheque into the office account first and move the funds to the client account on Thursday", "C": "Hold the cheque on the file until exchange, because the money is needed only for the deposit and is not yet due", "D": "Pay the cheque into the client account promptly and credit Isla's client ledger when it is received", "E": "Pay the cheque into the client account but record it on the ledger only when the funds are paid out"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pay the cheque into the client account promptly and credit Isla's client ledger when it is received): The Accounts Rules require client money to be paid promptly into a client account, and the receipt to be recorded on the client's ledger so that the firm can always see what it holds for her. Holding the cheque or paying it into the office account would mean client money is outside the client account, which is itself a breach. The risk of using other clients' money arises when money is paid out before the cheque clears, not from paying it in. The ledger must show the credit when the money is received."}, {"id": 1069, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "You act for Hartwell Autos, which sold a car to Mr Osei, aged 78, for £9,500. Mr Osei has paid nothing. His daughter says he lacked capacity because of advanced dementia, and medical evidence supports this. The salesman admits to you that Mr Osei seemed confused at signing. Hartwell's director wants to sue for the price and to say nothing about the salesman's observation. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The contract binds Mr Osei, because incapacity is a defence only where the supplier exploited him, and Hartwell may stay silent about the observation", "B": "The contract is voidable by Mr Osei because Hartwell had notice of his incapacity, and the salesman's observation cannot be concealed from the court", "C": "The contract is void from the outset because of his incapacity, so Hartwell has no claim and need not consider any question of disclosure", "D": "The contract is voidable only after a court has declared Mr Osei incapable, so Hartwell may sue and leave the point for him to raise", "E": "The contract binds Mr Osei if the price was fair, so Hartwell may sue and answer only the questions that the court puts to it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The contract is voidable by Mr Osei because Hartwell had notice of his incapacity, and the salesman's observation cannot be concealed from the court): A contract made by a person who lacks mental capacity is voidable at his option if the other party knew or ought to have known of the incapacity. The salesman noticed confusion at signing, so Hartwell had notice. A solicitor must not mislead the court or allow the client to do so, so the observation cannot be hidden if the issue arises. Fairness of the price does not prevent avoidance, and no earlier court declaration is needed."}, {"id": 1070, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "The SRA receives three separate reports that Tobias Reid, the senior partner of Reid and Calloway, a firm in Leeds, has transferred money from the client account to cover the firm's overdraft. A forensic review suggests that at least £420,000 of client money cannot be accounted for. The firm's lease was renewed last year and it employs eleven staff. Which of the following steps is the SRA most likely to take to protect clients' money immediately?", "options": {"A": "Ask the Legal Ombudsman to take over the complaints and order the firm to refund the missing sums to each affected client", "B": "Refer the matter to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal and await its decision before taking any step affecting the firm's client account, files or staff", "C": "Intervene in the firm, taking control of its client files and client account so that money and documents are secured", "D": "Impose a rebuke on Mr Reid and require the firm to submit monthly accountant's reports until the shortfall has been repaid", "E": "Wait for the firm's insurers to decide whether to meet the clients' claims, since insurance is the primary source of redress"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intervene in the firm, taking control of its client files and client account so that money and documents are secured): The SRA can intervene in a firm where there is reason to suspect dishonesty or that client money is at risk, which lets it take possession of files and the client account straight away. The suspected loss of £420,000 makes protection urgent on these facts. A referral to the Tribunal may follow but takes time and does nothing to secure funds meanwhile. A rebuke and monthly reports are far too mild for suspected misappropriation, and the Legal Ombudsman has no power to take control of a firm."}, {"id": 1071, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Gareth Williams died domiciled in England and Wales. His estate is worth £2.4 million in gross value, all of it in the UK, and the whole estate passes under his will to his surviving wife, Sian. He made no lifetime gifts that count for Inheritance Tax and had no interest in any trust. His executor wants to know whether a full Inheritance Tax account must be delivered to HMRC before applying for the grant of representation. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "No account is needed, because the estate of any married person passes without Inheritance Tax and cannot be an estate that is reportable", "B": "It can be an exempt excepted estate, because the spouse exemption leaves nothing chargeable and the gross value is within the limit", "C": "A full account is needed, because the spouse exemption cannot be taken into account when deciding whether an estate is excepted", "D": "A full account is needed, because the only excepted estate category is for estates below the nil rate band in total gross value", "E": "A full account is needed, because any estate with a gross value above the nil rate band cannot be an excepted estate"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It can be an exempt excepted estate, because the spouse exemption leaves nothing chargeable and the gross value is within the limit): An exempt excepted estate is one where the deceased was UK domiciled, the gross value is within a higher limit, and after spouse and charity exemptions no more than the nil rate band would be chargeable. Gareth's whole estate passes to his wife, so nothing chargeable remains. This means the executor need not deliver a full account, though the probate application must confirm the estate is excepted. The low value category requires the gross estate to be within the nil rate band, which is why the estate is not excepted under that route."}, {"id": 1072, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Joanna Fenwick, a trainee, is reading about how courts interpret statutes. Her supervisor asks her to explain, in one sentence for a client newsletter, what the literal rule requires of a judge when the words of a statute are clear, even if the result is harsh. She wants the statement that is accurate for the literal rule rather than the golden rule or the mischief rule. Which of the following should she use?", "options": {"A": "The judge identifies the gap in the earlier law and chooses the meaning that suppresses the mischief Parliament was addressing", "B": "The judge gives the words their plain, ordinary meaning and applies them, even if the result seems harsh or unreasonable", "C": "The judge looks to the purpose of the Act and prefers the meaning that best promotes that purpose, using Hansard if needed", "D": "The judge asks what a reasonable ordinary person would have expected the statute to mean and applies that reading to the facts", "E": "The judge gives the words their ordinary meaning unless the result is absurd, in which case the meaning is modified slightly"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The judge gives the words their plain, ordinary meaning and applies them, even if the result seems harsh or unreasonable): The literal rule requires the judge to apply the plain, ordinary meaning of the words, leaving it to Parliament to correct any harshness. The golden rule modifies the words where the literal meaning would produce an absurd result. The mischief rule looks at the gap in the earlier law, and the purposive approach looks at the purpose of the Act as a whole. A test of what a reasonable person expected is not one of the recognised rules of interpretation."}, {"id": 1073, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Rhea Alvarez lived with Joe Bernard for ten years in a house he owned. When Joe died, his will left everything to his brother. Rhea earns a modest income and has little capital. She makes a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 as a cohabitee, and the court is satisfied that she qualifies as an applicant. The brother says the estate should stay with him as Joe wished. Which of the following best describes how the court will approach the claim?", "options": {"A": "It will award Rhea half of the estate, because the length of the relationship gives her a presumed equal share", "B": "It will limit itself to periodical payments for Rhea, because lump sums and transfers of property are available only to married applicants", "C": "It will apply the spouse standard, because a cohabitee of ten years is treated exactly as a surviving spouse for this purpose", "D": "It will decide whether the will reflects Joe's wishes and refuse the claim if it does, because a testator's freedom is the primary consideration", "E": "It will ask whether the will fails to make reasonable provision for Rhea's maintenance under the section 3 factors, and may order a lump sum or settlement"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It will ask whether the will fails to make reasonable provision for Rhea's maintenance under the section 3 factors, and may order a lump sum or settlement): A cohabitee applicant must show that the will fails to make reasonable provision for her maintenance, and the court weighs the section 3 factors, such as needs, resources, the size of the estate and the obligations and responsibilities of the deceased. The court has wide powers, including periodical payments, a lump sum, a transfer of property or a settlement, so ordering a settlement of the house is open. The testator's wishes are a factor but not conclusive, and there is no presumed share."}, {"id": 1074, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Rivermead Components Ltd supplies Stanhope Assemblies Ltd, which depends on it for a part. In March Rivermead, with no justification, threatened to stop all deliveries unless Stanhope agreed to a 20% price increase on an existing contract running until December. Stanhope could not find another source in time and agreed, recording its objection by email. It paid the higher price under protest and sought to recover the extra within a month. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Stanhope cannot avoid it, because it agreed to the increase in writing and a promise made with the benefit of continued supply is binding", "B": "Stanhope may avoid it only if it proves that the pressure was the sole reason for its decision to agree", "C": "Stanhope may avoid the agreement for economic duress, because the threat to breach was illegitimate pressure and Stanhope had no realistic alternative", "D": "Stanhope may avoid it, but only if it can show Rivermead threatened physical harm, since economic pressure alone is insufficient", "E": "Stanhope cannot avoid it, because commercial parties are free to bargain hard and the threat was a threat to stop trading"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stanhope may avoid the agreement for economic duress, because the threat to breach was illegitimate pressure and Stanhope had no realistic alternative): Economic duress requires illegitimate pressure that was a significant cause of the agreement, and a threat to break an existing contract is illegitimate. Stanhope had no practical alternative source, objected at the time and acted promptly, which supports avoidance. Duress is not confined to physical threats. The pressure need not be the sole cause, only a significant one, so that element does not defeat the claim."}, {"id": 1075, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Corliss Print Ltd, a business, bought a printing press for £30,000 from Dyke Machinery, a dealer in industrial equipment, on Dyke's standard terms, which said nothing about quality. Corliss examined the press for twenty minutes before buying. The press arrived with a defective drive unit that stopped it feeding paper reliably, and the fault was internal and could not be seen on a short inspection. Corliss has told Dyke it rejects the press. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Corliss cannot reject, because the contract was silent on quality and no term as to quality can be implied in a business sale", "B": "Corliss cannot reject, because a buyer who examined the goods before the contract cannot complain of defects that closer inspection could have revealed", "C": "Corliss may reject, because the press is not of satisfactory quality and a short examination does not bar the claim where it would not reveal the defect", "D": "Corliss may reject, because a dealer in industrial equipment gives an implied promise that the goods fit whatever use the buyer privately intended", "E": "Corliss may claim only damages, because the right to reject is available solely to consumers under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 and not to businesses"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Corliss may reject, because the press is not of satisfactory quality and a short examination does not bar the claim where it would not reveal the defect): In a sale in the course of a business, the Sale of Goods Act 1979 implies a term that goods are of satisfactory quality unless the defect was specifically drawn to the buyer's attention or an examination ought to have revealed it. This fault was internal and invisible on a short inspection, so the exception does not apply. A business buyer can also reject for breach of an implied condition, subject to losing the right on acceptance. The implied term does not depend on the contract saying anything about quality."}, {"id": 1076, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Gwen owns a flat with a large roof terrace overlooking the bedroom windows of the neighbours. She let the flat to tenants under a lease that permits use of the terrace and has no covenant against noise. The tenants held loud parties on the terrace most weekends. The neighbours complained to Gwen, who said that she lives elsewhere and that the noise was the tenants' problem. The neighbours sue Gwen in private nuisance. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Gwen is not liable, because the tenants created the noise and a landlord cannot be answerable for a nuisance created by tenants", "B": "Gwen is liable only if the lease contained a covenant against nuisance that she failed to enforce, because liability depends on breach of covenant", "C": "Gwen is liable only if she expressly encouraged the parties, because authorisation requires positive approval of the nuisance", "D": "Gwen is liable only if she lives at the flat, because liability in nuisance attaches to a person in occupation", "E": "Gwen is likely to be liable, because by letting the terrace for use she authorised a nuisance that was the almost inevitable result"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Gwen is likely to be liable, because by letting the terrace for use she authorised a nuisance that was the almost inevitable result): A landlord is liable for a nuisance created by tenants where the landlord authorised it, which includes letting premises for a use that will almost inevitably cause the nuisance. The terrace overlooks bedrooms and its permitted use inevitably produces noise, and the lease contains no restriction. Gwen's absence from the property does not matter, because the liability is for authorising the activity, not for occupying the land. Express encouragement is not required, and no covenant is needed for authorisation to be found."}, {"id": 1077, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Dilys, a retired teacher with no business use of her home, invites friends to a garden party. A clear notice on her gate, which Cora read on entering, says 'Visitors enter at their own risk and the owner accepts no liability for injury'. Cora trips on a loose paving slab that Dilys had known about for months, and breaks her ankle. Cora sues Dilys under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957. Which of the following best describes the effect of the notice?", "options": {"A": "It is likely to be effective, because the restriction on excluding liability for personal injury applies only to business occupiers", "B": "It is ineffective, because the common duty of care is imposed by statute on every occupier and cannot be varied by notice", "C": "It is ineffective, because Dilys knew of the loose slab, and an exclusion cannot cover a danger the occupier had known about", "D": "It is effective only if it named the loose paving slab specifically, because an exclusion notice must identify the particular danger it covers", "E": "It is effective only if Cora signed a written agreement, because a notice alone cannot form the basis of an exclusion"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is likely to be effective, because the restriction on excluding liability for personal injury applies only to business occupiers): The 1957 Act allows an occupier to extend, restrict, modify or exclude the common duty of care by agreement or otherwise, which includes a notice reasonably brought to the visitor's attention. The Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 prevents the exclusion of liability for death or personal injury only where the occupation is for business purposes, and Dilys uses her home privately. Cora read the notice, so it was brought to her attention. Knowledge of the slab does not remove the effect of the notice. Naming the specific danger is a feature of warnings and is not needed for an exclusion."}, {"id": 1078, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Karl takes a colleague's season ticket for the last home match of the season, which is the only match remaining and after which the ticket will be worthless. He uses it to enter the stadium and then leaves it on the colleague's desk. The colleague gets the ticket back but it has lost all its value. Karl is charged with theft and accepts that he acted dishonestly. Is the intention to permanently deprive established?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because he must be taken to intend the natural consequence of dishonestly using a ticket that belongs to another", "B": "Yes, because any dishonest use of another's property for his own purposes satisfies the requirement, even where the item is returned", "C": "No, because he returned the ticket to the colleague's desk and so did not intend the owner to lose it for good", "D": "No, because borrowing amounts to theft only if the item is returned damaged or in a changed state", "E": "Yes, because he treated the ticket as his own to dispose of in circumstances making his borrowing equivalent to an outright taking"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, because he treated the ticket as his own to dispose of in circumstances making his borrowing equivalent to an outright taking): Section 6 treats a borrowing as an intention to permanently deprive where it is for a period and in circumstances making it equivalent to an outright taking or disposal. Using the ticket when its whole value would be exhausted amounts to that. Return of the ticket does not defeat the intention, because what the owner got back was worthless. Not every dishonest use qualifies, and the section is confined to borrowing of this kind."}, {"id": 1079, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Eluned is a fluent Welsh speaker who also speaks good English. She is to be tried for an assault at the Crown Court in Cardiff and wishes to give her evidence in Welsh. The prosecutor suggests she use English, as she can obviously manage it and it would save time. Her solicitor, who does not speak Welsh, is advising her. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She may speak Welsh but must pay for the interpreter herself, because her English is adequate", "B": "She may speak Welsh only if her solicitor also speaks Welsh, since otherwise the advocate cannot follow her evidence", "C": "She may speak Welsh only at the discretion of the judge, who may refuse where her English is adequate", "D": "She is entitled to speak Welsh in the proceedings and need not show that she cannot speak English, subject to giving any required notice", "E": "She may speak Welsh only if she satisfies the court that she cannot give evidence adequately in English"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She is entitled to speak Welsh in the proceedings and need not show that she cannot speak English, subject to giving any required notice): Under the Welsh Language Act 1993 anyone in legal proceedings in Wales may speak Welsh if they wish, subject to any prior notice required by the rules. She does not have to prove she cannot speak English, and the court cannot refuse because her English is good. The court arranges interpretation, so she does not bear the cost. The right also does not depend on her solicitor speaking Welsh."}, {"id": 1080, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Redwater Logistics Ltd was served with a claim form and particulars of claim on 2 March. It filed an acknowledgment of service on 10 March, indicating that it intended to contest jurisdiction. It then took no further step until 30 March, when its solicitors issued an application under CPR Part 11 arguing that the contract contained an exclusive foreign jurisdiction clause. The claimant objects that the application is late. What is the effect of the delay?", "options": {"A": "The application is in time, because the defendant has 28 days from service of the particulars of claim to challenge jurisdiction", "B": "The application is out of time, but jurisdiction is preserved because the acknowledgment of service recorded an intention to contest it", "C": "Redwater is treated as accepting that the court has jurisdiction, because the application was not made within 14 days of filing the acknowledgment of service", "D": "The application is in time, because the 14 days run from the date on which the defence would otherwise have been due", "E": "Filing the acknowledgment of service already amounted to acceptance of jurisdiction, so the application was not available to Redwater once that form had been filed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Redwater is treated as accepting that the court has jurisdiction, because the application was not made within 14 days of filing the acknowledgment of service): A defendant who wishes to dispute jurisdiction must first file an acknowledgment of service and then apply under Part 11 within 14 days of filing it, with supporting evidence. Redwater filed on 10 March, so the period ended on 24 March and the application on 30 March was late. A defendant that files an acknowledgment and fails to apply in time is treated as having accepted that the court has jurisdiction. Recording an intention to contest on the form does not preserve the point, and the period is not tied to the date for the defence."}, {"id": 1081, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Sol is charged with wounding with intent under section 18 after attacking a rival. He tells his solicitor, Imogen, that he planned the attack for weeks and deliberately drank half a bottle of whisky beforehand to give himself the nerve to do it. He instructs her to argue that he was too drunk to form any intention. How should Imogen advise and act?", "options": {"A": "Advise that voluntary intoxication is no defence to section 18, and withdraw from the case and inform the court of his admission", "B": "Advise that intoxication is a good defence to a specific intent offence, and run it because it is the client's decision how to defend himself", "C": "Advise that drinking for courage gives no intoxication defence, and decline to say he lacked intent when his own account shows otherwise", "D": "Advise that drinking for courage is a defence reducing liability to section 20, and run it because the prosecution must still prove intent", "E": "Advise that intoxication may negate intent, but call no evidence of drinking so as to avoid misleading the court on the amount consumed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise that drinking for courage gives no intoxication defence, and decline to say he lacked intent when his own account shows otherwise): Where a defendant forms an intent and drinks to give himself courage, the intoxication is no defence, as the House of Lords held in Attorney General for Northern Ireland v Gallagher. Imogen must give him that accurate advice. She cannot put forward a case she knows to be false, so she must decline his instruction to say he lacked intent, though he may still put the prosecution to proof. She need not withdraw or disclose his admission, which remains confidential."}, {"id": 1082, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Zoe Archer, aged 15, is arrested for shoplifting and brought to a police station. Her parents cannot be contacted, and no social worker is available. The custody officer asks who can act as her appropriate adult. Several people are willing to attend and none has been told about the evidence. Zoe is anxious and wishes to be accompanied. Which of the following may properly act as Zoe's appropriate adult?", "options": {"A": "Zoe's brother, aged 16, who was with her at the shop and has also been questioned by police about the same theft", "B": "The shop manager who reported the theft and has offered to attend to support Zoe during the interview", "C": "A police community support officer who lives nearby, knows Zoe well and is not involved in the case or its investigation", "D": "Zoe's aunt, aged 40, who was not at the shop, has no connection with the offence and is willing to attend", "E": "The duty solicitor who has come to advise Zoe and has agreed to act in both roles to save time at the station"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Zoe's aunt, aged 40, who was not at the shop, has no connection with the offence and is willing to attend): Under Code C, where neither a parent or guardian nor a social worker is available, a juvenile's appropriate adult may be another responsible adult aged 18 or over who is not a police officer or employed by the police. A person who is suspected of involvement, is a victim or witness, or acts as the suspect's solicitor cannot act as appropriate adult, because their roles conflict. Zoe's aunt has no involvement and so qualifies. A 16-year-old is not an adult for this purpose, and a police community support officer is employed by the police."}, {"id": 1083, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A trainee at a commercial firm asks her supervisor how the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 and the Money Laundering Regulations 2017 fit together. She has read that the regulations implement international standards derived from the Financial Action Task Force, and she is unsure whether the Act or the regulations create the criminal offences of laundering and failing to disclose suspicion. Which of the following correctly describes their relationship?", "options": {"A": "Both instruments create the same offences, so a firm is prosecuted under whichever one the National Crime Agency selects for the case", "B": "The Act applies only to regulated firms, while the regulations apply to every person who handles criminal property in any capacity", "C": "The regulations replace the Act for solicitors, so the Act's offences apply only to unregulated businesses and private individuals", "D": "The Act creates the main laundering offences, and the regulations impose preventative duties on firms, such as due diligence and training", "E": "The regulations create the principal laundering offences, and the Act sets out the due diligence and training duties for regulated firms"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Act creates the main laundering offences, and the regulations impose preventative duties on firms, such as due diligence and training): The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 contains the criminal offences of concealing, arranging, acquiring and failing to disclose, together with tipping off. Its main laundering offences apply to everyone, while some disclosure offences apply to the regulated sector. The Money Laundering Regulations 2017 are preventative rules, requiring firms to assess risk, adopt policies, carry out due diligence and train staff. They sit beside the Act rather than replacing it, and a breach of either can bring separate consequences."}, {"id": 1084, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya and Daniel Sharma live in Bristol and are buying a house in Cardiff for £320,000, with completion on 12 March 2026. Their solicitor in Bristol asks a junior colleague to complete the tax return. The junior colleague says it is a stamp duty land tax return for HM Revenue and Customs because the buyers live in England. The firm acts on the purchase only, and the buyers will use a mortgage. Which of the following is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "Stamp duty land tax applies because the buyers live in England, and the return goes to HMRC within 14 days of completion", "B": "Land transaction tax applies as the property is in Wales, and the return goes to the Welsh Revenue Authority within 30 days", "C": "Stamp duty land tax applies because it is charged on all property purchases in the United Kingdom, with a return within 30 days", "D": "Land transaction tax applies because the property is in Wales, and the return goes to the Welsh Revenue Authority within 14 days of completion", "E": "Land transaction tax applies because the property is in Wales, but the return goes to HMRC within 30 days of completion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Land transaction tax applies as the property is in Wales, and the return goes to the Welsh Revenue Authority within 30 days): Land transaction tax replaced stamp duty land tax for land in Wales, and the tax that applies depends on where the property is, not where the buyers live. The return is made to the Welsh Revenue Authority, and it and the tax are due within 30 days of the effective date, which is normally completion. The 14 day deadline belongs to stamp duty land tax returns in England, and HMRC does not collect the Welsh tax. The mortgage and the buyers' residence do not change the answer."}, {"id": 1085, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Eilidh, Tomasz and Wanjiru have run a bookbinding workshop as partners for two years from a rented unit. They never signed a partnership agreement. Eilidh and Wanjiru want to admit Callan as a fourth partner, but Tomasz objects. Eilidh says that, as two of the three partners, they form a majority and can outvote him. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Callan cannot be admitted, because new partners require a unanimous written decision recorded in an agreement signed by all the partners", "B": "Callan cannot be admitted without Tomasz's consent, because a new partner needs the agreement of all existing partners", "C": "Callan can be admitted, because ordinary differences between partners are settled by a majority and admitting a partner is an ordinary matter", "D": "Callan cannot be admitted unless he buys out Tomasz's share, because an objecting partner must be paid out of the business before anyone joins", "E": "Callan can be admitted, because a majority of partners can decide to admit a new partner provided Tomasz receives reasonable written notice"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Callan cannot be admitted without Tomasz's consent, because a new partner needs the agreement of all existing partners): In the absence of agreement the default rules in section 24 of the Partnership Act 1890 apply. Differences about ordinary matters are decided by a majority, but no person may be introduced as a partner without the consent of all existing partners. Tomasz therefore has an effective veto. No writing is needed for consent, and no payment to an objecting partner is required, so the majority argument fails."}, {"id": 1086, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "The trust instrument of the Birch family trust allows the trustees to be paid reasonable fees for acting as directors of any company in which the trust holds shares. The trust holds 60 per cent of Birch Ltd. Femi, one of the trustees, is appointed managing director of Birch Ltd and takes a salary of £50,000. A beneficiary says that Femi must account for the salary to the trust. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "Femi may keep the salary, because the instrument expressly authorised payment for directorships, so acting within that authority is no breach", "B": "Femi may keep it only if a majority of the beneficiaries ratify the salary by vote at the next meeting of the trustees", "C": "Femi must account unless she first obtained court approval, since a trust instrument cannot authorise a profit by a trustee", "D": "Femi may keep only part of it, because the court limits authorised director's pay to a reasonable amount in all cases", "E": "Femi must account for it, because a trustee cannot profit from a position held through trust shares even where the instrument appears to allow it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Femi may keep the salary, because the instrument expressly authorised payment for directorships, so acting within that authority is no breach): A profit made by a trustee is permitted where the trust instrument authorises it, and the settlor is entitled to do so. The instrument here allows fees for directorships of companies in which the trust holds shares, and Femi's salary falls within it. A strict reading may apply, but nothing here requires ratification or court approval. The benefit is therefore an authorised conflict and not a breach."}, {"id": 1087, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Nadia and Peter are the only directors of Kestrel Logistics Ltd, which has the model articles without amendment. Peter is travelling abroad and cannot be contacted for two weeks. Nadia asks the company's solicitor to prepare minutes recording a board meeting at which the directors resolved to sell the company's main warehouse for a price she has agreed. No meeting has taken place. Which of the following is the solicitor's correct response?", "options": {"A": "Stop acting for the company, because a deadlock between its two directors creates a conflict that requires the solicitor to withdraw", "B": "Prepare the minutes recording that Nadia alone decided, because the model articles allow one director to act when the other is unreachable", "C": "Prepare the minutes but date them after Peter returns, so that the record matches his eventual approval of the sale", "D": "Prepare the minutes with Nadia as chair, because the chair's casting vote allows her to carry a board decision on her own", "E": "Decline to prepare minutes of a meeting that did not happen, and advise that a valid decision needs a quorate meeting or a unanimous written decision"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to prepare minutes of a meeting that did not happen, and advise that a valid decision needs a quorate meeting or a unanimous written decision): The model articles require a quorum of two directors unless the board fixes otherwise, so Nadia cannot take the decision alone. A decision may instead be taken by unanimous written agreement of all eligible directors, which would need Peter's participation. A solicitor must not prepare a document that gives a false impression that a meeting took place, as that would mislead others and breach the duty of honesty. A casting vote cannot supply a missing quorum, and there is no conflict requiring withdrawal."}, {"id": 1088, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Lena Marsh, a shop manager, tells the police that the thief who took goods from her till was Carl Boyd, a regular customer whom she has served every week for six years. Carl is arrested. He admits that he was in the shop at the time but says that he paid for everything. The investigating officer wants Lena to attend a video identification procedure. Carl's solicitor is asked for her view. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "No identification procedure is needed, because Lena knows Carl well and the dispute is about what he did, not who he is", "B": "A parade should be held in preference to video, because Lena's identification will be the main evidence against Carl at trial", "C": "A confrontation should be arranged, because Lena knows Carl and a procedure involving other people would add nothing", "D": "No procedure is needed, but only if Carl first signs a waiver, because the police still need his consent to forgo one", "E": "A video identification must be held, because Carl has not admitted the theft and so his identification remains in dispute"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No identification procedure is needed, because Lena knows Carl well and the dispute is about what he did, not who he is): Code D provides that an identification procedure is not required where it would serve no useful purpose, for example because the witness knows the suspect so well, or where there is no dispute that the suspect was the person seen. Carl accepts that he was at the shop, so the real issue is whether he stole, which an identification procedure cannot resolve. A video procedure, parade or confrontation would therefore serve no purpose. A confrontation in particular is a last resort when other methods are impracticable, and no waiver by the suspect is needed for the police to decide that a procedure is unnecessary."}, {"id": 1089, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Harbour Fabrics Ltd has assets with a balance sheet value of £620,000 and total liabilities, including contingent and prospective liabilities, of £540,000. Its bank account holds £9,000. Trade creditors have demanded immediate payment of £85,000 that fell due last month, and there is no prospect of raising the money from any source. Its director, Ms Okafor, asks whether the company is insolvent in law. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The company is insolvent on the cash flow test because it cannot pay debts as they fall due, although it passes the balance sheet test", "B": "The company is not insolvent, because the balance sheet test is the sole test and its assets exceed its total liabilities", "C": "The company is not insolvent until a creditor serves a statutory demand and the company fails to comply within three weeks", "D": "The company is insolvent on both tests, because failing to pay a debt that has fallen due shows liabilities exceed assets", "E": "The company is insolvent on the balance sheet test because liabilities are counted, although it is able to meet its debts as they fall due from its cash"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The company is insolvent on the cash flow test because it cannot pay debts as they fall due, although it passes the balance sheet test): Section 123 Insolvency Act 1986 contains two alternative tests: inability to pay debts as they fall due, and assets worth less than liabilities once contingent and prospective liabilities are counted. Here assets of £620,000 exceed liabilities of £540,000, so the balance sheet test is passed. The company cannot meet £85,000 already due from £9,000 in the bank, so it fails the cash flow test, and failing either test is enough. A statutory demand is only one way of proving inability to pay, and the view that healthy assets keep the company solvent overlooks that the tests are alternatives."}, {"id": 1090, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Section 7 of the Fireworks Safety Act makes it an offence to 'offer for sale' any firework of Category 4 to a person under 18. A shop owner, Bashir Hussain, placed a box of Category 4 fireworks on a shelf in his shop window, with a price tag, on a Friday evening. A teenager looked at them but bought nothing, and an inspector charged Bashir. He says contract law treats a shelf display as an invitation to treat. The prosecution argues the Act aims to keep fireworks from children. Applying the literal rule, what is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "No offence, because the court may read in a requirement that a sale to a child must actually have taken place", "B": "No offence, because the words carry their settled legal meaning and a window display is an invitation to treat, not an offer", "C": "Offence committed, because the Act aims to protect children and the court must prefer the reading that best achieves that aim", "D": "Offence committed, because in everyday speech a shop that displays goods with a price is offering them for sale", "E": "Offence committed, because the display shows intention to sell and Parliament can have meant the offence to cover preparatory conduct"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No offence, because the words carry their settled legal meaning and a window display is an invitation to treat, not an offer): Under the literal rule the court gives words their plain meaning and, where a term has a settled legal meaning, it takes that meaning. In contract law a goods display is an invitation to treat and the customer makes the offer, so Bashir has not made an offer for sale. The court does not ask whether Parliament wanted to protect children, as that is the mischief or purposive approach. The outcome may seem unfortunate, but the literal rule leaves any gap for Parliament to close."}, {"id": 1091, "domain": "land_law", "question": "You act for Mrs Reeves, whom a bank requires to take independent advice before she charges the family home to secure her husband's business loan. At the meeting she says she has not read the documents, does not realise that the home could be sold if the business fails, and wants you simply to sign the bank's form confirming that she has been advised. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action?", "options": {"A": "Tell the bank directly that she does not understand the documents, because the duty to the lender overrides client confidentiality", "B": "Explain the nature and risks of the charge, and give the bank's confirmation only once you are satisfied she understands and has authorised it", "C": "Send the documents to her husband's solicitors to explain, since they know the business and the purpose of the loan", "D": "Sign the confirmation but add a note that she asked for no detailed advice, so the bank knows the position", "E": "Sign the confirmation as instructed, because the decision to proceed belongs to the client and she is entitled to decline detailed advice about the loan and its risks"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain the nature and risks of the charge, and give the bank's confirmation only once you are satisfied she understands and has authorised it): The point of the advice is that she understands the transaction and its risks, so you must explain them before confirming anything. A confirmation that advice had been given would be misleading if she had not understood it, and the Code forbids misleading others. You also need her authority before confirming or disclosing, so telling the bank directly would breach confidentiality. Her husband's solicitors have an interest that conflicts with hers and are not suitable advisers."}, {"id": 1092, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Gordon Hale is the sole registered proprietor of a house. His girlfriend Tessa moved in 18 months ago and contributed £35,000 to a kitchen extension on the understanding that she would own a share, giving her a beneficial interest. Gordon sells to Pete Lawson, who inspects the house for ten minutes while Tessa is at work. Her clothes and belongings are throughout the house. Pete asks Gordon whether anyone else has a claim and Gordon says no. Is Pete bound by Tessa's interest?", "options": {"A": "No, because Pete asked Gordon about other claims and Gordon's denial relieves Pete of any duty to inspect further", "B": "Yes, but only if Tessa registered a unilateral notice before completion, which would then make her interest binding", "C": "No, because an interest under a trust of land can be protected by a restriction and so cannot override", "D": "No, because Tessa was out when Pete visited, so she was not in actual occupation at the relevant time", "E": "Yes, because Tessa was in actual occupation and that occupation was discoverable on careful inspection"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, because Tessa was in actual occupation and that occupation was discoverable on careful inspection): Schedule 3 paragraph 2 to the Land Registration Act 2002 gives overriding status to an interest belonging to a person in actual occupation, including a beneficial interest under a trust of land. Tessa lives in the house and her belongings are everywhere, so her presence is evident and she was in actual occupation despite being at work at the time of inspection. The exception for occupation that would not have been obvious on a careful inspection does not apply. Asking only Gordon is not an enquiry of Tessa, and no registration is needed."}, {"id": 1093, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A Minister announces that she will amend a piece of assimilated direct legislation on chemical labelling by a statutory instrument, to bring it into line with new UK standards, without passing a new Act. The trade body, Chemical Packers Ltd, doubts that the Minister may alter what was originally EU regulation. The Minister relies on a power in a recent Act to amend assimilated law by regulations. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "The amendment is lawful if it falls within the power conferred by the enabling Act, because assimilated law can be amended by delegated power", "B": "The amendment is lawful under the royal prerogative, because the Crown retains inherent power to change law originating from treaties", "C": "The amendment is unlawful, because the law was made by the EU, and only the EU institutions can alter its content", "D": "The amendment is lawful, because a Minister may amend any assimilated law by regulations whether or not an Act gives the power", "E": "The amendment is unlawful, because assimilated law has the status of primary legislation and only a new Act of Parliament can change a measure of that kind"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The amendment is lawful if it falls within the power conferred by the enabling Act, because assimilated law can be amended by delegated power): Assimilated law is part of domestic law and can be changed by Parliament, or by a minister using a statutory power in an Act that authorises amendment by regulations. The Minister relies on such a power, so the amendment is lawful as long as it stays within the scope and procedure the enabling Act sets, and it remains open to the usual review for ultra vires. Assimilated direct legislation is not primary legislation merely because it was once EU law. The prerogative cannot alter statute law, and a minister has no free standing power."}, {"id": 1094, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A solicitor acts for Oakridge Developments Ltd, which wants an urgent injunction without notice to stop a council demolishing its site tomorrow. In the papers the solicitor has seen a council letter, sent last month, warning that the site was unsafe, which is harmful to the company's case. The managing director asks that the letter be left out of the evidence to keep the application strong. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Leave the letter out, because the solicitor owes a duty of confidentiality to the client and need not volunteer adverse documents", "B": "Include the letter only if the council's solicitor asks for it, because disclosure of adverse documents is required only after an inter partes request", "C": "Withdraw the application and issue on notice instead, because the duty of disclosure applies only to applications heard with notice", "D": "Leave the letter out but tell the judge orally that other evidence exists, because that satisfies the duty to the court without harming the client", "E": "Explain that the duty of full and frank disclosure applies on a without notice application, so the letter must be put before the court"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Explain that the duty of full and frank disclosure applies on a without notice application, so the letter must be put before the court): On a without notice application the applicant must make full and frank disclosure of all material facts, including those against its case, because the other side cannot correct the picture. A solicitor's duty not to mislead the court and to act with integrity overrides the client's wish to leave the letter out, and confidentiality does not permit concealment of a material fact. An oral hint that other evidence exists would still leave the court misled. If the director insists on suppression, the firm would have to stop acting."}, {"id": 1095, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Alder Foods Ltd sued Penrose Catering Ltd for £15,000 for goods sold. Penrose served a defence and, in the same document, a counterclaim for £22,000 for earlier defective deliveries. Both were served on 4 October. Alder does not dispute some of the facts in the counterclaim but intends to contest the amount, and its solicitor proposes simply to file a reply to the defence in due course. What should the solicitor advise?", "options": {"A": "No response is needed, because a counterclaim is taken to be denied until the court gives directions after allocation", "B": "A reply to the defence is enough, because it also responds to the counterclaim and the claimant need not plead separately", "C": "A defence to the counterclaim must be filed within 28 days of its service, as with any defence to a claim where an acknowledgment has been filed", "D": "A defence to the counterclaim is due 14 days after allocation to a track, when directions are given for statements of case", "E": "A defence to the counterclaim must be filed within 14 days of its service, which written agreement may extend by up to 28 days"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A defence to the counterclaim must be filed within 14 days of its service, which written agreement may extend by up to 28 days): A counterclaim is a claim in its own right, so the original claimant must file a defence to it, normally within 14 days of service. The parties can agree in writing to extend that time by up to 28 days without the court. A reply to the defence is optional and is quite different, because a claimant who files no reply is not taken to admit the defendant's case. If Alder files nothing, the defendant could seek judgment on the counterclaim."}, {"id": 1096, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Okoro Ltd, a private company with net assets of £5,000,000, agrees to sell a plot of land to Gwen, one of its directors, for its full market value of £150,000. Gwen declared her interest and the board approved the sale. No member has voted on the sale and contracts have not been exchanged. Gwen asks whether member approval is required under the Companies Act 2006 provisions on substantial property transactions.", "options": {"A": "No, because Gwen is paying full market value, and member approval is needed only for sales at an undervalue", "B": "Yes, but approval must be given by special resolution of the members before contracts are exchanged", "C": "Yes, but approval by the other directors who are not interested in the sale is enough to satisfy the statutory requirement for it", "D": "Yes, because the land is worth more than £100,000, so prior approval by ordinary resolution of the members is needed", "E": "No, because the price is under 10% of the company's net assets, so the approval of the board is enough"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Yes, because the land is worth more than £100,000, so prior approval by ordinary resolution of the members is needed): A director acquiring a non-cash asset from the company needs prior member approval where the value is more than £100,000, or exceeds £5,000 and 10% of net assets. At £150,000 the land passes the fixed limit even though it is a small fraction of net assets. The approval is by ordinary resolution. Paying a fair price does not remove the need, and board approval alone is not enough."}, {"id": 1097, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Greta enters a flat as a trespasser with intent to steal, carrying nothing. Inside, she takes a heavy kitchen knife from a block, intending to use it on anyone who disturbs her, and then steals a laptop. She leaves without seeing anyone. Greta is charged with aggravated burglary under section 10 of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following best describes her liability?", "options": {"A": "Not guilty, because the weapon must be present at the point of entry for a section 9(1)(a) offence and she had no intention then", "B": "Guilty, because aggravated burglary is committed whenever a burglar is in possession of any sharp object found inside a dwelling", "C": "Guilty, because the burglary under section 9(1)(b) was committed when she stole, and she then had with her an article intended to injure", "D": "Not guilty, because a kitchen knife is not made or adapted to injure and so is not a weapon of offence", "E": "Not guilty of aggravated burglary, because she did not bring the knife with her when she entered the flat"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Guilty, because the burglary under section 9(1)(b) was committed when she stole, and she then had with her an article intended to injure): A weapon of offence includes an article made or adapted for causing injury and one intended by the person having it for that use. Greta picked up the knife intending to use it on anyone who disturbed her, so it became a weapon of offence. The burglary under section 9(1)(b) was committed when she stole, and she had the knife with her then. The weapon need not be present at entry."}, {"id": 1098, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Fiona's neighbour, Idris, is away on a long holiday. His dead hedge has grown across her path and blocked her light. Fiona, who has never discussed it with Idris, honestly believes he would agree to the hedge being cut down if he knew how bad it had become, and she cuts it down. Idris was in fact opposed to this. Fiona is charged with criminal damage. Does she have a lawful excuse under section 5(2)(a) of the Criminal Damage Act 1971?", "options": {"A": "No, because the belief that the owner has consented must be reasonable, and Idris's later objection shows hers was not", "B": "Yes, because the hedge was dead and the damage was minor, so no offence is made out and no excuse is needed on these facts", "C": "Yes, because an honest belief that the owner would consent if he knew the circumstances is a lawful excuse, even if unreasonable", "D": "No, because the excuse applies only where the owner has actually given consent before the act of damage occurs", "E": "Yes, but only if a reasonable person in her position would also have held the belief that Idris would agree"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, because an honest belief that the owner would consent if he knew the circumstances is a lawful excuse, even if unreasonable): Section 5(2)(a) gives a lawful excuse where the defendant believed that the owner consented or would have consented had he known of the damage and its circumstances. Under section 5(3) it is the honesty of the belief that matters, not whether it is justified or reasonable. Idris's actual opposition does not remove the excuse. Triviality of the damage is not a defence."}, {"id": 1099, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Colm and Flora Brennan are registered co-owners, as tenants in common, of a house bought as a family home for themselves and their two young children. Colm moves out and applies under section 14 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 for an order for sale, saying he needs his share of the equity. Flora opposes sale because the children are settled at the local school and she cannot afford to rehouse them. The house is not mortgaged. Which of the following best describes the court's approach to Colm's application?", "options": {"A": "The court will refuse sale whenever children live in the house, because their welfare overrides the other factors", "B": "The court must order sale, because a tenant in common has a right to realise his share of the equity at any time", "C": "The court must order sale unless Flora can show that Colm has behaved unreasonably in leaving the family home", "D": "The court applies the section 15 factors, including the purpose of the trust and the welfare of the children in occupation, and may refuse or postpone sale", "E": "The court looks at the parties' financial contributions and will order sale if Colm paid more than Flora"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court applies the section 15 factors, including the purpose of the trust and the welfare of the children in occupation, and may refuse or postpone sale): Applications under section 14 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 are decided by reference to the factors in section 15, which include the intentions of the person who created the trust, the purposes for which the property is held, the welfare of any minor who occupies or might reasonably be expected to occupy it as a home, and the interests of any secured creditor. The family home purpose and the children's welfare weigh against immediate sale, so the court may refuse or postpone it. There is no automatic right to sale, and the welfare of children is weighed, not decisive."}, {"id": 1100, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Lorna Farrell, a widow, died in 2026 domiciled in England and Wales. Her estate is worth £1,100,000, including her home worth £500,000, and everything passes to her two children. Her late husband left everything to her, so none of his nil rate band or residence nil rate band was used and both are fully transferable. Lorna made no lifetime gifts. Assume the nil rate band is £325,000, the residence nil rate band is £175,000, no taper applies and the rate of tax is 40 per cent. What is the Inheritance Tax payable on her estate?", "options": {"A": "£240,000, because neither transferred band is available and each band is applied once only to the estate", "B": "£40,000, because the estate exceeds the combined nil rate bands, including both transferred bands, by £100,000", "C": "£180,000, because the residence nil rate bands are lost and only the two nil rate bands are deducted from the estate", "D": "£170,000, because the transferred nil rate band is ignored while both residence nil rate bands are applied against the home", "E": "£110,000, because the transferred residence nil rate band is ignored and only a single £175,000 band applies against the home"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (£40,000, because the estate exceeds the combined nil rate bands, including both transferred bands, by £100,000): Lorna has two nil rate bands totalling £650,000 and, because her home worth £500,000 passes to her children, two residence nil rate bands totalling £350,000, making £1,000,000 in all. The estate of £1,100,000 exceeds this by £100,000, and tax at 40 per cent on that is £40,000. Leaving out either transferred band increases the taxable amount, which is the common error in the other figures."}, {"id": 1101, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Hamza and Ruth work for Alder Plastics Ltd. On a Saturday evening, Hamza attended a birthday gathering at Ruth's house, to which most colleagues had been invited by Ruth personally. No work was discussed. After a quarrel over a private debt between the two, Hamza punched Ruth, breaking her nose. Alder Plastics Ltd played no part in organising or paying for the gathering. Ruth sues Alder Plastics Ltd. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails, because vicarious liability cannot extend to intentional torts, which fall outside the scope of any employment", "B": "The claim fails, because the assault was not closely connected with Hamza's work and arose from a personal quarrel", "C": "The claim succeeds, because Hamza remained in Alder Plastics Ltd's employment when he acted, so his torts are attributed to it", "D": "The claim succeeds, because Hamza and Ruth were colleagues and the gathering was attended mainly by Alder Plastics employees", "E": "The claim succeeds, because deliberate wrongdoing by an employee falls within the course of employment where it harms a fellow employee"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim fails, because the assault was not closely connected with Hamza's work and arose from a personal quarrel): Under the close connection test, an employer is liable for an employee's wrongful act only where there is a sufficient link between what the employee was employed to do and the wrong, so that it is right to hold the employer responsible. Here the gathering was social, not organised by the employer, and the quarrel was about a private debt. That separates this case from one where a dispute arises from work tasks or a work discussion. Deliberate torts can create vicarious liability, so the intentional nature of the assault is not the reason the claim fails."}, {"id": 1102, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A lay trustee sells trust land at a price recommended by a local estate agent, who the trustee honestly believed to be experienced. She does not obtain an independent valuation and the land is later found to have been sold for £20,000 less than its market value. The trust instrument has no exemption clause. The beneficiaries sue her for the shortfall and she asks the court to excuse her. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court may relieve her wholly or partly under section 61 Trustee Act 1925 if she acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused", "B": "Relief is available only if every beneficiary agrees in writing to excuse her for the breach she committed", "C": "Relief is given automatically where a trustee acted honestly, because reasonableness is not a requirement of section 61", "D": "Relief is not available, because section 61 applies only to professional trustees who are paid for their services to the trust", "E": "Relief is available only where the trust suffered no loss, since compensation for loss rules out the court's power to excuse"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court may relieve her wholly or partly under section 61 Trustee Act 1925 if she acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused): Section 61 Trustee Act 1925 lets the court relieve a trustee wholly or partly from liability if she acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused. A lay trustee who relied on an apparently competent agent may show that, though failure to obtain a valuation may count against her. Honesty alone is not enough, and the section is not confined to unpaid trustees, although paid professionals find it harder. Loss does not bar relief."}, {"id": 1103, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A borough council decides to close its only public swimming pool because of rising costs. It considered a costings report, an equality impact assessment and the consultation responses, and then voted by a narrow majority. A local swimming club wants to challenge the decision on the ground of irrationality. The club's solicitor needs to explain the test the court will apply. Which of the following best describes the Wednesbury test?", "options": {"A": "The decision is irrational if the majority of residents opposed it, because a public body is bound to follow the view of the majority", "B": "The decision is irrational if it caused harsh results for the club and a reasonable authority would have used a less damaging measure", "C": "The decision is irrational if the court considers that a better decision was available to the council on the evidence it had", "D": "The decision is irrational if the court would itself have kept the pool open after weighing the same costings and consultation responses", "E": "The decision is irrational only if no reasonable authority could have made it, and the court does not substitute its own view"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The decision is irrational only if no reasonable authority could have made it, and the court does not substitute its own view): Wednesbury unreasonableness is a high threshold: the decision must be so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could ever have reached it. The court does not ask whether it would have decided differently or whether a better choice existed, since the merits are for the council. The council weighed a costings report and consultation responses, so it is hard to call its decision irrational. Opposition from residents and harshness to the club do not meet the test."}, {"id": 1104, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "The SRA writes to a firm in Plymouth requesting a client file and a copy of the firm's client account records as part of an investigation into a complaint about one of its solicitors. The managing partner says that the file contains confidential client information and that confidentiality entitles the firm to refuse to hand it over. The file also contains some advice from counsel on the strength of the client's case. Which of the following best states the firm's position?", "options": {"A": "The firm must provide everything requested, including counsel's advice, because the duty to cooperate overrides legal professional privilege", "B": "The firm may provide the file after each client has consented in writing to disclosure to the SRA", "C": "The firm may delay its response until the investigation has been completed, because the duty to respond arises after a formal charge", "D": "The firm may refuse, because the duty of confidentiality to clients takes precedence over a request from the SRA", "E": "The firm must respond promptly and provide the information, withholding only privileged documents, because confidentiality alone does not justify refusing a regulator"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The firm must respond promptly and provide the information, withholding only privileged documents, because confidentiality alone does not justify refusing a regulator): Firms and individuals must cooperate with the SRA and respond promptly to its requests, and a duty of confidentiality is not a ground for refusing. Client consent is not needed for the SRA to see confidential material. Legal professional privilege is different, so counsel's advice may be withheld. The duty to respond arises during the investigation, not after any charge."}, {"id": 1105, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Dylan Thorne is charged with arson of a warehouse. At his trial the prosecution wishes to prove that, in conversation with a friend, Dylan said, 'Yes, I was at the warehouse that night, but I only went to look and I did not light anything.' The friend is not a person in authority. Dylan's counsel argues that the remark cannot be a confession because it contains a denial of the offence. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is a confession, but only the adverse part is admissible and the denial is excluded as self-serving", "B": "It is not a confession, because the definition covers only full admissions made to a police officer or other person in authority", "C": "It is a confession only if Dylan made it in a police interview recorded in accordance with the Codes of Practice", "D": "It is a confession, because the definition covers any statement partly adverse to the maker, whoever it was made to", "E": "It is not a confession, because a statement that contains a denial must be treated as wholly exculpatory"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is a confession, because the definition covers any statement partly adverse to the maker, whoever it was made to): Under section 82 of PACE, a confession includes any statement wholly or partly adverse to the person who made it, whether made to a person in authority or not. Dylan's admission of being at the warehouse is partly adverse, so the statement qualifies. A mixed statement is put before the jury in full, both the adverse and exculpatory parts. A recorded interview is not required."}, {"id": 1106, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Lena Ford instructs a solicitor to bring judicial review against a council that has refused her a market pitch, arguing that an officer promised her the pitch in an email, which gives her a legitimate expectation. When the solicitor looks at the email file she sees that its properties show it was created last week. Lena then admits that she wrote it herself to strengthen her case, and asks the solicitor to disclose it with the claim. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Disclose the email but add that its authenticity is a matter for the court, because the solicitor is not required to verify a client's documents", "B": "Disclose the email with the claim, because the solicitor must act on instructions and the council can challenge its authenticity at trial", "C": "Continue to act and omit the email while pleading the promise orally, because oral assurances can found a legitimate expectation too", "D": "Decline to put the email forward, advise Lena it cannot be used, and stop acting if she insists, so as not to mislead the court", "E": "Report Lena to the police at once without telling her, because the duty of confidentiality does not apply to a dishonest client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to put the email forward, advise Lena it cannot be used, and stop acting if she insists, so as not to mislead the court): A solicitor must not mislead the court or others and must not put forward evidence that the solicitor knows to be false, whatever the client instructs. Lena's admission means the email cannot be disclosed as genuine, and pleading the promise on the strength of it would also mislead. The solicitor should advise Lena of the position and must stop acting if she insists on using it. Reporting her to the police without more would breach confidentiality, which is not removed by the client's dishonesty alone."}, {"id": 1107, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Josh, aged 16, and Liam, aged 19, are jointly charged with an assault and both attend the same firm, Ceri and Co, for advice. Josh tells the solicitor that Liam started the fight and that he joined in only because Liam threatened him. Liam says he and Josh acted together in self-defence. The firm has a good relationship with both families and has acted for each of them on earlier minor matters. Which of the following is the correct approach?", "options": {"A": "The firm cannot act for both because their defences conflict, and may have to stop acting for both if confidential information is relevant", "B": "The firm may act for both if different solicitors handle each case, because a separate team removes the conflict", "C": "The firm may continue to act for Josh alone, because he is the younger client and needs more protection", "D": "The firm may act for both but must disclose each client's account to the other, to keep the position open", "E": "The firm may act for both if each gives informed consent in writing, because consent removes a conflict of interests"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm cannot act for both because their defences conflict, and may have to stop acting for both if confidential information is relevant): Josh's account blames Liam, so their interests are in actual conflict and the firm cannot act for both. Consent does not solve a conflict where the clients' defences are inconsistent, and a separate team within the same firm does not remove it either. Whether the firm can keep either client depends on whether it holds confidential information from one that is material to the other. It must not share one client's account with the other."}, {"id": 1108, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Fraser is seriously injured in a collision caused by a negligent driver and spends six weeks in hospital in pain before he dies from his injuries. He was 52 and earning a good salary. His wife, as his executor, brings a claim on behalf of his estate under the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1934. Which of the following best describes what the estate can recover?", "options": {"A": "The estate can recover damages for his pain and suffering and also for the earnings he would have received to retirement", "B": "The estate can recover damages for his pain and suffering and expenses, but not for earnings lost after death", "C": "The estate can recover only his funeral expenses and bereavement damages, which are fixed by statute and paid to his wife", "D": "The estate can recover nothing, because a personal action for negligence ends when the injured person dies", "E": "The estate can recover damages for loss of expectation of life as a separate head in addition to pain and suffering"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The estate can recover damages for his pain and suffering and expenses, but not for earnings lost after death): The 1934 Act preserves the deceased's own cause of action for the benefit of the estate, so the claim survives death. The estate recovers losses suffered up to death, including pain and suffering and expenses, and funeral costs. It cannot claim for earnings that would have been received after death, and loss of expectation of life no longer counts as a head of damage. Bereavement damages belong to the separate dependants' claim."}, {"id": 1109, "domain": "land_law", "question": "For fourteen years Wen has occasionally parked a caravan and stored ladders on a corner of an unregistered field owned by Aled, who lives abroad. The corner is unfenced, and Wen has never marked it as his own or prevented anyone else from using it. Aled returns and tells Wen to remove his things. Wen claims the corner by adverse possession under the Limitation Act 1980. Which of the following best describes his claim?", "options": {"A": "It succeeds only if Wen has also paid the rates or taxes on the land during the period", "B": "It fails, because adverse possession does not apply to unregistered land and cannot extinguish the owner's paper title however long the use lasts", "C": "It succeeds, because more than twelve years have passed and Aled took no step to stop the use of the corner", "D": "It fails, because occasional use of unfenced land does not show factual possession and intention to exclude the owner", "E": "It succeeds, because Aled's long absence abroad shows that he abandoned the corner to anyone using it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It fails, because occasional use of unfenced land does not show factual possession and intention to exclude the owner): A squatter must show factual possession and an intention to possess, which means acting as an owner would and keeping the owner out. Occasional parking and storage on unfenced land, without any marking or exclusion of others, is trespass rather than possession. The twelve year period under the Limitation Act 1980 therefore never began to run in Wen's favour. The owner's absence does not amount to abandonment, and the Act does apply to unregistered land."}, {"id": 1110, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Hugo resigned as a director of Maple Ltd on 3 March. The company secretary, who is unsure of the rules, asks by what date the company must notify the registrar at Companies House of the end of Hugo's appointment. Maple is a private company, Hugo's resignation letter was delivered to the registered office on 3 March, and the board formally accepted it on 6 March.", "options": {"A": "By 17 March, because notice of a change among directors is due within 14 days of the date the appointment ended", "B": "At the next confirmation statement date, because changes among directors are reported only in that annual filing", "C": "By 3 April, because the company has one month after the change in which to notify the registrar", "D": "By 20 March, because the 14 days run from the board's acceptance of the resignation rather than its delivery", "E": "By 31 March, because notification of a change in directors is allowed 28 days from the change"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (By 17 March, because notice of a change among directors is due within 14 days of the date the appointment ended): Notice of a change in directors must reach the registrar within 14 days of the change. Hugo's appointment ended when his resignation took effect on delivery on 3 March, so the deadline is 17 March. The board's later acceptance does not move the date. Failure to notify is an offence by the company and its officers."}, {"id": 1111, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ms Gill's will, made last year, appoints five of her close friends as executors, and all of them are adults of sound mind who are willing to act. The will contains no provision limiting the number who may prove it. Her solicitor is preparing the application for a grant of probate and asks how many of the five can be named in the grant.", "options": {"A": "All five can take the grant, because the testator's choice of executors binds the court", "B": "All five can take the grant, but only four may sign any transfer of land in the estate", "C": "Only two can take the grant where the will creates a trust, and the court selects which two", "D": "Only one can take the grant, because probate is granted to a single executor chosen by agreement among the executors named in the will", "E": "No more than four can take the grant, because the Senior Courts Act 1981 limits any grant to four persons"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No more than four can take the grant, because the Senior Courts Act 1981 limits any grant to four persons): A grant of probate or administration can be made to a maximum of four persons. The testator's appointment of five does not bind the court, because the limit is statutory. The others are not removed as executors but a power to prove the will can be reserved to them, and they may take the grant later if a place becomes free. Executors do not need to apply singly, and there is no separate limit of two for trusts."}, {"id": 1112, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trust created in 2018 gives income to Ruth for life and the capital to her son Seb, now aged 20, contingently on reaching 30. Seb's presumptive share is worth £200,000. The trustees want to advance £30,000 to Seb to help him buy a flat. The trust instrument does not change section 32 Trustee Act 1925. Ruth has not been asked about the advancement. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The trustees may advance it if they give Ruth written notice and she does not object within twenty eight days", "B": "The trustees may advance it only if Ruth, who has the prior life interest, consents in writing, because section 32 requires her consent", "C": "The trustees may advance it without further consent, because they can advance up to one half of Seb's presumptive share without any person's agreement", "D": "The trustees cannot advance it, because section 32 permits advancement only for education or training and not for housing", "E": "The trustees cannot advance it, because an advancement can be made only to a beneficiary with a vested interest"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The trustees may advance it only if Ruth, who has the prior life interest, consents in writing, because section 32 requires her consent): Section 32 allows trustees to advance capital for the benefit of a person entitled to it, even if the interest is contingent, and the purpose of buying a home is a benefit. For a trust created after 1 October 2014 the whole of the presumptive share may be advanced, but a person with a prior interest must be of full age and consent in writing. Ruth's life interest would be reduced, so her written consent is required. Notice alone is not enough."}, {"id": 1113, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Shabana Iqbal is a solicitor advising a developer on the Property Safety Act 2025. Section 20 provides that 'Schedule 3 (rules on remediation orders) has effect.' Schedule 3 contains detailed procedural rules, including a requirement that notice be given to freeholders 14 days before an order is sought. The developer's finance director says the schedule is an appendix of guidance and can be ignored in favour of the clearer wording of the numbered sections. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The schedule has force once a court has decided that it supplements the sections, which needs a judicial ruling first", "B": "The schedule is part of the Act and has the same legal force as the sections, so the notice requirement must be followed", "C": "The schedule is binding on public bodies but not on private developers, because it deals with the powers of local authorities", "D": "The schedule binds the developer, but the numbered sections prevail if they are inconsistent with it, so the clearer wording governs", "E": "The schedule is a form of guidance that helps readers to understand the sections but binds nobody, so the notice period is optional"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The schedule is part of the Act and has the same legal force as the sections, so the notice requirement must be followed): A schedule forms part of the Act and is given effect by the section that introduces it. It therefore has the same legal force as the numbered sections and must be applied as written. Nothing in the facts suggests any conflict between the schedule and a section. The developer cannot treat the notice period as optional, since the 14 day requirement is an enforceable condition."}, {"id": 1114, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Harriet is the sole trustee of a discretionary trust and, in breach of trust, pays £80,000 of trust money into a company that she controls. Her accountant, Dev, who knows the money belongs to the trust, designs the payment route so that it is hard to trace. Dev does not receive any of the money himself. The beneficiaries wish to sue him for dishonest assistance in a breach of trust. Which of the following best states what must be shown about Dev's state of mind?", "options": {"A": "That his conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people, judged in the light of what he actually knew about the trust money", "B": "That he knew or ought to have known of the breach, so that failing to make proper enquiries is enough to make him liable", "C": "That he intended to defraud the beneficiaries personally, which is tested only against his own moral code and what he privately believed to be honest", "D": "That Harriet herself acted dishonestly, because an assistant cannot be liable unless the trustee who committed the breach was also dishonest", "E": "That he received the trust money for his own benefit and that it would be unconscionable for him to retain it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (That his conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people, judged in the light of what he actually knew about the trust money): Dishonest assistance requires a breach of trust, assistance in it, and dishonesty on the part of the assistant. Dishonesty is judged by finding the defendant's actual knowledge or belief and then asking whether his conduct was dishonest by the objective standards of ordinary decent people. Receipt is not needed, which is why the knowing receipt formulation fails here, and negligence in not enquiring is not enough. The trustee's own state of mind is also irrelevant, because the breach need not have been dishonest."}, {"id": 1115, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "You act for the trustees of a fixed trust of £300,000. Three adult beneficiaries together hold all the beneficial interests, and they ask you to prepare a direction telling the trustees to end the trust and pay out the fund. You then learn from a family member that one beneficiary, Wes, has dementia and cannot understand the arrangement, although his signature has been obtained. The trustees are keen to finish before the end of the tax year. Which of the following is the most appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Decline to act further for anyone, because the information about Wes means the firm must stop acting for the trustees", "B": "Not rely on Wes's signature, and advise that his consent must come from someone authorised or the Court of Protection", "C": "Proceed after getting a statement from a family member that Wes understood the direction when he signed", "D": "Proceed, because the trustees can rely on a signed direction from every beneficiary without further enquiry", "E": "Proceed, but record Wes's lack of capacity in a file note, because the other two beneficiaries hold a majority"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Not rely on Wes's signature, and advise that his consent must come from someone authorised or the Court of Protection): The rule in Saunders v Vautier needs all beneficiaries to be of full capacity, so a signature from someone who lacks capacity does not bind him. Having learned of the problem, the solicitor cannot ignore it or rely on a bare signature, because proceeding would risk a breach of trust by the trustees. Valid consent has to come through someone with authority to act for Wes or through the Court of Protection. A majority is not enough, and withdrawing from the retainer is unnecessary when the defect can be put right."}, {"id": 1116, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Hartfield Bakeries Ltd, a UK trading company with profits well below £1.5 million and no associated companies, prepares accounts for the twelve months ending 31 March 2026. Its corporation tax liability for that period is £42,000. The finance director has asked the company's solicitor when payment of that tax is due. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Payment is due in four quarterly instalments, the first falling in the seventh month of the accounting period", "B": "Payment is due nine months and one day after the end of the accounting period, which is 1 January 2027", "C": "Payment is due on 31 December 2026, nine months after the end of the accounting period", "D": "Payment is due on 31 January 2027, aligned with the self assessment date for individuals who file returns", "E": "Payment is due three months after the accounts are filed at Companies House for the accounting period"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Payment is due nine months and one day after the end of the accounting period, which is 1 January 2027): A company that is not large pays corporation tax nine months and one day after the end of its accounting period. For a period ending 31 March 2026 that is 1 January 2027. Quarterly instalments apply only to large companies, those with profits above £1.5 million, and individual self assessment dates are irrelevant to a company. Filing of accounts is a separate deadline, and the nine months run from the end of the period and not from filing."}, {"id": 1117, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Alison Reed is a solicitor attending the first hearing in the magistrates' court of Roy Dunn and Sam Ellis, who are jointly charged with burglary. Roy tells her privately that he intends to plead guilty and to give evidence against Sam. Sam, who intends to plead not guilty, has asked Alison to act for him as well, and neither man knows what the other has said. What should Alison do?", "options": {"A": "Act for Sam alone and use what Roy said in cross-examining him, because that information is no longer confidential", "B": "Continue for both with their written informed consent, because the conflict relates only to the plea and not to liability for the offence", "C": "Continue for both after telling Sam of Roy's plan, because their shared interest in the same charge takes priority", "D": "Stop acting for at least one of them and keep Roy's intentions confidential, because their interests now clearly conflict", "E": "Tell the court of Roy's plan and then withdraw, because the duty to the court outweighs the duty of confidentiality"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stop acting for at least one of them and keep Roy's intentions confidential, because their interests now clearly conflict): Roy's intention to give evidence against Sam creates a conflict of interests between two clients in the same matter, so Alison cannot continue to act for both. She owes continuing duties of confidentiality to Roy and cannot pass his information to Sam or use it against him, which is why consent cannot cure the problem. She also cannot tell the court, as no duty to the court requires it. She must stop acting for at least one of them and may have to stop acting for both."}, {"id": 1118, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Nadia Rahman is the sole registered proprietor of a house in Leeds, which she holds on trust for herself and her former partner, Ewan, who contributed to the deposit and has a one third beneficial share. Ewan still lives in the house, but his name is not on the title and he has asked for no entry on the register. Nadia sells to a buyer, who pays the whole price to Nadia alone and has no notice of Ewan's rights. Which of the following is the best analysis of Ewan's position?", "options": {"A": "Ewan's share is overreached because Nadia is a trustee and trustees may give a valid receipt for capital money alone", "B": "Ewan's share is not overreached because the money went to one trustee, so his occupation makes it bind the buyer", "C": "Ewan's share binds the buyer only if he can show that Nadia sold in breach of her duty as a trustee", "D": "Ewan's share does not bind the buyer, because it was not protected by a notice or restriction on the register", "E": "Ewan's share is overreached because the buyer paid full value and was unaware of his rights when completing the purchase"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ewan's share is not overreached because the money went to one trustee, so his occupation makes it bind the buyer): Overreaching requires the purchase money to be paid to at least two trustees or a trust corporation. Here Nadia is a sole trustee, so Ewan's beneficial interest is not overreached. Because Ewan remains in actual occupation, his interest takes effect as an overriding interest under Schedule 3 paragraph 2 to the Land Registration Act 2002, and the buyer's lack of notice does not help. A failure to register a protective entry would matter only if he were not in occupation."}, {"id": 1119, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Two sisters, Eleri and Cerys Bowen, bought a seaside cottage as joint tenants to run as a holiday letting business. The business has now closed, and Eleri wants to sell. Cerys wants to keep the cottage for family holidays and says the original purpose is still being pursued. The cottage is unmortgaged and no child lives there. Eleri applies for an order for sale. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The court will order Cerys to buy Eleri's share at market value, because a co-owner may require a buyout in place of sale", "B": "The court will refuse sale, because both co-owners must agree before a jointly owned property can be sold", "C": "The court is likely to order sale, because the trust's original purpose of running a holiday letting business has ended and no minor occupies the property", "D": "The court must order sale, because joint tenants hold on trust for sale and the court has no discretion to refuse", "E": "The court will refuse sale, because Cerys's wish to keep the property is a factor that outweighs the others"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court is likely to order sale, because the trust's original purpose of running a holiday letting business has ended and no minor occupies the property): On an application under section 14 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 the court considers the section 15 factors, including the purposes for which the property is held. Where the original commercial purpose has ended and no child lives there, that factor supports sale, and Cerys's preference for continued family use does not outweigh the other co-owner's wish to realise her share. The court has a discretion and is not bound to order sale, and it cannot compel one co-owner to buy out the other. The consent of both owners is not needed."}, {"id": 1120, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Fiona Yeo, a wealthy widow, is considering two lifetime gifts: £60,000 in cash outright to her adult son, and £60,000 to the trustees of a discretionary trust for her grandchildren. She has made no previous gifts and has used none of her nil rate band. She is in good health but wants to understand the Inheritance Tax position of each gift at the date of the gift and if she dies within seven years. Which of the following is a correct statement?", "options": {"A": "Both gifts are chargeable lifetime transfers taxed at the lifetime rate at the date of the gift, because neither is made to a spouse", "B": "The gift to her son is a chargeable lifetime transfer taxed at once, while the trust gift is a potentially exempt transfer", "C": "The gift to her son is a potentially exempt transfer, exempt if she survives seven years, while the trust gift is a chargeable lifetime transfer", "D": "Both gifts are potentially exempt transfers that become exempt after seven years, because any lifetime gift to an individual or a trust is treated alike", "E": "The gift to her son is exempt immediately because it is to an individual, while the trust gift is a potentially exempt transfer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The gift to her son is a potentially exempt transfer, exempt if she survives seven years, while the trust gift is a chargeable lifetime transfer): An outright gift to an individual is a potentially exempt transfer, with no tax at the time and full exemption if the donor survives seven years. A gift to a discretionary trust is a chargeable lifetime transfer, taxed at once at the lifetime rate on any excess over the nil rate band. Fiona's £60,000 falls within her nil rate band, so no lifetime tax is due on the trust gift, but it is still chargeable and uses up part of the band."}, {"id": 1121, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Brindle Ltd is a private company with 100,000 shares carrying one vote each. The directors want to pass an ordinary resolution at a general meeting to be held in seven days. Members holding 92,000 shares have each said in writing that they agree to the shorter notice. Another member holding 8,000 shares objects. May the meeting go ahead on seven days' notice?", "options": {"A": "No, because the notice required for any general meeting is at least 21 days, and short notice is not permitted", "B": "Yes, but only if members holding at least 95% in nominal value agree, as is required for a public company's meeting", "C": "No, because every member entitled to attend and vote must agree to short notice, so the objection of the minority member prevents the meeting going ahead", "D": "Yes, because only a simple majority of the voting rights is needed to agree short notice for an ordinary resolution", "E": "Yes, because a private company's general meeting may be called on short notice if members holding at least 90% in nominal value agree"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, because a private company's general meeting may be called on short notice if members holding at least 90% in nominal value agree): Short notice of a general meeting of a private company is valid if members holding at least 90% in nominal value of the shares giving the right to attend and vote agree. Here 92% have agreed, so the objection of the minority does not stop the meeting. A simple majority is not enough, and unanimity is not required. The 95% figure applies to public companies."}, {"id": 1122, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "In June 2026 your client Nasir asks for advice. In January 2026 a pressure cooker exploded in his kitchen and he suffered burns. The cooker was manufactured by Tolland Appliances Ltd, which first put it into circulation in March 2015. Nasir did not previously consult a solicitor and has heard that claims for defective products must be brought within three years. He wants to bring a claim for his injuries. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Advise that the claim must be brought against the retailer, because liability for a defective product lies only with the supplier after ten years", "B": "Advise that the Consumer Protection Act claim is in time, because the three-year period runs from the injury and the long-stop does not affect it", "C": "Advise that no claim is possible, because a product that has been in use for over ten years is presumed to have been misused", "D": "Advise that a Consumer Protection Act claim is barred by the ten-year long-stop, but negligence remains available within three years of injury", "E": "Advise that both claims are barred, because the ten-year period from supply applies to every product claim whatever its legal basis"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Advise that a Consumer Protection Act claim is barred by the ten-year long-stop, but negligence remains available within three years of injury): The Consumer Protection Act 1987 extinguishes the right to claim ten years after the producer put the product into circulation, whether or not the three-year limitation period is still running. March 2015 plus ten years has passed, so that route is closed. A claim in negligence for personal injury is not subject to the ten-year long-stop and is governed by the three-year period from the date of injury or knowledge, so it remains open. The solicitor owes a duty of competence and must advise the client of every route open, so omitting the negligence claim would fail Nasir. Switching to the retailer does not help, as the long-stop applies to the statutory claim itself."}, {"id": 1123, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A 25-year lease of a warehouse contains a tenant's covenant 'not to assign the term of this lease'. After eight years the tenant, Doran Ltd, wishes to sell its business and asks the landlord to consent to an assignment to a buyer of sound financial standing. The landlord refuses, giving no reason. Doran Ltd says the refusal is unreasonable. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The refusal is unreasonable, because the Landlord and Tenant Act 1988 requires the landlord to give reasons and a decision within a reasonable time", "B": "The refusal is unreasonable, because section 19 of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927 qualifies every covenant against assignment and implies a duty to act reasonably", "C": "The landlord may refuse for any reason, because the covenant is absolute and the statutory qualification applies only to covenants requiring consent", "D": "The refusal is lawful only if the landlord can show that the proposed assignee is a less suitable tenant than Doran Ltd", "E": "The landlord must consent, because a tenant with more than seven years of the term left is entitled to assign"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The landlord may refuse for any reason, because the covenant is absolute and the statutory qualification applies only to covenants requiring consent): A covenant not to assign at all is an absolute covenant. Section 19(1)(a) of the 1927 Act implies that consent is not to be unreasonably withheld only where the covenant is against assigning without consent, so it does not soften an absolute bar. The 1988 Act duties also relate to applications under a covenant requiring consent. Doran Ltd therefore has no ground to challenge the refusal, however sound the buyer."}, {"id": 1124, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Donald Fraser died on 5 January 2025. His will gave a pecuniary legacy of £20,000 to his niece, Isla, and the residue to his son. The will does not say when the legacy is to be paid or provide for interest. It is now March 2026. The executors have been slow in selling Donald's property, though there is cash in the estate to pay the legacy now, and they have not yet paid it. Isla has asked whether she is owed interest. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Interest is payable only from the date Isla makes a written demand, because a legatee must formally ask before interest can run", "B": "Interest is payable from the first anniversary of death, because the executor's year has ended, at the rate the court applies to legacies", "C": "Interest is not payable, because the executors' delay was caused by the sale of the property and not by any default on their part", "D": "Interest is payable from the date the grant was issued, because from that day the executors can lawfully pay legacies so that interest starts from that later date", "E": "Interest is payable from the date of death, because a legacy is a debt owed to the legatee from that day"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interest is payable from the first anniversary of death, because the executor's year has ended, at the rate the court applies to legacies): A pecuniary legacy carries interest from the first anniversary of death, the end of the executor's year, unless the will provides otherwise, and this applies whether or not the delay was the executors' fault. Donald died in January 2025, so interest has been running since January 2026. It does not run from death or from the grant, and no demand by the legatee is needed."}, {"id": 1125, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Naomi Quigley is charged with theft from her employer. During interview at the police station, with her solicitor present, the officer said, 'If you admit it now, I will make sure you get bail and the matter will go no further.' Naomi then admitted the theft. At trial, her counsel seeks to exclude the confession under section 76 PACE. Naomi's trainee solicitor says that Naomi, who is anxious, must prove the confession was unreliable. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Naomi must prove on the balance of probabilities that the inducement made her confession unreliable, because she is asserting that it should be excluded", "B": "The confession is excluded only if it is shown to be false, because truth is the only test of reliability", "C": "Once the issue is raised, the prosecution must prove beyond reasonable doubt that the confession was not obtained in unreliable circumstances", "D": "The judge must exclude the confession automatically, because any inducement by an officer amounts to oppression under the section", "E": "The confession is admissible, because the presence of a solicitor at the interview cures any inducement offered by the officer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Once the issue is raised, the prosecution must prove beyond reasonable doubt that the confession was not obtained in unreliable circumstances): Section 76(2)(b) requires exclusion where anything said or done was likely, in the circumstances, to make any confession unreliable. Once the defence raises the point, the prosecution carries the burden of disproving it beyond reasonable doubt. An offer of bail and no further action is a classic inducement. A solicitor's presence does not remove it, and the test is the likely effect on reliability, not the truth of the confession."}, {"id": 1126, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mrs Teague, a widow aged 79, made a will leaving most of her estate to her son Ben, who lives with her, drives her to appointments and often urged her to change her earlier will. Her daughter Carys, now largely excluded, alleges undue influence and relies on the close relationship between Ben and his mother. Which statement correctly describes what Carys must establish?", "options": {"A": "She must show that Ben took a substantial benefit under the will, because that shifts the burden to him to disprove that he influenced her decision", "B": "She must show that Ben persuaded or urged Mrs Teague, because persuasion that results in a benefit is sufficient", "C": "She must prove that Mrs Teague lacked capacity when signing, because undue influence is a species of incapacity", "D": "She must prove that Ben actually coerced Mrs Teague, overpowering her will, because no presumption of undue influence arises for a will", "E": "She need prove only the relationship of trust and confidence, because that raises a presumption which Ben must then rebut"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She must prove that Ben actually coerced Mrs Teague, overpowering her will, because no presumption of undue influence arises for a will): In probate, undue influence means coercion that overpowers the testator's free will, and the person alleging it must prove it. The presumption of undue influence applying to lifetime gifts between people in a relationship of trust does not apply to wills. Persuasion, argument and appeals to affection fall short, so Ben's urging and the closeness of the relationship are not enough on their own. Capacity is a separate ground of challenge."}, {"id": 1127, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Sasha Novak and Dan Whitfield are buying a house together. Sasha is contributing £90,000 from an inheritance and Dan £20,000. They tell the solicitor they are happy to be joint legal owners and do not wish to discuss beneficial shares. Sasha then tells the solicitor privately that she wants her larger contribution recognised if the relationship ends, and asks that Dan is not told. Both ask the solicitor to act for them. What is the most appropriate course of action?", "options": {"A": "Decline to act for both, because Sasha's wish conflicts with Dan's position and cannot be shared with him", "B": "Act for both and record equal beneficial shares, because joint legal ownership means the law presumes equal beneficial ownership", "C": "Act for Dan alone and decline Sasha, because he is the client with the smaller contribution and needs more advice", "D": "Act for both and keep Sasha's wish confidential from Dan, because confidentiality to each client outweighs the duty to disclose", "E": "Act for both after obtaining their written consent, since consent from each client removes any conflict between them on the shares"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to act for both, because Sasha's wish conflicts with Dan's position and cannot be shared with him): A solicitor cannot act where there is a conflict of interest between clients unless a limited exception applies, and one such exception requires that the clients' common interest is substantial and that information can be shared openly. Sasha's wish to keep her position from Dan means the solicitor would either breach confidentiality to her or the duty to disclose to Dan, so the exception is unavailable. Consent does not cure that. The solicitor should explain the position and suggest separate advice for each of them."}, {"id": 1128, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Community Housing Act 2026 received Royal Assent on 1 March. Section 12 provides that the Act's provisions 'come into force on such day as the Secretary of State may by statutory instrument appoint'. No statutory instrument has yet been made. On 10 April, Ravi Menon, a landlord, tells you he is planning to follow a new procedure in section 4 for ending tenancies and asks whether he can rely on it now. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "He can rely on it now, because an Act takes effect on the day of Royal Assent unless each individual section says otherwise in express terms", "B": "He can rely on it after both Houses have voted to approve the commencement regulations, because Parliament must confirm the date", "C": "He can rely on it from the start of 1 April, because provisions take effect one month after Royal Assent unless a different date is appointed", "D": "He can rely on it once the Act has been published on legislation.gov.uk, because publication brings an Act into force", "E": "He cannot rely on it yet, because the section is not in force until a commencement order is made, even though the Act has Royal Assent"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (He cannot rely on it yet, because the section is not in force until a commencement order is made, even though the Act has Royal Assent): Where an Act provides that it comes into force on a day appointed by the Secretary of State, the provisions have no legal effect until a commencement order is made. The default rule that an Act takes effect at the start of the day of Royal Assent applies where the Act is silent. Here the Act is not silent, so neither Royal Assent nor publication switches on the new procedure. Ravi should wait for the order, or a notice served under the new procedure may be invalid."}, {"id": 1129, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Larkfield Developments Ltd engaged Penhale Builders Ltd to refurbish offices for £100,000, with a delay penalty of £2,000 a week. Partway through, Penhale told Larkfield that it had underpriced the job and would struggle to finish on time. Larkfield, to avoid delay claims from its own tenant, promised an extra £10,000 if Penhale completed by the agreed date. Penhale did so. There was no pressure or threat by Penhale. Larkfield now refuses to pay the extra sum. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Larkfield need not pay, because a variation increasing the price is enforceable only if made by deed", "B": "Larkfield must pay, because a promise to pay more is binding once the other party relies on it, even without any benefit", "C": "Larkfield must pay, because it obtained a practical benefit from the promise and there was no duress or fraud", "D": "Larkfield must pay only if Penhale carried out additional work beyond the original specification, because that is new consideration", "E": "Larkfield need not pay, because Penhale was already bound to finish the work and so gave no fresh consideration"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Larkfield must pay, because it obtained a practical benefit from the promise and there was no duress or fraud): Where a party promises extra payment to secure performance of an existing obligation, the promise is supported by consideration if the promisor obtains a practical benefit and there was no duress or fraud. Larkfield avoided delay claims by its tenant, which is such a benefit. The pre-existing duty rule is the tempting objection, but it gives way in these circumstances. A deed is not needed because consideration is present."}, {"id": 1130, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Jessop Motors Ltd sold a car to Amara Obi, stating that it had covered only 20,000 miles. The mileage had in fact been wound back and the true figure was 60,000. Jessop's sales manager had read the service record that showed the true figure but had overlooked it. Amara bought the car for £12,000 and claims damages under section 2(1) of the Misrepresentation Act 1967. She does not allege that anyone at Jessop acted dishonestly. Which of the following best describes what Jessop must show to defeat the claim?", "options": {"A": "That it was not dishonest, because the statutory claim succeeds where the representor acted fraudulently", "B": "That Amara could have discovered the true mileage by inspecting the service record before buying, because contributory fault bars the claim", "C": "That the statement was not a term of the contract, because section 2(1) applies only to statements incorporated into the written agreement of sale", "D": "That the loss was foreseeable at the time of contract, because section 2(1) damages are limited by the remoteness rule in contract", "E": "That it had reasonable grounds to believe, and did believe up to the time of contract, that the statement was true"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (That it had reasonable grounds to believe, and did believe up to the time of contract, that the statement was true): Section 2(1) places the burden on the representor, who escapes liability only by proving that it had reasonable grounds to believe, and did believe up to the time the contract was made, that the facts represented were true. The claimant need not prove negligence or dishonesty. Misrepresentations that become terms are not outside the section, so that argument fails. Damages follow the tort measure for fraud rather than the contractual foreseeability limit, so the remoteness point does not help Jessop."}, {"id": 1131, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Employment Rights Act gives workers a statutory right to take part in a collective redundancy consultation scheme. A government minister announces that, by exercising the royal prerogative, the government will treat the statutory right as abolished with immediate effect, without introducing a Bill. Fatima Haider, a union member, asks whether ministers may use the prerogative to take away rights given by Parliament. Which of the following is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "Yes, provided the minister first obtains the approval of the Privy Council, because that body can authorise changes to statute", "B": "No, because the prerogative extends to foreign affairs but cannot affect any matter within the United Kingdom, including the rights of workers", "C": "No, because ministers cannot use prerogative powers to remove or change rights created by an Act of Parliament", "D": "Yes, because the prerogative is a source of Crown power that is superior to any statute passed by Parliament", "E": "Yes, because the minister acts for the monarch and the monarch may suspend the operation of statutes"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because ministers cannot use prerogative powers to remove or change rights created by an Act of Parliament): Under the constitutional settlement following the Bill of Rights 1689, the Crown cannot use prerogative powers to dispense with or suspend statute or to change rights that Parliament has conferred. Only Parliament can change those rights by legislation. The prerogative remains a real source of power in areas such as foreign affairs, but it cannot be used to override an Act. Privy Council approval does not alter this."}, {"id": 1132, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Brindle Marketing Ltd's latest accounts show distributable reserves of £12,000. The sole shareholder and director, Mr Osei, tells his solicitor that he wants the company to pay him a £30,000 dividend now because he needs funds for a house purchase, and he will lend the money back to the company later if needed. The company is trading at a loss this year. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "A dividend may be paid only from distributable profits, so a larger payment is unlawful and repayable by a shareholder who knew, and he should not proceed", "B": "He may pay a £12,000 dividend and treat the other £18,000 as a loan to himself, repayable on demand and needing no further approvals", "C": "He may pay the full £30,000 as sole shareholder, because his own later ratification of the payment would cure any shortfall in the company's reserves", "D": "The dividend is lawful if funded by an overdraft, because a distribution depends on the company's cash position and not on its reserves", "E": "The dividend is lawful provided the board passes a minute authorising it and a dividend voucher is issued to him as the sole shareholder"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A dividend may be paid only from distributable profits, so a larger payment is unlawful and repayable by a shareholder who knew, and he should not proceed): A company may make a distribution only out of profits available for the purpose, so a £30,000 dividend against £12,000 of reserves would be an unlawful distribution. A shareholder who knows or has reasonable grounds to know of the shortfall must repay it, and the directors may also face liability, so the solicitor should advise against proceeding. A minute and voucher do not make a dividend lawful, and ratification by the same member does not cure the breach. Lending money to himself raises its own approval and tax issues and would not be an easy workaround."}, {"id": 1133, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Three friends, Pearl, Reuben and Ines, own a house as beneficial joint tenants. Without telling the others, Pearl sells and transfers her beneficial interest in the house to Gus, a stranger, in return for £60,000. Pearl then dies. Reuben and Ines say that Pearl's death means that the joint tenancy continues between all of them and Gus's purchase is of no effect. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Pearl's transfer severed her share, so Gus holds one third in common and Reuben and Ines remain joint tenants of the rest", "B": "The transfer is ineffective unless Reuben and Ines consented, because joint tenants must act together to dispose of any share", "C": "Gus holds one third as tenant in common, and Reuben and Ines hold the remaining two thirds as tenants in common", "D": "Pearl's death ends the joint tenancy of her share and the survivors take the whole house, so Gus acquires nothing", "E": "Gus becomes a joint tenant with Reuben and Ines, because a buyer takes the same kind of interest that the seller held"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pearl's transfer severed her share, so Gus holds one third in common and Reuben and Ines remain joint tenants of the rest): A disposition of a joint tenant's beneficial interest to a third party severs the joint tenancy as to that share, because it destroys the unities of title and time, and the purchaser holds as a tenant in common. The other two remain joint tenants between themselves as to their two thirds, since nothing has acted on their shares. Pearl's later death does not revive survivorship over the share she has already sold. The consent of the others is not required."}, {"id": 1134, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Sasha Mahon died on 1 April 2026. Her executors, Cyrus and Dee, sold a portfolio of shares in September 2026 for £62,000. The shares had a probate value of £50,000, and Sasha had bought them for £20,000 many years earlier. The estate has enough funds to pay any tax due. The executors ask the solicitor how the sale is treated for Capital Gains Tax and who is liable. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The executors are liable on a gain of £42,000, being the proceeds less Sasha's original cost, because her base cost passes to the executors", "B": "The executors are liable on a gain of £12,000, proceeds less probate value, and can use the annual exempt amount for the year of death and next two", "C": "The residuary beneficiaries are liable personally on the gain of £12,000 in the tax year in which the estate is wound up", "D": "The executors are liable on a gain of £12,000 but have no annual exempt amount, because an estate is not an individual", "E": "No Capital Gains Tax is payable, because assets are not chargeable to the tax while they are held in the estate during administration"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The executors are liable on a gain of £12,000, proceeds less probate value, and can use the annual exempt amount for the year of death and next two): Personal representatives acquire the assets at their probate value, so the gain on a later sale is measured from that value and not from the deceased's original cost, and the death itself produces no charge. The representatives are liable for the tax, and they have an annual exempt amount in the tax year of death and the following two years. The sale in September 2026 falls within that period."}, {"id": 1135, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Vic intends to kill his former business partner Wes. He loads a shotgun, drives to Wes's house, rings the bell and, when Wes opens the door, points the gun at his chest. Before Vic can pull the trigger, Wes slams the door shut and calls the police. Vic is charged with attempted murder. His solicitor asks whether the acts were more than merely preparatory. Which of the following is the most accurate advice?", "options": {"A": "A jury may find the acts more than merely preparatory, because Vic had embarked on the crime proper though he had not yet pulled the trigger", "B": "The acts were merely preparatory, because Vic had not yet performed the final act he thought necessary, which was pulling the trigger on the shotgun", "C": "The acts were merely preparatory, because an attempt is committed only once the defendant has passed the point of no return", "D": "The acts were more than merely preparatory only because Wes saw the gun, since an attempt requires the victim to be placed in fear", "E": "The acts are more than merely preparatory as a matter of law, because the judge must direct the jury to convict once a gun is pointed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A jury may find the acts more than merely preparatory, because Vic had embarked on the crime proper though he had not yet pulled the trigger): The question of whether acts are more than merely preparatory is one for the jury, and the test is whether the defendant had embarked on the crime proper. In R v Jones a defendant who pointed a loaded gun at the victim was held to have done enough even though the trigger was not pulled. There is no requirement of a last act or of a point of no return. The victim's reaction is irrelevant to whether an attempt was made."}, {"id": 1136, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trust fund of £90,000 is held by trustees 'for Ben if he reaches 25'. Ben is now 19. There is no direction about income and no gift over. Ben is healthy and lives with his parents. Ben's mother says Ben is entitled to the capital now because the trust is for him alone. Which of the following correctly states Ben's position?", "options": {"A": "Ben has a vested interest in remainder, which entitles him to demand the capital once he reaches 21", "B": "Ben has a contingent interest that vests only on reaching 25, so he cannot call for the capital now", "C": "Ben has a vested interest in possession, because the trust names him as the only beneficiary of the fund", "D": "Ben has a contingent interest, and he can call for the capital as soon as he is 18 with his parents' agreement", "E": "Ben has no interest yet, because he holds a mere expectancy until the trustees decide to distribute"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ben has a contingent interest that vests only on reaching 25, so he cannot call for the capital now): An interest that depends on a condition not yet satisfied, here reaching 25, is contingent and does not vest until the condition is met. Until then Ben cannot demand the capital, and being the only named beneficiary changes nothing. Reaching 18 or 21 does not alter the age fixed by the settlor. Ben's interest is more than a mere hope, because the trustees must hold the fund for him if he meets the condition."}, {"id": 1137, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Omar, a solicitor, helped a trustee to misapply £120,000 of trust money by arranging for it to be paid through a client account to a third party, knowing the payment was a breach of trust. Omar received none of the money. The trustee is now bankrupt, but Omar is solvent and holds a large professional indemnity policy. The beneficiaries want to know how Omar's liability works. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Omar's liability is proprietary, so the beneficiaries can claim assets that he holds and rank ahead of his own unsecured creditors", "B": "Omar is liable only for a share of the loss proportionate to his own part in it, as the court fixes under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978", "C": "Omar is personally liable for equitable compensation, jointly and severally with the trustee, so the whole loss is recoverable from him", "D": "Omar holds only what he received on constructive trust, so he has no liability because he received nothing from the misapplied funds", "E": "Omar is liable only if the beneficiaries first show that the trust fund cannot be restored in any other way, including from the trustee"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Omar is personally liable for equitable compensation, jointly and severally with the trustee, so the whole loss is recoverable from him): A dishonest assistant is liable in equity as an accessory, and the liability is personal, not proprietary, because he holds nothing of the trust. He is liable jointly and severally with the trustee, so the claimants may recover everything from the solvent defendant and leave him to seek contribution. Receipt is not required, so the first suggestion fails. Nothing obliges the beneficiaries to pursue the bankrupt trustee first."}, {"id": 1138, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Gareth bought a house on 1 January 2016 for £300,000 and lived in it as his only home until 31 December 2019, when he moved in with his partner in another city and let the house. He never returned. He sold it on 31 December 2025, making a gain of £120,000 after costs. For this question, ignore the annual exempt amount and lettings relief, and assume the final period of ownership qualifying for private residence relief is nine months. Which of the following is Gareth's chargeable gain?", "options": {"A": "Nil, because the house was his only residence for part of the time and a home is wholly exempt on sale", "B": "£63,000, because relief covers the four years of occupation plus the final nine months of ownership", "C": "£111,000, because relief covers only the final nine months of ownership, as he left the house before selling", "D": "£27,000, because relief also covers the three years after he moved out as a period of deemed occupation", "E": "£72,000, because relief covers only the four years of actual occupation and the final period is ignored"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (£63,000, because relief covers the four years of occupation plus the final nine months of ownership): Private residence relief covers the periods of actual occupation and the final nine months of ownership. Gareth owned the house for 120 months and occupied it for 48, so 57 months qualify and 63 do not, which is 52.5% of the gain. The chargeable gain is therefore £63,000. The three years of deemed occupation for any absence does not help because it requires the owner to live in the home again afterwards, and he never did."}, {"id": 1139, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Rashid Hussain is to be tried in the Crown Court for burglary. Police say that a nearby shop's CCTV was viewed but not retained, yet Rashid's solicitor believes that footage is held and that it might support his account. The prosecution's schedule of unused material lists no footage. The Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing has passed and the defence statement has not been served. A colleague proposes an immediate application under section 8 of the Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996. What is the most appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Ask the judge for a public interest immunity ruling, because the footage might otherwise be withheld from the defence by the police", "B": "Serve a defence statement setting out only the defence Rashid has instructed, then apply under section 8 if the prosecution still withholds the footage", "C": "Write informally to the investigating officer and wait, because a request to the police is the proper route before the court is asked", "D": "Apply under section 8 now, because the court can order disclosure of any material that the defence reasonably believes is held by the police or prosecution", "E": "Wait until trial and cross-examine the officers, because the right to use unused material arises only once evidence is called"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Serve a defence statement setting out only the defence Rashid has instructed, then apply under section 8 if the prosecution still withholds the footage): A section 8 application can be made only after the defence statement has been served and the prosecution has responded or failed to respond. The solicitor must therefore serve a statement that accurately sets out the defence as instructed, without adding a case the client has not given. If the prosecution still withholds the footage, the application can be made. Waiting for trial would deprive Rashid of the chance to use the material, and public interest immunity is a prosecution application, not a defence one."}, {"id": 1140, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Sam has occupied a strip of garden beside his house for eleven years, treating it as his own by fencing it and planting a hedge, without permission from Olga, the registered proprietor of the adjoining land. Olga was unaware of this. Sam applies to HM Land Registry to be registered as proprietor of the strip, and the registrar notifies Olga, who serves a counter-notice objecting to the application. None of the statutory exceptions applies to Sam. Which of the following best states the outcome?", "options": {"A": "The application will be rejected, because once the owner objects the squatter must prove one of three statutory conditions and Sam cannot", "B": "Sam will be registered, because ten years' adverse possession entitles him to be registered despite any objection by Olga as owner", "C": "Sam will be registered, because Olga cannot defeat the claim unless she recovers possession of the strip within 65 days", "D": "The application will be rejected and Sam may reapply at once, because the ten-year period starts afresh from the date of the rejection", "E": "The application will be rejected, because twelve years' possession, not ten, is needed in registered land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The application will be rejected, because once the owner objects the squatter must prove one of three statutory conditions and Sam cannot): Under Schedule 6 to the Land Registration Act 2002 a squatter with ten years' adverse possession may apply, and the owner is notified. If the owner objects by counter-notice, the application fails unless the squatter proves one of three conditions: an estoppel, some other entitlement to the land, or a reasonably mistaken belief about the boundary. Sam can show none of these, so he is not registered. He cannot reapply at once, since a later application requires a further two years of possession after rejection. Twelve years is the unregistered land period."}, {"id": 1141, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Kwame Mensah and his lender on a standard residential purchase. While reviewing his documents you notice that the income stated on his mortgage application is much higher than the figure on the payslips he has just given you. Kwame says the payslips are old and the application is right. He does not want the lender told. What is the correct course of action?", "options": {"A": "Complete the purchase and report the discrepancy to the lender immediately afterwards, because the loan has been agreed", "B": "Stop acting for the lender but continue for Kwame, telling the lender that you cannot say why you have withdrawn", "C": "Tell Kwame the lender must be informed, and if he refuses, cease to act for both because you cannot keep the information from the lender", "D": "Continue acting for Kwame and the lender, keeping the information confidential from the lender because it was learned from the client", "E": "Ask Kwame to sign a statement that his income is correct and rely on it, because you may rely on the client's word"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell Kwame the lender must be informed, and if he refuses, cease to act for both because you cannot keep the information from the lender): Information that bears on the lender's decision to lend must be shared with the lender, and a joint retainer cannot continue if the borrower will not allow that. The solicitor should explain the position to Kwame and, if he refuses, stop acting for both clients. Keeping the information back, or completing and reporting later, would mislead the lender. A signed statement from the client does not remove the apparent discrepancy in his own documents."}, {"id": 1142, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Oluwaseun Adeyemi, aged 34, appears before the magistrates' court charged with theft of power tools worth £4,200 from his former employer, an either-way offence. He indicates a not guilty plea. The prosecutor outlines the facts and the magistrates, after hearing representations from both sides and considering the allocation guidelines, decide that their sentencing powers are sufficient and that the case is suitable for summary trial. Oluwaseun asks his solicitor whether that decision ends the matter. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "He may elect Crown Court trial only if the magistrates certify that his case involves complex questions of law or fact", "B": "The prosecution may insist on Crown Court trial, because the Crown Prosecution Service can overrule a magistrates' decision on mode of trial", "C": "He may still elect Crown Court trial, because the magistrates must explain that right and ask whether he consents to summary trial", "D": "He may change venue only after conviction, because the right to elect arises when the court considers its sentencing powers", "E": "The decision binds him, because the magistrates alone decide venue once they accept jurisdiction after hearing the prosecution facts"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He may still elect Crown Court trial, because the magistrates must explain that right and ask whether he consents to summary trial): Once magistrates accept jurisdiction, the defendant is told the court considers the case suitable for summary trial and is asked to choose between summary trial and trial on indictment. The right to elect belongs to Oluwaseun alone, and the prosecution has no power to overrule or insist on a venue. Committal for sentence is a separate step that arises only after conviction. The magistrates' acceptance of jurisdiction therefore starts the choice rather than ending it."}, {"id": 1143, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Ben, aged 16, ordered a smart suit costing £480 from Larkin Tailors for a job interview at a bank, and wore it to the interview. He paid nothing, and he later said he did not want to pay because he is a minor. The suit was well made and of a standard suited to his position and actual needs. Which of the following best describes Larkin Tailors' position?", "options": {"A": "Ben must pay a reasonable price for the suit if it is a necessary suited to his condition in life and actual needs", "B": "Ben need not pay anything, because all contracts made with a person under eighteen are void against that person", "C": "Ben must pay the full £480 agreed, because a minor who obtains a benefit under a contract is bound by the price he agreed", "D": "Ben is bound to pay only if he affirms the contract within a reasonable time after reaching eighteen", "E": "Ben is bound only if his parents stood surety for payment, because a sale on credit to a minor takes effect only with adult support"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ben must pay a reasonable price for the suit if it is a necessary suited to his condition in life and actual needs): A minor is liable to pay a reasonable price for necessary goods, meaning goods suited to his condition in life and his actual requirements at the time of delivery. The suit was needed for a genuine job interview and was of an appropriate standard, so it is likely to be a necessary. Liability is for a reasonable price rather than the contract price. Other contracts of a minor are not simply void, and ratification on reaching majority no longer applies."}, {"id": 1144, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Daniel Okafor instructs your firm to sell his registered freehold house in Leeds, and the contract will be based on the Standard Conditions of Sale. You propose to follow the Law Society Conveyancing Protocol. Daniel owns the house outright, he has a mortgage that will be repaid on completion, and a buyer has been found through an estate agent. Which documents should you send to the buyer's solicitor at the outset of the transaction?", "options": {"A": "Only the official copies and title plan, because the buyer's solicitor prepares the contract and the property information forms", "B": "The draft contract and a draft transfer, with the property information forms to follow only after the buyer's survey has been completed and reported", "C": "The draft contract, official copies and the results of the local authority and drainage searches obtained by the seller's own conveyancer", "D": "The draft contract, official copies of the register and title plan, and the completed Property Information Form and Fittings and Contents Form", "E": "The draft contract and the mortgage redemption statement, with the title documents to follow once the buyer has exchanged contracts"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The draft contract, official copies of the register and title plan, and the completed Property Information Form and Fittings and Contents Form): Under the Protocol the seller's solicitor supplies the draft contract, the title evidence (official copies and plan) and the seller's completed property information and fittings forms at the outset, so that the buyer can raise enquiries early. Searches are commissioned by the buyer's solicitor, not the seller's. The transfer deed is prepared later, and a redemption statement is needed nearer completion. In a sale the seller's side prepares the contract, so the buyer does not draft it."}, {"id": 1145, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mrs Dunmore, aged 80, is at home with her solicitor to sign her will, which leaves a legacy of £20,000 to her friend Gareth. Gareth is present, and Mrs Dunmore wants him to act as one of the two witnesses because the solicitor's secretary has not arrived. The solicitor is the only other person in the room besides Gareth. What advice should the solicitor give?", "options": {"A": "Gareth may witness, because the solicitor is an independent second witness and the legacy therefore stays valid", "B": "The will would be invalid in its entirety if a beneficiary witnesses it, so Gareth must be replaced as a witness before signing", "C": "Gareth should not witness, because his legacy would fail although the will stays valid, so independent witnesses are needed", "D": "Gareth may witness if he disclaims the legacy afterwards, because a disclaimer cures the defect in attestation", "E": "Gareth may witness if Mrs Dunmore confirms in writing that she wants him to take the legacy despite his role"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gareth should not witness, because his legacy would fail although the will stays valid, so independent witnesses are needed): Under section 15 of the Wills Act 1837 a gift to a person who witnesses the will, or to the spouse of a witness, is void, although the will itself remains valid. The gift is saved only if the will is duly attested by two other witnesses, and here there would be just one besides Gareth. The solicitor should therefore advise finding two independent witnesses, protecting both the will and the legacy. A disclaimer or written wish does not change the rule."}, {"id": 1146, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Anglia Law LLP prepared a will for Mr Hardy, who has now died. On re-reading the file, a partner sees that a clause giving £20,000 to a local hospice, as Mr Hardy instructed, was left out of the typed will through the firm's own error. The executors have asked the firm to obtain probate. The partner is unsure whether the gift could be restored. What should the firm do?", "options": {"A": "Apply for rectification before probate without raising the error with the executors, because correcting it at no cost to them resolves the matter fairly", "B": "Tell the executors promptly of the omission and recommend independent advice, because the firm's own interests may conflict with theirs", "C": "Tell the hospice about the omission directly, because the gift was intended for it and the firm owes the hospice a duty of candour", "D": "Tell the executors but continue to advise them on rectification, because the firm knows the file best and the conflict is slight", "E": "Obtain probate on the will as signed and say nothing, because the executors' instructions cover only the grant of probate"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the executors promptly of the omission and recommend independent advice, because the firm's own interests may conflict with theirs): Solicitors must tell clients promptly when something has gone wrong and act in their best interests. The omission is the firm's own error, so the firm has a conflict with the executors, who may have a claim and may wish to rectify the will. The firm should therefore inform them and recommend independent advice. Keeping silent or acting without instructions breaches duties of honesty and client care, and speaking to the hospice would breach confidentiality."}, {"id": 1147, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "In 2015 Parliament passed the Harbour Fees Act, which states that no harbour fee may be raised without a vote of the Harbour Board, and adds that 'this provision shall not be repealed or amended by any later Act.' In 2024 Parliament passed the Ports Funding Act, which allows the Secretary of State to set harbour fees directly and conflicts with the 2015 rule. Neither Act is a constitutional statute. A harbour owner says the 2015 Act still controls. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The 2015 Act prevails, because a statement that a provision cannot be repealed is binding on later Parliaments", "B": "The later Act prevails, because Parliament cannot bind its successors and the conflicting earlier provision is treated as impliedly repealed", "C": "The 2015 Act prevails until the courts have declared the 2024 Act valid, because they must approve a conflicting later Act", "D": "Both Acts are valid and the harbour owner may choose which to follow, because the courts treat conflicting Acts as equal in force", "E": "The later Act prevails if it expressly names the 2015 Act and repeals it, since implied repeal is not recognised"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The later Act prevails, because Parliament cannot bind its successors and the conflicting earlier provision is treated as impliedly repealed): Parliamentary sovereignty means that Parliament can make or unmake any law and that no Parliament can bind its successors. A provision purporting to prevent later repeal therefore has no entrenching effect. Where a later Act conflicts with an earlier ordinary Act, the later one prevails, and the earlier is impliedly repealed to the extent of the conflict. Courts do not need to approve the later Act, and a harbour owner has no choice between the two."}, {"id": 1148, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Peartree Interiors Ltd has proposed a company voluntary arrangement. At the creditors' decision procedure, creditors holding 76% by value of those voting approved it. Much of that support came from the directors, who are owed £300,000 in loans. The unconnected creditors are owed £200,000 in total, and those who voted against the proposal are owed £120,000. The members also approved the proposal. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The creditors have not approved it, because unconnected creditors owed more than half of all unconnected debt voted against the proposal", "B": "The proposal is not approved, because a voluntary arrangement needs approval by 90% in value of all creditors, whether or not they voted at the decision procedure", "C": "The proposal is approved, because unconnected creditors who voted against hold less than 75% by value of the creditors voting overall", "D": "The proposal is approved, because creditors holding at least 75% by value of those voting approved it and the members approved it too", "E": "The proposal is not approved, because the directors' loans are connected debts and connected creditors cannot vote on a proposal"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The creditors have not approved it, because unconnected creditors owed more than half of all unconnected debt voted against the proposal): A proposal needs approval by 75% or more in value of the creditors voting, but it fails if more than half the total value of the unconnected creditors vote against it. Unconnected creditors are owed £200,000 and £120,000 of that voted against, which is 60%, so the creditors' approval fails despite the 76% headline figure. Connected creditors such as the directors may vote, which is why the extra test exists to protect outsiders. The members' approval cannot rescue the proposal, since the creditors' decision is the one that counts where the two differ."}, {"id": 1149, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Digital Records Bill is introduced in the House of Commons by the government. A trainee prepares a briefing for a client company, Orchard Data, that is worried about the Bill, and sets out the stages it must pass before it becomes law. The Bill is an ordinary public Bill, not a money Bill, and no Parliament Act procedure is used. Which of the following sequences of events correctly describes the route to becoming an Act?", "options": {"A": "First reading, second reading and committee stage in the Commons, then Royal Assent, followed by the Lords' second reading and third reading", "B": "First reading, committee stage, second reading, report stage and third reading in each House, followed by Royal Assent when both Houses have approved the same text", "C": "Second reading, first reading, committee stage, report stage and third reading in the Commons, then Royal Assent without consideration by the Lords", "D": "First reading, second reading, report stage, committee stage and third reading in each House, followed by Royal Assent after any amendments", "E": "First reading, second reading, committee stage, report stage and third reading in each House, followed by Royal Assent after any amendments are agreed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (First reading, second reading, committee stage, report stage and third reading in each House, followed by Royal Assent after any amendments are agreed): A Bill goes through first reading, second reading, committee stage, report stage and third reading in the first House and then through the same stages in the second House. Any amendments made by one House must be agreed by the other, and the Bill then receives Royal Assent. The order of committee and report stage cannot be reversed, and Royal Assent is the final step, not an early one. The Lords cannot be bypassed unless the Parliament Acts apply, which they do not here."}, {"id": 1150, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Pike and Partners LLP has three members and is registered under the Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. It ordered goods worth £60,000 from a supplier and cannot pay. One member, Dora, signed the order. The supplier asks whether it may sue the members personally for the debt. None of the members gave a personal guarantee. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The LLP alone is liable, because it is a separate legal person and members are not personally liable without a guarantee", "B": "The members are liable up to the value of their capital contributions, because that is the limit of the risk", "C": "Only the designated members are personally liable, because they sign the accounts and deal with the regulators", "D": "Only Dora is personally liable, because a member who signs on behalf of an LLP is liable as an agent", "E": "All three members are jointly and severally liable, because an LLP is treated as a partnership for liability to creditors"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The LLP alone is liable, because it is a separate legal person and members are not personally liable without a guarantee): An LLP is a body corporate with its own legal personality, and a member who acts as its agent does not become personally liable for its debts. The supplier's claim lies against the LLP alone unless a member gave a personal guarantee or took on personal responsibility. Designated members have extra filing duties but no extra liability for debts. The rules for general partnerships do not apply."}, {"id": 1151, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Mrs Singh is the sole director of Vale Packaging Ltd. In March she tells her solicitor that the company has lost its main customer, owes HMRC and suppliers £240,000, and has no realistic prospect of recovery. She hopes to keep trading for six months in the hope that a new contract may come, and to keep suppliers supplying on credit. She asks what her personal risk is. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Advise that she faces liability only if she intended to defraud creditors, so continuing to trade in hope of a new contract is safe if she stays honest", "B": "Advise that she must put the company into liquidation within 28 days or she will be automatically disqualified from acting as a director", "C": "Advise that limited liability protects her personally, so she faces exposure only if she has given personal guarantees to suppliers or the bank", "D": "Warn her that she risks personal liability for wrongful trading if losses to creditors increase, and advise her to take insolvency advice and minimise further loss", "E": "Advise that she may keep trading for a reasonable period, provided she tells creditors the position and they do not object"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warn her that she risks personal liability for wrongful trading if losses to creditors increase, and advise her to take insolvency advice and minimise further loss): Wrongful trading under section 214 Insolvency Act 1986 applies where a director knew or ought to have concluded that there was no reasonable prospect of avoiding insolvent liquidation or administration, and did not take every step to minimise loss to creditors. Honesty is no answer, because the test is objective and subjective combined, unlike fraudulent trading which needs dishonesty. Mrs Singh should therefore take advice from an insolvency practitioner and protect creditors. Telling creditors the position helps but does not by itself discharge the duty, and limited liability does not shelter a director from this personal liability."}, {"id": 1152, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Rosa Ketteridge agreed with her former employer, Brandon Fitness Ltd, not to solicit its clients for two years after leaving, and the covenant is enforceable. Three months later she incorporated Ketteridge Training Ltd, with £100 share capital, of which she is sole shareholder and director, and the company began approaching Brandon's clients. Brandon wants an injunction against both Rosa and the company. Which of the following best describes the likely position as regards the company?", "options": {"A": "The court may lift the veil, but only to award Brandon damages against Rosa, since an injunction cannot bind a company that signed no covenant", "B": "The court will lift the veil, because Rosa owns all the shares and controls the company, and that control alone justifies disregarding its separate personality", "C": "The court cannot grant relief against the company, because it was lawfully incorporated and is a separate person that was not a party to the covenant", "D": "The court will lift the veil, because the company was formed to conceal Rosa's involvement, and concealment alone justifies disregarding separate personality", "E": "The court may grant relief against it, because the company was interposed to evade an existing legal obligation"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court may grant relief against it, because the company was interposed to evade an existing legal obligation): The covenant was an existing obligation binding Rosa, and the company was formed to avoid it by carrying on the same activity through a new person. That is the evasion situation seen in Gilford Motor Co v Horne, where the court may treat the company as a device and grant an injunction against it. Control alone does not justify piercing, and merely concealing a person's involvement does not either. The remedy against the company is therefore not ruled out simply because it was never a party to the covenant."}, {"id": 1153, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Elena Kowalski, an EU citizen who has lived in the UK since 2015, holds settled status under the scheme that implemented the Withdrawal Agreement. A later Act of Parliament, passed in 2026, restricts access to a social security benefit in a way that Elena argues breaches the citizens' rights provisions of the Withdrawal Agreement. She asks her solicitor whether she can rely on those rights in a UK court. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "She cannot rely on them, because the repeal of the European Communities Act 1972 removed every right derived from EU membership", "B": "She cannot rely on them, because the later Act is an Act of Parliament and parliamentary sovereignty requires the court to follow it whatever the Withdrawal Agreement says", "C": "She can rely on them, because Withdrawal Agreement rights stay enforceable and prevail over inconsistent later legislation unless it expressly provides otherwise", "D": "She can rely on them only in the Court of Justice, because that court alone interprets the Withdrawal Agreement for the UK", "E": "She can rely on them only through a declaration of incompatibility, because the Agreement is treated like a Convention right"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She can rely on them, because Withdrawal Agreement rights stay enforceable and prevail over inconsistent later legislation unless it expressly provides otherwise): The European Union (Withdrawal Agreement) Act 2020 preserved the citizens' rights in the Agreement, which remain enforceable in UK courts and are given priority over inconsistent domestic legislation, unless a later enactment expressly says otherwise. Elena can therefore rely on those rights in domestic proceedings even though the 1972 Act has gone. Parliamentary sovereignty is respected because Parliament may override them in express terms. The Court of Justice no longer hears these claims, and the Human Rights Act mechanism is unrelated."}, {"id": 1154, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Mehmet Kaya is charged with burglary of a house. The prosecution says he forced a rear window, entered while the occupants were out and took only jewellery. Mehmet has three convictions for burglary, each involving a forced rear window and the taking of only jewellery, the most recent six years ago. Mehmet denies being the burglar. The prosecutor wishes to adduce the convictions and asks which gateway under section 101 of the Criminal Justice Act 2003 is most appropriate.", "options": {"A": "The gateway for an attack on another person's character, because Mehmet has denied involvement and so attacked the prosecution case", "B": "The gateway for important explanatory evidence, because the jury could not properly understand the case without hearing of the convictions and the background to them", "C": "The gateway for evidence relevant to an important matter in issue between prosecution and defence, because the convictions show a propensity to burgle in this way", "D": "The gateway for evidence between co-defendants, because the convictions have substantial probative value on an important matter between them", "E": "The gateway for agreement between the parties, because the defendant has consented to the convictions being admitted at trial"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The gateway for evidence relevant to an important matter in issue between prosecution and defence, because the convictions show a propensity to burgle in this way): The convictions share a distinctive method with the present allegation, so they are relevant to whether Mehmet has a propensity to commit burglary in that way, which is an important matter in issue between the prosecution and the defence. Explanatory evidence is needed only where the jury would otherwise find the case impossible to understand. A denial of the offence is not an attack on another person's character, there is no co-defendant, and Mehmet has not agreed. The court may still exclude the evidence for unfairness."}, {"id": 1155, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ken made a will leaving the whole of his estate to his wife Tia and naming her as sole executor. In 2026 they began divorce proceedings, and a conditional order was made on 1 May. Ken did not apply for a final order and died suddenly on 20 May, before the final order was made. He had not changed his will. Does Tia take under it?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the divorce provisions apply only once the final order has been made, so the will is unaffected", "B": "Yes, but she cannot act as executor, because a conditional order ends an appointment and not the gift", "C": "No, because a conditional order ends the marriage, so Tia is treated as having died when it was made", "D": "No, because commencing divorce proceedings revokes the will, leaving the estate to pass under the intestacy rules", "E": "Yes, but only as to half the estate, because a conditional order halves the rights of a spouse under a will"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because the divorce provisions apply only once the final order has been made, so the will is unaffected): The marriage continues until the final order is made, and the effect on a will under section 18A applies only on that final order. A conditional order, and the earlier start of proceedings, leave the will untouched. Ken died before the final order, so Tia remains his spouse, takes the gift and may act as executor. The rule does not halve her entitlement."}, {"id": 1156, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Harcourt Pharma Ltd is the claimant in a multi track claim in the County Court in which standard disclosure applies. The search is expected to cover about 40,000 emails held on the company's servers. Its solicitor is unsure what is expected of the parties in relation to the electronic documents before the first case management conference. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "The parties must exchange electronic documents in native format with metadata, and every list must record the metadata for each document", "B": "The parties should discuss reasonable and proportionate use of technology in the search before the conference and complete an electronic documents questionnaire", "C": "Electronic documents fall outside the disclosure rules until the trial judge orders production at the pre trial review", "D": "The parties need discuss electronic documents only after specific disclosure has been ordered, because standard disclosure covers paper documents", "E": "The parties must agree before exchange of lists to search every custodian's emails, so that no relevant message is left out"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The parties should discuss reasonable and proportionate use of technology in the search before the conference and complete an electronic documents questionnaire): Practice Direction 31B requires the parties' legal representatives to discuss the use of technology and the scope of the electronic search before the first case management conference, and an electronic documents questionnaire may be exchanged to record the answers. The aim is a proportionate approach, not a search of every source. Electronic documents are documents for disclosure purposes just like paper, so nothing waits for a specific disclosure order or the trial."}, {"id": 1157, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Gareth is suing Moorside Builders Ltd in the county court over a conservatory that has begun to subside. The local authority's building control department inspected the foundations when they were poured and holds inspection notes and photographs. Neither Gareth nor Moorside has these records. Proceedings have been issued, and Gareth's solicitor believes the notes will support his claim that the foundations were too shallow. The authority has declined to hand them over voluntarily. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Apply for an order without notice to the authority, because non party applications are decided on paper and the authority need not be heard", "B": "Apply for specific disclosure against the builder, because the authority's records are treated as within the builder's control", "C": "Request standard disclosure from the authority, because every person holding relevant documents must list them after allocation", "D": "Apply on notice to the authority for non party disclosure, showing the records are likely to support the claim and disclosure is necessary", "E": "Apply under the pre action disclosure rule, because that rule allows disclosure against any person who holds relevant records"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply on notice to the authority for non party disclosure, showing the records are likely to support the claim and disclosure is necessary): Documents held by a person who is not a party can be obtained only through an application for non party disclosure under CPR 31.17. The application must be supported by evidence, the documents must be likely to support the applicant's case or adversely affect another party's, and disclosure must be necessary to dispose fairly of the claim. Pre action disclosure is for those likely to become parties before proceedings begin. Standard disclosure applies to parties, and the builder cannot be ordered to produce records outside its control."}, {"id": 1158, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A statute allows a prison governor to place a prisoner in segregation where this is necessary for the safety of others. It says nothing about the procedure. The governor segregates Ola Bakare on the basis of a confidential intelligence report, tells him only that he is a risk, and refuses to let him respond. Ola's solicitor considers judicial review for procedural unfairness. The governor says the statute prescribes no hearing. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The decision is unlawful only if the governor intended to deny a hearing, since fairness concerns the decision maker's purpose", "B": "No hearing is required, because a statute that is silent about procedure leaves no room for a duty of fairness at common law", "C": "A full oral hearing with legal representation is required, because fairness demands this in all decisions affecting liberty", "D": "The decision is lawful unless Ola can show that the governor's decision was irrational on its merits, since procedure is not reviewable separately", "E": "The common law implies fairness, so Ola must be told enough of the gist of the case to respond, unless disclosure would risk safety"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The common law implies fairness, so Ola must be told enough of the gist of the case to respond, unless disclosure would risk safety): Where a statute is silent on procedure, the common law supplies the procedure that fairness requires, and a prisoner facing segregation is entitled to know enough of the case against him to make a meaningful response. The duty is flexible, so the gist may be enough where full disclosure of intelligence would endanger others. A full oral hearing is not required in every case, and unfairness is judged by the procedure followed and not by the governor's purpose. Irrationality is a separate ground."}, {"id": 1159, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Marcus died in October 2026 owning 30% of the shares in Kestrel Bearings Ltd, an unquoted trading company. He bought the shares fourteen months before death from a retiring shareholder. They are not an excepted asset. His executor asks whether business property relief is available on the shares. Marcus also owned a house and a savings account. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Relief is not available, because the shares must have been owned for two years before death and no replacement property relief applies on these facts", "B": "Relief is available, because one year is the qualifying period for shares bought from an existing shareholder of the company", "C": "Relief is available, because shares in an unquoted trading company qualify for relief whatever the period for which the deceased owned them", "D": "Relief is available, because a holding above 25% counts as a substantial interest and the relief turns on the size of the holding he owned", "E": "Relief is not available, because only a controlling shareholding carrying more than 50% of the votes can qualify for the relief"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Relief is not available, because the shares must have been owned for two years before death and no replacement property relief applies on these facts): Business property relief on shares in an unquoted trading company requires that the company is not mainly engaged in investment, that the shares are not an excepted asset and that the deceased owned them for two years before death, or owned replacement property. Marcus held the shares for fourteen months and there was no replacement, so the ownership condition is not met. There is no minimum percentage holding for unquoted shares and no shorter period for shares bought from another shareholder."}, {"id": 1160, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Neil exchanged contracts to buy a cottage for £260,000 and paid a ten per cent deposit. Before the agreed completion date Neil died. His will leaves his whole estate to his sister Orla and appoints his friend Priya as executor. The seller's solicitor has written asking whether the sale will proceed. Which of the following best states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Priya may choose whether to complete, because an executor is free to disclaim a contract made by the deceased before death", "B": "The contract binds Neil's estate, so Priya must complete using estate funds, and the cottage then passes to Orla under the will", "C": "The seller may rescind because the purchase was personal to Neil, and may keep the deposit as compensation for the delay", "D": "The contract is discharged by Neil's death, so the deposit must be returned to his estate and Orla receives no interest in the cottage", "E": "Orla must complete in her own name, because she inherits the benefit and burden of the contract when the will takes effect"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The contract binds Neil's estate, so Priya must complete using estate funds, and the cottage then passes to Orla under the will): A contract for the sale of land is not discharged by the death of a party, and the buyer's obligations pass to his personal representatives. Priya as executor must complete using estate assets, and the cottage, once acquired, passes under the will to Orla. The seller cannot rescind because a land contract is not personal in this way. An executor cannot disclaim an enforceable contract without risking a claim against the estate."}, {"id": 1161, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Rhona, a solicitor, acts for the trustees of a discretionary trust. A beneficiary, who is within the class of objects of the discretion, writes to Rhona asking for a copy of the settlor's letter of wishes, which the trustees keep on the trust file. The trustees have not given Rhona any instructions about disclosure. Rhona is unsure how to reply. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Rhona should disclose it, because a beneficiary of a discretionary trust is entitled to see every trust document as of right", "B": "Rhona should disclose it, because trustees must give reasons for every exercise of a discretion and the letter explains those reasons", "C": "Rhona should not disclose it without the trustees' instructions, as the file is confidential to them, but the beneficiary may apply to court", "D": "Rhona should refuse and tell the beneficiary that only an absolute beneficiary has any right to ask the court for disclosure", "E": "Rhona should disclose it if the beneficiary agrees to keep it confidential, since a written assurance removes her duty of confidence to the trustees as her clients"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rhona should not disclose it without the trustees' instructions, as the file is confidential to them, but the beneficiary may apply to court): A beneficiary has no automatic right to every trust document, and the court has a supervisory jurisdiction to order disclosure, as in Schmidt v Rosewood. Rhona owes her clients, the trustees, a duty of confidentiality and needs their instructions before releasing a trust document. Trustees need not give reasons for discretionary decisions, and a beneficiary's undertaking does not release the solicitor's duty. The beneficiary can apply to the court."}, {"id": 1162, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Piers Whitlock, a solicitor, is instructed by a client, Dominic Hale, a retired diplomat who wants to issue judicial review proceedings against the Foreign Secretary for deciding to sign a bilateral trade treaty under the prerogative. Dominic says he disagrees with the treaty's policy, that no statute is involved and that he has no personal legal rights affected. He is willing to pay for the claim and wants it brought quickly for publicity. What should Piers do?", "options": {"A": "Issue the claim after sending a pre-action letter, because the prerogative is reviewable and Dominic has sufficient interest as a taxpayer", "B": "Advise Dominic frankly that the claim is not properly arguable, because courts do not review treaty-making as such, and decline to issue it", "C": "Issue the claim and warn Dominic of the risk of an adverse costs order, because an informed client may take that risk", "D": "Issue the claim as instructed, because Dominic is paying for it and the client's wishes decide whether proceedings are brought", "E": "Issue the claim but limit the grounds to procedural unfairness, because that avoids arguments about the justiciability of treaty decisions"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise Dominic frankly that the claim is not properly arguable, because courts do not review treaty-making as such, and decline to issue it): A solicitor must not make or advance a claim that is not properly arguable, and must act in the client's best interests and give an honest assessment of prospects. Entering into treaties is a prerogative function that the domestic courts do not review, since treaties do not create rights in domestic law unless Parliament legislates. Dominic's wish for publicity and willingness to pay do not make the claim arguable. Piers should explain this candidly and decline to issue the claim."}, {"id": 1163, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Catrin Hughes is a solicitor in Cardiff who is asked at a training session about the constitutional status of the Senedd and the UK Parliament. A colleague says that, because the Senedd is a directly elected legislature with its own Acts, it now has sovereign power of its own and Westminster cannot legislate on devolved matters. Catrin wants to give the correct account. Which of the following correctly states the status of the devolved legislatures?", "options": {"A": "They exist by Acts of Parliament, but Westminster has lost power to legislate on devolved matters since those Acts took effect", "B": "They derive their authority from the Crown by prerogative, so they can be abolished or altered without legislation", "C": "They exist by Acts of the UK Parliament, which retains the power to legislate on any matter, including those that are devolved", "D": "They are sovereign within the devolved areas, because the people of each nation voted for devolution in referendums", "E": "They derive power from international treaty arrangements, so removal of their powers would need a new treaty and not an Act of Parliament"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They exist by Acts of the UK Parliament, which retains the power to legislate on any matter, including those that are devolved): The Scotland Act 1998 and the Government of Wales Act 2006 created the devolved legislatures, so their powers rest on Acts of the UK Parliament. Under parliamentary sovereignty Westminster can legislate on any matter and could amend or repeal the devolution statutes. By convention, Westminster normally seeks consent before legislating on devolved matters, but the convention is not a legal limit. Referendums gave political legitimacy but did not alter that legal position."}, {"id": 1164, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "A firm acts for Ola, charged with robbery after a mugging in which a knife was shown. Ola's instructions are that he took the victim's phone but that his friend Dan produced the knife. Dan, charged with the same robbery, asks the firm to represent him as well and offers to pay the fees. The firm believes it has capacity to act for both men. What should the firm do?", "options": {"A": "Act for both men provided each gives informed written consent, because consent removes a conflict of interests between clients", "B": "Act for both men and refuse to share either client's account with the other, because confidentiality duties protect each client", "C": "Act for Ola only and decline to act for Dan, because their defences conflict and the firm cannot protect both clients' interests", "D": "Act for both men with separate fee earners, because information barriers within the firm prevent the conflict from arising", "E": "Act for both men only if they agree to run a common defence, because the interests of the two clients then align and no conflict arises"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Act for Ola only and decline to act for Dan, because their defences conflict and the firm cannot protect both clients' interests): Ola's account puts the knife in Dan's hands, so the two men have directly conflicting defences, and acting for both would be a conflict of interests under the SRA Code. Consent, information barriers or confidentiality cannot fix a conflict that arises from the clients' own competing accounts. The firm may continue for Ola but must decline Dan. A common defence is not realistic where each man's best case blames the other."}, {"id": 1165, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A boat operator negligently leaves a hire dinghy to drift in a fast flowing river with a young child on board. A passer-by, Mr Osborne, sees the child's danger and dives into the cold, fast water to rescue her. He succeeds in bringing the child to the bank but is injured by submerged debris. Mr Osborne sues the boat operator in negligence. The operator pleads that he voluntarily took on the risk and relies on volenti non fit injuria. Which of the following best describes the outcome of this defence?", "options": {"A": "The defence will succeed, because Mr Osborne chose to enter dangerous water knowing the risk of injury and could see submerged hazards", "B": "The defence will succeed, because a rescuer acts only out of choice and so impliedly waives any claim against the wrongdoer", "C": "The defence will succeed in part, so that Mr Osborne recovers only half of his damages for the risk he took", "D": "The defence will fail, but the rescuer's damages will be reduced for taking a rash risk by diving into the water", "E": "The defence will fail, because a rescuer responding to an emergency created by the defendant does not freely accept the risk"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The defence will fail, because a rescuer responding to an emergency created by the defendant does not freely accept the risk): Volenti needs a free and informed acceptance of the risk. A rescuer who intervenes in an emergency created by the defendant's negligence acts under a sense of moral duty and not freely, so the defence is not available. Damages are reduced only if the rescuer acted rashly in a way that would justify it, and that is a different question. Volenti is a complete defence or none, so it does not succeed in part."}, {"id": 1166, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Kate, a trustee, takes £10,000 of trust money and pays it into her own bank account, which already holds £5,000 of her own money. The balance is therefore £15,000. She then spends £12,000 on a holiday, leaving £3,000, and later pays in £20,000 of her own salary, so that the balance rises to £23,000. The beneficiaries wish to trace the trust money in equity. Which of the following correctly states their claim?", "options": {"A": "They can claim only £3,000, the lowest intermediate balance, since later payments of her own salary cannot restore the trust's claim", "B": "They can claim £3,000 and also the £20,000, because beneficiaries may claim the whole of an account as the mixture grows", "C": "They can claim the whole £10,000, because the trust money is treated as repaid by her later payment of £20,000 into the account", "D": "They can claim nothing in equity, because the trust money was spent on a holiday and an expended fund cannot be traced", "E": "They can claim £5,000, being the amount of her own money that was mixed with the trust money in the account"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They can claim only £3,000, the lowest intermediate balance, since later payments of her own salary cannot restore the trust's claim): Where a trustee mixes trust money with her own, she is presumed to spend her own money first, so the first £5,000 spent was her own and £7,000 of the trust money was dissipated. The trust's claim is limited by the lowest intermediate balance, here £3,000, because the dissipated money cannot be traced and her later salary was not paid in with that intention. The claim survives against the remaining balance, so it is not lost altogether."}, {"id": 1167, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "You act for Pam and Colin Reed, retired people with modest savings, who are disturbed every night by noise from a neighbouring music venue. They want an interim injunction to restrict the noise while their claim in private nuisance proceeds. The court will ordinarily require an undertaking in damages as the price of an interim injunction. The Reeds have not yet been told of this. What should you advise?", "options": {"A": "Explain that they will be asked to undertake to compensate the venue if they lose at trial, which could cost them money", "B": "Advise that the undertaking operates only if the injunction is granted at the final trial and not at the interim stage", "C": "Treat the undertaking as a formality, and obtain their signatures after the hearing without explaining its effect", "D": "Give the undertaking in your own name, so that the Reeds carry no personal risk if the claim later fails", "E": "Tell them the court will waive the undertaking, because individuals of limited means are not asked to give one"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that they will be asked to undertake to compensate the venue if they lose at trial, which could cost them money): An interim injunction is usually granted only if the applicant gives a cross-undertaking to compensate the respondent for loss caused by the injunction if it later proves unjustified. A solicitor must act in the client's best interests and give them the information they need to make informed decisions about their case, which includes the financial risk the undertaking carries. A solicitor would not ordinarily give such an undertaking personally. The court does not waive it automatically, as means are only one factor, and it applies at the interim stage, so presenting it as a formality would fall below the duty of client care."}, {"id": 1168, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Sunil contracted with Brightview Windows to install windows at his home. A term of the contract states that Sunil's sister, Anjali, who lives in the house, 'may require Brightview to provide the free three-year maintenance service'. Brightview later refused the service. Anjali is not a party to the contract and gave no consideration. The contract does not exclude the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Anjali cannot enforce the term, because a third party may rely on an exclusion clause but cannot sue on a positive benefit", "B": "Anjali can enforce the term, but only if she provided consideration to Brightview for the maintenance service", "C": "Anjali can enforce the term, but only if Sunil first assigns his rights under the contract to her in writing", "D": "Anjali can enforce the term, because the contract identifies her and expressly confers a benefit on her", "E": "Anjali cannot enforce the term, because only the parties to a contract can sue on it and she is a stranger to it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anjali can enforce the term, because the contract identifies her and expressly confers a benefit on her): The 1999 Act allows a non-party to enforce a term if the contract expressly says so or the term purports to confer a benefit on her, and she is identified by name, class or description. Anjali is named and the maintenance service is a benefit for her. She does not need to give consideration, and she does not need an assignment. The Act allows positive claims as well as reliance on exclusions."}, {"id": 1169, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Crumb and Co, a bakery run as a sole trader by Hugh Crumb, bought a commercial oven from Delaney Catering Supplies Ltd for £14,000 on Delaney's standard terms. A term excludes all liability for the oven being of unsatisfactory quality. The oven was defective and Hugh lost a week's trade. Hugh says that the exclusion is an unfair term under Part 2 of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 because he is an individual. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The exclusion is void in all cases, because the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 bans clauses excluding the quality terms in sales", "B": "Part 2 applies, because Hugh is an individual and the Act protects every individual who buys on a trader's standard terms", "C": "Part 2 does not apply as Hugh bought for business, but the exclusion must be reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977", "D": "The exclusion is valid, because both parties contracted in the course of business and the court will not review their bargain on fairness grounds", "E": "Part 2 applies, because a sole trader with a small business is treated as a consumer where the seller drafted the terms"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Part 2 does not apply as Hugh bought for business, but the exclusion must be reasonable under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977): A consumer is an individual acting wholly or mainly outside his trade, business or profession, so Hugh, buying an oven for his bakery, is not one. Part 2 of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 therefore does not apply. Exclusion of the implied quality term in a sale between businesses is subject to the reasonableness test under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, which is why the clause is not simply valid. It is not void outright either, because only an exclusion of the implied term as to title is."}, {"id": 1170, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Corbett Estates Ltd is suing a builder in the county court over defective drainage. Its case depends on the evidence of an independent surveyor, Mr Lindqvist, who inspected the site in 2023 and wrote to the company. He is willing to help but says his employer will not let him attend the trial, which is in five weeks, unless he is legally compelled to do so. Corbett's solicitor wants to ensure he attends and gives oral evidence. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a witness summons from the court and serve it at least seven days before the hearing, tendering expenses and compensation for lost time", "B": "Obtain a witness summons from the court and serve it at least 28 days before the hearing, tendering only reasonable travel expenses as conduct money", "C": "Serve the surveyor's witness statement on the defendant, since service of a statement binds the maker to attend the trial", "D": "Serve a notice to admit facts on the surveyor, which obliges him to attend trial once the notice has been served on him", "E": "Apply on the day of trial for the court to order his attendance, because a summons cannot be issued before the trial begins"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Obtain a witness summons from the court and serve it at least seven days before the hearing, tendering expenses and compensation for lost time): A witness who will not attend voluntarily can be compelled by a witness summons under CPR Part 34. The summons must be served at least seven days before the witness is required to attend, and the party must offer the witness a sum to cover travel expenses and compensation for loss of time. A notice to admit is directed at the other party's concessions, not at securing a third party's attendance. Serving a witness statement does not oblige the maker to come to court."}, {"id": 1171, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Phil, angry at his landlord, fires a shotgun at the landlord's front window from the garden, intending to frighten him and to break the glass. A pellet passes through the broken pane and wounds the landlord, who is standing inside. Phil knew the landlord was in the room. Phil is charged with aggravated criminal damage under section 1(2) of the Criminal Damage Act 1971. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Not guilty, because the danger to life must arise from the damage to property and not from the act that caused the damage", "B": "Not guilty, because section 1(2) requires life to be actually endangered by the damage and the landlord survived", "C": "Guilty, because he intended to damage property and was reckless that life would be endangered by his act of firing the gun", "D": "Guilty, because the pellet that wounded the landlord was a result of the damage to the window that Phil intended", "E": "Not guilty, because section 1(2) applies only where the property damaged belongs to the defendant and not to another person"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Not guilty, because the danger to life must arise from the damage to property and not from the act that caused the damage): Under section 1(2) the defendant must intend or be reckless that life would be endangered by the damage, and the danger must come from the damage itself, such as flying glass, not from the means used to cause it. Here the danger came from the shot, not from the broken window, so the aggravated offence is not made out. Ownership of the property is not relevant to section 1(2). Phil is liable for simple criminal damage and for offences against the person."}, {"id": 1172, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Nina is walking down the street holding her handbag firmly in her hand. Paul runs up behind her, wrenches the bag from her grip with a sharp pull that makes her stumble, and runs off with it. Nina is not hurt. Paul is charged with robbery under section 8 of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following best describes the position on the force element?", "options": {"A": "Force is not shown, because force must be directed at the person's body rather than at the property she is holding", "B": "Force is shown only if Nina resisted, because the offence requires an actual struggle between the victim and the thief", "C": "Force is shown, because any taking from a person amounts to the use of force whether or not any struggle occurs", "D": "Force is not shown, because snatching an item without injury is only theft and robbery requires the victim to be hurt", "E": "Force is shown, because wrenching a bag from a person's grip is force used on the person in order to steal"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Force is shown, because wrenching a bag from a person's grip is force used on the person in order to steal): Force on a person includes force applied to something the person is holding, so wrenching the bag from Nina's grip so that she stumbles is force on her. No injury or resistance is needed, and the question of force is for the jury. A mere pickpocketing without any pulling would be theft only. Paul used force in order to steal, so the robbery requirement is met."}, {"id": 1173, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Chloe Barnard instructed a licensed conveyancer, Vale Conveyancing Ltd, on a house sale. She believes the firm's delay cost her a purchase. She complained in writing to the firm eleven weeks ago, and the firm has not given a final response. Chloe now asks where she can take the complaint, and whether her choice depends on the fact that the firm is not a solicitors' practice. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "She must complain to the SRA, because the SRA handles service complaints about all firms that conduct conveyancing for the public", "B": "She should sue in the county court, because the Legal Ombudsman cannot consider complaints about delay and poor service", "C": "She may go to the Legal Ombudsman, which handles complaints about all authorised providers, once the firm has had eight weeks to respond", "D": "She may take the complaint to the Legal Ombudsman only if the firm is regulated by the SRA, as licensed conveyancers have their own scheme", "E": "She must wait for the firm's final response however long it takes, since the Legal Ombudsman considers only complaints that the firm has refused"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She may go to the Legal Ombudsman, which handles complaints about all authorised providers, once the firm has had eight weeks to respond): The Legal Ombudsman provides a single scheme for service complaints against all authorised providers, including licensed conveyancers. A client may bring a complaint after the provider's own procedure has been used and a final response given, or once eight weeks have passed without one. Chloe's eleven weeks since complaining therefore opens the Ombudsman route, subject to the usual time limits. The SRA does not resolve service complaints for consumers, and a court claim is not required first."}, {"id": 1174, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Merebank Workers' Alliance plans a march through the centre of a town. The senior police officer present at the planning stage is told by intelligence that rival groups intend to attack the march and that serious public disorder may result. She wishes to impose conditions on the route and timing. The Alliance's organiser asks a solicitor when police may impose conditions on a public procession. Which of the following best states the rule?", "options": {"A": "A senior officer may impose conditions only if the organisers have failed to give written notice of the procession to the police", "B": "A senior officer may impose conditions only with the written consent of the Home Secretary after consultation with the local authority", "C": "A senior officer may impose conditions if she suspects that any criminal offence, however minor, might be committed by a person taking part in or watching the procession", "D": "A senior officer may impose conditions only after a magistrates' court has found that serious public disorder is likely to result", "E": "A senior officer may impose conditions if she reasonably believes the procession may cause serious disorder, serious damage or serious disruption to community life"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A senior officer may impose conditions if she reasonably believes the procession may cause serious disorder, serious damage or serious disruption to community life): Section 12 of the Public Order Act 1986 allows a senior police officer to impose conditions where she reasonably believes the procession may result in serious public disorder, serious damage to property or serious disruption to the life of the community. The Alliance's intelligence about an attack fits the first limb. Conditions do not depend on a lack of notice, a court finding or ministerial consent. A suspicion of a minor offence by one participant does not meet the statutory threshold."}, {"id": 1175, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "The shareholders of Hargreaves Ltd approved by ordinary resolution on 1 June a final dividend of £20,000, as recommended by the directors, payable on 30 June. On 15 June the company's biggest customer collapses and the directors want to cancel the payment. Distributable profits on the last accounts remain sufficient, and no shareholder has agreed to waive the dividend. Can the directors cancel the dividend?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the directors' duty to promote the success of the company overrides a shareholder resolution declaring a dividend", "B": "No, but the directors may defer payment indefinitely, since the declaration creates only a right to be paid when the company can afford it", "C": "Yes, because directors may revoke any dividend before it is paid, as no entitlement arises until the money leaves the company", "D": "No, because a final dividend becomes a debt owed to the shareholders once declared and cannot be cancelled by the directors", "E": "Yes, if the directors pass a board resolution recording that the company can no longer afford to pay the dividend on the due date"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because a final dividend becomes a debt owed to the shareholders once declared and cannot be cancelled by the directors): A final dividend is declared by the shareholders on the directors' recommendation, and once declared it is a debt owed to the shareholders that can be enforced. Interim dividends differ because they may be revoked before payment, but that rule does not apply here. The directors' duties do not allow them to cancel a declared dividend. A board resolution cannot override a decision of the members, though the shareholders could agree to waive."}, {"id": 1176, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Lena Whitmore bought a registered freehold house six months ago. Before completion her solicitor discovered that a tenant, Rory, occupies the garage under a written lease by deed granted by the seller for five years, which Rory did not register. Rory pays rent and is in occupation. Lena asks whether the lease can bind her despite the absence of any entry on the register. Which of the following best states the rule?", "options": {"A": "No, because a lease binds a buyer only if it has been protected by a notice entered on the register", "B": "No, because every legal lease must be registered with its own title to bind a later buyer of the freehold", "C": "Yes, a legal lease for seven years or less is an overriding interest under Schedule 3 paragraph 1", "D": "Yes, but only if Rory can show that he was in actual occupation and that the lease was visible on inspection", "E": "Yes, because a lease for any term is an overriding interest as long as the tenant has paid rent for a year"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, a legal lease for seven years or less is an overriding interest under Schedule 3 paragraph 1): Schedule 3 paragraph 1 to the Land Registration Act 2002 makes a legal lease granted for a term of seven years or less an overriding interest, binding a buyer without any entry on the register. The lease here is a five year legal lease, so Rory does not need to show occupation or register it. Leases of more than seven years are registrable dispositions that must be completed by registration to take effect at law. A notice would be needed to protect an equitable lease, not this one."}, {"id": 1177, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Priya Sharma was wrongly strip searched by police officers on 4 March. She spent several months abroad caring for a sick relative and did not consult a solicitor until 10 June of the following year, which is fifteen months later. She wishes to bring a claim against the police under the Human Rights Act 1998 for breach of article 8. The police say she is out of time. Which of the following best states the position under section 7?", "options": {"A": "The claim must be brought within six years of the act complained of, as for most claims in tort or contract", "B": "The claim must normally be brought within one year of the act, but the court may allow a longer period if it considers that equitable", "C": "The claim must be brought within three months of the act complained of, as for judicial review, and the court has no discretion to extend", "D": "The claim must be brought within three years of the act, which is the period for personal injury claims in negligence", "E": "The claim must be brought within one year, and the court has no power to extend that period"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim must normally be brought within one year of the act, but the court may allow a longer period if it considers that equitable): Section 7(5) of the Human Rights Act provides that proceedings must be brought within one year beginning with the date of the act complained of, or within such longer period as the court considers equitable having regard to all the circumstances. Priya is outside the year, but her absence abroad caring for a relative may support an extension. The three month rule belongs to judicial review, and the six year and three year periods belong to other kinds of claim."}, {"id": 1178, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Dr Fiona Marsh sold her dental practice in a market town to Brightsmile Dental Ltd for £400,000, including £250,000 for goodwill. The sale agreement contains her promise not to practise dentistry within five miles of the practice for three years. Brightsmile's patients come from within five miles. Eighteen months later Fiona opened a clinic two miles away. Brightsmile seeks to enforce the covenant. Which of the following best describes the likely position?", "options": {"A": "The covenant is enforceable for twelve months only, because the law limits post sale restrictions on a seller to one year", "B": "The covenant is likely to be enforced, because it protects the purchased goodwill and is no wider than reasonably necessary", "C": "The covenant is void, because the court applies the strict test for employees and Fiona's livelihood is affected by a restriction of three years", "D": "The covenant is enforceable, but only if Brightsmile can show Fiona has taken patients away from it by name", "E": "The covenant is void, because covenants in restraint of trade are unenforceable unless supported by separate consideration"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The covenant is likely to be enforced, because it protects the purchased goodwill and is no wider than reasonably necessary): A restraint of trade is enforceable if it protects a legitimate interest and goes no further than reasonably necessary. On a sale of a business the buyer has a real interest in protecting the goodwill it paid for, and the courts give such covenants more latitude than employment covenants. Five miles matching the patient catchment and three years are likely to be upheld. No statute caps the period and no proof of actual poaching is needed."}, {"id": 1179, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ella Fraser is arrested for criminal damage and asks for a solicitor. The custody officer does not authorise any delay, but the interviewing officer starts the interview without waiting for the solicitor, who is on the way. Ella says no comment to every question. At trial she gives evidence that the damage was caused by a neighbour, which she never mentioned. The prosecution applies for an inference under section 34. What is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "An inference may be drawn only if the jury is satisfied that she remained silent in order to mislead the police", "B": "No inference can be drawn, because section 34 applies only to suspects who handed over a prepared statement before the interview", "C": "No inference can be drawn, because section 34 applies only if she also stays silent at the trial itself", "D": "An inference may be drawn, because her silence was a failure to mention a fact later relied on and the caution gave her adequate warning", "E": "No inference can be drawn, because section 34 does not apply where the accused was not allowed to consult a solicitor before questioning"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No inference can be drawn, because section 34 does not apply where the accused was not allowed to consult a solicitor before questioning): Section 34(2A) provides that no inference may be drawn from silence at interview where the accused was not allowed an opportunity to consult a solicitor before being questioned. Ella asked for a solicitor and no delay had been authorised, so the interview should not have started without waiting. The general rule that failure to mention a relied-upon fact can attract an inference is therefore displaced. The inference does not depend on proof of an intention to mislead, nor on the presence of a prepared statement."}, {"id": 1180, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya Menon is charged with criminal damage to a parked car. A repair estimate puts the cost of the damage at £3,200. The offence is not charged as arson and no one was endangered. At her first hearing before the magistrates' court, her solicitor is asked how the offence is classified for the purpose of mode of trial. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "It is triable only summarily, because Priya has not yet entered a plea and so the court must keep the case", "B": "It is triable only summarily, because criminal damage valued at £5,000 or less is treated as a summary offence", "C": "It is triable either way, but only if the prosecutor asks for it, because the value limit is merely a guideline", "D": "It is triable only on indictment, because damage to another person's vehicle is aggravated criminal damage", "E": "It is triable either way, because criminal damage is an either way offence whatever the value of the damage"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is triable only summarily, because criminal damage valued at £5,000 or less is treated as a summary offence): Under section 22 of the Magistrates' Courts Act 1980, criminal damage where the value involved is £5,000 or less is treated as triable only summarily, so there is no choice of venue. The exception is damage by fire, which is charged as arson and remains either way. Classification depends on the offence and its value, not on the plea or the prosecutor's wishes. Damage to a car is simple criminal damage, not aggravated criminal damage, unless life is endangered."}, {"id": 1181, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Melissa Hartigan, a solicitor in Norwich, realises that she failed to serve a claim form before it expired, so the client's claim for £50,000 can no longer proceed without an extension that is unlikely to be granted. The client, Ben Osei, knows nothing of this. Melissa's supervisor suggests trying to arrange a settlement with the defendant so the mistake never comes to light. Which of the following is the most appropriate step for Melissa to take?", "options": {"A": "Tell Ben promptly what has gone wrong and its likely effect, and advise him that he may wish to take independent advice", "B": "Follow her supervisor's suggestion, because a settlement would put Ben in no worse a position than winning at trial", "C": "Report the matter to the SRA and wait for its guidance before disclosing anything to Ben", "D": "Try to rescue the claim quietly through an extension application and tell Ben only if the application fails", "E": "Notify the firm's insurers before speaking to Ben, and tell Ben nothing further until the insurers have given their view on the claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell Ben promptly what has gone wrong and its likely effect, and advise him that he may wish to take independent advice): When something has gone wrong, a solicitor must tell the client promptly, explain what happened and its likely impact, and consider what steps can put matters right. The firm now has an own interest conflict, so Ben should be told he may want independent advice. Insurers and the SRA may need to be told, but not instead of the client and not before him. Arranging a settlement to hide the mistake would be dishonest and a breach of the duty of candour."}, {"id": 1182, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Lorna Stedman runs an unregulated will-writing business in Norwich. A client's father has died leaving a valid will, and the client asks Lorna to prepare all the papers needed to apply to the Probate Registry for a grant of probate, for a fee of £600. Lorna's business partner says that because will-writing is unreserved, the work is lawful. The estate includes a house, savings and a small shareholding. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "Lorna acts lawfully, because will-writing is unreserved and preparing the estate papers is part of the same unreserved service", "B": "Lorna acts lawfully, because the executor and not Lorna applies for the grant and she is merely helping the client with forms", "C": "Lorna commits an offence only if she holds herself out as a solicitor, because the offence concerns misleading descriptions of status", "D": "Lorna acts lawfully if she tells the client she is not a solicitor, because disclosure of her status removes the restriction", "E": "Lorna commits an offence, because preparing papers to apply for a grant of probate is reserved and she is not authorised"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Lorna commits an offence, because preparing papers to apply for a grant of probate is reserved and she is not authorised): Probate activities are reserved, and they include preparing papers on which a grant of probate is applied for. Lorna is unauthorised and the work is for a fee, so she risks committing an offence despite her partner's view. Will writing is unreserved, but that does not carry over to the later estate application. Telling the client she is not a solicitor does not remove the restriction, and the offence does not depend on pretending to be one."}, {"id": 1183, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Vale Interiors Ltd sued Pike Builders Ltd for £28,000 for unpaid fitting out work. Pike filed and served a defence denying the debt on the due date. Three weeks later, Pike's solicitor realises that Pike also has a claim of £15,000 against Vale for defective earlier work and wants to bring it as a counterclaim in the same proceedings. No directions have been given and no case management conference has taken place. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Pike may file the counterclaim without permission at any time before the first case management conference, as of right", "B": "Pike may file the counterclaim without permission within 14 days of the defence being served, as a matter of course", "C": "Pike must bring separate proceedings, because a counterclaim cannot be added once the defence has been served", "D": "Pike needs Vale's written consent, and no court permission is then required, because the claim is within the same dispute", "E": "Pike needs the court's permission, because a counterclaim is filed without permission only if filed with the defence"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Pike needs the court's permission, because a counterclaim is filed without permission only if filed with the defence): A defendant may make a counterclaim without the court's permission only if it is filed with the defence. After that, permission is required, and the court decides whether to allow it. Pike's counterclaim is out of time for the as of right route, though the court is likely to look favourably on one that arises from related dealings. A counterclaim is not barred altogether, so separate proceedings are unnecessary, and the opponent's consent does not remove the need for permission."}, {"id": 1184, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Hartley Estates granted Pria a headlease of a unit for 15 years, containing a tenant's covenant to use it only as offices. Pria sublet to Quinn for five years, and Quinn's sublease says nothing about use. Quinn, who has seen the headlease, has opened a late-night bar in the unit. Hartley wishes to stop the use and asks whether it can sue Quinn directly. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Hartley can enforce the covenant against Quinn through privity of estate, because the headlease and the sublease are linked tenancies of the same unit", "B": "Hartley can sue Quinn directly on the covenant, because the burden of tenant covenants passes to every person in occupation of the premises", "C": "Hartley has no claim against Quinn, because a restrictive covenant in a lease binds only the original tenant", "D": "Hartley has no privity with Quinn, but can enforce the restrictive user covenant against him in equity because he has notice of it", "E": "Hartley has no direct claim against Quinn and can pursue only forfeiture of the headlease or a claim against Pria"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hartley has no privity with Quinn, but can enforce the restrictive user covenant against him in equity because he has notice of it): There is no privity of contract or estate between a head landlord and a subtenant, so Hartley cannot sue Quinn on the covenant as a matter of contract or estate. A negative covenant in the headlease can nonetheless bind a subtenant in equity under the principle in Tulk v Moxhay where he has notice, and Quinn has seen the headlease. Hartley can therefore seek an injunction against him as well as consider forfeiture or a claim against Pria."}, {"id": 1185, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Kingsley Law acts jointly for two friends, Owen Tait and Ibrahim Malik, in setting up a limited company to run a cafe. During a private call, Owen tells the solicitor that he has already agreed a side arrangement with the landlord that will give him a personal share of the lease profits, and asks that Ibrahim is not told. Which of the following is the correct course for the solicitor?", "options": {"A": "Continue to act for Owen alone, because he is the client who raised the concern, and ask Ibrahim to instruct others", "B": "Disclose the arrangement to Ibrahim at once without speaking to Owen, because Ibrahim's interests outweigh Owen's wish for secrecy", "C": "Keep the information confidential from Ibrahim and continue to act for both, because Owen told the solicitor it was private", "D": "Explain to Owen that the information cannot be kept from Ibrahim and, if he will not agree to disclosure, stop acting for both", "E": "Carry on for both, but record the secret in a sealed note on the file, since separate records protect each client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Explain to Owen that the information cannot be kept from Ibrahim and, if he will not agree to disclosure, stop acting for both): In a joint retainer there are no secrets between the joint clients, because the firm must disclose to each client all information material to the matter. The solicitor should explain this to Owen and seek his agreement to disclose. If he refuses, the conflict cannot be managed and the firm must stop acting for both. A sealed note does nothing to cure the problem, and disclosing without warning Owen or dropping only Ibrahim would be unfair to one of the clients."}, {"id": 1186, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Neil, the executor named in his late mother's will, has instructed a solicitor to apply for probate. While clearing the house, he found a later will in a drawer, signed and witnessed, which leaves most of the estate to a charity. Neil says the earlier will is more generous to him and asks the solicitor to apply on the earlier will and say nothing about the later one. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the later will and apply on the earlier will, because the solicitor may rely on the executor's honesty", "B": "Apply on the earlier will but tell the charity privately, because the solicitor must protect the charity as a third party", "C": "Advise Neil that the later will must be disclosed and the solicitor cannot apply on the earlier will, and stop acting if he refuses", "D": "Apply on the earlier will and disclose the later will only if the charity makes a claim", "E": "Apply on the earlier will as instructed, because the executor is the client and the solicitor must follow lawful instructions"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise Neil that the later will must be disclosed and the solicitor cannot apply on the earlier will, and stop acting if he refuses): A solicitor owes a duty to the court and must not mislead it or be complicit in concealing a will. The later will may revoke the earlier one, so applying on the earlier will knowing of the later one would be misleading. The solicitor must advise Neil that it has to be disclosed and, if he will not agree, must stop acting. Confidentiality does not permit going behind the client to the charity."}, {"id": 1187, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr and Mrs Yates were in a car accident. Mr Yates, who had made no will, died at the scene. Mrs Yates died in hospital twenty days later, without having made a will. They had one child, Zoe, aged 30. Mr Yates's estate was £400,000 after debts. Mrs Yates's family say her estate should receive the spouse's entitlement from his estate. Is this correct?", "options": {"A": "No, because the spouse takes only if the couple had no children, so Zoe takes everything", "B": "Yes, because survival for twenty days is enough where the deaths result from the same accident", "C": "Yes, but only the personal chattels, because a spouse who survives for any period takes those outright", "D": "No, because a spouse must survive the intestate by twenty-eight days to benefit, so Zoe takes the whole estate", "E": "Yes, because Mrs Yates survived her husband, so her estate takes the statutory legacy and half the residue of his estate under the intestacy rules"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because a spouse must survive the intestate by twenty-eight days to benefit, so Zoe takes the whole estate): For intestacy, a spouse must survive the intestate by twenty-eight days, otherwise the spouse is treated as having predeceased. Mrs Yates survived only twenty days, so she is treated as having died first. The estate therefore passes to Zoe as the only issue. A spouse can take alongside children, so the existence of Zoe is not the reason."}, {"id": 1188, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Eastgate Couriers agreed to deliver a sealed bundle of documents for £60 to a council by noon on Friday. The sender, Fenwick Construction, told Eastgate only that the documents were important and the deadline firm. Eastgate delivered at 3 pm, and the council rejected Fenwick's £2 million tender as late. Fenwick would have earned £150,000 profit had it won the tender. It sues Eastgate for that profit. There is no exclusion clause. Which of the following is the best view?", "options": {"A": "Fenwick can recover the profit, because Eastgate was told the deadline was firm and so knew that the sender would lose something of value on lateness", "B": "Fenwick is unlikely to recover the profit, because Eastgate did not know the bundle was a tender and could not have contemplated that loss", "C": "Fenwick can recover the profit, because the lateness was the factual cause of the lost tender and that causation is enough for liability", "D": "Fenwick can recover the profit, because loss of profit from a lost contract is a loss arising naturally from late delivery in the usual course of things", "E": "Fenwick can recover nothing, because remoteness defeats any claim where the sum in question is large compared with the £60 fee"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fenwick is unlikely to recover the profit, because Eastgate did not know the bundle was a tender and could not have contemplated that loss): A loss must be caused by the breach and also be within the reasonable contemplation of the parties, either as arising naturally or because of special facts made known to the defendant. A firm deadline and importance do not tell a courier that a huge tender is at stake. Loss of a contract of this size does not arise naturally from late delivery of documents. Factual causation is necessary but does not decide remoteness, and the fee size is not the test."}, {"id": 1189, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Isla Brennan stands quietly in a market square holding a placard criticising a local council policy. A crowd gathers, and some onlookers begin shouting threats and moving towards her. A police constable, fearing violence, tells Isla to leave. Isla refuses because she has committed no offence and is on public land. The constable arrests her for breach of the peace. Isla asks a solicitor whether the arrest was lawful. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "The arrest was lawful, because the constable honestly feared violence, and an honest belief alone makes the arrest valid", "B": "The arrest was lawful, because an officer may arrest anyone whose presence makes a breach of the peace imminent, whatever fault lies with that person", "C": "The arrest was unlawful, because a breach of the peace requires that violence has already occurred in the presence of the constable", "D": "The arrest was lawful, because any person who refuses a police request to move on commits a breach of the peace by refusing", "E": "The arrest was probably unlawful, because Isla acted reasonably and lawfully, so the constable should have acted against those threatening violence"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The arrest was probably unlawful, because Isla acted reasonably and lawfully, so the constable should have acted against those threatening violence): A person acting lawfully and reasonably is not arrested for a breach of the peace that is threatened by the hostile reaction of others, unless that person's own conduct is unreasonable, as Redmond-Bate v DPP shows. Isla was peacefully expressing a view, so the proper response was to deal with those making threats. A breach of the peace can be anticipated, which is why the argument that violence must already have occurred fails. Refusing a request to move is not itself a breach of the peace."}, {"id": 1190, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Under the convention of collective ministerial responsibility, the Cabinet is expected to speak with one voice. Maya Bhatt, a Cabinet minister, disagrees privately with a Cabinet decision to cut a transport subsidy. After the decision, she is asked on a radio programme what she thinks of it. A trainee asks you what the convention expects of her, for inclusion in a short explainer on how government works in the UK. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She may criticise the decision openly, because each minister is answerable individually to Parliament for her own department", "B": "She may vote against the decision in the Commons, because a minister remains a Member of Parliament with a free vote", "C": "She may criticise the decision if she first informs the Prime Minister, because that preserves the principle of united government", "D": "She should publicly support the decision, or resign if she cannot, because Cabinet members are bound to a united public position", "E": "She must resign immediately, because disagreement with any Cabinet decision breaches a legal rule that the courts enforce"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She should publicly support the decision, or resign if she cannot, because Cabinet members are bound to a united public position): Collective responsibility is a convention under which Cabinet decisions are supported publicly by all ministers, even those who argued against them in private. A minister who cannot support a decision is expected to resign. Individual ministerial responsibility concerns accountability to Parliament for a minister's own department and does not permit public dissent from Cabinet decisions. Conventions are political rules, so the courts do not enforce them."}, {"id": 1191, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Hollis Ltd has agreed to buy a warehouse from Penrose Ltd. The contract includes a special condition stating that the buyer bears the risk of loss or damage from the date of exchange and expressly excludes any right of the buyer to rescind for damage, and the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) otherwise apply. After exchange but before completion, a storm destroys the roof and the warehouse cannot be used for storage. Hollis Ltd has not insured the property. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Hollis Ltd remains bound to complete at the full price, because the special condition displaces the standard right to rescind", "B": "Hollis Ltd may complete at a reduced price, because a seller must give a buyer an allowance for physical damage after exchange", "C": "Penrose Ltd must bear the loss, because the seller holds the legal estate until completion and therefore keeps the risk of damage", "D": "Hollis Ltd may rescind and recover its deposit, because the standard condition applies where the property becomes unusable for its purpose", "E": "The contract is frustrated by the destruction of the roof, because the warehouse can no longer be used in the way both parties intended"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hollis Ltd remains bound to complete at the full price, because the special condition displaces the standard right to rescind): Special conditions override the standard conditions where they conflict. Here the parties agreed that the buyer takes the risk from exchange and expressly excluded the buyer's right to rescind for damage, so the contract remains fully enforceable. Hollis Ltd must therefore complete at the full price and its failure to insure is its own problem. Frustration does not usually apply to a land contract where the risk has been allocated by agreement."}, {"id": 1192, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Eaton's will leaves 'my grand piano to my niece Hester'. Two years before his death he sold the grand piano and bought a smaller upright piano, which he kept in the same room. He did not change his will. After his death, Hester asks the executors for the upright piano or, failing that, the proceeds of the sale. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "Hester takes the sale proceeds, because they represent the piano she was left", "B": "Hester takes the upright piano, because it replaced the original and the testator intended her to have a piano", "C": "Hester takes the upright piano only if the executors agree, because the court treats a replacement as an informal codicil", "D": "The gift is adeemed, because the piano described was not in his estate at death, so Hester takes nothing in its place", "E": "Hester takes the value of the grand piano from residue, because the gift was specific and must be honoured in money"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The gift is adeemed, because the piano described was not in his estate at death, so Hester takes nothing in its place): A specific gift is adeemed if the item no longer forms part of the estate at death. The grand piano was sold, so the gift fails, and the beneficiary has no claim to the replacement or to the proceeds. The upright piano is a different asset not described in the will, and it falls into residue. Nothing in the rules treats a replacement as a codicil."}, {"id": 1193, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Rosalind is a consulting engineer instructed by the claimant's solicitors, Whitcombe LLP, to report on the cause of a collapsed retaining wall. Her draft report concludes that two of the three possible causes favour the defendant. The partner at Whitcombe emails her asking whether she could remove the paragraph on those causes and focus on the cause that helps the claimant, as the client is paying her fee. Rosalind asks the solicitor for guidance. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "Rosalind may leave out the paragraph provided the report states that it covers only the issues chosen by the instructing party", "B": "Rosalind's duty to the court arises only once the report has been served, so until then the solicitor may direct its contents", "C": "Rosalind may remove the paragraph if instructed, because the report belongs to the party who pays her fee until it is served", "D": "Rosalind's overriding duty is to the court, so she must give her honest opinion and cannot be asked to remove it", "E": "Rosalind may soften the paragraph by agreement, because her opinion should reflect the interests of the party who instructed her"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rosalind's overriding duty is to the court, so she must give her honest opinion and cannot be asked to remove it): Under CPR 35.3 an expert's duty is to help the court on matters within her expertise, and this overrides any obligation to the person who instructs or pays her. The solicitor must not seek to influence her opinion or have unfavourable conclusions removed, which would also compromise the solicitor's duty not to mislead the court. A client may choose not to serve a report at all, subject to privilege, but cannot shape the expert's honest view of it. The duty to the court exists from instruction, not from service."}, {"id": 1194, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Ben owns a house registered in his sole name, bought with his own money before he met Cat. Cat moved in two years later and, over time, paid for a new kitchen costing £8,000 and contributed to household bills. They never discussed who would own the house. After they separate, Cat claims a beneficial interest. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Cat has a share proportionate to her kitchen spending, because spending on improvements founds a beneficial share", "B": "Cat has an interest by proprietary estoppel, because her spending on the kitchen was relied on in the house", "C": "Cat has no constructive trust interest, because bills and improvements do not show a common intention to share", "D": "Cat has a share under a resulting trust, because her kitchen payment was an indirect contribution to the purchase price", "E": "Cat has an equal share under a constructive trust, because she lived there as a partner and paid household expenses"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cat has no constructive trust interest, because bills and improvements do not show a common intention to share): Where the home is in one name, the claimant must show a common intention to share, either by express discussion or by inference from direct contributions to the price or mortgage. Cat made neither, and household bills and a later kitchen are not enough to infer that intention (Lloyd v Rosset). A resulting trust is excluded because her spending came long after purchase. There was also no assurance by Ben, so proprietary estoppel is not made out."}, {"id": 1195, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Rachel and Sam bought a house in joint names for £300,000. Rachel paid £240,000 and Sam paid £60,000. The transfer form states that they hold the property on trust for themselves as beneficial joint tenants. There was no mistake or undue influence when it was signed. After separation Rachel claims 80 per cent of the proceeds of sale. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court decides a fair share, because the declaration is only one factor in the whole course of dealing", "B": "Rachel takes 80 per cent, because a declaration of equal shares is overridden by unequal contributions to the price", "C": "The declaration is void, because beneficial interests in land must be declared in a separate deed from the transfer", "D": "Rachel takes 80 per cent, because a resulting trust arises in proportion to contributions despite the signed form", "E": "Rachel is held to the declaration, so shares are equal, because it is conclusive absent fraud or mistake"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rachel is held to the declaration, so shares are equal, because it is conclusive absent fraud or mistake): Where the legal owners expressly declare their beneficial interests in a signed document, the declaration is conclusive in the absence of fraud or mistake (Goodman v Gallant). Resulting and constructive trust analysis and inference of intention from conduct come into play only when there is no express declaration. Rachel's larger contribution is therefore irrelevant on these facts. The signed form is valid, and no separate deed is needed."}, {"id": 1196, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Tahlia Booth holds a lease of a workshop granted to her in 2022 for a term ending on 28 February 2032. The lease prohibits assignment entirely but permits underletting of the whole with the landlord's consent. Tahlia obtains the landlord's consent and grants Orla Fenwick a lease of the whole workshop starting on 1 March 2026 for a term ending on 28 February 2032, reserving a rent payable to Tahlia. Orla Fenwick moves in. Which of the following best describes the effect of the transaction?", "options": {"A": "It takes effect as an underlease, because the tenant keeps the right to receive rent from Orla Fenwick under the new letting", "B": "It takes effect as an underlease, because its commencement date is later than the date on which the head lease was granted", "C": "It takes effect as an assignment, because a grant of the whole of the remaining term leaves the tenant without any reversion", "D": "It takes effect as an assignment, but only if Orla Fenwick also takes over the tenant's repairing covenants expressly", "E": "It takes effect as an underlease, because the parties have called it a sublease and their intention governs how the transaction is classified in law"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It takes effect as an assignment, because a grant of the whole of the remaining term leaves the tenant without any reversion): A lease granted out of another lease is an underlease only if it is for a term shorter than the tenant's own term, so that the tenant keeps a reversion. Here Tahlia has granted the whole of her remaining term, so nothing is left to revert to her and the transaction is in law an assignment. Reserving a rent and calling the document an underlease do not change that, because the classification depends on the length of the term granted. The lease prohibits assignment entirely, so the transaction is a breach of covenant even though the landlord consented to an underletting."}, {"id": 1197, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Trustee Quin transferred £40,000 of trust money to his nephew Ray in breach of trust. Ray knew at the time that it was trust money and that his uncle had no right to give it away. Ray spent the whole sum on an expensive holiday and holds no assets traceable to it. The beneficiaries want to recover the money. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Ray is liable only for any profit he made, because a recipient who spends the money has nothing left to account for", "B": "The beneficiaries can claim only against Quin, because a recipient is liable only if he holds the money when sued", "C": "Ray has a proprietary claim to the holiday, because the trust money was used to buy it", "D": "Ray is personally liable to restore £40,000 as constructive trustee, as the money is spent and cannot be traced", "E": "Ray is not liable, because he did not dishonestly assist the breach and so cannot be personally liable"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ray is personally liable to restore £40,000 as constructive trustee, as the money is spent and cannot be traced): Ray received trust property for his own benefit with knowledge that made retention unconscionable, so he is personally liable as a constructive trustee for knowing receipt. His liability is to restore the value received, and it does not depend on his still holding the money. A proprietary claim by tracing fails because the money was spent on an experience that left no asset. Dishonest assistance is a separate head of liability and is not required here."}, {"id": 1198, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Jon is the sole trustee of a trust of land. He executes a single deed appointing two new trustees, Kim, who is aged 17, and Lara, who is aged 30. The trust instrument is silent on the appointment of trustees. Jon intends the new trustees to take over the management of the land so that he can retire next year. A buyer has also shown interest in a sale of the land. Which of the following best states the effect of the deed?", "options": {"A": "Kim's appointment is valid but she holds only a nominal office until she is eighteen, while Lara's appointment is also valid", "B": "The appointment of Kim is void because a minor cannot hold the office of trustee, but the appointment of Lara is valid", "C": "Both appointments are void, because they were made in a single deed and one of them was invalid", "D": "Both appointments are valid, because Kim will become a full trustee on reaching eighteen without any further act by Jon", "E": "Neither appointment is valid without a court order, because a sole trustee cannot exercise a power of appointment"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The appointment of Kim is void because a minor cannot hold the office of trustee, but the appointment of Lara is valid): The appointment of a minor as trustee is void, so Kim does not become a trustee and does not take office on her eighteenth birthday. The appointment of Lara is a separate valid act and is not affected by the invalid appointment of Kim. A sole trustee can use the statutory power of appointment without a court order. Jon should therefore take care to appoint a second adult trustee if the land is to be sold."}, {"id": 1199, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Catherine Doyle's will, made in 2019, leaves 'my Ford Fiesta registration number LK19 ABC' to her godson Jamie and the residue to her sister. In 2024 Catherine sold the Fiesta and bought a Toyota Yaris with part of the proceeds, which she still owned when she died in 2026. The will was not changed and there is no codicil. Jamie asks the solicitor whether he is entitled to the Toyota or to the money Catherine received for the Fiesta. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Jamie takes the money received from the sale of the Fiesta, because the proceeds of a specifically given asset are traced into the estate", "B": "Jamie takes the value of the Fiesta from the residue, because a failed specific gift is converted into a pecuniary legacy", "C": "Jamie takes the Toyota only if the executors can show that Catherine intended it to replace the Fiesta", "D": "Jamie takes the Toyota, because a gift of a car of that kind passes whatever vehicle the testator owns at death as a replacement", "E": "Jamie takes nothing, because the specific gift adeems once the Fiesta is no longer in the estate and does not extend to a replacement car"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Jamie takes nothing, because the specific gift adeems once the Fiesta is no longer in the estate and does not extend to a replacement car): A specific gift adeems if the item is no longer part of the estate at death, and the legatee takes nothing in its place. Catherine sold the Fiesta, so Jamie's gift fails, and the Toyota is a different asset that the will does not describe. The sale proceeds and the residue are not substituted for a failed specific gift. Intention to replace is not enough to save it, so the Toyota falls into the residue."}, {"id": 1200, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "On 10 June 2019 a trustee invests trust money in a way that is not authorised by the trust instrument, causing a loss of £50,000. The beneficiary, Ben, is an adult with an immediate entitlement to income. Ben did not learn of the breach until 2024 and issues proceedings in September 2026. There is no fraud and nothing was concealed. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "The claim is in time, because the period for breach of trust claims is twelve years, as it is for claims involving land", "B": "The claim is in time, because the six year period runs from the date on which the beneficiary first discovered the breach", "C": "The claim is out of time only if the trustee pleads laches and proves that Ben delayed unreasonably in bringing proceedings", "D": "The claim is in time, because there is no limitation period for a claim against a trustee", "E": "The claim is out of time, because the period is six years from the breach and the beneficiary's lack of knowledge does not postpone it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The claim is out of time, because the period is six years from the breach and the beneficiary's lack of knowledge does not postpone it): Section 21(3) Limitation Act 1980 sets a six year period for breach of trust claims, running from the date of the breach. Ben's interest is immediate, so time runs from June 2019 and expired in June 2025. Postponement for knowledge applies only to fraud, concealment or mistake, which are not present here. The claim in September 2026 is therefore out of time."}, {"id": 1201, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Larch Ltd, a private company. The directors instruct you to draft a payment of £80,000 to Ravi, the managing director, who is leaving after the board decided to ask him to resign. The sum is a gift and not required by his contract, which separately entitles him to six months' pay in lieu of notice worth £30,000. The directors say the members will be told after the payment is made. What advice should you give?", "options": {"A": "The £80,000 needs approval by special resolution of the members, but the contractual notice pay needs no approval of any kind", "B": "Neither payment needs member approval, because the board may authorise payments to a departing director and report them afterwards", "C": "The £80,000 needs member approval, but this may be given after payment because members can ratify the board's decision", "D": "The £80,000 needs prior approval by ordinary resolution of the members, the contractual notice pay does not, and nothing should be paid first", "E": "Both payments need prior member approval by ordinary resolution, because each is made in connection with Ravi's loss of office as managing director"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The £80,000 needs prior approval by ordinary resolution of the members, the contractual notice pay does not, and nothing should be paid first): A payment to a director for loss of office needs prior approval of the members by ordinary resolution, unless it discharges a legal obligation. The £30,000 notice pay is a contractual entitlement so it is outside the rule, but the £80,000 gift is not. Approval must come before payment, so telling members afterwards does not work. A solicitor should not draft or facilitate the payment until the approval is obtained."}, {"id": 1202, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Four partners run a pub in a partnership. Their agreement says no partner may sign a contract worth over £5,000 without the consent of all partners. Ngozi, without asking the others, signs a £20,000 contract with Delta Builders to refit the pub kitchen. Delta did not know of the restriction. Kitchen refits are a normal part of running a pub. The other partners refuse to pay. Is the firm bound?", "options": {"A": "Yes, but only if Delta proves that the other partners later affirmed the contract by conduct after it was made", "B": "Yes, because Ngozi acted in the usual way of the firm's business and Delta did not know of her lack of authority", "C": "No, because a contract worth more than £5,000 needs the consent of every partner for the firm to be bound", "D": "No, because the agreement limits her authority and the restriction binds the firm whether or not Delta knew of it", "E": "Yes, but only Ngozi is personally liable, because she acted outside her authority under the partnership agreement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because Ngozi acted in the usual way of the firm's business and Delta did not know of her lack of authority): Under section 5 of the Partnership Act 1890 each partner is the firm's agent for acts done in the usual way of its business, and the firm is bound. The exception applies only if the partner had no authority and the other party knew that or did not believe her to be a partner. Delta did not know of the restriction, so the internal limit does not protect the firm. All partners are liable on the contract."}, {"id": 1203, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Albion Close Farm is registered land. In 2012 the then owner granted the owner of the neighbouring registered field, Darius, an express right of way over the farm track by a properly executed deed, but no application was ever made to register it. Fiona bought the farm in September 2023 for value and was never told of the right. Darius last used the track in May 2022, and the track is lightly worn. Fiona now blocks the track. Which of the following is the most accurate analysis of whether the easement binds her?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because Darius has acquired the easement by long use and prescription takes effect independently of registration", "B": "Yes, because Darius holds a legal easement and every legal easement is an overriding interest whether or not it is used", "C": "No, because Fiona bought without notice and a legal easement binds only a buyer who knew about it", "D": "Yes, because the easement was expressly granted by deed and its existence is obvious on inspection of the worn track", "E": "No, because the unregistered grant is equitable only, and a buyer for value takes free of it without a notice"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No, because the unregistered grant is equitable only, and a buyer for value takes free of it without a notice): Since the Land Registration Act 2002, an express easement over registered land takes effect at law only when the grant is completed by registration (section 27(2)(d)). Darius never registered the 2012 grant, so it takes effect in equity only. Equitable easements are not overriding under Schedule 3 paragraph 3, which is confined to legal easements, and no notice was entered. Fiona, a purchaser for value, therefore takes free under section 29. Darius might have a different remedy against his grantor, but not against Fiona."}, {"id": 1204, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Ruth Okeke lives with her husband, Paolo, in an unregistered house which Paolo bought in his sole name. Ruth contributed to the price and has a beneficial interest under a trust of land. Paolo, as sole legal owner, sells to Anna Briggs, who pays all the money to Paolo, inspects the house once while Ruth is out, and is told by Paolo that no one else has any interest. Ruth's belongings are visible throughout the house. Is Anna bound by Ruth's interest?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because every beneficial interest under a trust of land binds a buyer of unregistered land whatever the buyer knew", "B": "Yes, because the interest is not overreached and Anna had constructive notice from signs of Ruth's occupation", "C": "No, because Anna paid the price to a sole legal owner, which overreaches the beneficial interest in the same way as two trustees", "D": "No, because Ruth's beneficial interest is a registrable land charge and was not registered, so it is void against Anna", "E": "No, because Anna was a bona fide purchaser who relied on Paolo's assurance, and a seller's assurance removes any duty to inquire further"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because the interest is not overreached and Anna had constructive notice from signs of Ruth's occupation): In unregistered land, an equitable interest that is not registrable as a land charge is governed by the doctrine of notice. A buyer is bound unless she is a bona fide purchaser of the legal estate for value without actual, constructive or imputed notice. Payment to one trustee does not overreach. A careful inspection revealed Ruth's occupation, and Anna should have asked Ruth rather than rely on Paolo's word, so Anna is fixed with constructive notice. A beneficial interest under a trust is not a land charge, so section 4 of the Land Charges Act 1972 is not engaged."}, {"id": 1205, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Chidi was one of five trustees of the newly formed Greenfield Foundation trust. On his deathbed he said in front of witnesses, 'I give all my shares in Orion Ltd to the Greenfield Foundation trust.' He signed no transfer document, and the other four trustees had no part in any transfer. After he died, his family argued that the gift failed because the shares were never transferred to all five trustees. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The gift takes effect only if the other four trustees formally accept the shares after Chidi's death", "B": "The gift fails, because shares can pass only by signed transfer to all the trustees and equity will not perfect an incomplete gift", "C": "The gift takes effect, because Chidi was himself a trustee, so his words bound his conscience as trustee of the foundation", "D": "The gift takes effect, because words of a deathbed gift alone are enough to transfer any asset to the intended donee", "E": "The gift fails, because a donor cannot be both a trustee and the settlor of the same trust property"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The gift takes effect, because Chidi was himself a trustee, so his words bound his conscience as trustee of the foundation): Under Choithram v Pagarani, where a donor who is one of the trustees declares a gift to the trust, equity treats the gift as made to all the trustees and the donor's conscience binds him to hold as trustee. Chidi held legal title to the shares, so the trust was constituted without any further transfer. The family's argument about a transfer to all five fails because the donor himself became a trustee of the trust property. A mere oral gift of an asset would not otherwise work, and later acceptance is not needed."}, {"id": 1206, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A Minister makes the Widgets (Safety) Regulations 2026 under an enabling Act that provides that the regulations are subject to the negative procedure. The regulations are laid before Parliament and come into force on the date stated in them. A trade association asks a solicitor what that procedure means for the regulations during the following weeks. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The regulations cannot take effect until both Houses have approved them by a positive resolution after a debate on the draft", "B": "The regulations lapse after forty days unless Parliament passes a further Act confirming them within that period of time", "C": "The regulations take effect as made and remain in force unless either House passes a motion to annul them within the statutory period", "D": "The regulations become law only once the Minister has obtained the Royal Assent to the statutory instrument from the Crown", "E": "The regulations remain in force unless the House of Lords alone votes to reject them within forty days, because the Commons has no role in annulment"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The regulations take effect as made and remain in force unless either House passes a motion to annul them within the statutory period): Under the negative procedure the instrument is made, laid and takes effect without any vote, but either House may pass a prayer to annul it within the statutory period, usually forty days. The enabling Act chose this procedure, so no positive approval is needed before commencement. The tempting alternative describes the affirmative procedure, which applies only where the Act demands approval of a draft or of the instrument. Royal Assent is given to Acts and not to statutory instruments."}, {"id": 1207, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Karim holds 15% of the shares in Silverline Ltd, a company run by him and two friends as a quasi-partnership on the understanding that all three would work in the business. The two friends have now excluded Karim from management and stopped his salary. He petitions under section 994 of the Companies Act 2006 and succeeds. What is the most common order a court would make?", "options": {"A": "An order that the majority buy Karim's shares, valued at a discount to reflect the size of his minority holding", "B": "An order that the majority buy Karim's shares at a fair price, normally valued pro rata without a discount for his minority holding", "C": "An order that the company be wound up, because winding up is the first remedy a court considers on a successful petition by an excluded member", "D": "An order that the directors pay Karim personally compensation for his lost salary, with no change to his shareholding", "E": "An order that the company reinstate Karim as a director for a fixed term, with his salary restored"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An order that the majority buy Karim's shares at a fair price, normally valued pro rata without a discount for his minority holding): On a successful section 994 petition the court has wide discretion, and by far the most common remedy is an order that the majority buy out the petitioner. In a quasi-partnership the shares are normally valued pro rata, without a minority discount, because the exclusion caused the problem. Winding up is a last resort, and reinstatement or personal compensation are rarely the order made."}, {"id": 1208, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "The magistrates' court grants bail to Frank Leigh, who is charged with burglary of a dwelling, at the end of a hearing at 11.30 am on a Wednesday. The prosecutor, who believes that Frank may interfere with a witness, wishes to appeal against the decision to the Crown Court. Frank is due to be released unless the prosecution acts. What must the prosecution do?", "options": {"A": "Give written notice within 24 hours and apply to the High Court, because only that court can reverse a grant of bail", "B": "Give written notice to the Crown Court within seven days, with Frank remaining on bail until the appeal is heard", "C": "Give oral notice at the end of the hearing and written notice within two hours, with Frank kept in custody pending the appeal", "D": "Give oral notice at the end of the hearing and have the appeal heard within 14 days, with Frank released meanwhile", "E": "Obtain Frank's agreement to the appeal, because bail granted by the magistrates for an imprisonable offence is otherwise final"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give oral notice at the end of the hearing and written notice within two hours, with Frank kept in custody pending the appeal): Under the Bail (Amendment) Act 1993 the prosecution may appeal to the Crown Court against a grant of bail for an offence punishable with imprisonment. It must give oral notice at the end of the hearing and serve written notice within two hours, and Frank is kept in custody until the appeal is determined. The appeal must be heard within 48 hours, excluding weekends and bank holidays. The High Court route and the seven day notice period are not part of this procedure, and the defendant's agreement is not needed."}, {"id": 1209, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Elena Costa is charged with robbery of a shopkeeper and, from the same incident, with assault occasioning actual bodily harm on that shopkeeper, an either-way offence. She appears before the magistrates' court, which sends the robbery to the Crown Court for trial under section 51 of the Crime and Disorder Act 1998. The prosecutor asks what should happen to the assault charge. What is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "It must be withdrawn, because an either-way assault cannot be joined with a robbery in one set of proceedings", "B": "It stays in the magistrates' court for allocation, because each charge is dealt with separately by its own procedure and the robbery does not affect it", "C": "It is sent only if Elena indicates a not guilty plea to the assault at the plea before venue stage", "D": "It is sent only if the prosecution applies for transfer of the assault within a short period after the first hearing", "E": "It must also be sent to the Crown Court, because it is an either-way offence that appears related to the indictable-only robbery"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It must also be sent to the Crown Court, because it is an either-way offence that appears related to the indictable-only robbery): Under section 51, once an adult is sent for an indictable-only offence, the court must also send any related either-way or summary offence that is imprisonable or carries disqualification. The assault arose from the same incident, so it is related. No plea indication or allocation decision is required for it. Dealing with the charges apart would defeat the purpose of trying connected matters together."}, {"id": 1210, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "In a civil claim Zara Whitehead, a solicitor, acts for a claimant, Colin Parry, who has signed a witness statement that she served on the other side. Colin now admits to Zara that one paragraph of the statement is untrue, and he refuses to allow it to be corrected. Trial is in three weeks. Zara knows that revealing the reason for withdrawing would breach confidentiality. Which of the following is the correct course for Zara?", "options": {"A": "Continue to act and say nothing, because the statement is Colin's evidence and he is responsible for its content", "B": "Stop acting for Colin, because she cannot continue while he insists on misleading the court, but she must not reveal why", "C": "Continue to act but avoid relying on the false paragraph at trial, since she herself will not have misled the court", "D": "Continue to act if Colin gives a written assurance that he will give truthful oral evidence at trial about the matter", "E": "Tell the court that the paragraph is untrue, because the duty to the court overrides her duty of confidentiality to Colin"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop acting for Colin, because she cannot continue while he insists on misleading the court, but she must not reveal why): A solicitor must not mislead the court or knowingly allow it to be misled. Zara served a statement she now knows to be partly false, and Colin will not correct it, so she cannot continue to act for him. Her duty of confidentiality still prevents her from telling the court or the other side why she has stopped. Continuing while leaving the false paragraph in place, or taking a promise about later oral evidence, would not undo the misleading statement."}, {"id": 1211, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Home Secretary decides to remove Imran Siddiqui to a country where he says he faces a real risk of being killed because of his political activities. The evidence on risk is mixed, and the Home Secretary's decision letter gives little weight to some reports. Imran's solicitor intends to rely on common law irrationality only, as he has not pleaded any Convention right. A colleague asks what standard of review will apply. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court may decide the merits of the risk afresh and substitute its own finding, because a fundamental right is engaged", "B": "Irrationality applies but with anxious scrutiny, because life is at stake, so the decision needs more compelling justification", "C": "The court applies proportionality, but only if a Convention right is pleaded, so the claim fails without it", "D": "Judicial review is not available, because immigration decisions are excluded from the supervisory jurisdiction of the Administrative Court", "E": "Ordinary Wednesbury review applies in the same way as to any other decision, because the claim is not based on the Human Rights Act"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Irrationality applies but with anxious scrutiny, because life is at stake, so the decision needs more compelling justification): At common law the court reviews for irrationality, but where a decision affects fundamental rights, especially life, it applies anxious scrutiny and requires a more substantial justification before the decision is accepted. This is still a review of lawfulness, so the court does not take over the Home Secretary's role and decide the risk afresh. Immigration decisions are open to judicial review. Heightened scrutiny does not depend on pleading a Convention right, so the claim can proceed on the common law basis."}, {"id": 1212, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Lyle's shop lease was forfeited when his landlord peaceably re-entered after Lyle fell three months behind with rent. Lyle has since borrowed enough to pay all arrears, interest and the landlord's costs, and three weeks after re-entry he asks his solicitor whether the lease can be restored. The landlord has not re-let the shop. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Lyle can apply to the court for relief, likely to be granted if he pays the arrears and costs and acts promptly", "B": "Relief is available only with the landlord's agreement, because the landlord has already recovered possession of the shop", "C": "Lyle must apply within 28 days of the re-entry, after which the court cannot grant relief", "D": "Relief is available only where the landlord has forfeited by court proceedings, so peaceable re-entry cannot be reversed", "E": "Relief is granted automatically once Lyle tenders the arrears and costs, because the court has no discretion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lyle can apply to the court for relief, likely to be granted if he pays the arrears and costs and acts promptly): The court has an equitable jurisdiction to grant relief from forfeiture for non-payment of rent even where the landlord has re-entered peaceably, and the tenant should apply promptly, with about six months being the usual outer limit. Relief is likely where the arrears, interest and costs are paid and no third party has acquired rights, as here. It is discretionary, so it is not automatic, and it does not depend on the landlord's agreement. There is no 28 day cut-off."}, {"id": 1213, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Pemberton Ltd's tenant, Hollis Dental Ltd, holds a five year lease of a surgery that is protected by Part II of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 and expires on 31 December 2025. Pemberton wishes to redevelop the building. On 15 July 2025 it served a section 25 notice on Hollis Dental Ltd stating a termination date of 31 December 2025 and opposing a new tenancy on the redevelopment ground. The notice is otherwise in the prescribed form. Which of the following best describes the effect of the notice?", "options": {"A": "The notice is invalid, but the lease still ends on 31 December 2025 because that is its contractual expiry date under the original lease", "B": "The notice is valid, because the termination date is the date the lease expires by effluxion and no minimum notice applies", "C": "The notice is invalid because it gave under six months' notice, so the tenancy continues under section 24 until a valid notice is served", "D": "The notice is valid, because three months' notice is enough where the landlord opposes a new tenancy on a statutory ground", "E": "The notice is valid but takes effect six months after service, so the tenancy ends on 15 January 2026"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The notice is invalid because it gave under six months' notice, so the tenancy continues under section 24 until a valid notice is served): A landlord's section 25 notice must specify a termination date not less than six nor more than twelve months after the notice is given, and not earlier than the contractual expiry. A notice given on 15 July 2025 for a termination date of 31 December 2025 gives under six months, so it is invalid and cannot be rescued by the ground of opposition. Because the tenancy is protected, it does not end on 31 December and continues under section 24 on the same terms. The landlord must serve a fresh, valid notice, and the court will not treat the defective one as taking effect later."}, {"id": 1214, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A contract between Marbrook Cleaners and Tyne Office Supplies says that Marbrook is 'not liable for any loss or damage to goods in its care'. Tyne's stock of paper was destroyed when a Marbrook employee negligently left a heater switched on. Tyne sues in negligence and for breach of contract. Both parties are businesses and the clause was properly incorporated. Which of the following best states how a court will approach the construction of the clause?", "options": {"A": "The clause is construed neutrally, and the court may add the words needed to reflect the parties' presumed commercial intention", "B": "The clause is construed against Tyne, because it accepted the terms and the burden of showing ambiguity rests on the claimant", "C": "Ambiguity is construed against Marbrook, as the party relying on it, and clear words are needed to cover its own negligence", "D": "The clause is construed in favour of Marbrook, because commercial parties are taken to have allocated risk by the words they chose", "E": "The clause covers negligence, because the words 'any loss or damage' include every cause of loss that might arise"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ambiguity is construed against Marbrook, as the party relying on it, and clear words are needed to cover its own negligence): An exclusion clause is construed strictly and any ambiguity is resolved against the party relying on it. Clear words are needed before the clause will be taken to cover that party's own negligence. General words such as 'any loss or damage' are not enough on their own to protect Marbrook where the loss resulted from its employee's negligence. Courts do not rewrite clauses for the parties."}, {"id": 1215, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A High Court judge has granted an injunction restraining Marlow Estates Ltd from demolishing a listed warehouse pending trial. The company's managing director tells his solicitor that he believes the order was wrongly made and that the diggers will start work on Friday as planned. He wants the solicitor to confirm that nothing will happen to the company because the firm will say it was unaware of the order. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Advise that the company may demolish on Friday if the director believes the order is wrong, and prepare a statement saying the firm was unaware", "B": "Advise that the order binds the company until discharged, decline to assist in any breach, and offer to apply to discharge it or appeal", "C": "Advise that the order is binding only once the judge has signed a written copy served on the director personally by the claimant", "D": "Advise that the company should demolish at once, so that the claim becomes academic and the court will then decline to enforce the order", "E": "Advise the director that any breach is a civil matter only, so continue to act while keeping the demolition plans from the court"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise that the order binds the company until discharged, decline to assist in any breach, and offer to apply to discharge it or appeal): An order of the court binds the party until it is set aside, and a person who believes it is wrong must challenge it by appeal or application and not ignore it. Respect for court orders is part of the rule of law, and a solicitor must not assist a client in a breach or mislead the court, as the SRA Code of Conduct requires. Personal service of a sealed copy is not needed where the company has notice of the terms. The solicitor should therefore decline to take part in the plan and offer a lawful route."}, {"id": 1216, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Contracts for the sale of a freehold house were exchanged on Monday 2 March 2026 on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition). The contract contains no special condition fixing a completion date, and no bank holidays fall in March. The buyer's solicitor asks the seller's solicitor to confirm the completion date. On which date is completion due?", "options": {"A": "Wednesday 1 April 2026, being the first day of the next month after exchange", "B": "Monday 23 March 2026, counting twenty calendar days from the contract and moving to the next working day", "C": "Monday 30 March 2026, the twentieth working day after the date of the contract, with completion due by 2pm", "D": "Friday 27 March 2026, counting the day of the contract itself as the first working day", "E": "Thursday 2 April 2026, being one calendar month after the date of the contract"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Monday 30 March 2026, the twentieth working day after the date of the contract, with completion due by 2pm): Where the contract fixes no date, the Standard Conditions make completion due on the twentieth working day after the date of the contract, and the day of the contract is not counted. Counting forward from Tuesday 3 March gives Monday 30 March. Counting the contract day, using calendar days, or using a calendar month all produce the wrong date, and completion must take place by 2pm to count as that day."}, {"id": 1217, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A city council has for many years held a public consultation with tenants of its housing estates before changing any rent policy, and its published charter says it will do so. The council now proposes to change the rent policy for one estate and says it will simply publish the new policy with immediate effect. A tenants' association asks a solicitor what a procedural legitimate expectation entitles them to. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "It entitles them to damages, because breach of a legitimate expectation is a wrong giving rise to compensation in public law", "B": "It entitles them to keep the existing rent policy in place, because a promise of consultation guarantees the substantive result of the exercise", "C": "It arises only if each tenant relied on the practice to their detriment, because reliance on it is an essential element", "D": "It entitles them to consultation before the change, unless an overriding public interest justifies the council departing from its practice", "E": "It arises only from a written contract between the council and tenants, because a public body is bound only by agreements"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It entitles them to consultation before the change, unless an overriding public interest justifies the council departing from its practice): A clear and consistent practice, backed by a published charter, creates a procedural legitimate expectation that the council will consult before changing course. The expectation protects the process, so the council need only consult, and it may depart from the practice if an overriding public interest justifies it. It does not guarantee the substantive outcome and does not give damages as of right. Reliance is not an essential element, and the expectation can arise from practice without any contract."}, {"id": 1218, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A solicitor at a regulated firm is asked by a long standing client for a one-off piece of advice on a lease for a fee of £600. The client's identity was verified when the relationship began three years ago. During the meeting the solicitor notices that the passport copy on file looks altered and begins to doubt whether the identity documents held are genuine. The firm's policy applies due diligence only on new matters above £5,000. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Due diligence need not be repeated, because the client was verified when the relationship began and the present fee is below the policy threshold", "B": "Due diligence need not be repeated, because it is carried out once at the start of the relationship and updated only at the client's request", "C": "Due diligence is required only if the fee exceeds the threshold for occasional transactions, which applies to existing clients as well", "D": "Due diligence is required only after the solicitor has reported the suspicion and the National Crime Agency has asked for fresh checks", "E": "Due diligence must be repeated, because doubts about the genuineness of identification documents already held trigger the duty, whatever the fee"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Due diligence must be repeated, because doubts about the genuineness of identification documents already held trigger the duty, whatever the fee): Under regulation 27 of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017, a firm must apply customer due diligence when it doubts the veracity or adequacy of documents or information previously obtained for the purpose of identification or verification. That duty arises regardless of the fee, so the internal £5,000 threshold in the firm's policy cannot override it. The occasional transaction threshold concerns one-off clients, not existing relationships. The solicitor should also consider whether the doubts amount to a suspicion that must be reported internally."}, {"id": 1219, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Omar is charged with fraud by false representation after inflating the value of goods on loan applications. At the police station, before interview, he tells his solicitor privately that he knew the figures were false and intended to obtain the loans. He nonetheless wants to plead not guilty and to tell the jury he honestly believed the figures were correct. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Continue to act but only for sentencing, because a client who has confessed cannot lawfully contest guilt at trial", "B": "Withdraw immediately from the case and report the admission to the police, because the solicitor cannot act for a client who has confessed", "C": "Continue to act and allow Omar to give evidence of honest belief, because the solicitor must follow the client's instructions on how to run the defence", "D": "Continue to act and put the prosecution to proof, but refuse to call Omar to give evidence of an honest belief that he has admitted is untrue", "E": "Continue to act and run an affirmative defence of honest belief through cross-examination, relying on the client's admission remaining confidential"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue to act and put the prosecution to proof, but refuse to call Omar to give evidence of an honest belief that he has admitted is untrue): A solicitor must not mislead the court, and cannot put forward a positive case or call evidence they know to be false. A client who admits guilt privately still has the right to plead not guilty and to make the prosecution prove its case, and the solicitor may test the Crown's evidence. The solicitor need not withdraw and must keep the admission confidential. What cannot be done is to assert, or allow the client to assert, an honest belief that the client has said was untrue."}, {"id": 1220, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Neil, a solicitor, is one of two trustees under a client's will. The will says nothing about trustees being paid. The other trustee, Rosa, is a lay trustee. Rosa signs a written agreement that Neil's firm may charge reasonable fees for the conveyancing work needed when the trust sells a house. The beneficiaries are adults and have not been consulted. Neil's firm completes the sale and sends a bill for £4,000. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The firm may charge only its out of pocket costs, because the profit element of its fees cannot be recovered from the trust", "B": "The firm may charge reasonable fees only if the beneficiaries as well as Rosa have given their consent in writing", "C": "The firm may charge, but only if Rosa's agreement is approved by the court, since remuneration of a trustee needs judicial authority", "D": "The firm cannot charge, because the duty not to profit bars payment for services unless the will contains an express charging clause permitting it", "E": "The firm may charge reasonable fees, because section 29 Trustee Act 2000 lets a professional trustee be paid if all co-trustees agree in writing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The firm may charge reasonable fees, because section 29 Trustee Act 2000 lets a professional trustee be paid if all co-trustees agree in writing): A trustee ordinarily cannot be paid unless the instrument, the court, the beneficiaries or statute allows it. Section 29 Trustee Act 2000 provides that a professional trustee is entitled to reasonable remuneration for services if every other trustee agrees in writing, and Rosa has done so. The statutory route avoids the need for a charging clause or the court. Neil's own interest still needs care under the SRA Code, but the fees are recoverable."}, {"id": 1221, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Nimrah Hussain's company, Hussain Retail Ltd, holds a 15 year lease of a shop granted in 2019 by Brockwell Estates Ltd. The lease prohibits assignment without the landlord's written consent, not to be unreasonably withheld, and provides that on any assignment the outgoing tenant must enter into an authorised guarantee agreement if the landlord requires it. Hussain Retail Ltd wishes to assign to Ashdown Foods Ltd, a company with a good trading record. The landlord will consent only if an authorised guarantee agreement is given. Hussain Retail Ltd objects that it should be released on assignment. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The landlord may require the guarantee only if the assignee's financial standing is in doubt, because the right to demand one depends on the assignee's covenant strength", "B": "The guarantee may be required, because the lease itself provides for it and a condition set by the lease is a reasonable condition of consent", "C": "The guarantee cannot be required, because insisting on it from the outgoing tenant is unreasonable where the assignee has a good trading record", "D": "The tenant is released on assignment, because under a lease granted after 1995 the original tenant's liability ends automatically and cannot be revived by a condition", "E": "A guarantee may be required only for leases granted before 1 January 1996, since privity of contract no longer applies to later leases"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The guarantee may be required, because the lease itself provides for it and a condition set by the lease is a reasonable condition of consent): Under the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 a tenant of a lease granted after 1995 is released on assignment, but an authorised guarantee agreement may be required as a condition of consent where the lease provides for it. Section 19(1A) of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927 lets conditions specified in the lease be treated as reasonable, so the landlord can insist on the guarantee whatever the assignee's strength. The assignee's good record would matter to whether refusing consent outright was reasonable, not to a condition the lease itself lays down. The guarantee only covers the assignee's performance and ends when the assignee itself assigns."}, {"id": 1222, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Sapphire Stationery Ltd is a private company incorporated several years ago with a financial year ending on 31 March. Its directors have approved accounts for the year ended 31 March 2026 and ask their solicitor by what date the accounts must be delivered to the Registrar of Companies. The company is not a public company and has no special filing arrangements. What is the deadline?", "options": {"A": "31 December 2026, which is nine months after the end of the financial year, the period allowed for private companies", "B": "31 January 2027, which is ten months after the year end, to allow time for the directors' report", "C": "30 September 2026, which is six months after the year end, the period allowed for public companies", "D": "31 March 2027, which is twelve months after the year end, to match the annual confirmation statement cycle", "E": "30 June 2026, which is three months after the year end, because accounts must be filed once they are approved"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (31 December 2026, which is nine months after the end of the financial year, the period allowed for private companies): A private company must deliver its annual accounts within nine months of the end of its financial year. For a year ending on 31 March 2026 that gives 31 December 2026. Public companies have the shorter six month period, which would mean 30 September 2026. The confirmation statement runs on a separate twelve month cycle and has no bearing on the accounts deadline."}, {"id": 1223, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Daniel has no previous convictions and is charged with an either way offence of actual bodily harm. At his first hearing at the magistrates' court he indicates that he will plead guilty, and he confirms the plea when it is taken. The court is applying the Sentencing Council guideline on reduction in sentence for a guilty plea. What is the maximum reduction available to him?", "options": {"A": "Up to one tenth, because the plea was entered at the door of the court on the day set for trial", "B": "Up to one third, because the plea was indicated at the first stage of the proceedings, before any trial date", "C": "Up to one half, because he has no previous convictions and pleaded at the earliest opportunity", "D": "A fixed reduction of one third, since the guideline gives the court no discretion over its size", "E": "Up to one quarter, because the plea was entered after the first hearing and before the trial date was fixed by the court"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Up to one third, because the plea was indicated at the first stage of the proceedings, before any trial date): The guideline gives a maximum one third reduction where a plea is indicated at the first stage of proceedings. The reduction falls to a maximum of one quarter after the first stage, and to one tenth for a plea on the day of trial. One third is the ceiling, so the court can award less if the plea comes with other features. Good character is dealt with as mitigation and does not increase the plea discount."}, {"id": 1224, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "The trustees of a £500,000 discretionary trust wish to review how the fund is invested. They have the general power of investment given by the Trustee Act 2000 and the trust instrument does not change it. One of the trustees asks which matters make up the standard investment criteria that the trustees must have regard to when exercising any power of investment. Which of the following correctly states them?", "options": {"A": "The suitability to the trust of investments of the kind proposed, and the need for diversification so far as appropriate to the circumstances", "B": "The ethical acceptability of each investment and the age of the youngest beneficiary at the date of each review", "C": "The expected rate of return and the tax position of the settlor, weighed against the risk of loss of capital", "D": "The cost of acquiring each investment and the liquidity needed to meet the distributions due in the next two years", "E": "The advice of an authorised person and the views of the majority of the adult beneficiaries"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The suitability to the trust of investments of the kind proposed, and the need for diversification so far as appropriate to the circumstances): Section 4 Trustee Act 2000 requires trustees to have regard to the standard investment criteria: the suitability to the trust of investments of the kind proposed and of the particular investment as an investment of that kind, and the need for diversification so far as appropriate to the circumstances. Trustees must also review investments from time to time. Advice is a separate requirement under section 5, and the other matters listed are not part of the statutory criteria."}, {"id": 1225, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Dara instructed her solicitor on Monday to exchange contracts for the purchase of a maisonette on Tuesday. At 11.40am on Tuesday the two solicitors agreed by telephone to exchange using the Law Society telephone formula, and each undertook to send their client's signed part by post that day. At 12.15pm Dara telephoned her solicitor to say she wished to withdraw. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Dara may withdraw until the signed parts are received by post, because exchange is complete only when the documents physically change hands", "B": "Dara may withdraw, because a client can revoke her solicitor's authority to exchange at any time before the contract is completed", "C": "The contract was made at 11.40am when the solicitors agreed to exchange, so Dara is bound despite her later instruction", "D": "The contract became binding when the parts were posted that afternoon, so Dara is bound only from the moment of posting", "E": "The contract is binding only when the deposit has cleared in the seller's solicitor's account, so Dara may withdraw until then"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The contract was made at 11.40am when the solicitors agreed to exchange, so Dara is bound despite her later instruction): Under the telephone formulae the contract is made at the moment the solicitors agree to exchange, with each holding the other's signed part to the other's order. Dara's authority had been given and acted on before her call, so the revocation came too late. The later posting of the parts only performs the undertakings. The deposit is an obligation under the contract and not a condition of its formation."}, {"id": 1226, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Hargreave & Co is a software company arguing in the Court of Appeal (Civil Division) about the meaning of a contract term. Two earlier Court of Appeal decisions on the same point, decided in different years, reach opposite conclusions. Neither court was shown the other decision, and no Supreme Court decision touches the point. The three judges hearing the present appeal are considering how far they are bound by those earlier decisions. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "They must follow the earlier of the two decisions, because the court is bound by the first decision to settle the point", "B": "They are free to depart from both decisions, because Court of Appeal decisions are merely persuasive on the point of law", "C": "They may choose which of the two earlier decisions to follow, as a recognised exception to being bound by the court's own past decisions", "D": "They must follow the later of the two decisions, because a more recent Court of Appeal decision replaces an earlier one on the same point", "E": "They must refer the conflict to the Supreme Court, because the Court of Appeal has no power to resolve a conflict of authority"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They may choose which of the two earlier decisions to follow, as a recognised exception to being bound by the court's own past decisions): The Court of Appeal in civil cases is bound by its own earlier decisions, subject to the exceptions in Young v Bristol Aviation. One exception applies where two earlier Court of Appeal decisions conflict, because the court may then select which to follow. A rule that simply favoured the later or the earlier decision would not reflect that freedom. The court can depart from its own decisions in other limited situations, but treating them as merely persuasive would overstate the exceptions."}, {"id": 1227, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Kestrel Action Group intends to hold a procession to demonstrate its opposition to a new power station. The organiser delivers written notice of the date, time and route to the local police station by hand on Tuesday 3 November, for a procession to take place on Monday 9 November. No exemption applies, and it was reasonably practicable to give more notice. The police later say the notice was out of time. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The notice was too late, because it should have been given in writing to the magistrates' court and not to the local police", "B": "The notice was in time, because notice of a procession need be given only at least forty eight hours before the event takes place", "C": "The notice was too late, because fourteen clear days are required before a procession that is intended to protest against government policy", "D": "The notice was in time, because counting both 3 November and 9 November gives seven days, which exceeds the six day rule", "E": "The notice was too late, because six clear days are required and the days of delivery and procession are excluded, leaving five"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The notice was too late, because six clear days are required and the days of delivery and procession are excluded, leaving five): Section 11 of the Public Order Act 1986 requires written notice delivered to the police at least six clear days before the procession. Clear days exclude both the day on which notice is delivered and the day of the procession, so 4 to 8 November gives only five. Counting both end days is the common error that produces seven. Forty eight hours and fourteen days are not the statutory period, and notice goes to the police and not to a court."}, {"id": 1228, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Tomas works abroad for several months. Before leaving, he promised his wife Elena £400 a month for household costs while they were apart, and they remained on good terms. He paid for the first month and then stopped. Elena sues for the unpaid sums. There was no written agreement. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Elena succeeds, because a fixed monthly sum shows an intention to create legal relations and she supplied consideration by running the household", "B": "Elena succeeds, because Tomas paid the first month, and that payment shows the parties intended the promise to be binding", "C": "Elena fails, because spouses on good terms are presumed not to intend legal relations in domestic arrangements like this one", "D": "Elena succeeds, because the presumption against legal relations applies only to couples whose marriage has broken down and who live apart", "E": "Elena fails, because an oral promise between spouses is unenforceable unless the terms are recorded in a signed written agreement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Elena fails, because spouses on good terms are presumed not to intend legal relations in domestic arrangements like this one): In domestic and social agreements the courts presume that the parties did not intend legal consequences. Where spouses are living together or apart on good terms, as here, an arrangement for household money is the sort of promise the presumption covers. Where the relationship has broken down, the presumption can be rebutted and the position is different. There is no general rule that spousal promises must be in writing, so the first payment does not alter the presumption."}, {"id": 1229, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Lucas Bennett is charged with assault. The prosecution has a witness who will say that, six months before the alleged assault, Lucas was seen hitting a man outside a nightclub. Lucas was not charged or convicted in relation to that incident. Lucas's solicitor asks whether this evidence is bad character evidence under the Criminal Justice Act 2003. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, but only because the incident occurred within twelve months of the alleged offence charged in the present case", "B": "Yes, because bad character covers evidence of misconduct, which includes reprehensible behaviour or an offence, even where no conviction followed", "C": "Yes, and it is admissible automatically as propensity evidence, because the definition and the gateways coincide", "D": "No, because evidence of an earlier incident is bad character only if it resulted in a charge being brought", "E": "No, because bad character evidence covers only previous convictions and cautions recorded against the defendant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because bad character covers evidence of misconduct, which includes reprehensible behaviour or an offence, even where no conviction followed): Under the Criminal Justice Act 2003, bad character is evidence of, or of a disposition towards, misconduct, meaning the commission of an offence or other reprehensible behaviour. A fight observed by a witness fits that description even without a charge or conviction. Falling within the definition does not make the evidence admissible, because the prosecution still needs a gateway and must overcome the court's discretion to exclude."}, {"id": 1230, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Hartley and Co is a small firm of solicitors that acts only for claimants in defamation and privacy litigation. It does not handle property purchases, company formations or trusts, and it holds client money only to pay counsel's fees and court fees from sums sent for that purpose. The managing partner asks whether the firm is within the regulated sector for the purposes of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017. Which of the following is the most accurate answer?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because the firm receives money from clients and so handles client funds in the course of its business", "B": "Yes, because every firm of solicitors regulated by the SRA is automatically an independent legal professional within the regulated sector", "C": "No, because the regulations apply to firms with turnover above a threshold that the SRA sets each year after consultation", "D": "Yes, because litigation is a form of legal advice and all legal advice given to clients falls within the Money Laundering Regulations 2017", "E": "No, because the regulations cover only legal work involving certain financial or property transactions, which litigation does not"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No, because the regulations cover only legal work involving certain financial or property transactions, which litigation does not): The Money Laundering Regulations 2017 apply to an independent legal professional only when it participates in specified transactions, such as buying and selling real property or business entities, managing client money or assets, or creating and running companies and trusts. A practice limited to defamation and privacy litigation does not do that work. Handling fees for counsel and the court does not amount to managing client assets. Being regulated by the SRA does not by itself bring a firm into the regulated sector, and there is no turnover threshold."}, {"id": 1231, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Gillian Marsh is a partner at a small firm in Shrewsbury that specialises in English landlord and tenant work. A client, Dewi Hopkin, asks her to advise on whether a notice to end a tenancy of a house in Wrexham is valid. The tenancy is governed by legislation passed by Senedd Cymru, which is different in important ways from the English legislation Gillian knows. Nobody else in the firm has experience of the Welsh scheme. Dewi is keen that the firm acts because he trusts it. What should Gillian do?", "options": {"A": "Decide whether she can acquire the needed competence in time, and if not tell Dewi and help him to find a suitably experienced adviser", "B": "Refuse the instruction, because a firm based in England cannot lawfully advise on the law applying in Wales", "C": "Accept the instruction because the legislation is freely published, and rely on her general skills to read it before advising", "D": "Accept the instruction because Dewi has chosen the firm, and include a note that Welsh law has not been checked", "E": "Accept the instruction and apply the English legislation by analogy, because the policy of both schemes on ending tenancies is similar"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decide whether she can acquire the needed competence in time, and if not tell Dewi and help him to find a suitably experienced adviser): England and Wales is a single legal jurisdiction, so an English firm may advise on Welsh legislation, but the SRA Code requires a solicitor to maintain competence and to ensure the service is competent. Gillian knows the English scheme differs and has no relevant experience, so she must decide honestly whether she can reach the necessary competence in time. If she cannot, she should be open with Dewi and help him find a suitable adviser. Analogy with English law or a disclaimer would not meet her duty."}, {"id": 1232, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Marlow Quarry Ltd owns a disused quarry with a deep flooded pit on its boundary. The company has twice found children's footprints near the edge, and a gap in the fence has been reported to it. A teenager who has climbed through the gap is injured when the pit wall collapses. The teenager sues the company under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984. Which of the following best states what the teenager must establish for the company to owe a duty?", "options": {"A": "The company knew or had grounds to believe in the danger and in the likelihood of trespassers near it, and protection was reasonable to expect", "B": "The company actually knew the teenager was on the land that day and that injury from the pit wall was foreseeable to a reasonable owner", "C": "The company owed the common duty of care to all entrants once it knew trespassers had previously entered, so reasonable care for their safety was needed", "D": "The company created the danger by its own deliberate act, and the teenager had not seen any warning sign on the boundary", "E": "The company was negligent in maintaining the pit wall, and the teenager suffered either personal injury or damage to property"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The company knew or had grounds to believe in the danger and in the likelihood of trespassers near it, and protection was reasonable to expect): The 1984 Act imposes a duty to non-visitors only if three conditions are met: the occupier is aware of the danger or has reasonable grounds to believe it exists, knows or has reasonable grounds to believe that the other person is in or may come into the vicinity of the danger, and the risk is one against which the occupier may reasonably be expected to offer some protection. Actual knowledge of the individual teenager's presence is not needed. The common duty of care belongs to the 1957 Act, and the 1984 Act does not cover damage to property."}, {"id": 1233, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Yusuf Demir sued Harcourt Haulage Ltd after a collision, and his solicitor served a witness statement from Mr Demir supporting an interim payment application. After service, Mr Demir tells the solicitor that he exaggerated his time off work: he returned after five weeks, not five months. The hearing is next week. Mr Demir asks the solicitor to say nothing and hopes the payment will be ordered. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Keep the statement in the bundle but avoid relying on the exaggerated period in oral submissions at the hearing", "B": "Rely on the statement at the hearing, because the inaccuracy concerns one head of loss and not liability", "C": "Withdraw the interim payment application without correcting anything, since the statement then has no further effect", "D": "Tell the court and the defendant at once, because the duty to the court overrides client confidentiality when evidence is wrong", "E": "Advise that the statement must be corrected or withdrawn, and stop acting if Mr Demir refuses, without disclosing his confidences"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Advise that the statement must be corrected or withdrawn, and stop acting if Mr Demir refuses, without disclosing his confidences): Once a solicitor knows that evidence already served is false, he must not allow the court to be misled. The first step is to explain to the client that the statement must be corrected or withdrawn, and if the client refuses, the solicitor must stop acting. Client confidentiality means the solicitor cannot simply tell the other side. Relying on the statement, or using it quietly while avoiding the figure, would still mislead the court, and a silent withdrawal leaves the false document uncorrected."}, {"id": 1234, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Contracts for the sale of a freehold house are exchanged on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) at a price of £340,000, and the contract contains no special condition about the deposit. The buyer's solicitor sends the deposit to the seller's solicitor on exchange. Which statement correctly describes the deposit and how it is held?", "options": {"A": "It is ten per cent of the price, £34,000, and the seller's solicitor may pay it to the seller on exchange", "B": "It is five per cent of the price, £17,000, and the seller's solicitor holds it as agent for the seller", "C": "It is ten per cent of the price, £34,000, and the buyer's solicitor holds it until completion on the buyer's behalf", "D": "It is ten per cent of the price, £34,000, and the seller's solicitor holds it as stakeholder until completion", "E": "It is twenty per cent of the price, £68,000, and the seller's solicitor holds it as stakeholder until completion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is ten per cent of the price, £34,000, and the seller's solicitor holds it as stakeholder until completion): Under the Standard Conditions the deposit is ten per cent of the price unless the contract says otherwise, so here it is £34,000. It is paid to the seller's solicitor and held as stakeholder, which means it is held for both parties and is not released to the seller before completion. Agency terms would let the seller use it earlier, but those apply only if the contract so provides."}, {"id": 1235, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Siobhan Doyle is charged with burglary. Her neighbour of ten years, Mr Pearce, says he saw a figure leave the house at about 11pm under a street light, for about four seconds, from 25 metres away, and that it was Siobhan. Siobhan says she was at home alone and that Mr Pearce is mistaken. Police held no identification procedure because Mr Pearce knew her well. The prosecution argues that recognition of a known person removes any need for caution. What is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The case must be withdrawn, because there was no identification procedure and the sighting was brief", "B": "The warning applies, but the judge must stop the case, because four seconds is below the minimum viewing time", "C": "A warning is needed only if Mr Pearce is shown to have poor eyesight, since recognition is otherwise a matter of fact", "D": "The Turnbull warning is still needed because recognition can be mistaken, but the case may go to the jury if quality is adequate", "E": "No warning is needed, because recognition by a long-standing neighbour is treated as reliable evidence as a matter of law"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Turnbull warning is still needed because recognition can be mistaken, but the case may go to the jury if quality is adequate): Recognition of someone well known is usually more reliable than identification of a stranger, but the guidelines still apply because honest witnesses can be mistaken even about people they know. The judge warns the jury and examines quality: length of observation, distance, light and any obstruction. Where the quality is adequate, the case can go to the jury. No procedure is required where the witness already knows the suspect, and there is no set minimum viewing time."}, {"id": 1236, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Six friends decide to buy a large house together as an investment, each contributing equal sums. The transfer is drafted so that all six are named as legal owners. Their solicitor explains how legal and beneficial ownership will work. The friends want to know whether all six can hold the legal estate and who will hold the beneficial interest. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "Four may hold the legal estate and the remaining two hold a separate legal interest in the house for their shares", "B": "All six may hold the legal estate as tenants in common, since friends buying an investment must hold at law in shares", "C": "Four may hold the legal estate, but the other two have no interest in the land and only a contractual claim for repayment", "D": "All six may hold the legal estate as joint tenants, because the number of legal owners of land is not limited by statute", "E": "A maximum of four people may hold the legal estate as trustees, who then hold it for all six as beneficiaries"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A maximum of four people may hold the legal estate as trustees, who then hold it for all six as beneficiaries): Under section 34 of the Trustee Act 1925 and the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996, no more than four persons can be trustees of land, and a transfer to more than four vests the legal estate in four of them. Co-owned land is held on a trust of land, with the legal estate held as joint tenants on trust for all the beneficial owners. The remaining two friends take equitable interests, not legal ones. A legal tenancy in common cannot exist after 1925."}, {"id": 1237, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Tanya Boyle is charged with assault after a fight in a pub. The only evidence against her is that a bystander, standing across the room, says she saw Tanya punch the victim. Tanya says that she was not present at the pub that night. The Crown Court judge is preparing the summing up for the jury. In what circumstances must the judge give the Turnbull warning?", "options": {"A": "Only in Crown Court jury trials, because magistrates are not required to direct themselves on the risks", "B": "Only where the identifying witness is a stranger to the defendant, as recognition evidence needs no warning", "C": "Where any witness gives identification evidence, even if the defendant admits being present and disputes only what happened", "D": "Only where the judge has already found the identification evidence to be unreliable and the trial proceeds", "E": "Where the prosecution case rests wholly or substantially on identification evidence which the defence alleges is mistaken"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Where the prosecution case rests wholly or substantially on identification evidence which the defence alleges is mistaken): The Turnbull guidelines apply where the prosecution case depends wholly or substantially on the correctness of identification evidence that the defence says is mistaken. Tanya denies presence, so identity is the live issue and the warning is needed. It does not apply if identity is admitted. Recognition evidence still requires caution, and magistrates must direct themselves in the same way."}, {"id": 1238, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Brightwell Ltd, a VAT registered company, has agreed to buy a warehouse that was completed eleven years ago. The seller, Orme Investments Ltd, opted to tax the building seven years ago and notified HMRC, and that option remains in force. The contract provides that the price of £750,000 is exclusive of VAT. Brightwell will use the warehouse for its own taxable business. Which of the following best describes the VAT position on the sale?", "options": {"A": "The sale is zero rated, because the freehold sale of a commercial building is zero rated within the general scheme of VAT", "B": "The sale is exempt, because a commercial building more than three years old is an exempt supply of land under the general rule", "C": "The sale is standard rated because of the option to tax, so VAT is added and Brightwell can normally recover it", "D": "The sale is exempt, because the option to tax lapsed after five years and must be renewed to remain effective", "E": "The sale is standard rated, but Brightwell cannot recover the VAT because VAT on land purchases is blocked input tax"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The sale is standard rated because of the option to tax, so VAT is added and Brightwell can normally recover it): The sale of a commercial building more than three years old is normally an exempt supply of land, but a seller can opt to tax and then the sale is standard rated. Orme Investments Ltd has a valid option in force, so VAT is chargeable on the price and the contract makes the price exclusive, so it is added. Brightwell Ltd is VAT registered and will use the building for taxable supplies, so it can normally reclaim the VAT as input tax. Zero rating applies only to certain new dwellings and similar supplies, and an option to tax does not lapse after five years."}, {"id": 1239, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Shirin receives a text from a friend, Olu, saying 'Pop round to mine tomorrow when you like.' The next day she goes to the flat next door by mistake, honestly believing it is Olu's, and enters through an unlocked door, believing she has permission. Seeing a ring on a shelf, she dishonestly takes it. The occupier prosecutes. Shirin is charged with burglary under section 9(1)(b) of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Guilty of burglary, because her mistake was careless and carelessness as to entry is enough for the offence", "B": "Not guilty of burglary, because she neither knew nor was reckless as to being a trespasser, although she may be guilty of theft", "C": "Guilty of burglary, because entering premises without the occupier's permission makes her a trespasser whatever she believed", "D": "Not guilty of theft or burglary, because her honest belief that she had permission to enter removes any dishonesty on her part", "E": "Not guilty of burglary, because Olu's invitation amounted to consent from the occupier of the flat she entered"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Not guilty of burglary, because she neither knew nor was reckless as to being a trespasser, although she may be guilty of theft): A defendant is a trespasser for burglary only if she knows or is reckless as to the fact that she is entering without permission. Shirin honestly believed she had permission, so the entry element fails and burglary is not made out. Her honest belief about entering says nothing about whether the later taking of the ring was dishonest, so theft remains possible. Olu's invitation could not authorise entry to a different flat."}, {"id": 1240, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Kendra was injured at work by a machine with a defective guard supplied by Marsh Machinery Ltd and negligently maintained by her employer, Ironbridge Ltd. Kendra sued only Ironbridge, which is solvent, and recovered £150,000 in full. The court later accepts that Marsh Machinery Ltd was 60 percent responsible for the injury and Ironbridge 40 percent. Ironbridge has an annual turnover of £8 million. Ironbridge brings a contribution claim. Which of the following best describes the likely result?", "options": {"A": "Ironbridge can recover £60,000 from Marsh, because the contribution is measured by Ironbridge's own 40 percent share", "B": "Ironbridge can recover £75,000 from Marsh, because contribution between liable parties is divided equally between them", "C": "Ironbridge can recover the full £150,000 from Marsh, because the supplier's defective guard was the original cause of the injury", "D": "Ironbridge recovers nothing from Marsh, because a defendant that has paid the claimant in full cannot claim over", "E": "Ironbridge can recover £90,000 from Marsh, being the share found just and equitable by reference to Marsh's 60 percent responsibility"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ironbridge can recover £90,000 from Marsh, being the share found just and equitable by reference to Marsh's 60 percent responsibility): Each tortfeasor liable for the same damage is liable for all of it, so Kendra was entitled to recover in full from Ironbridge alone. Under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978, a party who has paid may claim from another liable party such contribution as is just and equitable having regard to that party's responsibility for the damage. On the court's assessment of 60 percent, Marsh's share is £90,000. Equal division and the reversed 40 percent figure ignore the apportionment, and a full indemnity is available only in exceptional cases."}, {"id": 1241, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Beatrice is a partner in a firm that is within the regulated sector. Her nominated officer has submitted an authorised disclosure about a client, Mr Okafor, who is buying a flat. Mr Okafor asks why completion has been delayed. Wanting to be open with him, Beatrice says the firm had to report a concern about his funds to the National Crime Agency and is waiting for a response. No investigation has yet been opened. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Beatrice has committed no offence, because tipping off requires a police investigation to be under way when the disclosure is made", "B": "Beatrice may have tipped off, because she disclosed that a report was made and the disclosure is likely to prejudice an investigation", "C": "Beatrice has committed no offence, because tipping off applies to employees of the firm who are not partners", "D": "Beatrice has committed the offence of failing to disclose, because she did not first obtain Mr Okafor's consent to the report", "E": "Beatrice has committed no offence, because a solicitor owes a duty of candour to the client and must explain the reason for a delay"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Beatrice may have tipped off, because she disclosed that a report was made and the disclosure is likely to prejudice an investigation): Section 333A of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 makes it an offence for someone in the regulated sector to disclose that a report has been made if the disclosure is likely to prejudice any investigation that might be conducted as a result. An investigation need not already exist, so the absence of one does not help Beatrice. Partners are covered as much as other staff, and no duty of candour to the client allows the disclosure. The offence of failing to disclose concerns a different omission altogether."}, {"id": 1242, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Section 5 of the Rail Passengers Act 2024 states plainly that a train operator 'shall refund the full fare where a train is cancelled'. Dilys Prowse bought a ticket and her train was cancelled. The operator offers a half refund and says that during debate on the Bill a minister said refunds would be 'subject to a deduction for administration'. Dilys's solicitor considers using the ministerial statement from Hansard. There is no ambiguity in the section, and the debate was on the Bill itself. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court will rely on the statement, because ministerial statements made in debate carry decisive weight on interpretation", "B": "The court will disregard the statement, because Hansard is excluded as an aid by the Bill of Rights 1689 in every circumstance", "C": "The court will rely on the statement, because Hansard can be used to show what Parliament intended in any case concerning an Act of Parliament", "D": "The court will disregard the statement, because ministerial statements in Hansard may be used where legislation is ambiguous or obscure, and this section is clear", "E": "The court will allow the statement, because it is clear and made by the minister promoting the Bill, which is enough on its own"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court will disregard the statement, because ministerial statements in Hansard may be used where legislation is ambiguous or obscure, and this section is clear): Under Pepper v Hart, a court may refer to ministerial statements in Hansard where the legislation is ambiguous, obscure or leads to an absurdity, and the statement relied on is clear and made by the minister promoting the Bill. Section 5 is clear, so the first condition is not met and the debate cannot be used to introduce a deduction. A clear statement by the minister is not enough in itself, and Hansard is not excluded in every case."}, {"id": 1243, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Ewan is an adult beneficiary with a contingent interest in a family trust. Colin, one of the trustees, offers to buy Ewan's interest for £20,000. Colin knows that the trust's main asset has just attracted a takeover offer worth far more than its book value, but he does not mention it to Ewan. Ewan accepts, receives advice from a friend, and later learns of the offer. He wants to challenge the sale. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Ewan can set the sale aside only if the other trustees refuse to ratify it, because ratification by trustees validates the sale", "B": "Ewan cannot set the sale aside, because the fair-dealing rule applies only where a trustee buys land held on trust", "C": "Ewan cannot set the sale aside, because he was an adult who agreed freely to the price and took advice from a friend", "D": "Ewan can set the sale aside, because a buying trustee must disclose material information and Colin withheld the takeover offer", "E": "Ewan can set the sale aside only if he proves that Colin pressured him, since the fair-dealing rule needs proof of undue influence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ewan can set the sale aside, because a buying trustee must disclose material information and Colin withheld the takeover offer): A trustee who buys a beneficiary's interest is under the fair-dealing rule: the purchase stands only if the trustee made full disclosure of material facts, paid a fair price and took no advantage. Colin knew of the takeover offer and did not disclose it, so the sale can be set aside. Proof of pressure is not needed, and advice from a friend does not replace disclosure by the trustee. The rule applies to any beneficial interest, not only to land."}, {"id": 1244, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A school governors' panel hears an appeal against the permanent exclusion of a pupil, Kofi. One panel member, Mrs Daley, has been in a bitter and public boundary dispute with Kofi's mother for several years and has told others she cannot stand the family. She sits on the panel and votes to uphold the exclusion. Kofi's mother asks whether the decision can be challenged. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because a fair minded and informed observer would conclude there was a real possibility of bias, given the known hostility", "B": "No, because the panel decided by a majority and so one member's attitude cannot have affected the outcome", "C": "Yes, because any personal acquaintance between a decision maker and a party leads to automatic disqualification from the decision", "D": "No, because the neighbours' dispute is not a financial interest, so the panel member was not disqualified from sitting", "E": "Yes, but only if Kofi's mother proves that Mrs Daley actually decided the matter on the basis of her dislike"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because a fair minded and informed observer would conclude there was a real possibility of bias, given the known hostility): Outside automatic disqualification, the question is whether a fair minded and informed observer, having considered the facts, would conclude there was a real possibility that the decision maker was biased. Mrs Daley's public hostility towards the family satisfies that test. Actual bias does not need to be proved, and a financial interest is not the only basis for challenge. A mere acquaintance would not suffice, and one biased member can taint the whole panel's decision."}, {"id": 1245, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Bryn Joinery agreed to build a bespoke staircase for Tavistock Homes for a fixed price of £14,000 payable on completion. After about half the work was done, Tavistock wrongfully repudiated the contract and Bryn accepted the repudiation. Bryn did not complete the staircase, and its work to date is worth £6,500 on a reasonable valuation. Bryn wants to know how it might be paid for the work it has done. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Bryn may claim nothing for the half finished staircase, because payment arises only on complete performance and Bryn did not complete the work", "B": "Bryn may claim a reasonable sum for the work done, as restitution following Tavistock's repudiation, instead of damages for the lost contract", "C": "Bryn may claim only the contract price of £14,000, because a party who accepts repudiation can sue in debt for the agreed sum as a right", "D": "Bryn may claim a quantum meruit only if the contract is void, because restitution cannot coexist with a valid express contract on any footing", "E": "Bryn may claim £7,000, being half the contract price, because on repudiation the price is apportioned rateably to the work completed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bryn may claim a reasonable sum for the work done, as restitution following Tavistock's repudiation, instead of damages for the lost contract): Where the other party repudiates and the innocent party accepts that, the innocent party may sue in restitution for the reasonable value of work done, which is a quantum meruit. This is an alternative to expectation damages. The entire obligations rule cannot be used by Tavistock, because it caused the failure to complete. The price was payable on completion, so a debt claim for it fails, and there is no automatic half price apportionment."}, {"id": 1246, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Mia wants her husband Noel and their two sons to benefit from £50,000 of her savings. The money is the entire balance of her own current account. She signs a document declaring: 'I hold the £50,000 in my account on trust for my husband and sons in equal shares.' She tells nobody and does not open a new account. She later refuses to pay Noel anything, saying a trust needs a transfer to another trustee. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The trust is constituted, because Mia declared herself trustee of identifiable money, so no transfer to another person was needed", "B": "The trust is constituted but Mia can revoke it, because a settlor who is also trustee can end the trust when she chooses", "C": "The trust is constituted only if Mia tells Noel and the sons, because beneficiaries must accept before they can enforce", "D": "The trust is not constituted, because Mia must transfer the money to a separate trustee before the family can take any interest", "E": "The trust is not constituted, because Noel and the sons gave no consideration and cannot enforce a gratuitous trust"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The trust is constituted, because Mia declared herself trustee of identifiable money, so no transfer to another person was needed): Where the settlor declares herself trustee, the trust is constituted by the declaration itself because she already holds the property. The money is identifiable as the account balance, and certainty of intention is clear from the written declaration. The beneficiaries are volunteers, but the volunteer rule matters only for an incomplete transfer, not a completed declaration. Once constituted, the trust binds Mia and cannot be ended by her at will."}, {"id": 1247, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Hannah Okafor died on 3 March 2026 owning shares in a company quoted on the London Stock Exchange. Her executors, Rory and Imani, are preparing the Inheritance Tax account and need a date-of-death value for those shares. On the day of death the stock exchange daily official list showed a lowest and highest quoted price for the shares and also recorded bargains marked at different prices. The executors ask the solicitor how HMRC expects quoted shares to be valued for Inheritance Tax. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The price at which the executors later sell the shares, provided the sale takes place within twelve months of the date of death", "B": "The lower of the quarter-up price and the midpoint between the highest and lowest marked bargains recorded on the day of death", "C": "The higher of the quarter-up price and the highest marked bargain, because quoted shares are valued at their best price on the day of death", "D": "The average of the highest and lowest quoted prices over the seven days before death, to smooth out daily movements in the market", "E": "The original purchase price paid by Hannah, increased by the retail prices index up to the date of her death"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The lower of the quarter-up price and the midpoint between the highest and lowest marked bargains recorded on the day of death): Quoted shares are valued for Inheritance Tax at the lower of two figures taken from the stock exchange daily official list for the date of death: the quarter-up price, and the midpoint of the highest and lowest marked bargains. The date of death is what matters, so a seven day average is not used. A later sale price is relevant only to loss on sale relief, which is a separate claim made after the tax has been calculated. Original cost is irrelevant to the Inheritance Tax value."}, {"id": 1248, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Amir's solicitor receives the landlord's heads of terms, which cover only the rent and the length of the term. Amir is anxious about unknown costs and wants to secure the premises quickly by signing an agreement for lease this week. The landlord's agent says the full terms will follow in the draft lease. Amir has no previous experience of commercial leases. What is the best advice for Amir's solicitor to give?", "options": {"A": "Ask for fuller heads of terms covering costs such as service charge, repair and alienation, as the Code recommends, before drafting", "B": "Sign the agreement for lease now to secure the unit, because the full terms can be negotiated after the contract is made", "C": "Apply to court for an order that the landlord provide full terms, because the Code is a legal requirement for new business leases", "D": "Wait for the draft lease before raising anything, because heads of terms are only a courtesy and carry no weight in negotiations", "E": "Insist the landlord comply with the Code, because failure to follow it makes the heads of terms unenforceable against the tenant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask for fuller heads of terms covering costs such as service charge, repair and alienation, as the Code recommends, before drafting): The Code recommends that heads of terms are agreed before the lease is drafted and cover the main costs and obligations. Amir is inexperienced, and his solicitor must act in his best interests and protect him from committing to unknown terms. Asking for fuller heads of terms is the proper step and is consistent with the Code. Signing an agreement for lease before the costs are known would remove his bargaining position, and the Code is guidance and not a legal requirement."}, {"id": 1249, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Cole's will says: 'I give 100 ordinary shares in Hartley plc to my goddaughter Mia.' The will does not use the word 'my' before 'shares'. Mr Cole owned 300 Hartley shares when he made the will but sold them all two years before he died. His estate now holds no Hartley shares, though it has ample cash. Mia claims the gift. What is the position?", "options": {"A": "The gift is specific and adeemed because the shares were sold, so Mia takes nothing", "B": "The gift is specific, but Mia is entitled to the proceeds of sale of the shares, because they remain traceable", "C": "The gift is a general legacy, so it is not adeemed, and the executors must buy 100 Hartley shares or pay their value for Mia", "D": "The gift fails for uncertainty, because a legacy of shares must identify the particular certificates owned", "E": "The gift is demonstrative, so Mia takes 100 Hartley shares from the estate if any remain and nothing otherwise"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The gift is a general legacy, so it is not adeemed, and the executors must buy 100 Hartley shares or pay their value for Mia): A gift of a number of shares not identified as the testator's own, such as 'shares in Hartley plc' without 'my', is construed as a general legacy. A general legacy is not adeemed when the testator sells his shares, because it can be satisfied from the estate generally. The executors must acquire 100 shares or pay their value to Mia. Had the gift been of 'my shares', it would have been specific and adeemed."}, {"id": 1250, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A firm advises several private clients on different transactions this month. A trainee is asked to identify, from the assets involved, which is a specified investment, so that the firm can consider whether its work is a regulated activity under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000. The supervisor reminds her that real property, physical commodities and ordinary contracts for goods are treated differently from investments. Which of the following is a specified investment?", "options": {"A": "A freehold office building let to a commercial tenant, because it generates rent that investors treat as an income return", "B": "A collection of gold coins held in a bank vault, because bullion is a recognised store of value for investors", "C": "Ordinary shares in a private company, because shares in any company, listed or not, are specified investments", "D": "A long lease of a residential flat, because it is an interest in land that can be bought and sold", "E": "A contract for the sale of a motor car to a dealer, because it is a binding agreement involving the transfer of value"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ordinary shares in a private company, because shares in any company, listed or not, are specified investments): The Regulated Activities Order lists specified investments, which include shares in any company, whether public or private, along with debentures, units in collective investment schemes, options and certain insurance contracts. Private company shares are a common trap because candidates assume only listed securities count. Freehold and leasehold interests in land, physical bullion and ordinary contracts for the sale of goods are not specified investments, even though some may be held for investment purposes."}, {"id": 1251, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Brightwater Joinery Ltd is the defendant to a £22,000 claim in the County Court, allocated to the fast track. Its director has decided not to instruct lawyers. The company wants Anita Bose, its office manager, who is an unqualified employee authorised in writing by the company, to represent it at the trial. No application has yet been made to the court about her role. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She may represent the company at trial if the court gives permission, which is usually granted unless a particular reason makes it inappropriate", "B": "She may represent the company at a small claims hearing but not in the fast track, where lay representation is excluded", "C": "The director must attend and speak for the company, because a company can be represented at trial by its officers but not by other staff", "D": "She may represent the company without permission, because a company acts through its employees and so an employee has rights of audience at the trial hearing", "E": "She cannot represent the company, because a company is not a natural person and must instruct a solicitor or barrister at trial"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She may represent the company at trial if the court gives permission, which is usually granted unless a particular reason makes it inappropriate): A company may be represented at trial by an employee if the employee has been authorised by the company to appear on its behalf and the court gives permission. Permission is normally given unless there is a particular reason why it would be inappropriate, so the application should be made in good time. The fast track does not exclude this, and the company is not obliged to use a lawyer. Treating representation as limited to officers of the company would be too narrow a description of the rule."}, {"id": 1252, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Waverley Investments bought the freehold of a parade of shops three years and four months ago. Its tenant Doyle Bakery Ltd occupies one shop under a protected lease expiring in four months. Waverley wants to take the unit for its own new business, which it will carry on there, and serves a section 25 notice relying on the ground of intention to occupy for its own business. Doyle Bakery Ltd has no breaches. Which of the following best describes the position on the landlord's ground of opposition?", "options": {"A": "The ground is available, because the landlord genuinely intends to occupy and need prove only a firm and settled intention", "B": "The ground is not available, because the landlord's interest was acquired less than five years before the tenancy's termination date", "C": "The ground is available, but the landlord must pay the tenant double compensation because the ground relies on owner occupation", "D": "The ground is not available, because it applies only where the tenant has breached the lease and been given notice", "E": "The ground is available, because the landlord is the freehold owner and the tenant's lease is within its final year"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The ground is not available, because the landlord's interest was acquired less than five years before the tenancy's termination date): The ground of intention to occupy for the landlord's own business is not available if the landlord's interest was purchased or created within five years before the termination date of the tenancy. Waverley bought the freehold only about three years and four months ago, so even a genuine, firm intention cannot support the ground. The tenant's lack of breaches is irrelevant, because the ground is not fault based. Waverley would need another ground or to wait until it has held the interest for five years."}, {"id": 1253, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Nkechi is the nominated officer of a firm. A new client, Mr Adeyemi, wants the firm to act on the purchase of a £3 million London property. He is the son of a serving minister in an overseas government and will fund the purchase from a family business. The firm's risk assessment rates property purchases as medium risk. Nkechi asks what additional steps are required before the firm can accept instructions. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Standard due diligence is enough, because a family member of a politician is not treated as a politically exposed person by the regulations", "B": "Enhanced due diligence is required, but a check of the client's identity documents against an overseas government database is all that is needed", "C": "Simplified due diligence is available, because the purchase is funded from a family business and the firm rates property purchases as medium risk", "D": "Enhanced due diligence is needed, with senior management approval to accept him and steps to establish his source of wealth and funds", "E": "Enhanced due diligence is required, but approval from senior management is needed only after completion, once the source of funds has been confirmed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Enhanced due diligence is needed, with senior management approval to accept him and steps to establish his source of wealth and funds): A family member of a politically exposed person is within the regulations' definition, so Mr Adeyemi must be treated as one. Regulation 35 then requires enhanced due diligence, which includes approval from senior management for establishing the relationship and adequate measures to establish source of wealth and source of funds. Enhanced monitoring of the relationship follows. The approval must come before the firm accepts instructions, and an identity check alone does not meet the standard."}, {"id": 1254, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Halloran suffers a leg injury caused by the negligence of a lorry driver. The leg is left weak and occasionally gives way without warning. Two weeks later, against medical advice and without a handrail or anyone to help him, Mr Halloran descends a steep flight of stairs at a friend's house. His leg gives way and he falls, breaking his ankle. He seeks damages for the broken ankle from the lorry driver's employer. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The employer is liable, because the fall would not have occurred but for the original injury caused by its driver", "B": "The employer is not liable, because any act by the claimant after the first injury ends the defendant's responsibility for later harm", "C": "The employer is liable for the whole loss, with damages reduced only for contributory negligence on the claimant's part", "D": "The employer is not liable for the ankle, because his unreasonable conduct broke the chain of causation from the original negligence", "E": "The employer is liable, because the claimant did not intend to cause himself further harm by using the stairs"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The employer is not liable for the ankle, because his unreasonable conduct broke the chain of causation from the original negligence): A claimant's own act breaks the chain of causation if it is so unreasonable that it eclipses the defendant's wrong. Mr Halloran knew his leg was unreliable, had been warned, and chose to take a steep staircase with no support, so the second injury is attributed to his own conduct. But-for causation is satisfied, but that alone does not decide the matter. A claimant's later act does not break the chain every time, only when it is unreasonable in this way."}, {"id": 1255, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Home Secretary decides to deport Yusuf Karimi, who has been convicted of serious offences and is considered a threat to national security, to a country where credible evidence shows he faces a real risk of torture. The Home Secretary argues that the danger to the public outweighs the risk to Yusuf, so that article 3 can be balanced against national security. Yusuf's solicitor considers the claim. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Article 3 allows a derogation in time of public emergency, so a threat to national security can justify the deportation", "B": "Article 3 applies only to treatment inflicted within the United Kingdom, so deportation abroad does not engage it", "C": "Article 3 is limited, so it can be restricted where the state shows a legitimate aim and that deportation is a proportionate means of achieving that aim", "D": "Article 3 is qualified, so the court weighs the risk to Yusuf against the danger to the public and may allow deportation", "E": "Article 3 is absolute, so the risk of torture cannot be balanced against the threat Yusuf poses, and deportation would breach it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Article 3 is absolute, so the risk of torture cannot be balanced against the threat Yusuf poses, and deportation would breach it): Article 3 is an absolute right, which means no balancing exercise is allowed and no derogation is permitted even in an emergency. The state cannot remove a person to a place where there is a real risk of torture or inhuman treatment, however dangerous that person is. The Home Secretary's argument therefore fails. Removal to a country where such a risk exists engages article 3 even though the harm would occur abroad, and the proportionality structure of qualified rights does not apply."}, {"id": 1256, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Aaliyah owns all the shares in Brook Interiors Ltd, a trading company that pays corporation tax. She plans to put £100,000 into the company and could do so either as a loan at 6% interest or by subscribing for shares that would pay a dividend. She asks which route is better for the company's own tax position. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Interest is deductible only if Aaliyah is a higher rate taxpayer, because the company's relief follows the personal tax rate of the lender", "B": "Interest is deductible only if the loan is secured by a charge, because unsecured shareholder loans give the company no corporation tax relief", "C": "Dividends are deductible in computing profits and interest is not, because interest paid to a shareholder is treated as a distribution", "D": "Interest on a loan is normally deductible in computing the company's profits, whereas dividends are paid from taxed profits and cannot be deducted", "E": "Neither is deductible, because both interest and dividends are appropriations of profit made after the company's tax liability is calculated"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Interest on a loan is normally deductible in computing the company's profits, whereas dividends are paid from taxed profits and cannot be deducted): Interest on a loan is a trading expense or loan relationship debit of the company and normally reduces its profits chargeable to corporation tax. Dividends are distributions of profit after tax and give the company no deduction. This is the key corporate difference between funding by debt and by equity. Interest is still taxable income for Aaliyah, but the company's relief does not depend on her rate of tax, and interest is only treated as a distribution in limited anti avoidance cases."}, {"id": 1257, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Ellis Retail Ltd is claimant in a High Court dispute about unpaid rent. Its solicitor wishes to apply for an order extending the time for service of the claimant's witness statements. The application is ordinary in nature and no special rule requires any longer notice. The court lists it for a hearing in a fortnight. What is the minimum period of notice of the hearing that the respondent must be given?", "options": {"A": "At least three days before the court is to deal with it, with the notice served as soon as practicable after issue", "B": "At least 14 days before the hearing, as applies to an application for summary judgment and similar applications under the rules", "C": "At least 24 hours before the hearing, because an application on notice needs only a short warning", "D": "At least 21 days before the hearing, because the application seeks to vary a court timetable", "E": "At least seven days before the hearing, to give the respondent a fair opportunity to prepare evidence in reply"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (At least three days before the court is to deal with it, with the notice served as soon as practicable after issue): An application notice must be served as soon as practicable after it is issued and in any event at least three days before the court is to deal with the application. That is the general rule for ordinary interim applications. The 14 day period belongs to summary judgment and similar applications that have their own rule. Shorter notice can be given only with the court's permission."}, {"id": 1258, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Ashgrove Law gave an undertaking to Beaumont Solicitors to pay £15,000 of sale proceeds to a third party creditor within 14 days. The firm failed to do so, because the client told it to hold the money. Beaumont's client has lost a related completion date. Beaumont asks how the failure may be addressed, and the firm's managing partner says that undertakings are a matter of courtesy between firms. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court can enforce the undertaking if it was given in writing and Beaumont gave value in exchange for it", "B": "The failure is a matter of courtesy between firms and creates no professional or legal consequence unless Beaumont's client sues", "C": "The undertaking is unenforceable by the court because it is not a contract, so Beaumont's remedy is a complaint to the Legal Ombudsman", "D": "The undertaking can be enforced through disciplinary action by the SRA, because the court has no jurisdiction over solicitors' promises", "E": "The court can enforce the undertaking summarily because it supervises solicitors, and the failure may also lead to SRA discipline"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court can enforce the undertaking summarily because it supervises solicitors, and the failure may also lead to SRA discipline): The court has an inherent jurisdiction over solicitors as officers of the court and can enforce an undertaking summarily without any need for a contract or consideration. Failure to perform an undertaking is also a serious professional conduct matter for the SRA. The managing partner is wrong to call it courtesy, and the client's instruction to hold the money is no defence. The Legal Ombudsman deals with complaints by clients, so it is not the remedy for another firm."}, {"id": 1259, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Cedric exchanged contracts to sell his house to Fenella for £375,000, with completion in three weeks. Fenella has paid a deposit and registered a notice of the contract on Cedric's title. Cedric has now been offered £395,000 by Greta and tells his solicitor he wants to accept it and cancel the sale to Fenella because the first contract was only agreed a fortnight ago. Greta has not been told about the notice. What advice should the solicitor give?", "options": {"A": "Cedric may sell to Greta if he refunds the deposit to Fenella with interest, because damages would then be an adequate remedy", "B": "Cedric must complete with Fenella, but he may agree to sell to Greta conditionally, so that both contracts proceed until one fails", "C": "Cedric may withdraw within a short cooling off period after exchange, because that period protects a seller who receives a higher offer", "D": "Cedric is bound to Fenella, who could obtain specific performance, and a sale to Greta would be a breach and subject to her notice", "E": "Cedric may sell to Greta, because Fenella has only an equitable interest and a purchaser of the legal estate for value takes free of it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cedric is bound to Fenella, who could obtain specific performance, and a sale to Greta would be a breach and subject to her notice): Once contracts are exchanged both parties are bound, and the buyer can seek specific performance because land is treated as unique. Fenella has protected her interest by a notice, so a transfer to Greta would take effect subject to it and Cedric would also be in breach. Offering to refund the deposit does not discharge the contract. There is no cooling off period after exchange of contracts for the sale of land."}, {"id": 1260, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Trent Rail Heritage Ltd owns a disused tunnel with an unguarded shaft in the floor. The company erected two-metre fences with prominent danger signs at both ends and inspected them monthly. A man cut through the fence one night, ignoring the signs, entered the tunnel and fell down the shaft. The fence was painted dark green. He sues under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The company is liable in damages, but the claim is reduced by half automatically because the claimant was a trespasser who cut the fence", "B": "The company is liable, because a duty arises whenever the occupier knows of a danger, and the trespasser's deliberate cutting of the fence is irrelevant", "C": "The company probably discharged any duty by taking reasonable steps to keep people out and warn them, so the claim is likely to fail", "D": "The company is liable, because the duty requires the occupier to make the danger itself safe, and a warning sign cannot discharge it", "E": "No duty arose, because a person who damages a fence to enter is a trespasser by conduct and the 1984 Act does not protect him"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The company probably discharged any duty by taking reasonable steps to keep people out and warn them, so the claim is likely to fail): Under the 1984 Act the standard is reasonable care in all the circumstances, and a duty may be discharged by taking reasonable steps to warn of the danger or to discourage people from incurring the risk. The company fenced both ends with prominent signs and inspected regularly, which is likely to be reasonable for a disused tunnel. The Act does protect trespassers, so a duty may arise, but here it is probably met. The man's deliberate cutting of the fence also weighs heavily against him."}, {"id": 1261, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Carla Jensen is charged with theft of goods worth £800 from her employer, an either way offence. She indicated a not guilty plea, and after an allocation hearing the magistrates accepted jurisdiction. Carla wants her case dealt with quickly but is worried that her defence of honest belief will be better received by a jury. Her solicitor explains the position. Which of the following correctly states it?", "options": {"A": "She may consent to summary trial or elect trial in the Crown Court, and the magistrates cannot override her election", "B": "She may elect Crown Court trial, but if convicted there the case must return to the magistrates for sentence", "C": "She may elect Crown Court trial only if the prosecution agrees, because election requires the prosecutor's consent", "D": "She may elect Crown Court trial only if the magistrates had declined jurisdiction, because election arises only after a refusal", "E": "She must be tried in the magistrates' court, because once jurisdiction is accepted the defendant cannot elect Crown Court trial"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She may consent to summary trial or elect trial in the Crown Court, and the magistrates cannot override her election): Where magistrates accept jurisdiction over an either way offence, the defendant decides whether to consent to summary trial or to elect trial on indictment in the Crown Court. If she elects Crown Court trial, the magistrates must send the case there, and the prosecution's agreement is not needed. A defendant who chooses the Crown Court will be sentenced there if convicted. Carla's wish for a jury to hear her honest belief defence is a legitimate reason to elect, so her solicitor should explain the trade-off between speed and sentencing powers."}, {"id": 1262, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Corinne Dale and Alex Pryor bought a house for £400,000, of which Corinne paid £320,000 and Alex £80,000. The transfer, which both signed, stated that they held the property as beneficial joint tenants. Years later the relationship ends and Corinne claims a greater share, arguing that her much larger contribution shows they intended a different split. There is no allegation of fraud or mistake. Which of the following best describes how the court will approach her claim?", "options": {"A": "The court will treat the declaration as void, because beneficial interests under a trust of land need a deed to take effect", "B": "The express declaration is conclusive absent fraud or mistake, so they share equally", "C": "The court will award shares by reference to what is fair in all the circumstances, including the length of the relationship", "D": "The court will infer a resulting trust in proportion to contributions because the purchase money was paid in unequal shares", "E": "The court will start from equality but will vary the shares if Corinne's contributions show a different common intention"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The express declaration is conclusive absent fraud or mistake, so they share equally): Where the transfer or a separate declaration of trust expressly states the beneficial interests of co-owners, that declaration is conclusive, subject to fraud or mistake. Corinne and Alex stated that they held as beneficial joint tenants, so equity follows the declaration, and the court does not imply shares from contributions. The common intention analysis applies where the document is silent. A written declaration signed by both satisfies section 53(1)(b), and no deed is needed."}, {"id": 1263, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "The partners of a three-partner firm in Exeter have agreed to retire and close the practice at the end of the month. The firm has no successor practice and has insured its liabilities each year under the minimum terms required by the SRA. One partner suggests that, once the firm stops practising, the cover can simply be allowed to end because the firm will no longer be acting for anyone. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "The firm must obtain run-off cover, because claims can arrive after closure and the minimum terms require it for a set period", "B": "The firm should obtain cover for one further year, because the rules treat a year as enough time for claims to emerge", "C": "The partners should rely on the SRA Compensation Fund, which exists to meet negligence claims against firms that have closed", "D": "No further cover is needed, because qualifying insurance responds to negligence that occurs while the firm is still practising or acting for clients", "E": "Further cover is needed if a client has already notified a claim, because run-off protects against known claims alone"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm must obtain run-off cover, because claims can arrive after closure and the minimum terms require it for a set period): Qualifying insurance is written on a claims-made basis, so a claim first made after the policy has lapsed would find no cover. The SRA rules therefore require a closing firm to buy run-off cover that continues for the prescribed period. The Compensation Fund is a discretionary last resort for certain losses and is not a substitute for negligence insurance. Cover limited to claims already notified would leave clients exposed to later claims."}, {"id": 1264, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Priya offered to sell the lease of her flat to Marcus for £210,000, saying that the offer was open for seven days. Marcus emailed back: 'I will pay £195,000.' Priya did not reply to that email. Two days later Marcus wrote: 'I accept your original price of £210,000.' By then Priya had agreed to sell to someone else for £220,000. Marcus's solicitor says the £210,000 offer was still open and that a contract exists. Which of the following is the best advice for Priya?", "options": {"A": "Priya revived the original offer by not rejecting the counter-offer, so Marcus could still accept it within the seven days", "B": "The original offer remained open for its seven days, so Marcus's later acceptance created a binding contract at £210,000", "C": "Priya's silence in response to the counter-offer amounted to acceptance of it, so a contract exists at £195,000", "D": "Marcus's email at £195,000 was a counter-offer that ended the original offer, so his later purported acceptance creates no contract", "E": "Marcus's email was merely a request for information, so the original offer survived and his later acceptance created a binding contract"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Marcus's email at £195,000 was a counter-offer that ended the original offer, so his later purported acceptance creates no contract): A response that proposes a different price is a counter-offer and operates as a rejection of the original offer, even if the offer had a stated period. Marcus's email was a firm proposal at £195,000, not a mere enquiry, so the £210,000 offer ended when it was made. His later acceptance cannot revive it. Priya's silence is not acceptance of the counter-offer, so no contract exists at either price."}, {"id": 1265, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Dylan Frost is charged with theft of a bicycle, an offence punishable with imprisonment, and appears before the magistrates' court for his first hearing. He has two previous convictions for dishonesty. The prosecutor opposes bail. Dylan's solicitor, who has just been instructed, reminds the court of the Bail Act 1976. Which of the following correctly states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Bail may be refused because of previous convictions for any offence, since bail turns on the defendant's antecedent history", "B": "The right to bail applies only to summary offences, and for either way offences the court has a free discretion", "C": "Bail may be refused where the court considers the offence serious enough, because seriousness alone justifies a remand in custody", "D": "Bail is a matter for the court's discretion with no presumption in favour, so the defence must first give reasons to obtain it", "E": "There is a general right to bail, which can be refused only if a Schedule 1 exception applies, such as a risk of absconding"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (There is a general right to bail, which can be refused only if a Schedule 1 exception applies, such as a risk of absconding): Section 4 of the Bail Act 1976 creates a general right to bail for a person accused of an offence, subject to the exceptions in Schedule 1. For an imprisonable offence the court may refuse bail only if there are substantial grounds to believe that the defendant would fail to surrender, commit an offence on bail or interfere with witnesses, or if another listed ground applies. Seriousness and previous convictions are relevant to assessing those risks, but neither is a free-standing ground to refuse. The presumption applies to either way and indictable offences as well."}, {"id": 1266, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Dunmore attends a hospital casualty department late at night complaining of severe vomiting. The nurse on duty telephones the duty doctor, who negligently decides not to see him and tells him to go home and consult his GP. Mr Dunmore dies five hours later. Expert evidence at trial establishes that he was suffering from a poisoning for which no effective treatment could have been given in time, even if he had been examined and admitted promptly. His widow sues the hospital trust. Which of the following best describes the outcome of the claim?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds in part, because the hospital deprived Mr Dunmore of a chance of treatment that could have been valuable", "B": "The claim fails, because the hospital trust owed no duty of care to a patient who had not yet been admitted", "C": "The claim fails, because the death would have occurred anyway, so the breach was not a but-for cause", "D": "The claim succeeds, because the failure to examine him was negligent and exposed him to the risk of death", "E": "The claim succeeds, because the hospital materially contributed to the death by sending him away without any examination"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claim fails, because the death would have occurred anyway, so the breach was not a but-for cause): A claimant must prove that the harm would not have happened but for the breach. The evidence shows that Mr Dunmore would have died even with prompt examination and admission, so the negligent decision to send him home did not cause his death. The existence of a duty and a breach is therefore not enough. Loss of a chance of treatment does not help where the treatment could not have succeeded in time."}, {"id": 1267, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Ola holds a 20-year lease of a block of offices. He sublet the first floor to Pat for five years, and the sublease has three years left to run. Ola then buys the freehold reversion from the landlord and the purchase is registered. Pat wants to know whether his sublease survives and who is now his landlord. Which of the following best describes the effect of Ola's purchase?", "options": {"A": "Neither interest ends, because merger occurs only if the freehold and the lease are held by different persons", "B": "The sublease merges into the freehold instead, so Pat becomes a licensee of Ola for the remaining three years", "C": "The headlease merges in the freehold and ends, but Pat's sublease continues with Ola as his immediate landlord", "D": "The sublease ends with the headlease, because a subtenant's interest cannot survive the merger of the superior estate", "E": "The headlease continues for its full term, so Pat's landlord remains the former freeholder until the sublease ends"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The headlease merges in the freehold and ends, but Pat's sublease continues with Ola as his immediate landlord): Merger occurs when the lease and the immediately superior reversion come into the same hands, here Ola holding both the headlease and the freehold. The headlease is extinguished, but a sublease granted out of it is not destroyed, and the freeholder steps into the position of landlord to the subtenant. Pat's sublease therefore continues for its remaining three years with Ola as immediate landlord. Merger requires one person to hold both estates, not different persons."}, {"id": 1268, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Stone took out a life policy on his own life which, by its terms, was held on trust for his three children in equal shares, and he named his brother as trustee. His will leaves everything to his wife. Mr Stone died and the insurer paid out £90,000. The wife's solicitors ask whether the money forms part of his estate and passes under his will. What is the correct answer?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because all of a person's assets at death pass under the will unless it excludes them", "B": "No, but the proceeds belong to the trustee personally, because the brother is the legal owner of the proceeds", "C": "No, but the proceeds pass to his wife, because a policy on a married man's life is for his spouse", "D": "No, because the trustee holds the proceeds for the children under the policy trust, outside the will", "E": "Yes, because the policy was on his own life, so the insurer pays the proceeds to his personal representatives"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because the trustee holds the proceeds for the children under the policy trust, outside the will): A policy written in trust for named beneficiaries pays out to the trustee, who holds the proceeds for them, so the money never forms part of the settlor's estate. The will cannot give it away, and the insurer will pay the trustee rather than the personal representatives. The brother holds as trustee and not for his own benefit. The wife takes nothing from the policy unless she is among the beneficiaries."}, {"id": 1269, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Treena Ballantyne is a shareholder and director of Moss Interiors Ltd, a private company. She is told that the company may pay a dividend only out of 'profits available for distribution'. She asks what the phrase means in law and how it is measured. The company has accumulated profits, some losses brought forward, and an unrealised gain on a revalued property. Which of the following correctly explains the phrase?", "options": {"A": "Net assets exceeding called-up share capital, whether or not those surplus assets arise from realised gains on disposals", "B": "Accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses, as shown in the accounts, excluding unrealised revaluation gains", "C": "Accumulated profits including unrealised revaluation gains on property, less accumulated losses of every kind shown in the accounts", "D": "The profit for the latest financial year alone, disregarding earlier retained profits and any losses brought forward from previous years", "E": "Any amount standing in the share premium account together with accumulated profits, less the losses brought forward from earlier years"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Accumulated realised profits less accumulated realised losses, as shown in the accounts, excluding unrealised revaluation gains): A private company's distributable profits are its accumulated realised profits less its accumulated realised losses, measured by the relevant accounts. An unrealised revaluation surplus is not realised and is excluded. The share premium account is capital that cannot be paid out as a dividend. The net assets surplus test is an additional requirement for public companies and is not the basis for a private company."}, {"id": 1270, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Quinn Plant Hire Ltd and Strand Quarries Ltd exchanged letters headed 'without prejudice' during a payment dispute. In a letter dated 12 May, Strand's managing director wrote that Strand would accept £30,000 in full settlement, and Quinn's reply by email on 14 May said 'agreed, please send the terms'. Strand later denied any agreement and sued for £55,000. Quinn wishes to put the 12 May letter and the reply before the court. Is the correspondence admissible?", "options": {"A": "No, it becomes admissible only if Strand waives privilege by consenting in writing to its production at trial", "B": "Yes, it is admissible to show that a settlement was concluded, which is an exception to the without prejudice rule", "C": "Yes, but only the judge may read it, after judgment, to decide the question of costs between the parties", "D": "Yes, because a letter marked without prejudice is admissible whenever it contains an admission about the sum owed", "E": "No, the rule excludes the correspondence, because any communication marked without prejudice is inadmissible for every purpose"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, it is admissible to show that a settlement was concluded, which is an exception to the without prejudice rule): Without prejudice negotiations genuinely aimed at settlement are generally inadmissible. There are recognised exceptions, including that the communications may be used to prove whether a settlement agreement was reached and on what terms. Quinn is relying on the correspondence for exactly that purpose. The label alone does not make a document inadmissible for every purpose, and admissions in such letters cannot otherwise be used against the party who made them."}, {"id": 1271, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Leon is a director of Kestrel Ltd, which operates three pubs. At a trade event he hears that a nearby pub will shortly be offered for sale. He does not mention it to the board, which has never discussed expansion, and he buys the pub personally using his own money. Kestrel's other directors learn of this a year later. Has Leon breached his duty to avoid conflicts of interest under section 175 of the Companies Act 2006?", "options": {"A": "Yes, but only if the company could have afforded the pub and would have pursued it had the board been told", "B": "No, because the company had no plan to buy the pub and so suffered no loss that could found a claim against him", "C": "Yes, because he exploited a business opportunity without board authorisation, and the company's lack of any plan to expand does not prevent a breach", "D": "Yes, but only if Leon had learned the information while attending a board meeting as a director rather than at a trade event", "E": "No, because he used his own money and not company funds or property to acquire the pub for himself"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, because he exploited a business opportunity without board authorisation, and the company's lack of any plan to expand does not prevent a breach): Section 175 prevents a director exploiting any property, information or opportunity, and it applies whether or not the company could have taken it up. Leon did not disclose the opportunity and no authorisation was given by the other directors or members. Use of his own money is no answer because the duty concerns the conflict, not whose funds were spent. The fact that the company had no plan to expand does not remove the breach."}, {"id": 1272, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Brindle Hardware, a client of your firm, displays a cordless drill in a self-service aisle with a price tag of £19.99. The tag is a printing error, as the intended price was £199.99. A customer, Mr Okafor, takes the drill to the till and tenders £19.99. The cashier refuses to sell at that price, and Mr Okafor says that a binding contract already exists. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The tagged price is an offer accepted when Mr Okafor lifted the drill from the shelf, so a contract exists at the tagged price", "B": "The display is an invitation to treat, so Mr Okafor makes the offer at the till and Brindle Hardware is free to refuse it", "C": "The display is an offer to the world that Mr Okafor may accept by conduct, but Brindle Hardware may withdraw it before payment is made", "D": "The tagged price is an offer accepted when Mr Okafor tendered payment, so Brindle Hardware is bound unless it proves he knew of the error", "E": "The display is a unilateral offer accepted by performance when Mr Okafor presented the drill, so only the price error can defeat the contract"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The display is an invitation to treat, so Mr Okafor makes the offer at the till and Brindle Hardware is free to refuse it): Goods on display in a self-service shop are an invitation to treat, not an offer. The customer makes the offer by presenting the goods at the till, and the shop accepts only when the cashier agrees to sell. Brindle Hardware has not yet accepted, so it can decline to sell at the mistaken price. The argument that the tag is an offer fails because it would bind a shop to every labelling error."}, {"id": 1273, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Mrs Edith Corrigan, aged 86, asks Philip Bryant, a solicitor in Stoke, to draft a new will leaving half of her estate, worth about £600,000, to Philip personally in gratitude for his years of help. She is mentally capable and says she wishes to leave it to him. Which of the following best describes how Philip should proceed?", "options": {"A": "He should decline to act, because the substantial gift creates an own interest conflict that consent cannot cure", "B": "He may act if she is mentally capable, because the conflict arises only where the client lacks mental capacity", "C": "He may act provided a colleague at the firm supervises the drafting and checks that she understands the gift", "D": "He may act provided the gift is recorded in a letter to Mrs Corrigan and he tells his firm's COLP and the other partners about it", "E": "He may act if Mrs Corrigan confirms her wishes in writing, because her informed consent removes the conflict"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He should decline to act, because the substantial gift creates an own interest conflict that consent cannot cure): A solicitor must not act where there is an own interest conflict or a significant risk of one, and consent does not remove that bar. A gift of half the estate gives Philip a major personal stake in the instructions he would be taking. Supervision by a colleague at his own firm does not make the advice independent of his interest, and her capacity is a separate question. He should decline and arrange for independent advice elsewhere."}, {"id": 1274, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Ken and Lee agree to burgle a warehouse. Ken, the lookout, knows Lee sometimes carries a knife and thinks Lee might use it if disturbed, though Ken does not want that to happen. During the burglary the night watchman disturbs Lee, who stabs him fatally, intending to kill. Ken is charged with murder as a secondary party. Which of the following best states what the prosecution must prove against Ken?", "options": {"A": "That Ken intended to assist or encourage Lee to kill or cause really serious harm, foresight being only evidence of intention", "B": "That Ken took part in the burglary, because a participant in a joint enterprise is liable for any offence committed by another participant", "C": "That Ken intended Lee to commit the burglary and knew a knife was carried, because knowledge of the weapon establishes murder liability", "D": "That Ken was present when the stabbing occurred and did nothing to prevent it, because presence at the scene can amount to encouragement", "E": "That Ken foresaw Lee might kill or cause really serious harm in the course of the burglary, because foresight is enough for murder"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (That Ken intended to assist or encourage Lee to kill or cause really serious harm, foresight being only evidence of intention): Since R v Jogee the mental element for secondary liability is an intention to assist or encourage the principal to commit the offence, with knowledge of the facts that make it criminal. Conditional intention is enough, but foresight of what the principal might do is only evidence from which the jury may infer that intention. The old rule that foresight alone sufficed was overruled. Taking part in the burglary and knowing about a knife do not alone make Ken guilty of murder."}, {"id": 1275, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Zainab Hussain is buying an unregistered freehold house. The seller's solicitor has produced three documents: a conveyance of the whole freehold on sale dated 12 years ago, a legal charge over the whole property dated 18 years ago, and a conveyance of the whole freehold on sale dated 23 years ago. Nothing earlier than these has been traced. Which document is a good root of title?", "options": {"A": "The conveyance dated 12 years ago, because the buyer is entitled to the most recent document that deals with the whole estate", "B": "The legal charge dated 18 years ago, because it is more than 15 years old and shows the owner's title to the land", "C": "Any of the three, because a root of title may be a document of any age that describes the land", "D": "The conveyance dated 23 years ago, because it is more than 15 years old and deals with the whole legal and equitable estate", "E": "None of them, because a good root of title must be at least 40 years old and describe the land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The conveyance dated 23 years ago, because it is more than 15 years old and deals with the whole legal and equitable estate): A good root of title must be at least 15 years old, deal with the whole legal and equitable estate, and contain a description of the land. The 23 year old conveyance on sale satisfies all three requirements. The 12 year old conveyance is too recent, and a legal charge deals only with a mortgagee's interest rather than the whole estate. The forty year period was replaced by the shorter statutory period."}, {"id": 1276, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Liability has been admitted by the defendant in a claim brought by Ms Rowan, a 29-year-old who suffered a spinal injury in a cycling accident. The defendant is insured and trial on quantum is not expected for fifteen months. Ms Rowan needs to adapt her home and pay for private rehabilitation now. Her solicitor wants to obtain money in advance of trial without giving up any part of the claim. Which of the following is the most appropriate step?", "options": {"A": "Apply for an interim payment, but only with the defendant's written consent, as insured defendants cannot be compelled", "B": "Apply for a periodical payments order, which can only be made once the final award of damages has been assessed", "C": "Apply for provisional damages, which the court can order now and then top up once the true extent of loss is known", "D": "Apply for an interim payment, which the court can order as liability is admitted, up to a proportion of likely damages", "E": "Wait until trial, because the court has no power to order payment before the damages have been finally assessed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply for an interim payment, which the court can order as liability is admitted, up to a proportion of likely damages): Where liability is admitted and the defendant is insured, the court has power to order an interim payment of a reasonable proportion of the likely final damages. It is an advance on the eventual award, so Ms Rowan keeps her whole claim. Provisional damages are a different remedy, used where a specific disease or deterioration may follow. The court can order an interim payment without the defendant's agreement."}, {"id": 1277, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Esme Walsh and her lender in the purchase of a freehold house for £275,000. The mortgage offer is based on a deposit of £27,500 which Esme says comes from her father, Mr Walsh, as a gift. Mr Walsh would like to live in the house during his retirement. The lender's instructions require confirmation that any gifted deposit is not repayable and gives the donor no interest. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a signed letter from Mr Walsh confirming a genuine gift, and tell the lender before completion if he expects a home there", "B": "Obtain the letter, but tell the lender only if Esme gives written permission, because her instructions on confidentiality come first", "C": "Accept Esme's word that the deposit is a gift, because a lender does not need evidence of the source of a deposit", "D": "Ask Mr Walsh to take a second charge over the house to protect his gift, so that the lender's security is not affected", "E": "Draw up a deed recording that Mr Walsh may live in the house, and send the lender a copy after completion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Obtain a signed letter from Mr Walsh confirming a genuine gift, and tell the lender before completion if he expects a home there): Where a lender requires confirmation that a deposit is a gift with no repayment and no interest for the donor, the solicitor must obtain proper evidence, usually a signed letter. If Mr Walsh expects a right to live there the position differs from the lender's assumptions, and acting for both parties the solicitor must tell the lender before completion. Esme's confidentiality cannot override the lender's instructions, and a second charge would damage the lender's security."}, {"id": 1278, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Sam pleaded guilty at the magistrates' court to common assault and was sentenced to a community order. He thinks the order is too harsh and asks his solicitor what rights of appeal he has and how quickly he must act. His case was heard in the magistrates' court on a Tuesday morning. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "He may appeal to the Crown Court against sentence only, giving written notice within 15 business days of the sentence", "B": "He may appeal to the Crown Court against conviction and sentence, giving notice within 15 business days of the sentence", "C": "He may appeal to the Crown Court against sentence only, but only with leave granted by the magistrates' court", "D": "He may appeal to the High Court by case stated against the sentence, giving notice within 21 days of sentence", "E": "He may appeal to the Crown Court against sentence only, giving notice within 28 days of the date of sentence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He may appeal to the Crown Court against sentence only, giving written notice within 15 business days of the sentence): A defendant who pleaded guilty can appeal to the Crown Court against sentence only, unless the plea was equivocal. Notice of appeal goes to the magistrates' court and the prosecution within 15 business days of sentence. No leave is needed. Case stated is limited to errors of law or excess of jurisdiction and cannot challenge a sentence merely as too severe."}, {"id": 1279, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A firm has received a production order from the Crown Court requiring it to hand over its file on a client, Mr Fraser, who is under investigation for fraud. The senior partner, Gillian, knows of the order. A junior colleague suggests removing a few internal notes that record the firm's own doubts about Mr Fraser's explanations, because they might embarrass the firm. Gillian is tempted to agree because the notes are not privileged. Which of the following is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The notes may be removed if the senior partner records her reasons, because the offence applies to documents belonging to the client", "B": "Removing the notes risks the offence of prejudicing an investigation, which covers concealing documents, so the file must be produced intact", "C": "The notes may be removed once a copy is kept, because the offence is committed if the information cannot be recovered from any source", "D": "The notes may be removed if the nominated officer approves, because the offence applies to individual staff and not to the firm's policy", "E": "The notes may be withheld, because the firm's own internal opinions are not client documents and fall outside the production order"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Removing the notes risks the offence of prejudicing an investigation, which covers concealing documents, so the file must be produced intact): Section 342 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 makes it an offence to falsify, conceal, destroy or dispose of documents that are relevant to an investigation, knowing or suspecting that an investigation is under way. Internal notes on the file are within the scope of the order and cannot be removed because they are embarrassing. Keeping a copy or recording reasons does not cure the concealment, and no internal approval can authorise it. The firm must produce the file intact and take advice on any privilege claims."}, {"id": 1280, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Your client, a landlord, has agreed to grant a ten-year lease of a shop and the lease will be signed and delivered on 1 September. The client tells you to date the document 1 June so that the rent review date and a tenant's break date calculated from it fall earlier than the parties agreed, which would suit the landlord. The tenant's solicitors have not noticed the point. Which of the following is the correct action?", "options": {"A": "Insert 1 June as the date on the counterpart only, keeping the original dated 1 September, so each copy is accurate", "B": "Stop acting immediately and report the client to the SRA, because the instruction shows an intention to deceive", "C": "Date the lease 1 June as instructed, because the term, rent review and break dates are matters for the parties and the client is entitled to decide them", "D": "Date the lease 1 June but write to the tenant's solicitors afterwards to explain that the date is not the date of signing", "E": "Refuse to misdate the lease, and advise that the real date of delivery must be shown while any agreed term start date is stated separately"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Refuse to misdate the lease, and advise that the real date of delivery must be shown while any agreed term start date is stated separately): You must not mislead the other side or the court and must act with integrity, and a false date designed to benefit the client at the tenant's expense is misleading. The correct step is to decline and to advise the client that the document carries its true date of delivery, with any agreed term start date set out in its own provision. An afterthought letter does not cure the problem. Reporting the client at once is premature when advice may resolve the instruction, and different dates on two parts of one deed would create confusion."}, {"id": 1281, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Uma bought a house in 2015 in her sole name, paying the deposit herself and taking out a mortgage. In 2018 her friend Vik moved in and, from then until 2024, paid £400 each month directly to the mortgage lender. Nothing was said about ownership. Uma and Vik have fallen out and Vik claims a share of the house based on his payments. Which of the following is the best analysis?", "options": {"A": "Mortgage payments made after purchase do not found a resulting trust, so Vik needs a common intention constructive trust", "B": "Vik has a half share under a resulting trust, because he and Uma shared the house and its running costs", "C": "Vik has no claim of any kind, because a person who is not on the legal title cannot acquire a beneficial interest", "D": "Vik has a resulting trust share in proportion to the instalments he paid, because mortgage payments count as purchase money", "E": "Vik has a presumed resulting trust, because he made voluntary payments for Uma's benefit towards the house"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mortgage payments made after purchase do not found a resulting trust, so Vik needs a common intention constructive trust): A resulting trust depends on contributions to the purchase price at the time of acquisition, and payments made years after the purchase do not qualify (Curley v Parkes). Vik's route to a share therefore lies in a constructive trust, which needs a common intention shared by the parties. Being off the title does not rule out a beneficial interest, but the resulting trust analysis fails on these facts. Sharing running costs does not itself produce any particular fraction of the property."}, {"id": 1282, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Hugh Armstrong died leaving a will that gives a pecuniary legacy of £15,000 to a friend and the whole of the residue to his nephew. The will fails to dispose of a small plot of land Hugh owned, which therefore passes on intestacy. The will says nothing about how debts are to be paid. Hugh's estate is solvent, but there is not enough cash to pay his £8,000 of unsecured debts. The executor asks the solicitor which assets are applied first to pay the debts under the statutory order. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The plot of land is exempt from debts, because land passing on intestacy is taken by the heirs free of the deceased's liabilities", "B": "The plot passing on partial intestacy is used first, because undisposed property is applied before the residue and before any legacies", "C": "The pecuniary legacy is cut back first, because general legacies bear debts before any property passing to a residuary beneficiary", "D": "The residue is used first, because the residuary gift is the primary fund for debts and undisposed property is applied only later", "E": "The debts are shared between the plot of land, the legacy and the residue in proportion to their values or the order the testator would have wished"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The plot passing on partial intestacy is used first, because undisposed property is applied before the residue and before any legacies): Under the statutory order for a solvent estate, property not disposed of by the will is applied first, then the residue, and legacies and specific gifts come later. The plot of land, passing on partial intestacy, is therefore the first fund. Because the will does not give a different direction, the statutory order applies and there is no proportionate sharing. Land passing on intestacy remains subject to the deceased's debts."}, {"id": 1283, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Karl was the sole director of a company that went into insolvent liquidation. The liquidator reported on his conduct and the Secretary of State applied to the court, which found that Karl's conduct as a director made him unfit to be concerned in the management of a company. A friend of Karl asks what follows from the finding. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court must disqualify him for at least two years, but acting in breach of the order carries only civil liability and no criminal penalty for him", "B": "The court must disqualify him for a minimum of five years, and the order is limited to directorships of insolvent companies", "C": "The court has a discretion whether to disqualify, and any order it makes cannot exceed five years in length", "D": "The court must make a disqualification order of at least two years, and acting as a director during it is an offence that risks personal liability", "E": "The court must disqualify him for at least two years, and the order lapses automatically if the liquidator is paid in full"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court must make a disqualification order of at least two years, and acting as a director during it is an offence that risks personal liability): Under section 6 Company Directors Disqualification Act 1986 the court must make a disqualification order where a director of an insolvent company is found unfit, and the minimum period is two years with a maximum of fifteen. Acting as a director, or being involved in managing a company, while disqualified is a criminal offence and makes the person personally liable for the company's debts incurred in that time. The order is not confined to insolvent companies and does not lapse because the liquidator is paid."}, {"id": 1284, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Ingrid has sued her former landlord for a sum she says was agreed in an oral variation of her lease. Before issuing, she secretly recorded a telephone call with the landlord's agent, Mr Farrow, in which he appears to confirm the variation. She did not tell him she was recording. The landlord's solicitor has said the recording must be excluded because it was made without consent. The call was recorded on her own telephone in her own flat. How is the court likely to treat the recording?", "options": {"A": "It is automatically inadmissible, because evidence recorded without the other party's consent is a breach of the right to privacy", "B": "It is admissible as of right, because relevance alone decides admissibility and the court has no power to exclude relevant evidence", "C": "It is inadmissible unless it was disclosed to the landlord with the claim form, because late disclosed recordings are excluded", "D": "It is likely to be admitted if relevant, because the court has a discretion over evidence and weighs how it was obtained", "E": "It is admissible only if Ingrid first gives the landlord 21 days' notice under the Civil Evidence Act 1995 of her intention to rely on it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is likely to be admitted if relevant, because the court has a discretion over evidence and weighs how it was obtained): In civil proceedings relevant evidence is generally admissible, and the court has wide powers under CPR 32.1 to control evidence, including excluding it. Evidence obtained in a way the other party dislikes is not automatically inadmissible; the court balances the manner of obtaining it against the interests of justice and the overriding objective. A recording of the landlord's own agent is not excluded just because Ingrid made it covertly, though the court may reduce its weight. No special 21 day notice applies to a recording of this kind."}, {"id": 1285, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Fairfield Packaging Ltd is defendant to a claim. The directions order required witness statements to be exchanged by 4 pm on 15 July. The solicitor, Brendan, realises on 18 July that he diarised the wrong date and did not serve the statements. The claimant has not applied for any sanction, and the trial is six months away. The client has not been told. Brendan is tempted to serve the statements quietly and hope that the other side does not object. What should Brendan do?", "options": {"A": "Serve the statements at once and say nothing more unless the claimant complains, because no sanction has been sought", "B": "Tell the claimant's solicitors about the lateness but not the client, because the client's interests do not require a report", "C": "Tell the client promptly of the error and its possible consequences, then seek the other side's agreement or apply for relief without delay", "D": "Wait until the claimant objects, then rely on the delay being minor and the trial being far off to resist any sanction", "E": "Apply for relief from sanctions at once but delay telling the client until the outcome is known, to avoid needless alarm"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tell the client promptly of the error and its possible consequences, then seek the other side's agreement or apply for relief without delay): A solicitor who discovers that he has made an error that may harm the client must tell the client promptly and explain what is being done to put it right. The sensible practical steps are to seek the other side's agreement to the late exchange and, if that is refused, to apply promptly for relief. Quietly serving the statements or concealing the error from the client is dishonest conduct towards the client. Waiting for the claimant to complain also risks a worse position, since promptness counts in any application for relief."}, {"id": 1286, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Winnie and Beryl Adeyemi, sisters, hold a house as beneficial joint tenants, subject to a bank mortgage which both are paying. Winnie dies suddenly without having made a will. Her adult son, Tobi, says that under the intestacy rules he inherits his mother's half of the house, and asks that Beryl pay him half of the value. No notice of severance was ever served, and nothing was written about the shares. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Beryl takes the house but must account to Tobi for half its equity, because the mortgage was jointly paid", "B": "Tobi takes half the house, because Winnie's contribution to the mortgage created an equal beneficial share in her own right", "C": "Beryl takes the whole house, because survivorship passes Winnie's interest to her before any intestacy rules apply", "D": "Tobi and Beryl hold the house as tenants in common, because Winnie's death severs the joint tenancy automatically", "E": "Tobi takes half the house, because on intestacy a child inherits the share of a parent who dies without a will"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Beryl takes the whole house, because survivorship passes Winnie's interest to her before any intestacy rules apply): Beneficial joint tenants hold the whole property together with no separate shares, so on a joint tenant's death the survivor takes the whole by survivorship. This operates automatically and outside the deceased's estate, so neither a will nor the intestacy rules can pass Winnie's interest to Tobi. Paying the mortgage jointly does not sever the tenancy or create separate shares. Tobi has no claim against the property, though Beryl remains liable to the lender for the mortgage."}, {"id": 1287, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Hodge & Co acts for Dilys, charged as principal with robbery, and for her co-defendant Ewan, charged as an accomplice who drove the getaway car. Both are interviewed on the same day. Ewan tells the firm he only drove because Dilys threatened him and he wishes to give evidence against her. Dilys has told the firm she acted alone. What should the firm do?", "options": {"A": "Continue to act for both but tell each only what suits their defence, so that neither is prejudiced by the other's instructions during the trial", "B": "Continue to act for Ewan and pass his instructions to Dilys, because disclosure of the duress claim is in the public interest", "C": "Continue to act for Dilys, and use what Ewan said to challenge his account because both are current clients owed equal duties", "D": "Continue to act for both, because the firm can run a joint defence and ask each client to waive confidentiality in writing", "E": "Cease acting for at least one of them, and probably both, because their interests conflict and the firm holds each client's confidences"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cease acting for at least one of them, and probably both, because their interests conflict and the firm holds each client's confidences): The SRA Code does not allow a firm to act for two clients in the same matter where their interests conflict, unless there is a substantial common interest, and none exists when one blames the other. The firm also holds confidential information from both and cannot use or disclose either client's information for the other. It cannot run a joint defence when one claims duress from the other. The firm should stop acting for at least one and, given what it knows, probably both."}, {"id": 1288, "domain": "land_law", "question": "In 2005 a developer sold a house to Alan and Alan covenanted with the developer, for himself and his successors, not to use the land other than as a private dwelling. The covenant was expressed to benefit the developer's retained land, and it was noted on the charges register of Alan's title. In 2024 Alan sold to Beth, who has opened a dental practice in the house. Cora, who now owns part of the developer's retained land, wants to stop the use. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Beth is bound, but Cora can sue only if she can show the developer assigned the covenant to her expressly", "B": "Beth is not bound, because the burden of a covenant cannot run with freehold land at law and she gave no express promise", "C": "Beth is not bound, because the covenant restricts business use and a restriction on use cannot touch and concern the land", "D": "Beth is bound only to the extent that Alan remains liable, so Cora must first sue Alan and cannot sue Beth directly for an injunction", "E": "The burden passed to Beth in equity because the covenant is restrictive and protected by notice on the register, so Cora may seek an injunction"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The burden passed to Beth in equity because the covenant is restrictive and protected by notice on the register, so Cora may seek an injunction): At common law the burden of a freehold covenant does not pass, but in equity a restrictive covenant binds a successor under the rule in Tulk v Moxhay where it is negative, touches and concerns the land, was intended to bind successors and, here, is protected by notice on the register. The covenant expressly benefits the retained land, so its benefit was annexed and passes to Cora without a separate assignment. A restriction to residential use plainly touches and concerns land. Beth is therefore directly bound and Cora may seek an injunction against her."}, {"id": 1289, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A notice to complete was served on Bianca, the buyer of a house for £340,000, and expired without her completing. Her deposit of £34,000 is held by the seller's solicitor. The seller, Desmond, was ready to complete throughout and has found a new buyer at £325,000 who wants to proceed quickly. Desmond has also said he is concerned about recovering his costs. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice for Desmond?", "options": {"A": "Seek specific performance against Bianca first, because the contract cannot be ended without an order of the court", "B": "Rescind and keep the deposit, but make no claim for damages, because forfeiture of the deposit is the whole of the remedy", "C": "Rescind the contract, forfeit the deposit and resell, then claim any further loss from Bianca as damages", "D": "Rescind and return the deposit to Bianca, because a seller who resells cannot also keep the deposit paid", "E": "Resell first and then serve a fresh notice to complete on Bianca, because the contract still has to be ended after the resale"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rescind the contract, forfeit the deposit and resell, then claim any further loss from Bianca as damages): Once a valid notice to complete has expired, the seller may rescind, forfeit the deposit and resell, and may also claim damages for any loss beyond the deposit. Desmond has a ready buyer, so specific performance would be inconsistent with his plans. Resale before rescission risks a conflict between two contracts. The deposit is forfeited by the defaulting buyer, and further loss such as a lower resale price can be claimed."}, {"id": 1290, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Femi Adeyemi, who is buying a freehold semi-detached house for £385,000 with cash. The local authority search shows that a rear extension, built in 2018 by the seller, had planning permission but no building regulations completion certificate. The seller says the builder dealt with it, and Femi is keen to exchange within the week. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Proceed and let Femi apply for a completion certificate after completion, because only the owner of the property can apply for one", "B": "Ask the seller for a regularisation certificate, or suggest indemnity insurance if the council has not been approached, then report to Femi", "C": "Write to the council asking whether it intends to enforce, then buy indemnity insurance once the council has replied favourably to the buyer", "D": "Require the seller to demolish the extension before completion, because work without a completion certificate cannot lawfully remain", "E": "Exchange as planned because planning permission was granted, and planning permission is sufficient evidence that the extension is lawful and safe"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the seller for a regularisation certificate, or suggest indemnity insurance if the council has not been approached, then report to Femi): Planning permission and building regulations approval are separate, so the missing completion certificate is a genuine issue to deal with before exchange. The usual solutions are a regularisation certificate obtained by the seller or, if the council has not been contacted, indemnity insurance. Contacting the council first is a trap, because insurers refuse cover where the authority has already raised the problem. Demolition is an excessive response, and delay until after completion leaves Femi with the risk."}, {"id": 1291, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Mr Quinn paid £25,000 into a savings account in the sole name of his daughter Rhiannon, then aged 15, in 2010. He told nobody why. In 2024 he said that he had meant her to hold it only for safekeeping and asked for it back. Rhiannon, now an adult, says the money was a gift. Which of the following is the most likely position?", "options": {"A": "Mr Quinn recovers it, because a voluntary transfer to any recipient raises a presumption of a resulting trust for the transferor", "B": "Rhiannon holds the money on constructive trust for her father, because keeping it would be unconscionable now he has asked for it", "C": "Rhiannon keeps it, because a gift to a minor cannot be recovered by the donor after she reaches 18", "D": "Rhiannon keeps it, because the presumption of advancement covers a father's gift and his later words carry little weight", "E": "Mr Quinn recovers it, because his own evidence of his intention outweighs the presumption once the daughter is an adult"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rhiannon keeps it, because the presumption of advancement covers a father's gift and his later words carry little weight): Where a father makes a voluntary transfer to his child, the presumption of advancement replaces the presumption of resulting trust and treats the transfer as a gift. It can be rebutted only by evidence of the donor's intention at the time of the transfer, and what Mr Quinn said fourteen years later carries little weight. Rhiannon's age does not change the presumption. A constructive trust is not available just because the donor wants the money back."}, {"id": 1292, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Okonkwo, aged 41, fractured his knee in a workplace accident caused by his employer's negligence. Medical evidence shows a 20 per cent risk that he will develop serious osteoarthritis in the joint within ten years. The employer's insurer offers £60,000 in full and final settlement, which covers his present losses. Mr Okonkwo is keen to accept and end the matter, and he asks his solicitor for advice. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Advise him to ask for an interim payment now, which will let him claim more later if osteoarthritis develops", "B": "Advise him to accept, because the risk is below fifty per cent and the court would discount the claim for it", "C": "Advise him to accept and then issue a second claim if osteoarthritis develops, as a settlement bars only present losses", "D": "Advise him to delay settlement for ten years until the osteoarthritis risk can be assessed with certainty", "E": "Advise that provisional damages would allow a later claim if osteoarthritis develops, which a full settlement would bar"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Advise that provisional damages would allow a later claim if osteoarthritis develops, which a full settlement would bar): Where there is a proved chance of a serious deterioration, the court can award provisional damages on the basis that it will not occur, with the claimant free to return for more if it does. A full and final settlement ends the claim for all future loss, so accepting it would leave Mr Okonkwo uncompensated if osteoarthritis develops. The solicitor's duty to act in the client's best interests and to give him the information he needs to decide requires advice on this remedy before he accepts. An interim payment is only an advance on the final award and gives no right to a later claim."}, {"id": 1293, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Priya Nair, who is buying a registered freehold house from Gareth Pryce for £420,000. The official copies show Gareth as sole proprietor with title absolute and no restriction. In his replies to the Property Information Form, Gareth says that his partner Lina has lived in the house with him for eight years and that she contributed to the original deposit. Gareth's mortgage will be repaid on completion. What is the most appropriate step for you to take?", "options": {"A": "Accept Gareth's written assurance that Lina has no interest, because the seller's replies bind him and protect the buyer on completion", "B": "Require Gareth to register a restriction against his own title before exchange, so that Lina's interest is protected and the buyer is safe", "C": "Raise an enquiry about Lina's interest and require her to sign a waiver or postponement of it, because she may be in actual occupation", "D": "Advise Priya that Lina's contribution to the deposit is only a personal claim against Gareth and cannot affect the house", "E": "Rely on the register, because no restriction or notice protects Lina and a buyer for value takes free of any interest that is not entered"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raise an enquiry about Lina's interest and require her to sign a waiver or postponement of it, because she may be in actual occupation): A person in actual occupation with a beneficial interest, here Lina through her contribution to the deposit, can hold an overriding interest that binds a buyer even though it is not on the register. The sensible step is to enquire and to require Lina to sign a waiver or postponement so that the buyer takes free of her claim. Gareth's assurance would not bind Lina, and reliance on the register alone is the error that overriding interests are designed to punish."}, {"id": 1294, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Sam is walking home when Gareth, who dislikes him, punches him in the face intending to break his jaw and cause him serious injury. Gareth did not want Sam to die and had not thought about it. Sam falls backwards, strikes his head on the kerb and dies at the scene from a fractured skull. Gareth is charged with murder. Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": {"A": "Gareth has the malice aforethought for murder, because an intention to cause really serious bodily harm is sufficient without any intention to kill", "B": "Gareth is guilty of murder only if he foresaw death as a virtually certain consequence, since foresight of serious harm cannot found the mental element", "C": "Gareth is guilty of unlawful act manslaughter and not murder, because the death resulted from the fall rather than the punch itself", "D": "Gareth has a defence, because the death of the victim was an unforeseeable consequence of a single punch directed at the jaw", "E": "Gareth can be convicted only of manslaughter, because murder requires proof that he intended to kill the victim when he struck the blow"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gareth has the malice aforethought for murder, because an intention to cause really serious bodily harm is sufficient without any intention to kill): Murder requires an unlawful killing with malice aforethought, which is either an intention to kill or an intention to cause grievous bodily harm. Gareth intended to break Sam's jaw, which is really serious harm, so the mental element is made out even though death was not wanted. The fall is a natural result of being punched and does not break the chain of causation. The suggestion that only manslaughter is available fails because it wrongly demands an intention to kill."}, {"id": 1295, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Hana, aged 15, pleads guilty at the youth court to actual bodily harm. She has never been convicted of any offence before. The offence was a single punch in a school playground during an argument, causing a bruise. The court considers that custody and an absolute discharge are both unnecessary. Which of the following is correct about the sentence?", "options": {"A": "The court must make a referral order, as it is her first conviction, she pleaded guilty and no excluded sentence applies", "B": "A referral order is available only if Hana pleaded not guilty and was convicted after a trial", "C": "A referral order cannot be made for an offence of violence, which requires a youth rehabilitation order instead", "D": "A referral order is available only if Hana consents and her parent agrees to attend panel meetings", "E": "A referral order may be made at the court's discretion, but a youth rehabilitation order is the usual sentence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court must make a referral order, as it is her first conviction, she pleaded guilty and no excluded sentence applies): A referral order is compulsory where a young offender pleads guilty to all the imprisonable offences before the court and has no previous convictions, unless the court imposes custody, a hospital order or an absolute discharge. Hana meets those conditions, so a youth offender panel will agree a contract with her. The order does not depend on her consent, and violence does not exclude it."}, {"id": 1296, "domain": "land_law", "question": "In 2015, Ingrid Bray sold off part of her registered freehold land to Joel Pryce, who covenanted for himself and his successors not to use the plot for business purposes. The covenant was never entered as a notice against Joel's title. In 2022 Joel sold the plot for market value to Sunita Basra, who plans to run a commercial bakery there. Sunita registered as proprietor and did not know of the covenant. Can Ingrid enforce the covenant against Sunita?", "options": {"A": "No, because a restrictive covenant lapses on a sale of the burdened land and cannot bind a later buyer", "B": "No, because the covenant was unprotected and Sunita's registered purchase for value takes priority under section 29", "C": "Yes, because Ingrid has an overriding interest as the original covenantee and a purchaser takes subject to it", "D": "Yes, because Sunita is bound once she is shown to have constructive notice of the covenant through inspection of the title", "E": "Yes, because a restrictive covenant burdens the land in equity and binds every successor in title, including those unaware of it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, because the covenant was unprotected and Sunita's registered purchase for value takes priority under section 29): In registered land a restrictive covenant is an equitable interest that must be protected by a notice against the burdened title, since it is not an overriding interest. Ingrid failed to protect it, so Sunita's registered purchase for valuable consideration takes priority under section 29 of the Land Registration Act 2002. Knowledge, actual or constructive, does not alter priority in the registered system. Ingrid may have a personal claim against Joel on his covenant, but not against Sunita."}, {"id": 1297, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Tariq's bank mistakenly credits £800 to his current account instead of to the intended customer's. Tariq notices the extra money two days later, realises it is a mistake, and spends it on a holiday, intending never to repay. He is charged with theft. His solicitor argues that the money was never property belonging to another once it reached his own account. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The money is treated as belonging to the bank, because Tariq was under an obligation to restore it, so theft can be established", "B": "The money belongs to Tariq once credited, because ownership of money in a bank account passes to the account holder, so theft fails", "C": "Theft can be established only if the bank told Tariq of the error, because knowledge of the mistake is required", "D": "The money belongs to the bank, but theft cannot arise because Tariq did not take it from the bank by any act of appropriation", "E": "Theft cannot be established, because Tariq received the money innocently and his later dishonesty cannot turn it into stolen property"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The money is treated as belonging to the bank, because Tariq was under an obligation to restore it, so theft can be established): Where a person receives property by another's mistake and is under an obligation to make restoration, the property or its proceeds are treated as belonging to the other person. Tariq realised the mistake and spent the money, which is an assumption of the owner's rights, so the usual elements are met if he was dishonest. An innocent receipt does not prevent later appropriation. Notice from the bank is not needed, because the obligation arises from the mistake itself."}, {"id": 1298, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Priyanka acts for the defendant, Mr Hollis, in a contract claim. Disclosure has been given and the other side relies on an email from Mr Hollis in the trial bundle. In a conference, Mr Hollis admits to Priyanka that he edited the date on that email before it was disclosed, and tells her to continue relying on it at trial and not to tell the court. Trial is next week. Which of the following best describes what Priyanka should do?", "options": {"A": "She may continue to act if she cross examines on other documents, because the court is protected where the email is not central to the case", "B": "She must advise Mr Hollis to correct the position and, if he refuses, stop acting, because she cannot mislead the court or allow it to be misled", "C": "She may carry on acting but must not refer to the email herself, because confidentiality stops her telling the court and she need only avoid stating falsehoods", "D": "She must report Mr Hollis to the police at once, because the discovery of falsified evidence overrides her duty of confidentiality", "E": "She must continue to act until after trial, because ceasing to act a week before trial would prejudice the client and the court timetable"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She must advise Mr Hollis to correct the position and, if he refuses, stop acting, because she cannot mislead the court or allow it to be misled): A solicitor must not mislead or attempt to mislead the court, whether by their own acts or by allowing a client to do so. The duty to the court prevails over the duty to the client and over confidentiality where the two conflict. Priyanka must advise Mr Hollis to correct the evidence, and if he will not, she cannot continue to act because she would be allowing the court to rely on a document she knows is false. Confidentiality does not require her to report him to the police, and the nearness of trial does not change the position."}, {"id": 1299, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ella, aged 16, is convicted at the youth court of repeated theft from shops. Previous reprimands and a fine have not stopped her. The court is considering a youth rehabilitation order with supervision, a curfew and unpaid work. The report also describes some mental health needs. Ella's solicitor is advising on the order. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The order can last up to 12 months, which is the maximum for any youth court community sentence", "B": "The order can last up to 2 years and may combine requirements, but the court needs a parent's consent to each", "C": "The order can last up to 3 years, but unpaid work cannot be included unless she is aged 18 or over", "D": "The order can last up to 3 years but may contain only one requirement at a time", "E": "The order can last up to 3 years and may combine requirements, including unpaid work as she is 16"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The order can last up to 3 years and may combine requirements, including unpaid work as she is 16): A youth rehabilitation order can last up to three years and the court can combine several requirements in one order. The unpaid work requirement is available only to 16 and 17 year olds, which makes it open here. Parental consent is not needed for each requirement. A one year cap and a single requirement rule do not exist."}, {"id": 1300, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Pat hits Quinn on the head with a baseball bat, intending to cause him really serious injury but not to kill him. Quinn is rushed to hospital and recovers. Pat accepts the blow was struck deliberately and that Quinn could easily have died. Pat is charged with attempted murder. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Attempted murder is made out, because recklessness as to death is sufficient mens rea for an attempt on these facts", "B": "Attempted murder is made out, because the mental element for an attempt is the same as for the full offence of murder", "C": "Attempted murder fails, because the offence requires that Pat should have performed the final act he thought necessary to kill Quinn", "D": "Attempted murder requires an intention to kill, so an intention to cause really serious harm is not enough and the charge fails", "E": "Attempted murder is made out, because a defendant who intends serious harm and strikes the head is taken to intend death in law"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Attempted murder requires an intention to kill, so an intention to cause really serious harm is not enough and the charge fails): Under section 1 of the Criminal Attempts Act 1981 the defendant must intend to commit the full offence, and for murder that means an intention to kill. The wider murder mental element of intending really serious harm does not carry over to an attempt. Pat's intention to cause serious injury is therefore not enough, and recklessness as to death is also not enough. Pat may still face an attempt under section 18 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861 instead."}, {"id": 1301, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Yolanda Clarke instructed a solicitor to buy an unregistered freehold barn from Kenneth Vaughan under a written contract. The solicitor failed to register the contract as a land charge before Kenneth sold the barn to a third party, who bought for value and took the legal title. Yolanda has now lost the barn. The solicitor discovers the omission while reviewing the file and is worried about the firm's reputation. What must the solicitor now do under the SRA Code of Conduct?", "options": {"A": "Tell Yolanda promptly what went wrong and its effect, advise her that she may have a claim, and report the matter to the firm's insurers", "B": "Tell Yolanda about the omission but keep the matter within the firm, because insurers need be told only if she sues", "C": "Try to repair the position quietly by negotiating with the new owner, and tell Yolanda only if the negotiation fails", "D": "Offer Yolanda a discount on future work and record the error on file without giving a formal explanation", "E": "Wait to see whether Yolanda raises a complaint herself, since a solicitor need disclose an error only when asked"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tell Yolanda promptly what went wrong and its effect, advise her that she may have a claim, and report the matter to the firm's insurers): The SRA Code of Conduct requires a solicitor to tell a client promptly if something has gone wrong, to explain the position, and to put matters right where possible. The solicitor must also act honestly and in line with the firm's duty to notify its insurers in the proper way. Silence or delay breaches those duties, and a discount does not replace an explanation. An unregistered estate contract is void against a purchaser for value of the legal estate, so the loss of the barn follows directly from the omission."}, {"id": 1302, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A district council has power under statute to grant market stall licences. It adopts a policy that no applicant who previously breached any licence condition will be granted a new licence. Daniel Osei applies, having breached a minor condition twelve years ago, and the licensing officer refuses at once without considering his circumstances, saying the policy leaves no room for exceptions. Daniel asks which ground of judicial review is most apt. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Illegality, because a body may adopt a policy but unlawfully fetters its discretion by refusing to consider any case that merits an exception", "B": "Irrationality, because a policy based on a past breach of a minor condition is so unreasonable that no council could adopt it", "C": "Procedural impropriety, because the officer decided without giving Daniel any hearing or chance to explain his circumstances before the refusal was made", "D": "No ground, because consistency is the aim of a licensing policy and a body may apply it strictly in every case", "E": "Illegality, because the council had no power to adopt a licensing policy and so the policy itself is ultra vires"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Illegality, because a body may adopt a policy but unlawfully fetters its discretion by refusing to consider any case that merits an exception): A public body with a statutory discretion may adopt a policy to guide its decisions, but it must remain willing to consider whether an individual case justifies an exception. Refusing to look at Daniel's circumstances because the policy 'leaves no room' is an unlawful fetter on the discretion, which is a form of illegality. The council did have power to adopt a policy, and the problem lies in how it was applied. The harshness of the policy and the lack of a hearing are secondary to that flaw."}, {"id": 1303, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Norwood Bakery had its industrial oven repaired by Selby Engineering, which was told only that the oven was needed for normal baking. Selby returned the oven two weeks late. Norwood lost £8,000 of ordinary trade profit during the delay and lost a £30,000 one-off contract to supply an event, which Selby did not know about. Norwood claims both sums. Which of the following describes the damages Norwood can recover?", "options": {"A": "Nominal damages, because Norwood had not warned Selby of the amount of profit it would lose in the event of delay", "B": "£38,000, because Selby knew the oven was for baking and the type of loss, loss of profit, was therefore foreseeable", "C": "£38,000, because the late repair was the factual cause of both losses and any loss caused by the breach can be recovered", "D": "£8,000 plus the event loss, because remoteness is judged by what Norwood contemplated and not by what Selby knew", "E": "£8,000 only, because the ordinary profit loss was within contemplation but the event contract was an unusual loss Selby did not know about"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (£8,000 only, because the ordinary profit loss was within contemplation but the event contract was an unusual loss Selby did not know about): Recovery is limited to loss that was reasonably contemplated by both parties at the time of contracting as likely to result from breach. Ordinary trade profit lost while a commercial oven is out of use is the kind of loss that arises naturally. The one off event contract was a special loss, and Selby had no knowledge of it. Remoteness looks at the defendant's knowledge as well, and a warning of the exact amount of loss is not required for the ordinary loss."}, {"id": 1304, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A planning committee votes to grant permission for a retail development. One councillor on the committee, Mr Hughes, owns most of the shares in the developer company but did not declare this and voted in favour. Residents who live next to the site learn of the shareholding after the decision. They ask a solicitor whether the decision can be challenged for bias and what they must prove.", "options": {"A": "The residents must show a real possibility of bias in the view of a fair minded observer, because automatic disqualification does not apply to councillors", "B": "The decision is voidable without proof of actual or apparent bias, because a decision maker with a direct financial interest is disqualified", "C": "The residents must prove actual bias on Mr Hughes' part, because bias is established only by evidence of a closed mind", "D": "The decision stands unless the residents prove that Mr Hughes' vote was needed to reach the majority in favour", "E": "The decision is valid, because bias is relevant only where the decision maker has acted as a judge in a court"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The decision is voidable without proof of actual or apparent bias, because a decision maker with a direct financial interest is disqualified): A decision maker with a direct financial interest in the outcome is automatically disqualified, so the decision can be set aside without any inquiry into actual or apparent bias. Mr Hughes owns most of the developer, which is a clear pecuniary interest. The apparent bias test applies to other kinds of connection, and it is not the starting point here. It does not matter that his vote was not decisive, and the rule applies to local authority committees as well as courts."}, {"id": 1305, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Aisha Rahman is a newly qualified solicitor preparing a short talk for trainees on how the Solicitors Regulation Authority supervises the profession. She explains that the SRA does not inspect every firm on a fixed rota. Instead it gathers information from reports of concern, accounts monitoring and client complaints, and then directs its resources towards the firms and conduct where the potential harm to the public is greatest. Which of the following best describes the approach Aisha is explaining?", "options": {"A": "A rules-based approach, under which every firm is inspected on a fixed cycle and each technical breach of the rules attracts the same standard sanction", "B": "A risk-based approach, under which supervision is focused on the firms and conduct posing the greatest risk to clients and the public", "C": "A co-regulatory approach, under which the Legal Services Board investigates individual solicitors and the SRA merely applies the sanctions it recommends", "D": "A complaints-led approach, under which the SRA acts only once a client has obtained a decision from the Legal Ombudsman", "E": "A self-regulatory approach, under which the Law Society's members vote on which firms are investigated and what sanction should be imposed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A risk-based approach, under which supervision is focused on the firms and conduct posing the greatest risk to clients and the public): The SRA applies risk-based regulation, using reports, monitoring and complaints to concentrate its attention where harm to clients and the public is most likely. That is why Aisha describes no fixed rota of inspections. A fixed cycle with uniform sanctions reflects a prescriptive model that the SRA does not follow and would spread resources thinly. The Legal Services Board oversees approved regulators but does not investigate individual solicitors, and the SRA does not wait for an Ombudsman decision before acting."}, {"id": 1306, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Whitcombe works at a rolling mill, where a dangerous unguarded roller breaches the employer's statutory duty to fence machinery. During a repetitive task late on a shift, with noise and fatigue, he momentarily forgets about the roller and his hand is drawn in. He is badly injured. The employer pleads contributory negligence, pointing to his experience and training. The mill's canteen had also just been refurbished. Which of the following best describes how the court is likely to approach the plea?", "options": {"A": "The court will reject the plea outright, because contributory negligence cannot apply where the employer is in breach of statutory duty", "B": "The court will apply a stricter standard to him than to ordinary people, because his training makes him a skilled worker", "C": "The court will reduce damages by half, because fault is split equally where both parties contribute to an accident", "D": "The court will reduce the damages substantially, because an experienced worker is expected to remember a danger he knows about", "E": "The court is slow to find fault in a worker's momentary inattention caused by repetitive work, so any reduction is likely to be small"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court is slow to find fault in a worker's momentary inattention caused by repetitive work, so any reduction is likely to be small): Contributory negligence is available against an employee, but the courts take account of the realities of the workplace, including noise, fatigue and repetition. A momentary lapse of attention by a worker doing a repetitive task is not treated harshly, and where the employer's breach created the danger, any reduction is small. The plea is not barred in principle, and there is no fixed equal division of fault. The canteen refurbishment is irrelevant."}, {"id": 1307, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya Nair is a duty solicitor called to a police station where Callum Reid has been arrested for criminal damage. While she waits to see him, Callum's brother Owen, who was with him that night and has not been arrested, phones the station and asks her to act for him too, saying he will be interviewed voluntarily next week and wants the same advice. Priya has so far seen only the custody record. What should Priya do?", "options": {"A": "Act for both brothers, provided each gives informed written consent after hearing about the risk that their interests may conflict", "B": "Decline both instructions, because Owen's approach means Priya can no longer advise Callum independently without a conflict of loyalties", "C": "See Callum in private and decline to act for Owen, because the brothers may give differing accounts of the same incident", "D": "See Owen first, because he is not in custody and can be advised without the delay that a detained suspect faces", "E": "Take Callum's instructions, then tell him what Owen said on the phone, because anything relevant to his defence must be passed on"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (See Callum in private and decline to act for Owen, because the brothers may give differing accounts of the same incident): Two people involved in the same incident are likely to have interests that diverge, for instance if one wishes to blame the other, so acting for both creates a significant risk of conflict under the SRA Code of Conduct. Consent does not cure a conflict of this kind between suspects who may give different accounts at interview. Priya's duty is to Callum, the client she was called to see, and what Owen said in confidence cannot be passed to him. Declining Owen does not stop her acting for Callum, as she has received nothing material from Owen that would limit her advice."}, {"id": 1308, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Immigration Fees Act requires a council to refuse accommodation to people with no recourse to public funds, in terms that leave it no choice. Mei Ling is refused housing by the council, which applies the Act exactly. The Act is later held by the court to be incompatible with article 8 and a declaration of incompatibility is made. Mei Ling wants damages from the council under section 8 of the Human Rights Act. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The council did not act unlawfully under section 6, because it was bound by primary legislation, so no damages are available against it", "B": "The council is liable in damages, because it infringed article 8 and should have declined to apply the incompatible Act", "C": "The council is liable only if it acted in bad faith, because good faith application of a statute is a complete defence", "D": "The council is liable in damages, because the declaration of incompatibility makes the Act void from the date it came into force", "E": "Damages are available against Parliament itself, because a declaration of incompatibility places responsibility on the legislature that passed the Act"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The council did not act unlawfully under section 6, because it was bound by primary legislation, so no damages are available against it): Under section 6(2)(a), a public authority does not act unlawfully if, because of primary legislation, it could not have acted differently. The council had no choice under the Act, so there is no unlawful act and no basis for damages under section 8. A declaration of incompatibility does not make the Act void and does not create liability for the authority that applied it. Parliament cannot be sued for damages, and the remedy for the incompatibility lies in amendment of the law."}, {"id": 1309, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Sentinel Metals plc publishes audited annual accounts, which the auditors, Marsh and Pyke, negligently approve despite serious overstatement of profits. Mr Dalton, an existing shareholder, reads the accounts and buys more shares to try to take over the company. The shares are in fact worth far less, and he loses £200,000. The auditors did not know of Mr Dalton's plan. He sues them in negligence. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because it was foreseeable that a shareholder would rely on the audited accounts to value shares", "B": "The claim fails, because the audit duty arises from the statute and does not cover investors making purchase decisions", "C": "The claim succeeds, because auditors owe a duty to every person who reads published accounts and then buys shares", "D": "The claim fails, because auditors owe a duty only to the company itself and not to individual shareholders in respect of any decision", "E": "The claim fails, because shareholders cannot rely on accounts and must carry out their own investigation of the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim fails, because the audit duty arises from the statute and does not cover investors making purchase decisions): Auditors preparing statutory accounts owe a duty to the company, and to shareholders collectively for the purpose of overseeing management, but not to individual investors using the accounts to decide whether to buy more shares. The purpose of the statement defines the scope of the duty, and Mr Dalton used the accounts for a different purpose. Foreseeability that someone may rely on the accounts is not enough. A shareholder can rely on accounts for the purpose for which they were prepared, so the rule is narrower than denying all reliance."}, {"id": 1310, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Priya Nair, a solicitor with a current practising certificate, acts for a client buying a villa in Portugal. The Portuguese vendor's lawyers ask that the client's power of attorney be authenticated by a notarial act so that it will be accepted abroad. Priya has not been appointed a notary public. Which of the following is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "Priya may delegate the act to a supervised paralegal, because notarial work is not reserved and can be done by anyone", "B": "Priya may perform the act herself, because holding a practising certificate authorises a solicitor to carry out any reserved legal activity", "C": "Priya may perform the act herself, provided that she obtains the written consent of the SRA for this single transaction", "D": "Priya may perform the act herself, provided that a partner at her firm who is a commissioner for oaths counter-signs it", "E": "Notarial activities are reserved to notaries and others authorised for them, so Priya should refer the client to a notary public"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Notarial activities are reserved to notaries and others authorised for them, so Priya should refer the client to a notary public): Notarial activities are a reserved legal activity open to notaries and certain other authorised persons, and being a solicitor does not itself confer that authority. Priya should therefore explain this and refer her client to a notary public. A commissioner for oaths can administer oaths but cannot perform a notarial act. Delegation to a paralegal makes matters worse, and the SRA has no power to grant consent for a single transaction."}, {"id": 1311, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Rhys is tried in the Crown Court for the murder of his business partner. He admits killing him but says that at the time he was suffering from an abnormality of mental functioning that substantially impaired his ability to exercise self-control, so that he should be convicted of manslaughter by reason of diminished responsibility. Who bears the burden of proof on this issue, and to what standard?", "options": {"A": "Rhys bears an evidential burden only, and the prosecution must then disprove the defence on the balance of probabilities", "B": "The prosecution bears the burden, and must disprove the defence beyond reasonable doubt once the issue has been raised", "C": "The judge decides the issue alone on the balance of probabilities, after hearing medical evidence from both sides, because insanity-type issues are legal", "D": "Rhys bears the legal burden of establishing the defence, and the standard required of him is the balance of probabilities", "E": "Rhys bears the legal burden of establishing the defence, and must satisfy the jury beyond reasonable doubt like the prosecution"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rhys bears the legal burden of establishing the defence, and the standard required of him is the balance of probabilities): Diminished responsibility is one of the few defences where the legal burden sits with the defendant, who must prove it on the balance of probabilities. The prosecution keeps the burden on the elements of murder, but the partial defence is raised by the defendant and decided by the jury. The tempting view that the prosecution must disprove it applies to defences such as self-defence, where the defendant only has to raise sufficient evidence."}, {"id": 1312, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Wren Logistics Ltd instructs its solicitors on 4 November about a breach of contract claim for £60,000. The breach occurred on 22 November six years ago, so limitation expires in under three weeks. The solicitors issue proceedings on 6 November without sending a letter before claim. Peverell Ltd, the defendant, complains that the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct was ignored and asks the court to dismiss the claim. What is the most likely approach of the court?", "options": {"A": "Strike out the claim for non-compliance, because failing to send a letter before claim bars the claimant from proceeding", "B": "Order the claimant to pay the defendant's costs of the whole action, because breach of the Practice Direction justifies an automatic costs penalty", "C": "Set aside the claim form, because pre-action steps are a condition of a valid claim and the court has no power to stay for them", "D": "Make no order, because the Practice Direction is only guidance and the court may not consider non-compliance when deciding costs", "E": "Treat the issue as justified by the approaching limitation date and stay the claim so that the pre-action steps can be completed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Treat the issue as justified by the approaching limitation date and stay the claim so that the pre-action steps can be completed): The Practice Direction recognises that a claimant may start proceedings to protect its position under a limitation period, and in that situation the court will usually stay the claim so that the pre-action steps can be taken. Strike out is a disproportionate response to non-compliance and the court looks at the reason for it. Here the approaching expiry of limitation gives Wren a good reason. Sanctions on costs or interest remain available for unjustified failure, but they are not automatic."}, {"id": 1313, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "You act for Marlowe Haulage, which has contracted with Dunstan Foods about forty times over four years. Each time, Marlowe's invoice, delivered after the haulage, referred to its standard conditions limiting liability to £1,000 per load, and Dunstan never objected. A pallet worth £14,000 has now been lost and Dunstan sues. Marlowe's director asks you to plead that a copy of the conditions was given to Dunstan at the outset, which Marlowe cannot show. What is the best course?", "options": {"A": "Advise that the conditions cannot be incorporated by course of dealing, because they were sent only after the contract was made each time", "B": "Plead that a copy was given at the outset and rely on the director's recollection, since the client has instructed you on the facts", "C": "Plead that the conditions bind because Dunstan did not object, since silence over forty dealings is the same as written acceptance", "D": "Advise that the conditions apply only if Marlowe proves that Dunstan actually read them on at least one earlier occasion", "E": "Plead incorporation through a consistent course of dealing, and decline to assert that a copy was given at the outset"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Plead incorporation through a consistent course of dealing, and decline to assert that a copy was given at the outset): Terms can be incorporated by a consistent and regular course of dealing, even where Dunstan did not read them, provided the earlier dealings were on the same terms and were notified. A solicitor must not mislead the court or allow the client to do so, so the factual allegation that cannot be supported must not be pleaded. The course-of-dealing case is available without it. Actual reading is not required, and the sequence of invoices does not stop the rule applying."}, {"id": 1314, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Pascoe Marine Ltd lost a contract claim in the county court before a circuit judge. Its solicitor, Dhillon, wants to appeal and is preparing the application for permission to appeal. The appeal would be the first appeal in the case. Dhillon recalls that two tests are discussed in the procedural rules and wants to be sure which applies to this first appeal. Which of the following correctly states the test for permission?", "options": {"A": "Permission is given if the sum involved exceeds £5,000 and the outcome was unfavourable, because permission turns on the value at stake", "B": "Permission is given if the appellant shows the judge made any error of fact, because an appeal is a full rehearing", "C": "Permission is given if the appeal has a better than even chance of success, because the court will not allow weak appeals to proceed", "D": "Permission is given if the appeal has a real prospect of success or there is some other compelling reason for it to be heard", "E": "Permission is given where the case raises an important point of principle or practice, because that is the test on a first appeal"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Permission is given if the appeal has a real prospect of success or there is some other compelling reason for it to be heard): On a first appeal, CPR 52.6 provides that permission will be given only where the court considers the appeal has a real prospect of success or there is some other compelling reason for the appeal to be heard. The test is not whether success is more likely than not. Appeal is normally a review, not a rehearing. The important point of principle or practice test belongs to second appeals, which is where the confusion arises."}, {"id": 1315, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Mapleton Gardens Ltd wants a without notice injunction to stop a former supplier posting its confidential price list online. Its solicitor learns from the client that, last Tuesday, the supplier's solicitors offered a written undertaking not to post the list, which Mapleton has not answered. The client asks that the offer is not mentioned, because it might make the judge think the application is not urgent. What must the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Mention the offer only if the judge asks about earlier contact, because the duty is to answer the judge's direct questions fully", "B": "Tell the court about the offer of an undertaking, because a without notice applicant must disclose all material facts including those against it", "C": "Omit the offer, because it is for the defendant to raise at the return date once it has received notice", "D": "Include the offer in the draft order only, because it does not bear on urgency and need not be in the evidence", "E": "Omit the offer but tell the supplier's solicitors that an application is being made, so that the order is effectively on notice"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tell the court about the offer of an undertaking, because a without notice applicant must disclose all material facts including those against it): A party applying without notice owes the court a duty of full and frank disclosure, which includes facts that tell against the application. The offer of an undertaking goes directly to whether an injunction is needed at all, so it is material. Leaving it to the defendant at the return date misses the point of the duty, as does disclosing only on direct questioning. A solicitor must also not mislead the court, so the client's wish to conceal the offer cannot be followed."}, {"id": 1316, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Jed and Ola spend an evening drinking together in a pub. Jed offers Ola a lift home in his insured car. Ola knows Jed is very drunk and accepts the lift anyway. Jed drives badly and crashes, and Ola suffers serious injuries. Jed was negligent. Ola sues Jed, and Jed's insurer deals with the claim. Jed pleads the defence of volenti non fit injuria. Which of the following best describes whether the defence will succeed?", "options": {"A": "It will fail, because knowing a driver is drunk shows only awareness of risk and not agreement to waive a legal claim", "B": "It will succeed in part, because Ola's knowledge of the driver's state reduces her damages by a proportion", "C": "It will fail, because statute prevents volenti being relied on against a passenger injured in a vehicle covered by compulsory insurance", "D": "It will succeed, because Ola and Jed had been drinking together and so shared the common risk of the journey", "E": "It will succeed, because Ola knew the full extent of the risk and freely accepted it by getting into the car"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It will fail, because statute prevents volenti being relied on against a passenger injured in a vehicle covered by compulsory insurance): Under the Road Traffic Act 1988 a driver cannot rely on volenti against a passenger where the vehicle is subject to compulsory insurance, so Ola's knowledge of the driver's condition does not defeat her claim. The common law view that mere knowledge of drunkenness does not amount to consent would lead to the same result, but the statute is the reason here. Her knowledge may be relevant to contributory negligence, not to a complete defence."}, {"id": 1317, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Hamid is a director and 30% shareholder of Orchard Mills Ltd. He approved the purchase of new machinery at an inflated price from a supplier owned by his close friend, and the company lost £40,000 as a result. Another shareholder, Priya, holds 10% of the shares and asks to whom Hamid owed his duty under section 172 of the Companies Act 2006 to promote the success of the company. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The duty was owed to the company itself, so any claim for the £40,000 loss ordinarily belongs to the company rather than Priya", "B": "The duty was owed to Hamid's fellow shareholders as a class, so Priya may sue for the loss only with the consent of the majority", "C": "The duty was owed to each shareholder individually, so Priya may sue Hamid in her own right for her share of the loss", "D": "The duty was owed to the supplier and the company's creditors, so unpaid creditors may enforce it directly against Hamid in court", "E": "The duty was owed to the board collectively, so only the other directors may bring a claim against Hamid for the loss"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The duty was owed to the company itself, so any claim for the £40,000 loss ordinarily belongs to the company rather than Priya): Directors' general duties under sections 171 to 177 are owed to the company, not to individual members. The loss from the inflated purchase was suffered by Orchard Mills Ltd, so the company is the proper claimant. Priya's only route would be a derivative claim brought on the company's behalf with the court's permission. The suggestion that she could sue personally fails because the duty was never owed to her."}, {"id": 1318, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Brightwater Ltd, a company that opted to tax a freehold warehouse in 2014, is selling it to Calder Ltd for £800,000. The warehouse was built more than ten years ago and the buyer is not buying it as a going concern. The draft contract is silent on VAT. Which statement correctly describes the VAT position on the sale?", "options": {"A": "The sale is outside the scope of VAT, because a transfer of land is not a supply of goods or services", "B": "The sale is zero rated, because commercial freehold sales to a company are taxed at 0 per cent", "C": "The sale is exempt, because the warehouse is more than three years old and the age of the building alone decides the VAT treatment", "D": "The sale is standard rated only if the buyer also opts to tax, because both parties must have opted for VAT", "E": "The sale is standard rated because the seller opted to tax, and the contract should state expressly that VAT is payable in addition"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The sale is standard rated because the seller opted to tax, and the contract should state expressly that VAT is payable in addition): A sale of a commercial building more than three years old is exempt unless the seller has opted to tax, and Brightwater has done so, so the sale is standard rated. The buyer's own position does not change that. Because the draft is silent, the seller should add a special condition that VAT is payable in addition to the price. Land is a supply for VAT purposes, so it is not outside the scope."}, {"id": 1319, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Hollis Cycles started trading in July 2025 and all its sales are standard rated. Its taxable turnover for the twelve months to the end of June 2026 was above the VAT registration threshold of £90,000 for the first time. The owner, Mr Hollis, has not yet registered for VAT and asks his solicitor what he must do. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "He must notify HMRC by 30 July 2026, and registration takes effect from 1 August 2026 unless HMRC agrees an earlier date", "B": "He must notify HMRC by 30 September 2026, three months after the end of the month in which the threshold was exceeded", "C": "He need not register yet, because the threshold applies to taxable turnover within a single tax year and not a rolling twelve months", "D": "He must register within seven days of the end of June, and registration takes effect from the date he applies", "E": "He must notify HMRC by 30 July 2026, and registration takes effect from 1 July 2025 when trading began"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He must notify HMRC by 30 July 2026, and registration takes effect from 1 August 2026 unless HMRC agrees an earlier date): A person must register for VAT where taxable turnover over any rolling twelve month period exceeds the registration threshold. The notification must reach HMRC within 30 days of the end of the month in which the threshold was exceeded, here by 30 July 2026, and registration takes effect from the first day of the second month after that month, 1 August 2026, unless HMRC allows an earlier date. The test is a rolling period, not a tax year, and registration is not backdated to the start of trading."}, {"id": 1320, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Penny Rhodes brings a claim against Denholm Estates Ltd. Her solicitor issues an application for an order for specific disclosure. The application notice sets out the facts, including how the documents were requested and refused, and carries a statement of truth signed by Penny herself. No witness statement is filed. At the hearing, the defendant objects that no evidence is before the court. How will the court treat the application notice?", "options": {"A": "The notice is not evidence, but the court will make the order because the defendant has filed no evidence in reply", "B": "The notice can be treated as evidence at the hearing, because it contains a statement of truth verifying the facts it states", "C": "The notice becomes evidence only if the court gives permission at the hearing, after the defendant has had time to respond to it", "D": "The notice cannot be evidence, because evidence on an application must be given by witness statement or affidavit", "E": "The notice is evidence of the procedural history only, so the court must adjourn for a witness statement on the main facts"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The notice can be treated as evidence at the hearing, because it contains a statement of truth verifying the facts it states): An application notice may be relied on as evidence if its contents are verified by a statement of truth. Penny signed one, so the facts about the document requests can be treated as evidence at the hearing even without a witness statement. A separate statement is common practice but not mandatory. The court still decides the merits of specific disclosure, so the defendant's silence would not guarantee success."}, {"id": 1321, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Dee, Eli and Fay are the trustees of a trust that holds shares and cash. The trust instrument is silent about retirement. Dee wishes to retire because she is moving abroad. Eli and Fay both consent by deed to her retirement but neither of them has found a replacement. The beneficiaries have not been told. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Dee can retire by deed, because two trustees will remain and they have consented, so no replacement is needed", "B": "Dee cannot retire until a replacement is appointed, because a trust of this kind must have at least three trustees", "C": "Dee can retire if Eli alone consents in writing, because the consent of one co-trustee is sufficient under section 39", "D": "Dee can retire only if all the beneficiaries consent, since they are the persons who are entitled to the trust property", "E": "Dee needs the court's permission, because retirement without a replacement is a breach of trust unless the court allows it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dee can retire by deed, because two trustees will remain and they have consented, so no replacement is needed): Section 39 Trustee Act 1925 lets a trustee retire by deed without a new appointment if at least two trustees (or a trust corporation) remain and the co-trustees consent by deed. Eli and Fay consent and two trustees remain, so the condition is met. There is no minimum of three, and neither the court nor the beneficiaries need approve. The consent of all remaining trustees is needed, not just one."}, {"id": 1322, "domain": "land_law", "question": "You act for Ravi, who is selling his house. The buyer's solicitors ask in pre-contract enquiries whether there are any disputes with neighbours about boundaries, access or rights over the property. Ravi tells you that a neighbour has recently written claiming a right of way over his drive by long use, but he instructs you to reply that there are no disputes, because he fears the buyer will withdraw. Which of the following is the correct course of action?", "options": {"A": "Reply that there are no disputes, because the buyer must rely on their own inspection and survey", "B": "Refuse to give a misleading reply, advise Ravi that the dispute must be disclosed, and stop acting if he continues to insist", "C": "Disclose the dispute to the buyer's solicitors without telling Ravi, because the duty not to mislead them overrides confidentiality", "D": "Reply as instructed but tell Ravi in writing that the answer is his responsibility, to protect the firm", "E": "Give an evasive answer that neither confirms nor denies any dispute, so that Ravi's instructions are followed without a false statement"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refuse to give a misleading reply, advise Ravi that the dispute must be disclosed, and stop acting if he continues to insist): You must not mislead the other side, whether by a false statement or by an evasive answer designed to create a false impression, and Ravi cannot override that duty. The right course is to decline the instruction, advise him that the neighbour's claim must be disclosed and explain the consequences of not doing so. If he still insists, you must stop acting, taking care over confidentiality. A letter recording that the reply is his responsibility does not make a false reply acceptable, and secret disclosure skips the advice you owe him."}, {"id": 1323, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Kofi completed the purchase of a registered house. His solicitor had obtained an official search with priority at the outset. Kofi's application to register the transfer was lodged on the thirty-third business day after the search certificate was issued. On the thirty-first business day, a charge in favour of a creditor of the seller had been registered against the seller's title. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The charge is void, because the seller no longer owned the property after completion and could not have charged it", "B": "Kofi's application ranks first, because priority runs from the date of completion and not from the date of the search", "C": "Kofi's application has no priority protection, so the creditor's charge, entered first, ranks ahead of his interest", "D": "Kofi's application keeps priority over the charge, because the transfer was completed within the period of the search", "E": "Kofi takes free of the charge, because a purchaser for value who is in possession overrides later registered charges"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Kofi's application has no priority protection, so the creditor's charge, entered first, ranks ahead of his interest): The protection period lasts thirty business days from the search certificate, and Kofi lodged on the thirty-third day. His application is therefore outside the period, and the creditor's charge, which was registered on the thirty-first day, takes priority. Legal title passes only on registration, so the seller could still be charged. Completion does not itself fix priority."}, {"id": 1324, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Priya, a solicitor, acts for Gareth, the sole trustee of a family trust. Gareth tells her that he intends to move £150,000 of trust money into a friend's failing business and that he will repay it once the business recovers. Priya knows that the trust instrument gives no power to do this, yet she drafts the transfer documents for a fee. The business fails and the beneficiaries sue the firm. Which of the following best describes Priya's position?", "options": {"A": "She is liable only if she received the trust money into the firm's account, since the liability of a stranger to the trust depends on receipt", "B": "She risks liability for dishonest assistance, because honest solicitors do not help with a known breach, and a promise to repay does not alter that position", "C": "She is not liable, because she gained no personal benefit from the transfer and dishonest assistance requires the assistant to have profited from the breach", "D": "She is not liable, because acting on a client's instructions as a professional adviser is a full answer to a claim by the beneficiaries", "E": "She is liable only if the beneficiaries prove that she personally intended to cause them loss, and Gareth's promise to repay shows she did not"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She risks liability for dishonest assistance, because honest solicitors do not help with a known breach, and a promise to repay does not alter that position): Priya knew the transfer was outside the trustee's powers, so assisting it is conduct that ordinary honest people would regard as dishonest. Dishonest assistance does not need the assistant to benefit or to receive property, and the fee is irrelevant to liability. Acting on instructions is no defence where the solicitor knows the instructions involve a breach of trust, and the SRA Principles on honesty and integrity point the same way. Gareth's intention to repay might help him on motive but does not make the breach lawful."}, {"id": 1325, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Marlowe Developments Ltd alleges that the defendant, a director of Penhale Homes Ltd, fraudulently told it that planning permission had been granted for a site, inducing it to pay a £400,000 deposit. The claim is being heard in the High Court. The defendant's solicitor tells the client that, because fraud is such a serious allegation, Marlowe must prove it to a higher standard than usual, and that the burden will move to the defendant once the claim is pleaded. The client's accountant had also checked the deposit figure. Which of the following is correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Marlowe carries the burden of opening the case, but the burden of proving honesty passes to the defendant after the claimant's evidence", "B": "Marlowe carries the burden throughout and need prove fraud only on the balance of probabilities, the ordinary civil standard", "C": "Marlowe carries the burden throughout and must prove fraud beyond reasonable doubt, because the allegation is serious and has criminal overtones", "D": "The defendant carries the burden of disproving fraud on the balance of probabilities once the claimant has shown the statement was untrue", "E": "Marlowe carries the burden throughout and must prove fraud to a standard between civil and criminal, because dishonesty is alleged"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Marlowe carries the burden throughout and need prove fraud only on the balance of probabilities, the ordinary civil standard): In civil proceedings the legal burden stays with the party who asserts the fact, here the claimant alleging fraud. The standard is the balance of probabilities even for fraud, with no intermediate or criminal standard. The seriousness of the allegation is relevant to the inherent probability of events, which the court considers, but it does not raise the standard itself. The burden does not shift to the defendant merely because the claim has been pleaded."}, {"id": 1326, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Kim sold a plot to Leo in 2015. The transfer gave Leo a right to use the private road over Kim's retained land, and Leo covenanted to pay half the cost of maintaining the road. Leo sold the plot to Mo in 2023. Mo drives along the road every day but has refused to contribute to repairs, saying he never promised Kim anything. The road needs resurfacing. Kim's solicitor is considering how to recover Mo's share. Which of the following is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Kim can recover from Mo only if the covenant is registered as a restriction against his title at HM Land Registry", "B": "Kim can enforce the covenant against Mo in equity under Tulk v Moxhay, because it touches and concerns Kim's retained land", "C": "Kim can recover only from Leo, because the burden of a positive covenant does not run with freehold land to a successor such as Mo", "D": "Kim has no remedy against either of them, because Leo's liability ended when he parted with the plot to Mo", "E": "Kim can recover a share from Mo, because he enjoys the road under a right granted conditionally on payment in the same transfer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Kim can recover a share from Mo, because he enjoys the road under a right granted conditionally on payment in the same transfer): The burden of a positive covenant does not pass to a successor, and Tulk v Moxhay concerns only restrictive covenants. Under the benefit and burden principle in Halsall v Brizell, however, a successor who chooses to take the benefit of a right granted in the same transaction must accept the conditional burden, here a fair share of maintenance costs. Mo uses the road under the right that was conditional on payment, so Kim can claim from him. Leo also remains liable on his original contract, so the suggestion that nobody can be sued is wrong."}, {"id": 1327, "domain": "land_law", "question": "For twenty-five years Mr Ibrahim, who owns the freehold of Hill Farm, has walked and driven a tractor along a track over his neighbour's field to reach the lane. He never asked permission and never used force, and the neighbour, who lives next door and saw him daily, never objected. The neighbour has now locked the gate. Mr Ibrahim claims a right of way by prescription under the Prescription Act 1832. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails because the neighbour's knowledge of his daily use amounts to implied permission, which prevents the use from being as of right during the period", "B": "The claim can succeed because the use was as of right, without force, secrecy or permission, for at least twenty years", "C": "The claim fails because a prescriptive easement needs forty years of use where the neighbour's land is registered, and only twenty-five years have passed", "D": "The claim fails because easements cannot arise by long use and must be granted expressly by deed or implied on a sale", "E": "The claim succeeds only if Mr Ibrahim also shows the track is necessary for reasonable access to Hill Farm from the lane"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim can succeed because the use was as of right, without force, secrecy or permission, for at least twenty years): Prescription requires use as of right, which means nec vi, nec clam, nec precario: without force, secrecy or permission, for the statutory period of twenty years. Mr Ibrahim's use was open and unchallenged, so all three elements are met. Mere knowledge of the use by the neighbour is not permission, since permission must be given, and the registered status of the land does not lengthen the period. Necessity is a requirement for easements implied on a sale, not for prescription."}, {"id": 1328, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Marta, a Jehovah's Witness, is stabbed in the chest by Callum, who intends to cause her really serious harm. The wound is serious but treatable. Doctors tell Marta that a blood transfusion is needed to save her life. She refuses on religious grounds and dies from loss of blood. Medical staff did everything else properly. Callum is charged with murder and argues that Marta's refusal caused her death. Which of the following is the most accurate statement of the law?", "options": {"A": "The refusal breaks the chain of causation, because a victim's unreasonable decision is a free and deliberate act that supersedes the original wound", "B": "The refusal does not break the chain, because a defendant takes the victim as he finds her, including her religious beliefs", "C": "Callum is liable for manslaughter only, because the victim's refusal of treatment reduces the intention to cause serious harm that he held", "D": "The refusal does not break the chain, but only if a reasonable person in Marta's position would also have declined treatment", "E": "Callum is liable only if he knew of Marta's religious beliefs, because he must foresee her refusal for the chain to remain intact"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The refusal does not break the chain, because a defendant takes the victim as he finds her, including her religious beliefs): Under the thin skull rule the defendant must take his victim as he finds her, and that covers religious beliefs as well as physical frailty. Marta's refusal of the transfusion therefore leaves the stab wound as the operating and substantial cause of death. It does not matter whether her decision was reasonable or whether Callum knew of her beliefs. Intent is judged at the time of the attack, so a later refusal cannot reduce murder to manslaughter."}, {"id": 1329, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Gareth Pugh is tried in the Crown Court for robbery. The judge ordered a defence statement and Gareth's solicitors failed to serve one. At trial Gareth gives evidence of an alibi and names a witness who was never mentioned to the prosecution beforehand. Prosecution counsel wishes to criticise the late disclosure of the alibi in front of the jury. What may the court permit?", "options": {"A": "No comment is permitted, because a defendant's silence about his defence before trial cannot be used against him", "B": "The judge may comment, but only after a formal warning has been given to the defendant at the start of the trial", "C": "The alibi evidence must be excluded, because failure to comply with the defence statement requirement makes any new defence inadmissible", "D": "The jury may convict on that failure alone, since the inference from non-compliance is sufficient proof of guilt", "E": "The court or a party, with leave, may comment on the failure, and the jury may draw such inferences as appear proper"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court or a party, with leave, may comment on the failure, and the jury may draw such inferences as appear proper): Under the Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996, a failure to serve a required defence statement, or reliance at trial on a defence not previously disclosed, allows comment by the court or by a party with leave. The jury may draw such inferences as appear proper in deciding guilt. A conviction cannot be based solely on that inference. The judge does not exclude the alibi evidence merely because the statement was missing."}, {"id": 1330, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Harriet, a solicitor, is considering a conditional fee agreement with a client who wants to sue a contractor for defective building work. The matter is a commercial dispute and not a personal injury claim, and the agreement is made in 2026. The client asks how large the success fee can be and who will pay it if the case is won. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "The success fee can be up to 100 per cent of base costs, and the losing opponent must pay it as part of recoverable costs", "B": "The success fee can be up to 50 per cent of base costs, and it is payable by the client from the damages recovered", "C": "The success fee can be up to 25 per cent of the damages recovered, and the losing opponent must pay it as part of costs", "D": "The success fee can be up to 100 per cent of base costs, and the client pays it rather than recovering it from the opponent", "E": "The success fee can be up to 25 per cent of base costs, and the losing opponent pays it if the client wins"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The success fee can be up to 100 per cent of base costs, and the client pays it rather than recovering it from the opponent): Under the Conditional Fee Agreements Order 2013 a success fee in a conditional fee agreement cannot exceed 100 per cent of the base costs. Since April 2013 the success fee is no longer recoverable from the losing opponent in this type of claim, so the client pays it from damages or otherwise. The 25 per cent figure relates to the cap in personal injury cases, where it applies to damages and not to base costs. The commercial nature of the dispute means those limits do not apply."}, {"id": 1331, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Pike's will gives 'my gold watch to my godson Ben'. When he died, his estate included two gold watches: a 1960s Omega that he wore daily and a pocket watch inherited from his father. The executors cannot tell which he meant, and Ben's mother says the pocket watch was promised to Ben. The residuary beneficiary says the gift fails. How will the court approach the clause?", "options": {"A": "The gift fails for uncertainty, because a will cannot be saved by evidence of what the testator meant", "B": "The court will divide the two watches equally, because it cannot choose between two items", "C": "Only evidence of the words in the will may be considered, because outside evidence is admissible solely to rectify a clerical error", "D": "It may admit extrinsic evidence of his intention, because the words are ambiguous in the light of the surrounding circumstances", "E": "The court will give Ben the more valuable watch, because ambiguity is resolved in favour of the donee"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It may admit extrinsic evidence of his intention, because the words are ambiguous in the light of the surrounding circumstances): Section 21 of the Administration of Justice Act 1982 allows extrinsic evidence, including of the testator's intention, to help interpret a will where the language is meaningless, ambiguous on its face or ambiguous in the light of the surrounding circumstances. A latent ambiguity of this kind, with two watches matching the description, falls within that rule. The court is not limited to the words alone, and the gift does not fail merely because the executors cannot tell which watch was meant."}, {"id": 1332, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Nash died intestate and unmarried. He had lived for fifteen years with his partner Eve, but they never married or registered a civil partnership. He had no children. His father died years ago, but his mother is alive, and so is his brother Frank. His estate is worth £280,000. Eve says she contributed to the household and should receive the estate. Who is entitled?", "options": {"A": "Frank takes the whole estate, because siblings of the whole blood rank before a surviving parent", "B": "His mother takes the whole estate, because parents rank before siblings and a cohabitant has no entitlement", "C": "Eve takes the whole estate, because fifteen years of cohabitation gives her the rights of a spouse", "D": "His mother and Frank share equally, because both are close blood relatives in the same class", "E": "His mother takes half and Eve takes half, because the court will recognise Eve's long contribution to the household"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (His mother takes the whole estate, because parents rank before siblings and a cohabitant has no entitlement): With no spouse, civil partner or issue, the estate goes to the parents, and if both survive they share equally, but a sole surviving parent takes the whole. Siblings take only if no parent survives. A cohabitant has no automatic entitlement under the intestacy rules, however long the relationship, though Eve might consider a claim under the 1975 Act. The mother therefore takes everything."}, {"id": 1333, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Penhallow Gym Ltd sues Orrin Fitness Ltd for £18,000 for breach of a sponsorship agreement. The claim is allocated to the fast track. The claimant's solicitor, who is new to fast track cases, asks, before the first directions are given, what the court will normally do in its directions about the trial date and the steps leading up to trial.", "options": {"A": "List a case management conference to set the trial date, with standard directions made after costs budgets are filed", "B": "Fix a trial date up to 52 weeks ahead, since the court allows more time than on the small claims track", "C": "Make no order on the trial date until the parties file pre-trial checklists, which are used to set the date", "D": "Give general directions and leave the parties to agree a trial window of up to six months between themselves", "E": "Give standard directions and fix a trial date or period no more than 30 weeks ahead, without a case management conference"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Give standard directions and fix a trial date or period no more than 30 weeks ahead, without a case management conference): On allocation to the fast track the court normally gives standard directions for disclosure, witness statements and any expert evidence, and fixes the trial date or a trial period not more than 30 weeks ahead. A case management conference is not usual, because the track is designed to keep procedure simple and costs fixed. Costs budgets belong to the multi-track. Pre-trial checklists come later, after the trial date has already been set."}, {"id": 1334, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Ahmed takes a laptop from an unattended office on Monday and leaves the building unnoticed. On Wednesday the owner, Bryony, sees Ahmed in a cafe with the laptop and demands it back. Ahmed punches her in the face to get away and keeps the laptop. He is charged with robbery under section 8 of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Guilty of robbery, because force used to keep stolen property from its owner is force used in order to steal", "B": "Not guilty of robbery, because robbery requires the threat of force to be made before the property is taken, not the actual use of force afterwards", "C": "Not guilty of robbery, because the force was used long after the theft was complete and not immediately before or at the time of it", "D": "Guilty of robbery, because the owner's demand revived the theft so that the force was used at the time of a fresh appropriation", "E": "Guilty of robbery, because appropriation of the laptop continues for as long as Ahmed holds it dishonestly against the owner's will"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Not guilty of robbery, because the force was used long after the theft was complete and not immediately before or at the time of it): Robbery requires force or a threat of force immediately before or at the time of the theft and in order to steal. The theft was complete on Monday, and the punch two days later falls outside that window. The punch can be charged as an assault or an offence against the person, and Ahmed remains guilty of theft. Continuing appropriation does not stretch to a later confrontation of this kind."}, {"id": 1335, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mrs Abbott's will contains the clause: 'I give £10,000 to my nephew Callum, to be paid out of my savings account with Bridgeway Bank.' At her death the Bridgeway account holds only £6,000, and her estate has ample other funds, including £30,000 in a current account elsewhere. Callum asks how his gift is classified and what he can claim. What is the correct analysis?", "options": {"A": "It is a residuary gift, so Callum takes whatever remains after all other gifts and debts are paid", "B": "It is a demonstrative legacy, so Callum takes the £6,000 from the account and the £4,000 balance as a general legacy", "C": "It is a general pecuniary legacy, so Callum is paid £10,000 without regard to the account named", "D": "It is a specific legacy, so Callum receives only the £6,000 in the account and the gift fails for the remainder of the sum", "E": "It is a specific legacy, which is adeemed because the account no longer holds the full £10,000"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is a demonstrative legacy, so Callum takes the £6,000 from the account and the £4,000 balance as a general legacy): A demonstrative legacy is a general legacy of money directed to be paid from a particular fund. If the fund is insufficient, the gift is treated as general for the balance, so the shortfall comes from the rest of the estate. Callum is therefore entitled to the full £10,000. It is not specific, so ademption does not apply, and it is not the residue."}, {"id": 1336, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Mark was injured in a road traffic accident in 2026 and instructed a solicitor under a conditional fee agreement with a 25 per cent success fee. The solicitor also arranged after the event insurance for Mark. The claim succeeds and the defendant's insurer agrees to pay Mark's costs. The solicitor is drafting the final bill and Mark asks whether the success fee and the insurance premium can be added to the costs the defendant pays. The claim is not for clinical negligence. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Neither is recoverable from the defendant, so Mark bears them, the success fee coming from his damages within the statutory cap", "B": "Both are recoverable from the defendant, because Mark is the winning party and costs follow the event in full", "C": "Both are recoverable if the defendant's insurer has agreed to pay Mark's costs, because the agreement overrides the statutory rules", "D": "The premium is recoverable from the defendant but the success fee is not, because insurance is a cost of bringing the claim", "E": "The success fee is recoverable from the defendant but the premium is not, because only the solicitor's own charges can be passed on"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Neither is recoverable from the defendant, so Mark bears them, the success fee coming from his damages within the statutory cap): For agreements made after April 2013, neither the success fee nor an after the event insurance premium can be recovered from the paying party in an ordinary personal injury claim. The success fee is taken from the client's damages, subject to the cap of 25 per cent of certain heads of damages. The limited exception for expert report premiums in clinical negligence claims does not arise here. Costs still follow the event, but only for the costs that the rules allow to be recovered."}, {"id": 1337, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Wayne Ashcroft is charged with robbery of a late-night kiosk. The only witness, who did not know Wayne, saw the robber for about two seconds in a poorly lit alley from 20 metres away. Three weeks later she picked Wayne out at an identification parade. There is no other evidence connecting Wayne with the robbery, and Wayne says he was elsewhere. At the close of the prosecution case, defence counsel makes a submission of no case to answer. How should the judge approach it?", "options": {"A": "The judge should stop the case only if the witness admits she was uncertain when giving her evidence in court", "B": "The judge should leave the case to the jury with a warning, as a parade identification cures concerns about the original sighting", "C": "The judge should stop the case only if Wayne gives alibi evidence that a jury would be likely to accept", "D": "The judge should allow the case to proceed, since the weight of identification evidence is for the jury whatever its quality", "E": "The judge should stop the case, because the identification is of poor quality and nothing else supports it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The judge should stop the case, because the identification is of poor quality and nothing else supports it): Where identification evidence is of poor quality, such as a fleeting glance in bad light by a stranger, and there is no supporting evidence, the judge must withdraw the case from the jury at the close of the prosecution case. A later parade does not repair a weak original observation. Wayne need not call any evidence for the submission to succeed, and the witness's candour in court is not the test."}, {"id": 1338, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mrs Shah's husband and two children are involved in a serious road accident caused by a negligent driver. She is told at home and reaches the hospital two hours after the accident, where she sees her husband and children, still covered in dirt and oil, with obvious severe injuries. She was not at the scene. She suffers a recognised psychiatric illness as a result of what she saw. She sues the driver. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "She may succeed as a secondary victim, because she has a close tie and perceived the aftermath in close time and space", "B": "She cannot succeed, because psychiatric illness is recoverable only by a person who suffers physical injury in the same accident", "C": "She cannot succeed, because a secondary victim must be present at the scene of the accident when it occurs", "D": "She may succeed, because a close tie is unnecessary when the claimant arrives at the scene shortly after the accident", "E": "She may succeed, because she is a primary victim who foreseeably suffered psychiatric harm from the accident to her whole family"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She may succeed as a secondary victim, because she has a close tie and perceived the aftermath in close time and space): A secondary victim must have a close tie of love and affection with the person injured, be close in time and space to the event or its immediate aftermath, and perceive it with her own unaided senses. Seeing her family at the hospital two hours later, still in the condition caused by the accident, falls within the immediate aftermath. Presence at the scene is not necessary, and physical injury is not required. She was not herself in danger, so she is not a primary victim."}, {"id": 1339, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Dev, a bouncer, stabs Priya in the thigh during a dispute outside a club, intending to cause her really serious injury. The wound would normally have been treated successfully in hospital. Priya, a devout member of a faith that forbids blood transfusions, refuses one on religious grounds and dies from blood loss two days later. Dev is charged with murder. Which of the following best describes the legal position on causation?", "options": {"A": "Dev's act remains an operating and substantial cause of death, because the victim's religious refusal of treatment does not break the chain of causation", "B": "Dev can be convicted of manslaughter but not murder, because the death was caused partly by the victim's own decision about treatment", "C": "Dev is liable only if the refusal of treatment was objectively reasonable, because a victim must take sensible steps to limit the harm caused to her", "D": "Dev is liable for murder only if death was a virtually certain result of the stabbing when he inflicted the wound on Priya", "E": "The chain of causation is broken, because the victim's free decision to refuse a life-saving transfusion is a new intervening act of her own"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dev's act remains an operating and substantial cause of death, because the victim's religious refusal of treatment does not break the chain of causation): Under the thin skull rule the defendant takes his victim as he finds her, and that includes her religious beliefs. A victim's refusal of medical treatment, even if unreasonable, does not break the chain unless the treatment alone is the operating cause, which it was not here. The stabbing remained an operating and substantial cause of death, and Dev intended serious harm, so the mental element of murder is satisfied. The argument that the refusal is a new intervening act fails because the victim's reaction was not so extraordinary as to make the wound a mere setting for her death."}, {"id": 1340, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Zoran drives a van owned by his employer, Cobalt Couriers Ltd, delivering parcels along a fixed route. At 11.30 am he leaves the route for about 600 metres to buy lunch from a bakery he sees, intending to rejoin the route immediately. On the way, he drives carelessly and knocks down Priya, a cyclist. Zoran is insured in his own name but has few assets. Cobalt Couriers Ltd says Zoran was on a personal errand. Which of the following best describes Cobalt's position?", "options": {"A": "Cobalt is not liable, because Zoran was insured personally and the claim lies only against the person who drove carelessly", "B": "Cobalt is liable only if Zoran cannot pay, because the employer's liability is secondary to that of the employee", "C": "Cobalt is not liable, because leaving the authorised route for a private purpose takes the employee outside the scope of his employment", "D": "Cobalt is likely vicariously liable, because a short deviation to buy lunch during the working journey remains within the course of employment", "E": "Cobalt is liable only if it expressly allowed drivers to stop for meals, because the employer must authorise the particular deviation"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cobalt is likely vicariously liable, because a short deviation to buy lunch during the working journey remains within the course of employment): A minor deviation made while doing the job is treated as part of the employment, so long as the employee has not embarked on a separate frolic of his own. A 600 metre detour for lunch with an intention to rejoin the route straight away is a modest departure, and the careless driving occurred while Zoran was doing his work in the employer's van. Express authority for the stop is not required for the employer to be answerable. The employer's liability does not depend on the employee's insurance or means, as Priya may pursue either defendant for the whole loss."}, {"id": 1341, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A trade body is alarmed after a newspaper reports that a government minister has said she will 'have a word' with the Lord Chancellor about removing a High Court judge who ruled against the department in a high profile case. The trade body asks its solicitor how the constitution protects the independence of judges of the senior courts. Which of the following best describes the protection?", "options": {"A": "Senior judges can be removed by a simple majority of the House of Commons, because the Commons is the elected chamber of Parliament", "B": "Senior judges can be removed by the Lord Chief Justice if the Judicial Appointments Commission recommends removal after a complaint is made", "C": "Senior judges hold office at the pleasure of the Crown, but the government must consult the Lord Chancellor before removing any of them", "D": "Senior judges serve for a fixed renewable term and can be removed by the Lord Chancellor on the advice of the Prime Minister", "E": "Senior judges hold office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the Crown following an address from both Houses of Parliament"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Senior judges hold office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the Crown following an address from both Houses of Parliament): Security of tenure for senior judges rests on the Act of Settlement principle, now reflected in the Senior Courts Act 1981, that they hold office during good behaviour. Removal needs an address from both Houses, so a minister cannot dismiss a judge, which supports the separation of powers. A fixed term or removal at the Crown's pleasure describes the position before the Act of Settlement, and a single House cannot act alone."}, {"id": 1342, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Ifeoma Danjuma, a solicitor-advocate, represents the claimant in a County Court hearing about a lease. In preparing, she finds a recent Court of Appeal decision on the interpretation of the lease covenant that is directly in point and that goes against her client's case. The defendant, who has no lawyer, has not cited it and appears unaware of it. Her client has told her to say nothing about authorities that harm the claim. What must Ifeoma do?", "options": {"A": "Say nothing, because the other party has the burden of finding relevant authority and her duty is to promote her own client's case with vigour", "B": "Mention the decision briefly after judgment is given, because oral submissions should focus on arguments supporting her client", "C": "Say nothing unless the judge asks, because the duty to disclose adverse authority applies when the opponent is legally represented by a solicitor or barrister", "D": "Seek her client's consent before referring to the decision, because the client's instructions control what she says to the court", "E": "Draw the court's attention to the decision, because her duty to the court overrides her client's instruction and the decision binds the County Court"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Draw the court's attention to the decision, because her duty to the court overrides her client's instruction and the decision binds the County Court): A solicitor owes a duty to the court that overrides the duty to the client, and must not mislead the court or allow it to be misled. A binding authority that is directly in point and adverse must be drawn to the court's attention even if the opponent has missed it, and even if the client instructs otherwise. Ifeoma cannot cure the position by seeking consent or by mentioning the decision after judgment. Her client's instruction to conceal it would conflict with the SRA Principles and Code of Conduct."}, {"id": 1343, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Dr Priya Nair is in her first week as a foundation doctor and is covering a night shift when senior colleagues are busy in theatre. She inserts a central line into Mr Hughes without direct supervision, in a way a competent doctor in her post would not, and he suffers a punctured lung. The hospital accepts that she was inexperienced and had not been trained for this procedure. Mr Hughes sues the hospital as employer. Which of the following best describes how the court will assess whether there was a breach of the standard of care?", "options": {"A": "The standard is met if she honestly did her best, since the law judges the personal effort of the individual doctor", "B": "The standard is met if a responsible body of junior doctors would have acted the same way on the night", "C": "The standard is lowered to reflect her first week in the post, so only gross incompetence would amount to a breach", "D": "The standard is that of a reasonably competent doctor in her post, so her inexperience does not lower the standard expected", "E": "The standard is that of the most skilled specialist in the field, because patients are entitled to the best available treatment"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The standard is that of a reasonably competent doctor in her post, so her inexperience does not lower the standard expected): A professional is judged against the standard of a reasonably competent person exercising the skills of the post, and the standard does not fall because the individual is new or untrained. Inexperience may explain why the error happened but is not a defence, and the hospital's failure to supervise is a separate matter that may add to its own liability. The standard also does not rise to that of the most expert specialist. The honest effort of the doctor, the tempting argument, is irrelevant to breach."}, {"id": 1344, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Fairway Law acts for Joanne Pike, who is selling her house in Cambridge for £410,000. The buyer, Ravi Menon, has been a client of the firm for ten years and asks the firm to act for him on the purchase too. Both clients would give written consent, and the firm proposes to run each file in a separate office with a different fee earner. The estate agent has agreed a price and the contracts are being drawn up. Which of the following is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The firm may act for both, because the price has already been agreed and no negotiation remains to cause a conflict", "B": "The firm may act for both if the transaction is residential, because the SRA treats residential sales as presenting low risk", "C": "The firm cannot act for both, because buyer and seller negotiate against each other and share no substantially common interest or objective", "D": "The firm may act for both, because written consent and separate teams are sufficient to manage the conflict between seller and buyer in the transaction", "E": "The firm may act for both, because Mr Menon has been a client for ten years and that relationship creates no divided loyalty"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The firm cannot act for both, because buyer and seller negotiate against each other and share no substantially common interest or objective): A firm may act for two clients with conflicting interests only where they have a substantially common interest or are competing for the same objective, and then only with consent and effective safeguards. A buyer and a seller negotiate against each other on terms, title and conditions, so neither exception applies. Consent and separate teams cannot cure a conflict that the rule forbids the firm to accept. An agreed price does not remove the remaining points on which their interests diverge."}, {"id": 1345, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for Hollis, a sole trader, in raising a £150,000 loan from Stoneham Bank. The bank requires Hollis's wife, Rani, to guarantee the loan, secured by a charge over the jointly owned family home. Rani, who has little involvement in the business, tells the solicitor in Hollis's presence that she will sign whatever is needed. Hollis asks the solicitor also to advise Rani and to sign the bank's certificate that she received independent advice. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Advise Rani only on the effect of the charge over the home, because the guarantee itself is a matter for the bank's own solicitors", "B": "Advise Rani and certify that she received independent advice, provided the solicitor explains the guarantee in writing and keeps a note of her instructions", "C": "Advise Rani on her own with Hollis's written consent, because a conflict of interests can be cured by the informed consent of the husband alone", "D": "Advise Rani jointly with Hollis in his presence, because her stated willingness to sign shows she understands the transaction and no conflict exists", "E": "Decline to advise Rani, because there is a significant risk of conflict with Hollis's interests, and tell her to use a separate solicitor"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to advise Rani, because there is a significant risk of conflict with Hollis's interests, and tell her to use a separate solicitor): A solicitor must not act where there is a conflict or significant risk of conflict between clients, unless an exception applies. Rani is exposed to the loss of her home if Hollis's business fails, so her interests conflict with his. Consent from Hollis cannot solve that, and safeguards do not make the advice independent. Rani needs her own solicitor, and the solicitor should explain this to her and decline the request."}, {"id": 1346, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Fenwick Print Ltd prepares accounts to 31 December 2026. During the year it spent £90,000 on a new printing press and £30,000 on office equipment, all of which qualifies for the annual investment allowance. The company's profit is £400,000 after charging accounting depreciation of £18,000. The main corporation tax rate is 25% and the annual investment allowance limit is £1 million. What is the company's taxable trading profit?", "options": {"A": "£262,000, because both the depreciation and the £120,000 allowance are deducted from the accounting profit as separate reliefs", "B": "£280,000, because the £120,000 allowance is deducted but depreciation is left as a deductible expense of the trade", "C": "£298,000, because depreciation is added back and the full £120,000 of expenditure is deducted as annual investment allowance", "D": "£396,400, because depreciation is added back and only an 18% writing down allowance is claimed on the £120,000 of expenditure", "E": "£418,000, because depreciation is added back and the allowance is treated as an additional trading receipt of the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (£298,000, because depreciation is added back and the full £120,000 of expenditure is deducted as annual investment allowance): Depreciation is not deductible for tax and is added back, taking profit from £400,000 to £418,000. The company then deducts capital allowances in its place, and because the expenditure of £120,000 is within the £1 million annual investment allowance limit, the whole amount is deducted in the year. That leaves taxable profit of £298,000. Claiming only writing down allowance would understate the relief available, and the allowance is a deduction, not a receipt."}, {"id": 1347, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "A firm of solicitors acts as executor and solicitor in the administration of Mr Lawal's estate, which includes a portfolio of shares worth £500,000. The estate will take at least three years to settle because of a dispute. The beneficiaries ask the firm to manage the portfolio during that time, buying and selling holdings at its discretion to seek growth. The firm is an exempt professional firm and has no authorisation from the Financial Conduct Authority. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "The firm may manage the portfolio itself for the first year, because the exemption allows incidental investment activity for short periods", "B": "The firm may manage the portfolio itself if the beneficiaries sign a consent, because consent removes the need for authorisation", "C": "The firm may manage the portfolio itself if it charges no separate fee, because the exemption turns on whether remuneration is paid", "D": "The firm should not manage the portfolio itself and should arrange an authorised investment manager, as this is beyond incidental activity", "E": "The firm may manage the portfolio itself, because investment management of estate assets is treated as part of the administration of the estate"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The firm should not manage the portfolio itself and should arrange an authorised investment manager, as this is beyond incidental activity): An exempt professional firm may carry on only regulated activities that are incidental to its professional services. Ongoing discretionary management of a large portfolio over several years is a separate investment management business, not a step in administering the estate. Neither consent nor a waiver of fees brings it within the exemption, and there is no short term allowance. The firm acting within its competence should arrange for an authorised manager to run the portfolio, and the executors can approve that arrangement."}, {"id": 1348, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Colin, a company director, tells his bookkeeper Brenda to send a customer invoices for work never done. Brenda honestly believes Colin that the work was done and sends the invoices. Colin knows they are false and intends to make a gain for the company. The customer pays £15,000. Which of the following best describes the liability of Brenda and Colin for fraud by false representation under section 2 of the Fraud Act 2006?", "options": {"A": "Colin is guilty only of fraud by abuse of position, because he used his authority over Brenda rather than making a representation", "B": "Neither is guilty, because the representation was made by Brenda who lacked dishonesty and Colin made no representation himself", "C": "Colin is guilty as principal through an innocent agent, and Brenda is not guilty because she lacked dishonesty and knowledge of falsity", "D": "Both are guilty, because Brenda sent the invoices and liability turns on the false representation made and not on her state of mind when she acted", "E": "Colin is guilty only as an accessory, and cannot be convicted unless Brenda is first convicted as the principal offender"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Colin is guilty as principal through an innocent agent, and Brenda is not guilty because she lacked dishonesty and knowledge of falsity): Brenda made the representation without dishonesty or knowledge that it was false, so she lacks the mental element of the offence. Colin used her as an innocent agent to make the false representation on his behalf, which makes him the principal offender, with dishonesty and intent to make a gain. His liability does not depend on any conviction of Brenda. Section 4 is not the apt charge because the conduct is a false representation, which section 2 directly covers."}, {"id": 1349, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "A solicitor acts for Winston, charged under section 47 Offences Against the Person Act 1861 with punching Gary outside a bar. At the first meeting Winston tells the solicitor that he was not threatened and that he hit Gary first because he was annoyed. Winston now wants the solicitor to tell the court that he acted in self-defence. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action for the solicitor?", "options": {"A": "Advance self-defence but only in cross-examination of Gary, because the solicitor may suggest it to him without asserting it as a fact", "B": "Disclose Winston's admission to the prosecution, because the duty to the court overrides the duty of confidentiality owed to the client", "C": "Continue to act and put the prosecution to proof, but not advance a positive case of self-defence that contradicts Winston's instructions", "D": "Cease acting immediately, because the solicitor now knows that Winston is guilty and cannot continue to represent him in the proceedings", "E": "Advance self-defence as instructed, because the solicitor must follow the client's instructions on how the defence is to be put"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue to act and put the prosecution to proof, but not advance a positive case of self-defence that contradicts Winston's instructions): A solicitor may continue to act for a client who admits guilt privately and may require the prosecution to prove its case, but must not mislead the court by advancing a case known to be false. Self-defence contradicts Winston's own account, so it cannot be put forward positively or by implication in cross-examination. Withdrawal is not required, and confidentiality means the admission cannot be disclosed to the prosecution. The duty to follow instructions does not extend to deceiving the court."}, {"id": 1350, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Farthing Ltd is buying the freehold of a warehouse in England for £450,000, and the sale is not standard rated for VAT so no VAT is added to the price. For this question, stamp duty land tax on non residential property is 0% on the first £150,000, 2% on the next £100,000 and 5% on the portion above £250,000. Its solicitor is asked to calculate the tax due on completion. Which of the following is the amount payable?", "options": {"A": "£22,500, because the top rate of 5% is applied to the whole price once it exceeds the £250,000 threshold in the table", "B": "£12,000, being the 2% charge on the middle band plus the 5% charge on the balance above £250,000", "C": "£10,000, because only the slice above £250,000 is taxed and the middle 2% band carries no charge", "D": "£9,000, because the 2% rate is applied to the whole price once it exceeds the £150,000 threshold", "E": "£2,000, because only the 2% band is charged and the slice above £250,000 is exempt from the tax"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (£12,000, being the 2% charge on the middle band plus the 5% charge on the balance above £250,000): Non residential stamp duty land tax is also charged in slices. The first £150,000 is at nil, the next £100,000 at 2% gives £2,000, and the remaining £200,000 above £250,000 at 5% gives £10,000, so the total is £12,000. Applying the top rate to the whole price, or the middle rate to the whole price, treats the table as a slab and gives the wrong figure. Omitting a band, as the other figures do, leaves part of the price untaxed."}, {"id": 1351, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Rosa signs a deed stating that she gives £20,000 from her savings account to her friend Ben, as trustee, to hold for her son Callum. She tells Ben and Callum about it but never pays any money to Ben or changes the account. Rosa dies and Callum, who gave no consideration, asks the court to enforce the arrangement. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The trust was not constituted, so Callum, a volunteer, cannot enforce it and the money stays in Rosa's estate", "B": "The trust is constituted, because Ben was told of it and accepted the role of trustee before Rosa died", "C": "Callum can enforce it, because equity treats as done what ought to be done and the deed binds Rosa's conscience", "D": "Callum can enforce it, because a gift made by deed is binding even if no property passes to the trustee", "E": "Callum can enforce it, because Rosa's deed is a declaration that she herself holds the money on trust for him"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The trust was not constituted, so Callum, a volunteer, cannot enforce it and the money stays in Rosa's estate): A trust is constituted only by a transfer of the trust property to the trustee or by the owner declaring herself trustee. Rosa chose the first method and never completed it, and the court will not treat her failed transfer as a declaration of trust (Milroy v Lord). Callum gave no value, and equity will not assist a volunteer to complete an incomplete gift. The savings therefore remain part of Rosa's estate."}, {"id": 1352, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Fatima Begum is charged with theft in the Crown Court. The prosecutor reviews the police schedule of unused material and must decide what to disclose to the defence. One item is a statement from a neighbour who did not see the incident but saw a different person leaving the area shortly beforehand. The prosecutor wants to confirm which legal test governs the duty of disclosure. Which of the following states that test?", "options": {"A": "Material must be disclosed if the defendant requests it in a defence statement, because disclosure depends on a specific defence request", "B": "Material must be disclosed if it supports the defence and would be admissible in evidence at trial, because inadmissible items cannot affect the jury", "C": "Material must be disclosed if the officer in the case believes that it is relevant to a live issue in the trial", "D": "Material must be disclosed if it is likely to lead to an acquittal, because only strong exculpatory items affect the fairness of the trial", "E": "Material must be disclosed if it might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence case"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Material must be disclosed if it might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence case): The prosecution test under the Criminal Procedure and Investigations Act 1996 is whether the material might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the case for the prosecution or assisting the case for the accused. The neighbour's statement about a different person leaving the area plainly meets that standard. The test does not require a likely acquittal, a defence request or admissibility. It is applied objectively and not by the officer's personal view."}, {"id": 1353, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Under the Rent Act, a person who lived with a protected tenant 'as his or her wife or husband' may succeed to the tenancy on the tenant's death. Dev Patel lived with his same sex partner, the tenant, for twenty years until the tenant died. The landlord argues that Dev is not a surviving spouse as the wording refers to a husband or wife. Dev's claim rests on section 3 of the Human Rights Act 1998 and article 14. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Section 3 applies only to legislation passed after the Human Rights Act came into force, which excludes the Rent Act", "B": "Section 3 binds only public authorities, so it cannot be used against a private landlord in a possession claim", "C": "The court cannot depart from the natural meaning of the words, so it can only declare the provision incompatible under section 4", "D": "The court should strike the words out, because a provision that breaches a Convention right is invalid in any claim brought against a landlord", "E": "Section 3 requires the court to read the wording compatibly so far as possible, so it can be read to include a same sex partner"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Section 3 requires the court to read the wording compatibly so far as possible, so it can be read to include a same sex partner): Section 3 of the Human Rights Act requires primary and subordinate legislation to be read and given effect compatibly with Convention rights so far as it is possible to do so, and it applies whenever the legislation was passed. The courts have accepted that this may involve reading words in a way that departs from their natural meaning, provided no fundamental feature of the scheme is contradicted. Reading the phrase to include a same sex partner achieves that here. The duty is on the court and is not limited to public authorities."}, {"id": 1354, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for Lionel Mayfield in selling his house. The buyer's solicitor sends pre-contract enquiries asking whether any person claims a right of way across the property. Lionel tells his solicitor that a neighbour regularly uses the side path and has threatened legal action, but says the solicitor should answer no. Lionel refuses to allow the dispute to be mentioned. The solicitor is concerned that a false reply would mislead the buyer. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Refuse to give a false answer, explain the position to Lionel, and cease to act if he will not permit the dispute to be disclosed", "B": "Tell the buyer's solicitor about the dispute without Lionel's consent, because the duty to the court and the public outweighs confidentiality", "C": "Leave the enquiry unanswered and proceed to exchange, because a buyer should rely on his own inspection of the path", "D": "Answer no as instructed, because the solicitor is bound to follow a client's lawful instructions and the buyer can protect himself through searches", "E": "Answer no but add a statement that the answer is given on the seller's instructions, which removes any risk of misleading the buyer"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refuse to give a false answer, explain the position to Lionel, and cease to act if he will not permit the dispute to be disclosed): A solicitor must not mislead the buyer's solicitor, either by a false statement or by silence that creates a false impression, and must not allow the client to do so. The solicitor also owes the client confidentiality, so cannot disclose the dispute without consent. The proper course is to decline to give the false answer, advise Lionel of the consequences of non-disclosure, and cease to act if he refuses to permit disclosure. Qualifying the answer as given on instructions would still mislead."}, {"id": 1355, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Marlowe and Pryce LLP act for Kwame Adjei, tenant of a shop under a lease granted in 2021. He wishes to assign the lease to Saoirse Bell for a premium of £40,000, and the landlord's draft licence requires Kwame to give an authorised guarantee agreement guaranteeing Saoirse's covenants. Saoirse, who has been a client of the firm on earlier small matters, asks the firm to act for her as well to save costs. The senior partner knows that the two sets of instructions are not identical. What should the firm do?", "options": {"A": "Act for both, but only after creating an information barrier between two fee earners who will handle each side separately", "B": "Act for both, because the assignee's earlier instructions mean the firm already knows her financial position and can deal quickly", "C": "Decline to act for the assignee, who should take separate advice, because the parties' interests conflict over the premium and guarantee", "D": "Act for both, because a firm may act for parties to the same transaction provided each gives written consent to the arrangement", "E": "Act for the assignee and decline to act for the tenant, because the assignee is the party paying the premium for the lease and has the larger stake"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to act for the assignee, who should take separate advice, because the parties' interests conflict over the premium and guarantee): Under paragraph 6.2 of the SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors a firm must not act where there is a conflict or significant risk of conflict between two clients, unless a specific exception applies. The assignor and assignee are negotiating the premium, and the tenant is being asked to guarantee the assignee's performance, so their interests diverge. Consent alone does not cure the problem, and an information barrier is relevant to confidential information, not to a conflict between two current clients. The firm should keep Kwame as its client and tell Saoirse to obtain her own advice."}, {"id": 1356, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Aldous Engineering Ltd, a company with its sole place of business in Sheffield, agreed to sell a milling machine to Iberia Taller SL, a company based in Valencia, Spain. The written contract does not contain a choice of law clause. The machine was delivered in Valencia and the price was paid in euros. Aldous issues a claim in the High Court in England for the unpaid balance. Which law will the English court apply to the contract?", "options": {"A": "The law of the country with which the contract is most closely connected, to be decided on all the circumstances of the case by the judge hearing it", "B": "English law, but only because the court is hearing the claim and applies its own law to all contracts before it", "C": "Spanish law, because the goods were delivered in Spain and that is the place where the contract was performed by the seller", "D": "English law, because absent choice a contract for the sale of goods is governed by the law of the seller's habitual residence", "E": "Spanish law, because the law of the buyer's habitual residence governs a commercial purchase made without a chosen law"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (English law, because absent choice a contract for the sale of goods is governed by the law of the seller's habitual residence): Where the parties have made no choice, the retained Rome I rules make a contract for the sale of goods subject to the law of the country in which the seller has its habitual residence. Aldous is based in Sheffield, so English law applies. The place of delivery and the currency are not connecting factors under that rule. The English court does not apply its own law merely because it is the forum, and the closest connection test applies only if the specific rules do not point to a law."}, {"id": 1357, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Bethan Rowe bought a registered freehold house for full value and was registered as proprietor. Before her purchase, the previous owner had granted a neighbour an equitable right of way, which was never protected by any entry on the register and was not an overriding interest. The neighbour now asserts the right against Bethan. Which of the following correctly states the effect of section 29 of the Land Registration Act 2002 on the neighbour's right?", "options": {"A": "The right is defeated if Bethan carried out an official search with priority before completion of the purchase", "B": "A registered disposition for value takes priority over the right, which was unprotected, so Bethan takes free", "C": "The right is defeated because Bethan paid full value, and her purchase also extinguishes every interest that was protected on the register", "D": "The right binds Bethan, because section 29 protects a buyer only if she had no knowledge of any adverse right", "E": "The right binds Bethan, because any interest existing before registration is preserved unless the buyer gives notice that she rejects it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A registered disposition for value takes priority over the right, which was unprotected, so Bethan takes free): Section 29 of the Land Registration Act 2002 gives priority to a registered disposition of a registered estate made for valuable consideration over any interest affecting the estate immediately before the disposition, unless the interest is protected by a notice, falls within Schedule 3, or is otherwise excluded. The neighbour's equitable right is unprotected and not overriding, so Bethan takes free. Her good faith and knowledge are irrelevant under the 2002 Act, and an official search affects priority only against later entries."}, {"id": 1358, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Naomi is convicted at the magistrates' court of theft from her employer. The offence is serious enough for a community order but not for custody. The pre-sentence report says her offending is linked to alcohol dependency and recommends an alcohol treatment requirement and unpaid work. Her solicitor, who also notes that Naomi works nights, is advising on the likely order. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Neither requirement needs her consent, because the order is an alternative to custody that the court may impose as it sees fit", "B": "An alcohol treatment requirement can be included only if Naomi is willing to comply with it, while unpaid work needs no consent", "C": "Unpaid work needs her consent but alcohol treatment does not, because treatment is imposed to protect the public", "D": "Alcohol treatment is available only on a medical report from a doctor, so the probation report alone cannot support the requirement", "E": "Both requirements need her consent, because every community order requirement is voluntary on the offender's part"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An alcohol treatment requirement can be included only if Naomi is willing to comply with it, while unpaid work needs no consent): Treatment requirements, such as alcohol treatment, drug rehabilitation and mental health treatment, can only be included if the offender expresses willingness to comply. Most other requirements, including unpaid work, can be imposed without consent. The court must also be satisfied that she is dependent on alcohol and that arrangements are available. A report from the probation service can properly support the requirement."}, {"id": 1359, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Ngozi and Chidi Eze are buying a freehold house for £360,000. The charges register contains a covenant imposed in 1962 that no trade or business may be carried on at the property. After you report, Ngozi tells you she plans to run a home-based dressmaking business with customers visiting. The covenant benefits neighbouring owners. How should you advise?", "options": {"A": "The covenant can be ignored because they were not parties to the 1962 deed and so are not bound by it", "B": "The covenant binds only the first owner after 1962, so business use is safe once title has passed to them", "C": "Indemnity insurance can be bought to cover the proposed business use and protect them from any enforcement action", "D": "Business use would breach the covenant, which neighbours could enforce by injunction, so they should seek a release or reconsider", "E": "The covenant is unlikely to matter because it is more than 50 years old and covenants lapse once they have stood unchallenged for that long"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Business use would breach the covenant, which neighbours could enforce by injunction, so they should seek a release or reconsider): A restrictive covenant entered on the charges register binds successors in title, and the owners with the benefit can seek an injunction if it is breached. The clients' plan is a deliberate business use, so they need to know the risk before they commit to buying. Indemnity insurance is not available for a breach that the buyer intends to commit, and age alone does not extinguish a covenant. A good report advises on releasing the covenant or reconsidering the plan."}, {"id": 1360, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Witness Sheila Corrigan saw a man run from a robbery. Police arrest Tariq Yilmaz, who is 6 feet 8 inches tall with extensive facial tattoos, and who disputes being the robber. Tariq is willing to take part in an identification procedure. The identification officer decides that, because of Tariq's very unusual appearance, a video identification and an identification parade are not practicable, since too few volunteers resembling him can be found. Which procedure is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "A video identification using fewer than eight other people, because close resemblance matters more than the number shown", "B": "A confrontation, because it is the quickest method available and Sheila has already described the tattoos to the police", "C": "Showing Sheila a single photograph of Tariq, because police already know who he is and the other methods have failed", "D": "A parade with volunteers who share only his height, because a parade is the first method to offer when appearance is unusual in height or features", "E": "A group identification, in which Sheila sees Tariq among an informal group of people, because the other methods are not practicable"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A group identification, in which Sheila sees Tariq among an informal group of people, because the other methods are not practicable): Code D lists video identification first, then a parade, then group identification, with confrontation only as a last resort. Where video and parade are not practicable, for example because enough similar-looking volunteers cannot be found, a group identification can be used. A confrontation is available only if no other method is practicable, and showing a single photograph of a known suspect to a witness is improper. Reducing the number of other people below the required minimum would make the procedure unfair."}, {"id": 1361, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Section 12 of the Security Industry Act gives the Secretary of State power to revoke a licence where she is satisfied the holder is not fit and proper. A decision to revoke Calder Guarding Ltd's licence is taken by a senior civil servant in the department and sent out in the Secretary of State's name. The Secretary of State was told about the decision after it was made. The company's chair points out that the Act refers to the Secretary of State. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The decision is valid, because any employee of the department may exercise a statutory power given to a minister in the department's name", "B": "The decision is invalid, because the Secretary of State did not know of it until after it was made, which shows her mind was not applied", "C": "The decision is valid only if the Secretary of State later ratified it in writing, since ratification is required to cure a delegation defect", "D": "The decision is valid, because under the Carltona principle a senior official's decision counts as the minister's own", "E": "The decision is invalid, because a discretion given to a named minister must be exercised by that minister personally"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The decision is valid, because under the Carltona principle a senior official's decision counts as the minister's own): Under the Carltona principle, where a statute confers a power on a minister, a decision taken by a suitably senior official on the minister's behalf is treated in law as the minister's own decision. The Act does not require personal exercise, and the decision was taken by a senior civil servant, so it is valid even though the Secretary of State learned of it later. The principle does not stretch to any employee, but seniority was not in doubt here. No ratification is needed."}, {"id": 1362, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Contracts were exchanged on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) for a freehold house, with completion due on Friday 5 June. The buyer's lender has delayed funds, and the buyer, Mr Lowe, did not complete. The seller's solicitor served a notice to complete on Monday 8 June. Mr Lowe tells you he is not worried because the seller needs the money more than he does. Which advice is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Tell him he has twenty working days from the notice, because the standard completion period applies again after a missed date", "B": "Tell him the notice is valid only if the buyer also serves one, because both parties must serve notices for time to be of the essence", "C": "Tell him the seller may rescind immediately and resell the property, because completion was not made on the contractual date", "D": "Tell him the notice has no effect unless the seller applies to court for an order, because time cannot be made of the essence by notice", "E": "Warn him that time is now of the essence, so unless he completes within ten working days the seller may rescind and keep the deposit"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Warn him that time is now of the essence, so unless he completes within ten working days the seller may rescind and keep the deposit): Either party who is ready, able and willing to complete may serve a notice that makes time of the essence, and under the Standard Conditions the period is ten working days. If Mr Lowe fails to complete within that period the seller can rescind, keep the deposit and resell. The seller's urgency does not weaken the notice. Rescission straight after the missed date, or a fresh twenty working day period, is not what the conditions provide."}, {"id": 1363, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Grace is to be sentenced at the magistrates' court for theft. Her solicitor, Idris, has seen her record, which shows two convictions for dishonesty in the last five years. Grace tells Idris to say in mitigation that she is a woman of previous good character who has never been in trouble before. The court has her record available. What should Idris do?", "options": {"A": "Tell the court that Grace has convictions and invite it to ignore her instruction, to uphold his duty to the court", "B": "Make the statement as instructed, because mitigation is a matter for the client and the court has her record anyway", "C": "Make the statement but tell the court he has not checked her record, so that his own position is protected", "D": "Make the statement but limit it to the period since her last conviction, which does not mislead the court", "E": "Decline to make that statement, since he must not mislead the court, and cease acting if Grace insists on it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to make that statement, since he must not mislead the court, and cease acting if Grace insists on it): A solicitor must never mislead the court, either deliberately or recklessly, and Idris knows Grace is not of good character. He should refuse to make the statement and explain why to her. If she insists, he must cease acting, though he cannot reveal her instructions or her confidences. Pointing out that the court holds her record does not cure a known falsehood, and a narrowly worded version would still create a false impression."}, {"id": 1364, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trustee, Olu, transfers £50,000 of trust money in breach of trust to his friend Pat as a gift. Pat spends it on a holiday. The beneficiaries want to sue Pat for knowing receipt. They say Pat received the money for her own benefit, but it is not clear what Pat knew when she received it. Which of the following states the knowledge the claimants must prove?", "options": {"A": "Knowledge of facts that made it unconscionable for Pat to retain the money, which can fall short of dishonesty", "B": "Negligence, because Pat should have made enquiries as a reasonable person receiving a large gift of money", "C": "No knowledge, because liability for receipt depends on receipt of trust property for her own benefit", "D": "Dishonesty, because Pat must have known the transfer was a breach of trust and have acted dishonestly", "E": "Actual knowledge that the money was trust property, because anything less cannot found a personal claim in equity"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Knowledge of facts that made it unconscionable for Pat to retain the money, which can fall short of dishonesty): Liability for knowing receipt needs a disposal of trust property in breach of trust, beneficial receipt by the defendant, and knowledge that makes it unconscionable for her to retain the benefit (BCCI v Akindele). Dishonesty is the test for dishonest assistance and is not required for receipt. Mere negligence is not enough, and knowledge is still required, so receipt alone does not make Pat liable. Actual knowledge is not needed in every case."}, {"id": 1365, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Paula Whitcombe, a trainee solicitor, is asked by her supervisor to find the part of the Waste Reduction Act that states, in general terms, what the Act sets out to do before she examines the detailed provisions. The Act has been published in full, with a title, preliminary words, numbered sections and several schedules. Paula has a complete printed copy in front of her. Which part of the Act should she look at?", "options": {"A": "The schedules, which are placed after the numbered sections and summarise the aim of the whole Act", "B": "The long title, which sets out in general terms the purposes the Act is intended to achieve", "C": "The enacting formula, which states the object of the Act before the first numbered section begins", "D": "The interpretation section, which defines the key terms and records the broad policy objectives behind the legislation as a whole", "E": "The short title, which sets out the purposes of the Act in a form that can be cited"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The long title, which sets out in general terms the purposes the Act is intended to achieve): The long title is the opening description that says in general terms what the Act is for, and it can be considered when interpreting the Act. The short title is merely the name by which the Act is cited. The enacting formula records that Parliament has passed the Act, not its purposes. The interpretation section defines terms, and the schedules contain detail, so neither summarises the Act's aim."}, {"id": 1366, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for the seller of a freehold house under the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition). Your firm holds the £28,000 deposit in its client account as stakeholder. Your client, Mr Webb, is buying a new home and asks you to release £20,000 now to pay his own deposit, promising to repay it if his sale falls through. The buyer has not been told. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Release half, £10,000, and keep the rest, because stakeholders may release up to half of a deposit to the seller before completion", "B": "Refuse, because the deposit is held on stakeholder terms and cannot be released to the seller before completion without the buyer's agreement", "C": "Release it once a written indemnity from Mr Webb has been signed, because the Accounts Rules allow client money to be advanced on a client's indemnity", "D": "Release it, because it is the seller's money under the contract and the seller's written promise to repay protects the buyer", "E": "Release it but tell the buyer's solicitor afterwards, because a stakeholder must report any release within the same week"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refuse, because the deposit is held on stakeholder terms and cannot be released to the seller before completion without the buyer's agreement): A deposit held as stakeholder is held for both parties until completion, and the stakeholder must not release it to the seller without the buyer's agreement. Releasing it on the seller's promise or indemnity would misuse client money and breach the duty owed to the buyer. The solicitor should refuse and explain to Mr Webb that early release needs the buyer's consent or a contract term making the solicitor an agent."}, {"id": 1367, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Pavel Novak is charged with handling stolen goods. At trial, police officer PC Allen tells the court that a member of the public telephoned the police station and said that a van with a particular registration was being used to move stolen items. The caller cannot be traced. The prosecution wishes to rely on PC Allen's account to show that the van was in fact used to move stolen items. How should the court regard this evidence?", "options": {"A": "It is hearsay, being a statement made out of court and offered to prove the matter stated, so it needs a gateway for admission", "B": "It is bad character evidence, because it implies that Pavel has a general disposition to deal in stolen goods", "C": "It is not hearsay, because a statement made to a police officer in the course of duty falls outside the rule", "D": "It is inadmissible in all circumstances, because the unidentified caller cannot be cross-examined about the allegation", "E": "It is admissible direct evidence, because PC Allen heard the call and is a witness who can be cross-examined about it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It is hearsay, being a statement made out of court and offered to prove the matter stated, so it needs a gateway for admission): A statement made outside the courtroom and adduced to prove the truth of what it says is hearsay. The caller's words about the van are offered for that purpose, so the evidence is hearsay even though PC Allen can be questioned about what he heard. It is not automatically excluded, because statutory gateways can admit it. It tells the court nothing about Pavel's character."}, {"id": 1368, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Rhona gives her housemate Fay a quantity of heroin, which Fay freely takes. Fay, an adult, collapses and becomes unresponsive, and Rhona realises Fay is seriously ill. Rhona, frightened of being arrested, puts her to bed and does not call an ambulance for six hours. Fay dies from the overdose, and medical evidence is that prompt treatment would probably have saved her. Which of the following best describes Rhona's potential liability?", "options": {"A": "Rhona is liable for murder, because she realised death was a possible result and deliberately did nothing to prevent it", "B": "Rhona cannot be liable, because a failure to call an ambulance is an omission and there is no general duty to rescue a friend", "C": "Rhona is liable for unlawful act manslaughter only, because the supply of heroin was the unlawful act and her failure to act is irrelevant", "D": "Rhona cannot be liable for any homicide offence, because Fay's free decision to take the drug breaks the chain of causation for her death", "E": "Rhona may be liable for gross negligence manslaughter, because by supplying the drug she helped create the danger and so owed Fay a duty to act"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rhona may be liable for gross negligence manslaughter, because by supplying the drug she helped create the danger and so owed Fay a duty to act): Liability by omission needs a duty of care. A person who creates or contributes to a dangerous situation, here by supplying the heroin, can owe a duty to take reasonable steps to rescue, as in Evans. Fay's free decision to take the drug broke the causal chain for the supply itself, but the later failure to summon help is a separate, gross breach that probably caused death. Murder fails because Rhona did not intend death or serious harm."}, {"id": 1369, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A cricket club plays on a ground surrounded by a high fence, with the pitch about 100 yards from the nearest road. In the previous thirty years a ball has been hit out of the ground only six times, and no one was hurt. A batsman at a match hits a ball over the fence and it strikes a passer-by on the road, causing serious injury. The passer-by sues the club in negligence. Which of the following best explains why the club is unlikely to be found in breach of duty?", "options": {"A": "The likelihood of injury was so small that a reasonable person would not have taken further precautions against it", "B": "The seriousness of the possible injury is irrelevant, so only the number of previous incidents matters to breach", "C": "A club cannot be in breach where the injury results from the act of a third party, namely the batsman", "D": "The injury was caused by a lawful sporting activity, and lawful activity of that kind is not capable of breaching a duty", "E": "The passer-by accepted the risk of walking past a cricket ground, so the club did not breach its duty to him"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The likelihood of injury was so small that a reasonable person would not have taken further precautions against it): Breach depends on balancing the likelihood of harm, its seriousness, the cost of precautions and the social value of the activity. Where the chance of injury is very small, a reasonable person need not guard against a risk that is merely foreseeable. Six escapes in thirty years with no injury, behind a high fence, meant the risk was so slight that the club was entitled to do nothing more. The seriousness of an injury is still relevant but does not outweigh such a remote probability."}, {"id": 1370, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Callum, aged 34, is killed by the negligence of a delivery driver. He was unmarried and had lived with his partner, Niamh, as if they were married for three years, though they never became civil partners or spouses. He had no children, and both his parents are alive and were not financially dependent on him. Callum's executor asks a solicitor who can claim the statutory bereavement award under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Niamh cannot claim it, because the award is limited to those who were legally married to or civil partners of the deceased", "B": "Niamh and Callum's parents can all claim it, and the court divides the fixed sum equally between them", "C": "Callum's parents can claim it, because bereavement damages are payable to the parents of any deceased adult child", "D": "Niamh can claim it, but only if she was financially dependent on Callum at the time of his death", "E": "Niamh can claim it, as she lived with Callum as a spouse for at least two years before his death, but his parents cannot"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Niamh can claim it, as she lived with Callum as a spouse for at least two years before his death, but his parents cannot): The bereavement award is a fixed sum available to a limited class: a spouse or civil partner, a person who lived with the deceased as a spouse or civil partner for at least two years before death, and, in some cases, the parents of a minor unmarried child. Niamh qualifies as a cohabitant of three years. Callum's parents do not, because he was an adult. Dependency matters for the separate dependency claim, not for the bereavement award."}, {"id": 1371, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "The articles of Granger Ltd provide that on a resolution to remove a shareholder who is also a director, that shareholder's shares carry three votes each. Adwoa, Brian and Carys each hold 100 shares and are the only directors. Brian and Carys propose an ordinary resolution under section 168 to remove Adwoa. Adwoa will vote against. Which of the following is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The resolution fails, because the weighted voting right is valid and Adwoa's 300 votes outweigh the 200 votes of Brian and Carys", "B": "The resolution is invalid, because articles cannot vary the rule of one vote per share on a poll", "C": "The resolution passes, because section 168 overrides any article that gives extra votes to a shareholder director who is facing removal from the board", "D": "The resolution passes, because Brian and Carys together hold two thirds of the issued shares, which is a majority", "E": "The resolution fails, because a director who is also a shareholder has a statutory right of veto over her own removal"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The resolution fails, because the weighted voting right is valid and Adwoa's 300 votes outweigh the 200 votes of Brian and Carys): Articles may give weighted votes to shares on particular resolutions, and such a right is effective on a resolution to remove a director. Adwoa casts 300 votes against 200 for Brian and Carys, so the ordinary resolution is not passed. Section 168 gives members the power to remove but does not displace valid weighted voting. No statutory veto exists, but the weighting has the same effect here."}, {"id": 1372, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Colm was validly removed as a director of Vance Ltd by ordinary resolution under section 168. His service contract, which has two years left to run, entitles him to a salary of £60,000 a year and says nothing about early termination. The company has stopped paying him and says that his removal ends all of its obligations to him. Colm asks whether he has any remedy. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "He may apply to court to set aside the removal, because it was a breach of his service contract with the company and caused him loss", "B": "He may keep his office until the contract ends, because the contract prevails over the company's power of removal", "C": "He has no further claim, because the statutory power of removal overrides the terms of any service contract made with the company", "D": "He may claim only the salary due up to the date of the resolution, because later pay depends on holding office", "E": "His removal is effective, but he may claim damages for breach of his service contract, as section 168 preserves compensation rights"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (His removal is effective, but he may claim damages for breach of his service contract, as section 168 preserves compensation rights): The members' power to remove a director by ordinary resolution cannot be excluded by contract, so the removal stands. Section 168 expressly does not deprive the director of compensation or damages for termination of the appointment or any contract. Colm's remedy lies in a claim for damages for breach of the service contract, subject to mitigation. He cannot undo the removal or insist on remaining in office."}, {"id": 1373, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Dora fenced off a strip of Iris's registered garden nine years ago and has used it as her own ever since, without permission. Iris has just discovered this and, worried that Dora's claim will soon ripen, asks what to do. Iris has not yet written to Dora. The strip is of real value to Iris because it gives her access to a side gate. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Enter a restriction on Dora's title to the strip, because that prevents her from applying for registration of it", "B": "Lodge a notice of the dispute on the register of Iris's own title, which stops the ten-year period running", "C": "Wait until ten years have passed and then serve notice on Dora, because the owner may object once the period ends", "D": "Write to Dora denying her right to use the strip, because a written objection stops time running against Iris", "E": "Recover possession now, by taking back the strip or issuing proceedings, before Dora completes ten years and can apply to be registered"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Recover possession now, by taking back the strip or issuing proceedings, before Dora completes ten years and can apply to be registered): The ten year period runs while Dora remains in adverse possession, and only recovery of possession by Iris stops it. Iris should therefore retake the strip peaceably or issue possession proceedings before the period ends. A restriction cannot be entered against a title that Dora does not have. A notice on Iris's own title or a letter of objection does not interrupt possession, and waiting leaves her relying on a counter-notice after Dora has already earned the right to apply."}, {"id": 1374, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Aisha Rahman is buying a freehold cottage. The draft contract, on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition), says the property is sold subject to the entries in the charges register. After exchange she discovers that a neighbour has an unregistered right to run a drain beneath the garden, which the seller has known about for years and which could not be discovered by inspection. It is not mentioned in the contract. Which statement best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The contract is void, because the seller's failure to disclose the right is a fundamental mistake about the identity of the land", "B": "The seller is in breach of the title condition, because the right was known to the seller but not disclosed or discoverable", "C": "Aisha takes subject to the right, because a buyer is deemed to have notice of every right that affects the land", "D": "Aisha takes subject to the right, because the contract is expressed to be subject to the charges register and that covers all rights", "E": "The seller is not liable, because the seller's duty is limited to the matters shown in the official copies"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The seller is in breach of the title condition, because the right was known to the seller but not disclosed or discoverable): Under the Standard Conditions the seller sells the property free of encumbrances other than those mentioned in the contract, those the seller did not and could not know about, and those discoverable on inspection or matters of public knowledge. This drain right is none of these, because the seller knew of it and it could not be seen. The statement that the sale is subject to the charges register does not extend to unregistered rights, so the seller is in breach."}, {"id": 1375, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Soprano Isla Duncan agreed with Harbour Opera to sing exclusively for it for 12 months, promising not to sing for any other opera company in that time. Midway through, she agreed to perform for Cranleigh Opera on four nights. Harbour can still use her services on those nights and does not seek to make her sing for Harbour. It wishes to stop the Cranleigh performances. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The court will award damages only, because an injunction is available only against a party who has first been warned by letter before action", "B": "The court will grant an injunction as of right, because breach of a negative covenant entitles the claimant to the injunction it seeks", "C": "The court is likely to grant an injunction enforcing her promise not to sing elsewhere, provided this does not in effect compel her to perform for Harbour", "D": "The court will refuse any order, because a contract for personal services cannot be enforced indirectly by an injunction against working for others", "E": "The court will order her to sing for Harbour, because specific performance of an exclusive services contract is available where she remains able to perform"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court is likely to grant an injunction enforcing her promise not to sing elsewhere, provided this does not in effect compel her to perform for Harbour): A court will not order a person to perform personal services, but it may restrain her from breaching an express negative covenant by working for a rival. The injunction is discretionary, and will not be granted if its effect is to force the person to perform for the claimant or to leave her unable to earn a living. Duncan can still earn from Harbour, so the injunction is a likely result. It is not available as of right, nor is a warning letter a requirement."}, {"id": 1376, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Marcus enters a neighbour's house through an unlocked back door without permission, intending only to look around. Once inside he sees a watch on a table and takes it dishonestly. Marcus had no intention to steal when he entered. Which of the following best describes his liability for burglary under section 9 of the Theft Act 1968?", "options": {"A": "He is not guilty of burglary, because a door left unlocked amounts to an implied licence to enter that removes trespasser status for any visitor", "B": "He is guilty under section 9(1)(a), because an intention to look around is the intention to commit an offence required inside", "C": "He is guilty under section 9(1)(b), because having entered as a trespasser he stole while inside, and intent on entry is not needed", "D": "He is guilty under section 9(1)(a), because he entered as a trespasser and the offence is complete on entry whatever later occurs", "E": "He is not guilty of burglary, because section 9 requires an intention to steal at the time of entry and Marcus formed none"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He is guilty under section 9(1)(b), because having entered as a trespasser he stole while inside, and intent on entry is not needed): Section 9(1)(a) requires intent on entry to steal, inflict grievous bodily harm or cause criminal damage, but section 9(1)(b) is committed where the trespasser, having entered, steals or attempts to steal. Marcus formed the intent only after entering, so only section 9(1)(b) applies. An unlocked door gives no licence to enter. Looking around is not one of the listed ulterior offences."}, {"id": 1377, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Gabriel Okafor is sole director of Calder Plastics Ltd, which has three shareholders. The latest accounts show distributable profits of £10,000, and the company has since made a loss. Gabriel instructs the company's solicitor to draft board minutes declaring a £50,000 dividend, saying the money can be paid back into the company later if the auditors complain. How should the solicitor respond?", "options": {"A": "Draft the minutes as an interim dividend instead, because interim dividends are not subject to the distributable profits requirement", "B": "Draft the minutes but record the payment as a director's loan instead, which avoids the unlawful dividend and meets Gabriel's wishes", "C": "Draft the minutes and add a covering letter warning Gabriel of possible personal liability, since the decision is a commercial matter for the director", "D": "Draft the minutes provided the shareholders approve by ordinary resolution, because shareholder approval makes the dividend lawful whatever the profits", "E": "Decline to draft the minutes and explain that the dividend would be unlawful, as later repayment does not cure a distribution made without profits"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to draft the minutes and explain that the dividend would be unlawful, as later repayment does not cure a distribution made without profits): A distribution may be made only out of profits available for distribution, and the accounts show far less than £50,000. A solicitor must not assist a client in conduct that is unlawful or in misleading others, and drafting the minutes would do so. Interim and final dividends are both subject to the profits rule, and shareholder approval cannot make an unlawful distribution lawful. Relabelling the payment as a loan to conceal its nature would involve dishonesty."}, {"id": 1378, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Ellison is killed in a road accident caused by the admitted negligence of another driver. He leaves his wife, Sofia, who was financially dependent on him, and their 15 year old daughter, Zara, who was also dependent. Sofia instructs a solicitor to bring a Fatal Accidents Act 1976 claim and says she wants the claim to cover only her own dependency, because she will look after Zara's needs herself. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Follow Sofia's instructions and bring a separate claim for Zara afterwards, since a child's claim has its own limitation period", "B": "Explain that only one action can be brought for all dependants, so Zara's dependency must be included, and advise Sofia on this", "C": "Bring the claim for Sofia alone, but record her instruction in writing to protect the firm against a later claim", "D": "Follow Sofia's instructions, because the client decides which losses to claim and a later action can be brought for Zara", "E": "Decline to act for Sofia, because acting for the widow and a child dependant would amount to an unresolvable conflict of interest"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that only one action can be brought for all dependants, so Zara's dependency must be included, and advise Sofia on this): A Fatal Accidents Act claim is a single action brought for the benefit of all dependants, so the solicitor cannot confine it to one of them. Zara's dependency must be included, and the court apportions the award between dependants. The solicitor's duty is to explain this clearly to Sofia rather than follow an instruction that cannot properly be carried out. Recording the instruction does not cure a claim that would lose Zara's entitlement."}, {"id": 1379, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for Hexham Components in negotiating the sale of its business to Tolland Group. Tolland's solicitor asks whether Hexham has any pending disputes with its major customer. The solicitor knows from the client that the customer has just threatened proceedings for £200,000, but the client tells the solicitor to reply 'no disputes of any substance'. The sale is not yet agreed, and a warranty clause is in draft. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Send the reply but add that it is given on the client's instructions only, because attributing the statement to the client removes the solicitor's responsibility", "B": "Stop acting immediately and tell the SRA, because an instruction to mislead the other side requires the solicitor to withdraw and report at once", "C": "Refuse to send a reply known to be misleading, advise the client of the risk of misrepresentation, and not volunteer the dispute without the client's consent", "D": "Send the reply as instructed, because the solicitor may rely on the client's description of the dispute and Tolland should check the point in due diligence", "E": "Tell Tolland's solicitor about the threat, because the duty not to mislead requires disclosure of all material facts even against the client's instructions"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refuse to send a reply known to be misleading, advise the client of the risk of misrepresentation, and not volunteer the dispute without the client's consent): A solicitor must not mislead the other side, and cannot give an answer known to be misleading, even on the client's instructions. There is no general duty to volunteer information to the other party in negotiation, and the client's confidentiality must be kept unless the client agrees to disclosure. The solicitor should advise that a misleading answer risks a claim for misrepresentation and breach of warranty. Attribution of the statement to the client does not cure the problem, and withdrawal is not needed if the client accepts the advice."}, {"id": 1380, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Harlow Plastics Ltd entered creditors' voluntary liquidation last month. Its director and client, Mr Barker, tells his solicitor that, shortly before the liquidation, he moved a valuable machine to a unit owned by his cousin for no payment. The liquidator has asked the directors to deliver up all books and records and to explain what happened to the company's assets. Mr Barker asks the solicitor to draft a reply stating that all assets are accounted for. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Decline to draft a statement known to be false and advise Mr Barker that directors must cooperate with the liquidator and disclose the transfer", "B": "Continue to act but refuse to advise about the transfer, because it relates to events before the liquidation began and is outside the retainer", "C": "Draft the reply as instructed, because the solicitor acts on the client's instructions and the liquidator may investigate further using statutory powers", "D": "Draft a reply that is literally accurate but silent about the machine, because the solicitor owes no duty to volunteer facts to the liquidator", "E": "Report Mr Barker to the Secretary of State, because the solicitor's duty to the court overrides the duty of confidentiality owed to the client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to draft a statement known to be false and advise Mr Barker that directors must cooperate with the liquidator and disclose the transfer): A solicitor must not mislead the liquidator or assist a client to do so, and a statement known to be false, or one that is true but deliberately incomplete, would breach the duty not to mislead and the duty to act with integrity. Directors also have a statutory duty to give the liquidator information about the company's property and dealings. The solicitor should therefore decline the instruction and advise Mr Barker to disclose the transfer, which may be challengeable as a transaction at an undervalue. Confidentiality continues, so there is no duty to report the client, and the transfer is plainly within the retainer."}, {"id": 1381, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "In 1994 Larchfield Properties granted Cordelia Marsh a 25 year lease of a unit, which she assigned in 2001 to Dev Patel, who assigned it in 2009 to Greenway Cafes Ltd. Greenway Cafes Ltd has fallen into rent arrears of £18,000 and the landlord served a notice under section 17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 on Cordelia Marsh within six months of the arrears falling due. Cordelia Marsh says she was released on assigning in 2001. Which of the following best describes her liability?", "options": {"A": "She is liable, because as original tenant under a pre 1996 lease her contractual liability lasts for the whole term and the notice was in time", "B": "She is liable only for arrears falling due before her 2001 assignment, since her contractual liability stops once she assigns", "C": "She is liable only if her 2001 assignment was made under an authorised guarantee agreement in the landlord's favour", "D": "She is not liable, because after assignment liability for rent rests with the current tenant through privity of estate alone", "E": "She is not liable, because the 1995 Act releases every original tenant automatically when the lease is assigned with the landlord's consent"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She is liable, because as original tenant under a pre 1996 lease her contractual liability lasts for the whole term and the notice was in time): A lease granted before 1 January 1996 is a legacy lease, so the 1995 Act's automatic release on assignment does not apply to the original tenant. Cordelia Marsh remains liable on privity of contract for the whole term, and the landlord kept its claim by serving the section 17 notice within six months of the arrears becoming due. Authorised guarantee agreements belong to post 1995 leases and are irrelevant here. Liability of the current tenant under privity of estate runs alongside her liability, not in place of it."}, {"id": 1382, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Whitlow and Pryce LLP acted for the claimant in an interim application in the county court that lasted three hours and was heard by a district judge. The claimant succeeded in obtaining the order sought. The judge indicates that costs will follow the event and asks counsel whether there is anything else. The solicitor wants to know how the court will deal with the costs of the application. Which of the following best describes the usual position?", "options": {"A": "Summary assessment applies to hearings over a day long, and requires the losing party to serve a statement of costs", "B": "Costs are reserved to the trial judge by default, because interim hearings do not carry separate costs consequences", "C": "Detailed assessment is the norm after any interim hearing, because the court cannot fix costs until the claim has ended", "D": "The statement of costs may be served within 14 days after the hearing, unless the judge directs a different period", "E": "Summary assessment is usual after a hearing lasting a day or less, and the winning side should have served a statement of costs before the hearing"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Summary assessment is usual after a hearing lasting a day or less, and the winning side should have served a statement of costs before the hearing): The court will normally summarily assess costs at the end of a hearing that has lasted no more than a day, so that the amount is fixed there and then. To make that possible the receiving party must file and serve a statement of costs before the hearing. Detailed assessment is the usual route where costs are for later or the hearing is longer. Interim costs are routinely dealt with at the hearing itself, not automatically reserved."}, {"id": 1383, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Eastfold Builders Ltd runs a concrete-crushing site whose dust has coated roads, cars and washing across several streets, affecting hundreds of residents. The council's environmental health officer has received many complaints, and the company ignores warnings. The council's solicitor is asked how the council can seek to stop the nuisance by civil proceedings. The company's turnover is £4 million. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "The council may not bring civil proceedings, because the control of nuisances is reserved to the police and the criminal courts", "B": "The council may sue only in a relator action, because the Attorney General's consent is needed before any public body sues", "C": "The council may apply for an injunction in its own name, if it considers it expedient to protect residents, without proving special damage", "D": "The council may sue only after the company has been prosecuted and convicted, because the criminal case must come first", "E": "The council must first show special damage to itself, because it sues as an ordinary claimant in public nuisance"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The council may apply for an injunction in its own name, if it considers it expedient to protect residents, without proving special damage): A local authority that considers it expedient for the promotion or protection of the interests of the inhabitants of its area may institute civil proceedings in its own name, under the Local Government Act 1972. It can therefore seek an injunction against a public nuisance affecting many residents, without showing special damage to itself. The relator procedure, with the Attorney General's consent, is the older route and is not the only one. No prior prosecution is needed, and civil control of nuisances is not reserved to the police."}, {"id": 1384, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Hartley and Voss, a firm of three solicitor partners in Sheffield, wishes to admit Daniel Okafor, an accountant with no legal qualification, as a manager and 30 percent owner. The partners want him to share in profits and help run the firm but not to give legal advice. The firm is currently an SRA recognised body. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The firm needs to notify the SRA within seven days, because his role is limited to non-legal work", "B": "The firm may admit him as an owner but not as a manager, because only managers must be authorised persons", "C": "The firm may admit him as a manager of its existing recognised body, because non-lawyers can own up to half of any such firm", "D": "The firm would become a licensable body and must obtain an alternative business structure licence from the SRA before admitting him", "E": "The firm cannot admit him in any capacity, because non-lawyers are barred from owning or managing a firm that provides legal services"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The firm would become a licensable body and must obtain an alternative business structure licence from the SRA before admitting him): A firm with a non-authorised person as manager or holding a material interest is a licensable body under the Legal Services Act 2007. It must therefore be licensed as an alternative business structure, and the SRA must approve the owner as suitable. A recognised body has no allowance for non-lawyer owners, and the business cannot avoid the licence by describing his role as non-legal. Non-lawyer ownership is permitted, but only through the licensing route."}, {"id": 1385, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Vale Interiors employs Gareth as its sales manager. The board told Gareth he could agree sales up to £20,000. Without telling the board, Gareth agreed to sell a £90,000 consignment of furniture to Linton Hotels. Linton's purchasing manager had dealt with Gareth many times, had been given a Vale business card describing him as 'Sales Manager', and was not told of his limit. Vale refuses to deliver, saying Gareth had no authority. Which of the following is the best analysis?", "options": {"A": "Vale is not bound, because apparent authority needs a direct statement from the board to Linton and a job title does not amount to a representation", "B": "Vale is bound, because Gareth had implied actual authority from his job title, which overrides any limit the board set in private", "C": "Vale is not bound, because Gareth had no actual authority for a sale of that size and so could not create a contract binding Vale", "D": "Vale is bound only to the extent of £20,000, because a third party can enforce the contract only up to the agent's actual limit", "E": "Vale is probably bound, because holding Gareth out as sales manager gave him apparent authority, which Linton relied on without notice of the limit"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Vale is probably bound, because holding Gareth out as sales manager gave him apparent authority, which Linton relied on without notice of the limit): A principal is bound by an agent's act within the authority the agent appears to have, where the principal has represented that he has it and the third party relied on that in good faith. Placing Gareth in the sales manager role and giving him a card with that title is such a representation. Linton did not know of the private limit. Actual authority was exceeded, but that does not defeat apparent authority, and implied actual authority cannot override an express restriction."}, {"id": 1386, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Joel, aged 19, sets fire to a pile of rubbish bags behind a block of flats late at night as a prank. The fire spreads to the building and causes serious damage. Joel had seen that the flats' windows were dark and had been told by a friend that the whole block was empty during a refurbishment, which he believed. In fact two residents were inside but escaped uninjured. He is charged with arson with intent or recklessness as to endangering life. Which of the following best describes his liability?", "options": {"A": "Not guilty of any offence, because he did not intend to damage the building and intended only to burn the rubbish", "B": "Guilty of aggravated arson, because a reasonable person would have realised that the fire might endanger anyone in the block", "C": "Guilty of aggravated arson, because recklessness as to damage to property extends automatically to recklessness about danger to life", "D": "Guilty of arson only, because he was reckless as to damage but honestly believed no person was in the building", "E": "Guilty of aggravated arson, because the residents' lives were in fact endangered by the fire that Joel started"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Guilty of arson only, because he was reckless as to damage but honestly believed no person was in the building): Aggravated arson needs intention or recklessness as to endangering life, judged subjectively. Joel honestly believed the block was empty, so he was not aware of any risk to life, and actual danger to the residents is not enough. He was reckless as to the fire spreading to the building, so simple arson is made out. Recklessness about damage does not by itself supply the mens rea for danger to life."}, {"id": 1387, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A shop is let to Tomas on a commercial lease containing a proviso for re-entry if the rent is unpaid for twenty-one days, 'whether formally demanded or not'. The quarter's rent has been unpaid for six weeks. The shop is closed and unoccupied. The landlord has not accepted rent since the default and has done nothing to suggest it accepts the situation. The landlord changes the locks. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The forfeiture is invalid, because the landlord must obtain a court order before re-entering any commercial premises", "B": "The landlord has validly forfeited by peaceable re-entry, because the clause removes the need for a formal demand and the premises are not residential", "C": "The forfeiture is invalid, because rent must be six months in arrears before a landlord may re-enter", "D": "The forfeiture is invalid, because the landlord must first serve a notice under section 146 of the Law of Property Act 1925 and allow time to pay", "E": "The forfeiture is invalid, because the landlord must make a formal demand for the exact rent on the premises despite the clause"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The landlord has validly forfeited by peaceable re-entry, because the clause removes the need for a formal demand and the premises are not residential): For non-payment of rent a landlord may forfeit by peaceable re-entry if the lease reserves a right of re-entry and no waiver has occurred. The clause dispenses with the common law requirement of a formal demand, and rent has been unpaid beyond the stated period. A section 146 notice is not needed for rent arrears, and a court order is needed only for residential premises that are occupied or where re-entry would not be peaceable. The six month period relates to the time for seeking relief, not to the right to forfeit."}, {"id": 1388, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Fenwick Interiors Ltd is claiming unpaid fees from a client. During the search for documents for standard disclosure, the solicitor finds an internal email from Fenwick's own director saying that the work was not completed to specification, which damages Fenwick's case. The director tells the solicitor the email is confidential and asks for it to be left off the list. The other side has not asked for any documents by name. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "List the email as privileged, because a damaging internal email is protected from inspection by confidentiality", "B": "Leave the email off the list but keep the original, because standard disclosure requires only documents that help the client's case", "C": "Disclose the email but redact the damaging passages, because a party may edit documents to protect commercially sensitive content", "D": "Disclose the email only if the other side makes a specific request, because the duty under standard disclosure arises on request", "E": "Advise that the email must be disclosed as it adversely affects the client's case, and stop acting if the client refuses"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Advise that the email must be disclosed as it adversely affects the client's case, and stop acting if the client refuses): Standard disclosure under CPR 31.6 requires disclosure of documents that adversely affect the party's own case as well as those that help it or hurt or help the opponent. Confidentiality is not a ground for withholding, since privilege is a separate protection. The solicitor owes a duty to the court and cannot take part in concealing a disclosable document. If the director insists, the solicitor must stop acting, and cannot omit, redact or wrongly list the email."}, {"id": 1389, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Lakeview Hotel Ltd engaged Volt Services, a firm it found online, to replace a bedroom socket. The hotel did not check Volt's qualifications or insurance and did not inspect the work after completion. The socket was wired incorrectly. Three months later a guest, Tamsin, received an electric shock when she used it and was injured. She sues the hotel under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957. The hotel says it is not liable because the fault was Volt's. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The hotel is not liable, because the fault lay in Volt's wiring and the hotel had no knowledge of it before the shock", "B": "The hotel is not liable, because an occupier who entrusts work to an independent contractor is relieved of the common duty of care", "C": "The hotel is likely to be liable, because it took no steps to check Volt's competence or that the work was properly done", "D": "The hotel is liable only if Tamsin can show that the hotel's own employees had carried out the electrical work themselves", "E": "The hotel is liable, but only for the cost of rewiring the socket, as personal injury claims lie solely against the contractor"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The hotel is likely to be liable, because it took no steps to check Volt's competence or that the work was properly done): An occupier may avoid liability for danger due to a contractor's faulty work only if it was reasonable to entrust the work to a contractor, the occupier took reasonable steps to satisfy itself that the contractor was competent, and, where practicable, checked that the work was properly done. The hotel did none of these things, so the statutory defence is not available. Merely engaging a contractor is not enough, and lack of knowledge of the fault does not help an occupier that failed to check. The suggestion that only the contractor can be sued is wrong, because the hotel's own duty to its guest remains."}, {"id": 1390, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Edwin Moss died intestate aged 61. For the last three years of his life he lived in the same household as Tariq Rahim, as if they were a married couple, though they never married or entered a civil partnership. Edwin's estate of £280,000 passes on intestacy to his brother, Leonard, who lives in Canada. Tariq has no right to a share on intestacy and asks whether he can apply to the court under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, but only if he can show that the deceased was maintaining him before death, as cohabitees must prove dependency", "B": "No, because only a surviving spouse or civil partner is entitled to apply as a partner of the deceased under the Act", "C": "No, because a cohabitee must have lived with the deceased for at least five years to qualify as an applicant", "D": "Yes, because a cohabitee has the same standing as a spouse and can seek the more generous standard of provision on the facts of the relationship", "E": "Yes, as a cohabitee who lived with Edwin as a couple in the same household for at least two years immediately before his death"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, as a cohabitee who lived with Edwin as a couple in the same household for at least two years immediately before his death): A person who lived with the deceased as if a spouse or civil partner in the same household for at least two years immediately before death can apply as a cohabitee. Tariq had lived with Edwin for three years, so he has standing. He is not required to prove dependency, although he could alternatively qualify on that basis. Cohabitees are entitled only to the maintenance standard of provision and not to the spouse standard."}, {"id": 1391, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Firefighter Mehmet attends a serious building collapse with his crew and spends several hours recovering injured people and bodies. He is not himself in danger of being hurt, and he does not believe he is. Some weeks later he is diagnosed with post traumatic stress disorder. He sues the owner of the building, whose negligence caused the collapse, arguing that as a rescuer he is a primary victim. Which of the following best describes his status?", "options": {"A": "He is a primary victim, because every rescuer who attends an incident is treated as having been within the zone of danger", "B": "He cannot recover, because professional rescuers are barred from claiming for psychiatric harm suffered in the course of duty", "C": "He is not a primary victim, as a rescuer must be in physical danger or reasonably believe himself to be, so the control mechanisms apply", "D": "He is a primary victim, because the owner's negligence foreseeably caused psychiatric harm to professional rescuers called to the scene", "E": "He is a secondary victim exempt from the control mechanisms, because rescuers need not show a close tie to those injured"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He is not a primary victim, as a rescuer must be in physical danger or reasonably believe himself to be, so the control mechanisms apply): A rescuer is a primary victim only if he is exposed to danger or reasonably believes himself to be. Mehmet was not, so he is treated as a secondary victim and must satisfy the control mechanisms, including a close tie to those injured and proximity in time and space. Professional rescuers are not barred from claiming, and no special exemption from those mechanisms applies. The owner's foreseeable harm to rescuers does not itself create primary victim status."}, {"id": 1392, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Marlow Builders Ltd is sued by a homeowner for defective underpinning work. Marlow served its defence on 5 May. It believes a subcontractor, Tidewell Piling Ltd, carried out the work and wants a contribution from it. On 20 May Marlow issues a Part 20 claim form against Tidewell. No order of the court has been obtained. Is the Part 20 claim validly made?", "options": {"A": "No, because contribution claims must be brought in separate proceedings once the principal claim has been defended", "B": "Yes, because the claim form was issued within 28 days of the defence and permission is needed after that period", "C": "No, because a contribution claim can be made only by adding the subcontractor as a second defendant with the claimant's consent", "D": "No, because the Part 20 claim was issued after the defence was filed, so Marlow needed the court's permission", "E": "Yes, because Marlow may issue a Part 20 claim without permission within 14 days after filing its defence"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because the Part 20 claim was issued after the defence was filed, so Marlow needed the court's permission): A defendant may issue a Part 20 claim without permission only if the claim form is issued before or at the same time as the defence is filed. Marlow's claim came about two weeks after the defence, so the court's permission was required and has not been obtained. There is no grace period after the defence. A Part 20 claim is the correct route for contribution, and the claimant's consent is irrelevant."}, {"id": 1393, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Pawel complained in writing to his solicitors' firm on 3 September about the handling of his employment claim. The firm acknowledged the complaint but has not issued a final response. It is now 12 November. Pawel is unhappy about the delay and asks a new solicitor what he can do. The firm's complaints procedure promises a decision within twelve weeks. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "He must first complain to the Solicitors Regulation Authority, because the Legal Ombudsman accepts only complaints referred through the regulator", "B": "He can go to the Legal Ombudsman only after the firm issues a final response, because the eight week period does not apply to unanswered complaints", "C": "He must wait until the end of the firm's twelve week period, because the Legal Ombudsman will accept a complaint only after the firm's own timetable", "D": "He can take the complaint to the Legal Ombudsman now, because the firm has had eight weeks to resolve it and has not issued a final response", "E": "He should refer the matter to the Legal Ombudsman only after six months, because that is the minimum delay before it will accept a complaint"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (He can take the complaint to the Legal Ombudsman now, because the firm has had eight weeks to resolve it and has not issued a final response): A client who has complained to the firm may refer the matter to the Legal Ombudsman once eight weeks have passed since the complaint, or earlier if the firm has issued its final response. Pawel complained on 3 September, so eight weeks expired at the end of October and he can refer now. The firm cannot extend the period by publishing a longer one in its own procedure. The six months in the scheme rules is a limit running from the final response, not a minimum waiting time."}, {"id": 1394, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A scrap dealer, Calder Metals Ltd, stockpiled 5,000 old tyres on its yard. Without negligence, a fire started and spread onto the neighbouring house owned by Maud, where her daughter Amara lived as a licensee with no tenancy. Amara was burned and her laptop and clothing destroyed. The tyres had been bought from a foreign supplier. Which of the following best describes the position on claims by Amara and Maud under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher?", "options": {"A": "Both Amara and Maud may claim, but only if Calder was negligent in storing the tyres, because the rule depends on fault", "B": "Amara cannot claim, having no interest in the land and suffering personal injury, but Maud may claim for the damage to the house", "C": "Amara can recover for her belongings but not for her injuries, because the rule protects property wherever it is kept", "D": "Amara may claim under the rule if she can show that she lived at the house as her home for many years", "E": "Amara can recover for her injuries and belongings, because strict liability protects all persons harmed by an escape from the defendant's land"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Amara cannot claim, having no interest in the land and suffering personal injury, but Maud may claim for the damage to the house): The rule in Rylands v Fletcher is a species of nuisance and protects only those with an interest in the land affected. Amara is a licensee, so she cannot sue under the rule at all. In any event, personal injury is not recoverable under it, which would also bar her claim for her burns. Maud, as owner, may claim for damage to the house. Length of residence does not create the necessary proprietary interest, and the rule does not require proof of negligence."}, {"id": 1395, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Marlow Fabrics Ltd has 5,000 ordinary shares and 1,000 preference shares, each class carrying its own rights in the articles. The preference shares carry a fixed dividend payable in priority to ordinary dividends. Seren holds 800 preference shares and Ewan holds 200. The ordinary shareholders pass a special resolution to amend the articles and remove the dividend priority. Seren supports the change, Ewan opposes it, and the articles contain no separate provision on varying class rights. What is the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The change needs the unanimous consent of the preference shareholders, so Ewan can block it, because every holder in that class must agree", "B": "The change needs the support of three quarters of all shares in issue, ordinary and preference voting together, as one combined class of members", "C": "The change needs three quarters of the preference shares by value, which Seren alone provides, though Ewan may seek cancellation by the court as unfairly prejudicial", "D": "No class consent is needed, because the special resolution already passed is enough to amend the articles for every shareholder", "E": "The change needs consent from three quarters of the preference shareholders counted by number, so Ewan's agreement is required as one of two holders"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The change needs three quarters of the preference shares by value, which Seren alone provides, though Ewan may seek cancellation by the court as unfairly prejudicial): Removing the dividend priority varies the rights attached to the preference class. Section 630 requires written consent from holders of at least 75 per cent in nominal value of the class, or a special resolution at a separate class meeting. Seren holds 80 per cent of the class, so her consent alone satisfies the test, and the vote is by value, not by the number of holders. Ewan holds enough shares to apply to the court within the statutory period to cancel the variation as unfairly prejudicial."}, {"id": 1396, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Pickard Interiors Ltd has a county court judgment for £14,000 against Mr Danvers, who trades as a sole trader fitting kitchens. He has not paid the sum, although he has been sent a formal demand. Pickard does not know which bank Mr Danvers uses, whether he owns property, or whether he has vehicles or equipment of value. Pickard wishes to recover the money as efficiently as possible. What should the solicitor do first?", "options": {"A": "Apply for a third party debt order against his bank, which can be made before the creditor has any evidence about the accounts", "B": "Apply for a charging order over his bank account, because charging orders are used to secure money held in accounts", "C": "Apply for an attachment of earnings order, because a sole trader's regular profits can be attached in the same way as wages", "D": "Apply for an order that Mr Danvers attend court to give information about his means and assets, before choosing a method", "E": "Instruct a High Court enforcement officer to seize goods, because a county court judgment must be transferred to the High Court before any enforcement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Apply for an order that Mr Danvers attend court to give information about his means and assets, before choosing a method): Where the creditor does not know what the debtor owns, the sensible first step is an order under CPR Part 71 requiring the debtor, or an officer of a company, to attend court and answer questions about his means and assets. The answers show which method will work. Attachment of earnings applies to employees rather than the self employed. A charging order attaches to land or securities, and a third party debt order needs the identity of the person who owes the debtor money."}, {"id": 1397, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ben, who has no previous convictions, is tried at the magistrates' court for drink driving. The bench acquits him, having decided that a statutory provision on breath test procedure required something different from what its wording says. The prosecutor believes the bench misinterpreted the statute. Ben was represented throughout. What is the prosecution's route to challenge the acquittal?", "options": {"A": "Apply to the magistrates to state a case for the High Court on the point of law, within 21 days", "B": "Ask the Attorney General to refer the acquittal to the Court of Appeal to obtain a ruling on the law", "C": "Appeal to the Court of Appeal against the acquittal on a point of law, with leave of that court", "D": "Appeal to the Crown Court against the acquittal within 15 business days, as the defendant could against a conviction by the magistrates", "E": "Ask the bench to reopen the trial and substitute a conviction, because the error was one of law"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply to the magistrates to state a case for the High Court on the point of law, within 21 days): The prosecution has no appeal to the Crown Court from a magistrates' acquittal. Its route is to ask the magistrates to state a case for the Divisional Court of the King's Bench Division on a point of law, within 21 days, or to seek judicial review. The Attorney General's reference on a point of law applies only to acquittals on indictment in the Crown Court. The bench cannot reverse its own acquittal."}, {"id": 1398, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Under a contract incorporating the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition), the buyer, Ellis, fails to complete on the contractual completion date because his lender's funds arrived two days late. The seller, Marlowe, was ready to complete throughout. The price is £500,000 and Ellis paid a deposit of £50,000. Which of the following correctly describes the compensation Marlowe may claim?", "options": {"A": "Nothing, because compensation is due only after a notice to complete has been served and has expired", "B": "A fixed sum of ten per cent of the price, because the deposit is forfeited on any late completion", "C": "Interest at the contract rate on the price less the deposit, for the days between contractual and actual completion", "D": "Interest at the contract rate on the full price, for each day between the contractual and actual completion dates", "E": "Damages for loss of the use of the whole price at the bank base rate, for the whole period of delay"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Interest at the contract rate on the price less the deposit, for the days between contractual and actual completion): The standard conditions provide for contractual compensation calculated as interest at the contract rate on the price less any deposit paid. It runs from the contractual completion date to actual completion, and it is available whenever completion is delayed by the other party's default. A notice to complete is not needed for this. The deposit is only forfeited on rescission after a failure to complete."}, {"id": 1399, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Halden Logistics contracted to ship a client's machinery from Tilbury on 10 November on a vessel named in the contract, which Halden chartered. Both parties knew when contracting that the vessel's class certificate expired on 1 November and needed renewal. Halden failed to arrange renewal because it was short of funds, and the vessel was detained on 5 November. The client says the contract is frustrated and wants its prepayment returned under the 1943 Act. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The contract is not frustrated, because frustration arises only where an event makes performance illegal, and mere impossibility does not discharge a contract", "B": "The contract is frustrated, because detention of the only named vessel made performance impossible and the client may recover its prepayment under the 1943 Act", "C": "The contract is not frustrated, because the detention was a foreseen risk caused by Halden's own conduct, so Halden is in breach", "D": "The contract is frustrated, because the detention was outside Halden's control on 5 November, even though the lapse of the certificate was foreseeable earlier", "E": "The contract is frustrated, but the client must forgo its prepayment, because Halden's fault prevents the 1943 Act operating in the client's favour"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The contract is not frustrated, because the detention was a foreseen risk caused by Halden's own conduct, so Halden is in breach): Frustration is not available where the event was foreseen by the parties or was brought about by the act or election of the party relying on it. Halden knew the certificate would expire and chose not to renew it, so the detention flows from its own default. Impossibility can frustrate a contract, but not where it is self induced. The contract therefore remains in force, and Halden's failure to ship is a breach for which the client may claim damages."}, {"id": 1400, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Raj Menon buys a house in his sole name and, over dinner, tells his sister Anita that he holds the house on trust for her and himself in equal shares. Nothing is written down at that time. Two years later, Raj signs a short handwritten note saying that he holds the house for Anita and himself half each, but Raj now disputes that Anita's interest is enforceable. Is the declaration of trust enforceable by Anita, and why?", "options": {"A": "No, because a declaration of trust of land must itself be made by deed, and a signed note cannot satisfy that requirement", "B": "Yes, because section 53(1)(b) requires written evidence signed by the person declaring the trust, and that evidence may be produced after the declaration", "C": "No, because the note was signed by Raj alone and section 53(1)(b) demands signatures from both settlor and beneficiary", "D": "No, because the writing was produced two years after the oral declaration and written evidence must exist when the trust is declared", "E": "Yes, because an oral declaration of trust of land is fully effective and the note merely fixes the shares for evidential purposes"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because section 53(1)(b) requires written evidence signed by the person declaring the trust, and that evidence may be produced after the declaration): Section 53(1)(b) of the Law of Property Act 1925 provides that a declaration of trust respecting land must be manifested and proved by some writing signed by the person able to declare the trust. This is a rule of evidence, not of validity, so the writing can be created later, as Raj's note was. The beneficiary does not have to sign. An oral declaration alone would be unenforceable, but no deed is needed and the later note cures the evidential gap."}, {"id": 1401, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Karl and Leo agreed to steal copper cable from a depot at night. They drove there in a van owned by Karl. During the getaway, with security guards in pursuit, Karl drove at excessive speed and negligently crashed the van, injuring Leo, who was a passenger. Karl holds compulsory motor insurance. Leo sues Karl in negligence for his injuries. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because the injury was caused by careless driving, which was separate from the theft that was the crime", "B": "The claim fails, because the injury arose from the joint criminal enterprise and allowing recovery would harm the integrity of the legal system", "C": "The claim fails, because Leo is taken to have consented to the risk of careless driving by joining a getaway in the van", "D": "The claim succeeds, because Karl owed Leo a duty as a passenger and his compulsory motor insurance means illegality cannot be raised against Leo", "E": "The claim succeeds with a reduction for contributory negligence, because Leo chose to take part in the venture and share its risks"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The claim fails, because the injury arose from the joint criminal enterprise and allowing recovery would harm the integrity of the legal system): Where a claimant is injured in the course of a joint criminal enterprise and the claim is closely connected with the crime, the court will refuse relief if allowing it would harm the integrity of the legal system. Leo was hurt during the getaway, so his injury arose directly from the joint theft. Compulsory insurance does not remove that bar. Consent is the wrong route to the result, because a passenger's acceptance of the risk of careless driving cannot defeat a claim that compulsory insurance covers."}, {"id": 1402, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Mrs Fraser promised her carer Dee that Dee would inherit her house, worth £500,000. Dee gave up a job paying £30,000 a year and cared for Mrs Fraser for five years. Mrs Fraser's will left the house to her son. The court accepts that proprietary estoppel is made out. The son argues that Dee should receive only compensation for her lost earnings. Which of the following is the most accurate statement of how the court will approach the remedy?", "options": {"A": "The court will award the house or its value, because Dee's expectation measures the remedy once unconscionability is shown", "B": "The court must award only the lost earnings, because the remedy is limited to compensating the loss shown", "C": "The court must award the house, because the remedy gives the claimant exactly what was promised", "D": "The court will award half the house, because estoppel remedies divide the property equally between promisor and claimant", "E": "The court awards the minimum needed to satisfy the equity, which may be less than the house, in proportion to the detriment"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court will award the house or its value, because Dee's expectation measures the remedy once unconscionability is shown): Proprietary estoppel remedies are discretionary, but following Guest v Guest the promise is the starting point, and in a bargain-like case such as care given for a promised house the court will normally award the house or its value unless that would be disproportionate to the detriment. Compensation limited to lost earnings and a remedy fixed at the minimum equity are not the approach. No rule divides the property equally."}, {"id": 1403, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Tamsin is the sole director of Fyne Ltd, a private company. On 4 June the members passed a special resolution to change the company's name. The resolution was passed at a general meeting held at the registered office and the company's constitution has no unusual filing provisions. Tamsin asks how long she has to send a copy of the resolution to the registrar. Which of the following periods is correct?", "options": {"A": "Within 15 days after the resolution was passed, and failure is an offence by the company and every officer in default", "B": "Within 21 days after the resolution was passed, which is the period allowed for registering a charge", "C": "Within one month after the resolution was passed, which is the period allowed for filing a return of allotment", "D": "No fixed period applies, provided the resolution is filed before the next confirmation statement is delivered to the registrar", "E": "Within 14 days after the resolution was passed, which is the period allowed for notifying a change of directors"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Within 15 days after the resolution was passed, and failure is an offence by the company and every officer in default): A copy of every special resolution must be delivered to the registrar within 15 days after it is passed. Failure is an offence by the company and every officer in default. The other periods are real deadlines but belong to different filings: director changes, charges and allotments. Waiting for the next confirmation statement would breach the 15 day rule."}, {"id": 1404, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Sinead is a member of Orchid Advisory LLP. There is no LLP agreement. She wishes to leave to start her own business and has told the other three members. They say she cannot leave without their unanimous agreement. The LLP trades profitably and Sinead's capital contribution is £15,000. She has been a member for three years and no other member has served notice on her. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "She may leave only by applying to court, because an LLP member can end membership only through a court order", "B": "She may leave only at the end of the financial year, after giving three months' notice to the LLP", "C": "She may leave immediately without notice, because an LLP member has no continuing duties to the other members", "D": "She may leave only if all the other members agree, because members of an LLP cannot withdraw unilaterally", "E": "She may retire by giving reasonable notice to the other members, because the default rules allow retirement on reasonable notice"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (She may retire by giving reasonable notice to the other members, because the default rules allow retirement on reasonable notice): Where an LLP has no agreement, the default rules in the 2001 Regulations allow any member to retire on giving reasonable notice to the other members. Unanimous consent is not required. Notice is still needed, so Sinead cannot walk away immediately, and no court order is involved. The default rules also contain no fixed year end timetable."}, {"id": 1405, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Brightway Couriers Ltd bought a second-hand van from Kestrel Motors Ltd for £15,000 and used it daily for six months. Brightway then learned that the van had been stolen before Kestrel bought it, and the true owner reclaimed it. Kestrel had no right to sell the van. Brightway now wants its money back in full, and Kestrel says the six months of use must be set against the price. Both companies deal in the course of business. Which of the following best describes Brightway's position?", "options": {"A": "It must show a loss, because the statutory term as to title is a warranty and not a condition", "B": "It is limited to damages, because Brightway accepted the van by using it and so lost the right to reject", "C": "It has no remedy against Kestrel, because Brightway had a reasonable time to check the title and failed to do so", "D": "It can recover the price less a deduction for six months of use, because Brightway has had some real benefit from the van in its daily courier work", "E": "It can recover the whole price, because breach of the implied condition as to title means there was a total failure of consideration"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It can recover the whole price, because breach of the implied condition as to title means there was a total failure of consideration): The term implied by section 12 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 that the seller has the right to sell is a condition. Where the seller had no title, the buyer received none of what it contracted for, so there is a total failure of consideration and the whole price is recoverable even after use. The acceptance argument fails because the buyer never got the benefit of ownership. The reduction for use is the tempting error, but the law does not apportion in this situation."}, {"id": 1406, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Priya's will leaves her residuary estate of £90,000 'to such charitable or benevolent objects as my executor shall select'. Priya's niece, who would take on intestacy, says the gift is void. The executor, a respected local solicitor, says it must be valid because the whole fund will be spent on good causes. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The gift takes effect as a non-charitable power, because the executor is an ascertainable person who can take the fund", "B": "The gift is valid, because benevolent purposes are treated as charitable in law and so the words add no extra purpose", "C": "The gift fails, because 'benevolent' objects can go beyond charitable ones, so the purposes are not exclusively charitable", "D": "The gift is valid, because the executor will choose only charitable causes and a trustee's good faith validates the gift", "E": "The gift is saved by cy près, because the testator's wish to benefit good causes shows a general charitable intention"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The gift fails, because 'benevolent' objects can go beyond charitable ones, so the purposes are not exclusively charitable): A charitable trust must be exclusively charitable, and the word 'benevolent' allows funds to be used for objects that are not charitable in law. Because the executor could lawfully apply the fund to a non-charitable object, the trust cannot stand as charitable, and it also fails as a purpose trust. What the executor intends to do does not matter, since validity depends on the width of the language. Cy près applies only to a valid charitable gift that cannot be carried out, not to one that was never exclusively charitable."}, {"id": 1407, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Beacon Marine Ltd sues Orwell Shipping Ltd for £120,000 for an unpaid invoice. Orwell has filed a defence saying that the service was defective, but it gives no details and Orwell has disclosed no documents. Beacon's solicitor plans to apply for summary judgment. Beacon believes the defence is a bare assertion. Which test will the court apply to the application?", "options": {"A": "Whether Orwell's defence is frivolous or vexatious, so that it amounts to an abuse of the court's process", "B": "Whether Beacon has shown that its claim is more likely than not to succeed at trial, on the balance of probabilities, as at a final hearing", "C": "Whether Orwell has no real prospect of successfully defending the claim and there is no other compelling reason for a trial", "D": "Whether Orwell has no arguable defence, meaning that it has not advanced even a point that is fanciful", "E": "Whether Orwell's case is weaker than Beacon's, after the court has weighed the competing evidence in detail"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Whether Orwell has no real prospect of successfully defending the claim and there is no other compelling reason for a trial): The court may give summary judgment if the respondent has no real prospect of succeeding on the claim or issue and there is no other compelling reason why the case should go to trial. A real prospect is more than fanciful but need not be probable. The court must not conduct a mini trial or decide which side is more likely to win. Abuse of process is a ground for striking out, which is a different application."}, {"id": 1408, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Marlow and Pratt LLP issued a claim for £55,000 and served the particulars of claim on Dalton Ltd by first class post on Monday 9 March 2026. The particulars were served separately from the claim form, which Dalton had received earlier. Dalton did not acknowledge service. On Wednesday 25 March 2026 at 11 am, the solicitors filed a request for default judgment, and judgment was entered the same day. Dalton applies in May 2026 to set the judgment aside, but admits its delay was long and its prospects are modest. What will the court do?", "options": {"A": "Set it aside, because Dalton was not given a notice warning that default judgment would be sought against it", "B": "Set it aside if Dalton shows a real prospect of defending the claim and that it applied promptly", "C": "Refuse to set it aside, because the judgment was correctly entered once 14 days had passed from posting", "D": "Refuse to set it aside, because the delay was long and the prospects of success are modest", "E": "Set it aside as of right, because judgment was entered before the time for acknowledging service had expired"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Set it aside as of right, because judgment was entered before the time for acknowledging service had expired): Particulars posted on Monday 9 March are deemed served on Wednesday 11 March, the second day after posting. The period for acknowledging service is 14 days after service of the particulars and so runs to the end of Wednesday 25 March. Judgment was therefore entered before the right to it had arisen, and the court must set aside a judgment entered wrongly, whatever Dalton's delay or prospects. The discretionary test applies only to a judgment properly entered."}, {"id": 1409, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A ten-year lease of an office was granted in 2018 by Landlord Ltd to Tara, who agreed to pay rent and observe the tenant covenants. In 2022, with Landlord Ltd's consent, Tara assigned the lease to Uma. Tara gave no authorised guarantee agreement. Uma has now fallen two quarters behind with the rent, and Landlord Ltd wants to claim from Tara because she remained the original contracting party. Which of the following best describes Tara's liability?", "options": {"A": "Tara remains liable only if Landlord Ltd serves a notice on her within six months of each arrear becoming due", "B": "Tara has no liability, because on a lawful assignment of a lease granted after 1995 the outgoing tenant is released from the covenants", "C": "Tara is liable for the rent only, because rent is a payment obligation and not a covenant touching and concerning the land", "D": "Tara remains liable for the whole term, because privity of contract continues after assignment for her original promises to Landlord Ltd", "E": "Tara is released unless the landlord's consent to the assignment was given on the condition that she stays liable"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tara has no liability, because on a lawful assignment of a lease granted after 1995 the outgoing tenant is released from the covenants): For a new tenancy, meaning one granted on or after 1 January 1996, the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 releases the tenant from the tenant covenants on a lawful assignment. Tara assigned with consent and gave no authorised guarantee agreement, so she is released, rent included. The old privity of contract rule applies only to older leases. The six month notice relates to former tenants who remain liable, so it does not arise here."}, {"id": 1410, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Ahmed owns the freehold of a shop in his sole name. In March he told his brother Bilal in conversation, 'I now hold the shop on trust for you.' Nothing was written down. In June Ahmed sent Bilal's solicitor a signed letter that said, 'I confirm that since March I have held the shop on trust for Bilal.' Ahmed has now changed his mind and says no trust exists. The shop has not been transferred to anyone. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "No trust exists, because legal title must be transferred to Bilal before a trust of land can be constituted", "B": "A trust exists but is unenforceable, because evidence in writing for land must be a formal deed executed with witnesses", "C": "A trust exists only from June, because the signed letter is the date on which the trust was created", "D": "A trust exists and is enforceable, because signed written evidence of a land trust declaration can be produced later", "E": "No trust exists, because a declaration of trust of land made orally in March is void and cannot be validated later"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A trust exists and is enforceable, because signed written evidence of a land trust declaration can be produced later): A declaration of trust of land needs only signed written evidence under section 53(1)(b) of the Law of Property Act 1925, and the evidence can come into existence later. The trust is created when Ahmed declared himself trustee in March, and the later letter merely proves it. Because Ahmed is the legal owner declaring himself trustee, no transfer is needed, and no deed is required. The tempting view that the oral trust is void fails because the lack of writing only makes it unenforceable until evidence exists."}, {"id": 1411, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Gwen is a partner in a firm. A client, Mr Rahman, complains that a bill for £6,500 on a failed property purchase was too high. The firm accepts it spent more time than it should have and Gwen proposes to settle by reducing the bill by £2,000, but only if Mr Rahman signs a clause agreeing not to complain to the Legal Ombudsman or the SRA about the matter. The offer is in writing and Mr Rahman is advised by a friend who is a barrister. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The clause is improper only because the reduction is too small, because a settlement must cover at least half of the disputed bill", "B": "The clause is acceptable, because the client is advised and the reduction is fair consideration for giving up the right to complain", "C": "The clause is improper, because a solicitor cannot seek to prevent a client from complaining to the Legal Ombudsman or the regulator", "D": "The clause is acceptable if it excludes complaints to the SRA, because only the right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman can be waived by agreement", "E": "The clause is acceptable if the firm tells the client he may take independent advice, because that cures any unfairness in the arrangement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The clause is improper, because a solicitor cannot seek to prevent a client from complaining to the Legal Ombudsman or the regulator): A solicitor must not seek to restrict a client's ability to complain to the Legal Ombudsman or to report concerns to the SRA, and an agreement that tries to do so is improper. It makes no difference that Mr Rahman is advised or is being offered a reduction. The firm can settle the fee dispute on proper terms, for example by reducing the bill and recording that the matter is resolved, without a term that prevents any complaint. The size of the reduction is not the problem, and no independent advice cures the clause."}, {"id": 1412, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trust created in 2016 gives a fund to Marcus contingently on his reaching 25. The trust instrument does not exclude section 31 Trustee Act 1925. Marcus has just turned 18, and during his minority the trustees accumulated £20,000 of unused income. Marcus asks the trustees what he is now entitled to receive. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "Marcus is entitled to the accumulated £20,000 immediately, because accumulations belong to a beneficiary once he is eighteen", "B": "Marcus is entitled to receive the income from now, but the accumulated £20,000 stays with the capital and passes with it if his interest vests", "C": "Marcus has no right to income until he reaches 25, because his interest is contingent on reaching that age", "D": "The trustees can pay him income at their discretion until he is 25, since section 31 gives no entitlement after eighteen", "E": "Marcus is entitled to income only if he gives the trustees written notice, since beneficiaries aged eighteen must claim it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Marcus is entitled to receive the income from now, but the accumulated £20,000 stays with the capital and passes with it if his interest vests): Where a beneficiary reaches eighteen with a contingent interest, section 31(1)(ii) requires the trustees to pay him the income until his interest vests or fails. Accumulated income does not become his at eighteen, because the interest is still contingent, so the accumulations stay with the capital and follow it. If Marcus reaches 25 he takes both. Nothing requires a written claim."}, {"id": 1413, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Priya agrees in a signed written contract to buy a freehold house from Marcus Fitzgerald for £320,000 and pays a ten per cent deposit. Completion is set for 14 October. Before completion, Marcus's mortgage lender asks Priya's solicitor which interest Priya currently holds in the property. No transfer deed has yet been executed and nothing has been registered at HM Land Registry. Which of the following best describes the nature of Priya's interest in the house at this stage?", "options": {"A": "She holds a personal contractual right against Marcus, which cannot bind anyone who later acquires the property from him", "B": "She holds the legal estate already, because a specifically enforceable contract signed by both parties transfers ownership at law", "C": "She holds a legal interest in the house, because payment of the deposit is sufficient to vest the freehold in the buyer", "D": "She holds an equitable interest under the contract, and the legal estate passes on execution of the transfer and registration", "E": "She holds a bare licence to enter the property, since an uncompleted contract confers no proprietary right in the land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She holds an equitable interest under the contract, and the legal estate passes on execution of the transfer and registration): A specifically enforceable contract for the sale of land gives the buyer an equitable interest, usually called an estate contract, from the moment the contract is made. Section 52 of the Law of Property Act 1925 requires a deed to transfer a legal estate, and in registered land the transfer must also be registered under the Land Registration Act 2002. Paying a deposit does not alter this. Because the interest is proprietary, it can be protected and can bind some third parties, which is why it is more than a personal right or licence."}, {"id": 1414, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Mark, obsessed with his former colleague Sara, sends her threatening letters over eight months and follows her at a distance without ever touching her or threatening her with immediate violence. Sara develops a severe depressive illness that a psychiatrist confirms is serious psychiatric injury. Mark knew his campaign might cause her some harm. He is charged under section 20 Offences Against the Person Act 1861 with inflicting grievous bodily harm. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "He can be convicted, because inflicting does not require a direct assault and serious psychiatric illness is grievous bodily harm", "B": "He can be convicted only of actual bodily harm, because grievous bodily harm requires a physical injury to the body", "C": "He cannot be convicted, because inflicting requires direct application of force to the body and there was no assault or battery", "D": "He can be convicted only if he intended to cause serious psychiatric illness, because section 20 requires proof of intent to cause grievous harm", "E": "He cannot be convicted, because serious psychiatric illness is not bodily harm and is excluded from section 20"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He can be convicted, because inflicting does not require a direct assault and serious psychiatric illness is grievous bodily harm): Inflicting under section 20 does not need an assault or battery, so harm caused indirectly by a campaign of conduct can qualify. Serious psychiatric illness is bodily harm and can be grievous. Mark foresaw that some harm might result, which satisfies the mens rea of section 20. Intent to cause grievous harm is the section 18 requirement, not the section 20 one."}, {"id": 1415, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Hargreaves Ltd has granted a lease of a registered freehold shop to Nia for exactly seven years at a market rent. The lease is made by deed, Nia has taken possession and the landlord's title is registered. Nobody has applied to register the lease. Which of the following best describes the status of the lease?", "options": {"A": "The lease is a legal estate without registration, because only leases granted for more than seven years must be registered", "B": "The lease takes effect only in equity until registered, because every lease made by deed for more than three years must be registered", "C": "The lease needs neither a deed nor registration, because a lease of seven years or less may be created informally", "D": "The lease is void until registered, because a lease of seven years or more is a registrable disposition under the Land Registration Act", "E": "The lease is a legal estate, but it binds a buyer of the landlord's title only if it is noted on the register"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The lease is a legal estate without registration, because only leases granted for more than seven years must be registered): A lease must be made by deed if it is for more than three years, and it must be registered only if it is granted for more than seven years. This lease is exactly seven years and made by deed, so it is a legal estate without registration, and Nia's possession gives it overriding status against a buyer. The suggestion that seven years triggers registration misstates the threshold. The informal lease rule applies only to leases of three years or less."}, {"id": 1416, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Sasha Lindqvist is a partner advising the board of Harrow Components Ltd on whether a planned dividend is lawful. She concludes, after reviewing the accounts, that the company lacks sufficient distributable reserves. The chief executive, who introduces about a third of the firm's corporate work, tells her to advise the board that the dividend is lawful so that the shareholders are paid before the year end. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Decline to advise and withdraw from the matter immediately, because the pressure means she can no longer act for the company", "B": "Give the board her honest advice that the dividend is unlawful, because independence requires advice free from commercial pressure", "C": "Give the advice requested but add a disclaimer saying it is based on information supplied by the chief executive", "D": "Follow the chief executive's instruction, because the company is the client and its instructions about the content of advice must be obeyed", "E": "Advise the board that the position is uncertain, because this protects the firm's relationship while avoiding an outright false statement"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Give the board her honest advice that the dividend is unlawful, because independence requires advice free from commercial pressure): Independence and integrity require Sasha to give advice that reflects her own professional judgment, however valuable the chief executive is as a source of work. Her honest advice is that the dividend is unlawful, and the board is entitled to hear it. Telling the board that the position is uncertain would mislead it, and a disclaimer cannot make an opinion she does not hold acceptable. Withdrawing straight away would leave the board without the advice it needs."}, {"id": 1417, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Rennie assigns the residue of a lease of an office suite to Tomlinson Ltd, giving full title guarantee. Unknown to Tomlinson Ltd, Rennie had allowed the rent to fall several months into arrears, so that the landlord had a right to forfeit. After completion, the landlord forfeits. Which implied covenant for title has Rennie breached?", "options": {"A": "That Rennie has the right to dispose of the lease, which is breached whenever the landlord later has a right to forfeit", "B": "That the lease is a good lease, which is breached when the rent is overdue at the date of the assignment", "C": "That Rennie will give further assurance, which is breached when the landlord takes back possession after completion", "D": "That the lease is subsisting with no breach of tenant's obligation that would make it liable to forfeiture", "E": "That the lease is free from charges and encumbrances, which includes any right of the landlord to forfeit for arrears"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (That the lease is subsisting with no breach of tenant's obligation that would make it liable to forfeiture): On an assignment of a lease with full title guarantee the Law of Property (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1994 implies a covenant that the lease is subsisting and that there is no subsisting breach that makes it liable to forfeiture. Rennie left the rent in arrears, so the landlord could forfeit, and the covenant was broken when the assignment was made. The covenant for the right to dispose concerns Rennie's capacity to assign and is not breached merely because rent is owed. Further assurance and encumbrances are separate covenants dealing with other matters."}, {"id": 1418, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Tessa is a chartered surveyor who acts without payment as one of two trustees of a family trust. The trust owns a let flat. A survey report sent to the trustees shows roof defects that any surveyor would regard as urgent, but Tessa does not arrange repairs and water damage costing £15,000 follows. Her co-trustee is a lay person with no property knowledge. The beneficiaries claim that Tessa breached her duty of care. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Tessa is liable only if the trust instrument expressly requires trustees to maintain and repair trust property", "B": "Tessa is not liable, because her lay co-trustee alone bears responsibility for decisions that need no special professional skill in a family trust", "C": "Tessa is not liable unless the beneficiaries prove that she acted in bad faith in failing to arrange the repairs", "D": "Tessa is judged as an ordinary lay trustee, because the higher standard applies only to trustees who are paid for their work", "E": "Tessa is held to the higher standard that her professional knowledge makes reasonable, even though unpaid, so she may be liable for the loss"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Tessa is held to the higher standard that her professional knowledge makes reasonable, even though unpaid, so she may be liable for the loss): Under section 1 Trustee Act 2000, the standard of care takes account of any special knowledge or experience that the trustee has, whether or not she is paid. Tessa is a surveyor, so she is expected to recognise urgent roof defects and act on them. Bad faith is not required for liability for negligence. Trustees are jointly responsible for decisions, so her co-trustee's lack of expertise does not excuse her."}, {"id": 1419, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Alessandro is a solicitor acting for a property developer, Mr Ward. A local journalist has published an article on planning concerns about Mr Ward's scheme, based on council documents. Mr Ward instructs Alessandro to send a letter demanding immediate removal of the article, threatening ruinous litigation in several jurisdictions and personal claims against the journalist's family, although the letter would not identify any false statement. Which of the following best describes how Alessandro should act?", "options": {"A": "He may send the letter if Mr Ward confirms in writing that he accepts responsibility for the threats, because informed instructions transfer the risk to the client", "B": "He should decline to send a letter in that form, because correspondence meant to intimidate rather than assert an arguable claim breaches his conduct duties", "C": "He may send the letter if it is marked without prejudice, because such correspondence cannot be relied on later as evidence of intimidation", "D": "He may send the letter if he adds that the claim is subject to a conditional fee agreement, because that shows the firm's confidence in it", "E": "He must send the letter as instructed, because a solicitor's duty is to advance the client's interests and the journalist can respond if wrongly accused"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (He should decline to send a letter in that form, because correspondence meant to intimidate rather than assert an arguable claim breaches his conduct duties): The SRA's warning notice on strategic lawsuits against public participation says that solicitors must not use their position to take unfair advantage of others or to bring claims or send letters that are intended to harass or silence rather than pursue a proper legal remedy. Threats against the journalist's family and an unspecified demand fall into that category. Labels such as without prejudice, funding arrangements or a client's acceptance of responsibility do not change the nature of the letter. Alessandro should explain this and offer to send a proportionate letter if a real claim exists."}, {"id": 1420, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Chloe owns all the shares in Wren Design Ltd, a trading company with taxable profits of £100,000 before any payment to her. The company will pay corporation tax at 25%. Chloe is deciding between an extra £10,000 bonus taxed as employment income and an extra £10,000 dividend. Ignoring National Insurance contributions and her own personal tax, how does each choice affect the company's corporation tax?", "options": {"A": "Both are deductible in full, because payments made to an owner manager are treated as costs of the trade whatever their form", "B": "Neither is deductible, because payments to an owner manager are treated as withdrawals of profit and not as costs of the trade", "C": "The bonus is deductible and cuts corporation tax by £2,500, while the dividend is paid from post tax profit and cuts it by nothing", "D": "The bonus reduces corporation tax by £10,000, because relief is given for the full amount of the payment and not at the 25% rate", "E": "The dividend reduces corporation tax by £2,500 and the bonus does not, because a bonus is an appropriation of profit"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The bonus is deductible and cuts corporation tax by £2,500, while the dividend is paid from post tax profit and cuts it by nothing): A bonus paid as employment income is a deductible cost in computing the company's profits, so a £10,000 payment reduces taxable profit by £10,000 and corporation tax at 25% by £2,500. A dividend is a distribution of profits that have already been taxed and gives no deduction. The saving is the tax on the amount deducted, not the amount itself. Real planning must also weigh employer National Insurance and Chloe's personal tax, which this question ignores."}, {"id": 1421, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Anita is a conveyancing assistant at a firm of solicitors. While reviewing a client's mortgage paperwork she comes to suspect that the deposit came from the proceeds of a benefit fraud. The firm's policy names the managing partner as its nominated officer, and Anita is worried about doing something wrong. She wants to know what she should do first to discharge her own reporting obligation. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She should tell the nominated officer promptly, which discharges her own duty, and that officer decides on any external report", "B": "She should report to the Solicitors Regulation Authority, which then decides whether the matter must be passed to the National Crime Agency", "C": "She should submit a suspicious activity report directly to the National Crime Agency, because an employee cannot satisfy the duty by reporting internally", "D": "She should wait until the transaction completes, because a report made before completion would prejudice the client's right to confidentiality", "E": "She should first ask the client to explain the source of the deposit so that her suspicion is confirmed before anything is reported"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She should tell the nominated officer promptly, which discharges her own duty, and that officer decides on any external report): An employee in the regulated sector discharges the duty to disclose by making an internal report to the nominated officer as soon as practicable. The nominated officer then considers the information and decides whether a suspicious activity report goes to the National Crime Agency. Questioning the client to confirm the suspicion risks tipping off and is not required. The SRA is not the body that receives these reports, and waiting until completion could expose the firm to an offence."}, {"id": 1422, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Priya, a solicitor, acts for Marcus, a finance director on bail and charged under section 4 of the Fraud Act 2006 with abusing his position. A detective rings Priya asking whether she knows where Marcus is staying, as officers wish to execute a search warrant. Marcus has told Priya in confidence that he is at his sister's house. He has not instructed Priya to disclose it. How should Priya respond?", "options": {"A": "Tell the officer she does not know where Marcus is, because that protects the client's confidence without disclosing anything", "B": "Refuse to speak to the officer and cease acting immediately, because the request creates a conflict between duty to the court and the client", "C": "Disclose the address, but only after warning Marcus she intends to do so, because the duty to the court prevails over client confidentiality", "D": "Tell the officer the address, because the duty not to obstruct the investigation overrides confidentiality where a warrant is sought", "E": "Decline to disclose the address, because it is confidential, while taking care not to say anything untrue or misleading"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to disclose the address, because it is confidential, while taking care not to say anything untrue or misleading): A solicitor must keep client affairs confidential unless the client consents or the law compels disclosure, and a police request for an address is not compulsion. The solicitor must also never mislead anyone, so she cannot say she does not know. The proper course is to decline to answer. No conflict arises between her duties because she can honour both by being open that she will not provide the information."}, {"id": 1423, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Sam is a director and holds 40% of the shares in a trading company. He has held them for six years. He sells all his shares for £310,000, having paid £60,000 for them. For this question, the annual exempt amount is £3,000, the rate of capital gains tax on gains qualifying for business asset disposal relief is 18%, and the rate on other gains is 24%. Sam has not used the relief before. How much capital gains tax does Sam pay?", "options": {"A": "£55,260, because the gain is calculated on the proceeds less the exempt amount, ignoring his £60,000 cost, and taxed at 18%", "B": "£59,280, because the £247,000 gain after the exempt amount is taxed at 24% since only trading assets qualify for the lower rate", "C": "£45,000, because the whole £250,000 gain is taxed at 18% and the annual exempt amount cannot be used against relieved gains", "D": "£60,000, because the whole £250,000 gain is taxed at 24% and neither the exempt amount nor the relief applies", "E": "£44,460, because the gain is £250,000, the exempt amount is deducted and the £247,000 balance is taxed at 18%"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (£44,460, because the gain is £250,000, the exempt amount is deducted and the £247,000 balance is taxed at 18%): The gain is the proceeds of £310,000 less the cost of £60,000, which is £250,000. The annual exempt amount of £3,000 is deducted to leave £247,000. Sam is an officer holding at least 5% of the shares for more than two years in a trading company, so business asset disposal relief applies and the 18% rate is used, giving £44,460. Applying the 24% rate, forgetting the exempt amount or ignoring the cost would each give a wrong figure."}, {"id": 1424, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "A scaffolding collapse injured a worker at the site of Rowan Contractors Ltd. The next day the company's HR manager interviewed the staff present and wrote up notes. In her own mind her aim was to review safety procedures and to meet internal reporting requirements, although the company feared a claim might follow. Three months later the injured worker sued, and his solicitor has asked to inspect the notes. The company's solicitors, who were not involved in the interviews, want to resist. How is the notes' status best analysed?", "options": {"A": "They are not protected by litigation privilege, because the dominant purpose of the interviews was a safety review rather than preparing for litigation", "B": "They are covered by litigation privilege, because a claim was feared when the interviews took place and the notes were created because of that fear", "C": "They are covered by litigation privilege, because any document created after an accident is protected from inspection by the injured party", "D": "They are not protected, because litigation privilege applies only to communications passing between a client and its solicitor", "E": "They are covered by legal advice privilege, because communications between an employer and its own staff about an accident are protected"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They are not protected by litigation privilege, because the dominant purpose of the interviews was a safety review rather than preparing for litigation): Litigation privilege requires litigation to be reasonably in contemplation and the dominant purpose of creating the document to be conducting that litigation. A mere fear that a claim may arise is not enough if the main aim was a safety review. Legal advice privilege protects communications with a lawyer, not interviews by an HR manager. Litigation privilege can cover non lawyer communications with third parties, so the last view is wrong in principle even though the notes here are not protected."}, {"id": 1425, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Hartley Plastics Ltd is sued in the High Court for breach of a supply contract. It files an acknowledgment of service and, within 14 days, issues a CPR Part 11 application with a witness statement arguing that a foreign jurisdiction clause removes the court's jurisdiction. Before the application is heard, the claimant's solicitors apply for default judgment, saying that 28 days from service of the particulars of claim have passed without a defence. How will the court most likely deal with the request for default judgment?", "options": {"A": "Grant it, because default judgment is available whenever a defence is late, and the defendant may later apply to set it aside", "B": "Refuse it, but only because the claimant failed to give the defendant 14 days' written notice of the request for default judgment", "C": "Refuse it, because a defendant who applies in time under Part 11 need not file a defence until that application has been determined", "D": "Grant it, because the 28 days for filing a defence ran from service of the particulars and a Part 11 application does not stop time running", "E": "Grant it, because the defendant's application shows that it accepts the court's jurisdiction and so must now plead to the claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refuse it, because a defendant who applies in time under Part 11 need not file a defence until that application has been determined): A defendant that files an acknowledgment of service and applies under Part 11 within the 14 day period is not treated as accepting jurisdiction, and its time for filing a defence is held back until the application is decided. The claimant therefore cannot show that the defendant has failed to file a defence in time, and a default judgment would be wrongly entered. The argument that the 28 days simply ran from the particulars overlooks that suspension. Making a Part 11 application is the opposite of accepting jurisdiction."}, {"id": 1426, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Leon, aged 16, and Karl, aged 30, are jointly charged with robbery, an offence triable only on indictment when committed by an adult. Both appear first before the magistrates' court. The court sends Karl to the Crown Court for trial. Leon has no previous convictions, and the court has not yet decided where he should be dealt with. Which of the following is correct about Leon?", "options": {"A": "The court may send Leon to the Crown Court only if he consents, since a youth cannot otherwise be sent", "B": "The court may send Leon to the Crown Court only if the prosecution proves that he is a persistent offender", "C": "The court must send Leon to the Crown Court, because a youth charged jointly with an adult must be tried with him", "D": "The court must transfer Leon to the youth court, because a 16 year old can be tried only there", "E": "The court may also send Leon to the Crown Court, if it considers it necessary in the interests of justice"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court may also send Leon to the Crown Court, if it considers it necessary in the interests of justice): Where a youth is charged jointly with an adult sent to the Crown Court for an indictable offence, the magistrates may send the youth too if they consider that necessary in the interests of justice. It is a discretion, not an obligation, and the test is not tied to consent or to a persistent offender finding. A youth can therefore be tried in the Crown Court alongside an adult, although it is not required."}, {"id": 1427, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Protesters block the only road into an industrial estate for three days, a public nuisance. Thompson Haulage Ltd's lorries are trapped, and it loses a contract and a load of perishable goods worth £15,000. Other motorists are delayed by about thirty minutes. Thompson wants to sue the protest organisers in public nuisance. Which of the following best describes its position?", "options": {"A": "It cannot sue, because only the Attorney General or a local authority may bring proceedings for a public nuisance", "B": "It can sue, because it has suffered particular damage that is beyond the general inconvenience felt by other road users", "C": "It can sue, but only for physical damage to its property, as pure financial loss cannot be recovered in public nuisance", "D": "It can sue, because every person who suffers delay on the road has the same right to damages as Thompson", "E": "It cannot sue, because the obstruction was not on Thompson's own land and a claimant must have an interest in affected land"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It can sue, because it has suffered particular damage that is beyond the general inconvenience felt by other road users): A private claimant may sue in public nuisance only if he suffered special damage, meaning loss that goes beyond what the public at large suffered. Thompson's lost contract and perishable cargo are particular losses of a different kind from a thirty minute delay. Public nuisance does not require an interest in land, which separates it from private nuisance, and consequential economic loss can be recovered as special damage. The Attorney General or a local authority may sue, but that does not stop a claimant with special damage from suing in its own right."}, {"id": 1428, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Owen Rees, who was recalled to prison under a statutory scheme, brought a claim in the High Court arguing that the scheme breached his Convention right to a fair hearing. The judge found that the scheme could not be read compatibly with the Convention right, despite the duty to read legislation compatibly so far as possible. The judge made a declaration of incompatibility. The Act remains on the statute book. What is the legal effect of the declaration on the Act and on the recall?", "options": {"A": "The Act ceases to have effect after six months, unless Parliament re-enacts it, because the declaration gives a period to remedy the breach", "B": "The Act is disapplied in Owen's case, because the court must give him an effective remedy by setting aside the incompatible words", "C": "The Act remains valid and enforceable against Owen, and the declaration leaves it to Parliament and ministers to decide whether to amend the law", "D": "The recall provision is quashed from the date of the declaration, because the courts may strike down legislation that breaches Convention rights", "E": "The declaration is binding on the government, which must repeal the scheme, because the court's finding is enforceable by order"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The Act remains valid and enforceable against Owen, and the declaration leaves it to Parliament and ministers to decide whether to amend the law): A declaration of incompatibility under the Human Rights Act 1998 does not affect the validity, continuing operation or enforcement of the legislation, and it is not binding on the parties to the proceedings. The sovereignty of Parliament means that the courts cannot strike down or disapply an Act, so the recall continues. The declaration signals the incompatibility to government and Parliament, who then decide whether to amend. Quashing, disapplication and time limits are not part of the scheme."}, {"id": 1429, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Bright Spark Ltd, an electrical retailer, engages Ayesha as a self-employed fitter to install products at customers' homes. She uses her own van and tools, sets her own working days, invoices Bright Spark for each job and works for two other firms. While installing a cooker in a customer's kitchen, Ayesha negligently damages the customer's worktop. Which of the following best describes whether Bright Spark is vicariously liable for the damage?", "options": {"A": "No, because vicarious liability applies only to personal injury caused by negligence and not to damage to property", "B": "No, because vicarious liability arises only where the business expressly authorised the particular careless act that caused the damage", "C": "No, because vicarious liability needs a relationship of employment or one akin to it, and Ayesha ran her own independent business", "D": "Yes, because a business that engages another person for payment to work for its customers is vicariously liable for that person's damage", "E": "Yes, because Bright Spark chose Ayesha and benefited commercially from her work, so it must answer for her careless acts"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because vicarious liability needs a relationship of employment or one akin to it, and Ayesha ran her own independent business): Vicarious liability requires a relationship of employment or one sufficiently akin to employment, assessed by how integrated the worker is in the business and how far the business controls the work. Ayesha supplied her own equipment, chose her days, billed per job and served other firms, so she was in business on her own account. Benefit to Bright Spark is not enough, because every business benefits from its contractors. Vicarious liability does extend to property damage, and express authority for the act is not required."}, {"id": 1430, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Barlow Tools Ltd issued a claim for £8,000, a specified sum, for goods sold and delivered to Trent Hardware. The claim form and particulars of claim were served with a response pack. The 14 day period for acknowledging service has expired. Trent has filed neither an acknowledgment of service nor a defence, and has not applied to strike out the claim or for summary judgment. How can Barlow obtain default judgment?", "options": {"A": "By waiting a further 14 days after the date for acknowledging service, because time for a defence must expire first", "B": "By making an application on notice supported by a witness statement, because judgment requires the judge's decision on the evidence", "C": "By writing to Trent warning of default judgment and, after seven days, asking the court office to enter it without a request", "D": "By filing a request for judgment in the prescribed form, because the claim is solely for a specified sum of money", "E": "By making an application for summary judgment, because default judgment is not available where a defendant has been served properly"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (By filing a request for judgment in the prescribed form, because the claim is solely for a specified sum of money): Where the claim is only for a specified sum of money, the claimant may obtain default judgment by filing a request in the prescribed form, without a hearing, once the defendant has failed to acknowledge service within the time allowed. If no acknowledgment is filed, the defendant's time for a defence does not need to expire first. An application is needed only in other cases, such as claims for non-money remedies. Summary judgment is a different procedure that requires the defendant to have a defence."}, {"id": 1431, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Loud amplified music from a bar nightly disturbs the flats above and beside it. Among those complaining are Una, who holds a tenancy of her flat, and her adult son Eli, who lives with her and has no tenancy or other interest in the flat. Both say they cannot sleep. The bar's operator says that only some of them may bring a claim in private nuisance. Which of the following statements is correct?", "options": {"A": "Neither can sue, because private nuisance is limited to the freehold owner, and a tenant has rights only against the landlord", "B": "Una can sue only if she holds a lease of more than seven years, because shorter tenancies give no proprietary interest", "C": "Una can sue, as she has an interest in the land affected, but Eli cannot, as he has no interest in the land", "D": "Both can sue, because private nuisance protects any person whose use and enjoyment of the home is affected by the noise", "E": "Both can sue, but only if the noise caused each of them a medical condition that a doctor has diagnosed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Una can sue, as she has an interest in the land affected, but Eli cannot, as he has no interest in the land): Private nuisance protects interests in land, so a claimant must have a right to the land affected, such as ownership or a tenancy with exclusive possession. Una holds a tenancy and so has standing. Eli has no proprietary interest, and the law does not allow a mere occupier living with a tenant to sue, even though his enjoyment of the home is affected. The freehold is not required, and a tenancy of any length qualifies, so Una does not need a long lease or a medical diagnosis."}, {"id": 1432, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Highfield Retail Ltd runs a store with a hillside car park. At the entrance a small sign reads 'Surfaces may be icy in winter'. The only pedestrian route to the store runs down a steep ramp, which was not gritted despite forecast frost, and no alternative route exists. Idris, a customer, read the sign, walked down the ramp, slipped on ice and broke his wrist. He sues under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Idris succeeds only if the sign was not visible, because a visible warning is conclusive evidence that the occupier took reasonable care", "B": "Idris fails, because by reading the sign and proceeding he freely accepted the risk, so the defence of consent applies", "C": "Idris succeeds in full, because an occupier must make premises safe, and a warning has no effect on the extent of the occupier's liability to a visitor", "D": "Idris is likely to succeed, because the warning did not enable him to be reasonably safe when no alternative route existed and gritting was possible", "E": "Idris fails, because giving a warning that a danger exists discharges the occupier's duty under the Act"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Idris is likely to succeed, because the warning did not enable him to be reasonably safe when no alternative route existed and gritting was possible): A warning discharges the occupier's duty under the 1957 Act only if, in all the circumstances, it was enough to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe. A vague winter notice at the entrance did not do that where the only way into the store was down an ungritted ramp, with no alternative and a forecast of frost. Reading the notice does not show consent, because consent requires a real choice and full acceptance of the risk. A warning is relevant to liability, so it is wrong to say it carries no weight, but here it was insufficient."}, {"id": 1433, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Oakfield Law, a firm in Reading, sends a new client, Tariq Mahmood, a client care letter confirming the fee estimate, the name of the solicitor with day to day conduct and the likely stages of his employment dispute. The letter does not mention how he can complain, and the firm has not told him orally. Which of the following best describes the firm's position?", "options": {"A": "The firm is in breach, because the client care letter must also be countersigned by Tariq before the firm acts", "B": "The firm is not in breach, because the right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman must be explained after a final response has been given", "C": "The firm is in breach if Tariq later complains and the firm fails to deal with it within eight weeks", "D": "The firm is not in breach, because the complaints procedure need be provided when a client first makes a complaint", "E": "The firm is in breach, because clients must be told in writing how to complain and of any right to go to the Legal Ombudsman"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The firm is in breach, because clients must be told in writing how to complain and of any right to go to the Legal Ombudsman): The Code requires a firm to give clients information about its complaints handling procedure and any right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman at the time of engagement, in writing. Tariq's letter covers price and the team but omits this, so the firm is already in breach. The Ombudsman must also be mentioned again when a final response is given, but that does not remove the earlier duty. There is no rule requiring the client to countersign the letter."}, {"id": 1434, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Vantage Drone Ltd, a limited company, has sued Barnsley Surveys LLP for £120,000 in the High Court. The defendant's solicitor has seen the claimant's latest accounts, which show falling income, large trade creditors and negative net assets, and thinks the claimant may not be able to pay the costs if the defendant wins. The claimant is registered in England and trades only here. What must the defendant show to obtain security for costs?", "options": {"A": "That the claimant has not paid earlier costs orders, because security is ordered to enforce costs that remain unpaid", "B": "That the claim is weak, because security for costs is available where the claimant has no real prospect of success", "C": "That the claimant is a limited company, because any defendant sued by a company may require security as of right", "D": "That the claimant has become insolvent by the date of the application, because security requires a formal insolvency event", "E": "That there is reason to believe the claimant company will be unable to pay the defendant's costs, and that an order is just"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (That there is reason to believe the claimant company will be unable to pay the defendant's costs, and that an order is just): Under CPR 25.13, where the claimant is a company, the court may order security if it is satisfied, having regard to all the circumstances, that it is just to make the order and there is reason to believe the company will be unable to pay the defendant's costs if ordered. Merely being a company, or having a weak claim, is not enough, and formal insolvency is not required. The accounts here provide evidence of the inability to pay."}, {"id": 1435, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Naomi Fraser is selling her house and has told her solicitor that a loft conversion costing £38,000 was finished eight months ago by a local builder. No application was made under the Building Regulations and no inspector was involved. The buyer's solicitor has asked for the completion certificate, and exchange is planned in three weeks. Planning permission was granted for the conversion. What is the most appropriate advice for Naomi?", "options": {"A": "Apply to the council for a regularisation certificate, because the works were carried out without approval and the council can still enforce", "B": "Rely on the planning permission, because planning approval of the conversion is accepted as evidence of compliance with the Building Regulations for the finished work", "C": "Offer indemnity insurance in place of a certificate, because the council's period for enforcement has already expired", "D": "Wait until twelve months after completion of the works, because no certificate can then be demanded by the buyer", "E": "Ask an approved inspector to issue a final certificate, because an inspector may certify work that has already been completed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply to the council for a regularisation certificate, because the works were carried out without approval and the council can still enforce): Planning permission and building regulations approval are separate controls, so the planning consent says nothing about compliance. Work done without an application can be regularised by applying to the local authority for a regularisation certificate, which involves inspection and possibly opening up the work. An approved inspector cannot be brought in afterwards, and the council's power to require alteration or removal runs for twelve months from completion, so eight months is not enough for the exposure to have lapsed. Indemnity insurance is a poor substitute while the council can still act, and delay only lets the problem harm the sale."}, {"id": 1436, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Selena, a director of Wren Ltd, diverted a contract worth £200,000 from Wren to a company she owns. The board, which Selena controls, refuses to sue her. Idris holds 8% of Wren's shares and wants to take action against Selena. He bought his shares last year, well before the diversion occurred, and Wren's articles say nothing relevant. Which of the following is the correct advice to Idris?", "options": {"A": "Idris may sue Selena in his own name, because her duty was owed to him as a member, and keep his proportionate share of any damages awarded", "B": "Idris may bring a derivative claim, but any damages are paid to him as claimant to compensate his own loss", "C": "Idris has no remedy, because the board has decided not to sue and the court cannot interfere with that decision", "D": "Idris may bring a derivative claim without court permission, because the board refuses to act and he is a member", "E": "Idris may bring a derivative claim on behalf of Wren, but he needs the court's permission and any recovery goes to the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Idris may bring a derivative claim on behalf of Wren, but he needs the court's permission and any recovery goes to the company): The wrong done was to the company, so the proper claimant is Wren. Where the board will not act, a member may bring a derivative claim under Part 11 of the Companies Act 2006. He must first obtain the court's permission, and any damages belong to the company. A personal action would fail because the loss is the company's, and the board's refusal does not shut out the claim."}, {"id": 1437, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Callum and Ruth are buying their first home in England for £400,000, completing on 1 July 2026. For this question, stamp duty land tax on residential property is charged at 0% on the first £125,000, 2% on the next £125,000 and 5% on the portion above £250,000 up to £925,000. No reliefs or surcharges apply. Their solicitor must prepare the return and calculate the tax. Which of the following is the amount of stamp duty land tax payable?", "options": {"A": "£20,000, because the 5% rate applies to the whole price once the price rises above the £250,000 threshold", "B": "£10,000, because each rate is charged only on the slice of the price that falls within its band", "C": "£7,500, because the 2% band is not charged and only the slice above £250,000 is taxed", "D": "£8,000, because the 2% rate is applied to the whole price once it exceeds the £125,000 threshold", "E": "£2,500, because only the 2% band is charged and the slice above £250,000 is exempt from the tax"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (£10,000, because each rate is charged only on the slice of the price that falls within its band): Stamp duty land tax on residential purchases is charged in slices, so each rate applies only to the part of the price within its band. The first £125,000 is taxed at nil, the next £125,000 at 2% gives £2,500, and the remaining £150,000 at 5% gives £7,500, a total of £10,000. Applying one rate to the whole price is the old slab method and overstates the tax. Leaving out either slice also produces a wrong figure."}, {"id": 1438, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Senedd passes an Act that includes a section regulating the rules for admission to a UK-wide professional register, a matter that the Government of Wales Act 2006 reserves to the UK Parliament. Rhian Evans, a Welsh practitioner, is told by the register's regulator that she must follow the new section. She asks whether she must comply. A court is asked to decide whether the section is valid. What is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The section is valid as an Act of a democratically elected legislature, and the courts cannot question its competence", "B": "The section is valid in Wales but ineffective in England, because Senedd legislation applies within Wales for reserved matters too", "C": "The section is outside the Senedd's legislative competence and is therefore not law, so Rhian need not comply", "D": "The section is invalid unless the UK Parliament later confirms it by statute, so it is suspended pending confirmation", "E": "The section is valid until the Supreme Court has quashed it, because a Senedd Act has full effect until a court strikes it down"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The section is outside the Senedd's legislative competence and is therefore not law, so Rhian need not comply): A provision of a Senedd Act that is outside legislative competence is not law, which means it has no legal force from the outset. The subject matter here is reserved to Westminster, so the section is outside competence. It does not need to be struck down before it fails to bind Rhian, although a court is the body that decides competence. Validity does not depend on democratic election or on later confirmation by Parliament."}, {"id": 1439, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Quill Orchards Ltd, a private company, filed its annual accounts with the Registrar three days after the deadline. The directors, Mary and Samir, argue that they cannot be in trouble because the accounts were completed in good time and the lateness was caused by a delay in posting the signed copy. The company is small and has paid on time in earlier years. What is the correct position on late filing?", "options": {"A": "No penalty arises for a small company, because small companies are exempt from the filing deadline if they filed on time in earlier years", "B": "The company is penalised only after the Registrar issues a warning notice and the directors then fail to file within the period given", "C": "No penalty arises, because a filing made within fourteen days of the deadline is treated as timely for private companies", "D": "The directors are personally liable for the civil penalty, but the company itself cannot be penalised because it acts through its directors", "E": "The company faces an automatic civil penalty, and each director may also commit an offence unless they show they took all reasonable steps to file on time"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The company faces an automatic civil penalty, and each director may also commit an offence unless they show they took all reasonable steps to file on time): Late filing of accounts leads to an automatic civil penalty on the company, and the amount rises with the length of delay. Separately, each person who was a director commits an offence unless they prove they took all reasonable steps to ensure filing on time. There is no grace period and no warning notice requirement. Small companies are exempt from audit, not from the deadline, and a good record elsewhere does not excuse the delay."}, {"id": 1440, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Dev suffered a psychiatric illness after a rail accident caused by the negligence of Northern Lines Ltd. Several months later, while still affected by the illness, he committed a serious assault, was convicted and was sentenced to two years' imprisonment. Dev now sues Northern Lines Ltd for the earnings he lost during his imprisonment, in addition to damages for the illness itself. Which of the following best describes the position on the claim for lost earnings?", "options": {"A": "It succeeds if his responsibility was diminished by the illness, because diminished responsibility removes the taint of illegality from the claim", "B": "It is barred, because a claimant cannot recover loss that flows from a criminal sentence imposed for his own deliberate offence", "C": "It is barred only if the offence carries a mandatory life sentence, because only the gravest crimes engage the illegality defence", "D": "It succeeds in full, because the negligence of Northern Lines Ltd was the original cause of the illness that led to the assault", "E": "It succeeds but is reduced for contributory negligence, because the assault was Dev's own act and contributed to the loss"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is barred, because a claimant cannot recover loss that flows from a criminal sentence imposed for his own deliberate offence): A claimant cannot recover damages for loss that is the consequence of a sentence lawfully imposed for his own criminal act, because the law will not let the civil courts undercut the criminal law. The negligence of Northern Lines Ltd remains a cause in fact of the illness, so the damages for the illness itself are not affected. The tempting argument that the illness diminished Dev's responsibility fails, because the loss still flows from a sentence that reflects a finding of personal responsibility. Contributory negligence is the wrong tool, as it reduces a recoverable claim rather than barring one."}, {"id": 1441, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Clarke and Dunn Solicitors receives a complaint from a client, Mrs Osei, about delay and an unexpected bill. The partner investigates and sends a letter rejecting the complaint in full and explaining the reasons. The letter does not mention the Legal Ombudsman, although the client care letter at the start of the matter did refer to the right to complain. The partner believes the earlier client care letter was enough. Which of the following best describes the firm's position?", "options": {"A": "The firm has breached its obligations, but only if Mrs Osei asks in writing for details of any external redress available to her", "B": "The firm has breached its duties, because it must tell the client in writing at the end of its process of the Legal Ombudsman and the time limit", "C": "The firm has complied, because telling the client about the Legal Ombudsman in the initial client care letter satisfies the duty for the whole matter", "D": "The firm has breached its obligations, because its letter must also offer mediation before the client can refer the matter onwards", "E": "The firm has complied, because it need mention the Legal Ombudsman only where the complaint is upheld and compensation is offered"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The firm has breached its duties, because it must tell the client in writing at the end of its process of the Legal Ombudsman and the time limit): The SRA Code requires a firm to tell clients at the time of engagement about their right to complain and to the Legal Ombudsman, and again at the end of its own complaints process. At that stage the firm must explain in writing that the client may take the matter to the Legal Ombudsman and give the time limit and contact details. The initial client care letter does not satisfy the later duty, and it does not depend on the complaint being upheld or on a request. There is no requirement to offer mediation."}, {"id": 1442, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "The directors of Lakeside Gardens Ltd, a solvent company whose two shareholders hold 60% and 40% of the shares, wish to retire and propose a members' voluntary liquidation. The company has no debts other than ordinary trade creditors who are being paid on time. A solicitor is asked to explain what is needed to start the process. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The directors must swear a declaration of solvency, made within five weeks before the resolution, that the company can pay its debts within twelve months", "B": "The directors must apply to the court, which appoints the liquidator after considering a statement of the company's assets and liabilities", "C": "The members must pass an ordinary resolution, because a members' voluntary liquidation needs only a simple majority of shareholders voting", "D": "The directors must obtain a creditors' decision approving the liquidation, because creditors must consent before any company can be wound up voluntarily by its members", "E": "The directors must swear a declaration of solvency, made within six months of the resolution, that the company can pay its debts within five years"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The directors must swear a declaration of solvency, made within five weeks before the resolution, that the company can pay its debts within twelve months): A members' voluntary liquidation is for a solvent company. The directors must make a statutory declaration that the company will be able to pay its debts, with interest, within twelve months of the start of the winding up, and it must be made within the five weeks before the resolution. The members then pass a special resolution, requiring 75%, and appoint the liquidator. Creditors do not vote, and the court plays no part, which distinguishes this route from a creditors' voluntary liquidation."}, {"id": 1443, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for Brindle Retail, which is suing Calloway Fit-Out for late completion of a shop refit. While preparing the schedule of loss, the solicitor learns from Brindle's finance director that Brindle earned £12,000 from a pop-up trader who used the empty space during the delay, although the draft schedule claims the full £40,000 of lost rent. The finance director confirms the income arose only because the space was empty and asks that it be left out. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Put forward the schedule as drafted, because the burden of proving any failure to mitigate is on the defendant and the solicitor need not volunteer it", "B": "Advise that the income reduces the recoverable loss, and refuse to put forward a schedule the solicitor knows overstates the claim", "C": "Put forward the schedule as drafted, but tell the court the figure is subject to the defendant's evidence on mitigation, as that protects the solicitor", "D": "Reduce the schedule by £12,000 without telling the client, because the duty to the court overrides the duty to act in the client's interests", "E": "Cease to act at once and report the client to the SRA, because an attempt to overstate a claim is dishonesty that must be reported immediately"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise that the income reduces the recoverable loss, and refuse to put forward a schedule the solicitor knows overstates the claim): A solicitor must not mislead the court, and cannot put forward a loss figure known to be overstated. The pop-up income came from the same space and reduces what Brindle can claim, as avoided loss. The solicitor should first advise the client and seek instructions, since the client is entitled to know the position and to correct the schedule. Altering it secretly would breach duties to the client, and stopping to act and reporting would be premature before advice has been given."}, {"id": 1444, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Thirteen year old Maya runs across a quiet road from behind a parked van to retrieve a ball, and is struck by a car driven negligently over the speed limit. The driver accepts that he was in breach of duty. He argues that Maya should have looked before running out and that her damages should be reduced for contributory negligence. Which of the following best describes how a court will assess Maya's conduct?", "options": {"A": "Her conduct is judged by her own personal maturity, so a reduction depends on how mature she was", "B": "Her conduct is irrelevant, because contributory negligence is not available against any claimant under the age of sixteen", "C": "Her damages must be reduced, because a child over twelve is old enough to share responsibility for road safety", "D": "Her conduct is judged by the standard of an ordinary child of her age, so a reduction follows only if she fell short of it", "E": "Her conduct is judged by the standard of a reasonable adult pedestrian, as the danger of the road is obvious to everyone"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Her conduct is judged by the standard of an ordinary child of her age, so a reduction follows only if she fell short of it): A child's contributory negligence is judged by the standard of an ordinary child of the same age. A court is slow to find a young child at fault for running into a road, and a reduction follows only if Maya failed to take the care expected of a child of thirteen. The adult standard does not apply, and there is no age at which a reduction is automatic. There is also no rule excluding contributory negligence for claimants under sixteen."}, {"id": 1445, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Hartley Estates is landlord of a shop let to Priya Chowdhury, who on 3 March granted an underlease of the whole premises to Colin Reeves without the consent that her lease requires. Hartley's managing agent discovered this on 20 March. On 25 March, the usual quarter day, the agent demanded the quarterly rent and banked the payment from Priya Chowdhury. Hartley now wishes to forfeit the lease for the unauthorised underletting. The lease contains a forfeiture clause. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Hartley has lost the right to forfeit, because demanding and accepting rent with knowledge of a once and for all breach waives it", "B": "Hartley has lost the right to forfeit only until the next quarter day, when it may again rely on the underletting", "C": "Hartley can forfeit, because it may reserve its rights by receiving the rent through its agent rather than directly from the tenant", "D": "Hartley can forfeit, because the underletting is a continuing breach and only acceptance of rent for the period before the breach waives the right", "E": "Hartley can forfeit, but only after serving a section 146 notice and allowing the tenant a reasonable time for the breach to be remedied"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hartley has lost the right to forfeit, because demanding and accepting rent with knowledge of a once and for all breach waives it): An unauthorised underletting is a breach that is complete once the underlease is granted, so it is a once and for all breach rather than a continuing one. A landlord who knows of the breach and then demands or accepts rent falling due afterwards affirms the lease and waives the right to forfeit for that breach for good. The agent's knowledge is the landlord's knowledge, so banking the payment through an agent does not help. A section 146 notice is a procedural step before forfeiture and cannot revive a right already waived."}, {"id": 1446, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Raj Mehta paid £50,000 to Owen Daly, who agreed to use it to buy shares on the basis of confidential price-sensitive information that Owen said he had obtained from a friend at a listed company. Owen had promised a 40% return. Both knew that dealing on that information would be a criminal offence. Owen never made the trades and kept the money. Raj sues for its return. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Raj cannot recover, because a claimant who took part in an illegal scheme is barred from any claim linked to it", "B": "Raj may recover the money, because the illegal purpose was not carried out and refusing relief would be disproportionate to the illegality", "C": "Raj may recover only if he had no knowledge that the dealing would be unlawful, because knowledge defeats a claim", "D": "Raj may recover the profit Owen promised, because the agreement is enforceable and Raj's expectation is the measure", "E": "Raj cannot recover, because the claim depends on the unlawful agreement and the court applies a strict rule against relying on it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Raj may recover the money, because the illegal purpose was not carried out and refusing relief would be disproportionate to the illegality): Under Patel v Mirza, the court asks whether denying relief would be a proportionate response to the illegality, looking at the purpose of the prohibition, other public policies and the seriousness of the conduct. Where money was paid for an unlawful purpose that was never carried out, return of the money is the likely result because withholding it would reward Owen. The old reliance test and rigid bars on claimants have been displaced. Raj does not seek to enforce the illegal agreement, and expectation damages would be unavailable."}, {"id": 1447, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Harrow Dairy and Pellman Cafés signed a two-year agreement for the supply of milk at fixed prices. The final clause stated: 'This arrangement is binding in honour only and is not subject to the jurisdiction of the courts.' Harrow Dairy stopped deliveries after one year, and Pellman sues for the extra cost of buying replacement milk elsewhere. Both are businesses and the clause was clearly drafted. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Pellman fails, because a two-year supply is too long a period to be treated as a binding commitment between businesses", "B": "Pellman succeeds, because the clause is meaningless and can be severed, leaving the rest of the agreement enforceable", "C": "Pellman succeeds, because the clause is an exclusion of liability that fails the reasonableness test under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977", "D": "Pellman fails, because the honour clause expressly rebuts the presumption that parties to a commercial agreement intend legal relations", "E": "Pellman succeeds, because commercial agreements are binding in law and the parties cannot remove the presumption by their own wording"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pellman fails, because the honour clause expressly rebuts the presumption that parties to a commercial agreement intend legal relations): Commercial agreements are presumed to be legally binding, but the presumption can be displaced by clear words. An honour clause stating that the arrangement is binding in honour only and outside the courts shows the parties did not intend legal consequences. The clause is not an exclusion of liability, so the Unfair Contract Terms Act does not apply to it. Pellman therefore has no enforceable claim."}, {"id": 1448, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Dev owed Karina £5,000, due on 1 May under a loan agreement. On 1 May he paid her £3,000 by transfer. Later that day Karina emailed him agreeing to accept the £3,000 in full settlement. Dev did nothing else in reliance on the email, and gave nothing further. Karina later sued for the remaining £2,000. Which of the following best describes the legal position at common law?", "options": {"A": "Karina can recover the £2,000, because Dev gave no fresh consideration for her promise to accept less than the sum due", "B": "Karina cannot recover the £2,000, because a creditor's written agreement to accept part payment in full settlement discharges the whole debt", "C": "Karina can recover the £2,000 only after giving Dev reasonable notice that she is withdrawing her promise to accept less", "D": "Karina cannot recover the £2,000, because Dev's reliance on her promise is presumed and presumed reliance is enough for estoppel", "E": "Karina cannot recover the £2,000, because paying on the due date is itself good consideration for a promise to forgo the balance"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Karina can recover the £2,000, because Dev gave no fresh consideration for her promise to accept less than the sum due): Under the rule in Pinnel's Case, payment of a smaller sum on the due date does not discharge a larger debt, because the debtor gives nothing extra. Karina's promise to accept less was therefore not supported by consideration. Promissory estoppel does not help Dev since he did nothing in reliance on the email. Karina can therefore claim the £2,000 balance."}, {"id": 1449, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Ellis Tanner posted a letter on Monday offering to sell his van to Fiona for £4,000, stating that Fiona could reply by post. Fiona posted a letter of acceptance on Wednesday. The letter was lost in the post and never arrived. On Friday, having heard nothing, Ellis sold the van to another buyer. Fiona says that a contract was formed on Wednesday. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "No contract was formed, because the postal rule does not apply where a letter is lost through no fault on the part of the offeree", "B": "A contract was formed on Wednesday when the acceptance was posted, so Ellis is in breach even though the letter never arrived", "C": "No contract was formed, because an acceptance takes effect only when it is actually received by the person who made the offer", "D": "A contract was formed on Wednesday only if Fiona can prove the date and time of posting by a certificate of posting", "E": "A contract was formed on Friday, when Ellis could reasonably have expected the reply and failed to enquire whether one had been sent"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A contract was formed on Wednesday when the acceptance was posted, so Ellis is in breach even though the letter never arrived): Where post is a contemplated method of reply, acceptance takes effect when the letter is posted, not when it arrives. The rule operates even if the letter is delayed or lost, because the offeror chose the risk of post by inviting a reply that way. A contract therefore existed from Wednesday, and Ellis's later sale breached it. Receipt is the general rule only for instantaneous communications, which this was not."}, {"id": 1450, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Garner Pharma Ltd's defence is due on 14 February. Its solicitor agrees with the claimant's solicitor, in writing, that Garner may have until 28 March, six weeks later, because key documents are with the client's overseas parent. The claimant's solicitor confirms by email. No directions questionnaire has been filed and no trial date exists. Garner's solicitor asks whether the agreement is effective.", "options": {"A": "The agreement is fully effective, since a limit applies only once a directions questionnaire has been filed and a trial date fixed", "B": "The agreement is effective, but Garner must file it at court within seven days to preserve the new date", "C": "The agreement is ineffective as a whole, because any extension to the time for a defence needs an order of the court", "D": "The parties may agree up to 28 days only, so for the full six weeks Garner needs a court order, which the claimant can consent to", "E": "The agreement is fully effective, because the parties may agree any extension if they confirm it in writing and the claimant consents"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The parties may agree up to 28 days only, so for the full six weeks Garner needs a court order, which the claimant can consent to): Parties may agree to extend the time for filing a defence, but by no more than 28 days from the original date. Garner's six weeks exceeds that, so the extra time needs the court's order, normally obtained by a consent application. An agreement within 28 days would need no court involvement. Wholly rejecting the agreement is wrong because the first 28 days are effective, and no filing of the agreement is required."}, {"id": 1451, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A contract between Hallam Haulage and Ingram Retail for delivery of pallets provided that for each day of late delivery Hallam would pay £200, the daily figure being Ingram's estimate of storage and staffing costs. Ingram's actual cost of late delivery was usually about £180 a day. Hallam delivered 10 days late, and Ingram claims £2,000. Hallam says the clause is a penalty. Both are commercial parties of equal bargaining power who took legal advice. Which is the best view?", "options": {"A": "The clause is enforceable only to the extent of £180 a day, because the court may reduce an excessive figure to the actual loss", "B": "The clause is a penalty, because £200 exceeds the £180 loss Ingram would usually suffer, so the clause cannot be a genuine pre-estimate", "C": "The clause is probably enforceable, because £200 a day is not out of all proportion to Ingram's legitimate interest in timely delivery", "D": "The clause is a penalty, because a daily rate of damages for delay is not allowed and Ingram must prove its actual loss", "E": "The clause is enforceable, because a clause negotiated between commercial parties with legal advice cannot be challenged as a penalty"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The clause is probably enforceable, because £200 a day is not out of all proportion to Ingram's legitimate interest in timely delivery): The question is whether the sum is out of all proportion to Ingram's legitimate interest in performance. A figure only £20 above the usual daily cost, agreed between commercial equals with advice, is far from that. Daily sums for delay are common and acceptable. A clause that is a penalty is unenforceable and is not trimmed down, while a clause between commercial parties can still be challenged, though a court will be slow to interfere."}, {"id": 1452, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Naomi Fletcher is registered proprietor of a house. Her brother, Stefan, has an equitable charge over the house securing a £30,000 loan, but nothing is entered on the register. Naomi transfers the house as a gift to her adult daughter, Kira, who is registered as proprietor. Kira paid nothing and knew nothing of the loan. Stefan remains unpaid. Which of the following best states whether Stefan's charge binds Kira?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because an equitable charge is an overriding interest under Schedule 3 and so binds a donee and a buyer alike", "B": "No, because section 29 protects every registered disposition whether it is made for value or by way of gift", "C": "Yes, but only after Stefan has registered a notice against the title, which will then date back to the gift", "D": "No, because Kira was registered as proprietor and acted without knowledge, which defeats any interest not protected by an entry", "E": "Yes, because Kira gave no valuable consideration, so section 29 does not apply and the earlier interest keeps priority"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, because Kira gave no valuable consideration, so section 29 does not apply and the earlier interest keeps priority): Section 29 of the Land Registration Act 2002 protects a registered disposition only if made for valuable consideration. A gift to Kira therefore falls outside it, and priority is governed by section 28, under which an earlier interest in registered land keeps priority over a later disposition unless a statutory exception applies. Stefan's equitable charge was created first, so it binds Kira even though unprotected. Her innocence does not matter because the protection depends on value, not good faith."}, {"id": 1453, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Fenwick Law LLP has just been authorised and will start acting on residential and commercial property sales next month. The managing partner proposes to appoint a nominated officer only when the firm first meets something suspicious, and to write the anti-money laundering policies once there are enough clients to justify the effort. Meanwhile she says the partners will simply remember their training. The compliance partner objects. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "The firm must have a written firm-wide risk assessment, written policies and procedures, and a nominated officer in place before relevant work begins", "B": "The firm may start acting once a nominated officer is appointed, because written policies are optional for firms with fewer than ten partners", "C": "The firm may defer written policies until it has at least ten clients, provided each partner completes personal training before accepting instructions", "D": "The firm needs a risk assessment only after its first suspicious activity report, because the duty is triggered by actual suspicion about a client", "E": "The firm may rely on the published sector risk assessment instead of preparing one tailored to its own clients, services and delivery channels"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm must have a written firm-wide risk assessment, written policies and procedures, and a nominated officer in place before relevant work begins): Regulation 18 of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017 requires a firm to carry out and document its own risk assessment, and regulation 19 requires written policies, controls and procedures proportionate to its size and nature. A nominated officer must also be appointed. These are preventative duties that apply from the point the firm begins relevant work, not once suspicion arises. National or sector assessments must be taken into account but cannot replace the firm's own assessment."}, {"id": 1454, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Zane holds a registered lease of a shop with eight years left, granted by Cedar Estates. They agree that Zane will take a new ten-year lease of the same shop at a higher rent, starting immediately, and Cedar Estates grants it by deed. Zane moves into the shop under the new lease. Two years later Zane breaches a repairing covenant that appears only in the old lease. Cedar Estates sues on it. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The old lease continues beside the new one, because a surrender must be made by deed to be effective", "B": "The old lease ended by surrender by operation of law when Zane accepted the new lease, so its covenants no longer bind him", "C": "The old lease ended when the new one was granted, but Zane remains liable for breaches arising after that date", "D": "The old lease merged in the freehold when the new one was granted, so the repairing covenant was released at that point", "E": "The old lease runs until the eight years expire, so Zane stays liable for the breach"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The old lease ended by surrender by operation of law when Zane accepted the new lease, so its covenants no longer bind him): A surrender by operation of law occurs when the tenant accepts a new interest that is inconsistent with the continuation of the old lease, as with a new lease of the same premises granted to the tenant by the landlord. The old lease therefore ended when Zane took the new one, and its covenants cannot be enforced for a breach two years later. Surrender need not be by deed where it arises by operation of law. Merger concerns the lease and the reversion coming into one hand, which has not occurred here."}, {"id": 1455, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Fenwick Finance Ltd lent £400,000 to Granite Print Ltd, a company run by experienced directors who took legal advice, secured by a legal charge on its factory. The charge says the company cannot repay the loan before the end of 25 years, but the lender cannot call it in earlier unless the company defaults. After ten years the company sells its business and wants to redeem the charge immediately by paying the capital and interest. Which of the following best describes whether the postponement of redemption can be enforced?", "options": {"A": "It cannot be enforced, because equity allows a mortgagor to redeem on reasonable notice whatever the terms of the charge say", "B": "It can be enforced only if the term is also registered as a restriction against the company's title at HM Land Registry", "C": "It can be enforced, because the parties are commercial bodies bargaining on equal terms and the postponement is not oppressive or unconscionable", "D": "It can be enforced, but only if the lender also holds an option to buy the factory when redemption occurs", "E": "It cannot be enforced, because any postponement of the date for redemption is a clog on the equity of redemption that equity will strike down"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It can be enforced, because the parties are commercial bodies bargaining on equal terms and the postponement is not oppressive or unconscionable): A postponement of the contractual redemption date is not automatically a clog on the equity of redemption. In Knightsbridge Estates v Byrne the court upheld a long postponement between commercial parties who were advised and bargaining at arm's length, since the term was neither oppressive nor unconscionable. The same reasoning applies here, so the company must wait. Equity's right to redeem after the legal date does not allow a mortgagor to override a valid, fair postponement."}, {"id": 1456, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Elena Voss is charged with actual bodily harm against her former partner, Ben, who lives two streets away. She is 28, has lived locally for ten years, works full time and has never failed to attend court. The prosecutor says there is a risk that she will approach Ben, who is a witness. The magistrates are considering bail. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Bail with conditions that she live at her sister's address and not contact Ben, because these address the risk of interference", "B": "Bail with a surety of £10,000, because only a financial sum can reduce a risk of witness interference", "C": "Refuse bail, because a risk of contact with a witness means substantial grounds exist and conditions cannot deal with it in this case", "D": "Bail with a condition to report to a police station daily, because reporting prevents contact with witnesses", "E": "Unconditional bail, because the court cannot impose conditions on a defendant with no history of failing to attend"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bail with conditions that she live at her sister's address and not contact Ben, because these address the risk of interference): The only identified risk is interference with a witness, and Elena's community ties, employment and record of attendance suggest she is not at risk of absconding. Conditions must be necessary and linked to the risk, so a residence condition away from Ben and a no-contact condition directly address it. Refusal is not justified when conditions can manage the risk. A surety or daily reporting is aimed at absconding and does not deal with contact with a witness, and unconditional bail ignores the risk the prosecutor raised."}, {"id": 1457, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Frank punches Lena once in the face during an argument. She suffers heavy bruising, internal bleeding in the cheek and a fractured cheekbone, but her skin is not broken. Frank is charged under section 20 Offences Against the Person Act 1861 with unlawfully and maliciously wounding. Which of the following best describes whether the wounding element is satisfied?", "options": {"A": "No, because a wound requires a break in the continuity of the whole skin, and internal bleeding alone does not suffice", "B": "Yes, because heavy bruising shows damage to the skin which is enough to amount to a wound", "C": "Yes, because a fractured cheekbone is a serious injury and any injury involving bleeding is a wound in law", "D": "No, because a wound requires a cut caused by a sharp instrument and a blow with a fist cannot cause one", "E": "Yes, because the wounding element is satisfied by any harm that is really serious, and a fracture meets that test"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because a wound requires a break in the continuity of the whole skin, and internal bleeding alone does not suffice): A wound requires a break in the continuity of the whole skin, which means both layers, and internal bleeding or bruising does not satisfy it. A fist can cause a wound if it breaks the skin, so a sharp instrument is not required. The seriousness of the injury is relevant to grievous bodily harm, which is a separate alternative limb of section 20. Here the wounding limb fails, though the fracture may well amount to grievous bodily harm."}, {"id": 1458, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Dr Priya Nair, Dr Callum Ferrie and Dr Aisha Bello plan to open a private physiotherapy clinic in leased premises. They want to share profits equally and be taxed as self-employed individuals rather than through corporation tax. Each is anxious not to be personally liable for the debts of the business or for the negligence of the others, and they are content for annual accounts to be filed publicly. Which of the following vehicles is most suitable?", "options": {"A": "A limited partnership with all three as limited partners, because each then risks only capital contributed and all three may run the clinic", "B": "An unlisted public company, because its shareholders have limited liability and its profits can be shared in whatever proportions the owners choose", "C": "A private limited company, because its shareholders have limited liability and its profits are taxed directly on the shareholders as the profits arise", "D": "A general partnership, because the partners are taxed individually and the firm itself shields each partner from the business debts owed to creditors", "E": "A limited liability partnership, because its members are taxed as partners and generally risk only their agreed contributions to the firm"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A limited liability partnership, because its members are taxed as partners and generally risk only their agreed contributions to the firm): An LLP combines corporate status and limited liability with tax transparency, so the doctors are taxed as self-employed partners on their profit shares and are not generally liable for each other's negligence. They accept public filing of accounts, which the LLP requires. A general partnership leaves each partner with unlimited liability, while a company is taxed on its own profits. A limited partnership fails because limited partners must not take part in management, and the doctors intend to run the clinic together."}, {"id": 1459, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Tanya is buying a leasehold flat with completion on 1 October. The lease requires a service charge of £1,460 for each year ending 31 December, paid in advance. The seller has already paid this year's charge in full. The contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) and the flat is sold with vacant possession. Assume a 365 day year and that the day of completion counts for the buyer. What apportionment should appear on the completion statement?", "options": {"A": "The seller is credited £360, because the buyer owes the seller for only ninety days after the month of completion has ended", "B": "The seller is credited £1,460, because the seller has paid the whole sum in advance and the buyer must reimburse all of it", "C": "The seller is credited £372, because the buyer owes for ninety-three days, counting both the day of completion and the day before", "D": "The buyer is credited £1,092, because the seller must pay the buyer for the nine months of the year that have already elapsed", "E": "The seller is credited £368, because the buyer takes over from 1 October and must repay the seller for the 92 days to 31 December"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The seller is credited £368, because the buyer takes over from 1 October and must repay the seller for the 92 days to 31 December): Outgoings are apportioned day by day from actual completion when the property is sold with vacant possession. The charge is £4 a day (£1,460 divided by 365). From 1 October to 31 December inclusive is 92 days, which is 31 plus 30 plus 31, so the buyer reimburses £368. The seller paid the whole year in advance, so the credit is to the seller. Counting ninety or ninety-three days gives the wrong figure."}, {"id": 1460, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Under a trust created by Hettie's will, a fund of £120,000 is held 'for Faye for life and then for Gus if he survives Faye'. There is no gift over if Gus does not survive Faye. Gus died last year leaving two children. Faye has now died. Hettie's will left her residuary estate to her nephew Ivor. The trustees ask who is entitled to the fund. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The fund passes to Faye's estate, because she was entitled to the income and the capital for her life", "B": "The fund passes to Gus's children, because they are the next of kin of the intended beneficiary", "C": "The fund is bona vacantia and passes to the Crown, because the trust instrument leaves no beneficiary", "D": "The fund returns to Hettie's estate on a resulting trust, because Gus's interest failed and no one else takes capital", "E": "The fund passes to Gus's estate, because his interest was vested subject to being divested if he did not survive Faye"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The fund returns to Hettie's estate on a resulting trust, because Gus's interest failed and no one else takes capital): Gus's gift depended on his surviving Faye, and he died first, so his interest never vested. The will gave nobody else the capital, so the beneficial interest in the fund was not exhausted. It therefore results back to the settlor's estate, which here means it passes under Hettie's residuary gift to Ivor. Faye had only a life interest, and Gus's children take nothing, because the gift was to Gus personally on a condition he failed."}, {"id": 1461, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Orchard Studios Ltd licenses a unit to Pelham Print Ltd under an agreement stating that any variation must be in writing and signed by both parties. In January the parties' managers orally agreed to cut the monthly fee from £4,000 to £3,000 for six months because Pelham's customer base had shrunk. Pelham paid the lower sum for six months in reliance on that promise, and Orchard accepted each payment without complaint. In July Orchard demanded the £6,000 shortfall. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The clause prevents an effective oral variation, though Orchard may be estopped from claiming the shortfall because Pelham relied on a clear promise", "B": "The clause is void as an attempt to oust the parties' freedom to vary their contract, so the oral agreement is binding", "C": "The oral agreement is unenforceable and Orchard may recover the shortfall in full, because estoppel cannot arise where a contract has a written variation clause", "D": "The oral agreement is binding, because Pelham provided consideration by continuing to pay the fee in a reduced amount", "E": "The oral agreement binds Orchard as a variation, because the parties may waive a formality by agreement or conduct"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The clause prevents an effective oral variation, though Orchard may be estopped from claiming the shortfall because Pelham relied on a clear promise): A no oral modification clause is effective, so an oral agreement does not operate as a contractual variation. Equity can still intervene where the promisor made a clear representation, the other party relied on it and it would be inequitable to go back on it. Pelham paid the reduced fee for six months in reliance on the promise, which supports estoppel against the shortfall for that period. The argument that the clause is void is wrong, and consideration cannot overcome the clause."}, {"id": 1462, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Isaac Brennan has owned an unregistered freehold cottage for 15 years but lost the title deeds in a flood and cannot produce evidence of the earlier chain of ownership. He sells the cottage to Rosa Delgado for £240,000, and Rosa applies for first registration. HM Land Registry is satisfied of Isaac's ownership but cannot approve the title absolutely because of the missing deeds. Which class of title is Rosa most likely to be given, and what is its effect?", "options": {"A": "Absolute title, which guarantees that the proprietor holds the estate free of every adverse right existing when the title was first registered", "B": "Qualified title, which excludes a specific defect shown by the deeds from the guarantee given on first registration", "C": "No class of title, because first registration is refused until the original title deeds are located and produced", "D": "Possessory title, which leaves the registered proprietor subject to any adverse rights that existed at first registration, such as those of an earlier owner", "E": "Absolute title subject to a note on the register, which leaves the missing deeds as a matter between buyer and seller"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Possessory title, which leaves the registered proprietor subject to any adverse rights that existed at first registration, such as those of an earlier owner): Possessory title is given where the applicant cannot prove title from the deeds but is shown to be in possession. Under section 11(6) of the Land Registration Act 2002 it leaves the proprietor subject to estates, rights or interests adverse to the proprietor's title that exist at first registration. Absolute title would be the usual grant if the deeds were available, and qualified title is reserved for a specific identified defect. Registration is not refused simply because deeds are lost."}, {"id": 1463, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Rowe died intestate leaving no spouse and three children: Abel, aged 21, Bea, aged 19, and Cal, aged 15. His net estate is £150,000. The children's mother is alive but she and Mr Rowe had divorced. Eleven months later, Cal died unmarried and without children, before reaching 18. Abel and Bea ask the administrators what happens to the share that was held for Cal.", "options": {"A": "Cal's interest never vested, because he did not reach 18 or marry, so his share passes to Abel and Bea", "B": "Cal's share is paid to the children's mother, as his only surviving parent, because the interest vested at death", "C": "Cal's share is held until the date he would have reached 18, and is then divided among Abel and Bea", "D": "Cal's share is treated as vested at Mr Rowe's death and forms part of Cal's estate, passing to Abel and Bea by intestacy", "E": "Cal's share passes to the Crown as bona vacantia, because he had no issue of his own"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cal's interest never vested, because he did not reach 18 or marry, so his share passes to Abel and Bea): Under the statutory trusts a child's interest vests only on reaching 18 or marrying or forming a civil partnership earlier. Cal did neither, so his interest failed and the share passes to the other children of the intestate. It does not form part of Cal's estate, and so his mother does not benefit. There is no need to wait until the age he would have reached, and the Crown takes only where no one entitled survives."}, {"id": 1464, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Officials from a government department enter the premises of Brightwell Publishing in the early morning, search the filing cabinets and remove a set of editorial records. They say the search is justified because the records may embarrass the department and because the public interest favours preventing publication. No statute or warrant authorises the search. Brightwell asks whether the entry was lawful. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The entry was lawful, because a government department may rely on the public interest as a general justification for searching private premises", "B": "The entry was unlawful, because a public official may interfere with private property only where the law gives authority to do so", "C": "The entry was lawful provided the department can show that it caused no lasting damage to the premises or to the records", "D": "The entry was lawful, because the prerogative gives the Crown inherent power to search for documents that threaten the department's reputation", "E": "The entry was lawful, because the Minister in charge of the department has approved it and ministerial approval supplies the authority needed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The entry was unlawful, because a public official may interfere with private property only where the law gives authority to do so): Under the rule of law, as stated in Entick v Carrington, the state and its officials need positive legal authority for any interference with a person's property. The department identified no statute, warrant or common law power, so the public interest argument cannot supply one. The prerogative has not been extended to searches, and the Minister's approval cannot create authority that the law does not give. Lack of damage does not cure a trespass."}, {"id": 1465, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Odette owns her house, registered at HM Land Registry in her sole name. She signs a short document, witnessed by a neighbour, which says: 'I declare that I hold my house on trust for my two children in equal shares.' She does not execute any transfer of the house, and the register is not changed. Her will leaves everything to a charity. Odette later argues that no trust of the house was created. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "No trust was created, because the children gave no consideration and equity will not help volunteers to enforce a trust", "B": "A trust was created only when the register was changed to show the children as owners of the house", "C": "No trust was created, because a deed of transfer registered at HM Land Registry is needed to vest legal title in trustees", "D": "A trust was created but is void, because the document should have been executed as a deed rather than merely witnessed", "E": "A valid trust arose, because Odette declared herself trustee in signed writing, so no transfer of legal title was needed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A valid trust arose, because Odette declared herself trustee in signed writing, so no transfer of legal title was needed): The owner of land can create a trust by declaring herself trustee, and the only formality is signed writing under section 53(1)(b) of the Law of Property Act 1925. Odette's signed declaration satisfies that. The trust is constituted at once because she already holds the legal title, so there is nothing further to transfer. The volunteer rule matters only where a transfer to someone else is incomplete, and the register shows only the legal title, which stays with Odette."}, {"id": 1466, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A waste company burns scrap plastic daily on open ground, sending thick smoke across an estate of about 300 homes. Ilse owns and lives in one of these homes, has asthma and was admitted to hospital after a particularly heavy smoke day. She seeks advice on a civil claim to recover compensation for her illness and her hospital costs. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "She should sue in private nuisance only, because she owns her home and public nuisance can be pursued only by the Attorney General", "B": "She may claim for her illness in public nuisance, as personal injury can be special damage, while private nuisance protects only land interests", "C": "She should sue in negligence only, because nuisance does not apply to emissions of smoke that are not noise or vibration", "D": "She can recover for the illness in both torts equally, because the smoke affected her land and her health together", "E": "She has no claim, because the smoke affected about 300 homes and so she cannot show damage particular to her"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She may claim for her illness in public nuisance, as personal injury can be special damage, while private nuisance protects only land interests): In public nuisance, a claimant who suffers special damage may recover for it, and that can include personal injury, so Ilse's hospital admission is damage particular to her. Private nuisance, by contrast, protects the use and enjoyment of land and does not compensate for personal injury. The number of affected homes does not prevent special damage where one resident suffers a distinct injury. Smoke is a classic nuisance emission, so the nuisance torts are open, and public nuisance is not limited to claims by the Attorney General."}, {"id": 1467, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Sam and Tina, friends who are not in a relationship, buy a flat for £100,000. The flat is registered in Tina's sole name. Sam paid £70,000 and Tina paid £30,000 towards the price. There was no discussion about ownership and no declaration of trust. The flat has since risen in value. Sam claims an interest in it. Which of the following is the most likely position?", "options": {"A": "Sam has a share fixed by what the court regards as fair after considering the whole course of dealing between them", "B": "Sam is owed repayment of £70,000 as a debt, because the legal owner holds the flat free of any trust", "C": "Sam has a half share under a constructive trust, because shared contributions to a purchase show a common intention to share equally", "D": "Sam has a 70 per cent share under a presumed resulting trust, because he contributed that proportion of the purchase price", "E": "Sam has no interest, because the flat is in Tina's sole name and no written declaration of trust exists"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sam has a 70 per cent share under a presumed resulting trust, because he contributed that proportion of the purchase price): Where someone contributes to the price of property bought in another's name, a resulting trust arises in proportion to the contribution. The friends are not family members, so there is no presumption of a gift, and nothing in the facts rebuts the presumption. A resulting trust needs no written formalities. The fairness approach in Jones v Kernott concerns shares in a family home where intention is inferred, not a straightforward contribution case."}, {"id": 1468, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Caldwell, aged 62, is diagnosed with mesothelioma. Over a thirty year career he worked for three different employers, each of which negligently exposed him to asbestos dust for several years. Medical science cannot establish which period of exposure caused the single asbestos fibre that began the disease, and all three employers are solvent. He sues all three. One employer argues that Mr Caldwell cannot satisfy the but-for test against it. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "No employer is liable, because the claimant cannot show on the balance of probabilities which employer's breach caused the disease", "B": "Only the final employer is liable, because the last exposure completed the sequence of events leading to the disease", "C": "Each employer is liable for materially increasing the risk, and the Compensation Act 2006 makes liability joint and several", "D": "Each employer is liable only for a share matching its own period of exposure, because the law apportions liability by time", "E": "Only the employer responsible for the longest period of exposure is liable, as it most probably caused the harm"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Each employer is liable for materially increasing the risk, and the Compensation Act 2006 makes liability joint and several): Mesothelioma cases fall within a special exception to the but-for test: where several employers breached their duty and the claimant cannot prove which exposure caused the single initiating fibre, each is liable for materially increasing the risk. Statute has since made that liability joint and several, so the claimant can recover in full from any solvent employer. The ordinary rule would defeat the claim, which is why the tempting answer fails. Liability by length of exposure was the earlier proportionate approach and has been displaced."}, {"id": 1469, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Naomi Fairweather wants to name her new private company 'Royal Tay Boat Hire Ltd'. A search of the Companies House index shows that no existing company has the same name or one very similar, and the name ends with the required suffix. Naomi has no connection with any royal body. The solicitor is asked whether the name can simply be registered with the application. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The name contains a sensitive word, so approval must be obtained from the relevant authority before it can be registered", "B": "The name can be registered, but the company must then pass a special resolution confirming the name within one month", "C": "The name can be registered immediately, because it is not the same as any name on the index and carries the correct suffix", "D": "The name cannot be registered, because words suggesting a royal connection are prohibited outright in company names", "E": "The name needs approval only if Naomi intends to trade in a regulated sector such as banking or insurance"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The name contains a sensitive word, so approval must be obtained from the relevant authority before it can be registered): A company name must not be the same as one on the index, and a name containing a sensitive word such as Royal needs approval before registration. The name here is distinctive and has the correct suffix, but Royal implies a royal connection, so approval is required. Such words are not banned, since approval can be given where justified. No later resolution is needed once the name is properly approved and registered."}, {"id": 1470, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ibrahim Rahman died on 12 February 2026. His executors, Zainab and Dev, must pay £62,000 of Inheritance Tax before they can obtain a grant of representation. Neither executor has personal funds to advance. The estate consists of a house worth £480,000, which they intend to keep until the spring, and £95,000 held in Ibrahim's building society account. The building society confirms it operates the usual arrangements with HMRC. Which of the following is the most appropriate way for the executors to fund the tax promptly?", "options": {"A": "Obtain the grant first and then withdraw the money from the account, because the building society will not release funds without it", "B": "Ask HMRC to accept a delay in payment until the grant is issued, since interest runs only after probate has been obtained", "C": "Ask the building society to pay HMRC directly from Ibrahim's account under the direct payment scheme, quoting the payment reference", "D": "Sell the house at once to fund the tax, because tax cannot be paid from the deceased's bank accounts before a grant is issued", "E": "Withdraw the money by cheque signed by the executors under the authority in the will, because executors can deal with accounts before a grant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask the building society to pay HMRC directly from Ibrahim's account under the direct payment scheme, quoting the payment reference): The usual route is the direct payment scheme: the executors give the institution the payment reference and it pays HMRC from the deceased's account before any grant exists. The grant cannot be obtained until the tax due on delivery of the account has been paid, so waiting for the grant reverses the order. Building societies will not release funds to executors without the grant, and interest runs from the end of the sixth month after death, not from probate. Selling the house is unnecessary because the account alone covers the tax."}, {"id": 1471, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Ferndale Foods Ltd is the claimant in a multi track claim that has been listed for a three day trial starting on a Monday. The solicitors have agreed the documents to be included, and the claimant's paralegal asks the supervising solicitor when the trial bundle must reach the court and who is responsible for it. The solicitor remembers that the practice direction fixes a window rather than a single date. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court office must compile the bundle from documents already filed, and the parties need only agree the index", "B": "The defendant must file the bundle no later than 21 days before the trial, together with the completed pre trial checklist", "C": "The claimant must file the bundle no more than seven and no less than three days before the trial starts", "D": "The claimant must hand the bundle to the judge in court on the first morning, as the hearing begins", "E": "The claimant must file the bundle within 14 days of the pre trial checklist being filed, so that the judge has time to read it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claimant must file the bundle no more than seven and no less than three days before the trial starts): Under the practice direction supporting CPR Part 39, the trial bundle is the claimant's responsibility and must be filed not more than seven days nor less than three days before the start of the trial. The window ensures the judge can read the papers before the hearing without the bundle being compiled too early. The defendant does not file it, and the court office does not assemble it."}, {"id": 1472, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Evelyn died holding 40% of the shares in Granary Properties Ltd, an unquoted company she had owned for ten years. The company's main activity is letting a portfolio of residential flats and collecting the rent, although it employs a part time manager to deal with tenants and repairs. Her executor asks whether business property relief applies to the shares. What is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "Relief is not available, because the company's business consists wholly or mainly of making or holding investments such as let property", "B": "Relief is available, because ten years of ownership satisfies the conditions and the part time manager shows the company is a trading business", "C": "Relief is available, because a 40% holding is a substantial interest and the relief turns on the size of the deceased's shareholding", "D": "Relief is not available, because the relief covers only sole trader and partnership interests and does not extend to shares in a company", "E": "Relief is available at a reduced rate, because a company with investment business attracts a lower percentage of relief on its shares"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Relief is not available, because the company's business consists wholly or mainly of making or holding investments such as let property): Business property relief is denied for a business that consists wholly or mainly of making or holding investments, and a company whose main activity is letting residential property falls within that exclusion. Employing a part time manager does not turn a lettings business into a trading business, and ten years of ownership satisfies only the ownership condition. Company shares can qualify where the company trades, so the exclusion is based on the nature of the business and not on the form of the holding."}, {"id": 1473, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Ms Dale was a member of an occupational pension scheme. The scheme rules give the trustees a discretion to pay a lump sum death benefit to any of her dependants. Before she died she completed a nomination form naming her partner Zoe. Her will leaves all her property to her sister. Her executors ask who will receive the death benefit. What is the position?", "options": {"A": "The sister takes the lump sum, because the benefit forms part of the estate and passes under the will", "B": "The lump sum is divided between Zoe and the sister, because a nomination and a will carry equal weight", "C": "Zoe takes the lump sum, because the nomination form binds the trustees to follow her wishes", "D": "The lump sum is paid to the executors, who must distribute it under the intestacy rules", "E": "The trustees decide, taking the nomination into account, and the lump sum does not pass under the will"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The trustees decide, taking the nomination into account, and the lump sum does not pass under the will): A lump sum under a discretionary scheme is paid at the trustees' discretion and does not form part of the estate, so the will has no effect on it. The nomination is an expression of wish that the trustees should consider but need not follow. It is therefore wrong to say Zoe is entitled or that the benefit passes to the sister or the executors."}, {"id": 1474, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Marcus was injured when the car he was driving was hit by a negligent lorry driver. In conference, he admits to his solicitor that he was disqualified from driving at the time, which he had not previously mentioned. The defendant has not raised the point. Marcus instructs the solicitor to include in the statement of truth on the particulars of claim an assertion that he held a valid licence. The garage that repaired his car has also been paid by Marcus in cash. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Include the assertion but add a note to the court file that its accuracy rests on the client's word alone", "B": "Decline to include the assertion because it would be untrue, and advise how the admission may affect the claim through illegality", "C": "Cease acting at once without further advice, because a client who proposes a false statement cannot be properly advised", "D": "Include the assertion as instructed, because the solicitor acts on the client's instructions and the defendant must prove any illegality", "E": "Report Marcus to the police immediately, because knowledge of an offence overrides the duty of confidentiality owed to a client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline to include the assertion because it would be untrue, and advise how the admission may affect the claim through illegality): A solicitor must not mislead the court or others, and a statement of truth that the solicitor knows to be false would do exactly that. The proper course is to refuse the instruction and explain to Marcus the consequences, including that his disqualification may engage the illegality defence against the claim. Following instructions cannot justify putting forward a statement known to be untrue, and the defendant's silence does not change that. Reporting to the police would breach confidentiality without justification, and ending the retainer without advice would fail Marcus as a client."}, {"id": 1475, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Gwen and Hari Patel hold a house as trustees of land for themselves and for Hari's mother, Meera, who has a beneficial interest through her contribution to the purchase price. The trustees sell the house to a buyer for market value, and the purchase money is paid to Gwen and Hari together. The buyer knew that Meera lived in the house. Which of the following best explains the effect of the sale on Meera's beneficial interest?", "options": {"A": "Her interest is extinguished because the buyer paid full value and had no notice of her interest when completing", "B": "Her interest is overreached and shifts to the sale proceeds, because the buyer paid the money to two trustees together", "C": "Her interest binds the buyer unless she has registered a notice against the title before completion of the sale", "D": "Her interest binds the buyer, because a beneficial interest under a trust is a proprietary right in the land that survives a sale", "E": "Her interest is overreached if she consented in writing to the sale, because beneficiaries must approve any disposition of trust land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her interest is overreached and shifts to the sale proceeds, because the buyer paid the money to two trustees together): Under sections 2 and 27 of the Law of Property Act 1925, a beneficial interest under a trust of land is overreached when a purchaser pays the capital money to at least two trustees or a trust corporation. The beneficiary's interest then attaches to the proceeds, not the land, so the buyer takes free of it. Meera's consent is not required for overreaching, and registration of a notice is not the mechanism. The buyer's knowledge of her occupation is irrelevant once overreaching has occurred."}, {"id": 1476, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Aziz, Beth and Callum are partners in a landscaping firm without a written partnership agreement. Aziz and Beth are unhappy that Callum has been absent for much of the year and want to expel him. They have voted two to one in favour of expulsion. Callum says that they cannot do it. The firm's customers are happy with the service. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "They may expel him by giving reasonable notice, because his absence is a breach of his duty to work in the firm", "B": "They may expel him immediately by written notice, because persistent absence is a repudiatory breach by Callum", "C": "They may expel him, because ordinary differences are decided by a majority and the vote was two to one", "D": "They cannot expel him by majority vote, because no power to expel exists unless the partnership agreement expressly provides one", "E": "They may expel him only with the consent of the court, because every expulsion needs a court order"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They cannot expel him by majority vote, because no power to expel exists unless the partnership agreement expressly provides one): A majority of partners cannot expel another partner unless the agreement expressly gives that power. Expulsion is not an ordinary difference connected with the business, so a simple majority vote is not enough. Callum's absence may give grounds for the court to dissolve the firm, but the other partners cannot expel him alone. A court order is not needed where the agreement contains an express power."}, {"id": 1477, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "The Fenwick family trust has three trustees who must decide whether to sell a painting that forms part of the trust fund. The trust instrument is silent on how trustees reach decisions. Two trustees vote to sell the painting, but the third objects strongly and refuses to sign the contract. The two trustees ask a solicitor whether they can proceed with the sale. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "They can proceed if the objecting trustee is a lay trustee, since a professional trustee's view takes precedence", "B": "They cannot proceed, because the trustees of a private trust must act unanimously unless the trust instrument provides otherwise", "C": "They can proceed, because a majority of the trustees binds the others on decisions affecting the trust property", "D": "They can proceed if a majority of the beneficiaries approve the sale, because beneficiaries can overrule an objecting trustee", "E": "They can proceed if the objecting trustee retires at once, since retirement cures the disagreement for the sale"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (They cannot proceed, because the trustees of a private trust must act unanimously unless the trust instrument provides otherwise): The trustees of a private trust must act unanimously unless the instrument allows majority decisions. The objection of one trustee therefore prevents the others from selling, and the dissentient cannot be outvoted. Charitable trusts follow a majority rule, but this is not a charity. Beneficiary approval does not replace the trustees' joint decision, and professional status gives no casting vote."}, {"id": 1478, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A county council uses a byelaw to ban a local campaigner, Nia Roberts, from handing out leaflets criticising its planning policy outside its offices. The byelaw is clear and published, and the council says it protects public order and the free movement of visitors. Nia says the ban breaches her right to freedom of expression under article 10. Her solicitor must explain how the court will assess the interference. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Article 10 is limited, so the ban is lawful only if it was ordered by a court after a hearing on the facts", "B": "Article 10 is qualified, so the ban is lawful if it is prescribed by law and a majority of residents support it", "C": "Article 10 is absolute, so any interference by a public authority with expression is unlawful whatever the aim or the circumstances", "D": "Article 10 is qualified, so the ban is lawful if the council acted in good faith and followed its own published byelaw", "E": "Article 10 is qualified, so the ban is lawful only if it is prescribed by law, pursues a legitimate aim and is proportionate"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Article 10 is qualified, so the ban is lawful only if it is prescribed by law, pursues a legitimate aim and is proportionate): Article 10 is a qualified right, so interference is permitted if it is prescribed by law, pursues one of the legitimate aims in article 10(2), such as preventing disorder, and is necessary in a democratic society, which means proportionate to that aim. A clear byelaw satisfies the first requirement, but the council still has to justify the ban on proportionality grounds. Good faith or popular support does not replace that inquiry, and no court order is required."}, {"id": 1479, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Jarvis made a will in 2018 leaving his house to his wife Joan and appointing her sole executor, with the residue going to his son Kit. They divorced in 2023, and the final order was made on 1 June 2023. Mr Jarvis did not make a new will and died in 2025. The will contains no provision dealing with divorce. What is the effect of the divorce on the will?", "options": {"A": "The whole will is revoked by the divorce, so his estate passes under the intestacy rules", "B": "Joan is treated as having died on the date of the final order, so the gift and her appointment as executor fail", "C": "The gift to Joan stays valid, but her appointment as executor lapses because the law treats the roles differently", "D": "The gift to Joan stays valid, because a will is affected by divorce only if the testator confirms this in writing", "E": "The gift to Joan fails, but she remains executor, because divorce affects benefits and not the office"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Joan is treated as having died on the date of the final order, so the gift and her appointment as executor fail): Section 18A of the Wills Act 1837 provides that on a final order of divorce, any gift to the former spouse and any appointment of the former spouse as executor fail, and the former spouse is treated as having died on the date of the order. The rest of the will remains valid, so Kit still takes the residue. No confirmation by the testator is needed, and the divorce does not revoke the will as a whole."}, {"id": 1480, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Harrow Interiors Ltd is defending a claim for £75,000 for alleged delay on a refit. The particulars of claim refer to 'various communications' from which the claimant says Harrow accepted responsibility, but do not identify them. Harrow's solicitor wants to know which communications are relied on and has drafted an application for the court to order the claimant to provide further information. What should the solicitor do first?", "options": {"A": "Ask the court for a case management conference, where the judge will invite the claimant to clarify its pleading", "B": "Issue the application at once, since the court can order further information without a prior request, and a refusal to answer is not needed first", "C": "Serve a written request for the information on the claimant, stating a date for the reply, before applying to the court", "D": "Seek permission to amend the defence to plead the communications as unknown, so that the claimant must particularise them", "E": "Serve a notice to admit facts on the claimant, which will force it to identify each communication relied on"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serve a written request for the information on the claimant, stating a date for the reply, before applying to the court): A party who wants clarification of another party's case should first serve a written request under Part 18 giving a reasonable time for a reply. The court expects that step before an application for an order, which is a remedy where the request is refused or ignored. A notice to admit facts seeks admissions, not information, and amending Harrow's defence does not oblige the claimant to say more. Waiting for a case management conference wastes time that a simple request could save."}, {"id": 1481, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Mills died intestate leaving his wife Hana and one child, Isla, aged 9. His estate is worth £700,000, and Hana wishes to take out letters of administration alone. She says that as the surviving spouse she has priority and that Isla is too young to be involved. His brother has also offered to help but has no entitlement. Can Hana take the grant alone?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because a surviving spouse has priority and may take the grant alone whatever the age of the children", "B": "Yes, but only after Isla reaches 18, because a minor's interest delays all grants", "C": "No, because Isla has a minority interest in the estate, so the grant must go to at least two individuals or a trust corporation", "D": "Yes, because the court appoints a guardian for Isla, and the guardian then replaces the need for a second administrator", "E": "No, because the grant must go to Hana and the brother, as the nearest adult relatives"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because Isla has a minority interest in the estate, so the grant must go to at least two individuals or a trust corporation): Where a minority or life interest arises, the grant must go to at least two individuals or a trust corporation, unless the court orders otherwise. Isla takes half of the residue on the statutory trusts, so her interest is a minority interest and Hana needs a co-administrator. The brother has no entitlement but may be proposed with the court's agreement, and a grant is not delayed until Isla is 18."}, {"id": 1482, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Marek and Dilys Evans were married for forty years. Marek died with a will leaving his estate of £600,000 to his children from an earlier marriage and nothing to Dilys, who is seventy and has a small pension. Dilys brings a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975. Her solicitor explains that the standard of provision the court applies depends on the category of applicant. Which of the following correctly states the standard applying to Dilys?", "options": {"A": "The court asks only whether the will makes reasonable provision for her maintenance, which is the standard applying to every category of applicant", "B": "The court asks whether the testator acted unreasonably in making his will, which is a test of his conduct and not of the result", "C": "The court asks whether the will leaves her enough to meet her basic living needs, which is a test of bare subsistence", "D": "The court asks whether the will makes reasonable financial provision in all the circumstances, whether or not it is needed for her maintenance", "E": "The court asks whether she would have received half of the estate on divorce, applying the sharing principle as a fixed rule"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court asks whether the will makes reasonable financial provision in all the circumstances, whether or not it is needed for her maintenance): A surviving spouse or civil partner is judged by the wider standard of reasonable financial provision in all the circumstances, which is not limited to maintenance, whereas other applicants must show a failure to provide reasonable maintenance. Dilys can therefore rely on the higher standard. Divorce principles are only one factor, and the test looks at the result of the will, not at the testator's conduct."}, {"id": 1483, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Hana and Joel instruct a solicitor, Gareth Lindqvist, to draft a partnership agreement for their new landscaping business. Both clients attended the first meeting. Afterwards Hana telephones privately and says she intends to leave within a year, so she wants a generous exit payment clause, and asks Gareth not to tell Joel. Joel has said nothing about leaving and has asked for terms that assume a long partnership. What should Gareth do?", "options": {"A": "Keep Hana's plan confidential and draft balanced terms, because the duty of confidentiality to Hana overrides any duty to disclose to Joel", "B": "Stop acting for Hana and continue for Joel, since Joel's instructions were given earlier and Hana's plan is her own private matter", "C": "Explain to Hana that the plan is material to Joel and cannot be kept from him, and stop acting for both clients if she refuses disclosure", "D": "Continue for both and treat the information as confidential to Hana alone, provided she is warned in writing that Joel may later complain", "E": "Draft the exit clause as Hana wants and advise Joel in general terms to take independent advice, without explaining the reason for the advice"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain to Hana that the plan is material to Joel and cannot be kept from him, and stop acting for both clients if she refuses disclosure): A solicitor acting for joint clients in a matter where their interests are aligned must not withhold from one client information material to the retainer that is held for the other. Hana's intention to leave soon bears directly on the terms Joel is negotiating, so Gareth must ask her to agree to disclosure. If she refuses he cannot continue for both, because he would hold information he cannot share and would risk misleading Joel. Giving Joel a vague hint or acting for only one of them while holding Hana's secret does not resolve the conflict."}, {"id": 1484, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Mohan is buying a freehold house for £310,000 with a mortgage advance of £230,000. His lender's instructions state that buildings insurance must be in place from exchange of contracts. The draft contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition), and the seller, Ruth, has said she will keep her own policy running until completion. Mohan has not arranged cover and wants to exchange on Friday. His solicitor also acts for the lender. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Confirm to the lender that cover is in place under Ruth's policy, because the contract term makes the seller responsible for insuring the house", "B": "Rely on Ruth's policy, because the seller keeps the risk until completion and the lender will accept the seller's cover as sufficient", "C": "Advise Mohan to insure in his own name from exchange, because the seller has no duty to insure and the lender requires cover from that date", "D": "Arrange cover to start on completion, because the buyer's insurable interest begins when legal title passes and not before then", "E": "Exchange and tell Mohan to insure before completion, because the contract allows the buyer to arrange cover at any time before that date"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise Mohan to insure in his own name from exchange, because the seller has no duty to insure and the lender requires cover from that date): The standard conditions do not oblige the seller to insure for the buyer's benefit, and Ruth's policy protects her, not Mohan or the lender. The lender's instructions require cover from exchange, and the solicitor acts for the lender too, so the instruction must be followed and the lender must not be given a false impression. Mohan has an equitable interest from exchange and a real insurable interest, so cover from that date is proper. Waiting until completion would breach the lender's instructions and leave Mohan exposed if the seller's cover lapsed."}, {"id": 1485, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Hannah receives a text message from her former partner, Eliot, who lives forty miles away. It reads: 'I will find you next month and break your jaw.' Hannah is very frightened and stays with friends for a week. Eliot does not travel to her home. He is charged with common assault. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "He is guilty of assault, because causing a victim to fear force at a definite future date is enough where the threat is made directly to her", "B": "He is guilty of battery, because the message caused Hannah to suffer psychological harm through his threat", "C": "He is not guilty, because an assault cannot be committed by words alone and requires a physical gesture", "D": "He is guilty of assault, because words alone can amount to an assault and Hannah was caused to fear unlawful violence", "E": "He is not guilty of assault, because Hannah did not apprehend immediate unlawful force when she read the message"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (He is not guilty of assault, because Hannah did not apprehend immediate unlawful force when she read the message): Assault requires the victim to apprehend immediate unlawful force, and the defendant must intend or be reckless as to that apprehension. Words can be an assault, but this threat concerned force next month, which is not immediate. Battery needs an application of force, which did not occur. The conduct may be an offence under harassment or threats legislation, but it is not common assault."}, {"id": 1486, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Vale's will gives a legacy of £15,000 to his brother Hugh and the residue to a cancer charity. Hugh died five days before Mr Vale, leaving two children. The will has no gift over of Hugh's legacy and no survival provision. Hugh's children say they should take their father's legacy, and the charity says the legacy falls into residue. Who is right?", "options": {"A": "The charity, because the legacy lapses as Hugh is not the testator's issue, so section 33 cannot save the gift", "B": "Hugh's children, because the court will imply a substitution where the testator was close to the beneficiary and would have wanted the family to benefit", "C": "Hugh's estate, because the legacy vested in him when the will was signed and passes under his will", "D": "Hugh's children, because section 33 passes a gift to the children of any beneficiary who predeceases", "E": "The testator's next of kin, because a lapsed legacy falls into intestacy rather than residue"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The charity, because the legacy lapses as Hugh is not the testator's issue, so section 33 cannot save the gift): Section 33 applies only to gifts to the testator's own issue, such as a child or grandchild. A brother is not issue, so his legacy lapses when he predeceases and no substitution occurs. A lapsed pecuniary legacy falls into residue, which here goes to the charity. The testator's closeness to Hugh does not allow the court to imply a gift over."}, {"id": 1487, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Tess sees smoke from her neighbour's garden shed, which stands next to her own wooden greenhouse. She honestly believes the fire will reach her greenhouse within minutes. She smashes her neighbour's locked side gate so that she can run a hose through, causing £150 of damage. The fire was in fact small and would have burned out without spreading. The neighbour prosecutes. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "She may rely on lawful excuse, but only if she first asked the neighbour for permission, because consent must be sought where practicable", "B": "She cannot rely on lawful excuse, because her belief that the property was in immediate danger must be objectively justified on the facts", "C": "She may rely on lawful excuse, because an honest belief in immediate need to protect property and reasonable means suffices even if mistaken", "D": "She may rely on lawful excuse, because a person may damage any property to protect her own land and the extent of the damage is irrelevant", "E": "She cannot rely on lawful excuse, because the excuse of protecting property cannot justify damage to the property of another person"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She may rely on lawful excuse, because an honest belief in immediate need to protect property and reasonable means suffices even if mistaken): Section 5(2)(b) provides a lawful excuse where the defendant damaged property to protect her own or another's property, believing it was in immediate need of protection and that the means adopted were reasonable. Section 5(3) makes the honest belief the test, so a mistake about the fire's likely spread does not remove the excuse. The damage may be to another person's property. The means must be reasonable in the circumstances, so it is not a licence for unlimited damage."}, {"id": 1488, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Okoro & Lane solicitors hold £8,000 in client account for the estate of Mr Bell. The executors instruct the firm to pay a £10,000 pecuniary legacy to Mr Bell's niece today because she needs the money for a house purchase. Further estate funds of £30,000 are expected from a share sale in five days. The firm's client account also holds £60,000 for other clients. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action for the firm?", "options": {"A": "Pay £10,000 now from the client account, because the estate will shortly receive funds and a temporary debit balance is acceptable", "B": "Pay £10,000 now using other clients' balances and repay from the share sale proceeds, because client money in one ledger can cover another", "C": "Pay £10,000 now because the client account holds far more than that in total, and the rules require only that the whole account stays in credit", "D": "Not pay the legacy until the firm holds at least £10,000 for the estate, because payments cannot exceed the funds held for that client", "E": "Pay £10,000 from the client account if the executors sign a written indemnity for any shortfall, because their consent protects the firm"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Not pay the legacy until the firm holds at least £10,000 for the estate, because payments cannot exceed the funds held for that client): A firm must not pay out of client account more than it holds for that client, so the estate's ledger must not go into debit. The money held for other clients cannot be used, and neither the executors' consent nor an indemnity changes this. The test is applied client by client, not to the total in the account. The legacy should wait until enough cleared funds are held, or the firm should use its own money if it chooses to advance the sum."}, {"id": 1489, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Greenwood Law LLP, a firm in Cardiff, is appointing officers under the SRA Standards and Regulations. The partners ask what the role of its Compliance Officer for Legal Practice (COLP) involves, and one partner suggests that the COLP will take personal responsibility for running the client account day to day. Which of the following best describes the COLP's role?", "options": {"A": "Reporting to the Law Society the conduct of individual solicitors, and having no duty to report to the SRA", "B": "Ensuring the firm complies with the terms of its authorisation and SRA regulatory arrangements, and reporting any serious breach to the SRA", "C": "Ensuring the firm complies with the SRA Accounts Rules, which means running the client account and reporting any accounts breach", "D": "Acting as the firm's senior partner and taking decisions on behalf of the managers about client matters", "E": "Supervising the work of every fee earner and signing off the advice given on each client matter"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ensuring the firm complies with the terms of its authorisation and SRA regulatory arrangements, and reporting any serious breach to the SRA): The COLP is responsible for ensuring the firm complies with the terms and conditions of its authorisation and with SRA rules, and for reporting serious breaches to the SRA. Compliance with the Accounts Rules is the separate responsibility of the COFA, and neither officer runs the client account personally. The COLP does not take management decisions on client matters or supervise each fee earner. The duty to report is owed to the SRA, not the Law Society."}, {"id": 1490, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Nadia Chowdhury acts for a landlord in a county court claim. In a disclosure exercise her client tells her to omit from the list a damaging email that falls squarely within the order for standard disclosure, saying that the other side will not find out. Nadia worries that following his instruction serves his best interests but conflicts with her duty to the court. Which of the following correctly states how the SRA Principles resolve the conflict?", "options": {"A": "The client's best interests prevail, because the solicitor's duty to the client is the foundation of the retainer and the primary duty", "B": "The Principles carry equal weight, so Nadia may choose which to follow provided she records her reasons on the file", "C": "The Principles are applied in the order in which they are listed, so honesty prevails and the duty to the client is ranked last", "D": "The Principles protecting the rule of law and the administration of justice prevail over the duty to act in the client's interests", "E": "Nadia may follow the instruction if the client signs a written statement accepting responsibility for the omission"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Principles protecting the rule of law and the administration of justice prevail over the duty to act in the client's interests): Where the Principles conflict, those that safeguard the wider public interest, particularly the rule of law and the proper administration of justice, take precedence over the duty to a client. Omitting a disclosable damaging document would mislead the court and undermine that public interest, so Nadia cannot follow the instruction. Client responsibility statements do not change this. The Principles are not ranked by their listed order, and she should explain the position and cease acting if he persists."}, {"id": 1491, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Kellaway Foods Ltd obtained a default judgment for £22,000 against Wragg Bakeries Ltd, properly entered after Wragg failed to file an acknowledgment of service or a defence. Wragg's solicitor explains that the claim papers were misdirected within its office. Wragg applies to set aside the judgment five days after learning of it. Its draft defence shows that the contract was never signed by Wragg and that the goods were returned. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The court must set the judgment aside, because a judgment can be set aside as of right whenever a defence is attached", "B": "The court may set aside the judgment if Kellaway agrees, because Kellaway holds a vested right under the judgment", "C": "The court may set the judgment aside, because Wragg has shown a real prospect of defending and applied promptly", "D": "The court will refuse unless Wragg pays the judgment sum into court first, because security is a condition of setting aside", "E": "The court will refuse, because an administrative mistake in the defendant's office is not an acceptable reason for the failure to respond"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The court may set the judgment aside, because Wragg has shown a real prospect of defending and applied promptly): Where default judgment was properly entered, the court has a discretion to set it aside if the defendant has a real prospect of successfully defending the claim or there is some other good reason. The court also looks at how promptly the defendant applied. Wragg applied within days and its draft defence is substantial, so the court is likely to exercise its discretion in its favour. Attaching a defence does not give a right to set aside, and the claimant's consent and payment into court are not requirements."}, {"id": 1492, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Fay died intestate, survived by his wife Anya and an adult daughter Beth. His estate includes a house in his sole name and savings. Beth, who lives nearby, says she has dealt with the funeral and wants to apply for letters of administration without involving her mother, who is elderly but fully capable. Who has priority to take the grant?", "options": {"A": "Anya and Beth must apply together, because a spouse and a child have equal priority under the rules", "B": "Beth, because the person who arranged the funeral and administered the assets has priority", "C": "Anya, as surviving spouse, ranks first, and Beth may apply only if Anya renounces or does not take the grant", "D": "Whoever applies first, because the registry grants administration to the first applicant who is a beneficiary", "E": "Beth, because issue are entitled to the grant before a surviving spouse where the estate exceeds the statutory legacy"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anya, as surviving spouse, ranks first, and Beth may apply only if Anya renounces or does not take the grant): On an intestacy the order of priority under the Non-Contentious Probate Rules places the surviving spouse first, then the children. Beth can take a grant only if Anya renounces or fails to apply after being given notice. Arranging the funeral confers no priority. The size of the estate does not alter the order."}, {"id": 1493, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Rhys Pugh is prosecuted under a statute that bans 'cruelty to a captive animal'. The Act's explanatory notes and long title show it was passed to improve welfare for animals kept in captivity of any kind. Rhys kept a stray pigeon in a small box for a week without food. His lawyer argues a pigeon found in the street is not captive in the sense Parliament intended. The wording of the Act is open to either reading. Which approach is a modern court most likely to adopt?", "options": {"A": "Adopt the meaning that best furthers the Act's evident purpose of animal welfare, using the long title and explanatory notes as aids", "B": "Adopt the narrowest meaning of captive, because penal statutes must be read strictly and the court cannot widen an offence beyond its clear words", "C": "Leave it to the jury, because the meaning of ordinary English words is for them and not for the judge", "D": "Apply the ordinary dictionary meaning of captive and acquit, because the court cannot use extrinsic material when the words are open", "E": "Decide the case by the golden rule, since the literal result is absurd, and modify captive to include any animal under control"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Adopt the meaning that best furthers the Act's evident purpose of animal welfare, using the long title and explanatory notes as aids): Modern courts adopt a purposive approach, asking what the statute was designed to achieve and preferring the meaning that advances that purpose where the words are open to more than one reading. The long title and explanatory notes are aids that show the aim of improving animal welfare. The golden rule is for absurdity, which is not the problem here, and a strict reading of penal statutes does not override a clear purpose. Rhys's pigeon would therefore fall within the offence."}, {"id": 1494, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Fairbank Tools agreed to sell 200 specialist drills to Corrie Engineering for £40,000, delivery on 1 June, and failed to deliver. The market price for equivalent drills on 1 June was £46,000. On that day Corrie bought equivalent drills elsewhere for £46,000. Prices rose to £47,000 a week later. The drills were readily available throughout and Corrie acted reasonably. Which of the following is the likely measure of Corrie's damages?", "options": {"A": "£6,000, being the difference between the contract price and the market price on the date delivery should have been made", "B": "Nominal damages only, because Corrie obtained replacement goods on the delivery date and so suffered no loss recoverable at law", "C": "£46,000, being the full cost of the replacement drills, because Corrie must be put in the position of having bought from Fairbank", "D": "£40,000, being the contract price, because Corrie is entitled to be returned to the position it was in before it contracted", "E": "£7,000, being the difference between the contract price and the market price a week after the date fixed for delivery"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (£6,000, being the difference between the contract price and the market price on the date delivery should have been made): Where a seller fails to deliver and an available market exists, the buyer's damages are the difference between the contract price and the market price at the time delivery should have been made. That puts Corrie in the position it would have been in had Fairbank performed, so £46,000 less £40,000 gives £6,000. Later price movements do not alter the measure, and the full replacement cost ignores the £40,000 Corrie did not have to pay. Corrie did suffer loss, so damages are more than nominal."}, {"id": 1495, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Mistral Garage agreed to service a lorry for Corbett Haulage by Monday, after Corbett's manager told Mistral that the lorry was the only one available for a contract starting on Tuesday. Mistral returned the lorry on Friday of the following week with the service unfinished. Corbett lost the contract, on which its net profit would have been £1,200, and lost £700 of ordinary hire income for the idle days. Which of the following is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "£700 only, because the profit on the specific contract was a special loss and special losses are not recoverable in a contract claim", "B": "£700 only, because a loss from a particular contract is recoverable only if the defendant expressly agreed to accept responsibility for it", "C": "£5,700, being the £5,000 contract price and the £700 ordinary loss, because the whole contract value is recoverable where knowledge exists", "D": "£1,900, because the £700 is a natural loss and the £1,200 was within Mistral's contemplation through the information it was given", "E": "£1,200 only, because the defendant's special knowledge replaces the ordinary loss rule and ordinary income is then not recoverable"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (£1,900, because the £700 is a natural loss and the £1,200 was within Mistral's contemplation through the information it was given): Both limbs of the remoteness rule are in play. The idle hire income is a loss arising naturally from the delay. The profit on the Tuesday contract falls within the second limb, since Mistral was told that the lorry was needed for it. No express acceptance of the risk is needed beyond that knowledge. Damages are measured by net profit and not gross contract revenue, so both heads together give £1,900."}, {"id": 1496, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Rahul retired from the partnership Fenwick and Co on 1 June. The firm's customers were not told. In March, before he retired, the firm bought stock worth £18,000 on credit from Greene Supplies, which has not been paid. The remaining partners cannot pay. Greene has dealt with the firm for years and knew Rahul as a partner. Rahul says his retirement ended his liability. Which statement is correct about the March debt?", "options": {"A": "Rahul is liable only if Greene was not given notice of his retirement before the debt was incurred", "B": "Rahul is liable only for part of the debt, in proportion to his profit share in the firm", "C": "Rahul is liable only for debts incurred after 1 June to customers who knew him as a partner and were not told", "D": "Rahul is not liable, because retirement ends liability for all debts of the firm that are unpaid at the date he retires from it", "E": "Rahul remains liable, because he was a partner when the debt arose and only Greene's agreement can release him"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Rahul remains liable, because he was a partner when the debt arose and only Greene's agreement can release him): A partner who retires remains liable for debts incurred while he was a partner, and is discharged only by agreement with the creditor, such as a novation. Retirement alone does not release him, and the liability for the firm's contract debts is not limited to a profit share. Notice to customers matters for debts incurred after retirement, not before it. The March debt therefore remains his responsibility."}, {"id": 1497, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Alex Mercer died owning all the shares in Mercer Lettings Ltd, an unquoted company he had held for ten years. The company's business consists of buying residential properties and letting them to tenants, with no other activities, and Alex took a salary as a director. His executors, assuming the shares will attract business relief, plan to claim it in full. The shares are worth £900,000. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Business relief is available, because Alex worked as a director and so was actively involved in the business of the company", "B": "Business relief is not available, because the company's business consists mainly of holding investments, which includes letting property, despite long ownership", "C": "Business relief is available on the part of the value attributable to the properties, because rental activity counts as trading for that part", "D": "Business relief is not available, because relief applies only to shares in companies quoted on a recognised exchange or the Alternative Investment Market", "E": "Business relief is available, because the shares are in an unquoted company that Alex owned for more than two years before death"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Business relief is not available, because the company's business consists mainly of holding investments, which includes letting property, despite long ownership): Business relief is denied where a business consists wholly or mainly of making or holding investments, and letting residential property is treated as an investment business. The two year ownership test is met but is not enough. Alex's work as a director does not change the nature of the company's activity. Shares in unquoted trading companies do qualify, so the unquoted status is not the obstacle."}, {"id": 1498, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Lucia Fernandez died in June 2024. Her executors, Callum and Bethan, delivered an Inheritance Tax account valuing her house at £400,000 and paid the tax due. After redecorating, they sold the house in January 2026 on the open market for £355,000. The estate has been administered apart from the final distribution, and the executors have kept the sale documents. They ask whether any of the Inheritance Tax already paid can be recovered. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "No claim can be made, because the value fixed at death is final and later falls in the market price do not alter it", "B": "A claim can be made, but the relief takes the form of a capital loss set against the executors' gains and not a reduction in the tax", "C": "A claim can be made, but only by the beneficiaries, because the relief belongs to those who finally receive the sale proceeds", "D": "A claim can be made, because land sold by the personal representatives within four years of death below probate value is revalued at the sale price", "E": "A claim can be made, but only for land sold within twelve months of death, so a sale in January 2026 is too late"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A claim can be made, because land sold by the personal representatives within four years of death below probate value is revalued at the sale price): Where personal representatives sell land within four years of death for less than its probate value, they can claim loss on sale relief and the sale price is substituted for the value at death, reducing the Inheritance Tax. Here the sale was about nineteen months after death and the fall is well above the small de minimis threshold. The one year period applies to quoted shares, not land. The claim is made by the personal representatives who sold, and it reduces Inheritance Tax rather than creating a capital loss."}, {"id": 1499, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "In March 2026 a client, Rosa Delgado, writes to Pembroke Law LLP alleging that negligent advice given in 2021 caused her loss. The firm changed insurers on 1 October 2024, and both the earlier policy and the current policy meet the SRA's minimum terms. The firm renewed with the same insurer on 1 October 2025. Which policy responds to Rosa's claim?", "options": {"A": "The policy running in 2021 when the advice was given, because the insurer on risk at the time of the negligence must pay", "B": "The policy running when Rosa discovered her loss, because liability arises from the date the damage becomes known to the client", "C": "The policy running when Rosa first instructed the firm, because that policy covered the whole retainer from start to finish", "D": "None of them, because a claim made more than three years after the advice falls outside qualifying cover", "E": "The policy running when the claim is first made against the firm, because qualifying insurance is claims-made"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The policy running when the claim is first made against the firm, because qualifying insurance is claims-made): Qualifying insurance under the SRA minimum terms operates on a claims-made basis, so the relevant policy is the one in force when the claim is first made. Rosa's letter arrived in March 2026, which falls within the policy year renewed on 1 October 2025. The date of the negligent advice, the date of instruction and the date of discovery do not decide which policy responds. The minimum terms contain no three year cut off for claims."}, {"id": 1500, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Your client Dev is selling the bottom part of his garden to his neighbour Karen, who has chosen not to instruct a solicitor. Dev's own house drains across that plot. Dev tells you to say nothing about the drain and to leave any reservation out of the transfer, because he thinks he can argue later that a right is implied. You are drafting the transfer. Which of the following is the most appropriate course?", "options": {"A": "Refuse to conceal the drain, and advise Dev that the right should be expressly reserved because you must not take unfair advantage of an unrepresented buyer", "B": "Follow the instruction, because Dev is the client and an implied right is likely to arise in his favour, so no disclosure is owed to Karen", "C": "Reserve the right in the transfer and also advise Karen on its effect, so that both parties are protected by one firm", "D": "Follow the instruction but ask Karen to sign a note confirming she was told she could take independent legal advice before completing the transfer", "E": "Stop acting for Dev and tell Karen about his plan, because the duty to avoid misleading a third party overrides client confidentiality"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refuse to conceal the drain, and advise Dev that the right should be expressly reserved because you must not take unfair advantage of an unrepresented buyer): The SRA Code prohibits taking unfair advantage of third parties and requires that you do not mislead anyone, including an unrepresented buyer. The proper step is to refuse to hide the drain and to advise Dev to reserve the right expressly, which also serves his own interest. Following the instruction, even with a signed note, would still conceal a known fact. Revealing Dev's plan would breach confidentiality, and advising Karen as well would create a conflict because the parties are on opposite sides of the transaction."}, {"id": 1501, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Aldous Grain Ltd agreed to sell 500 tonnes of wheat lying in a named silo to Brandt Bakeries Ltd for £110,000. Unknown to both parties, a fire had destroyed the wheat the previous night, before the contract was made. The contract does not deal with this risk. Brandt has paid a £10,000 deposit, and Aldous says it is entitled to keep it and to sue for the price. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The contract is voidable at Brandt's option, because the mistake was shared and equity gives relief on terms", "B": "The contract is valid but frustrated by the fire, so Aldous may keep the deposit to cover its costs of preparing delivery", "C": "Aldous is in breach of contract for failing to deliver, so Brandt may recover damages for loss of its bargain", "D": "The contract is valid and Brandt must pay, because risk passes to the buyer once a price has been fixed for specific goods in a named silo", "E": "The contract is void under section 6 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, because the specific goods had perished without the seller's knowledge"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The contract is void under section 6 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, because the specific goods had perished without the seller's knowledge): Under section 6 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979, where there is a contract for specific goods that, unknown to the seller, have perished at the time of contract, the contract is void. Aldous cannot sue for the price, and the deposit must be returned because it was paid for no consideration. Risk rules apply only where goods exist, and frustration deals with events after contract formation. Aldous is not in breach because there was nothing to deliver."}, {"id": 1502, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Greg is the sole registered proprietor of a house bought three years ago. His partner Hana paid £50,000 of the purchase price on the understanding that she would own a share, and she has lived in the house since the purchase. Greg borrowed £120,000 from Orion Bank on a charge of the house, which the bank registered. The bank did not inspect the property or ask about other occupiers, and Greg has defaulted. The bank seeks possession. Which of the following best describes whether Hana's interest binds the bank?", "options": {"A": "It does not bind the bank, because a beneficial interest under a trust cannot be an overriding interest", "B": "It binds the bank only if the bank knew about her contribution when it made the loan", "C": "It does not bind the bank, because the interest was overreached when the loan money was paid to Greg as the registered proprietor", "D": "It binds the bank as an overriding interest, because she was in actual occupation and the loan money was not paid to two trustees", "E": "It does not bind the bank, because Hana's interest was not entered on the register before the bank's charge was registered against Greg's title"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It binds the bank as an overriding interest, because she was in actual occupation and the loan money was not paid to two trustees): A beneficial interest under a trust held by a person in actual occupation is an overriding interest under Schedule 3 paragraph 2 of the Land Registration Act 2002, unless an exception applies. Hana was in occupation and the bank made no inquiry or inspection. Overreaching requires the capital money to be paid to at least two trustees, but Greg was the only registered proprietor, so no overreaching occurred. Her interest therefore binds the bank, whether or not it knew of her contribution."}, {"id": 1503, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Harbour Foods advertised in a trade newsletter: 'We will pay £2,000 to any supplier who delivers 500 kg of organic oats to our depot by 30 June.' Supplier Ravi Menon loaded the oats and was halfway to the depot on 27 June when Harbour Foods emailed all suppliers withdrawing the offer. Ravi read the email but carried on and delivered on 29 June. Harbour Foods refuses to pay, and the advertisement did not say how any withdrawal would be notified. Which of the following is the best advice for Ravi?", "options": {"A": "Ravi has a good claim, because a withdrawal by email is ineffective unless the advertisement itself prescribed that method of withdrawal", "B": "Ravi has a good claim, because once he began performance the offer could no longer be withdrawn, provided he completed delivery by the deadline", "C": "Ravi has no claim, because acceptance of a unilateral offer requires notice to Harbour Foods before performance begins, and he gave none", "D": "Ravi has a claim only for the reasonable value of the oats delivered, because a withdrawn offer cannot support a contract for the advertised sum", "E": "Ravi has no claim, because an offer can be withdrawn at any time before acceptance and he knew of the email before he delivered"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ravi has a good claim, because once he began performance the offer could no longer be withdrawn, provided he completed delivery by the deadline): A unilateral offer is accepted by performing the act requested, and the offeror cannot withdraw once the offeree has begun to perform, because an implied promise not to revoke arises. Ravi started the journey before the email was sent, so the offer stayed open for as long as he completed the delivery on time. He did so on 29 June. The rule that offers are revocable before acceptance does not defeat him, since performance had already started."}, {"id": 1504, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Fenwick Rowe solicitors act for the executors of Mrs Ola Stern, who died last month. The grant has been issued, and the bank has sent the firm a cheque for £42,000 representing the balance of Mrs Stern's account, to be held for the executors pending distribution. The firm is not itself a personal representative. The firm's cashier asks whether the cheque can be banked in the firm's office account while the executors decide how to distribute the estate. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "It must be paid into the executors' personal bank accounts at once, because a firm that is not a personal representative cannot hold estate money", "B": "It must be paid into the firm's client account without delay, because it is client money held for the executors and not the firm's own funds", "C": "It can be paid into the office account until the executors give distribution instructions, because estate funds are the firm's money until then", "D": "It can be held in the office account if the firm keeps a separate ledger record of it, since the ledger distinguishes it from fee income", "E": "It can be paid into the client account only after the executors give written consent, because the firm acts on instructions in handling funds"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It must be paid into the firm's client account without delay, because it is client money held for the executors and not the firm's own funds): Money received by the firm for its clients, including estate money held for executors, is client money and must be paid promptly into a client account. It cannot be placed in the office account, whatever record is kept, since that mixes it with the firm's own funds. No consent from the executors is required before banking it. The firm may hold the money for them even though it is not a personal representative."}, {"id": 1505, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "In 2024 the Lins bought a new house on an estate built next to a motocross track that had operated on Sundays for fifteen years. The track holds no special statutory authority. The noise is substantial and interferes with the Lins' enjoyment of the garden. The track's owner says the Lins came to the nuisance and that the noise has been made for years. Which of the following best describes the likely effect of those arguments?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails, because the track existed first and a later arrival cannot complain of an existing activity", "B": "The claim fails, because noise can amount to nuisance only where it causes physical damage to the claimants' property", "C": "The claim succeeds in part, because coming to the nuisance halves the damages as a partial defence", "D": "Neither succeeds, because moving in near a nuisance is no defence, and twenty years of actionable nuisance have not elapsed", "E": "The claim fails, because fifteen years of noise gave the track a prescriptive right against the owners of surrounding land"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Neither succeeds, because moving in near a nuisance is no defence, and twenty years of actionable nuisance have not elapsed): Coming to a nuisance is no defence, because a claimant is entitled to the ordinary use of his land whenever he acquired it. A prescriptive right to commit a nuisance arises only after twenty years of the activity amounting to an actionable nuisance, and time runs from when the claimant's land was affected, so the Lins' use of the house from 2024 means the period has not run. Fifteen years of operation is not enough in any event. Noise can be a nuisance without physical damage, and there is no partial reduction for arriving later."}, {"id": 1506, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Hollis Estates Ltd's brochure for a farm said the land comprised 12 acres of grazing, when in truth it was 8. Tom Verrall, a buyer, took the brochure but did not read it. He told his agent he would rely on his own inspection and the agent's measurements, and he agreed to buy for £450,000 after walking the boundary himself. His solicitor had also advised him to commission a survey, which he declined. He later learned of the shortfall and sued Hollis for misrepresentation. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails, because Tom did not read or know of the brochure statement and so was not induced by it", "B": "The claim fails, because Tom could have discovered the truth by commissioning a survey and so cannot complain", "C": "The claim fails, because statements in a brochure are advertising puffs and not statements of fact", "D": "The claim succeeds, because the statement was untrue and material to a buyer of agricultural land", "E": "The claim succeeds, because Hollis was under a duty to verify the acreage before publishing the brochure"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The claim fails, because Tom did not read or know of the brochure statement and so was not induced by it): A misrepresentation is actionable only if it induced the contract, and a representee who did not read or know of the statement cannot have relied on it. Tom bought on the strength of his own inspection and his agent's measurements. The failure to commission a survey is not the reason the claim fails, since a representee need not check the truth of what he is told. The acreage figure is a precise statement of fact, not puff, so the answer turns on inducement alone."}, {"id": 1507, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Under Kofi Boateng's will, 2,000 shares in a quoted company are left as a specific legacy to his niece, Abena, and the residue goes to his wife. The shares were valued at £70,000 for probate and are worth £90,000 when the executors transfer them to Abena. Kofi's original cost was £15,000. The executors are concerned that the transfer will give rise to a Capital Gains Tax charge on the estate and ask the solicitor what the position is. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "The executors dispose of the shares at £90,000, but the gain is exempt because assets passing to a legatee under a will are outside the charge", "B": "The executors are treated as disposing of the shares at £90,000, so a gain of £20,000 arises on the estate when Abena takes them", "C": "The transfer is not a disposal, and Abena acquires the shares at their £90,000 value on the date she receives them", "D": "The transfer is not a disposal, but Abena acquires the shares at Kofi's original cost of £15,000 and takes on the earlier gain", "E": "The transfer is not a disposal by the executors, and Abena acquires the shares at the probate value of £70,000 for her own later gains"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The transfer is not a disposal by the executors, and Abena acquires the shares at the probate value of £70,000 for her own later gains): When personal representatives transfer an asset to a legatee under the will, there is no disposal for Capital Gains Tax, so no charge arises on the estate. The legatee is treated as acquiring the asset at the personal representatives' acquisition value, which is the probate value. Abena's later gain will therefore be measured from £70,000 and not from Kofi's cost or from the value on transfer."}, {"id": 1508, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Karim, a sales manager, enters on his expenses form that he attended a client meeting on 3 March, knowing he did not. He intends to repay the money within the month and believes his employer would not mind. At trial for fraud by false representation, Karim says he did not think he was being dishonest. What approach should the jury take to dishonesty?", "options": {"A": "It must acquit, because an intention to repay the money before any loss is suffered means the conduct cannot be dishonest", "B": "It must acquit if Karim genuinely believed he was acting honestly, because dishonesty depends solely on the defendant's own standards", "C": "It must apply the standards of the business sector in which Karim works, since the employer's own practice defines dishonesty", "D": "It must establish Karim's actual belief about the facts, then decide whether his conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people", "E": "It must convict only if Karim also realised that ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest, as the second limb of Ghosh requires"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It must establish Karim's actual belief about the facts, then decide whether his conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people): Under Ivey v Genting, followed in criminal cases in R v Barton, the tribunal first finds the defendant's actual knowledge or belief about the facts. It then asks whether the conduct was dishonest by the objective standards of ordinary decent people, without requiring that the defendant realised those standards. The old requirement that Karim must have appreciated how others would view his conduct no longer applies. An intention to repay is a relevant fact, but it does not itself make conduct honest."}, {"id": 1509, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Hugh Sanderson contracted in writing to buy an unregistered freehold field from Marian Doyle for £150,000. Hugh did not register the contract, and before completion Marian sold the field to Tariq Nasser, who bought for £160,000, took a conveyance, and had been told about Hugh's contract by a neighbour. Hugh now seeks to enforce his contract against Tariq. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Hugh can enforce it, because the contract is not registrable and so the doctrine of notice decides the matter", "B": "Hugh can enforce it, because Tariq knew of the contract from the neighbour and so is not a bona fide purchaser", "C": "Hugh cannot enforce it, because an unregistered Class C(iv) charge is void against a buyer for value despite notice", "D": "Hugh cannot enforce it, but only because Tariq paid more than the contract price and so gave full value", "E": "Hugh can enforce it, because an estate contract is an equitable interest and binds all buyers who are not overreaching trustees"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hugh cannot enforce it, because an unregistered Class C(iv) charge is void against a buyer for value despite notice): An estate contract is registrable as a Class C(iv) land charge under the Land Charges Act 1972. If it is not registered, section 4(6) makes it void against a purchaser of the legal estate for money or money's worth. The statutory rule is mechanical, so Tariq's knowledge from the neighbour is irrelevant, and the doctrine of notice does not apply to registrable interests. The price matters only to the extent that real value must be given. Hugh's remedy lies against Marian."}, {"id": 1510, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Mira Das is arrested for assault after a fight in a bar. The police disclose CCTV footage showing her striking the complainant, and photographs of his injuries. Mira tells her solicitor that she hit him only after he grabbed her by the throat and says she will rely on self-defence at trial. She has no previous convictions. The custody officer says an interview will begin shortly. What is the best advice for the solicitor to give?", "options": {"A": "Give a written statement only and refuse questions, because a statement cannot lead to any inference under the 1994 Act", "B": "Give no comment, because the right to silence means that nothing said at interview can later be used against her", "C": "Give no comment, because no inference can be drawn from silence before she has been charged with an offence", "D": "Answer questions and explain the self-defence account, because strong disclosed evidence and a defence she will rely on make silence damaging", "E": "Answer only questions about the injuries, because she need not mention self-defence until the prosecution case is fully served on the defence after charge"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Answer questions and explain the self-defence account, because strong disclosed evidence and a defence she will rely on make silence damaging): The prosecution evidence is strong and Mira has a clear defence of self-defence which she intends to run at trial. If she stays silent and later relies on that defence, the court may draw an adverse inference under section 34 of the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994 for failing to mention it. Giving her account at interview also gives the police a chance to reconsider the case. A written statement does not remove the risk, because facts not stated in it can still attract an inference."}, {"id": 1511, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Hartley Metals Ltd, which has owned its works for twenty years, asks Stefan, an experienced registered gas engineer, to inspect and repair a furnace flue. The company knows the flue is old but gives no specific warning. While working in the flue, Stefan is overcome by carbon monoxide that escapes from a crack, the very sort of risk that arises in repairing such flues, and is injured. He sues Hartley Metals Ltd under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because the common duty of care is owed equally to all visitors and the company gave Stefan no specific warning of the risk", "B": "The claim fails, because the 1957 Act does not apply to a person who enters premises to carry out work for payment", "C": "The claim is likely to fail, because an occupier may expect a skilled visitor to guard against special risks ordinarily incident to his calling", "D": "The claim fails, because Stefan impliedly agreed to run the risk, so the defence of consent defeats his claim in full", "E": "The claim succeeds, because the company knew the flue was old and so had to repair the defect before allowing anyone to work there"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claim is likely to fail, because an occupier may expect a skilled visitor to guard against special risks ordinarily incident to his calling): Under the Act, an occupier may expect a person in the exercise of his calling to appreciate and guard against special risks ordinarily incident to it, so long as the occupier otherwise acts reasonably. Stefan is a registered engineer and carbon monoxide escape is a risk that arises in flue repair work. The Act does apply to workers who enter at the occupier's invitation, so Stefan is a visitor, but the standard owed to him takes account of his skill. An implied agreement to take the risk is not needed to reach this result, and the age of the flue does not change the allocation of the risk."}, {"id": 1512, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A contractor's digger negligently cuts the electricity cable supplying Hartley Castings, a foundry, causing a power cut that lasts 14 hours. A melt of metal in the furnace at the time is spoiled and loses £3,000 in value, and Hartley would have made a profit of £400 on that melt. During the power cut it also cannot start four further melts, losing £1,600 of profit. Hartley does not own the cable. Hartley sues the contractor in negligence. Which of the following best describes what Hartley can recover?", "options": {"A": "It can recover nothing, because all of its loss is pure economic loss that negligence does not generally compensate", "B": "It can recover all of the £5,000, because loss of profit caused by the power cut was a foreseeable consequence of the negligence", "C": "It can recover the £3,000 and the £400 profit on the spoiled melt, but not the £1,600 profit on the melts it could not run", "D": "It can recover only the £3,000, because lost profit on the spoiled melt is also pure economic loss", "E": "It can recover the £3,000 only if it owned the cable, since damage to a third party's property is not actionable"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It can recover the £3,000 and the £400 profit on the spoiled melt, but not the £1,600 profit on the melts it could not run): Economic loss that is consequential on physical damage to the claimant's own property is recoverable, whereas pure economic loss is not. The spoiled melt was physical damage to Hartley's property, so its value and the profit on it are recoverable. The profit lost on the four melts it could not start is pure economic loss, since it flowed from the interruption of supply and not from damage to its property. The ownership of the cable matters to a claim for damage to the cable, not to this one."}, {"id": 1513, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Sam and Tariq are jointly charged with robbery and are represented by the same firm, whose solicitor Priya took instructions from both. Sam says Tariq carried the knife. Tariq says Sam planned the robbery. Tariq has two previous convictions for robbery with a knife, and Sam's defence would benefit if the jury heard of them. Sam asks Priya to apply to adduce the convictions. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Priya has a conflict of interests and cannot act for both, since Sam's defence requires attacking Tariq, so each must be separately represented", "B": "Priya must stop acting for Sam only and continue for Tariq, as Tariq was the first client to give instructions", "C": "Priya may continue for both and make the application, because the co-defendant gateway allows one defendant to adduce the other's bad character", "D": "Priya may act for Sam alone and use Tariq's convictions, because information about Tariq no longer attracts confidentiality", "E": "Priya may act for both if each gives informed written consent, because their interests in the defence remain broadly aligned and both deny the robbery"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Priya has a conflict of interests and cannot act for both, since Sam's defence requires attacking Tariq, so each must be separately represented): The co-defendant gateway does permit Sam to rely on Tariq's convictions where they have substantial probative value on an important matter between them, so the evidence itself may be admissible. The difficulty is that running that application means attacking one client on behalf of the other, a significant conflict of interests. The clients' interests are not substantially common, so consent cannot cure it, and the firm ordinarily cannot continue for either. Separate representation protects confidentiality and independence."}, {"id": 1514, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Greystone Events instructs its solicitor to terminate a catering contract with Fennick Foods with immediate effect. The only ground is that Fennick delivered a trial menu tasting one day late. The contract has nine months to run and no term makes time of the essence. Greystone has also withheld a £6,000 invoice that is now overdue, which it would prefer not to mention. Greystone has found a cheaper caterer. What is the most appropriate course for the solicitor?", "options": {"A": "Draft the notice relying on the late tasting alone, because the client has said it prefers not to raise the overdue invoice and the solicitor must follow that instruction", "B": "Advise that Greystone may terminate once it has paid the overdue invoice, because a party in breach loses the right to terminate until the breach is remedied", "C": "Advise that the late tasting is unlikely to justify termination and could expose Greystone to a repudiation claim, then act on its decision", "D": "Draft the termination notice as instructed, because the decision belongs to the client and the solicitor's role is limited to carrying out lawful instructions promptly", "E": "Decline to act further, because the client's wish to leave for a cheaper supplier makes the instruction improper and the solicitor cannot continue the retainer"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Advise that the late tasting is unlikely to justify termination and could expose Greystone to a repudiation claim, then act on its decision): A solicitor must make sure the client is in a position to take informed decisions about the services provided, which means explaining the risk before acting. A one day delay on a term that is not a condition is unlikely to deprive Greystone of substantially the whole benefit of the contract. Terminating on that basis could amount to a repudiation by Greystone and expose it to damages and a counterclaim for the unpaid invoice. A motive to save money does not make the instruction improper, so once advised the client may decide."}, {"id": 1515, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Thornbury Cars Ltd is the defendant in a fast track claim for £16,000 by a former customer. The date for filing pre trial checklists was 14 days ago. Thornbury's solicitor completed and filed its checklist on time. The claimant's solicitor, who is ill, has not filed one, and there has been no communication. Trial is in five weeks, and a hearing room is already provisionally reserved. What is the most likely consequence?", "options": {"A": "The court may give directions on the basis of the defendant's checklist and may proceed to fix the trial, with no automatic strike out", "B": "The defendant's checklist is ignored by the court, because it can act only on a checklist that both sides have signed", "C": "The trial date is vacated automatically until both sides have filed completed checklists, because the court will list only on joint information", "D": "The court must enter judgment for the defendant, because failure to file a checklist amounts to abandonment of the claim", "E": "The claim is struck out automatically on the date the claimant should have filed, because the sanction operates without an order"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court may give directions on the basis of the defendant's checklist and may proceed to fix the trial, with no automatic strike out): Where only one party files a pre-trial checklist on the fast track, CPR 28.5 allows the court to consider that party's checklist and give appropriate directions, or list the case for a hearing to deal with the default. There is no automatic strike out; that follows only if no party files, and then through a court order. The trial date is not vacated just because the claimant has defaulted, and judgment for the defendant is not entered automatically."}, {"id": 1516, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Damon Pryce is suspected of affray outside a club. A witness says she could identify him. Damon, who disputes that he was there, agrees to a video identification. The identification officer has assembled a film containing Damon's image and those of other people who resemble him. Damon's solicitor, Ms Shah, attends to represent him. Which of the following correctly describes what Ms Shah may do before the film is shown to the witness?", "options": {"A": "She may view the film beforehand and object to its content, and the identification officer must consider any reasonable objection", "B": "She may view it only after the witness has done so, because showing it first would risk her coaching Damon on the images shown", "C": "She may object, but the identification officer need not respond, because the content of the film is a matter for the police", "D": "She may view it only if Damon waives his right to privacy, as the other people shown have a right to confidentiality", "E": "She may require the film to contain at least four other people, because that is the minimum number Code D sets"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She may view the film beforehand and object to its content, and the identification officer must consider any reasonable objection): Under Code D Annex A, the suspect and his solicitor must be given a reasonable opportunity to view the film before it is shown to any witness, and may object to its content. The identification officer must try to deal with reasonable objections, for example by replacing unsuitable images. At least eight other people who resemble the suspect must be shown, so the suggestion of four is wrong. Viewing after the witness defeats the purpose of the right, which is to make sure the procedure is fair before the witness sees it."}, {"id": 1517, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Winston's solicitor acts for him and for his lender on the purchase of a flat. The seller's solicitor is pressing to exchange today and the mortgage offer has not yet arrived, although the lender has said it will issue tomorrow. Winston has phoned to say he wants to exchange today because he fears losing the flat, and the deposit is in the client account. The lender's instructions require the offer to be in place before contracts are exchanged. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Exchange today after telling the lender, because the lender's consent is not needed before exchange and the offer is expected shortly", "B": "Exchange today on Winston's instructions, because the client decides when to exchange and the offer will arrive before completion", "C": "Exchange today using the deposit funds, because the lender's instructions bind the solicitor from completion onwards and not before then", "D": "Decline to exchange until the offer arrives, and explain that exchanging unfunded risks a binding contract Winston cannot complete", "E": "Exchange today but add a clause making completion conditional on funding, because a condition protects Winston if the lender later refuses"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to exchange until the offer arrives, and explain that exchanging unfunded risks a binding contract Winston cannot complete): A solicitor must act in the client's best interests and follow the lender's instructions in a joint retainer. Exchanging before the mortgage offer exists would bind Winston to buy without secured funds, and he could lose his deposit or face a claim if the loan fell through. The lender's instructions expressly require the offer before exchange, so telling the lender is not enough. A conditional clause cannot be imposed unilaterally and does not cure the breach of the lender's instructions."}, {"id": 1518, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Imani is on trial at the Crown Court for fraud and is represented by counsel. She intends to give evidence on her own behalf but will call no other witnesses. The prosecution has closed its case, a submission of no case to answer has been rejected, and the jury has returned to court. What is the correct procedure for the defence case from this point?", "options": {"A": "Imani gives evidence without any speech from counsel, and the judge sums up immediately after her evidence ends, because the defence closing is lost", "B": "Imani is cross-examined by the prosecution first, and counsel then asks her questions, ahead of any defence speech", "C": "Imani gives evidence only after counsel makes a defence opening speech, which is allowed because she is the sole defence witness", "D": "Imani gives evidence without a defence opening speech, as that is allowed only if other witnesses are called, and counsel then closes", "E": "Counsel makes a defence opening speech to the jury, because the defence has a right to open its case in every Crown Court trial"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Imani gives evidence without a defence opening speech, as that is allowed only if other witnesses are called, and counsel then closes): A defence opening speech is permitted only where the defence will call witnesses other than the defendant. Imani is the only defence witness, so she goes straight into the witness box and gives evidence in chief, is cross-examined and may be re-examined. Counsel keeps the right to a closing speech before the judge sums up. Allowing an opening speech wrongly treats her as entitled to the same sequence as a defendant who calls other witnesses."}, {"id": 1519, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "The trustees of a discretionary trust hold £400,000 for 'my children and their descendants, in such shares as the trustees decide'. Mr Bell's son Dev is an adult and has been a beneficiary since the trust began. He has run up debts and demands £10,000 from the trustees, saying that as the eldest he has a right to a larger share. The trustees refuse. Which of the following best describes Dev's position?", "options": {"A": "Dev is entitled to the amount he demands, because he is the eldest and an adult beneficiary can call for income", "B": "Dev can end the trust and take his share, because he is an adult beneficiary who is able to give consent", "C": "Dev has no rights, because the trustees may decide to ignore him as they choose", "D": "Dev is entitled to an equal share of the fund, because each object of a discretionary trust has a fixed share", "E": "Dev has no right to a particular sum, but he is entitled to be considered and to have the trust properly run"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Dev has no right to a particular sum, but he is entitled to be considered and to have the trust properly run): Under a discretionary trust no object has an entitlement to any share until the trustees exercise their discretion. Each object does have the right to be considered, to have the trustees act properly and to enforce the trust. Dev's age and seniority give him no priority, and the trustees need not give him the sum he demands. He cannot end the trust alone, because the other beneficiaries have interests too and no one acting alone holds all the interests."}, {"id": 1520, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Dara Hussein was refused bail at his first hearing before the magistrates' court on a charge of burglary. His solicitor, Mr Webb, wants to make a fresh bail application a week later, relying on the same arguments about Dara's fixed address and family ties, with no change in circumstances. The prosecutor objects. Which of the following correctly states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The court must hear the application in full, as Dara is entitled to one further hearing of arguments already made", "B": "Dara must instead appeal to the Crown Court, because a refusal can be challenged only by appeal", "C": "The court may refuse to hear it unless there has been a change of circumstances since the first hearing took place", "D": "The court must hear it in full only if Mr Webb gave the prosecutor seven days' written notice", "E": "The court may hear it only if the prosecution consents, because the earlier refusal binds the defendant"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court must hear the application in full, as Dara is entitled to one further hearing of arguments already made): A defendant refused bail is entitled to one further application to the magistrates at which the court must hear arguments in full, even if they repeat earlier arguments. Only at later applications may the court refuse to hear arguments it has heard before unless there are new considerations. The prosecutor's consent is not required, and an appeal to the Crown Court is an additional route, not the only one. No notice period applies to the second application."}, {"id": 1521, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Jake Moran, aged 16, is arrested for criminal damage. His father attends as appropriate adult, and the solicitor, Ms Dunn, is also present. Jake tells Ms Dunn that he would like to talk to her alone because he is embarrassed about what happened. His father insists on staying in the consultation, saying that as appropriate adult he is entitled to hear everything. What should Ms Dunn do?", "options": {"A": "Consult Jake in private, because the appropriate adult has no right to attend a legal consultation or to be told what was said", "B": "Consult Jake privately but then tell the father everything said, because an appropriate adult must be told all", "C": "Hold the consultation with the father present, because a juvenile cannot properly give instructions without the appropriate adult", "D": "Allow the father to stay, because an appropriate adult is entitled to attend every consultation and what Jake says remains privileged", "E": "Consult Jake privately only if the custody officer permits it, because privacy for juveniles is at the officer's discretion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Consult Jake in private, because the appropriate adult has no right to attend a legal consultation or to be told what was said): A suspect is entitled to consult a solicitor in private, and this applies to a juvenile as much as to an adult. The appropriate adult's role is to support the juvenile, help communication and see that questioning is fair, not to take part in legal consultations. What Jake says to his father is not privileged, so it is wiser for Ms Dunn to speak to Jake alone. She owes duties of confidentiality to Jake, and the father has no right to be told what was said."}, {"id": 1522, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Ardent Foods Ltd issued a claim form against a sole trader, Mr Jonas Fell, and served it with particulars of claim last month. Mr Fell has since served his defence, in which he says that the contract was made by a partnership of which he and Ms Kiran Dhillon are the partners. Ardent's solicitor wishes to add Ms Dhillon as a second defendant. What is the correct procedure?", "options": {"A": "Ardent may add Ms Dhillon without permission within 14 days of receiving the defence, as of right", "B": "Ardent must issue a new claim against Ms Dhillon and cannot add her, because parties cannot change after service", "C": "Ardent may add Ms Dhillon by serving notice on Mr Fell, who must consent in writing before the addition takes effect", "D": "Ardent must apply to the court for permission to add Ms Dhillon, because the claim form has already been served", "E": "Ardent may add Ms Dhillon without permission by amending the claim form, because no directions questionnaire has yet been filed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ardent must apply to the court for permission to add Ms Dhillon, because the claim form has already been served): Once a claim form has been served, the court's permission is needed to add, remove or substitute a party. The application can be made by an existing party or by the person who wishes to become a party. Written consent from the person is needed only where a person is added as a claimant. The addition of a defendant after service is routine where the true contracting party emerges, so there is no need to start a new claim."}, {"id": 1523, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Dev instructs a firm on the sale of his flat for a fixed fee of £1,200 plus VAT, confirmed in the client care letter. The letter states that the fee covers all work to completion and lists only disbursements as extras. Midway through, the buyer's solicitor raises several late enquiries and completion is delayed by six weeks, doubling the time the solicitor spends. The firm now wants to bill an extra £800 for the additional work. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The firm may charge the extra amount if it delivers a bill at completion, because the client can then challenge it through assessment", "B": "The firm may charge the extra amount at its standard hourly rate, because a fixed fee applies only to work reasonably foreseeable at the outset", "C": "The firm cannot charge more, because the fixed fee covered all work to completion and the letter allowed no variation for added work", "D": "The firm may charge half of the extra amount, because the cost of the delay was caused partly by the other side", "E": "The firm may charge the extra amount, because a fixed fee can be increased where the work turns out to take longer than expected"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The firm cannot charge more, because the fixed fee covered all work to completion and the letter allowed no variation for added work): A fixed fee is a price for a defined scope of work. Where the client care letter states that the fee covers all work to completion and does not reserve a right to charge more in stated circumstances, the firm bears the risk that the work takes longer. Delay caused by the other side does not by itself change the contract. The firm could have set out in advance the events, such as delay, that would permit a variation, or could ask Dev to agree an increase, but it cannot impose one."}, {"id": 1524, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Hannah Price is charged with shoplifting. The prosecution applies to adduce, as propensity evidence, her conviction for theft from a market stall 14 years ago when she was 17, for which she was fined. That offence involved taking food, whereas the present allegation is that she stole clothing worth £300 using a foil-lined bag. Hannah denies being the person seen on CCTV. Her solicitor opposes the application. Which of the following is the correct approach for the court?", "options": {"A": "The conviction is excluded, because the offence was committed as a youth and juvenile convictions are inadmissible", "B": "The conviction is admitted only if Hannah agrees, since the court has no power to admit it over her objection", "C": "The conviction is admissible, because the propensity gateway applies automatically once a conviction for a theft offence is proved", "D": "The court must refuse if admission would adversely affect fairness so that it ought not to be admitted, given the age and dissimilarity", "E": "The conviction is excluded, because convictions older than ten years are inadmissible under the Criminal Justice Act 2003"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court must refuse if admission would adversely affect fairness so that it ought not to be admitted, given the age and dissimilarity): A conviction can pass through the important matter in issue gateway and still be excluded under the fairness provision, which applies where admission would have such an adverse effect on the fairness of the proceedings that it ought not to be admitted. The court considers the lapse of time and the dissimilarity between taking food as a teenager and a planned theft of clothing. The Act sets no fixed time limit, and juvenile convictions are not barred. Hannah's agreement is not required."}, {"id": 1525, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Your firm acts for the partnership Tyne Surveyors. One partner, Jago, privately asks you to advise him personally on how to leave and take the firm's main clients with him before the other partners find out. He says the advice is confidential. The partnership has not been dissolved and no partner has given notice. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Advise Jago provided he pays a separate fee, because this makes him a separate client of the firm", "B": "Advise Jago in confidence, because he is one of the partners who together make up the client", "C": "Advise Jago only on his legal rights to leave, and not on how to take clients with him", "D": "Advise Jago but tell the other partners, because the duty to the partnership overrides his confidentiality", "E": "Decline to advise Jago, because the firm's client is the partnership and acting for him would conflict with that duty"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Decline to advise Jago, because the firm's client is the partnership and acting for him would conflict with that duty): The firm's client is the partnership, and advising one partner on how to compete with it and take its clients would conflict with that duty. Taking the instruction and then telling the others would breach the confidentiality owed to Jago. A separate fee does not change who the existing client is, and narrowing the advice does not remove the conflict. The firm should decline."}, {"id": 1526, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Alder Joinery Ltd has model articles, with an added provision that directors may not borrow more than £50,000 without an ordinary resolution of the shareholders. The board approved borrowing £200,000 from Meridian Bank, but no shareholder resolution was passed. Meridian had a copy of the articles and knew of the limit but did not ask whether approval had been obtained. It acted honestly. The company now disputes liability on the loan. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The loan does not bind the company, because a limit in the articles restricts the company's capacity and acts beyond it are void", "B": "The loan binds the company, because the limit does not affect a bank acting in good faith, and knowing of it is not bad faith alone", "C": "The loan binds the company only up to £50,000, because the bank can rely on authority within the limit but not above it", "D": "The loan binds the company only if the bank asked and was told that the shareholders had approved the borrowing before it lent", "E": "The loan does not bind the company, because the bank is taken to know the contents of the articles from their registration at Companies House"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The loan binds the company, because the limit does not affect a bank acting in good faith, and knowing of it is not bad faith alone): Section 40 of the Companies Act 2006 provides that, in favour of a person dealing with a company in good faith, the directors' power to bind the company is free of any limitation under its constitution. The bank is not bound to enquire into limits, good faith is presumed, and knowing of a limit does not itself show bad faith. The whole loan therefore binds the company. Constructive notice of registered documents no longer applies, and a restriction on directors' powers is not a limit on the company's capacity."}, {"id": 1527, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Historically the Crown could, by prerogative, requisition private premises in wartime. A modern statute, the Emergency Premises Act 2020, sets out a comprehensive scheme for requisitioning premises in emergencies, including compensation, and covers the same ground. During a flood, the government wishes to take over a warehouse owned by Cormac Reid without following the Act, relying on the prerogative, and offering no compensation. Cormac's solicitor challenges this. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The prerogative power has been abolished, because any statute on the same subject extinguishes the prerogative for all future purposes", "B": "The government may use the prerogative if it pays compensation at common law, because compensation removes any conflict with the Act", "C": "The government may choose either power, because a prerogative power continues to exist beside a statute on the same subject", "D": "The prerogative power is in abeyance while the statute covers the same ground, so the government must use the statutory scheme", "E": "The government may use the prerogative, because statutes cannot limit prerogative powers that existed long before Parliament passed any legislation"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The prerogative power is in abeyance while the statute covers the same ground, so the government must use the statutory scheme): Where Parliament legislates on the same ground as a prerogative power, the statute takes precedence and the prerogative is in abeyance, so the government must act under the statutory scheme (the De Keyser's Royal Hotel principle). The prerogative is not abolished, and may revive if the statute were repealed, but it cannot be used to bypass the Act's conditions, including compensation. Offering common law compensation would not cure the breach, and the government has no free choice between the two routes."}, {"id": 1528, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Kyle goes into a convenience store as a customer during opening hours. Seeing no one at the counter, he steps behind it into the area marked 'Staff only', deciding as he does so that he will take cash from the till. He takes £120 from the till and leaves. Kyle is charged with burglary under section 9(1)(a) of the Theft Act 1968. Which of the following best describes his liability?", "options": {"A": "He is guilty, because he entered a part of the building as a trespasser with intent to steal when he stepped behind the counter", "B": "He is not guilty, because he entered the shop with permission as a customer and his entry as a member of the public was lawful", "C": "He is not guilty of burglary, because a till counter is not a part of a building in the sense of section 9", "D": "He is guilty, but only under section 9(1)(b), because he had no intention to steal when he first entered the store", "E": "He is guilty, because trespass is established merely by a dishonest intention formed at any time while he is inside the premises"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He is guilty, because he entered a part of the building as a trespasser with intent to steal when he stepped behind the counter): A person with permission to enter part of a building becomes a trespasser if he goes into a part where his permission does not extend, such as a staff area. Kyle formed the intent to steal as he entered that part, so section 9(1)(a) is made out. Entry as a customer into the shop floor does not protect him. Dishonest intent alone is not trespass, because the entry must go beyond the permission given."}, {"id": 1529, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "During a meeting at Westbrook and Co in Nottingham, Karl Sorensen, a client in a bitter family dispute, tells his solicitor, Beth Alderton, that he will set fire to his former partner's house that night while she is asleep inside. Beth believes he means it. Karl then says the conversation is confidential and leaves. Which of the following best describes what Beth may do?", "options": {"A": "She must keep the information confidential, because it is protected by legal professional privilege and the duty is not displaced by the risk", "B": "She may tell the police what is needed to prevent the harm, because the public interest in protecting life can override confidentiality", "C": "She may disclose only after a court has made an order allowing disclosure, because only a court can authorise a breach of confidentiality", "D": "She may disclose the full contents of Karl's file to the police, because the threat removes the protection of confidentiality", "E": "She must tell Karl that she intends to inform the police and wait for his reply before taking any step"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She may tell the police what is needed to prevent the harm, because the public interest in protecting life can override confidentiality): Confidentiality is not displaced by a client's say so, and in exceptional circumstances a solicitor may disclose to prevent serious harm. A threat to burn a house with a person inside engages the public interest in protecting life, so Beth may inform the police now. She should disclose only what is necessary for that purpose, not the whole file. Privilege does not protect a communication that concerns a future crime, and waiting for a court order or a reply from Karl would risk the harm."}, {"id": 1530, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Your client, Fenwick Fitness Ltd, runs a private gym open to paying members. It asks you to draft a notice for the entrance saying that members use the gym at their own risk and that the company excludes all liability, including for death or personal injury caused by its negligence. The managing director says the notice has been used for years and no one has complained. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Advise that the exclusion cannot be relied on for death or personal injury caused by negligence, though a clear warning notice can still be given", "B": "Advise that the exclusion is valid if the company holds public liability insurance, because insurance cover answers any statutory restriction on exclusion", "C": "Draft the notice as instructed, because the client has used it without complaint and the client is entitled to decide its wording", "D": "Advise that the exclusion is valid for personal injury but not for death, because only a deceased person's estate is protected by statute", "E": "Advise that the exclusion is valid if displayed prominently, because members who enter after seeing the notice have accepted its terms"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Advise that the exclusion cannot be relied on for death or personal injury caused by negligence, though a clear warning notice can still be given): A trader cannot exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence. For a consumer contract or notice, as with gym members, section 65 of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 makes such a term or notice ineffective (UCTA 1977 s2(1) applies the same rule to non-consumer dealings). The solicitor owes a duty to give competent advice and to act in the client's best interests, and must not allow the firm to be used to mislead others. Drafting a notice that purports to exclude that liability would put an ineffective and potentially misleading notice before members. Prominence and insurance do not affect validity, and the rule covers both death and injury. A suitable warning notice may still help discharge the duty."}, {"id": 1531, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A claimant's crops were destroyed when a toxic chemical stored in bulk in tanks on neighbouring industrial land leaked and flowed onto his farm. The neighbouring company, Orwell Chemicals Ltd, had followed all recommended storage practices. The claimant sues under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher. Which of the following is a necessary element of the claim?", "options": {"A": "The damage was of a type that was reasonably foreseeable as a result of an escape of the thing accumulated", "B": "The chemical was dangerous in itself, such as fire or explosives, and not merely harmful if it escaped", "C": "The claimant also suffered personal injury, since the rule compensates harm to the person as well as to land", "D": "Orwell was negligent in the way the chemical was stored, since the rule is a form of fault-based liability", "E": "Orwell intended the chemical to escape onto the claimant's land, since the rule requires a deliberate release"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The damage was of a type that was reasonably foreseeable as a result of an escape of the thing accumulated): The rule in Rylands v Fletcher is a form of strict liability, so the claimant need not prove negligence. It requires that the defendant brought and kept on his land, for his own purposes, something likely to do mischief if it escaped, that the use was non-natural, that the thing escaped from the defendant's land, and that damage of a foreseeable type resulted. Orwell's careful storage is therefore not a defence. Intention to release is not an element, the thing need not be inherently dangerous like fire, and personal injury is not recoverable under the rule."}, {"id": 1532, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Pryce, aged 70, signed a will leaving his entire estate to a wildlife charity and nothing to his daughter Carys. Medical evidence shows that, because of a paranoid illness, he held a fixed belief that Carys had been stealing from him, a belief with no factual basis. In every other respect he ran his finances sensibly and could describe his property and his family. Carys challenges the will for lack of testamentary capacity. Is the will valid?", "options": {"A": "No, because a will that leaves nothing to a child is invalid unless the testator recorded his reasons in writing", "B": "No, because his delusion about his daughter was a disorder of the mind that perverted his sense of right and affected the disposition", "C": "No, because any recognised mental illness at the date of signing removes capacity, whatever its effect on the will", "D": "Yes, because the court cannot inquire into why a testator excluded a child, provided the will is formally valid", "E": "Yes, because he understood the nature of the act and the extent of his property, which is all that testamentary capacity requires"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (No, because his delusion about his daughter was a disorder of the mind that perverted his sense of right and affected the disposition): The Banks v Goodfellow test asks whether the testator understood the nature and effect of making a will, the extent of his property and the claims he ought to consider, and whether any disorder of the mind perverted his sense of right or prevented the exercise of his natural faculties. Mr Pryce met the first parts but his delusion directly distorted how he treated his daughter, so the will fails. Understanding property alone is not enough. A testator may exclude a child, but not as the product of a delusion."}, {"id": 1533, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Wren Park Ltd bought a warehouse from Calder Property Ltd for £600,000 after Calder said, innocently but wrongly, that a loading bay had planning permission. Wren Park discovered the problem four months later, and it affects the value by about £20,000. The warehouse otherwise suits Wren Park, which has since invested in racking but has not resold or charged the building. Wren Park seeks rescission. Calder asks the court to award damages instead. Which of the following best describes the court's power?", "options": {"A": "It can award damages instead where the misrepresentation was fraudulent, because an innocent misrepresentation gives the buyer rescission alone as its remedy", "B": "It may refuse rescission and award damages instead if that is equitable, having regard to the misrepresentation and the loss rescission would cause Calder", "C": "It may award damages instead only if Wren Park agrees, because rescission is a right of election for the buyer", "D": "It must order rescission, because rescission is the primary remedy for an innocent misrepresentation once Wren Park elects it", "E": "It may award damages instead, but only if Wren Park has already lost the right to rescind by affirmation"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It may refuse rescission and award damages instead if that is equitable, having regard to the misrepresentation and the loss rescission would cause Calder): Section 2(2) of the Misrepresentation Act 1967 lets the court, where the claimant would be entitled to rescind, declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu if it is equitable to do so. The court looks at the nature of the misrepresentation and at the loss rescission would cause the other party. A modest loss in value against a building that suits the buyer makes damages in lieu a real possibility. The court is not bound to order rescission, and the buyer's consent is not required."}, {"id": 1534, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Fitzone Gyms Ltd offers consumers an annual membership for a price of £600, displayed clearly and prominently on its website and in the written contract. The contract also contains a term allowing Fitzone to close any branch on seven days' notice. Mrs Anand, a member, says the price is much higher than competitors charge and that this makes it unfair under the Consumer Rights Act 2015. Which of the following best describes how the court will treat Mrs Anand's complaint about the price?", "options": {"A": "The price term cannot be assessed for fairness, because it is a core term that is transparent and prominent", "B": "The price term is unfair if it causes a significant imbalance, which a price above the market rate necessarily does", "C": "The price term is not assessed unless it appears on the Act's grey list of terms that may be unfair", "D": "The price term is assessed for fairness like any other term, because the Act protects consumers against excessive prices charged by traders", "E": "The price term is assessed for fairness only if Mrs Anand shows that she did not read the contract"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The price term cannot be assessed for fairness, because it is a core term that is transparent and prominent): Under section 64 of the Consumer Rights Act 2015, a term that specifies the price or the main subject matter is not assessed for fairness if it is transparent and prominent. Fitzone displayed the price clearly, so its level cannot be attacked as unfair even if competitors charge less. The branch closure term is a different matter and remains open to challenge. The grey list does not govern price terms."}, {"id": 1535, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Samir is a higher rate taxpayer and receives dividends of £8,500 from a trading company in which he holds shares. For this question, the dividend allowance is £500 and the rate of income tax on dividends for a higher rate taxpayer is 33.75%. The ordinary rate for a higher rate taxpayer on employment income is 40%. How much income tax does Samir pay on the dividends?", "options": {"A": "£3,200, because the £500 allowance is deducted but the remaining £8,000 is taxed at the 40% rate applying to employment income", "B": "No tax, because dividends are paid from profit that has already borne corporation tax and a credit covers the tax due", "C": "£2,868.75, because the whole £8,500 is taxed at 33.75%, as the dividend allowance applies only to basic rate taxpayers", "D": "£700, because the remaining £8,000 is taxed at the basic dividend rate of 8.75% since the company has already paid corporation tax", "E": "£2,700, because the £500 allowance is deducted and the remaining £8,000 is taxed at the higher dividend rate of 33.75%"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (£2,700, because the £500 allowance is deducted and the remaining £8,000 is taxed at the higher dividend rate of 33.75%): Dividends within the allowance are taxed at nil, so £500 of the £8,500 is free of tax, leaving £8,000 taxable. As Samir is a higher rate taxpayer that balance is taxed at the higher dividend rate, giving £8,000 multiplied by 33.75%, which is £2,700. The allowance is available to all taxpayers, and dividends are taxed at their own rates, not the 40% employment rate. The company's corporation tax does not relieve the shareholder, since there is no credit for it."}, {"id": 1536, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "After a general election, no party has an overall majority in the House of Commons. The leader of the largest party, Marion Ellis, has 290 of 650 seats but has failed to secure support from other parties. Another leader, Jonas Barrett, whose party has 250 seats, has signed a written agreement with two smaller parties that gives him a majority. The outgoing Prime Minister has resigned. A client asks whom the monarch will invite to form a government. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Marion Ellis, because by convention the monarch appoints the leader of the party with the most seats", "B": "Neither of them, because the monarch must wait for the House of Commons to pass a vote nominating a Prime Minister", "C": "Whoever the monarch personally prefers, because the appointment of a Prime Minister is an exercise of royal discretion", "D": "Jonas Barrett, because by convention the monarch appoints the person who can command the confidence of the House of Commons", "E": "Marion Ellis, because a leader must be given the first chance to form a government as the leader of the largest party"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Jonas Barrett, because by convention the monarch appoints the person who can command the confidence of the House of Commons): By convention, the monarch appoints as Prime Minister the person most likely to command the confidence of the House of Commons, and the monarch takes no personal part in choosing between parties. Having the most seats does not decide the matter, particularly where that leader has failed to secure support. Jonas has a written agreement giving him a working majority. The monarch's role is therefore formal, and the Commons does not vote to nominate a Prime Minister."}, {"id": 1537, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Orrin Haulage Ltd issued a claim form on 9 February 2026. The court posted the claim form and the particulars of claim, contained in the same envelope, by first class post to Dacre Ltd on Thursday 12 February 2026. Dacre filed an acknowledgment of service on 20 February 2026. There are no bank holidays in the period. By what date must Dacre file its defence?", "options": {"A": "Monday 16 March 2026, being 28 days after service of the particulars because an acknowledgment of service was filed", "B": "Thursday 12 March 2026, because 28 days run from the date on which the claim form was posted by the court", "C": "Friday 20 March 2026, because 28 days run from the date on which the acknowledgment of service was filed", "D": "Tuesday 17 March 2026, because the period is 28 clear days and the day of service itself is not counted", "E": "Monday 2 March 2026, because the defence is due 14 days after service of the particulars of claim"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Monday 16 March 2026, being 28 days after service of the particulars because an acknowledgment of service was filed): A claim form served with particulars is deemed served on the second business day after posting, which is Monday 16 February 2026. Because Dacre filed an acknowledgment of service, the time for the defence is 28 days after service of the particulars, which is 16 March 2026. Without an acknowledgment the period would be only 14 days. The 28 days run from service, not from the filing of the acknowledgment, and the day of service is excluded from the count but no extra clear day is added."}, {"id": 1538, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Goldcrest Solicitors hold £30,000 in the client account for Harriet Lowe, paid on Monday as the deposit for her house purchase, with exchange planned for Thursday. A fee earner needs £8,000 today to pay a search fee and stamp duty land tax for another client, Dev Chaudhry, whose ledger shows no funds yet. The fee earner proposes to use Harriet's money and replace it on Friday from Dev's expected payment. Which of the following is the correct position under the SRA Accounts Rules?", "options": {"A": "The firm must not do this, because client money may be used only to pay the firm's own costs", "B": "The firm may do this, because the money is replaced within the week and neither client suffers any loss", "C": "The firm may do this if Harriet is told in writing and consents, since her agreement removes the breach", "D": "The firm must not do this, because one client's money may not cover another client's liabilities, even briefly", "E": "The firm may do this, because search fees are disbursements that can be paid from any money in the client account"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The firm must not do this, because one client's money may not cover another client's liabilities, even briefly): Under the SRA Accounts Rules client money must be held for the client it belongs to and must not be used for another client, so a ledger for one client must not be allowed to go into debit. Harriet's money is held as her deposit and cannot be borrowed to fund Dev's disbursements, however short the period and whatever the intention to replace it. Her consent would not cure the position as the money would be used for someone else. The firm should wait for Dev's funds, or use its own money if it chooses to cover the payment."}, {"id": 1539, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Fran and Gil buy a house in joint names for £400,000, with a £90,000 deposit from Fran and £10,000 from Gil. They keep separate bank accounts, pay the mortgage in the proportion 90 to 10 and each keep a record of their contributions. The transfer contains no declaration of the beneficial interests. After separating, Gil says the house is held equally because it is in joint names. Which of the following is the most likely approach of the court?", "options": {"A": "It will award shares of 90 and 10, because a resulting trust applies as soon as contributions are unequal", "B": "It will start with equal shares but may find from the course of dealing that shares matched contributions", "C": "It will award Fran the whole beneficial interest, because Gil's contribution was too small to give him any share", "D": "It will award shares the court considers fair, ignoring what the parties intended, because conduct alone decides entitlement", "E": "It will award equal shares, because the presumption of equality for joint names cannot be rebutted by financial evidence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It will start with equal shares but may find from the course of dealing that shares matched contributions): In Stack v Dowden the starting point for a family home in joint names is equal beneficial shares, but the presumption can be rebutted by evidence of a different common intention across the whole course of dealing. Here separate finances, proportionate mortgage payments and written records point towards shares reflecting contributions. The court looks for the parties' actual intention, and fairness only enters if intention can be neither found nor inferred. Gil does not lose his interest merely because his contribution was small."}, {"id": 1540, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Tom returns home early and finds his wife Ella in bed with a colleague, Raj. Furious, Tom loses his self-control and kills Raj. There was no violence or threats by Raj, and nothing was said or done other than the discovery of the infidelity itself. Tom is charged with murder and wants to rely on the partial defence of loss of control. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The defence succeeds if a person of Tom's age and sex with normal tolerance might have reacted in the same way to the discovery", "B": "The defence fails, because sexual infidelity must be disregarded as a qualifying trigger and there is no other qualifying trigger on these facts", "C": "The defence is available, because the loss of control need not be sudden and Tom killed in the heat of the moment after seeing the act", "D": "The defence is available, because the discovery was a thing done of an extremely grave character causing Tom a justifiable sense of being wronged", "E": "The defence is excluded, because loss of control applies where the defendant feared serious violence from the victim, which Tom did not"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The defence fails, because sexual infidelity must be disregarded as a qualifying trigger and there is no other qualifying trigger on these facts): Loss of control under the Coroners and Justice Act 2009 needs a qualifying trigger, either fear of serious violence or a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged by things said or done of an extremely grave character. Section 55(6)(c) provides that the fact that a thing done or said constituted sexual infidelity is to be disregarded. With infidelity removed, nothing remains to found a trigger, so the objective test of the person of normal tolerance is never reached. The defence is not limited to fear of violence, but here neither trigger is available."}, {"id": 1541, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Anson Wharf let a warehouse to Treble Storage for five years. A clause in the lease provides that Treble must pay £50,000 if it ends the lease early by exercising a break option on three months' notice. Treble exercised the option in time and in the proper form, and then refused to pay, arguing that £50,000 is a penalty because Anson's likely loss is only about £20,000. Anson has not been paid rent since the break date. Which is the best advice for Anson?", "options": {"A": "The sum is enforceable only if Anson proves that £50,000 was a genuine pre-estimate of its loss when the lease was made", "B": "The sum is a penalty, because it exceeds Anson's real loss and the court will strike down any sum greater than the loss", "C": "The penalty rule does not apply, because the sum is payable on a lawful exercise of an option, not on a breach, so it is a primary obligation", "D": "The sum is a penalty unless Treble had legal advice, because the rule against penalties turns on the parties' relative bargaining strength", "E": "The sum is irrecoverable, because Anson can claim only damages for the loss of the remainder of the term and not a fixed sum"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The penalty rule does not apply, because the sum is payable on a lawful exercise of an option, not on a breach, so it is a primary obligation): The penalty rule regulates secondary obligations arising on a breach. Treble was entitled to leave under the option, so the £50,000 is the price of exercising a right and is a primary obligation. The court does not test it against Anson's loss. Because there was no breach, there is no damages claim for the rest of the term, and the question is only whether the clause was agreed and its conditions met."}, {"id": 1542, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Redfern Properties Ltd is selling an office building to Sanjay. The transfer has been prepared and the seller's solicitor has returned it executed, signed on behalf of the company by one director alone. No one witnessed the signature. The company has no sole director and its articles contain no special execution provisions. Sanjay's solicitor is checking the pre-completion documents. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The transfer is valid, because a single director may bind the company by signing a deed without any witness to the signature", "B": "The transfer is invalid until the company seal is affixed, because only a sealed document counts as a deed for a company", "C": "The transfer is not validly executed, because a company deed needs two authorised signatories or one director and a witness", "D": "The transfer is valid because a director has signed it, so only the lack of a witness needs correcting after completion", "E": "The transfer is valid if Sanjay's solicitor accepts it, because a buyer may waive the formalities of execution on completion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The transfer is not validly executed, because a company deed needs two authorised signatories or one director and a witness): Under the Companies Act 2006 a document is validly executed as a deed by a company if signed by two authorised signatories, or by one director in the presence of a witness who attests the signature. One director signing alone without a witness satisfies neither method, so the transfer is not a valid deed. A seal is no longer needed, and the buyer cannot waive a statutory formality. The defect must be put right before completion."}, {"id": 1543, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Rafferty, aged 73, has a will signed in 2020 leaving £25,000 to a charity. Before going into hospital for surgery tomorrow, he telephones his solicitor and says he wants to reduce the legacy to £10,000. He suggests writing the new figure in the margin of his will and signing it, with a nurse on the ward as a witness. What advice should the solicitor give?", "options": {"A": "Advise that a margin change signed by him and one independent witness is enough, since only the alteration needs witnessing", "B": "Advise him to make a codicil or new will, signed in the presence of two witnesses, rather than alter the existing document", "C": "Advise that a margin change initialled by him alone is valid, because alterations need only his own signature", "D": "Advise him to dictate the change to the nurse, who can record it, because a recorded oral instruction binds the executors", "E": "Advise him to write to the executors recording the reduced figure, because the executors can then give effect to it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise him to make a codicil or new will, signed in the presence of two witnesses, rather than alter the existing document): A change to a signed will takes effect only if it is executed with the same formalities as the will itself under section 21 of the Wills Act 1837, meaning the testator's signature and two witnesses present together. The safest advice is a properly executed codicil or fresh will, which he can arrange with the firm before surgery. An informal margin note with one witness or none would be ineffective, and a letter or oral instruction does not alter a will. Acting competently here protects the client's intentions."}, {"id": 1544, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Nina is on trial at the Crown Court for robbery. The only evidence against her is a single identification by a passer-by who saw the robber for about two seconds from across a dark street and has never met her. There is no supporting evidence, and Nina was not interviewed. At the close of the prosecution case her barrister considers making a submission of no case to answer. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The submission can succeed only if the passer-by is shown to have lied, since an honest mistake goes to weight alone", "B": "The submission must be made to the jury in open court, and they decide whether the prosecution evidence is enough", "C": "The judge must leave the case to the jury, because the reliability of an eyewitness is a matter of fact for them to decide on all the evidence", "D": "The judge may stop the case only after Nina has given evidence, because her account is needed to test the identification", "E": "The judge should stop the case if the identification evidence is poor and unsupported, because a jury could not safely convict on it"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The judge should stop the case if the identification evidence is poor and unsupported, because a jury could not safely convict on it): On a submission of no case the judge applies the Galbraith approach and, for identification, the Turnbull guidelines. Where the case rests on a fleeting, poorly lit identification with nothing to support it, the evidence is so weak that a properly directed jury could not safely convict, so the judge should withdraw it. The argument that reliability is always for the jury fails because the judge must assess the quality of the evidence at this stage. The submission is heard in the jury's absence and does not depend on Nina testifying."}, {"id": 1545, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Jon, now deceased, settled £500,000 on trust 'for Kim for life, remainder to Liam'. Kim and Liam are both adults of full capacity. The deed says the fund must stay invested until Kim's death, and that the trustees may pay capital to Kim only in an emergency. Kim and Liam agree to end the trust and split the fund equally. The trustees object, saying that they must respect Jon's wishes. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Only Liam may call for the trust to end, because a remainder beneficiary holds the entitlement to the capital", "B": "Kim and Liam together can direct the trustees to end the trust and divide the fund, despite Jon's wishes", "C": "The trustees may refuse, because the settlor's clearly expressed wishes override the wishes of the beneficiaries", "D": "Kim and Liam may end the trust only with the court's approval under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958", "E": "Kim and Liam can end the trust only if the trustees agree, since the trustees hold the legal title to the fund"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Kim and Liam together can direct the trustees to end the trust and divide the fund, despite Jon's wishes): Under the rule in Saunders v Vautier, beneficiaries who are all adult, of full capacity and together absolutely entitled can direct the trustees to end the trust and hand over the property. Kim and Liam between them hold every beneficial interest, so their agreement binds the trustees. The settlor's wishes and the trustees' objection do not prevent this, and the court's approval is needed only where a beneficiary lacks capacity. Neither beneficiary can bring about this result alone."}, {"id": 1546, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Neil Gallagher, aged 31, pleaded not guilty in the Magistrates' Court to assault occasioning actual bodily harm and was convicted after a trial at which the bench preferred the complainant's evidence to his. He was fined £1,200. Neil tells you he is convinced the bench got the facts wrong and wants to challenge the conviction. He also says he is on a tight budget and wants a plain account of what will happen. Which of the following is the best advice to give him about the route and its risks?", "options": {"A": "Appeal to the Crown Court, which rehears the case, and tell him that the fine cannot be increased because he is the appellant in the Crown Court proceedings", "B": "Appeal by case stated to the King's Bench Divisional Court, which can reconsider the factual findings of the bench in full", "C": "Appeal to the Crown Court, which rehears the case, and warn him that it may confirm, reduce or increase the sentence within magistrates' powers", "D": "Appeal to the Court of Appeal Criminal Division, which hears appeals from convictions in the Magistrates' Court once permission is given", "E": "Ask the same bench to reopen the trial, because the court that convicted him is the body to revisit its findings of fact"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Appeal to the Crown Court, which rehears the case, and warn him that it may confirm, reduce or increase the sentence within magistrates' powers): A defendant convicted after a not guilty plea has a right of appeal to the Crown Court against conviction, and that appeal is a rehearing of the evidence. On such an appeal the Crown Court may confirm, reverse or vary the decision and may impose any sentence the Magistrates' Court could have imposed, so the sentence could go up. Good client care requires Neil to hear that risk, as well as the route, before deciding. Case stated is limited to errors of law or excess of jurisdiction, so it does not suit a complaint about findings of fact."}, {"id": 1547, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Nadia is charged with maliciously inflicting grievous bodily harm under section 20 following a pub fight. The prosecution offers to drop that charge if she pleads guilty to common assault. Nadia tells her solicitor that she struck the other woman first and is worried about prison. The solicitor believes the offer is realistic. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Accept the offer on her behalf at the hearing, because the solicitor has authority to settle plea issues in the client's interests", "B": "Advise her fully on the offer and the consequences of each plea, and leave the decision on plea to her", "C": "Tell the prosecutor she has admitted the assault to strengthen the bargaining position, because candour with the other side assists plea negotiation", "D": "Advise her to accept the offer without further discussion, because the solicitor's duty is to secure the outcome that minimises custodial risk", "E": "Withdraw from the case, because a solicitor who knows the client is guilty cannot negotiate a reduction of the charge"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise her fully on the offer and the consequences of each plea, and leave the decision on plea to her): The decision on plea belongs to the client, and the solicitor's task is to give clear, informed advice about the offer, the evidence and the likely sentence. The solicitor cannot decide for her, accept the offer without instructions or disclose her admission, since it is confidential. Knowledge of guilt does not prevent the solicitor from acting or from negotiating a lawful plea. Advising and leaving the choice to Nadia respects her autonomy and the duty to act in her best interests."}, {"id": 1548, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Mr Doyle's company wants a loan of £250,000 from Capital Bank, and Mr Doyle offers a charge over the family home, which he owns jointly with his wife. The bank knows that the loan is for the company only, that Mrs Doyle will receive no direct benefit, and that the couple are married. The bank's relationship manager is preparing the paperwork. What is the correct step for the bank to take to avoid being fixed with constructive notice of any undue influence by Mr Doyle?", "options": {"A": "Take no particular steps, because she is a joint owner of the home and so has her own interest to protect in the transaction", "B": "Have the bank's own solicitor explain the charge to Mrs Doyle in the presence of her husband before she signs", "C": "Obtain the confirmation of Mr Doyle's solicitor that Mrs Doyle has been told the risks and agrees to sign", "D": "Insist that Mrs Doyle receives advice from a solicitor of her choosing and obtain written confirmation from that solicitor that the advice was given", "E": "Ask Mr Doyle to confirm in writing that he has not put any pressure on his wife and that she fully understands the charge she is giving"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Insist that Mrs Doyle receives advice from a solicitor of her choosing and obtain written confirmation from that solicitor that the advice was given): Where a bank is put on inquiry because the transaction is not apparently to the wife's benefit, Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge sets out the steps it must take. It must insist on the wife receiving advice from a solicitor and obtain written confirmation from that solicitor that the advice was given. Assurances from the husband do not protect the wife, and advice in his presence is no advice. Her joint ownership makes the risk greater, not smaller, and the husband's solicitor is not an independent adviser for this purpose."}, {"id": 1549, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Mehmet Dogan slips on a wet floor at work and claims against his employer for personal injury. A medical report values his pain, suffering and loss of amenity at £2,500, and his lost earnings and expenses come to £1,500, a total of £4,000. The claim is defended and does not arise from a road traffic accident. Which track would the claim normally be allocated to?", "options": {"A": "The fast track, because damages for pain and suffering above £1,000 take a personal injury claim out of the small claims track", "B": "The small claims track, because the total value of £4,000 is below the £10,000 limit that applies to most claims", "C": "The multi-track, because the claim is for personal injury against an employer, which carries a heavier duty of care", "D": "The small claims track, because the total value is under £5,000 and that is the only limit that applies to personal injury claims", "E": "The intermediate track, because the claim involves a medical report and some expert evidence is expected"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The fast track, because damages for pain and suffering above £1,000 take a personal injury claim out of the small claims track): A personal injury claim is suitable for the small claims track only if the damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity do not exceed £1,000 and the overall value is not more than £5,000. Mr Dogan's general damages are £2,500, so the limit is exceeded even though the total is small. A claim under £25,000 that is not a small claim normally goes to the fast track. The intermediate track starts at £25,000, and the identity of the defendant does not decide the track."}, {"id": 1550, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Alpine Hire Ltd rents ski equipment to consumers. A notice on its counter states: 'We accept no liability for injury, however caused.' Ffion Gwyn rented skis from Alpine after being handed this notice. A binding was negligently serviced by an Alpine technician and released on the slope, and Ffion broke her leg. Alpine says that the notice excludes its liability. Which of the following best describes the effect of the notice?", "options": {"A": "It is binding as to the skis, because liability for the quality of goods may be excluded by a clearly worded term", "B": "It is binding on Ffion, because it was clearly displayed and brought to her attention before the contract was made", "C": "It is binding if it satisfies the reasonableness test, because the Act subjects such notices to that test and this one was clearly worded", "D": "It is binding unless Ffion shows that Alpine acted dishonestly, since negligence alone does not override a clear notice", "E": "It is not binding on Ffion, because a notice cannot exclude a trader's liability for personal injury caused by negligence"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (It is not binding on Ffion, because a notice cannot exclude a trader's liability for personal injury caused by negligence): Section 65 of the Consumer Rights Act 2015 prevents a trader from excluding or restricting liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence, whether by a contract term or a notice. The notice therefore has no effect against Ffion's injury claim. Prominence and timely delivery of the notice make no difference because the bar is not subject to a reasonableness test. The rule for goods does not help Alpine because Ffion's loss is bodily injury caused by negligent servicing."}, {"id": 1551, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Hope Bank has begun possession proceedings against Sam, who lives in the mortgaged house, after he fell eight months behind. Sam lost his job in January but has just accepted new work starting in six weeks, and he can show from the offer letter and his budget that he can afford the instalments plus a contribution to the arrears. The mortgage term has fifteen years left to run. Which of the following best describes the court's powers on the possession claim?", "options": {"A": "The court may refuse possession but must order the lender to appoint a receiver of the property instead", "B": "The court may adjourn only if the arrears can be cleared within the next six months, which is the statutory maximum", "C": "The court may postpone possession for 28 days at most, which is the maximum period allowed for a residential borrower", "D": "The court may adjourn or suspend possession if Sam is likely to be able to pay the sums due within a reasonable period", "E": "The court has no discretion to delay, because the lender's right to possession arises as soon as the legal charge is created and Sam is in default"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court may adjourn or suspend possession if Sam is likely to be able to pay the sums due within a reasonable period): A lender has a right to possession as soon as the mortgage is made, but for a dwelling-house section 36 of the Administration of Justice Act 1970 lets the court adjourn, suspend or postpone if the borrower is likely to be able to pay any sums due within a reasonable period. That period is not fixed, and the court can look at the remainder of the term, which here is fifteen years. Sam's evidence of new work supports use of the power. There is no 28 day or six month ceiling, and a receiver is a different remedy that the court cannot impose on the lender."}, {"id": 1552, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trustee invests trust money in a way that the trust instrument does not permit, and the investment loses a substantial sum. The beneficiaries are all adults and wish to bring a claim against the trustee. Before issuing proceedings they ask a solicitor what the basic remedy for a breach of trust of this kind is. Which of the following correctly states it?", "options": {"A": "Restoration of the fund by equitable compensation, putting the trust in the position it would have occupied without the breach", "B": "Rescission of the trust, so that the property returns to the settlor and the trustees' duties come to an end", "C": "Removal of the trustee from office, with no personal liability to restore the loss suffered by the fund", "D": "Damages assessed at the date of the breach, calculated in the same way as damages for breach of contract at common law, without hindsight", "E": "Recovery of any personal profit that the trustee made, with no liability to replace a loss that he did not benefit from"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Restoration of the fund by equitable compensation, putting the trust in the position it would have occupied without the breach): The primary remedy for a breach of trust causing loss is to require the trustee to restore the trust fund by paying equitable compensation. The aim is to put the fund in the position it would have been in but for the breach, taking causation into account. An account of profits is a different remedy for a different wrong, and removal does not make good the loss. Damages in the common law sense do not apply."}, {"id": 1553, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A 10 year lease of a restaurant contains a tenant covenant to keep the premises in repair and a clause allowing the landlord, after 28 days' written notice of disrepair, to enter, carry out the repairs and recover the cost from the tenant as a debt payable on demand. The lease has four years to run. The tenant ignores the landlord's notice about a failing roof. The landlord wants to know what the clause allows. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The landlord may recover the cost as a debt only after a court has made an order of specific performance against the tenant", "B": "The landlord may recover the cost only at the end of the term, when a claim for dilapidations can be brought against the tenant", "C": "The landlord may enter and do the work only if it first forfeits the lease and then claims the cost as damages", "D": "The landlord may do the work but can claim only damages, capped at the fall in the reversion's value by the 1927 Act", "E": "The landlord may enter, do the work and sue for the cost as a debt, without forfeiting or proving loss to its reversion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The landlord may enter, do the work and sue for the cost as a debt, without forfeiting or proving loss to its reversion): A clause of this kind, recognised in Jervis v Harris, gives the landlord a contractual right to enter, carry out the repairs and recover the cost as a debt due under the lease. Because the claim is a debt and not damages for breach, the cap in section 18 of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927, which limits damages to the fall in the reversion's value, does not apply. The landlord does not have to wait until the end of the term, and it keeps the lease alive because it never needs to forfeit. No order for specific performance is needed to enforce an agreed debt."}, {"id": 1554, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for the buyer of a freehold industrial unit that is let to two tenants and is being sold by a company that has opted to tax. The seller's solicitor has sent a draft contract on the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fifth Edition) with a short list of special conditions. The buyer will rely on the rental income. What should you do about the form of contract?", "options": {"A": "Propose the Standard Commercial Property Conditions, which are designed for commercial sales and deal with tenancies, rent and VAT", "B": "Replace them with the Standard Conditions of Sale (Fourth Edition), which include detailed provisions on tenant rent arrears and VAT", "C": "Accept the Standard Conditions of Sale because they are the only published conditions of sale and cover tenancies and VAT in full", "D": "Accept the draft and rely on the commercial enquiries to cure any gaps, since the contract conditions need not deal with tenancies", "E": "Accept the draft but insist on the Law Society Conveyancing Protocol, which regulates commercial sales and the position of tenants"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Propose the Standard Commercial Property Conditions, which are designed for commercial sales and deal with tenancies, rent and VAT): The Standard Conditions of Sale are drafted for residential sales and say little about tenancies, rent apportionment and VAT. The Standard Commercial Property Conditions are written for commercial transactions and address those matters, so proposing them, or adding detailed special conditions, protects a buyer who relies on rent. The Protocol applies to residential sales, and enquiries cannot create contractual rights that the contract omits."}, {"id": 1555, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Bryn is tried for common assault at the magistrates' court. The bench rejects a submission of no case to answer. Bryn has told his solicitor that he does not want to give evidence because he is nervous, and she has advised him that the decision is his. The legal adviser now asks whether he will give evidence. What is the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The court may not draw any inference from his silence, because the burden of proof stays on the prosecution", "B": "The court may convict him on his silence alone, because failure to testify is itself evidence of guilt", "C": "The court may draw an inference only if his solicitor agreed that he would remain silent at trial", "D": "The court must be satisfied he understands that silence may lead to inferences, but it cannot convict on his silence alone", "E": "The court may draw an inference only if he was first warned about silence by the police in interview"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The court must be satisfied he understands that silence may lead to inferences, but it cannot convict on his silence alone): Under section 35 of the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994 the court may draw such inferences as appear proper from a defendant's failure to give evidence, once it has satisfied itself that he is aware of that consequence. Silence alone cannot prove guilt, because the prosecution must still establish a case to answer. The decision to testify remains Bryn's, and the inference does not depend on what happened in the police interview or on his solicitor's view."}, {"id": 1556, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Wendy, a café owner, signed an equipment hire form from Kinver Catering without reading it. In small print above her signature, the form said that Kinver would not be liable for loss of trade if the coffee machine failed. Nothing Kinver said to her about the form was untrue. The machine broke down for a week and Wendy lost £2,000 in takings. Which of the following best describes whether the exclusion clause forms part of the contract?", "options": {"A": "Wendy is bound by it, but only if she had hired equipment from Kinver before on the same standard form", "B": "Wendy is not bound by it, because Kinver took no reasonable steps to draw the small print to her attention", "C": "Wendy is bound by it, but a court will disregard it because it removes liability for the central purpose of the hire contract itself", "D": "Wendy is bound by it, because a person who signs a contractual document is bound by its terms whether or not she read them", "E": "Wendy is not bound by it, because a signature binds a person only to the terms that she actually understood at signing"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Wendy is bound by it, because a person who signs a contractual document is bound by its terms whether or not she read them): Where a document is signed, the signer is bound by its terms, even if she did not read them, unless there was misrepresentation or fraud. Reasonable notice matters only for unsigned documents such as tickets and notices. No false statement was made here, so the clause is incorporated. Whether it is valid is a separate question under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977."}, {"id": 1557, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Hartley Plastics Ltd agreed in writing to buy a packing machine from Norcross Engineering Ltd for £85,000. Before signing, Norcross's sales manager told Hartley's director that the machine could package 500 units an hour. The signed contract does not mention output, but contains a clause stating that it is the entire agreement and supersedes all prior representations. The machine manages only 300 units an hour. Hartley, which relied on the sales manager's statement, wishes to sue for misrepresentation. Which of the following best describes the effect of the entire agreement clause?", "options": {"A": "It bars the claim, because the parties agreed that no earlier statement about output would form any part of their final bargain with Norcross and Hartley signed it", "B": "It is effective against Hartley, because a business buyer is presumed to have read and accepted the clauses before signing", "C": "It does not automatically exclude the claim, as the clause is subject to the reasonableness test in section 3 of the Misrepresentation Act 1967", "D": "It removes the claim in misrepresentation but leaves Hartley free to argue that the output statement was a term of the contract", "E": "It has no effect on the claim, because the Misrepresentation Act 1967 overrides exclusion clauses in commercial contracts"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It does not automatically exclude the claim, as the clause is subject to the reasonableness test in section 3 of the Misrepresentation Act 1967): An entire agreement clause stops pre-contract statements from becoming terms, but an attempt to exclude liability for misrepresentation is caught by section 3 of the Misrepresentation Act 1967 and is effective only if reasonable. Hartley can therefore still pursue the claim if the clause fails that test. The tempting view that signature closes off the claim ignores the statute. The statute does not strike down every such clause either, because the reasonableness test decides the matter."}, {"id": 1558, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Kyle, a doorman, was angry with Lenny, a regular customer. Kyle deliberately taunted Lenny about his family in order to make him lash out, intending to use any violent reaction as a reason to attack Lenny with a bottle. Lenny swung at Kyle, and Kyle immediately struck him fatally with the bottle, later saying he had lost his temper. Kyle is charged with murder and seeks to rely on loss of control. What is the most likely outcome of that reliance?", "options": {"A": "Kyle can rely on the defence, because the statute disregards the defendant's own earlier conduct when considering the qualifying trigger", "B": "Kyle can rely on the defence, because Lenny's violence was a real threat and the effect on a person of normal tolerance is a matter for the jury", "C": "Kyle cannot rely on Lenny's conduct as a qualifying trigger, because Kyle incited it to provide an excuse to use violence against him", "D": "Kyle can rely on the defence if the jury finds he lost self-control, because his earlier planning goes to intention and not to the trigger", "E": "Kyle cannot rely on the defence, because a doorman is expected to show a higher standard of self-restraint than an ordinary person"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Kyle cannot rely on Lenny's conduct as a qualifying trigger, because Kyle incited it to provide an excuse to use violence against him): Section 55(6)(a) excludes from a qualifying trigger anything the defendant incited to be done or said for the purpose of providing an excuse to use violence. Kyle taunted Lenny precisely to obtain a pretext, so Lenny's swing cannot found the defence even though it was a real threat. The comparison with a person of normal tolerance uses the defendant's age and sex, not his job. The jury is never asked to consider tolerance because no valid trigger exists."}, {"id": 1559, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Malik Anwar, aged 30, has a learning disability and is arrested for handling stolen goods. He speaks slowly and seems confused when the custody officer reads him his rights, although he says that he understands. The officer is unsure whether he needs an appropriate adult and proposes to interview him with his solicitor present only. Malik's solicitor asks whether an appropriate adult is required. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because a suspect who may be mentally vulnerable must be treated as such, even where the officer is uncertain", "B": "No, because appropriate adults are required only for suspects under 18, and an adult's solicitor fulfils that function", "C": "Yes, but only if Malik himself asks for an appropriate adult after being told that he may have one", "D": "Yes, but only after a doctor has examined Malik and formally certified that he is mentally vulnerable", "E": "No, because Malik has said that he understands his rights and so the officer can accept that statement"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Yes, because a suspect who may be mentally vulnerable must be treated as such, even where the officer is uncertain): Code C requires an appropriate adult for juveniles and for any adult who is, or appears to be, mentally disordered or otherwise mentally vulnerable. If the officer has any suspicion, or is told in good faith, that a person may be vulnerable, the person must be treated as such until an assessment shows otherwise. The solicitor's role is different, as the solicitor gives legal advice while the appropriate adult supports and assists communication. No medical certificate or request from the suspect is needed, and his own statement of understanding does not settle the matter."}, {"id": 1560, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Dmitri Volkov is charged with possessing an offensive weapon. Officers who had no grounds for suspicion stopped him in the street and searched him, finding a knife. They did not follow the statutory search procedure, and one later admitted in evidence that he had acted on a hunch. Dmitri's counsel applies under section 78 to exclude the knife and says that because the search was unlawful the knife must be excluded. The judge asks for submissions on the correct approach.", "options": {"A": "The knife can be excluded only under section 76, because the search was unlawful and no discretion arises under section 78", "B": "The knife should be admitted, because the officer's admission of acting on a hunch is irrelevant to the fairness of the trial", "C": "The knife cannot be excluded, because real evidence exists independently of how it was found and so cannot make the trial unfair", "D": "The unlawfulness is relevant, but the knife is excluded only if admitting it would make the trial unfair, judged on all the circumstances", "E": "The knife must be excluded, because evidence found in an unlawful search is automatically inadmissible"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The unlawfulness is relevant, but the knife is excluded only if admitting it would make the trial unfair, judged on all the circumstances): There is no automatic exclusion of evidence found through an unlawful search. Section 78 asks whether admission would have such an adverse effect on fairness that the evidence ought not to be admitted. The officers' lack of grounds and the admission of a hunch weigh in Dmitri's favour, but the court also weighs the real and reliable nature of the knife. Section 76 deals only with confessions."}, {"id": 1561, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Bashir has exchanged contracts to buy a registered freehold house from Gwen, with completion in four weeks. Bashir learns that Gwen is considering selling to a neighbour for a higher price. He wants to ensure that any purchaser from Gwen takes subject to his contract. Which of the following steps will best protect his position on the register of Gwen's title?", "options": {"A": "Rely on his equitable interest alone, because an estate contract binds a later purchaser automatically once contracts have been exchanged", "B": "Apply for a restriction against Gwen's title, because a restriction is the entry that protects an estate contract from a later buyer", "C": "Register a caution against first registration of the property, because that entry protects contractual rights in land that is unregistered", "D": "Register a Class C(iv) land charge against Gwen's name, because that registration protects an estate contract under the land charges system", "E": "Apply to enter a notice of his estate contract on Gwen's title, because a notice is the entry that protects an interest of this kind"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Apply to enter a notice of his estate contract on Gwen's title, because a notice is the entry that protects an interest of this kind): An estate contract is an equitable interest in registered land and is protected by a notice on the charges register of the seller's title. A restriction controls dealings but is not the entry for a contract. Class C(iv) land charges apply only to unregistered land, and a caution against first registration is for land that has not yet been registered. Without a notice, a later purchaser for value could take free of Bashir's interest."}, {"id": 1562, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Penhallow Bank lent £200,000 to Marlow Builders Ltd. In a signed document, Marlow's director, Joyce, agreed to be liable to the bank for the sum 'as principal debtor and not merely as surety'. Marlow's loan agreement was later found unenforceable because the company lacked authority to borrow, although the funds were advanced. The bank demands payment from Joyce. Which of the following best describes Joyce's liability?", "options": {"A": "Joyce is not liable, because a guarantor's liability is secondary and falls when the principal debt is unenforceable against the borrower", "B": "Joyce is liable only for the amount the bank can recover from Marlow's assets, because a primary obligation is limited to the borrower's own liability", "C": "Joyce is liable, but only if the bank first shows it has exhausted its remedies against Marlow, since the label principal debtor does not change a surety's rights", "D": "Joyce is not liable, because the document is a guarantee and the bank did not obtain Marlow's written consent to Joyce's liability", "E": "Joyce is liable, because the document makes her a primary obligor, so her liability is independent of the enforceability of Marlow's loan"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Joyce is liable, because the document makes her a primary obligor, so her liability is independent of the enforceability of Marlow's loan): A guarantee is secondary and depends on a valid principal debt, but an indemnity or undertaking to be liable as principal debtor creates an independent primary obligation. The wording here shows that Joyce undertook liability in her own right, so it survives even if the borrower's liability fails for want of authority. She is not entitled to insist that the bank pursue Marlow first. A signed written document also satisfies any formality that might be needed."}, {"id": 1563, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Colm runs a business that, for a fee, introduces members of the public to investment products and tells them which shares to buy. He is not authorised by the Financial Conduct Authority and does not fall within any exemption. After a client loses money, she asks a solicitor what the consequences are for Colm and for the agreement she signed with him. Which of the following is the most accurate summary?", "options": {"A": "Colm commits a criminal offence, and the agreement is unenforceable against the client, who may recover money paid and compensation", "B": "Colm commits a criminal offence, but the agreement remains fully enforceable against the client because she freely entered into it", "C": "Colm commits a regulatory breach only, and the Financial Conduct Authority may fine him but he faces no criminal liability under the Act", "D": "Colm commits no offence, because the general prohibition applies to dealing in investments and not to giving advice about them", "E": "Colm commits a criminal offence, but the agreement becomes enforceable once the Financial Conduct Authority authorises him after the event"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Colm commits a criminal offence, and the agreement is unenforceable against the client, who may recover money paid and compensation): Section 19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 prohibits carrying on a regulated activity in the United Kingdom unless the person is authorised or exempt, and section 23 makes a breach a criminal offence. Advising on investments is a regulated activity, so Colm falls within the prohibition. Under sections 26 and 27, an agreement made in the course of the unauthorised activity is unenforceable against the client, who may recover money paid and compensation for loss. Later authorisation does not cure the earlier agreement."}, {"id": 1564, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Consumer Fairness Act 2025 came into force on 1 January 2026. It imposes a civil penalty on traders who fail to provide a particular written warning with goods sold. Oakwood Supplies Ltd sold a batch of goods to retailers without the warning in November 2025, before the Act was in force. A regulator seeks to penalise Oakwood. The Act does not state whether it applies to sales before it came into force. Which of the following is the most likely position?", "options": {"A": "The penalty cannot be imposed, because the Act has no effect until the regulator makes rules for its operation", "B": "The penalty can be imposed, because the Act is silent and silence means Parliament did not exclude past sales", "C": "The penalty can be imposed, because the Act is remedial and statutes that protect consumers apply to past conduct", "D": "The penalty cannot be imposed, because there is a presumption that legislation is not retrospective unless Parliament makes that clear", "E": "The penalty can be imposed, because the presumption against retrospection applies to criminal laws and not to civil penalties"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The penalty cannot be imposed, because there is a presumption that legislation is not retrospective unless Parliament makes that clear): There is a strong presumption that legislation does not operate retrospectively, so a statute that adversely affects rights or imposes liability is not applied to past events unless it says so clearly or by necessary implication. This Act is silent on past sales, and Oakwood's conduct occurred before commencement. The presumption applies to civil penalties as well as to criminal offences. A remedial purpose does not displace it on its own."}, {"id": 1565, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "A national environmental charity, Greenway Trust, learns that a council has granted a permit allowing a developer to drain a protected wetland. The charity was not involved in the application and is not personally affected as a landowner, but it has campaigned for the wetland for years. It asks a solicitor whether it can apply for judicial review. Which of the following best states the test for standing?", "options": {"A": "The charity has no standing, because pressure groups are excluded from judicial review whatever their record of work on the issue", "B": "The charity needs a sufficient interest in the matter, which the court assesses flexibly alongside the merits and the nature of the public wrong", "C": "The charity needs to show that it took part in the original application process and made written representations to the council", "D": "The charity must obtain the consent of the Attorney General, because a body that represents the public interest requires that approval before issuing a claim", "E": "The charity needs to show that it is directly and personally affected by the decision in the way the developer's neighbours would be"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The charity needs a sufficient interest in the matter, which the court assesses flexibly alongside the merits and the nature of the public wrong): The Senior Courts Act 1981 requires a claimant to have a sufficient interest in the matter, and the courts apply this flexibly, looking at the strength of the challenge and the seriousness of the public wrong. A well established charity with a genuine concern for the wetland can qualify even if it has no property interest. Direct personal impact and participation in the earlier process are not required. No consent from the Attorney General is needed, and pressure groups are not excluded."}, {"id": 1566, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Kerry Lindqvist owns Meadow Cottage and her neighbour, Dean Osei, owns the adjoining farmhouse. Kerry asks her solicitor whether a proposed right for the farmhouse owner to walk across her garden to reach the lane could take effect as an easement. Her solicitor lists the requirements for a valid easement. Which of the following correctly states those requirements?", "options": {"A": "A dominant tenement alone, benefit to the dominant land, different owners of each plot, and a right registered at the Land Registry", "B": "A dominant and servient tenement, accommodation of the dominant land, adjoining plots, and a right that is exercised in return for payment", "C": "A dominant and servient tenement, accommodation of the dominant land, different owners, and a right capable of forming the subject matter of a grant", "D": "A dominant and servient tenement, benefit to the dominant land, different owners, and the servient owner's exclusive possession of the garden", "E": "A dominant and servient tenement, benefit to the owner personally, common ownership of both tenements, and a right capable of being granted"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A dominant and servient tenement, accommodation of the dominant land, different owners, and a right capable of forming the subject matter of a grant): The four characteristics of an easement are that there must be a dominant tenement and a servient tenement, that the right must accommodate the dominant tenement, that the two tenements must be owned or occupied by different people, and that the right must be capable of forming the subject matter of a grant. A right to walk over a neighbour's garden to reach a lane is a classic right of way and meets them. Benefit to the owner personally is not enough. Registration is relevant to legal status but is not a characteristic."}, {"id": 1567, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Kestrel Joinery Ltd issues a claim for £42,000 for unpaid invoices. The particulars of claim, signed by its solicitor, are served without a statement of truth. The defendant's solicitor notices this and writes to say that the claim is a nullity. The defendant has not yet filed a defence and the time for doing so has not expired. Kestrel's solicitor asks what position the claimant is in. Which of the following best describes it?", "options": {"A": "The particulars stand, but the claimant loses the right to claim interest and costs until the statement of truth is supplied", "B": "The particulars remain effective unless struck out, but the claimant cannot rely on them as evidence until a statement of truth is added", "C": "The particulars are void until verified, so the claim must be reissued and fresh service effected on the defendant", "D": "The particulars are effective and may be relied on as evidence, since the solicitor's signature has the same effect as a statement of truth", "E": "The particulars are automatically struck out after 14 days if the defendant gives notice, unless a verified copy is filed"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The particulars remain effective unless struck out, but the claimant cannot rely on them as evidence until a statement of truth is added): Under CPR Part 22 a statement of case that is not verified by a statement of truth is not a nullity: it stays effective unless the court strikes it out. The consequence is that the party cannot use the document as evidence in the case until it is verified. Merely signing the document does not amount to a statement of truth. The court can also order the statement to be added and fix a time for doing so."}, {"id": 1568, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Kavita Soames is a solicitor acting for Ross Pickering, who is selling his bakery business. On 1 March Ross's letter to the buyer stated, truthfully, that annual turnover was £500,000. On 20 May, before contracts were exchanged, Ross's largest customer left and turnover roughly halved. Ross tells Kavita he will say nothing, because the March figure was correct and the buyer has not asked again. Exchange is planned for 3 June. What is the most appropriate advice for Kavita to give?", "options": {"A": "Ross must correct the figure only if the buyer repeats the question, because a duty to update arises on request", "B": "There is no duty to volunteer information, so Ross may stay silent because the March statement was true when it was made", "C": "Ross must correct the figure, but Kavita may continue to act and leave the correction to Ross after exchange", "D": "Silence would amount to a misrepresentation as the earlier true statement is now false, so Ross must correct it or Kavita must stop acting", "E": "Ross may stay silent provided that the warranties in the contract are drafted to cover turnover, because warranties replace any need for disclosure before exchange"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Silence would amount to a misrepresentation as the earlier true statement is now false, so Ross must correct it or Kavita must stop acting): A statement true when made becomes a continuing representation, and if circumstances change before the contract is made the representor must correct it, otherwise silence is itself a misrepresentation. Kavita must not mislead others or allow the court or buyer to be misled by her client's conduct, so she should advise Ross to correct the figure and stop acting if he refuses. Leaving the correction until after exchange is too late. The bare rule that there is no duty to disclose does not fit where an earlier statement has become untrue."}, {"id": 1569, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Jade pleads guilty at the Crown Court to assault occasioning actual bodily harm. The prosecution says she punched a customer without provocation. Jade says the customer pushed her first and that she reacted. The difference between the two accounts would affect the sentence. Which of the following is the correct way for the court to proceed?", "options": {"A": "The judge holds a Newton hearing to decide the disputed facts, and Jade may lose some plea credit if her account is rejected", "B": "The judge decides on the papers which version is more likely, without hearing any witness give evidence on the disputed facts", "C": "The judge must sentence on Jade's version, because she has pleaded guilty and the prosecution may not contest her account", "D": "A jury must be sworn to decide the disputed facts, and the judge then passes sentence on their findings", "E": "The plea is equivocal and cannot be accepted, so a not guilty plea must be entered and a full trial held before the jury"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The judge holds a Newton hearing to decide the disputed facts, and Jade may lose some plea credit if her account is rejected): Where a defendant pleads guilty but disputes the prosecution's version in a way that matters to sentence, the judge may hold a Newton hearing. The judge hears evidence on the issue and makes findings without a jury. If the defendant's version is rejected, the court may reduce the credit given for the guilty plea. Her plea is not equivocal, because she admits the offence and disputes only the circumstances."}, {"id": 1570, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Redcliffe Joinery Ltd has fallen behind with its creditors and its directors, acting by board resolution, want to appoint administrators out of court. The company granted Northern Bank plc a qualifying floating charge over all its assets two years ago, and that charge is still enforceable. The directors have prepared a notice of intention to appoint and wish to appoint as soon as possible. Which of the following is the correct step?", "options": {"A": "They may appoint the same day, because notice to the holder of a qualifying floating charge need only be given after the appointment takes effect", "B": "They need the approval of a majority in value of the unsecured creditors before they may give notice of intention to the bank", "C": "They must give written notice of intention to Northern Bank plc and may appoint only after five business days, unless the bank consents sooner", "D": "They must apply to court for an administration order, because directors cannot make an out of court appointment where a floating charge exists", "E": "They must give the bank notice and wait ten business days, which is the period allowed for the bank to appoint its own administrator"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (They must give written notice of intention to Northern Bank plc and may appoint only after five business days, unless the bank consents sooner): Directors may appoint an administrator out of court by filing a notice of intention to appoint, but where a qualifying floating charge holder exists they must first give that holder written notice. The appointment may then be made after five business days, or earlier if the holder consents in writing. This gives the bank the chance to appoint its own choice of administrator. A court application is the other route, not a requirement, and unsecured creditors have no veto over the notice."}, {"id": 1571, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Jonas Whitlock instructs his solicitor to sue Calder Surveyors Ltd for a negligent valuation of a flat that caused a £60,000 loss. Mr Whitlock insists that the particulars should allege that Calder dishonestly inflated the valuation to please the lender, although he has no documents or witness suggesting this. He says the allegation will frighten Calder into settling quickly. Limitation has several years to run. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Plead dishonesty in the alternative to negligence, since a statement of truth signed by the client protects the solicitor from criticism", "B": "Plead dishonesty but mark it as unparticularised, because the court can allow detail to be added once disclosure has been given", "C": "Decline to act, because a client who instructs a solicitor to plead a speculative allegation has destroyed the retainer", "D": "Plead negligence only, and explain that dishonesty cannot be alleged without reasonably credible material to support it", "E": "Plead dishonesty and add a request for further information to Calder, so that evidence of the dishonesty emerges before trial"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plead negligence only, and explain that dishonesty cannot be alleged without reasonably credible material to support it): A solicitor must not put forward a case, and above all not allege dishonesty, unless there is reasonably credible material that establishes an arguable case. Mr Whitlock has only suspicion and wants the allegation as pressure, which would also mean misusing the court's process. Negligence is properly supported by the facts and gives him a full remedy. Pleading dishonesty hoping for disclosure fails because the material must exist when the allegation is made, and refusing to act would be an over reaction when advice can resolve the problem."}, {"id": 1572, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "On 1 September Calder Steelworks agreed to sell and deliver 40 fabricated steel frames to Brenin Construction on 30 September for £80,000. On 5 September Calder emailed to say it would not deliver because its costs had risen. Brenin replied that it expected delivery as agreed and did nothing further. On 15 September new safety regulations made it unlawful to sell frames of that specification. Brenin has bought replacements elsewhere at a higher price and now claims damages from Calder. Which of the following best describes Brenin's position?", "options": {"A": "Brenin cannot recover damages, because by insisting on performance it affirmed the contract, which frustration then discharged before any claim accrued", "B": "Brenin can recover damages, because affirming the contract preserved its right to sue for breach and the later illegality does not affect that earlier right", "C": "Brenin cannot recover damages, because Calder's failure to deliver became lawful on 15 September and so cannot amount to a breach of contract", "D": "Brenin can recover damages, because the email of 5 September was a repudiation that immediately ended the contract and fixed the date for assessing its loss", "E": "Brenin can recover only its wasted costs, because frustration removes expectation loss but leaves reliance loss recoverable under the 1943 Act"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Brenin cannot recover damages, because by insisting on performance it affirmed the contract, which frustration then discharged before any claim accrued): A repudiatory breach does not end the contract unless the innocent party accepts it. Brenin chose to insist on performance, so the contract stayed alive with both sides still bound. The new regulations then made performance unlawful and frustrated the contract, which discharged both parties from that point. Because Brenin had no accrued right to damages before then, and the date for performance had not arrived, the claim fails."}, {"id": 1573, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Garrity instructs a solicitor, Ms Vane, to prepare a new will leaving his house to his niece, Rosa. Ms Vane is told the matter is urgent because Mr Garrity is ill, but she does nothing for six weeks. He dies before a new will can be signed, and the house passes under an earlier will to a distant cousin. Rosa has no contract or other relationship with Ms Vane. Rosa sues Ms Vane for the value of the lost gift. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Rosa's claim succeeds, but only if Mr Garrity signed written instructions naming her at the first meeting with the solicitor", "B": "Rosa's claim succeeds, because losses that are reasonably foreseeable to a solicitor are recoverable by anyone who suffers them", "C": "Rosa's claim fails, because only Mr Garrity's estate could sue for the solicitor's breach of her contract with him", "D": "Rosa's claim can succeed, because the solicitor assumed responsibility to the testator and Rosa had no other remedy for the loss", "E": "Rosa's claim fails, because loss of an expected inheritance is pure economic loss and no duty is owed to non-clients"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rosa's claim can succeed, because the solicitor assumed responsibility to the testator and Rosa had no other remedy for the loss): A solicitor who negligently fails to carry out a client's instructions for a will can be liable to an intended beneficiary who loses the gift, because the solicitor has assumed responsibility to the testator in a way that extends to the beneficiary. The testator's estate has no loss and cannot sue, so without a remedy for Rosa the solicitor's breach would go unredressed. This is an exception to the rule against recovery for pure economic loss. The case also reflects the solicitor's professional duty to act promptly and competently on client instructions."}, {"id": 1574, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Several clients have asked the solicitor about the residence nil rate band on death. Tessa Quinn, whose estate is well below the threshold at which the band starts to taper away, has owned a home throughout her adult life and intends to leave it by will. She is considering leaving it to different people. Which of the following correctly identifies a disposition that would qualify for the residence nil rate band?", "options": {"A": "A gift of the home to a charity, because gifts to charity are exempt and the band is applied to that value", "B": "A gift of the home to her surviving civil partner, because the band is applied to transfers that are exempt as spouse gifts", "C": "A gift of the home to her stepchildren, because a stepchild is treated as a lineal descendant for the residence band", "D": "A gift of the home to her parents, because lineal ancestors fall within the band alongside lineal descendants as well as descendants of the deceased", "E": "A gift of the home to her nephews and nieces, because they are close family members of the deceased"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A gift of the home to her stepchildren, because a stepchild is treated as a lineal descendant for the residence band): The residence nil rate band is available where the home is left to lineal descendants, and these include children, grandchildren, stepchildren, adopted and foster children. A gift to stepchildren therefore qualifies. Nephews, nieces and parents are not lineal descendants, and exempt gifts to a spouse or a charity do not use the band, since they are not taxed at all. A transferable band can pass to a surviving spouse, which is a different point."}, {"id": 1575, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mr Gorman is driving when another motorist, Ms Kaplan, negligently crosses the central line and collides with his car at moderate speed. Mr Gorman suffers no physical injury, but he has a long history of depression, which the shock of the collision revives in a severe and lasting form. A person of normal fortitude would not have developed illness from such a collision. He sues Ms Kaplan for the recurrence. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "He may recover, because as a primary victim in danger of physical injury, he need not show psychiatric harm was foreseeable", "B": "He cannot recover, because psychiatric harm is only recoverable where a person of normal fortitude would have suffered it", "C": "He may recover only as a secondary victim, which requires proof of a close tie of love and affection with someone injured", "D": "He cannot recover, because he suffered no physical injury and psychiatric illness is not actionable on its own in negligence", "E": "He may recover, but only if Ms Kaplan knew of his history of depression before the collision"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He may recover, because as a primary victim in danger of physical injury, he need not show psychiatric harm was foreseeable): A claimant who is within the range of foreseeable physical injury is a primary victim, and for him foreseeability of some personal injury, physical or psychiatric, suffices. He then takes his victim as found, so the vulnerability of Mr Gorman does not defeat the claim. The normal fortitude test belongs to secondary victims. Physical injury is not required, and a close tie to another person is relevant only to secondary victims."}, {"id": 1576, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Mr Arkwright, a farmer, repeatedly told his nephew Ned over twenty years, 'This farm will be yours when I go.' Relying on that, Ned left a well-paid job, worked on the farm for low pay and invested his own savings in new buildings. Mr Arkwright died leaving the farm to a charity by his will. Ned claims against the estate. Which of the following states what Ned must show to succeed?", "options": {"A": "An assurance of an interest in the farm, reliance on it, and detriment, so that going back on it would be unconscionable", "B": "That he paid money for the farm, because proprietary estoppel needs consideration to turn a promise into an interest", "C": "That Mr Arkwright acted dishonestly, because the claim depends on fraud in the making of the assurance", "D": "A written agreement signed by Mr Arkwright, because a promise about land needs section 2 of the 1989 Act to be enforceable", "E": "A common intention that the farm should be shared, shown by direct payments towards its purchase price"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An assurance of an interest in the farm, reliance on it, and detriment, so that going back on it would be unconscionable): A claim in proprietary estoppel needs an assurance about rights in property, reliance on it by the claimant, and detriment, with the overall question being whether it would be unconscionable for the promisor to resile. These elements are linked, so that the assurance must have been made with the claimant relying on it. Written formalities are not required and consideration is not needed. Common intention constructive trusts are a different route, built on contributions to acquisition."}, {"id": 1577, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Walter Pryce died with a will leaving everything to his daughter Eleri. The estate included £120,000 in cash and a house worth £360,000 that Walter owned as beneficial joint tenant with his son Dafydd. Inheritance Tax is due on the whole of the assets, and the will contains no provision about who bears tax. The executors have paid the tax from the cash. Dafydd asks whether he must contribute towards the tax attributable to the house. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "Dafydd bears the tax only if the joint tenancy was severed before death, because severance would make the house pass under the will", "B": "Eleri bears all the tax as residuary beneficiary, because the will is silent and the residue pays the tax on every asset in the estate", "C": "Dafydd bears the tax on the house, recoverable by the executors, because property passing by survivorship does not form part of the residue", "D": "Dafydd bears none of it, because tax on a house must be paid from the cash in the estate before any other source is considered", "E": "The tax is shared between Eleri and Dafydd in proportion to the gross values each receives, because both benefit from the assets"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dafydd bears the tax on the house, recoverable by the executors, because property passing by survivorship does not form part of the residue): By default the residue bears the tax on UK property passing under the will, but property passing by survivorship does not form part of the residue or pass under the will. The burden therefore falls on the survivor who takes it, and the personal representatives, who are liable to HMRC for the tax, can recover it from Dafydd. Eleri's share is not reduced for it. Severance would have changed the route by which the house passed, but it would have been irrelevant to a house that did pass by survivorship."}, {"id": 1578, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Fatima Rahim is arrested on suspicion of fraud by false representation, an indictable offence. She asks the custody officer to tell her sister that she is being held and asks for a solicitor. An inspector authorises delay to both requests, believing that her sister might alert accomplices who have not yet been arrested. No superintendent has been consulted. Which of the following correctly describes the lawfulness of the delays?", "options": {"A": "Neither right can be delayed, because both are unqualified once the suspect has been brought into a police station", "B": "Both delays are valid, because an inspector may authorise delay of either right where the offence is an indictable one and the grounds are met", "C": "The delay to legal advice is valid, but the delay to informing her sister is not, because that needs a superintendent", "D": "Both delays are invalid, because a delay to either right requires the authority of a superintendent or above in every case", "E": "The delay to informing her sister is valid, but the delay to legal advice is not, because only a superintendent can authorise that delay"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The delay to informing her sister is valid, but the delay to legal advice is not, because only a superintendent can authorise that delay): Under PACE section 56, delay to a suspect's right to have someone informed may be authorised by an officer of at least inspector rank in an indictable case where the statutory grounds exist, such as the risk of alerting other suspects. Delay of the right to legal advice under section 58 is different, because it needs the authority of a superintendent. The inspector therefore had power over the first request but not the second. The view that both rights are unqualified is wrong, as the Act allows a short delay for indictable offences on defined grounds."}, {"id": 1579, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Glenmore Engineering Ltd granted Highland Bank plc a fixed charge over its factory. The company owes the bank £450,000. After default, a receiver was appointed under the charge and sold the factory for £600,000. The receiver's properly incurred costs were £20,000. The company has also gone into liquidation with substantial unsecured debts. How should the £600,000 be applied?", "options": {"A": "The bank takes the whole £600,000, because a fixed charge holder is entitled to all proceeds of the asset it holds as security", "B": "The receiver pays the unsecured creditors before the bank, because they are the creditors who will bear the loss on the insolvency", "C": "After the bank is paid, the surplus goes to the company's members, because they own the business and its remaining assets", "D": "The receiver deducts his costs, pays the bank £450,000 with interest, and passes the surplus to the liquidator for the general estate", "E": "The receiver pays preferential creditors first, then the bank, because preferential creditors rank ahead of fixed charge holders on fixed assets"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The receiver deducts his costs, pays the bank £450,000 with interest, and passes the surplus to the liquidator for the general estate): A receiver under a fixed charge pays his own proper costs and then the secured debt with interest from the sale proceeds, and the secured creditor can claim only what it is owed. Any surplus belongs to the company, and as the company is in liquidation it passes to the liquidator to be distributed under the statutory order among creditors, with members last. Preferential creditors rank ahead of a floating charge holder but not ahead of a fixed charge holder, and unsecured creditors cannot reach the proceeds until the secured debt is cleared."}, {"id": 1580, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Winston signed a share transfer form giving 1,000 shares in Kestrel Ltd to Yvonne as a gift and delivered it, with the share certificate, to the company secretary. The directors had not yet considered the registration of the transfer when Winston died. Winston's executors say the gift failed because legal title never passed and Yvonne gave no value. Which of the following best states the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The gift is effective only if Yvonne gave consideration, because a gift of shares is otherwise revocable until registration", "B": "The gift fails, because legal title cannot pass until the register is changed and equity will not assist a volunteer", "C": "The gift is effective in equity, because Winston did everything required of him and equity treats the gift as complete", "D": "The gift is effective, because the executors are bound by an implied promise to complete it on Winston's behalf", "E": "The gift fails, because a gift of shares must be made by deed and a share transfer form is not a deed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The gift is effective in equity, because Winston did everything required of him and equity treats the gift as complete): Under the rule in Re Rose, where a donor has done everything in his power to transfer property, equity treats the gift as complete even though the final act depends on a third party. Winston signed and delivered the transfer form and certificate, and only registration was outstanding. The volunteer rule therefore does not defeat Yvonne. Equity does not require a deed for shares or value from the donee in these circumstances."}, {"id": 1581, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Beatrice transfers £30,000 into the bank account of her brother Cedric and sends him a signed letter. The letter says: 'I hope you will use this money to look after Mum if she ever needs help, and I would like you to give some to the grandchildren if you can manage it.' Beatrice later dies. Their mother's carer claims that Cedric holds the £30,000 on trust for Mum. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Cedric holds the money on trust, because a signed letter satisfies the formalities for a declaration of a trust of money", "B": "Cedric holds the money on trust for Beatrice's estate, because the intended trust failed and a resulting trust arose", "C": "Cedric holds the money on trust for his mother, because the letter shows that Beatrice wanted her mother to benefit from it", "D": "Cedric holds the money on trust for his mother and the grandchildren, because the wording is certain enough about who should benefit", "E": "Cedric holds the money absolutely, because the wording expresses a hope rather than an intention to bind him to an obligation"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Cedric holds the money absolutely, because the wording expresses a hope rather than an intention to bind him to an obligation): Words that merely express a hope or wish are precatory and do not show the settlor intended to impose a binding obligation. Beatrice used soft wording ('I hope', 'if you can manage it') and made an outright transfer into Cedric's own account, so the natural reading is a gift. The beneficiaries would be sufficiently identifiable, but certainty of objects cannot make up for the lack of certainty of intention. A resulting trust is not triggered because the transfer is explained as a gift."}, {"id": 1582, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Delta Marine Ltd granted a floating charge over all its assets to Lender X on 1 March, which X registered on 20 March. On 4 March the company granted a second floating charge over the same assets to Lender Y, which Y registered on 6 March. Y had no knowledge of X's charge and neither charge contains a negative pledge. The company is now insolvent. Which of the following best describes the priority between the two charges?", "options": {"A": "Y ranks ahead of X, because Y had no notice of X's charge and took its own charge in good faith for value", "B": "X and Y rank equally and share the proceeds in proportion to their debts, because both are floating charges over the same assets", "C": "X ranks ahead of Y, because floating charges rank by creation date and X registered within the 21 day period", "D": "Y ranks ahead of X, because Y registered its charge earlier and the order of registration decides priority between floating charges", "E": "X ranks ahead of Y, but only if X gave notice of its charge to Y before Y lent any money to the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (X ranks ahead of Y, because floating charges rank by creation date and X registered within the 21 day period): Competing floating charges over the same assets rank by order of creation, provided each is registered in time. X was created on 1 March and registered on day 19, within 21 days, so it keeps its place ahead of the later charge. The date of registration only matters if a charge is registered late. Lack of notice does not help Y, as the creation order governs and there is no negative pledge on which to base a notice argument."}, {"id": 1583, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Joseph's firm acts for the seller of a house. The nominated officer, Ravi, suspects that the buyer's funds derive from tax evasion and submits an authorised disclosure to the National Crime Agency seeking a defence against money laundering before exchange. Seven working days later, counting from the day after submission, the agency has not replied. The seller is anxious to proceed and the buyer's solicitor is pressing for exchange. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The firm must wait a further thirty-one days, because the moratorium period applies automatically when the agency does not reply in time", "B": "Ravi may treat consent as given once the seven working day notice period ends without a refusal, and the firm can then proceed", "C": "The firm must report again to the Solicitors Regulation Authority, because the agency's silence shows that the first report was defective", "D": "The firm may proceed if the seller signs an indemnity, because the notice period is extended while the matter remains under review", "E": "The firm cannot proceed until the agency gives express written consent, because silence from the agency cannot amount to consent"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ravi may treat consent as given once the seven working day notice period ends without a refusal, and the firm can then proceed): Where an authorised disclosure is made before the act, the firm has a defence if it receives consent or if the seven working day notice period passes without a refusal. The moratorium period of thirty-one days begins only if the agency refuses consent within the notice period, and that has not occurred. Express written consent is therefore not required, and no indemnity from the seller changes the statutory timing. The firm must still avoid tipping off the buyer while it awaits the outcome."}, {"id": 1584, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Orlando is selling the residue of a fifteen year lease of a shop to Sunita. The lease says the tenant may assign only with the landlord's written consent, not to be unreasonably withheld. Contracts, which are not conditional, have been exchanged and completion is fixed for next Friday, but the landlord has not replied to the request made three weeks ago. Sunita's solicitor suggests completing anyway because the delay is unreasonable and consent must be taken as given. What is the best advice?", "options": {"A": "Complete in Orlando's name as trustee for Sunita, because that avoids an assignment and so avoids the need for consent", "B": "Complete, because the landlord can only claim damages for an assignment without consent and cannot forfeit the lease", "C": "Complete, because consent is deemed given after a reasonable time and the landlord's silence is therefore a waiver of the covenant", "D": "Do not complete until consent is given or declared unreasonably withheld, because assigning without consent breaches the covenant", "E": "Complete, because the assignment is valid once the landlord has been told of it in writing within twenty-eight days"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Do not complete until consent is given or declared unreasonably withheld, because assigning without consent breaches the covenant): A landlord has a duty to respond to a consent request within a reasonable time, but silence is not consent, and the statutory duty gives the tenant a remedy in damages or a declaration. An assignment made without consent breaches the covenant and gives the landlord a right to forfeit. Orlando and Sunita should press for a decision or seek a declaration before completing. Holding the lease on trust may itself breach the covenant against parting with possession."}, {"id": 1585, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Priya and Tom run a partnership in equal shares. For the tax year the partnership's adjusted trading profit is £80,000. During the year Priya drew £25,000 and Tom drew £50,000. Priya also earns £30,000 as a part time employee elsewhere. Priya asks how the partnership profit will be taxed on her. Which of the following is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "She is taxed on her £40,000 profit share, whatever she drew, and is personally responsible for her own tax on that share", "B": "She is taxed on half of the combined drawings of both partners, £37,500, because the drawings are shared equally for tax", "C": "The partnership pays income tax on the whole £80,000, and each partner is taxed only on any profit she withdraws", "D": "She is taxed on the £25,000 she drew, because partners are taxed on the profits they actually withdraw during the tax year", "E": "She is taxed on £40,000, but Tom is jointly liable for her tax because partners are jointly liable for each other's tax"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She is taxed on her £40,000 profit share, whatever she drew, and is personally responsible for her own tax on that share): A partnership is not itself taxed on income. Each partner is taxed on their allocated share of the partnership profit, here £40,000, whether or not it was drawn. Each partner is personally responsible for the tax on their own share, so Tom has no liability for Priya's tax, although the partnership files a return showing the split. Her employment income is separate and added to the profit share in working out her total liability."}, {"id": 1586, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Dunmore Cars Ltd claims £28,000 from Pritchard Motors Ltd for a vehicle delivered on 3 February and not paid for. The particulars allege the contract, delivery on 3 February, the price and non-payment. Pritchard's defence states: 'The defendant denies liability and puts the claimant to strict proof.' It says nothing specific about the allegation of delivery. What is the position on that allegation?", "options": {"A": "Delivery is treated as denied, because the court reads a defence as a whole and implies a denial of allegations it does not address", "B": "Pritchard is taken to admit delivery, because a defence must deal with each allegation and a general denial does not do so", "C": "Delivery is in issue unless Dunmore serves a request for further information and Pritchard fails to answer it within the time stated", "D": "Delivery remains in issue, because putting the claimant to strict proof requires every allegation to be proved at trial", "E": "Delivery is treated as not admitted, because a defendant need not plead to allegations of fact said to be within the claimant's knowledge"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pritchard is taken to admit delivery, because a defence must deal with each allegation and a general denial does not do so): A defendant must state which allegations are denied (with reasons and its own version), which it cannot admit or deny but requires the claimant to prove, and which it admits. An allegation that the defence does not deal with is taken to be admitted, unless the defendant has said that it must be proved. A bare statement that liability is denied does not meet the rule for the specific allegation of delivery. Pritchard's tactic of general proof therefore leaves delivery admitted."}, {"id": 1587, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Greta Lindqvist bought a small hotel from Oban Leisure Ltd in 2024, relying on a statement that occupancy averaged 80%, which was untrue. In March 2025 she discovered the true figure of 45%. She carried on running the hotel, took bookings for the summer and bought new furniture in April. In October 2025 she issued a claim for rescission, supported by a letter saying she had always intended to end the deal. Which of the following best describes her right to rescind?", "options": {"A": "She keeps the right, because the misrepresentation was fundamental and equity allows rescission whenever the seller's statement was untrue", "B": "She keeps the right, because a delay of more than twelve months after discovery is required before the court will bar rescission", "C": "She keeps the right, because her letter shows she did not intend to affirm and the court looks to her stated intention", "D": "She has lost the right, because the hotel cannot be returned in the same state as when it was bought", "E": "She has lost the right, because she affirmed the contract by continuing to run the hotel after learning the truth"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (She has lost the right, because she affirmed the contract by continuing to run the hotel after learning the truth): Rescission is lost if the representee, knowing the truth, acts in a way consistent only with continuing the contract. Greta ran the hotel, took summer bookings and bought furniture for months after March, which is affirmation. Her later letter cannot undo conduct that objectively showed she was treating the contract as continuing. Restitution would have been substantially possible, so that reasoning is not why the right is lost."}, {"id": 1588, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Dev is a sole trader who trades from rented premises. His accounts show a net profit of £50,000 for the year, after charging depreciation of £3,000 and £1,500 spent entertaining customers. Dev's capital allowances for the year are £4,000. No other adjustments are needed to arrive at his taxable profit, and personal allowances are to be ignored. What is Dev's taxable trading profit for the year?", "options": {"A": "£47,500, because customer entertaining is added back and the allowances are deducted, but the depreciation stays as a deductible cost", "B": "£50,500, because depreciation and customer entertaining are added back and the capital allowances are then deducted from the adjusted figure", "C": "£54,500, because depreciation and customer entertaining are added back but no deduction is made for the capital allowances to which Dev is entitled", "D": "£58,500, because depreciation, entertaining and capital allowances are all added back to the accounts profit as non deductible items", "E": "£49,000, because depreciation is added back and the allowances are deducted, but the entertaining stays deductible as a business cost"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (£50,500, because depreciation and customer entertaining are added back and the capital allowances are then deducted from the adjusted figure): Taxable trading profit starts from the accounts profit, adds back items not deductible for tax and deducts capital allowances in place of depreciation. Depreciation of £3,000 and customer entertaining of £1,500 are both added back, giving £54,500, and the allowances of £4,000 are then deducted to leave £50,500. Treating either add back as deductible, or forgetting the allowances, produces the other figures. Allowances are a deduction, never an addition, because they replace the accounting charge for wear and tear."}, {"id": 1589, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "For two years Maria has suffered serious violence from her partner Alan. One evening, after Alan told her he would kill her when the children were asleep, Maria waited quietly for two hours, then picked up a kitchen knife and stabbed him fatally while he watched television. Maria says she lost her self-control. She is charged with murder. Which of the following statements best describes whether the delay prevents reliance on loss of control?", "options": {"A": "The delay defeats the defence, because the loss of control must be sudden and temporary, and a two hour gap shows a considered response", "B": "The delay is irrelevant to the defence, because the test is whether Maria's reaction was one a reasonable person would regard as proportionate", "C": "The delay defeats the defence unless Maria can show a fear of immediate violence from Alan at the moment she struck the blow", "D": "The delay does not itself defeat the defence, because the loss of self-control need not be sudden, though it is relevant evidence on whether control was lost", "E": "The delay is irrelevant, because a defendant who has suffered prolonged abuse is entitled to the defence without proving a loss of self-control"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The delay does not itself defeat the defence, because the loss of self-control need not be sudden, though it is relevant evidence on whether control was lost): Section 54(2) of the Coroners and Justice Act 2009 expressly states that the loss of self-control need not be sudden, which departs from the old provocation rule. The fear of serious violence trigger also does not have to relate to immediate violence. The delay is still evidence the jury may weigh when deciding whether Maria genuinely lost control. Abuse history does not remove the need to prove loss of control, and the test of normal tolerance is not one of proportionate response."}, {"id": 1590, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Orla Tierney owns a terraced house with no off-street parking. Her neighbour, Samir Qureshi, owns a house with a large forecourt and, by deed, grants Orla the right to park one car anywhere on the forecourt at any time. Samir later argues that the right is too extensive to be an easement because it takes up space on his land. Samir can still use the forecourt for turning his own vehicle. Which of the following best describes whether the right is capable of being an easement?", "options": {"A": "It cannot be an easement, because a right to park is a right to occupy land and so is a form of possession", "B": "It is an easement because it benefits Orla personally and the grant by deed makes it capable of being legal", "C": "It is an easement only if Orla has also paid for the right, which shows that it benefits her land", "D": "It is capable of being an easement, because it does not leave Samir without reasonable use of the forecourt or amount to exclusive possession", "E": "It cannot be an easement, because a right that takes up space on the servient land is a personal licence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is capable of being an easement, because it does not leave Samir without reasonable use of the forecourt or amount to exclusive possession): A right to park a vehicle can exist as an easement provided that it accommodates the dominant land and does not leave the servient owner without reasonable use of his own land. Samir keeps use of the forecourt, including turning his vehicle, so the grant does not amount to exclusive possession. Taking up some space does not convert the right into a licence. Payment is not required, and a right that benefits only Orla personally would not qualify, but this one benefits her house."}, {"id": 1591, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Hassan Qureshi is charged with rape and has been in custody since his arrest. At his first hearing before the magistrates' court, his solicitor wishes to apply for bail. The prosecutor submits that because rape is an indictable-only offence the magistrates have no power to deal with bail and must simply send the case. The court clerk has a draft case management form ready. Which of the following best describes the magistrates' powers?", "options": {"A": "They must remand him in custody, because bail for rape can be granted only by a Crown Court judge", "B": "They can deal with bail only after holding a committal hearing, because the evidence must be reviewed before bail is decided", "C": "They can grant bail only if the prosecution consents, because indictable-only offences carry a presumption against bail", "D": "They must send the case at once and leave bail to the Crown Court, as they lose jurisdiction once the offence is identified as indictable only", "E": "They can decide the bail application and then send the case to the Crown Court for trial, where bail can be reconsidered"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (They can decide the bail application and then send the case to the Crown Court for trial, where bail can be reconsidered): Magistrates retain the power to hear a bail application at the first hearing, even for an indictable-only offence, and they may then send the case to the Crown Court. If bail is refused, the application can be made again at the Crown Court. The prosecution's consent is not required, and there is no committal hearing. The sending hearing does not strip the magistrates of their ordinary bail functions."}, {"id": 1592, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Kelly Brannigan is tried in the Crown Court for robbery. The only eyewitness, Mr Singh, gave a police statement but now tells the prosecutor he is too frightened to give evidence because of threats, and he will not attend. The police believe the threats are genuine. The prosecution wants to read his statement to the jury, and the defence objects that Kelly's rights will be affected. The judge also learns that Mr Singh intends to move abroad next year. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The statement is admissible without leave, because a statement from a frightened witness is admitted as of right once fear is shown", "B": "The statement is inadmissible, because fear of a witness is not a ground for admitting a statement that is hearsay", "C": "The statement is admissible as a business record, because police created it as part of an official record of the investigation", "D": "The statement is admissible only if Mr Singh also moves abroad, because absence from the United Kingdom is the relevant ground", "E": "The court may give leave if satisfied that his fear means he should not testify, weighing the risk of unfairness to Kelly"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The court may give leave if satisfied that his fear means he should not testify, weighing the risk of unfairness to Kelly): Where a witness does not give oral evidence through fear, a statement may be admitted only with the court's leave. The judge must be satisfied that, in view of the fear, the statement should be admitted, and must consider the statement's contents, the risk of unfairness to the defendant and the possibility of special measures. The later move abroad is not the ground relied on. A police witness statement is not a business document."}, {"id": 1593, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Callum Barker, arrested for burglary, asked for a solicitor on arrival at the police station. The custody officer, with no lawful reason for delay, started the interview within the hour without waiting for the solicitor, who was on her way. In the interview Callum admitted being at the house. He now says he would not have spoken if she had been present. The investigating officer says the admission is reliable because it matches CCTV. Which submission by the defence is most likely to succeed?", "options": {"A": "The confession must be excluded automatically, because any breach of the Codes of Practice by the police makes a confession inadmissible at trial", "B": "The confession can be excluded only if Callum proves he was a vulnerable suspect under the Codes at the time", "C": "The confession is admissible, because its accuracy is confirmed by the CCTV and reliability is judged by whether it is true", "D": "The confession should be excluded under section 76(2)(b), or under section 78, because the denial of legal advice created circumstances likely to make it unreliable", "E": "The confession should be excluded under section 76(2)(a), because denial of a solicitor amounts to oppression of the suspect"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The confession should be excluded under section 76(2)(b), or under section 78, because the denial of legal advice created circumstances likely to make it unreliable): A significant and substantial breach of the right to legal advice can make a confession unreliable under section 76(2)(b), and it also supports a discretionary exclusion for unfairness under section 78. Oppression under the other limb requires something like torture, inhuman or degrading treatment, or violence, which is not shown. A breach alone does not lead to automatic exclusion, and the court looks at likely effect, not at whether the confession matches other evidence."}, {"id": 1594, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Carlton Plastics offered to supply 2,000 casings to Dunmore Ltd on its standard terms, which limit liability to the price paid. Dunmore replied with an order on its own purchase terms, which contain no limit on liability, and stated that its terms alone applied. Carlton did not respond but delivered the casings, and Dunmore accepted and used them. The casings proved defective. Which of the following is the most likely analysis of the terms of the contract?", "options": {"A": "Neither set of terms governs, because the forms conflict and the parties are left with only the terms implied by statute", "B": "Carlton's terms govern, because its quotation was the first document sent and the terms of the first offer prevail in an exchange of forms", "C": "Dunmore's terms govern, because Carlton's silence after receiving the order amounted to acceptance of the terms stated in it", "D": "Carlton's terms govern, because Dunmore accepted the delivery and thereby accepted the terms of the original quotation", "E": "Dunmore's terms govern, because its order was a counter-offer that Carlton accepted by conduct when it delivered the casings"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Dunmore's terms govern, because its order was a counter-offer that Carlton accepted by conduct when it delivered the casings): Dunmore's order stated different terms and so was a counter-offer, which ended Carlton's original offer. Carlton did not reply, but it delivered the goods, and performance in line with the new offer is acceptance by conduct. English law therefore treats the last form in the exchange as governing if the other side performs. Silence alone could not have been acceptance, so the delivery is what matters."}, {"id": 1595, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Yusuf Khan is a solicitor attending Gareth Pike at a police station. Gareth has been arrested for obtaining money by fraud from a customer in March. The police disclosure consists of a single sentence giving the date and the name of the customer, with no documents. In private Gareth tells Yusuf that he did take the money and that he is worried about being asked detailed questions. What is the most appropriate advice for Yusuf to give?", "options": {"A": "Read out a statement denying the allegation, because a denial protects him against any adverse inference at trial", "B": "Answer no comment, having been warned of the risk of an adverse inference, because the thin disclosure prevents a fair response", "C": "Withdraw from acting, because a client's private admission means the solicitor cannot ethically advise silence", "D": "Answer questions about the March transaction but refuse any others, because partial answers carry no risk of an inference being drawn later", "E": "Answer all questions and admit the offence, because a solicitor must not allow a client who has confessed to stay silent"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Answer no comment, having been warned of the risk of an adverse inference, because the thin disclosure prevents a fair response): A solicitor may properly advise a client to give no comment where disclosure is inadequate, and an admission made in private does not oblige the client to confess to the police. The solicitor must explain the possible adverse inference under the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act 1994 so that the decision is informed. The solicitor must not, however, put forward or assist a false denial, as that would mislead the police and breach the duty of honesty. Withdrawal is not required because advising silence is not misleading."}, {"id": 1596, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Gareth Morland, an elderly client, paid £90,000 to a sole practitioner, Helen Frost, to hold pending a house purchase. She took the money for herself and has since been declared bankrupt, and her practice has been closed by the SRA. Her insurers have confirmed that no qualifying policy responds to the claim. Gareth has no other means of recovering the money. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Gareth may apply for a discretionary grant from the SRA Compensation Fund, a last resort where no other redress is available", "B": "Gareth should apply to the Legal Ombudsman, which can order payment from the Compensation Fund once it has found the service inadequate", "C": "Gareth cannot recover anything, because the Fund does not meet losses where the solicitor has been declared bankrupt", "D": "Gareth has a right to full reimbursement from the Compensation Fund, because the Fund ensures repayment of any client money lost through a solicitor's dishonesty", "E": "Gareth's only route is a claim against the Law Society as the body that issued Helen's practising certificate each year"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gareth may apply for a discretionary grant from the SRA Compensation Fund, a last resort where no other redress is available): The Compensation Fund can make grants where a client has suffered loss through a solicitor's dishonesty or failure to account and has no other source of redress. Insurance has been ruled out and Helen is bankrupt, so this is exactly the situation the Fund exists for. Grants are discretionary rather than a right, so full reimbursement cannot be promised. The Legal Ombudsman handles service complaints under its own scheme, and the Law Society is not liable for a solicitor's theft."}, {"id": 1597, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Joanna Whitlock instructs a solicitor to act on her purchase of a flat for £280,000, funded by a mortgage from a high street lender. The solicitor acts for both Joanna and the lender. The title shows the flat is held on a lease with 62 years left, but the lender's instructions require a lease of at least 85 years. Joanna asks the solicitor to report only that the title is good and marketable and to omit the remaining term, saying she will extend the lease later. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Complete the purchase and report the term after completion, because the lender can rely on a later notification once funds are released", "B": "Omit the remaining term from the report, because Joanna as the borrowing client is entitled to decide what the lender is told", "C": "Report the remaining term to the lender without telling Joanna, because the duty to the lender overrides the duty owed to a borrower", "D": "Report that title is good and marketable, as the length of the lease is a matter between Joanna and the lender rather than for the solicitor", "E": "Refuse to omit the term, explain that the lender must be told, and stop acting for the lender if she will not agree to disclosure"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Refuse to omit the term, explain that the lender must be told, and stop acting for the lender if she will not agree to disclosure): Where a solicitor acts for a borrower and a lender, information material to the lender's decision, such as a short lease contrary to its requirements, must be disclosed to the lender. Under the SRA Code of Conduct the solicitor must not mislead the lender, and must not withhold the information because the buyer prefers silence. The solicitor should explain this to Joanna and, if she will not agree to disclosure, must stop acting for the lender because a conflict arises. Waiting until after completion would defeat the lender's ability to lend on informed terms."}, {"id": 1598, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Juniper Tiles Ltd is a small private company with five shareholders. Its directors have approved the accounts for the year and their report, and one director has signed the balance sheet. The company has no auditor because it qualifies for the small company audit exemption. A new shareholder asks whether the directors must hold an annual general meeting to present the accounts. What is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "No annual general meeting is needed for a private company, but the accounts must be sent to the members and delivered to the Registrar", "B": "No annual general meeting is required, but the members must approve the accounts by ordinary resolution within nine months of the year end", "C": "No annual general meeting is required, and the accounts need not be sent to members because only the Registrar must receive them", "D": "An annual general meeting must be held within six months of the year end, at which the accounts are laid before the members", "E": "An annual general meeting must be held unless every member signs a written resolution dispensing with it for that financial year"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No annual general meeting is needed for a private company, but the accounts must be sent to the members and delivered to the Registrar): Since the Companies Act 2006, private companies are not required to hold an annual general meeting unless their articles say so. They must still send copies of the accounts and reports to every member and deliver them to the Registrar within nine months of the year end. Members do not approve the accounts by resolution, and there is no need for a written resolution to dispense with a meeting that is not required."}, {"id": 1599, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "A council contractor negligently drives a piling rig into the Marlow Bridge, damaging it so badly that the council closes the bridge for three months. Tomlin Haulage operates a delivery business from a depot next to the bridge. Because of the closure its lorries must take a 40 mile detour, costing it £75,000 in additional fuel and lost deliveries. Tomlin Haulage owns no part of the bridge and suffered no damage to its own property. It sues the contractor in negligence. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim succeeds, because the claimant used the bridge regularly and so was in sufficient proximity to the damaged structure", "B": "The claim fails, because the council is the only party that can sue where public property is damaged", "C": "The claim fails, because the loss is pure economic loss that does not result from damage to any property the claimant owns", "D": "The claim succeeds, because the contractor could reasonably foresee that closing the bridge would cause losses to nearby businesses", "E": "The claim succeeds, because the contractor assumed responsibility to road users in the way a professional adviser does"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The claim fails, because the loss is pure economic loss that does not result from damage to any property the claimant owns): Tomlin Haulage's loss, extra costs and lost deliveries, is relational pure economic loss: it flows from damage to someone else's property. The general rule is that such loss is not recoverable in negligence, however foreseeable. There is no assumption of responsibility by the contractor to Tomlin Haulage. The council, as owner of the bridge, may recover for the physical damage, but the haulage firm cannot recover its consequential commercial loss."}, {"id": 1600, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Rosalind holds 60% of the shares in Fenner Ltd, a private company with three directors. She wants to remove a director, Ahmed, who was appointed by the board. She proposes to circulate a written resolution to all members to remove him, because she holds a majority and wants to avoid holding a meeting. Ahmed has a service contract, and the articles contain no special provision on removal. Can Rosalind remove Ahmed in this way?", "options": {"A": "No, because removal under section 168 requires an ordinary resolution at a general meeting on special notice, and a written resolution cannot be used", "B": "No, because a director can be removed only by a special resolution passed by at least 75% of the votes cast", "C": "Yes, provided Ahmed also consents in writing, because a director cannot be removed against his will by written procedure", "D": "No, because a director appointed by the board can be removed only by the board and not by the members", "E": "Yes, because a written resolution signed by members holding more than 50% of the voting rights is an ordinary resolution, and Rosalind alone holds 60% of them"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No, because removal under section 168 requires an ordinary resolution at a general meeting on special notice, and a written resolution cannot be used): Section 168 lets members remove a director by ordinary resolution at a meeting, with special notice. The Companies Act 2006 excludes the written resolution procedure for removing a director, so Rosalind must hold a meeting and give Ahmed the chance to make representations. A simple majority is enough, so the 75% test is not relevant. The members' power does not depend on who appointed the director."}, {"id": 1601, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Lyndhurst Holdings owns a freehold building. Rowan Ltd holds a lease of it until 2035 and has underlet the first floor to Mina Sethi until 2029 under a lease granted in 2024. Lyndhurst Holdings and Rowan Ltd now agree by deed to surrender the head lease so that Lyndhurst can redevelop, and Lyndhurst assumes it will obtain vacant possession of the first floor. Rowan Ltd was paid £25,000 for the surrender. Which of the following best describes the effect on Mina Sethi?", "options": {"A": "Her underlease continues only if she consents to the surrender, since the surrender otherwise has no effect on her", "B": "Her underlease ends, but Lyndhurst Holdings must pay her compensation equal to the value of her remaining term", "C": "Her underlease continues with Lyndhurst Holdings as her landlord, because a surrender of the head lease cannot end it", "D": "Her underlease continues, but only until the next rent review, when Lyndhurst Holdings may serve notice to end it", "E": "Her underlease ends with the head lease, because a subtenant's estate depends on the tenant's lease continuing"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Her underlease continues with Lyndhurst Holdings as her landlord, because a surrender of the head lease cannot end it): A tenant can surrender its own lease to the landlord, but cannot by doing so prejudice the rights of its subtenant. Lyndhurst Holdings therefore takes the reversion subject to Mina Sethi's underlease and becomes her landlord for the rest of her term. Her consent is not required for the surrender to be effective between Rowan Ltd and Lyndhurst. If Lyndhurst wants vacant possession it must end her tenancy by a route available under her own lease or statute, not through the surrender."}, {"id": 1602, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Willow Ltd leases a workshop from Barnes. The lease contains an option for Willow to renew for five years 'at a rent to be agreed between the parties'. There is no valuation mechanism, arbitration clause or formula for fixing the rent. Willow validly gives notice exercising the option, but Barnes proposes a rent Willow rejects and the parties cannot agree. Willow asks the court to enforce the renewal. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The option is enforceable, because Willow has exercised it and the exercise of an option converts it into a concluded renewal lease", "B": "The option is unenforceable for uncertainty, because the rent is left to future agreement and no machinery exists to fix it", "C": "The option is enforceable, because Barnes is under an implied duty to negotiate in good faith and must therefore offer a fair rent", "D": "The option is enforceable, because a court will fill the gap by inserting a reasonable rent where parties leave the price to be agreed", "E": "The option is enforceable at the current rent, because the parties' previous dealings fix the price of the renewal by implication"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The option is unenforceable for uncertainty, because the rent is left to future agreement and no machinery exists to fix it): An agreement to agree a key term, such as the rent, is not enforceable where there is no objective method of fixing it. The court cannot supply a rent for a lease in the way it can for a sale of goods, where statute provides a reasonable price. Exercising the option does not cure the gap, and English law imposes no duty to negotiate in good faith. Willow therefore has no enforceable right to renew on these words."}, {"id": 1603, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Joe Patel is interviewed under caution on suspicion of theft, with his solicitor, Ms Hughes, present. After Joe says no comment, the officer repeats the same question four times in a raised voice. Ms Hughes says the questioning is oppressive and asks for a private consultation with Joe. The officer says that she is interfering and that he will have her removed from the interview. Which of the following correctly states the position?", "options": {"A": "She cannot be removed once the interview has started, because Joe's right to legal advice continues throughout questioning", "B": "She can be removed only by a custody officer, whose decision is final and cannot be reviewed by a court", "C": "She can be removed immediately, because the interviewing officer alone controls how an interview under caution is conducted", "D": "She can be removed if she advises silence, because advice to give no comment hinders the investigation of the offence", "E": "She can be removed only if her conduct prevents proper questioning, and a senior officer, not the interviewer alone, decides"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (She can be removed only if her conduct prevents proper questioning, and a senior officer, not the interviewer alone, decides): Under Code C a solicitor may intervene in an interview to seek clarification, challenge an improper question or the manner of questioning, and advise the client. A solicitor can be required to leave only where the conduct is such that the interviewer cannot properly put questions to the suspect, and the decision should involve a senior officer rather than the interviewer alone. Advising silence is not a ground for removal. Repeating one question in a raised voice is the kind of conduct a solicitor is entitled to challenge."}, {"id": 1604, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "A trust instrument says that the trustees are not liable for any loss to the trust fund unless it is caused by their own actual fraud. The trustees invest the whole fund in a single company in a way that a prudent trustee would regard as grossly negligent, but without any dishonesty. The company fails and the beneficiaries sue. Which of the following best states the position?", "options": {"A": "The clause protects the trustees only if the beneficiaries were told of it during the settlor's lifetime", "B": "The clause protects the trustees even for breaches of the duty to act honestly in the beneficiaries' interests, if it is clearly worded", "C": "The clause is valid only if it was drafted by an independent solicitor and not by one of the trustees", "D": "The clause protects the trustees, because liability even for gross negligence can be excluded provided there is no dishonesty", "E": "The clause is void, because trustees cannot exclude their liability for gross negligence in the management of trust funds"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The clause protects the trustees, because liability even for gross negligence can be excluded provided there is no dishonesty): An exemption clause can lawfully exclude liability for negligence, even gross negligence, as Armitage v Nurse confirms, but it cannot exclude liability for dishonest breach of trust. The trustees here were careless rather than dishonest, so the clause protects them. There is no requirement that beneficiaries be told or that independent drafting be used. The irreducible core of honesty and good faith remains outside any exemption."}, {"id": 1605, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Kofi, aged 38, pleads not guilty to a charge of handling stolen goods and gives evidence at his trial in the Crown Court. He has no previous convictions or cautions of any kind, and the evidence contains nothing else about his character. His counsel asks the judge to give a good character direction in the summing up. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Both limbs apply: his good character is relevant to his credibility and to his propensity to commit the offence charged", "B": "The judge should direct on credibility only, because Kofi gave evidence and propensity is irrelevant where the defendant testifies", "C": "The judge should give no direction unless the prosecution first accepts that his good character is established", "D": "The judge has a discretion whether to give any direction, and a decision to give none could not found an appeal", "E": "The judge should direct on propensity only, because Kofi's credibility is a matter for the jury without help from the judge"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Both limbs apply: his good character is relevant to his credibility and to his propensity to commit the offence charged): A defendant of good character, meaning no convictions or cautions, is entitled to a direction on both limbs under R v Vye and Hunter. The first limb says his good character supports his credibility when he gave evidence, and the second that it makes him less likely to have committed the offence. Giving only one limb is a misdirection. The direction does not depend on prosecution consent, and a failure to give it can ground an appeal."}, {"id": 1606, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Pat is lawfully arrested by Police Constable Hale for shoplifting. While being handcuffed, Pat slashes the officer's hand with a door key, causing a deep cut that bleeds heavily. Pat says he did not want to cause serious harm and only wanted to get away. The cut is a wound but is not grievous bodily harm. Pat is charged under section 18 Offences Against the Person Act 1861. Is Pat guilty?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because he was reckless as to causing serious injury by using a sharp object on a police officer", "B": "Yes, because wounding with intent to resist lawful apprehension is covered, and an intent to cause grievous harm is not needed", "C": "No, because section 18 requires an intention to cause grievous bodily harm, which Pat has expressly denied having when questioned", "D": "No, because the officer's injury is not grievous bodily harm, so the offence cannot be established on these facts", "E": "No, because resisting arrest is a separate offence and section 18 is reserved for assaults on civilians"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Yes, because wounding with intent to resist lawful apprehension is covered, and an intent to cause grievous harm is not needed): Section 18 covers wounding or causing grievous bodily harm with intent to cause grievous bodily harm or with intent to resist or prevent lawful apprehension. A wound suffices where the intent is to resist arrest, so Pat's lack of intent to cause serious harm is no answer. Recklessness is not the fault element. Nothing in the section confines it to assaults on civilians."}, {"id": 1607, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Rahul Mehta owns a detached house in a street where the council made an Article 4 direction two years ago withdrawing the permitted development right for single storey rear extensions. He plans a single storey rear extension 3 metres deep, within the normal size limits of that right. Two neighbours extended without applying for planning permission before the direction took effect, and Rahul says that shows none is needed. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Planning permission is needed only if the extension is deeper than 4 metres, which is the limit for a detached house", "B": "Planning permission is not needed, because a 3 metre single storey rear extension is within the size limit of the permitted development right", "C": "Planning permission is not needed, because neighbouring extensions built without consent set a precedent that creates a right for Rahul", "D": "Planning permission is needed, because the direction withdrew the permitted development right and an extension is development requiring consent", "E": "Planning permission is not needed, provided approval under the building regulations is obtained before the extension is started and the council is notified"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Planning permission is needed, because the direction withdrew the permitted development right and an extension is development requiring consent): Permitted development rights grant planning permission automatically, but a local planning authority can withdraw them for an area by an Article 4 direction. Once the direction has taken effect, the extension is ordinary development and needs express permission, even though its size would have fitted the right. Neighbours' earlier extensions were either built before the direction or without consent, and in neither case do they give Rahul a right. Building regulations approval is a separate regime and cannot replace planning permission."}, {"id": 1608, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Bethan, the sole director of Alder Ltd and its majority shareholder. Last year the company lent her £40,000 without member approval. She now asks you to prepare minutes and a members' resolution dated last March recording that the members approved the loan in advance, although no meeting or vote took place. She says the other two members would have agreed. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Prepare the documents but mark them as approximate in date, because this removes any risk of misleading others", "B": "Report the request to Companies House at once, because the duty to the public overrides client confidentiality", "C": "Decline to prepare the backdated records because they would mislead, and advise on lawful ways to seek the members' approval now", "D": "Prepare the documents, because Bethan controls the company and is entitled to instruct the firm on how the company's records are kept", "E": "Prepare them only if Bethan gives the firm a written indemnity covering any consequences of the backdating"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decline to prepare the backdated records because they would mislead, and advise on lawful ways to seek the members' approval now): A solicitor must act with honesty and integrity and must not mislead others, so false records dated before the event cannot be prepared. The firm should decline and advise on proper options, such as seeking approval now or ratification. An indemnity or a vague date does not remove the dishonesty. Reporting to Companies House is not required here, since nothing false has been filed and confidentiality still applies."}, {"id": 1609, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A firm acts for Sable Developments Ltd, which is granting leases of the units in a new retail park. A prospective tenant, Jaswinder, is a sole trader with no solicitor and has asked the firm to act for her too on the lease of unit four, as it knows the document best and would charge her a reduced fee. Jaswinder and the landlord are still negotiating the rent and the repairing obligations. What should the firm do?", "options": {"A": "Decline to act for Jaswinder, because the negotiation of terms creates a conflict of interests and no exception applies", "B": "Act for both but advise Jaswinder to seek independent advice, because a warning about the conflict is a sufficient safeguard", "C": "Act for both with their written informed consent, because the informed consent of both clients removes a conflict of interests", "D": "Act for Jaswinder after the lease terms are settled, because the conflict ends once the main terms have been agreed", "E": "Act for both using a separate team and an information barrier, because that safeguard permits acting where interests conflict"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to act for Jaswinder, because the negotiation of terms creates a conflict of interests and no exception applies): A solicitor must not act for two clients in the same matter where their interests conflict or there is a significant risk of conflict, unless a narrow exception applies. Landlord and tenant are negotiating against each other on rent and repair, so the exception for a substantially common interest or competing for the same objective does not fit. Consent and an information barrier cannot cure a genuine negotiating conflict. The firm should decline and suggest that Jaswinder obtains independent advice."}, {"id": 1610, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Eastgate Mortgages repossessed Lucy's house after serious arrears and sold it to a developer at an auction, after two weeks, for £180,000. The lender did not advertise beyond a single notice in a local paper and did not commission its own valuation. A valuation obtained by Lucy shows that the house was worth £260,000 on the day of sale. The sale produced £180,000 against a debt of £210,000. Lucy wants to challenge the sale. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The lender is free to sell at a price of its own choosing, because the power of sale exists to protect its own interests", "B": "The lender breached its duty to take reasonable care to obtain the true market value, so Lucy can claim compensation", "C": "The lender is liable only if it acted in bad faith, which Lucy must prove with evidence of improper motive", "D": "Lucy can have the sale set aside against the developer, because the lender sold at a substantial undervalue", "E": "The lender need only obtain a price at which it is satisfied, and an auction sale is conclusive evidence of the proper price"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The lender breached its duty to take reasonable care to obtain the true market value, so Lucy can claim compensation): A lender selling under its power of sale owes a duty to the mortgagor to take reasonable care to obtain the true market value. Selling after two weeks with minimal advertising and no valuation, at about 70 percent of the value, is strong evidence of breach, and Lucy can claim the shortfall as compensation from the lender. Bad faith is not required. A purchaser is protected from challenge under the Law of Property Act 1925, so Lucy's remedy lies against the lender and not the developer."}, {"id": 1611, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Rick parked in an automatic car park run by Goswell Parking. He took a ticket at the barrier which stated 'issued subject to conditions displayed inside'. Inside the building, a notice stated that the operator was not liable for damage to vehicles. A barrier arm later damaged Rick's car. He had used another Goswell car park once before and had not read the notice. Which of the following is the best advice on whether the notice binds him?", "options": {"A": "The notice does not bind him, because an exclusion can bind only if the customer has signed a document containing it", "B": "The notice binds him, because he had used another Goswell car park previously and so is taken to have known its usual terms", "C": "The notice binds him, because a customer who enters premises is taken to accept any terms displayed there", "D": "The notice does not bind him, because the contract was made when the ticket was taken at the barrier, before the notice could be seen", "E": "The notice binds him, because the ticket referred to conditions displayed inside and that gave him reasonable notice of them"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The notice does not bind him, because the contract was made when the ticket was taken at the barrier, before the notice could be seen): A term is part of the contract only if reasonable notice of it is given before or at the time the contract is made. Here the contract was formed at the barrier when Rick took the ticket, and the notice inside came afterwards. One earlier visit to a different car park is not a consistent course of dealing. A signature is not required for incorporation by notice."}, {"id": 1612, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Giles is a solicitor and one of three trustees of a family trust. He also acts as solicitor to the trustees and advises the two lay trustees that an investment in an unlisted company is authorised by the trust instrument. Giles privately doubts this but does not say so. The lay trustees rely on his advice and the trust loses £80,000. The beneficiaries recover the loss from all three trustees. Which of the following best describes the lay trustees' position against Giles?", "options": {"A": "They can claim an indemnity only if the trust instrument expressly allowed trustees to rely on legal advice", "B": "They can claim an indemnity only if Giles was paid for his legal advice as the solicitor to the trust", "C": "They have no claim against him, because taking professional advice is a complete defence for the lay trustees as well as for Giles himself", "D": "They can claim an indemnity from Giles, the solicitor trustee, because they relied on his advice and he controlled the decision", "E": "They can recover only an equal share from him, since the court divides loss by the number of trustees in every case"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (They can claim an indemnity from Giles, the solicitor trustee, because they relied on his advice and he controlled the decision): Where one trustee is a solicitor and the co-trustees rely on his advice, so that he has a controlling influence over the breach, the court may require him to indemnify them in full. Giles also failed in his professional duty to give honest, competent advice and tell clients what he knew. Contribution under the 1978 Act is a just and equitable share, not an equal one. Reliance on advice does not itself excuse the lay trustees."}, {"id": 1613, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "The Safe Play Areas Act prohibits operating a power tool 'in a public place' within 50 metres of a school. It was passed after a child was injured by debris from a contractor's strimmer on the pavement beside a school. Jenny Okafor, a landscaper, was operating a petrol hedge trimmer in a private car park next to a school, which is open to the public free of charge. She is prosecuted, and her lawyer argues the car park is private property. Applying the mischief rule, what is the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The car park is not a public place, because it is privately owned and the owner's rights are not restricted by the Act", "B": "The question cannot be decided, because the Act is ambiguous and the court must wait for Parliament to clarify the term", "C": "The car park is a public place, because a court should prefer the broadest meaning of the words in order to protect children", "D": "The car park is not a public place, because the words must be given their ordinary meaning and privately owned land is outside its scope in ordinary usage", "E": "The car park is probably a public place, because that reading suppresses the danger to children that the Act was passed to remedy"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The car park is probably a public place, because that reading suppresses the danger to children that the Act was passed to remedy): Under the mischief rule the court asks what defect in the previous law Parliament was dealing with and chooses the meaning that remedies it. The Act followed a child being hurt by flying debris near a school, and a car park open to the public next to a school poses exactly that risk. Ownership is not the point, since the danger is the same on public or private land. Reading the term narrowly would leave the mischief unaddressed, which the rule is intended to avoid."}, {"id": 1614, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Gwilym Rees is the defendant in a business debt claim to be tried in the county court at Newport. He is fluent in English but wants to give evidence in Welsh. His opponent's solicitor writes that this will cause delay and cost and suggests Gwilym should use English and bear any interpreter cost if he insists on Welsh. Trial is in eight weeks. Gwilym asks for advice. What should his solicitor advise?", "options": {"A": "Gwilym should use English, because a party who is fluent in English has no entitlement to use Welsh when the other side objects", "B": "Gwilym may use Welsh but should expect an adverse costs order against him, because the choice makes the hearing longer", "C": "Gwilym may use Welsh if the court decides that the extra delay and cost are proportionate to the claim's value", "D": "Gwilym may use Welsh, should tell the court promptly so that arrangements can be made, and cannot be disadvantaged by his choice", "E": "Gwilym may use Welsh but must bear the interpreter's cost, because he alone benefits from the choice of language and the delay"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gwilym may use Welsh, should tell the court promptly so that arrangements can be made, and cannot be disadvantaged by his choice): A person in proceedings in Wales may speak Welsh whether or not he also speaks English, and the court policy is equality between the two languages. Notice must be given in good time so that the court can arrange interpretation, which the court provides. The opponent's concern about delay can be met by early notice, and no party is to be treated less favourably for using Welsh. Fluency in English, objection by the opponent and extra cost do not defeat the right."}, {"id": 1615, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Bank plc agreed to lend Nia £200,000 secured by a first charge over her registered freehold house. Nia signed a deed of charge in the bank's standard form on 1 March and the bank advanced the money the same day. Through an administrative error, the bank did not apply to register the charge at HM Land Registry until the following month. On 15 March a creditor of Nia asks what interest the bank held at that date. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "The bank held only an equitable charge, because a legal charge of registered land is not created until it is completed by registration", "B": "The bank held an overriding interest, because its charge was created by deed and the bank had lent the money", "C": "The bank held no interest in the property, because a deed of charge has no effect in equity until it is registered", "D": "The bank held a legal charge from 1 March, because the deed was validly executed and delivered and the money had been advanced", "E": "The bank held a legal charge from 1 March, because a charge of registered land takes effect when delivered, with registration relating back"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The bank held only an equitable charge, because a legal charge of registered land is not created until it is completed by registration): For registered land a legal charge must be made by deed and then completed by registration under the Land Registration Act 2002. Until registration the charge takes effect in equity only, so on 15 March the bank had an equitable charge. The deed and the advance are enough to create an equitable interest, so the charge is not void. There is no relation back, and an unregistered charge does not qualify as an overriding interest."}, {"id": 1616, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Imogen's solicitor is acting on her purchase of a registered title, with exchange agreed and completion fixed for three weeks later. The solicitor wants to protect her against any entry made on the register after the search but before her own application to register is lodged. Which of the following describes the correct step and its effect?", "options": {"A": "Apply for an official search with priority, which protects her application for registration if it is lodged within thirty business days", "B": "Make an official search without priority, which protects her against all later entries for thirty business days from the search certificate", "C": "Make a local land charges search, which fixes her priority on the register for the period until the date of completion", "D": "Apply for an official search with priority, which protects her application for registration if it is lodged within fifteen business days", "E": "Apply to enter an agreed notice on the register, which protects any later application for registration without a fixed time limit"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Apply for an official search with priority, which protects her application for registration if it is lodged within thirty business days): An official search with priority gives the buyer a thirty business day protection period during which her application to register takes priority over any entry made after the search. A search without priority only reports the register and gives no protection. The period is thirty business days, not fifteen. A local land charges search deals with a different register and has no effect on priority at the Land Registry."}, {"id": 1617, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Lewis and Maxine Hart hold their family home, worth £420,000 with no mortgage, as tenants in common in equal shares. Lewis was declared bankrupt 18 months ago, and his trustee in bankruptcy now applies under section 14 of the Trusts of Land and Appointment of Trustees Act 1996 for an order for sale to pay Lewis's creditors, who are owed £90,000. Maxine and their teenage son live in the house, and Maxine says she needs time to find alternative housing. Which of the following best describes how the court will approach the application?", "options": {"A": "Sale will be ordered only if the trustee shows that Maxine has acted unreasonably in remaining in the property", "B": "Sale will be ordered immediately for any trustee in bankruptcy, because the court has no discretion once bankruptcy has occurred", "C": "Sale will be refused, because the welfare of a teenage child living in the home outweighs the interests of creditors", "D": "Sale is likely, because after one year section 335A of the Insolvency Act 1986 favours creditors unless circumstances are exceptional", "E": "Sale will be postponed until the son finishes his schooling, because the court must apply the usual section 15 balancing exercise"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sale is likely, because after one year section 335A of the Insolvency Act 1986 favours creditors unless circumstances are exceptional): Where a trustee in bankruptcy applies for sale of a bankrupt's co-owned home, section 335A of the Insolvency Act 1986 applies and replaces the ordinary section 15 balance. After one year from the first vesting of the bankrupt's estate in the trustee, the court must assume that creditors' interests outweigh all other considerations unless the circumstances are exceptional. Maxine's need to rehouse and her son's welfare are the ordinary consequences of sale, and not exceptional. The court therefore will probably order sale, though it may allow a short period."}, {"id": 1618, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Amit Rao completed the purchase of an unregistered freehold bungalow on 12 May, when the seller transferred the property to him by conveyance. Amit paid in cash and no lender is involved. The property has never been registered, and Amit's solicitor has not yet applied to HM Land Registry. By what date must the application for first registration be made to avoid the legal estate reverting to the seller?", "options": {"A": "12 July, because the application is due within two months of completion of a sale of unregistered land", "B": "12 November, because the period allowed for first registration is six months from completion of the sale", "C": "12 May the following year, because the period runs for twelve months from the date of the conveyance", "D": "No date applies, because the legal estate stays with the buyer until the buyer chooses to apply for registration", "E": "12 June, because first registration must be applied for within one month of the date of the conveyance"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (12 July, because the application is due within two months of completion of a sale of unregistered land): A conveyance on sale of unregistered freehold land triggers compulsory registration, and the buyer must apply within two months of completion. If the time passes without an application the transfer becomes void as to the legal estate and the seller holds it on trust for the buyer. Here the date is 12 July. A longer period or no deadline would leave the buyer holding only an equitable title, which is exactly what the rule is designed to prevent."}, {"id": 1619, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Delmar Garden Centre has a children's play area beside a display of shrubs. One shrub carries bright red berries that are poisonous, and there is no fence or sign. Five-year-old Joel, whose mother was a few metres away tying his sister's shoe for under a minute, picked and ate some berries and became seriously ill. His mother sues on his behalf under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails, because Joel's mother was responsible for supervising a five-year-old and her brief inattention breaks the chain of causation", "B": "The claim fails, because Joel left the play area, so he exceeded the permission given and became a trespasser at the shrub display", "C": "The claim fails, because poisonous plants are an obvious danger that a visitor is expected to appreciate and guard against", "D": "The claim is likely to succeed, because occupiers must expect children to be less careful and bright berries beside a play area attract them", "E": "The claim succeeds only if the garden centre planted the shrub deliberately to attract children, because the duty depends on intent"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The claim is likely to succeed, because occupiers must expect children to be less careful and bright berries beside a play area attract them): The common duty of care is owed to all lawful visitors, and the occupier must be prepared for children to be less careful than adults. Poisonous berries in a bright, tempting form next to a play area are the kind of allurement that makes harm to a young child foreseeable. A parent's momentary lapse does not remove the occupier's responsibility, and the part of the garden centre open to customers falls within the permission to enter. Intention to attract children is not required, as the test is whether reasonable care was taken for the visitor."}, {"id": 1620, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "The trustees of a £2 million family trust have a general power of investment. A trustee with strong moral views persuades the others to refuse to invest in tobacco companies. Professional advice states that excluding tobacco would reduce expected returns noticeably and increase risk because of a narrower spread of investments. A beneficiary objects to the exclusion. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The trustees must put the beneficiaries' financial interests first, so may exclude tobacco only if there is no significant financial loss", "B": "The trustees must invest in tobacco companies, because the duty to maximise return forbids any form of ethical screening of the fund's investments", "C": "The trustees may exclude tobacco freely, because trustees are entitled to let personal moral views take priority over financial return", "D": "The trustees may exclude tobacco only if every beneficiary, including any minors, agrees to it in writing", "E": "The trustees may exclude tobacco where the trust instrument is silent and a majority of them hold the same view"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The trustees must put the beneficiaries' financial interests first, so may exclude tobacco only if there is no significant financial loss): Trustees must act in the best financial interests of the beneficiaries, as Cowan v Scargill makes clear, and personal moral views cannot override that duty. Harries v Church Commissioners accepts that trustees may exclude investments on ethical grounds where there is no significant risk of financial detriment. Here the advice shows detriment, so the exclusion is not justified. Beneficiary consent or a trustee majority does not change the analysis."}, {"id": 1621, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Goldie Pharma employs Mr Anwar for six years before dismissing him for misconduct. When a prospective new employer asks for a reference, the HR manager writes that Mr Anwar was dismissed for dishonesty. She did not check the personnel file, which showed the allegation was unfounded. The new employer withdraws a job offer, and Mr Anwar suffers a financial loss. He sues Goldie Pharma in negligence. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Goldie Pharma owes Mr Anwar a duty to take reasonable care that the reference is true, accurate and fair", "B": "Goldie Pharma owes a duty only if the reference was given in writing and signed by a director of the company", "C": "Goldie Pharma owes a duty, but the claim fails because a reference is protected from challenge as a statement of opinion", "D": "Goldie Pharma owes a duty only to the recipient of the reference, because the recipient alone relies on its contents", "E": "Goldie Pharma owes no duty, because loss of a job opportunity is pure economic loss with no exception in this context"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Goldie Pharma owes Mr Anwar a duty to take reasonable care that the reference is true, accurate and fair): A former employer who gives a reference assumes responsibility to the subject as well as to the recipient, and must take reasonable care to ensure it is true, accurate and fair. Negligent misstatement is an exception to the bar on pure economic loss, and the employee's reliance on the employer's care is plain. The duty is not confined to the recipient and does not depend on formalities. A reference stating a factual allegation without checking it is not protected as opinion."}, {"id": 1622, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Lowther District Council discovers that five years ago Tomasz Kowal converted a barn on his farm into a separate dwelling without planning permission. The barn has been used continuously as a single dwellinghouse since the conversion, with no change of occupier. The council wishes to serve an enforcement notice and asks whether it is still entitled to do so. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "It can, because the time limit starts only on the date the council discovers the breach and not on the date the use began", "B": "It can, because the four year limit applies only to building operations and not to a change of use of the land", "C": "It can, because enforcement against a change of use to a dwelling is possible within ten years of the breach", "D": "It cannot, because a one year limit applies once the council has failed to act after the dwelling was first occupied", "E": "It cannot, because a change of use to a single dwellinghouse becomes immune from enforcement after four years of continuous use"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It can, because enforcement against a change of use to a dwelling is possible within ten years of the breach): Under section 171B of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 as amended by the Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023, a single ten year period has applied in England since 25 April 2024. A conversion made five years ago had not become immune under the old four year rule before that date, so it falls under the ten year period and the council may still serve an enforcement notice. The council need not have discovered the breach for time to run, and the limit is not confined to building operations."}, {"id": 1623, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A commercial lease granted in 2021 requires the tenant to obtain the landlord's consent to assignment, and says the landlord may require an outgoing tenant to enter into an authorised guarantee agreement. The tenant, Oakridge Ltd, now wishes to assign to Pellam Ltd. The landlord consents on condition that Oakridge Ltd guarantees the assignee's obligations. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The landlord may lawfully require the guarantee, which ends when Pellam Ltd itself lawfully assigns the lease to a later tenant", "B": "The condition is valid, but Oakridge Ltd stays liable only for the rent and not for other covenants such as repair", "C": "The condition is void, because on assignment a tenant under a post-1995 lease is released and no guarantee may then be required", "D": "The condition is unlawful unless Oakridge Ltd has previously defaulted, because the 1995 Act limits guarantees to tenants who have defaulted", "E": "The guarantee binds Oakridge Ltd for the remainder of the contractual term, even if Pellam Ltd later assigns the lease"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The landlord may lawfully require the guarantee, which ends when Pellam Ltd itself lawfully assigns the lease to a later tenant): Under the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 an outgoing tenant is released on assignment, but the lease may require an authorised guarantee agreement as a condition of consent. Here the lease expressly provides for it, so the condition is lawful. The guarantee covers the assignee's obligations but ends when the assignee lawfully assigns. It is not limited to rent and does not depend on a prior default."}, {"id": 1624, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Hamza, who has voluntarily taken a large quantity of vodka, smashes a bottle in the face of his rival Seb. Hamza is charged with wounding with intent to cause grievous bodily harm under section 18 of the Offences Against the Person Act 1861. Medical evidence suggests Hamza was so intoxicated that he may not have formed any intention to cause serious harm. Which of the following is the correct legal position?", "options": {"A": "Voluntary intoxication is no defence to section 18, because the offence is one of basic intent and recklessness is sufficient", "B": "Hamza can be convicted of section 18 only if the prosecution proves he took the drink to give himself courage to attack Seb", "C": "Hamza is guilty of section 18 even if he lacked intent, because self-induced intoxication is treated as supplying intention in specific intent offences", "D": "If Hamza was too intoxicated to form the intent, he is not guilty of section 18 but may be convicted of the basic intent offence under section 20", "E": "Hamza must be acquitted of all offences, because lacking the intention for the specific intent offence means no offence of violence was committed"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (If Hamza was too intoxicated to form the intent, he is not guilty of section 18 but may be convicted of the basic intent offence under section 20): Section 18 is a specific intent offence, so evidence that intoxication prevented Hamza forming the intent to cause grievous bodily harm can lead to an acquittal on that charge. Voluntary intoxication supplies recklessness for basic intent offences, so he can still be convicted of the lesser offence under section 20. The Crown does not need to prove Dutch courage. Intoxication never substitutes for intention."}, {"id": 1625, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Sophie, aged 15, appears before the youth court charged with burglary of a dwelling, an offence for which an adult could be sentenced to up to 14 years' imprisonment. The burglary involved an occupied house at night and a significant theft. Sophie has two previous convictions. The youth court is deciding on mode of trial. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court must try her summarily, because a youth charged with an either way offence has no right to be tried elsewhere", "B": "The court must decide whether this is a grave crime, so a sentence of detention beyond its powers is possible", "C": "The court must send her to the Crown Court, because every offence carrying 14 years for an adult is a grave crime", "D": "The court decides on the maximum sentence alone and cannot take the circumstances of the burglary into account", "E": "The court can send her only if Sophie elects Crown Court trial, as a youth has the same right of election as an adult"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The court must decide whether this is a grave crime, so a sentence of detention beyond its powers is possible): A youth is normally tried summarily in the youth court, but the court must consider whether the offence is a grave crime. That means an offence with a 14 year adult maximum where the court thinks a longer period of detention than its own powers allow is a real possibility. The assessment looks at the circumstances of the offence, not only the maximum penalty. A youth has no right to elect trial."}, {"id": 1626, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Ruth, a sole trader, owes unsecured creditors £90,000 and Finlay Bank £140,000, which is secured by a legal charge over her home. She proposed an individual voluntary arrangement and creditors holding 78% by value of those voting approved it. Finlay Bank did not vote and has not agreed to the proposal. After approval, Ruth falls behind with her mortgage payments. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Finlay Bank may enforce only with the court's permission, because every creditor needs permission to take action against a debtor under an arrangement", "B": "Finlay Bank may enforce only after waiting until the arrangement ends, because the interim moratorium continues throughout the whole term of the arrangement", "C": "The arrangement is invalid, because it needed approval by 90% in value of all creditors, including secured creditors, before it could take effect", "D": "Finlay Bank is bound by the arrangement, because it was entitled to vote and an approved arrangement binds every creditor entitled to vote", "E": "Finlay Bank is not bound without its consent and may still enforce its security, because an arrangement cannot affect a secured creditor's right to do so"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Finlay Bank is not bound without its consent and may still enforce its security, because an arrangement cannot affect a secured creditor's right to do so): An individual voluntary arrangement approved by 75% or more in value of those voting binds those entitled to vote, but it cannot affect a secured creditor's right to enforce its security without that creditor's consent. Finlay Bank has not consented, so it may enforce its charge over the home when Ruth defaults. The moratorium applies only while an interim order is in force, which ends on the creditors' decision. The 78% vote is enough for approval, so the arrangement itself is valid."}, {"id": 1627, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Your firm is instructed by Brightwell Foods Ltd to defend a claim by a visitor injured by a forklift. Both Brightwell and its driver, Sanjay, are named as defendants, and the claim alleges Sanjay drove carelessly. Brightwell's HR director tells you in confidence that Brightwell intends to seek a contribution or indemnity from Sanjay and dismiss him. Sanjay now asks your firm to act for him as well. Which of the following should you do?", "options": {"A": "Act for both defendants, because vicarious liability means the employer and employee share the same interest in defeating the claim", "B": "Decline to act for Sanjay, because there is a significant risk of a conflict, as Brightwell may pursue him for a contribution", "C": "Act for both if each gives informed written consent, because consent removes a conflict of interests between current clients", "D": "Act for Sanjay and tell him of Brightwell's intention, because honesty to one client overrides confidentiality owed to another client", "E": "Act for both, using separate teams and an information barrier, because that is enough to manage a conflict between clients"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline to act for Sanjay, because there is a significant risk of a conflict, as Brightwell may pursue him for a contribution): Where two clients' interests conflict, or there is a significant risk that they may, a firm may act for both only if there is a substantial common interest or they are competing for the same objective, with safeguards. Brightwell's intention to seek a contribution or indemnity from Sanjay means their interests are likely to diverge, and the firm holds confidential information from Brightwell that it cannot share with him. Informed consent alone does not cure a conflict outside those exceptions, and an information barrier cannot reconcile duties to two clients on the same matter. Telling Sanjay of the confidential intention would breach the duty owed to Brightwell."}, {"id": 1628, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "During a telephone call, Claire Whitmore, a solicitor at Marsh Legal, tells Duncan Pryor, who acts for the other side in a property sale, 'I will send you the signed transfer by Friday.' She does not confirm the promise in writing, and the signed transfer has not reached Duncan by Friday evening. Duncan relied on the promise in arranging his client's completion. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Claire has not given an undertaking, because an undertaking is binding if it is recorded in a signed letter", "B": "Claire has not given an undertaking, because a statement that she 'will' do something expresses an intention rather than a promise", "C": "Claire has given an undertaking, but only her firm is bound by it and not Claire, because she was acting as its agent", "D": "Claire has given an undertaking, because it can be oral, and she must perform it in the time agreed or face consequences", "E": "Claire has given an undertaking, but it is enforceable only if Duncan gave consideration for it, as with any contract"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Claire has given an undertaking, because it can be oral, and she must perform it in the time agreed or face consequences): An undertaking is a statement that a solicitor will do or refrain from doing something, given to someone who reasonably relies on it, and it may be oral or written. Claire's clear promise to send the transfer by a set day, relied on by Duncan, is an undertaking. She must perform it within the time agreed or within a reasonable time, and failure can lead to disciplinary action. No consideration is needed, and the individual solicitor is bound."}, {"id": 1629, "domain": "land_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for Gill Hartley in selling a house that is subject to a registered charge in favour of a bank. On exchange, the solicitor gives the buyer's solicitor a written undertaking to redeem the charge on completion from the sale proceeds and to send the buyer evidence of the redemption. On completion day, Gill instructs the solicitor to pay the whole proceeds to her so that she can settle another debt. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Pay the proceeds to Gill and tell the buyer's solicitor afterwards, because the undertaking binds the firm only on the day of exchange", "B": "Redeem the charge only if Gill agrees, since an undertaking given on a client's behalf binds only the client and not the firm", "C": "Comply with the undertaking and redeem the charge from the proceeds, even though the client instructs otherwise", "D": "Pay the proceeds to Gill and redeem the charge later from the firm's own funds if she fails to provide the money", "E": "Follow Gill's instruction, because the solicitor acts on the client's instructions and the buyer's position is a matter of contract"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Comply with the undertaking and redeem the charge from the proceeds, even though the client instructs otherwise): A solicitor who gives an undertaking is personally bound to perform it, and the SRA Code of Conduct requires undertakings to be performed within an agreed or reasonable time. The client's contrary instruction does not release the solicitor, because the undertaking is the solicitor's own promise to the buyer's solicitor. The solicitor should explain this to Gill and redeem the registered charge as promised. Only the person to whom the undertaking was given can release it."}, {"id": 1630, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Mrs Okafor owns a disused warehouse on an industrial estate. She padlocks the main gate but leaves a side door insecure, and there have been no previous break-ins or trespass in the area. One night teenagers enter through the side door and start a fire that spreads to the adjoining shop owned by Mr Brennan, destroying his stock. Mr Brennan sues Mrs Okafor in negligence for the loss. Which of the following best describes whether Mrs Okafor owed Mr Brennan a duty of care to prevent the fire?", "options": {"A": "No, because a defendant cannot owe a duty of care in respect of damage to property, only injury to persons", "B": "No, because the teenagers are the actual wrongdoers and a landowner is therefore exempt from a duty even where the risk was known", "C": "No, because she neither created the danger nor controlled the teenagers, and had no knowledge of a real risk of intrusion", "D": "Yes, because an occupier of land owes a duty to adjoining occupiers to prevent any fire starting on the premises", "E": "Yes, because leaving a side door insecure made damage to the neighbouring shop by fire a foreseeable consequence of her omission"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (No, because she neither created the danger nor controlled the teenagers, and had no knowledge of a real risk of intrusion): A defendant is not generally liable for harm caused by the deliberate wrongdoing of a third party. A duty arises only where the defendant created the source of danger, assumed responsibility, had control over the third party, or knew of a real risk and failed to deal with it. Here there had been no earlier intrusions, so Mrs Okafor had no knowledge of a risk of the kind that materialised. Foreseeability alone, the tempting argument, does not found a duty for an omission of this kind."}, {"id": 1631, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Four siblings hold a house on trust for themselves in equal shares. Dev, one of the beneficiaries, tells his friend Lorna in a telephone call, 'From today my quarter share in the house is yours, and I will tell the trustees.' Dev puts nothing in writing and signs no document. He dies a month later with the trustees still unaware of the call. Which of the following best describes the effect of Dev's words on his quarter share?", "options": {"A": "The disposition is effective in equity, because an oral gift of an equitable interest is valid once the donor has said that he will tell the trustees", "B": "The disposition is ineffective because the trustees did not consent, and the legal title cannot be moved without their agreement", "C": "The disposition is effective because Dev declared himself a trustee of his share for Lorna and that declaration needs no writing", "D": "The disposition is ineffective because section 53(1)(c) requires any such disposition to be in writing signed by Dev himself", "E": "The disposition is ineffective because it must be made by deed, which is required for every transfer of an interest in land"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The disposition is ineffective because section 53(1)(c) requires any such disposition to be in writing signed by Dev himself): A beneficiary's subsisting equitable interest is not itself an estate in land, but section 53(1)(c) requires any disposition of it to be in writing and signed by the person disposing, or by an agent. Dev's oral statement therefore passes nothing to Lorna, and the share forms part of his estate. A deed is not needed for this, and the trustees' consent is not a validity requirement. Telling the trustees would matter for priority between competing assignments, not for validity."}, {"id": 1632, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "On 1 March Pillar Foods agreed to supply 10 tonnes of organic flour to Bakehouse Co at £800 a tonne, delivery on 1 May. On 10 April Pillar said it would not deliver, and Bakehouse accepted that as ending the contract. The market price was £1,000 a tonne on 10 April, £900 on 1 May and £1,100 on 1 June. Bakehouse did nothing until 1 June, when it bought equivalent flour at £1,100 a tonne. Flour was available throughout. Which of the following is the likely measure of Bakehouse's damages?", "options": {"A": "£3,000, being the difference between the contract price and the £1,100 price when Bakehouse actually bought, as that is its true loss", "B": "No damages, because expectation loss is recoverable only where the buyer actually purchases substitute goods promptly at a lower price", "C": "Nominal damages only, because Bakehouse breached its duty to mitigate by delaying and so has no recoverable loss", "D": "£2,000, being the difference between the contract price and the price on 10 April, the date Pillar announced its refusal to deliver", "E": "£1,000, being the difference at the delivery date, because Bakehouse cannot recover the rise caused by its own delay in buying"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (£1,000, being the difference at the delivery date, because Bakehouse cannot recover the rise caused by its own delay in buying): Damages are assessed by the difference between the contract price and the market price when delivery should have occurred, which gives £100 a tonne, or £1,000 for ten tonnes. A claimant cannot recover loss it could reasonably have avoided, and Bakehouse could have bought on or near the delivery date with the market available. The later rise to £1,100 is loss caused by its own inaction. Failure to mitigate reduces recovery to the avoidable loss rather than removing it."}, {"id": 1633, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Aaron Doyle is charged with wounding after a fight outside a takeaway. He says the complainant, Mr Reyes, attacked him first and that he acted in self-defence. Aaron's solicitor discovers that Mr Reyes has two previous convictions for assault. Defence counsel intends to cross-examine Mr Reyes about them. Which of the following states the requirement the defence must satisfy?", "options": {"A": "The defence needs the prosecution's agreement, as the court has no power to admit a non-defendant's bad character otherwise", "B": "Leave is needed, but will be given if the convictions are simply relevant to an issue in the case and are not unfairly prejudicial to the complainant", "C": "Leave is needed, and will be given if the convictions are for offences of a similar kind to the wounding charged", "D": "Leave is needed, and will be given only if the evidence has substantial probative value on a matter in issue of substantial importance", "E": "Leave is not needed, because convictions of a prosecution witness are admissible whenever they are relevant to credibility"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Leave is needed, and will be given only if the evidence has substantial probative value on a matter in issue of substantial importance): Bad character evidence of a non-defendant is admissible only through the section 100 gateways: important explanatory evidence, substantial probative value on a matter in issue of substantial importance in the case as a whole, or agreement of the parties. Leave of the court is needed for the defence to use it. Mr Reyes's convictions could meet the substantial probative value test on self-defence, but mere relevance or similarity is not enough."}, {"id": 1634, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Redfern Cold Stores Ltd owns a disused warehouse with a fragile glass roof light. The company's caretaker reported that local teenagers had climbed onto the roof twice in the past year, and the company knew the roof lights would not bear weight, but it took no steps. Kai, aged 15, climbed up a drainpipe at night, stepped onto a roof light and fell through, suffering serious injuries. Kai sues under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Kai fails, because his age shows he accepted the risk and the defence of consent defeats a trespasser's claim", "B": "Kai is likely to succeed, because the three conditions for a duty were met, although a reduction for contributory negligence is possible", "C": "Kai succeeds in full, because the company owed him the common duty of care as it would to a lawful visitor", "D": "Kai fails, because the duty arises only for dangers the owner itself created, and the fragile roof light was an ordinary feature of the building", "E": "Kai fails, because a trespasser who commits an offence by entering the premises is owed no duty of any kind"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Kai is likely to succeed, because the three conditions for a duty were met, although a reduction for contributory negligence is possible): The company knew of the fragile roof light, knew that trespassers had climbed onto the roof, and could reasonably be expected to offer protection against a risk of serious injury from a fall. Those are the conditions that give rise to a duty under the 1984 Act, and the danger arises from the state of the premises. The duty is only to take reasonable care in the circumstances, but taking no steps is unlikely to meet it. A trespasser is not barred, and Kai's own conduct goes to contributory negligence rather than to the existence of the duty. Consent requires knowledge of the specific danger, which Kai is not shown to have had."}, {"id": 1635, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Darren Okafor is charged with fraud by false representation involving £9,000, an either-way offence. The magistrates have accepted jurisdiction. Darren has no previous convictions, believes his defence is strong, and wants the case over quickly. His solicitor Jessica Mallory is told by her supervisor that the firm's fixed fee is much higher for Crown Court work. Darren asks Jessica which court he should choose. Which of the following is the most appropriate course for Jessica?", "options": {"A": "Advise election for Crown Court trial, because a jury is more likely to acquit and the extra fee reflects the greater preparation work involved for the firm", "B": "Advise summary trial, because the supervisor's fee point means the firm should avoid any appearance of gaining from the venue", "C": "Explain the advantages and risks of each venue, including the Crown Court's greater sentencing powers, then leave the decision to Darren", "D": "Tell Darren to follow his wish for speed, because a client's convenience outweighs any need to compare sentencing powers", "E": "Decline to advise on venue, because the choice is a tactical matter for counsel once the case reaches the Crown Court"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explain the advantages and risks of each venue, including the Crown Court's greater sentencing powers, then leave the decision to Darren): A solicitor must act in the best interests of each client and give the client the information needed to make an informed decision. Venue is a decision for Darren, and Jessica must compare speed, jury trial, costs and sentencing exposure honestly. The firm's fee structure is irrelevant to the advice and must not influence it in either direction. Advising purely on convenience or declining to advise would each fall short of the duty to explain the real risks."}, {"id": 1636, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Derek was convicted by a jury at the Crown Court of robbery after a three day trial and wishes to appeal against conviction to the Court of Appeal. His solicitor explains that he needs leave and describes the test the Court of Appeal will apply when deciding whether to allow the appeal. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The court will allow the appeal only if it concludes that the conviction is unsafe, for any reason including legal error", "B": "The court will allow the appeal if it finds any procedural irregularity, even one that did not affect the result", "C": "The court will allow the appeal if it considers that the trial judge made any error of law during the trial", "D": "The court will allow the appeal only if fresh evidence not available at trial shows that he is innocent", "E": "The court will allow the appeal if it considers that the jury's verdict was against the weight of the evidence given at the trial"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The court will allow the appeal only if it concludes that the conviction is unsafe, for any reason including legal error): The Court of Appeal must allow an appeal against conviction if it thinks the conviction is unsafe and must dismiss it otherwise. An error of law or an irregularity does not automatically make a conviction unsafe, so those tests fail. Fresh evidence is one route to a finding of unsafety, but it is not the only one and innocence need not be proved."}, {"id": 1637, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "A solicitor acts for Kestrel Print Ltd, which paid a £25,000 deposit to Davenant Machines for a printing press due for delivery in four weeks. Davenant entered administration before delivery, and the administrator has disclaimed the contract. Kestrel's managing director tells the solicitor to claim the deposit from the administrator and to state in the claim that the press was specially built for Kestrel, which the director knows is untrue because it was a standard model. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Advise that the deposit cannot be recovered from the administrator, because the disclaimer ends the contract and removes any basis for restitution of money paid", "B": "Include the statement but say that it is the client's own assertion, because attributing it to the client discharges the solicitor's duty not to mislead", "C": "Decline to act any further for Kestrel, because a client who asks for a false statement has destroyed the trust needed to continue the retainer", "D": "Include the statement, because the solicitor may rely on the client's instructions about the facts and the administrator can test the assertion in due course", "E": "Advise that the deposit can be claimed for total failure of consideration without it, and decline to include a statement known to be untrue"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Advise that the deposit can be claimed for total failure of consideration without it, and decline to include a statement known to be untrue): A solicitor must not mislead or attempt to mislead others, whether by a statement or by omission, and cannot repeat an assertion known to be false, even on the client's instructions. Attributing the claim to the client does not cure that. The true position gives Kestrel a good claim, since it has received nothing for its deposit, so the false statement is unnecessary. Disclaimer by an administrator does not remove the right to prove for money paid. The solicitor can properly continue to act once the client has been advised."}, {"id": 1638, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Delroy is a trainee in a litigation department. He has dyslexia but has never mentioned it to anyone at the firm, and his application form said he had no health conditions. His supervisor gives him a long document review with a tight deadline and he makes several errors. The supervisor, who had no reason to suspect a disability, begins a capability process. Delroy then discloses his dyslexia and claims the firm failed to make reasonable adjustments. Which of the following is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The claim fails for the earlier period, because the duty needs the employer to know, or reasonably be expected to know, of the disability", "B": "The claim fails, because reasonable adjustments are required only where the disabled person has first made a written request to the firm's human resources department", "C": "The claim succeeds, because the firm should have asked about health conditions at interview and its failure to do so is itself a breach of the duty", "D": "The claim fails, because dyslexia is not a disability under the Equality Act 2010 unless a doctor appointed by the firm has diagnosed it", "E": "The claim succeeds, because the duty to make adjustments applies to a disabled employee from the start of employment whether or not the employer knows"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The claim fails for the earlier period, because the duty needs the employer to know, or reasonably be expected to know, of the disability): The duty in section 20 and Schedule 8 of the Equality Act 2010 applies only where the employer knows, or could reasonably be expected to know, that the worker has a disability and is likely to be placed at a substantial disadvantage. Delroy concealed the condition and nothing suggested it, so the firm is not liable for the period before disclosure. Once he discloses it, the firm must consider adjustments such as extra time or software support, and the capability process should be revisited. A request need not be written, and a disability does not depend on any employer led diagnosis."}, {"id": 1639, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Kofi Mensah is on bail for burglary and has a trial date set at the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing. In a confidential meeting he tells his solicitor that he committed the burglary but wants to plead not guilty to see what the prosecution can prove. The firm's trainee suggests that the safest course is to withdraw from the case immediately. Which of the following is the most appropriate course for the solicitor?", "options": {"A": "She may continue only if he agrees to change his plea to guilty at the next hearing, as a condition of representation", "B": "She may run any defence he chooses, including a positive alibi, because instructions from the client override the duty to the court", "C": "She must stop acting at once, because knowledge of guilt means she cannot represent a client who wishes to plead not guilty to the charge", "D": "She may continue to act, explaining the credit for a guilty plea, and if he maintains his plea she may only put the prosecution to proof", "E": "She must tell the court of his admission, because her duty to the court outweighs her duty of confidentiality in this situation"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She may continue to act, explaining the credit for a guilty plea, and if he maintains his plea she may only put the prosecution to proof): A solicitor may continue to act for a client who admits guilt but pleads not guilty, provided the court is not misled. She should explain the sentencing credit for an early guilty plea and warn of the consequences. If Kofi persists, she may test the prosecution evidence and argue it is insufficient, but she cannot advance a positive case, such as an alibi, that she knows to be false. Confidentiality prevents her from telling the court of the admission."}, {"id": 1640, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Two friends, Ali and Beth, act without payment as the trustees of a bare family trust for their niece. The trust instrument is silent about payment and the niece, an adult, has not been asked about it. Ali has spent many hours dealing with the trust's investments and now wishes to take a fee for her time. Neither trustee is a professional. Which of the following would make payment to Ali permissible?", "options": {"A": "The trust instrument expressly authorises payment, or all the beneficiaries, being adult and fully informed, consent to it", "B": "Beth agrees in writing, because section 29 Trustee Act 2000 then allows a reasonable fee for services provided to the trust", "C": "A majority of the beneficiaries consent, even where some adult beneficiaries have not been asked or have refused", "D": "Ali is also a beneficiary of the trust, because that status entitles a trustee to be paid out of income before others", "E": "Ali has spent substantial time on administration, so the court will imply a reasonable fee as of right in her favour"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The trust instrument expressly authorises payment, or all the beneficiaries, being adult and fully informed, consent to it): A lay trustee has no right to payment and must not profit unless the trust instrument, the court or fully informed adult beneficiaries acting together allow it. Section 29 applies only to trustees acting in a professional capacity or trust corporations, so it does not help Ali. Time spent does not create an implied right to a fee, and being a beneficiary does not authorise payment. Consent must come from all beneficiaries, so a majority is not enough."}, {"id": 1641, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "Police Constable Dobson directs Mal to park. Mal drives forward and, without realising, stops his car with a wheel on Dobson's foot. Dobson tells him loudly to move. Mal realises what has happened, deliberately says he will take his time, and leaves the wheel there for half a minute. Mal is charged with battery. Which of the following best explains the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "He is not guilty, because the actus reus occurred when the wheel first touched the foot and he had no mens rea at that moment", "B": "He is guilty, because recklessness as to touching is judged by what a reasonable driver would have noticed at the moment of impact", "C": "He is guilty, because the wheel resting on the foot is a continuing act and he formed the mens rea while it continued", "D": "He is not guilty, because his failure to move is an omission and omissions cannot found liability for battery", "E": "He is not guilty, because a battery requires an intentional application of force and pressure from a stationary wheel is not force"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He is guilty, because the wheel resting on the foot is a continuing act and he formed the mens rea while it continued): The act of the wheel on the foot was continuing, so the actus reus persisted while Mal acquired the mens rea. Coincidence is satisfied if the mens rea is present at any time during a continuing act. Treating it as a pure omission or insisting on mens rea at the first touch misreads the cases. Recklessness is judged subjectively, so what a reasonable driver would have noticed is irrelevant."}, {"id": 1642, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Parkside Solicitors, an exempt professional firm, plans a new wealth management service. Clients will pay a percentage of their assets each year for the firm to select and trade a portfolio of shares and funds on their behalf, with discretion to buy and sell without consulting them. Most of these clients will have no other matter with the firm. The managing partner says the firm's exempt status will cover the service. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "The service is not incidental to professional work, so the firm needs Financial Conduct Authority authorisation or a separate authorised business", "B": "The service is covered, because the exemption extends to any activity that the Solicitors Regulation Authority is willing to supervise as part of the firm", "C": "The service is covered, because managing a portfolio on a discretionary basis is a normal complement to any legal retainer with a private client", "D": "The service is covered if the firm discloses the percentage fee in writing, because disclosure of charges satisfies the incidental condition of the exemption", "E": "The service is covered if each client signs an agreement acknowledging the risk, because the exemption can be widened by agreement with the client"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The service is not incidental to professional work, so the firm needs Financial Conduct Authority authorisation or a separate authorised business): An exempt professional firm may carry on regulated activities only where they arise out of or complement its professional services and are incidental to them. A stand-alone discretionary investment management service for clients who have no other matter with the firm is a separate business and not incidental. The exemption cannot be extended by disclosure or by client agreement. The firm must therefore obtain Financial Conduct Authority authorisation, or provide the service through an authorised entity."}, {"id": 1643, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "You act for Imogen Hale, buying a freehold house on a modern estate for £330,000. The local authority search shows that the road outside the house is not maintained at public expense, and the developer never entered into an agreement to have it adopted. Imogen's mortgage offer is conditional on satisfactory legal title. She is worried about road repairs. What is the most appropriate advice?", "options": {"A": "Check that the title gives rights over the road and any maintenance duty, and warn that owners may bear repair or road adoption costs", "B": "Require the seller to indemnify Imogen against all future road costs, because an unadopted road is a defect in the seller's title", "C": "Tell Imogen that the council must adopt the road on request once the houses are occupied, so the repair costs will fall on the council", "D": "Tell Imogen that only the original buyer from the developer can be charged for the road, so she is protected as a later buyer", "E": "Tell Imogen she faces no risk, because years of public use give the road the status of a public highway maintainable by the council"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Check that the title gives rights over the road and any maintenance duty, and warn that owners may bear repair or road adoption costs): An unadopted road is maintained privately, so Imogen's access depends on rights granted over it, and the owners may be liable for repairs or for contributions if the authority later requires the road to be made up. The solicitor should check the title for the rights and any maintenance provision and report the risk. Councils are not obliged to adopt on request, and use by the public does not make a road maintainable at public expense. Nor is the seller liable, since the position is not a title defect."}, {"id": 1644, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Bellwood Roofing Ltd brings a fast track claim for £18,000 against a customer who refused to pay, saying the work was defective. Before issuing, Bellwood commissioned a report from a chartered surveyor, Mr Achebe, and its solicitor proposes to serve it with the particulars of claim and rely on it at trial. The customer's solicitor says no expert evidence can be used without the court's say so. The surveyor's fee has been paid and his report is signed with a statement of truth. What is the correct position?", "options": {"A": "The report can be relied on if the customer does not object within 14 days of service, because silence amounts to agreement", "B": "The report cannot be relied on without permission, so Bellwood should apply naming the expert and field at the directions stage", "C": "The report can be served with the particulars of claim and relied on, because the expert was instructed before proceedings began", "D": "The report needs permission only where the claim exceeds £25,000, because lower value claims allow each party one expert as of right", "E": "The report becomes admissible once listed under standard disclosure, because expert reports are documents falling within the disclosure rules"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The report cannot be relied on without permission, so Bellwood should apply naming the expert and field at the directions stage): CPR 35.4 provides that no party may call an expert or put in evidence an expert's report without the court's permission. The court restricts expert evidence to what is reasonably required to resolve the proceedings, and on the fast track it normally limits each party to one expert per field. The expert having been paid and the report having a statement of truth does not give it admissibility. Bellwood should apply for permission, identifying the expert and field, at the directions stage."}, {"id": 1645, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Four friends buy a house and take the transfer in all of their names. Their solicitor explains that, for them to hold as joint tenants, certain features must be present, and that a tenancy in common is different. The friends want to understand what the law requires for each form of co-ownership before they decide how to hold the property. Which of the following correctly identifies the unities required for a joint tenancy, compared with a tenancy in common?", "options": {"A": "A joint tenancy needs unity of possession alone, while a tenancy in common needs possession and separate shares", "B": "A joint tenancy needs the four unities of possession, interest, title and time, and a tenancy in common needs the same four", "C": "A joint tenancy needs possession, interest, title and time, while a tenancy in common needs possession alone", "D": "A joint tenancy needs possession and title, while a tenancy in common needs equal shares and unity of time", "E": "A joint tenancy needs possession and interest, while a tenancy in common needs possession, interest, title and time"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A joint tenancy needs possession, interest, title and time, while a tenancy in common needs possession alone): A joint tenancy requires the four unities of possession, interest, title and time, so each joint tenant holds the same interest, acquired by the same document at the same time, with a right to possession of the whole. A tenancy in common requires only unity of possession, so shares may be unequal and arise at different times. Joint tenants have no separate shares while the joint tenancy continues, whereas tenants in common hold distinct shares."}, {"id": 1646, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Joanne Frost is charged with possession of a class B drug. Officers who stopped her car searched it without recording the grounds, contrary to PACE Code A, and found cannabis in the glovebox. At trial her solicitor asks the judge to exclude the cannabis. The prosecutor says that real evidence is reliable and cannot be excluded merely because it was found. What test applies to the application under section 78 of PACE?", "options": {"A": "Whether the evidence was obtained in breach of a Code of Practice, because any breach requires the court to exclude it", "B": "Whether the evidence is reliable, because section 78 permits exclusion only of unreliable items such as confessions", "C": "Whether admitting it, given all the circumstances including how it was obtained, would so adversely affect fairness that it ought not to be admitted", "D": "Whether the prosecution can prove beyond reasonable doubt that the search was lawful and the evidence untainted", "E": "Whether the defence can show that the officers acted in bad faith, because impropriety is a requirement for exclusion"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Whether admitting it, given all the circumstances including how it was obtained, would so adversely affect fairness that it ought not to be admitted): Section 78 gives the court a discretion to refuse prosecution evidence if, having regard to all the circumstances including the way it was obtained, admission would have such an adverse effect on the fairness of the proceedings that it ought not to be admitted. A breach of the Codes is relevant but not decisive, and bad faith is not required. Real evidence can be excluded where its admission would make the trial unfair, although exclusion is uncommon."}, {"id": 1647, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Linford Ltd's solicitors want to serve a claim form on Ashby Components Ltd, which is acting through a firm of solicitors, Marsh and Co. Marsh and Co's letter to Linford's solicitors gave its email address at the foot of the letterhead and said: We act for Ashby Components Ltd. The letter did not say that the firm was authorised to accept service or that service could be made by email. The claim form has about two days left in its validity period. Linford's solicitors email the claim form and particulars to the address on the letterhead. Is service valid?", "options": {"A": "Yes, because a solicitor who confirms that they act is authorised to accept service of the claim form on behalf of the client", "B": "No, because email is not among the methods permitted for serving a claim form under the Civil Procedure Rules", "C": "Yes, because the email address on the letterhead was clearly provided for communications in the dispute and Ashby has seen the claim form", "D": "No, because a claim form can be served by email on a party who has not yet instructed solicitors in the matter", "E": "No, because an email address on a letterhead is not sufficient unless the letter also states that it may be used for service"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (No, because an email address on a letterhead is not sufficient unless the letter also states that it may be used for service): Service by email is valid only if the party to be served, or its solicitor, has previously indicated in writing that it is willing to accept service by email and given the address. An email address on a letterhead counts only if the writing paper states that the address may be used for service. Merely saying that the firm acts does not authorise service on it. Email is a permitted method in the right circumstances, so the defect is the absence of prior written indication, and the defendant's knowledge of the claim does not cure it."}, {"id": 1648, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Delphi Ltd has four members, each holding 100 shares carrying one vote. A written resolution to adopt new articles of association was circulated to all four on 1 March. By 10 March three members had signed and returned it. The fourth, Yusuf, has not replied, and the company has no provision in its articles dealing with written resolutions. Has the resolution been passed?", "options": {"A": "No, because it was signed by only 75% of the members, and a special resolution needs more than 75%", "B": "No, because a written resolution must be agreed by every eligible member, so Yusuf's failure to reply prevents the resolution from being passed", "C": "Yes, because it needed signatures from members holding at least 75% of the total voting rights, and three of the four have signed", "D": "Yes, but it takes effect only after 14 days have passed to allow Yusuf to object to the proposal", "E": "No, because new articles can be adopted only by special resolution passed at a general meeting of the company"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Yes, because it needed signatures from members holding at least 75% of the total voting rights, and three of the four have signed): A special resolution may be passed by written resolution when members holding at least 75% of the total voting rights of eligible members have signed. Three equal holdings out of four is exactly 75%, which is enough. Unanimity is not required and no waiting period applies. Adopting new articles is a special resolution matter that can be dealt with by written procedure."}, {"id": 1649, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Ruth's household insurance includes legal expenses cover. When her neighbour starts a boundary claim, Ruth tells the insurer, which accepts the claim in principle. The insurer then says Ruth must use its panel firm, Dunmore Legal, to handle the matter and that it will pay costs only to that firm. Ruth would prefer her long standing family solicitor, Mr Pryce, and asks him whether she can insist. Which of the following is the correct advice?", "options": {"A": "Ruth may choose Mr Pryce if the insurer agrees that its panel firm has a conflict of interests in the claim", "B": "Ruth may choose Mr Pryce if she pays the difference between his rates and the panel firm's rates from the outset", "C": "Ruth must use the panel firm, because the insurer's contract entitles it to nominate the solicitor throughout the claim", "D": "Ruth may choose her own solicitor for pre action advice only, and the insurer may nominate the firm once proceedings are issued", "E": "Ruth may choose her own solicitor once proceedings are contemplated, and the insurer must pay reasonable costs up to the limit"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Ruth may choose her own solicitor once proceedings are contemplated, and the insurer must pay reasonable costs up to the limit): Under the Insurance Companies (Legal Expenses Insurance) Regulations 1990, an insured has the right to choose a lawyer to represent them when they need to take or defend legal proceedings. The insurer may use a panel for early stages but cannot insist on it once proceedings are contemplated. The insurer must still be satisfied that the costs are reasonable and within the policy limit. Ruth does not need to show a conflict or pay a rate difference to exercise the right."}, {"id": 1650, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Edith Marlowe, aged 82, relied for ten years on her financial adviser, Lionel Cass, who managed all her savings and told her what to do with them. Lionel persuaded Edith to transfer her house, worth £400,000, to a company he controlled for £250,000 without advising her to take independent advice. Edith's family later challenged the transfer. No evidence shows Lionel threatened or pressured her. Which of the following best describes the legal position?", "options": {"A": "Edith must prove actual pressure by Lionel, because undue influence cannot be presumed in a financial advisory relationship", "B": "No presumption arises, because Edith had capacity and the sale price was not so low as to shock the court", "C": "The transfer is binding, because undue influence applies to gifts and not to sales at a price below value", "D": "A presumption arises on proof of the adviser relationship alone, because advisers fall within a recognised class where influence is presumed", "E": "A presumption of undue influence arises, because of the relationship of trust and a transaction that cannot be explained by ordinary motives"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A presumption of undue influence arises, because of the relationship of trust and a transaction that cannot be explained by ordinary motives): A presumption of undue influence arises where there is a relationship of trust and confidence and the transaction calls for explanation. Edith depended on Lionel for ten years and sold a house worth £400,000 for much less to his company, which ordinary motives do not explain. The burden then falls on Lionel to rebut the presumption, for example by showing independent advice. Actual pressure need not be shown, and sales as well as gifts can be challenged."}, {"id": 1651, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Quill Antiques agreed to sell a unique eighteenth century clock to Verity for £12,000, after Verity said she wanted it for her late father's collection. Quill then refused to sell, having been offered £15,000 by another buyer. The clock is the only one of its kind, and Verity wants the clock rather than money. She has paid nothing yet. Which of the following best explains why a court is likely to grant specific performance?", "options": {"A": "An order will be made because Verity's sentimental attachment alone shows that money cannot compensate her, whether or not the item is unique", "B": "An order will be made because Quill breached the contract deliberately, and deliberate breach entitles the innocent party to equitable relief as of right", "C": "Damages would be inadequate because the clock is unique and cannot be replaced from the market, so an order for delivery may be made", "D": "An order will be made because the price was agreed in writing, and a court of equity enforces every written contract for the sale of goods", "E": "An order will be made because Verity has paid part of the price, since part payment makes damages an inadequate remedy"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Damages would be inadequate because the clock is unique and cannot be replaced from the market, so an order for delivery may be made): Specific performance is discretionary and is granted where damages are inadequate, which commonly means the goods are unique or otherwise not available on the market. The clock is the only one of its kind, so money cannot buy a substitute. Deliberate breach, writing or part payment do not by themselves make the remedy available. Personal feeling of the buyer is not the test, since the nature of the subject matter matters."}, {"id": 1652, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Police officers execute a search warrant at the offices of Harrow & Finch LLP, a firm of solicitors, seeking documents about a client suspected of fraud. The officers begin to take files, including advice notes between the firm and the client. The supervising partner asks a trainee solicitor how the firm should respond, bearing in mind the firm's duty of confidentiality and the right to respect for private life and correspondence under article 8. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The firm should assert privilege and refuse to hand over the advice notes, since a warrant cannot authorise seizure of privileged material", "B": "The firm should hand over all the files requested, because a search warrant overrides the duty of confidentiality owed to the client", "C": "The firm should hand over the advice notes, because privilege protects only communications made in connection with litigation that has started", "D": "The firm should allow the search but obtain an undertaking from the police that the notes will be read by one officer only", "E": "The firm should hand over the files but report the search to the SRA, because that discharges the duty of confidentiality"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The firm should assert privilege and refuse to hand over the advice notes, since a warrant cannot authorise seizure of privileged material): Legal professional privilege protects confidential communications between solicitor and client made for the purpose of giving legal advice, as well as litigation material, and a search warrant does not authorise seizure of items subject to legal privilege. The firm owes the client a duty of confidentiality and should assert privilege and refuse to hand over the advice notes, which also respects the article 8 interest in confidential correspondence. Reporting the search to the SRA does not discharge those duties, and a police undertaking is no substitute for protection."}, {"id": 1653, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Imogen Fraser, a solicitor in Birmingham, applies without notice for an urgent injunction to freeze her client's former business partner's bank account. She knows that the former partner has a plausible defence based on a signed agreement, and that her client's earlier delay in raising the claim is a matter the judge might consider important. The judge has little time. Which of the following best states Imogen's duty on the application?", "options": {"A": "She may omit the adverse facts if she is satisfied that the client's case is strong enough to succeed anyway", "B": "She must give full and frank disclosure of all material facts, including those that are adverse to her client's case", "C": "She need only present her client's case and leave the other party to raise the defence at the return date", "D": "She must disclose the adverse facts only if the judge asks about them, because the judge controls the hearing", "E": "She must disclose the defence but need not mention the client's delay, because delay is a matter for the other side"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (She must give full and frank disclosure of all material facts, including those that are adverse to her client's case): On a without notice application the other side is absent, so the duty is to make full and frank disclosure of all material facts, including those that harm the client. Both the plausible defence and the delay are material, and it is for the judge to weigh them. Imogen cannot leave the defence for the return date or decide for herself that the case is strong enough. Failure to disclose could lead to the injunction being discharged and to professional consequences."}, {"id": 1654, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Lakeside Tiles Ltd issues a claim on 2 March for £18,000 for goods supplied, with particulars served on 4 March. On 12 March, before the defendant has filed any acknowledgment of service or defence, Lakeside's solicitor issues an application for summary judgment. The defendant's time for filing its acknowledgment of service has not expired. Is the application procedurally permitted?", "options": {"A": "No, because summary judgment can be sought by a defendant and not by a claimant in a money claim", "B": "Yes, provided the respondent is given at least 14 days' notice of the date fixed for hearing the application, as the rules require", "C": "Yes, because a claimant may apply for summary judgment at any time after serving the particulars of claim", "D": "No, because a claimant may not apply until the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of service or defence, unless the court gives permission", "E": "No, because the application must wait until the defendant's time for filing a defence has expired in full"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, because a claimant may not apply until the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of service or defence, unless the court gives permission): A claimant cannot apply for summary judgment until the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of service or a defence, unless the court gives permission or a practice direction allows it. Lakeside applied before either had been filed, so the application is premature without permission. The notice period for the hearing is a separate requirement and does not cure the timing bar. Claimants are among those who may apply, and the bar ends once an acknowledgment is filed, not when the whole defence period has expired."}, {"id": 1655, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Chidi is buying a house for £300,000 with a mortgage advance of £180,000. He paid a deposit of £30,000 on exchange, which the seller's solicitor holds. His solicitor has calculated stamp duty land tax at £5,000, the Land Registry fee at £270 and legal costs and disbursements at £1,230. All figures are as stated and there are no other adjustments. How much must Chidi provide to his solicitor before completion?", "options": {"A": "£126,500, being the price less the mortgage advance, plus the stamp duty, registry fee and costs, with no credit for the deposit", "B": "£90,000, being the price less the deposit and mortgage advance, because the buyer's taxes and costs are paid only after completion", "C": "£96,500, being the price less the deposit and mortgage advance, plus the stamp duty, registry fee and costs", "D": "£276,500, being the balance of the price after the deposit, plus the stamp duty, registry fee and costs, with the mortgage paid directly", "E": "£66,500, being the price less the deposit twice and the mortgage advance, plus the stamp duty, registry fee and costs"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (£96,500, being the price less the deposit and mortgage advance, plus the stamp duty, registry fee and costs): The balance of the price is £300,000 less the £30,000 deposit already paid, which is £270,000. The lender supplies £180,000, leaving £90,000 from Chidi. He must also fund the stamp duty land tax (£5,000), the registry fee (£270) and costs (£1,230), which brings the total to £96,500. Omitting the deposit credit or deducting it twice produces a wrong sum, and the taxes and fees must be in the solicitor's hands before completion so the return can be filed."}, {"id": 1656, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Ravi Menon, aged 31, is arrested on suspicion of burglary and taken to a police station. The custody officer reads him his rights and tells him that he may have free legal advice. Ravi says that he does not want a solicitor and wants to be interviewed quickly so that he can go home. An hour later, before any interview has begun, he changes his mind and asks to speak to a solicitor. Which of the following best describes the position?", "options": {"A": "His waiver was recorded on the custody record, so he can reverse it only with the written authority of an inspector", "B": "He may change his mind at any time, and the interview should wait until he has had advice, with limited exceptions", "C": "He may change his mind only before the custody officer has opened the custody record, after which his earlier waiver stands", "D": "Having declined advice once, he must wait until the first review of detention before the police are obliged to act on his request", "E": "The right to free advice arises only on being charged, so his request is premature and the police may refuse it for now"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (He may change his mind at any time, and the interview should wait until he has had advice, with limited exceptions): Under PACE section 58 and Code C, a suspect held at a police station is entitled to consult a solicitor privately and free of charge, and that right continues throughout detention. A decision to waive it is not final, so the suspect can change his mind at any time before or during questioning. Once he does, the police must give him the chance to take advice before the interview goes ahead, with only narrow exceptions such as an authorised delay. The suggestion that the right begins only on charge is wrong because it arises as soon as he is held at the station."}, {"id": 1657, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Brenda Tulloch died on 10 March 2026. Her husband, who had lived apart from her for several years, wishes to claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975 because her will left him nothing. Probate was granted to her executor, Brenda's sister, on 20 May 2026. The husband's solicitor asks what the time limit is for bringing the claim and from what date it runs. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Six months from the date of the grant of representation, although the court has a discretion to allow a claim made out of time", "B": "Twelve months from the date of death, as for other claims against an estate under the same Act", "C": "Six months from the date of the grant, and the court has no power to allow a late claim", "D": "Twelve months from the date of the grant of representation, which also applies to claims by a former spouse", "E": "Six months from the date of death, with no power for the court to extend the period once it has expired"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Six months from the date of the grant of representation, although the court has a discretion to allow a claim made out of time): A claim under the 1975 Act must normally be brought within six months of the grant of representation, and the court has a discretion to permit a late claim. The period runs from the grant and not from death. The claim here should therefore be made by 20 November 2026 unless the court allows more time."}, {"id": 1658, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Claimant Reza sued two defendants, Arlo and Beth, for the same indivisible injury. Judgment was entered for £80,000 against Arlo alone, and Arlo paid it in full on 3 March 2026. Arlo now wishes to claim a contribution from Beth under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978. Which of the following best states the period within which Arlo must start that claim?", "options": {"A": "One year from the date of payment, because contribution claims are subject to a short period to prevent delay", "B": "Six years from the date of the claimant's injury, because contribution is a claim in tort between wrongdoers", "C": "Two years from the date of the judgment against him, because that is when the right to a contribution accrues", "D": "Two years from the date on which Arlo knew Beth was also liable to the claimant, as a date of knowledge applies", "E": "Three years from the date on which Arlo paid the money, because payment is the damage Arlo has suffered"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Two years from the date of the judgment against him, because that is when the right to a contribution accrues): Under the Limitation Act 1980, a claim for contribution must be brought within two years of the date on which the right to contribution accrued, which for a claim after judgment is the date of that judgment. Arlo's contribution claim is therefore measured from the judgment, not from his later payment. The six-year and three-year periods apply to other claims, and the date of knowledge test does not govern contribution claims. No one year period exists for this type of claim."}, {"id": 1659, "domain": "trusts_law", "question": "Ines transfers £20,000 from her own account into the account of her friend Jo. She says nothing about the purpose, and there is no document. Jo is not related to Ines and gave nothing in return. Two years later Ines asks for the money back, saying she meant only to let Jo hold it. Jo says it was a gift. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Jo keeps the money, because a voluntary transfer between friends is presumed to be a gift unless there is a written trust", "B": "A constructive trust arises, because Jo's retention of the money without payment would be unconscionable", "C": "Ines must prove a written declaration of trust, because a resulting trust of money must satisfy section 53 of the Law of Property Act 1925", "D": "Jo holds on resulting trust only if Ines proves Jo knew the transfer was not intended as a gift", "E": "A resulting trust in Ines's favour is presumed, because the transfer was voluntary, and Jo must produce evidence that a gift was intended"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (A resulting trust in Ines's favour is presumed, because the transfer was voluntary, and Jo must produce evidence that a gift was intended): A voluntary transfer to a person who is not a child or spouse raises a presumption that the transferee holds on resulting trust for the transferor. The recipient can rebut it by showing that a gift was intended. Section 53 does not apply, because resulting trusts are expressly excepted from the formality rules. Jo gave nothing in return, so the presumption stands unless she produces contrary evidence."}, {"id": 1660, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Isla Brennan, aged 34, is charged with causing actual bodily harm, an either way offence, and appears at the magistrates' court. She receives a means-tested benefit, has no savings and has previous convictions for violence. She tells her solicitor that she expects to be given a custodial sentence if convicted and wants legal aid. The duty solicitor has not previously acted for her. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Apply for a representation order, which depends on her means alone, as the interests of justice are irrelevant to charges of an either way kind", "B": "Apply for a representation order, as the interests of justice test is likely met where she risks custody, and her means must be shown", "C": "Decline to apply, because legal aid in the magistrates' court is available only to defendants remanded in custody", "D": "Wait to apply until after plea, because a representation order cannot be granted before mode of trial is decided", "E": "Apply for a representation order, which is granted on request for any either way offence without a financial check"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply for a representation order, as the interests of justice test is likely met where she risks custody, and her means must be shown): A representation order in the magistrates' court depends on two tests: the interests of justice and the defendant's financial eligibility. A real risk of a custodial sentence is a classic factor showing that the interests of justice test is met, and Isla's benefit status and lack of savings support the means test. Legal aid is not tied to remand in custody, and it can be granted before plea. It is also wrong to treat means as the only test or to assume that no financial check applies."}, {"id": 1661, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Gareth was made bankrupt in March 2023. He owns a house jointly with his wife, who lives there with their children. The trustee in bankruptcy was told about the house within weeks but has taken no step to sell it, apply for an order for sale, or agree a settlement over Gareth's interest, and has not recorded an intention to take it. It is now April 2026. What is the legal position?", "options": {"A": "The interest reverts to Gareth only if the court orders it on his application, once a year has passed from the bankruptcy order", "B": "The interest remains vested in the trustee until it is sold, because a trustee may deal with a bankrupt's home at any time after the order", "C": "The interest passes to his wife as co-owner, because a share accrues to the other owner when the trustee fails to act within three years", "D": "The interest reverts to Gareth after twelve months, because the trustee must act within one year of learning of the property", "E": "Gareth's interest in the house automatically reverts to him, because the trustee did not deal with it within three years of the bankruptcy order"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Gareth's interest in the house automatically reverts to him, because the trustee did not deal with it within three years of the bankruptcy order): Section 283A Insolvency Act 1986 gives the trustee three years from the bankruptcy order to realise a bankrupt's interest in a dwelling house, apply for an order for sale or possession, or reach an agreement. If none of those steps is taken in that period, the interest ceases to be part of the estate and reverts to the bankrupt automatically. No court order is needed, and the share does not pass to the wife. The trustee's inaction for three years is exactly what triggers the reversion here."}, {"id": 1662, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Peter Lund died in 2026, leaving a death estate of £500,000 to his son. Six and a half years earlier Peter gave £300,000 in cash to his daughter, a potentially exempt transfer, and he made no other gifts. He lived in rented accommodation, so no residence nil rate band is available. Assume the nil rate band is £325,000 throughout, the rate of tax on death is 40 per cent, and taper relief reduces the tax on a gift made six to seven years earlier by eighty per cent. What Inheritance Tax is payable on the death estate?", "options": {"A": "£120,000, because only the gift is taxed at the death rate and the estate is covered by the nil rate band", "B": "£70,000, because the failed gift is ignored and the full nil rate band is set against the estate", "C": "£38,000, because taper relief reduces the tax on the estate by eighty per cent", "D": "£200,000, because no nil rate band applies once a lifetime gift has been taken into account", "E": "£190,000, because the gift uses £300,000 of the nil rate band, leaving only £25,000 to set against the estate"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (£190,000, because the gift uses £300,000 of the nil rate band, leaving only £25,000 to set against the estate): The gift to the daughter becomes chargeable because Peter died within seven years, and it is set against the nil rate band first, using £300,000 of it. Only £25,000 of the band remains for the estate, so £475,000 is taxed at 40 per cent, which is £190,000. No tax arises on the gift, so taper relief has nothing to reduce. The band cannot simply be applied to the estate while ignoring the earlier gift."}, {"id": 1663, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Ellis Joinery has traded for three years with annual taxable turnover well above the VAT registration threshold, but its owner, Mr Ellis, has not registered and has charged no VAT. He has instructed his solicitor, a partner in a firm with a money laundering reporting officer, to sell the business. He tells the solicitor that he knew he should have registered but wanted to save the tax. What should the solicitor do?", "options": {"A": "Report directly to HMRC, because the solicitor's duty to disclose tax offences overrides the duty of confidentiality owed to the client", "B": "Advise Mr Ellis to register and pay the VAT, and make a report only if he refuses to do so after receiving that advice", "C": "Make an internal report promptly to the firm's reporting officer, who decides whether to report to the NCA, and take care not to tip off Mr Ellis", "D": "Cease acting at once and return the papers, because a solicitor must not continue for a client who has admitted a tax offence", "E": "Continue to act without reporting, because the unpaid VAT is a civil debt to HMRC and does not amount to criminal property"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Make an internal report promptly to the firm's reporting officer, who decides whether to report to the NCA, and take care not to tip off Mr Ellis): Deliberately failing to register and charge VAT is tax evasion, and the money saved is criminal property, so the solicitor now has knowledge or suspicion and must make an internal report to the firm's reporting officer without delay. The reporting officer decides whether to make an authorised disclosure to the National Crime Agency, and the solicitor must not tip off the client or prejudice an investigation. Advising the client to correct the position does not remove the duty to report, and there is no direct duty to report to HMRC."}, {"id": 1664, "domain": "legal_services", "question": "Fiona, a senior partner, acts for a company settling an employment claim by Jerome, who alleges that a manager bullied and sexually harassed him. The company wants a confidentiality clause forbidding Jerome from speaking about the allegations to anyone, including the police and the regulator of the company's industry, and wants the clause drafted in general terms so that Jerome will assume it covers everything. Which of the following is the most appropriate course of action for Fiona?", "options": {"A": "Fiona may draft the clause as instructed, because her duty is to the client and the other side has independent advice", "B": "Fiona should refuse to draft it in that form, because an agreement cannot stop disclosures to regulators or the police and must not mislead Jerome", "C": "Fiona may draft the clause as instructed provided Jerome receives independent legal advice, because that cures any breach of her conduct obligations", "D": "Fiona may draft the clause as instructed if the company gives an undertaking to indemnify her against any later regulatory complaint", "E": "Fiona should draft the clause but add a warning to the company that it is probably unenforceable against any later disclosure"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fiona should refuse to draft it in that form, because an agreement cannot stop disclosures to regulators or the police and must not mislead Jerome): The SRA's warning notice on non-disclosure agreements says that a solicitor must not use them to prevent or discourage disclosures to regulators or law enforcement, or protected disclosures, and must not draft them to mislead the other party about their effect. A clause intended to deter Jerome from going to the police or a regulator is improper however it is worded. Independent advice for Jerome, an indemnity from the client or a warning to the client does not remove Fiona's own responsibility. She should explain to the company why a lawful confidentiality clause is needed."}, {"id": 1665, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Seyi Adewale has bought a terraced house in a residential street. It is not listed and not in a conservation area. He plans to remove two internal non load bearing walls, replace the kitchen and rewire the house, with no change to the outside of the building and no change in the way the house is used. His solicitor is asked whether planning permission is required. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Planning permission is required, because moving internal walls changes the use of rooms and is a material change of use", "B": "Planning permission is not required, because the works fall within a class of permitted development granted to every owner occupier", "C": "Planning permission is not required, but approval under the building regulations counts as consent under the planning legislation for works of this kind", "D": "Planning permission is not required, because works affecting only the interior of a building are excluded from the meaning of development", "E": "Planning permission is required, because any building operation, inside or outside, is development needing express permission from the local planning authority"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Planning permission is not required, because works affecting only the interior of a building are excluded from the meaning of development): Development in section 55 of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 includes building operations and material changes of use, but the section excludes maintenance, improvement or alteration that affects only the interior of a building or does not materially affect its external appearance. Seyi's works are wholly internal and the house stays a dwelling, so no planning permission is needed. Rearranging rooms is not a change of use, and permitted development rights are not engaged because the works are not development. Building regulations approval is a separate control and does not take the place of planning permission."}, {"id": 1666, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Alpha Bank agreed to lend Ravi money secured on his registered house and executed its charge on 1 March, registering it at HM Land Registry on 20 March. Beta Bank, which had no knowledge of Alpha's charge, took a charge from Ravi on 5 March and registered it on 10 March. Ravi has defaulted, and the house is worth less than the combined debts. Which of the following best describes the priority between the two banks?", "options": {"A": "Alpha Bank has priority, because its charge was created earlier and priority follows the date of creation", "B": "Beta Bank has priority only because it had no notice of Alpha's charge, which defeats an earlier unprotected charge", "C": "Alpha Bank has priority, because it advanced its money first and equity favours the earlier lender", "D": "The two banks rank equally and share the proceeds in proportion to their debts, because both charges were created in March", "E": "Beta Bank has priority, because registered charges rank in the order in which they are entered on the register"}, "flk": 2, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Beta Bank has priority, because registered charges rank in the order in which they are entered on the register): Under the Land Registration Act 2002 the priority of registered charges depends on the order in which they are entered on the register, not the date of creation or of the advance. Beta registered on 10 March, before Alpha on 20 March, so Beta ranks first. Notice is irrelevant to the order between registered charges, so Beta's lack of knowledge is not the reason it prevails. Equal ranking would apply only if the parties agreed it."}, {"id": 1667, "domain": "tort_law", "question": "Ms Cartwright is a front seat passenger in a car driven negligently by Mr Dobbs. The car collides head-on with a lorry. Ms Cartwright was not wearing her seat belt. Expert evidence is that, had she worn it, her injuries would have been considerably less severe but not avoided altogether. She sues Mr Dobbs, who pleads contributory negligence. Which of the following best describes the likely effect on her damages?", "options": {"A": "Her damages will be reduced by half, because fault is shared equally between a negligent driver and a careless passenger", "B": "Her damages will be reduced by about 15 per cent, as the belt would have made her injuries considerably less severe", "C": "Her claim will be defeated, because failing to wear a belt is a complete defence to the claim", "D": "Her damages will be reduced by 25 per cent, which is the usual starting point for failing to wear a belt in a collision", "E": "Her damages will not be reduced, because a passenger is entitled to rely on the driver to drive carefully"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her damages will be reduced by about 15 per cent, as the belt would have made her injuries considerably less severe): Contributory negligence reduces damages to the extent the court thinks just and equitable. For failing to wear a seat belt the guideline reductions are 25 per cent where the belt would have prevented the injury altogether and 15 per cent where it would have made it considerably less severe. Here the evidence supports the lower figure. Contributory negligence is a partial defence only, and the driver's fault does not remove the passenger's own."}, {"id": 1668, "domain": "business_law_and_practice", "question": "Nadia is a director of Pellet Ltd. The board proposes that the company will buy a fleet of vans from Axiom Ltd, a business in which Nadia holds 40% of the shares. The other two directors are unaware of her shareholding. Nadia asks when and to whom she must declare the nature and extent of her interest under section 177 of the Companies Act 2006. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "She must declare it to the other directors within 28 days after the company enters the transaction, and then record it in the minutes", "B": "She need declare it only if the other directors ask her, because the statutory duty arises on request and not automatically", "C": "She must declare it to the other directors before the company enters into the transaction, either at a board meeting or by notice", "D": "She must declare it to the members at the next general meeting, because the members are the persons to whom directors answer", "E": "She must declare it to the registrar at Companies House before the transaction, because the interest affects the public record"}, "flk": 1, "level": "recall", "ethics": false, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She must declare it to the other directors before the company enters into the transaction, either at a board meeting or by notice): A director interested in a proposed transaction must declare the nature and extent of the interest to the other directors before the company enters into it. The declaration may be made at a board meeting, by written notice or by general notice. Declaring afterwards is the rule for existing transactions, not proposed ones. Members and the registrar are not the recipients of a section 177 declaration."}, {"id": 1669, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Shaw Developments Ltd is a property developer. It wants to sue a groundworks contractor for £140,000 for defective piling that has caused cracking to a new apartment block. The dispute will turn on expert engineering evidence and the interpretation of a standard form building contract. Shaw's finance director says the company wants the claim determined quickly by a judge with experience of construction disputes. Which of the following is the best advice about where to issue?", "options": {"A": "Issue in the Chancery Division, because a claim about land and buildings falls within the jurisdiction of that Division", "B": "Issue in the Technology and Construction Court, because it is the specialist court for building disputes and has experienced judges", "C": "Issue in the Commercial Court, because it is the specialist court for contractual disputes between businesses worth over £100,000", "D": "Issue in the King's Bench Division general list, because only claims over £1 million may be started in a specialist list", "E": "Issue in the county court, because the county court has unlimited financial jurisdiction and will list the claim sooner than any High Court division"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Issue in the Technology and Construction Court, because it is the specialist court for building disputes and has experienced judges): The Technology and Construction Court deals with building and engineering disputes and its judges are experienced in the technical evidence these claims involve, which matches the client's wish. The claim exceeds £100,000, so the High Court is open. The Commercial Court is concerned with mercantile and financial contracts rather than building defects, and the Chancery Division deals with property and trusts matters. There is no £1 million threshold for specialist lists."}, {"id": 1670, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Zoe Palmer is charged with robbery of a jeweller's. A witness, Mrs Hale, saw the robber for several seconds in good light. Two hours later, police took Mrs Hale to the station, where Zoe was in a cell, and let her look through the door hatch. Mrs Hale said, 'That is her.' Zoe disputes that she was the robber. No video identification procedure was offered, although Zoe was available and asked for one. Zoe's solicitor wishes to challenge the identification evidence.", "options": {"A": "Make no application, because breaches of the identification Code affect only weight and cannot affect admissibility", "B": "Apply under section 78 to exclude it for the Code D breach, and seek a Turnbull direction if the judge admits it", "C": "Apply under section 76, because the identification is a confession obtained in circumstances making it unreliable", "D": "Ask for the case to be stayed as an abuse of process, because identification breaches mean a fair trial cannot occur", "E": "Apply under section 114 to exclude it as hearsay, because Mrs Hale's identification was made outside the courtroom"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Apply under section 78 to exclude it for the Code D breach, and seek a Turnbull direction if the judge admits it): Showing a suspect to a witness in a cell, when a formal video identification was available and requested, is a serious breach of the identification Code of Practice. The usual remedy is an application under section 78 to exclude the evidence for unfairness. If the judge admits it, the Turnbull direction must still be given to warn the jury of the risk of mistaken identification. Section 76 concerns confessions, and a stay for abuse of process is a more extreme remedy."}, {"id": 1671, "domain": "criminal_liability", "question": "At a party, Priti's drink is secretly laced with a strong sedative by a stranger. Priti becomes disinhibited and, finding her ex-partner Dean there, attacks him with a bottle intending to cause him serious injury, something she had often fantasised about. Medical evidence is that she would not have acted on her intention if sober. She is charged with wounding with intent under section 18. What is the most likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "Involuntary intoxication is not a defence if Priti in fact formed the required intent, even though the drug lowered her inhibitions", "B": "Priti is guilty of section 18 because she is treated as reckless in becoming intoxicated, as with voluntary intoxication", "C": "Priti has a defence only if she can show the sedative caused an abnormality of mental functioning arising from a recognised medical condition", "D": "Priti has a defence reducing liability to section 20, because involuntary intoxication operates as a partial defence to specific intent offences", "E": "Priti has a complete defence, because her intoxication was involuntary and she would not have committed the offence if sober"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Involuntary intoxication is not a defence if Priti in fact formed the required intent, even though the drug lowered her inhibitions): Involuntary intoxication is relevant only to whether the defendant formed the mental element. In Kingston the Court held that if the defendant had the intent, a drugged loss of inhibition is not a defence. Priti intended serious injury, so the offence is made out. Majewski recklessness is not the reasoning because her drinking was not voluntary, and the sedative is not a recognised medical condition for diminished responsibility."}, {"id": 1672, "domain": "criminal_law_and_practice", "question": "Nadia Okoye is arrested on suspicion of robbery, an indictable offence. The relevant time for her detention is 10.00 am on Monday. At 9.00 pm on Monday the investigating officer asks the station superintendent to authorise detention for up to 36 hours in total, and the superintendent agrees. The first review took place at 4.00 pm but the second review has not yet been held. Nadia's solicitor, Mr Patel, learns of the authorisation at 9.30 pm. What is the most accurate advice he can give her?", "options": {"A": "The authorisation is invalid, because no extension can be given before the second review, which is not due until about 1.00 am", "B": "The authorisation is valid, but it extends detention only for a further 24 hours from the time that it was given", "C": "The authorisation is invalid, because an inspector, not a superintendent, is the officer who can extend detention for robbery", "D": "The authorisation is valid, because a superintendent may extend detention to 36 hours at any point once the first review has taken place", "E": "The authorisation is invalid, because only a magistrates' court can authorise detention beyond 24 hours for an indictable offence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The authorisation is invalid, because no extension can be given before the second review, which is not due until about 1.00 am): Under PACE section 42, a superintendent may authorise detention up to 36 hours from the relevant time for an indictable offence, but not before the second review under section 40 has been carried out. The second review falls nine hours after the first, so it is not due until about 1.00 am, and a request at 9.00 pm is premature. The superintendent has the right rank, and a magistrates' court becomes involved only for a warrant of further detention beyond 36 hours. The extension should therefore be challenged and, if still wanted, sought again once the second review has taken place."}, {"id": 1673, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "A lease of an office suite granted in 2018 provides for rent review on the fifth anniversary and says the landlord must serve a trigger notice not later than three months before the review date. Nothing in the lease says time is of the essence for the review steps. The landlord served the trigger notice only two months before the review date. The tenant says the landlord is out of time and the review has been lost. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "The landlord may continue only with the court's permission, because late service of a trigger notice requires relief from the court", "B": "The review is valid, because time is not of the essence for rent review steps unless the lease says so or the circumstances clearly require it", "C": "The review proceeds, but the new rent runs only from the date the notice was served, because lateness postpones the effect", "D": "The review is valid only if the tenant shows no prejudice, because lateness is excused where it causes no harm to the tenant", "E": "The review is lost, because every step in a rent review timetable must be performed strictly on time to be effective"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The review is valid, because time is not of the essence for rent review steps unless the lease says so or the circumstances clearly require it): Time is presumed not to be of the essence for the steps in a rent review unless the lease expressly says so or the circumstances clearly indicate it. Nothing here makes time of the essence, so the late trigger notice does not lose the review. This differs from a break clause, where conditions are strictly enforced. No prejudice test applies and no court permission is needed."}, {"id": 1674, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Hargreave Antiques Ltd sold a landscape painting to Elias Rowe for £50,000. Both believed, from their own separate enquiries, that it was an early nineteenth century work, and neither said anything about its date to the other. Months later an expert showed that the painting is a modern copy worth about £500. Elias, who is an experienced collector, wishes to avoid the contract on the ground of mistake. Which of the following best describes the likely outcome?", "options": {"A": "The contract is void, because a mistake about the attribution of a painting is so fundamental that it destroys the agreement between the parties", "B": "The contract stands only if Hargreave can show it took reasonable care to check the date before selling the painting", "C": "The contract is void, because both parties shared the same mistaken belief and the price was agreed on that footing", "D": "The contract is voidable in equity, because the court may set aside a contract for common mistake on terms it thinks just", "E": "The contract stands, because the shared mistake as to quality does not make performance impossible and a buyer takes the risk of such a belief"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (The contract stands, because the shared mistake as to quality does not make performance impossible and a buyer takes the risk of such a belief): Common mistake renders a contract void only in narrow circumstances, where a shared assumption makes performance impossible or the subject matter essentially different. A mistake as to the quality or date of a painting does not meet that threshold, since the painting can still be delivered. The former equitable power to set aside such contracts was held not to exist, so that route is closed. A buyer who agrees a price on a belief about quality takes the risk it is wrong."}, {"id": 1675, "domain": "property_law_and_practice", "question": "Callum Reid instructs you to buy a freehold flat in Bristol for £255,000 with a mortgage. Searches are clear and the lender's offer has been received, but your report on title has not been sent because the lease contains an unusual ground rent review clause. The seller's agent says another buyer will exchange today unless Callum commits, and Callum telephones asking you to exchange contracts at once. What should you do?", "options": {"A": "Exchange now as instructed and send the report on the review clause afterwards, because the client's wish to proceed outweighs the need for advice beforehand", "B": "Decline to exchange until the seller's agent confirms in writing that the other buyer has withdrawn, so that Callum is not under time pressure", "C": "Exchange now but insert a special condition allowing Callum to withdraw if the ground rent review clause later proves unfavourable to him", "D": "Report on the ground rent review clause, obtain Callum's informed instructions, confirm funds and lender requirements, and exchange only when he authorises it", "E": "Telephone the lender for consent to exchange and treat that as sufficient, since the lender's offer already covers the leasehold terms"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": true, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Report on the ground rent review clause, obtain Callum's informed instructions, confirm funds and lender requirements, and exchange only when he authorises it): A solicitor must give the client the information needed to take informed decisions before a binding commitment, and exchange of contracts commits the buyer. The report must therefore deal with the review clause, and Callum must authorise exchange, with funds and the lender's requirements confirmed. Pressure from the seller's agent does not change that duty. Exchanging first and advising afterwards would leave Callum bound without having understood what he is buying."}, {"id": 1676, "domain": "land_law", "question": "Karl owns a house in which he does not live. He allowed Lena to occupy the upstairs room under a written agreement headed 'licence', which gives her a key and the right to stay for twelve months for £500 a month. A clause permits Karl to move other occupiers into the room at any time, but Karl has never done so and has no intention of doing so. Lena is now being asked to leave on one day's notice. Which of the following best describes her status?", "options": {"A": "She is a tenant, because she has exclusive possession for a term at a rent and the clause allowing others in is a pretence", "B": "She is a licensee, because the parties chose to call the agreement a licence, she signed it, and the label shows what both of them intended", "C": "She is a tenant only if Karl has granted her a written lease by deed for the term", "D": "She is a licensee, because a single room within a house cannot be the subject of a tenancy", "E": "She is a licensee, because Karl's reserved right to introduce other occupiers shows that she lacks exclusive possession"}, "flk": 2, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She is a tenant, because she has exclusive possession for a term at a rent and the clause allowing others in is a pretence): Under Street v Mountford the label is not decisive. A person with exclusive possession for a term at a rent is a tenant unless special circumstances apply. Lena has a key, a fixed term and a monthly rent, and the clause allowing others in, which Karl never intended to use, is a pretence that the court will disregard. A tenancy does not need a deed to exist, and a single room can be let."}, {"id": 1677, "domain": "dispute_resolution", "question": "Mellor Engineering Ltd issued a claim form on Monday 13 April 2026 and decided to serve it itself by first class post. On Thursday 13 August 2026 its solicitor posted the claim form and particulars of claim to the defendant's principal office, so under the deemed service rules the claim form is deemed served on Monday 17 August 2026. The defendant says that service was out of time because the four month validity period had ended by then. Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"A": "Service was in time, but Mellor must also obtain an order extending the claim form's validity before 17 August arrives", "B": "Service was out of time, because the claim form must be served within four months of the particulars of claim", "C": "Service was out of time, because deemed service on 17 August falls after the four month validity period has ended", "D": "Service was out of time, because first class post is not a permitted method for serving a claim form on a company", "E": "Service was in time, because the step of posting was taken before midnight on the final day of the validity period"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Service was in time, because the step of posting was taken before midnight on the final day of the validity period): The four month period in the Civil Procedure Rules is a period for taking the step required for service, such as posting, before midnight on the calendar day four months after the date of issue. Mellor posted on 13 August, which is exactly four months after 13 April, so the step was in time. The later deemed date of service does not matter for validity. First class post is a permitted method, and no extension order is needed where the step was taken in time."}, {"id": 1678, "domain": "wills_and_administration_of_estates", "question": "Mr Salmon signed his will alone at home on Monday, intending it to take effect as his will. On Friday he met two friends, Una and Vik, who were together in his living room. He showed them the signature page and said, 'That is my signature on this will.' Una and Vik then each signed the will beside his signature, in his presence, although neither had seen him sign it. Is the will validly executed?", "options": {"A": "No, because each witness must have read the will before signing, in order to attest to its contents", "B": "Yes, but only if the will contains an attestation clause stating that the witnesses saw him sign", "C": "No, because the witnesses should have signed in each other's presence rather than only in the testator's presence", "D": "No, because the testator must sign in the physical presence of both witnesses acting together, and acknowledgement cannot replace this", "E": "Yes, because he acknowledged his signature to two witnesses present together, and each then attested and signed in his presence"}, "flk": 2, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "E", "explanation": "The correct answer is E (Yes, because he acknowledged his signature to two witnesses present together, and each then attested and signed in his presence): Section 9 of the Wills Act 1837 requires the testator's signature to be made or acknowledged in the presence of two witnesses present at the same time, and each witness must then attest and sign in the testator's presence. The testator may acknowledge an earlier signature, so signing alone beforehand does not matter. Witnesses need not know the contents or sign in each other's presence, and an attestation clause is evidential rather than essential."}, {"id": 1679, "domain": "contract_law", "question": "Delia Frame agreed to redecorate Colm Reid's house for a fixed price of £10,000. She finished every room except for touching up paint on two skirting boards, which would cost about £150 to complete, and then left because of illness. Colm has paid nothing and refuses to pay until she finishes. The contract has no clause on stage payments. Which of the following best describes Delia's entitlement?", "options": {"A": "She may recover the full £10,000, because minor defects are Colm's concern and he has accepted the benefit of the work done", "B": "She may recover a reasonable sum for the work done, but not the contract price, because the contract is not fully performed", "C": "She cannot recover anything, because an entire obligation to decorate must be fully performed before any payment falls due", "D": "She may recover the contract price less the cost of completing the work, because she has substantially performed", "E": "She may recover only a proportion of the price for the rooms completed, because the work is divisible by room"}, "flk": 1, "level": "application", "ethics": false, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She may recover the contract price less the cost of completing the work, because she has substantially performed): Where an obligation is entire, performance must normally be complete, but substantial performance allows the contractor to claim the price less a deduction for incomplete or defective work. The £150 shortfall against £10,000 is a minor omission, so Delia has substantially performed. Colm is entitled to deduct the cost of finishing the skirting boards. Refusing payment altogether or allowing a pro rata sum ignores that doctrine."}, {"id": 1680, "domain": "legal_system_of_england_and_wales", "question": "Mrs Justice Larkin sits as a High Court judge hearing a negligence claim by Dunmore Farms against a supplier. A Court of Appeal decision from three years ago, directly on the legal point, supports the supplier. The claimant's counsel relies on a recent Supreme Court judgment in an unrelated case, in which the justices remarked, not as part of the decision, that the Court of Appeal decision was wrong. The facts of the present case are nearly identical to the Court of Appeal case. What approach should the judge take?", "options": {"A": "Follow the Court of Appeal decision, which is binding on her, and treat the Supreme Court remark as persuasive but not binding", "B": "Disregard the Court of Appeal decision, because the Supreme Court has criticised it and the facts are almost the same", "C": "Follow the Supreme Court remark, because a statement from a higher court overrides a Court of Appeal ratio on the same point", "D": "Choose freely between the two, because the Supreme Court remark and the Court of Appeal ratio are both persuasive in the High Court", "E": "Distinguish the Court of Appeal decision on its facts, because the judge may depart from a binding case whenever she doubts it"}, "flk": 1, "level": "diagnose", "ethics": false, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Follow the Court of Appeal decision, which is binding on her, and treat the Supreme Court remark as persuasive but not binding): A High Court judge is bound by the ratio of a Court of Appeal decision. Remarks by the Supreme Court that were not necessary to its decision are obiter, so they carry great weight but do not bind. A Supreme Court ratio on the point, or a later Court of Appeal decision, could displace the Court of Appeal authority in this court, but neither exists here. Distinguishing is available where there is a real difference in material facts, and here the facts are nearly identical."}];
